INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 6, 3, 'his soul. His person. goodness. Good things; see 2:24. no burial. Shameful, distressing, given his importance and large family; see Jer. 22:18–19; 36:30, about King Jehoiakim.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 6, 5, 'this. The fetus. has more rest. Is better off—on rest see 4:6–8; 5:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 6, 6, 'seen. Experienced. goodness. Good things; see 2:24. all go to one place. Stillborn and lifelong struggler both end up dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 6, 9, 'sight of the eyes. Means contentment; see 1 Tim. 6:6. wandering of desire. Longing for one thing after another. This. I.e., wandering desire. vanity … wind. Unsatisfying (1:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 6, 10, 'Life is decided beforehand; God and destiny overpower humans; compare Job 9:19–22; 30:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 6, 12, 'vain life. Life of vanity; see 1:14. like a shadow. Lacking concreteness, permanence. Who can tell. No one knows. under the sun. See 1:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 1, 'name. Same as reputation. ointment. Perfume.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 2, 'take it to heart. Think about it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 9, 'hasten in your spirit. See Prov. 14:29; 15:18; 16:32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 10, 'former days. See 1:11. you do not inquire wisely. Wisdom doesn’t prompt such thinking.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 12, 'excellence. Advantage. gives life. Preserves life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 14, 'find out nothing. Humans cannot predict the future, despite life’s monotony (1:4–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 15, 'vanity. Here emphasizes life’s brevity. prolongs life. Lives on.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 18, 'escape them all. Advice to avoid all extremes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 23, 'A restatement of 1:13, 17; 2:1–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 27, 'Adding … to find out. As I searched for life’s meaning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 7, 29, 'many schemes. Both good and bad ideas.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 8, 1, 'interpretation of a thing. The author continues his search for life’s meaning (1:13). makes his face shine. Wisdom produces more pleasant appearance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 8, 3, 'be hasty. A call to appreciate the privilege of the king’s presence. evil thing. An issue against the king. whatever pleases him. He can get back at you.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 8, 4, 'The king is authoritative due to his great power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 8, 5, 'keeps his command. He obeys the king. heart. Mind. time and judgment. Reckoning time will come; see 3:15, 17; 12:13–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 8, 8, 'Wind and life-breath (the same word translated “spirit”) are both beyond human control. that war. The struggle to live. not deliver. Evildoing doesn’t help.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 8, 15, 'commended enjoyment. To live as if there were no judgment; see 3:15–17. under the sun. See 1:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 1, 'There is one thing which is predictable: death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 1, 'Humans cannot see the future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 3, 'All are destined to die; see 1:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 4, 'Life is always better than death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 5, 'the dead know nothing. The dead are in a state of unconscious sleep and have no part in anything that is going on in earth or heaven. The Bible lends no support to the doctrine of a conscious state between death and the resurrection. For more on unconsciousness in DEATH, see Ps. 6:5; 115:17; 146:4. On DEATH being a sleep, see Job 14:12; Dan. 12:2; John 11:11–12; 1 Cor. 15:51; 1 Thess. 4:13–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 8, 'oil. Here it refers to “perfumed ointments.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 10, 'work … wisdom. There is no activity or intelligence in the grave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 11, 'I returned and saw. I also noticed something else; see 1:14. under the sun. See 1:3. time and chance. Uncertainty that contrasts with certainty of “time and judgment” (8:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 12, 'snared in an evil time. Death’s arrival is unexpected and unwelcome.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 9, 13, 'Life’s unfairness and meaninglessness (“vanity”) illustrated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 2, 'heart. Mind. at his right hand. In a position of power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 3, 'walks along the way. A description of general conduct.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 4, 'Do not leave your post. Or stay calm and continue to do your duty faithfully.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 6, 'great dignity. Holding prominent positions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 10, 'wisdom brings success. Wisdom makes life easier.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 12, 'Fools’ speech is self-destructive (v. 12), nonsensical (v. 13), and wordy (v. 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 15, 'labor of fools. The term for “labor” signifies hard work and its satisfying reward, but fools find it a burden. to the city. To any meaningful destination.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 16, 'a child. One who is unprepared to rule. feast in the morning. Suggests overeating as priority; see v. 17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 19, 'answers everything. Fixes everything, or, perhaps, drives everybody; compare 5:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 10, 20, 'Always hold your tongue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 1, 'Some lessons from nature.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 1, 'Good deeds will have their reward, see v. 2; 10:8–10; Luke 6:38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 2, 'seven … eight. Numerical progression meaning “as many as possible”; insurance for the time disaster (“evil”) strikes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 3, 'A description of life’s regularities (1:4–11), despite irregularities (3:11; 7:14; 8:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 6, 'sow your seed. A call to face life’s uncertainties with diligence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 7, 'light is sweet. Simple rejoicing in being alive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 8, 'days of darkness. They symbolize death. vanity. Satisfaction aborted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 9, 'heart cheer you. Let your mind be contented. Walk … heart. Follow your mind. judgment. The teacher’s full answer is joy and judgment (3:11–15; 12:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 11, 10, 'remove sorrow. This forms the first part of the answer (2:24–26; 3:12). put away evil. The final part of the answer in view of coming judgment, v. 9; 12:13–14. childhood and youth are vanity. Living merely for happiness is futile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 1, 'Concluding poetry for youth about aging and dying. Some metaphors of the process are more clear than others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 1, 'Remember. An appeal for reflection; see 11:8. Creator. The term evokes Gen. 1:1. difficult days. Old age and death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 2, 'sun … moon. Fading lights. This is a picture of increasing gloom. clouds. Rain clouds increase the image of gloom and darkness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 3, 'keepers of the house. A poetic reference to hands, arms, legs. strong men bow down. Back bent over. grinders. Teeth. those that look. Eyes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 4, 'doors. Perhaps sense of hearing. sound of a bird. Despite poorer hearing, one can still hear birds’ shrill calls. daughters of music. Sounds of music. brought low. Music is no longer audible.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 5, 'almond tree blossoms. Perhaps hair turning white. grasshopper is a burden. Perhaps alludes to locusts’ destructive power; or implies that the weight of a grasshopper is too heavy to carry. desire fails. Desire is no longer felt. eternal home. The grave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 7, 'dust will return … spirit will return. Death reverses God’s creation (Gen. 2:7). The word “spirit” appears 379 times in the OT, but not once does it denote an intelligent entity capable of existence apart from a physical body, so far as humans are concerned. That which returns to God is simply the life principle imparted by God to both humans and beasts (see Eccl. 3:19–21). For more on DEATH, see 9:5–6 and note on Gen. 2:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 8, 'Vanity of vanities … All is vanity. Death is the ultimate frustration of life and all efforts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 9, 'The teacher’s writing activity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 12, 'The author’s acknowledgement: research and writing are unending and exhausting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Eclesiastes', 12, 13, 'The teacher’s final answer: Live life with awareness of the final judgment. Judgment functions as a motivation for service to God. On the LAW being the standard of God’s JUDGMENT, see James 2:12.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 0, NULL, 'The title of the Song of Solomon (also called “The Song of Songs” or “Canticles”) may be literally translated, “The Song of Songs, which [is] to/belonging to/for Solomon” (1:1). The phrase “Song of Songs” expresses the superlative, “the best or most sublime of songs” (compare Ex. 29:37; Deut. 10:17; Eccl. 1:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 0, NULL, 'The weight of evidence favors the traditional interpretation of 1:1, “belonging to [i.e., composed by] Solomon.” In Hebrew this phrase is the normal way of expressing authorship when an author has written multiple works (as in the titles of many psalms; see the Introduction to Psalms, “Author and Date”).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 0, NULL, 'Until the time of the Protestant Reformation, allegorical interpretation of the Song of Solomon held sway. The literal sense of the Song, with its erotic imagery, was considered as the worthless “husk” to be taken away to find the “kernel,” the “true” meaning, i.e., the yearning of the soul for union with God, or the expression of Christ’s love for His church. John Calvin broke with the allegorical view and insisted that the Song be interpreted as a song of human love. In modern times a consensus has emerged that takes the Song of Solomon in its plain and literal sense.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 0, NULL, 'The singular “Song” (not “songs”) in the title indicates that this is a unified composition, not a collection of songs. The unity of the Song of Solomon is revealed by refrains and other repetitions, consistent portrayal of the characters involved, and a basic story line and careful literary structure.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 1, 'song of songs … Solomon’s. The most sublime of Solomon’s 1,005 songs (see Introduction).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 2, 'Let him kiss me. Or, “Oh that he would kiss me.” Expresses the wish of the woman before the wedding. 1:2–3:5 portray the courtship of Solomon and his beloved. The lovers enjoy times of intimate fellowship together, but there is no reference to sexual intercourse until the consummation of the marriage in 4:16–5:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 3, 'fragrance … ointments. Love in the Song is wonderfully sensuous, involving all the senses. name is ointment poured forth. Refers to attractiveness of the whole character and personality of Solomon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 4, 'chambers. The plural refers to the whole palace of the king, and not just his bedchamber. The woman’s female companions accompany her (see the parallel in Ps. 45:13–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 5, 'dark, but lovely. Deeply tanned by the sun, considered undesirable by the lighter-complexioned city maidens. daughters of Jerusalem. Or Zion, 3:11. The woman’s female companions, who sing, or are addressed, numerous times (1:4–5, 11; 2:7; 3:5, 10–11; 5:8–9, 16; 6:1, 8–9; 8:4). Solomon’s male companions are also mentioned (1:7; 3:7–8; 6:13; 8:13). Kedar. Nomadic tribe in Northern Arabia whose name means “dark”; noted for tents made of black goats’ hair.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 6, 'mother. Mentioned seven times (here; 3:4, 11; 6:9; 8:1–2, 5). my own vineyard. The woman’s body (compare 8:12). Busy in her outdoor work, she had not the leisure time or resources to give special attention to her appearance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 7, 'feed your flock. The Song portrays Solomon as both shepherd and king. Son of a former shepherd (David), Solomon may have even disguised himself as a shepherd in wooing the woman. Solomon typifies God, who in Scripture is also portrayed as Shepherd and King (Ps. 23; 95–97). one who veils herself. A prostitute. She did not want to be taken for a prostitute as she looked for her beloved among the shepherds.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 8, 'fairest among women. One of ten terms of endearment used by Solomon for his beloved (see v. 9; 2:10, 14; 4:8–9; 5:2; 7:1, 6). The woman also used pet names for Solomon (see 1:7, 13; 2:16; 5:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 9, 'Solomon compares his beloved to a mare exciting Egyptian stallions. This would speak to the heart of his beloved, Pharaoh’s daughter (see Introduction). The pharaoh was probably Siamun (978–959 B.C.), the next-to-last ruler in the 21st (Tanite) Egyptian Dynasty. Since the woman’s mother and brothers but not the father are mentioned in the Song, and since the setting seems to be northern Palestine, it is possible that Solomon’s beloved was fathered by Siamun in a sexual encounter while on a campaign in the Egyptian-dominated territory of northern Palestine, where she grew up and where Solomon met and fell in love with her.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 1, 12, 'spikenard … henna. Exotic, aromatic spices, here and elsewhere in the Song, suggest the fullness of sensuous delights (see also 3:6; 4:6, 13–14; 5:5, 13). En Gedi. An oasis along the western side of the Dead Sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 1, 'rose. Probably the crocus. Sharon. The coastal plain south of Mount Carmel. lily. The “lilies” of the Song (vv. 1–2, 16; 4:5; 5:13; 6:2–3; 7:2) are probably lotus blossoms. The woman is not modestly describing herself as an ordinary flower of the field; rather, she expresses confident awareness of her blossoming beauty, comparing herself with the two flowers that epitomize beauty in the symbolism of the Bible (see Is. 35:1–2; Hos. 14:6–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 3, 'apple tree … banqueting house. Metaphors for Solomon himself; his “fruit” sweet to her taste stands for the sweet fellowship between them. I sat down … great delight. Or, “I stayed on and on and took delight over and over.” A fitting description, typologically, of the believer’s experience of intimate communion with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 5, 'cakes of raisins … apples. Symbols of sexual passion in cultural love songs.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 6, 'The first refrain in the Song. Dividing the Song’s twelve stanzas, refrains are also found at vv. 16–17; 3:5, 11; 4:6–7; 5:1, 8; 6:3, 10; 7:10; and 8:3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 6, 'His left hand … embraces me. Better understood as a wish, the expression implies affection and support, not sexual stimulation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 7, 'gazelles … does of the field. Animals symbolizing love. The Hebrew for “gazelles” and “does of the field” sound similar to names for God. The refrain thus links love (personified in the oath) with the divine presence without making sex divine. Until it pleases. Or “until it is the right time.” The woman emphasizes appropriate restraint in the repeated solemn appeal to her female companions (3:5; 8:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 8, 'An exquisite portrayal of the lovers’ courtship period, enjoying springtime beauty in nature.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 9, 'gazelle. Noted for its swiftness, beauty, and gracefulness (see also v. 17; 4:5; 7:3; 8:14). The Hebrew word for “gazelle” means both the animal and “beauty.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 14, 'dove. Symbolic of purity and innocence (compare 5:2; 6:9; Matt. 10:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 15, 'little foxes … vines. Refers to various obstacles that could interfere with the development of the love relationship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 16, 'My beloved is mine, and I am his. See 6:3 and 7:10. These verses convey equality and mutuality between the lovers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 2, 17, 'Bether. Literally, “cleavage,” probably an allusion to the woman’s breasts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 3, 1, 'In her night dream, the woman searches anxiously for her lover (compare 5:2–8). As in the garden of Eden, lovers need each other to be whole.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 3, 4, 'her who conceived me. Although the Song mentions conception and birth (see also 6:9; 8:2, 5), sexual union of the lovers is never linked to having children. Sexuality is given independent meaning and value.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 3, 6, 'The wedding procession of Solomon and his bride coming up to Jerusalem, paralleling 1 Kin. 3:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 3, 7, 'couch … palanquin. A covered and curtained portable chair for royalty, carried on men’s shoulders.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 3, 10, 'love. Or leather.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 1, 'A portrayal of the wedding ceremony proper. Only here does Solomon address the woman as his “spouse” (4:8–12; 5:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 1, 'A portrayal of the wedding ceremony proper. Only here does Solomon address the woman as his “spouse” (4:8–12; 5:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 1, 'Both bride and groom describe their lover’s beauty with exuberant praise (vv. 1–15; 5:10–16; 6:4–10; 7:1–9), similar to the praise songs in modern rural Syrian weddings. The mutual praises present dynamic strength and movement and often refer to moral qualities of the lovers (see notes on 1:3; 2:14; 4:4, 7–8; 5:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 4, 'tower of David. The military tower imagery (see also 7:4; 8:10) symbolizes not only the woman’s beauty and strength, but purity and virginity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 6, 'myrrh … frankincense. The two mountains of spices may allude to the bride’s two breasts (mentioned in the previous verse).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 7, 'all fair … no spot in you. Description of the bride’s physical and moral qualities, typifying Christ’s bride, the church (compare Eph. 5:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 8, 'Lebanon … Hermon. Secluded mountain peaks on the distant northern horizon, symbolizing the inaccessible character of the woman (compare 2:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 9, 'sister. A common term of endearment in cultural love poetry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 12, 'A garden enclosed. Denotes virginity. At the time of the wedding the woman is still a virgin. In the Song sexual intercourse between the lovers occurs only within the context of the marriage covenant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 4, 16, 'Awake … milk. The highpoint and exact center of the book (with 111 poetic lines on either side of these two verses), equivalent to the modern wedding vows; the bride invites, and the groom accepts, the invitation to consummate marriage through sexual union. Eat, O friends! This authoritative voice, pronouncing approval and blessing upon the marriage and its physical consummation, is probably that of God Himself, who officiated at the first garden wedding in Eden (Gen. 2:22–24). For other references/allusions to God in the Song, see notes on 2:7 and 8:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 5, 2, 'The latter half of the book depicts the couple’s married life, and contains numerous references to foreplay and sexual union (see, e.g., 5:2–5; 7:7–8, 12–13; 8:2, 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 5, 2, 'The latter half of the book depicts the couple’s married life, and contains numerous references to foreplay and sexual union (see, e.g., 5:2–5; 7:7–8, 12–13; 8:2, 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 5, 10, 'Chief among ten thousand. Solomon’s distinguished qualities typify the beauty of Christ’s character (compare v. 16 and Ps. 45:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 5, 15, 'Lebanon … cedars. Tall, majestic evergreen trees symbolic of strength, steadfastness, and exquisite worth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 6, 4, 'Tirzah. A splendorous city in northern Palestine, name meaning “pleasure, beauty.” Reference to this city alongside Jerusalem implies authorship during the Solomonic period, the narrow window of history when Tirzah was prominent but not yet under control of an enemy state (see 1 Kin. 14:17; 15:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 6, 8, 'sixty queens And eighty concubines. This does not refer to Solomon’s harem, as many have supposed. For at least the first twenty years of Solomon’s reign he was monogamous (see Introduction), and the Song presupposes an exclusive monogamous relationship (e.g., v. 3; 1:7; 2:2–3, 7, 16; 3:6–11; 5:1, 10; 7:10; 8:6–7). Solomon does not say “I have” but “There are,” and mentions not only queens and concubines but also innumerable virgins. However many other women of whatever status there may be around, his beloved is to him by far the best, his “only one” (v. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 6, 10, 'Fair … banners. Solomon’s praise of his wife’s beauty, a typological description of the church triumphant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 6, 12, 'chariots of my noble people. May refer to the woman’s royal Egyptian lineage (see note on 1:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 6, 13, 'Shulamite. Probably the feminine form of the word “Solomon,” the equivalent of our modern “Mrs. Solomon.” two camps. Or Mahanaim (see Gen. 32:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 7, 1, 'prince’s daughter. Implies royal lineage (i.e., as Pharaoh’s daughter); compare Ps. 45:13; 72.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 7, 4, 'Heshbon. See Num. 21:26; Josh. 13:15–17. A city known for its large open-air water reservoir.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 7, 5, 'Mount Carmel. A fifteen-mile ridge of mountains in Northern Palestine covered with lush plant growth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 7, 9, 'Moving gently the lips of sleepers. Literally, gliding over the lips. Probably refers to her lips and teeth in the process of kissing (as interpreted by the ancient versions).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 7, 12, 'love … mandrakes. The Hebrew word for “love” sounds similar and is related to the word for “mandrake,” an ancient aphrodisiac.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 2, 'She who used to instruct me. Or “you will instruct me.” The Hebrew grammar allows for either the mother, or Solomon, as the instructor of the Shulamite.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 5, 'The Shulamite reflects on her love relationship with Solomon from its beginning to the present. Her wisdom statements about love deriving from the Lord (see note on 8:6) reveal that she is a believer in the true God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 5, 'awakened you. Alludes to sexual arousal (see 2:7; 3:5; 8:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 6, 'A host of literary devices converge to mark this verse as the climactic peak of the Song. Here we find the fullest description of the supreme subject of the Song, i.e., love. seal. Used to stamp documents (like the modern signature), worn on the “arm” (finger) or on a chain around the neck (over the heart). The Shulamite wishes to be permanently over Solomon’s heart, the seat of his affections, and on his arm, the source of his strength. Jealousy as cruel. Also, “intense love, unyielding.” Just as the grave does not give up its dead, so intense love will never give up the beloved. A most vehement flame. Best translated literally, “A flame of Yah,” i.e., the “LORD.” This expression, reserved for the thematic climax of the entire Song, reveals that Yahweh is the Source of human love, and thus provides the basis for the typological interpretation of the Song (see Introduction, “Content and Themes”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 8, 'The Shulamite reflects on her days of adolescence when she was under the care of her brothers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 9, 'wall … door. Symbolizes the two paths she could choose as a young woman—to be pure and inaccessible (a wall) or to be promiscuous and “available” (a doorway). The Shulamite makes clear that she has been morally pure, impregnable to the advances of anyone but her husband.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 11, 'Baal Hamon. Location unknown.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Cânticos', 8, 14, 'Make haste. The Hebrew word here may also mean “penetrate.” Thus the Song ends with tantalizing erotic ambiguity. The passionate, romantic love of Solomon and the Shulamite does not lessen in intensity as they continue to enjoy married life together.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 0, NULL, 'The book states that Isaiah, son of Amoz, is the author (1:1). His name means “the LORD saves,” which fits the book’s theme. Isaiah had a family and at least two sons, Shear-Jashub (7:3) and Maher-Shalal-Hash-Baz (8:3). His sons’ names also are related to his mission because their names mean, respectively, “a remnant shall return” and “speed the spoil, hasten the booty.” The themes of salvation, judgment, and remnant run throughout the book. Isaiah begins the major section of the Hebrew Bible called the “Latter Prophets” (Isaiah-Malachi) and is the longest book in that section. Some see him as the greatest of the biblical prophets.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 0, NULL, 'Interwoven throughout the book are oracles of judgment and salvation. Oracles of judgment are set in the form of indictments against God’s people. However, they go beyond God’s people to judge other nations or people groups as well. The language used for judgment is similar but not identical to ancient Near Eastern lawsuits, court hearings, or trials. The biblical covenant provides a unique frame of reference that affects the form of ancient Near Eastern treaties and contributes content that is particular to the unique relationship between the Lord and His covenant people and between the unique sovereign God and the nations. The language used in chapters 24–27 similarly expresses the universal nature of God’s judgment and salvation. The book also goes beyond current events of the eighth and seventh centuries to include events beyond Old Testament history. Messianic prophecies and eschatological events are part of the very fabric of the book of Isaiah.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 0, NULL, 'The portrait of God in the book of Isaiah reaches heights unparalleled in the Old Testament. God’s holiness is emphasized here more than anywhere else (“the Holy One of Israel” appears 26 times in Isaiah and only 6 times elsewhere in the OT). The holiness of God encapsulates His attributes of justice, righteousness, and love. This is the God who is determined to bring about a kingdom of justice, righteousness, and peace.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 0, NULL, 'Understanding the book of Isaiah is a pathway to grasping key components of Jesus’ life and teachings. Isaiah’s four “Servant Songs” (42:1–9; 49:1–13; 50:4–11; 52:13–53:12) describe the coming messianic King and are key to the New Testament understanding of Jesus.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 0, NULL, 'I.      Visions of judgment and salvation (1:1–35:10)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 1, 'vision of Isaiah. This expression most likely applies to the whole book. It is grounded in God’s covenant with Israel along with its stipulations or expectations of righteousness from God’s people. This vision is also an interpretation of and an insight into contemporary circumstances of God’s people. They are involved in religious rituals even while they have abandoned God, act unjustly, and oppress the poor. They trust world powers like Assyria or Egypt rather than God. Consequently, the book is a vision of judgment. The book is also a future vision of hope in God’s determination to fulfill His purpose through the Messiah and through a remnant. The vision culminates in the future creation of a new heaven and a new earth: a vision of the end of harm and evil, a vision of blessing, reconciliation, and peace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 2, 'This chapter diagnoses the problem of God’s people. It also indicates the solution to the problem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 2, 'The problem is identified as rebellion against God, lack of knowledge and understanding. God’s people have forsaken their God. The accusation of lack of knowledge and understanding is frequent in the prophetic books (see Hos. 4:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 2, 'Hear, O heavens. The way the book begins fits with a call to witnesses, typical of covenant/treaty lawsuits, or court scenes, common in the ancient Near East. In such cases a defendant is charged with having violated the covenant stipulations (see also Deut. 32; Jer. 2:9–19; Mic. 6:1–8). God is introduced as Judge and the witnesses are the heavens and the earth. The choice of witnesses adds solemnity to the trial and heightens the importance of the charges.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 4, 'Alas. An interjection variously translated as “Ah!” or “Oh!” It introduces messages of doom, and it portends tragedy. The accumulation of words that describe negative behavior and actions intensify the charge. sinful nation, a people laden with iniquity. Children who act destructively and have abandoned the Lord. They have despised the Holy One, and they literally have estranged themselves. God’s people had gone back to an alien status. What this verse portrays is a loss of status and dignity. The further comparison with Sodom and Gomorrah shows the outcome of their path leading to alienation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 5, 'Judgment has come to Israel along with its destruction and devastation. The image of a sick body and that of a city destroyed by war and subsequent hardship heighten the seriousness of what happens to God’s people. There is a striking connection between vv. 5 and 6 and chap. 53. The words “stricken,” “wound,” and “bruise” are applied to both God’s people as judgment against their sin and to the Servant’s vicarious suffering. The theme of the remnant appears for the first time in this setting. It indicates a few survivors that God preserved (see Rom. 9:29). The fact that God prevents total destruction opens the door for hope, a hope that will be developed later in the book. See Is. 6:13 and its reference to the image of a stump identified as a holy seed that will remain. See also 10:20; 11:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 9, 'LORD of hosts. “Hosts” can refer to the Lord’s armies, the angelic beings, or the whole of creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 10, 'The themes of this section address the religious situation of God’s people. It begins with the sentence “Hear the word of the LORD,” and concludes with “For the mouth of the LORD has spoken.” As paradoxical as it may seem, God’s people do not lack religiosity. The worship continues as usual while injustice grows. In the face of rampant injustice, a multitude of sacrifices, offerings, incense, appointed festivals, new moons and Sabbaths, and prayer all avail to nothing. They are actually described as an abomination (see also Amos 5:21–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 16, 'Ceremonial washing without giving up evil deeds and committing to justice avails nothing and consists of religious hypocrisy (see 52:11; 55:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 17, 'The basic intent of the covenant is justice or fairness to all people and respect of the rights of all people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 18, 'let us reason. The invitation to reason together reveals something attractive about God’s character and purposes. God is helping His people become mature. He engages them as partners rather than as a ruler only concerned about the carrying out of His will. The change He desires among His people requires their participation in the diagnosis of their true condition. God shows His willingness to forgive and to save His people. He pleads with them so that they might choose life in choosing the God of life. The other option is inevitable death. Later in the book, God’s people are accused of making a covenant with death (28:15, 18). Even so God wants to save them. This gives an idea of the depth of God’s love for His people, a God who would not let His people go without doing all He can to save them from disaster.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 21, 'These verses depict the corruption of the social structure. The judgment is coming because of the lack of justice and righteousness. Murders, rebellion, theft, the practice of taking bribes, and ignoring the cause of the defenseless were widespread. God intervenes in order to turn the unfaithful city into a city of righteousness. In v. 26 there is a note of hope, which constitutes a fitting transition for vv. 27–31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 27, 'The conclusion of the first chapter combines both messages of salvation and judgment, this time in the form of warning. God will prevail in redeeming His people by justice and righteousness; obviously redemption is for those who repent. Those who persist in rebellion will encounter executive judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 29, 'terebinth trees. The sites of terebinth trees and gardens were popular shrines for idolatrous worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 1, 31, 'no one shall quench. The fire will burn itself out; conveys the idea of total destru');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 1, 'This vision is found in Micah with slight variations (Mic. 4:1–3). in the latter days. The phrase has been interpreted as a vague expression equivalent to “in God’s time”; however, in Isaiah, it has an end-time connotation. The context indicates a time when all wars will cease. The equivalent expression “in that day,” found in Is. 19 for example, points to a time when God’s purpose will be fulfilled—a time of reconciliation and universal worship of the unique God. In Isaiah, universal peace is inseparably associated with the knowledge of God. This theme appears also in 11:9 where we learn that “they shall not hurt nor destroy in all My holy mountain, for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the LORD as the waters cover the sea.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 3, 'Zion. Refers to Jerusalem or God’s people. Initially it referred to the oldest part of Jerusalem, the “City of David.” It can also refer to “Mount Zion,” the hill to the north of David’s city. In 51:16 it designates the Israelites. In chap. 2, Zion is a place where the law is taught. It is also the seat of the universal Judge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 4, 'They shall beat. The transformation of swords into plowshares and spears into pruning hooks demonstrates responsible re-use of valuable material. In the absence of war the metals of the weapons are turned into agricultural tools. The opposite was also true in time of war (Joel 3:10). The work of the metal smiths to transform weapons of war into agricultural tools indicated a time of peace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 5, 'The glorious vision that begins the second chapter is a fitting introduction and an incentive to motivate God’s people to repentance in order that God’s purposes might be fulfilled among them and with them. The problems identified in this section are idolatry, false worship, arrogance, and dependence on humans instead of trust in God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 6, 'eastern ways. Designate evil non-Jewish practices that the law condemns (see Lev. 19:26; Deut. 18:10–11). Reference is made to soothsayers like the Philistines, and idol worship (Is. 2:8) of Mesopotamian rituals (44:25; 47:8–11). Consequently the judgment of God is falling against the people to strip from them the false support of foreign worship practices. Moreover, the fall of Judah and Jerusalem is attributed to the fact that their speech and deeds are against the Lord (3:8). The indictment against the leadership is based on their social injustice, in particular, taking advantage of the poor (3:13–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 11, 'that day. In this verse is a variant of the expression “the day of the LORD.” It shows another aspect of the day of the Lord, namely, the vindication of God who will be exalted in that day (see v. 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 12, 'the day of the LORD. In Isaiah this expression refers to God’s judgment against the wicked among God’s people or among the nations. It is also connected to the idea of salvation. It can also indicate the messianic age as in 10:20. The context clarifies the particular connotation (see 10:3; 13:6, 9; 34:8; 49:8; 61:2; 63:4). In the context of chap. 2, several themes are woven together to provide the meaning of the expression “the day of the LORD.” It is a day of judgment against idolatry, spiritualism, and pride. In the NT, the expression the “day of Jesus Christ” (Phil. 1:6), and the other expression “the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ” as in 2 Thess. 2:1–10, both capture the essence of God’s victory over His enemies and their demise on the one hand, and the salvation of His people on the other hand.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 18, 'idols. The day of the Lord is the day when idolatry will be abolished.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 2, 19, 'to shake the earth. This whole section uses apocalyptic language to describe the terror of the wicked: hiding in the holes of rocks and caves, trying to escape the shaking of the earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 3, 1, 'Social structure breaks down and the leadership is warned about their loss of power. Many of Israel’s leadership figures are listed: mighty man, man of war, judge, prophet, diviner, elder, captain of fifty, honorable man, counselor, skillful artisan, and expert enchanter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 3, 13, 'the elders … His princes. The leadership is judged for having exploited and crushed the poor. Moreover, their pride also works against them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 3, 18, 'The passage describes the horror of exile. It also gives a detailed description of the ornaments and cosmetic apparel current for women in the eighth century B.C. Jewelry was symbolic of human pride and arrogance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 4, 1, 'Because so many men are lost in war, the number of women greatly exceeds that of men.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 4, 2, 'The judgment messages turn into words of hope: a messianic prophecy and an image of protection remind us of the exodus from Egypt (e.g., cloud by day and fire by night, v. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 4, 2, 'the Branch of the LORD. Isaiah first uses the word “Branch” in this verse. The word is also used in Jer. 23:5–6; 33:15–16; Zech. 3:8; 6:9–13 to describe Israel’s future leader. The prophecy concerning this future leader indicates He is a divine Messiah (Jer. 23:5–6). Another major occurrence of the word Branch in Isaiah is 11:1. The NT begins (Matt. 1:1) with a reference to Jesus as “Son of David,” signaling that He was the awaited Messiah from the line of King David, Himself the ultimate King. See also the title ascribed to Him in Rev. 19:16: “KING OF KINGS AND LORD OF LORDS”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 4, 3, 'The language of this section, mostly recalling the exodus, signifies God’s protective presence (Ex. 13:20–22; 40:34–38; Num. 9:15–23). God Himself is the protector of His people. The presence of a holy God can only materialize among people who are holy; therefore the need to wash and purge anything contrary to the holiness of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 5, 1, 'A song of … vineyard. See chap. 27 for a positive song of a vineyard. The parable starts well but sets the stage for a surprising turn. The prophet asks his hearers for their opinion, a device meant to lead them to an obvious conclusion: the problem does not lay with the owner of the vineyard who did all that there was to be done. This parable clears God’s name in reference to the plight of His people. The meaning of this section is quite straightforward. The indictment against God’s people is that they have not lived up to God’s expectations. The major virtues that sum up what God requires are expressed in terms of justice and righteousness. These two words are often found in most prophetic writings (see Amos 5). For future development of this parable, see Mark 12:1–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 5, 8, 'Woe. Six “woes” (vv. 8, 11, 18, 20, 21, 22) are introduced by the Hebrew particle translated as “woe” or “alas.” They function as warning devices. They clearly intensify the force of the condemnation to exile. The charges against God’s people are as follows: they do not pay attention to the Lord; they have rejected the law of the Lord of hosts and despised the word of the Holy One of Israel; they call evil good and good evil; and they are engaged in sin. The evils God judges in this setting are greed (first woe); self indulgence (second woe); moral perversion (third woe); delusions of grandeur (fourth woe); self indulgence and the resulting social abuses (fifth and sixth woes).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 5, 10, 'ten acres of vineyard. Would produce extremely meager results because of divine judgment. one bath. About 6 gallons (22 l). See the covenant curses in Deut. 28:38–39. a homer. About 6 bushels (211 l). one ephah. About 3/5 bushel (21 l). The amount sowed was more than the amount reaped.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 5, 14, 'sheol. The grave or realm of the dead. Here it is personified as eager to swallow covenant violators. The language is poetic and symbolic; therefore it should not be taken literally.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 5, 26, 'A frightening description of the invading Assyrian military machinery. They are relentless warriors always ready for action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 6, 1, 'Isaiah’s call and commissioning is to be understood as participation in God’s own mission. The setting of this chapter is a sanctuary scene (see Ezek. 1; Rev. 4). This chapter contains key contrasts: The real King, God, contrasts with the death of King Uzziah. The holy God contrasts with a man with unclean lips, Isaiah, and a people of unclean lips. The judgment seems to leave no future for God’s people, so devastating the destruction seems to be. However, the chapter ends with the mention of a holy seed. The theme of the remnant becomes the unexpected demonstration of God’s grace. Already, the prophet Isaiah himself benefited from God’s grace. He is cleansed: a pure act of grace. The threefold repetition of “holy” (Is. 6:3) indicates intensification to show the utter holiness of God (see Jer. 22:29; Ezek. 21:27). All four Gospels mention Isaiah’s commissioning (Matt. 13:14–15; Mark 4:12; Luke 8:10; John 12:40). See also Acts 28:26–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 6, 2, 'seraphim. Literally, “burning ones”; heavenly beings who surround God’s throne. Even they could not contemplate the holiness of God. In Rev. 4:8 also they never stop singing about the holiness of God. The threefold declaration of holiness functions as a superlative beyond measure. It emphasizes the transcendence of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 6, 9, 'These verses seem to make Isaiah’s commission the hardening of Israel’s heart. One has to remember that Isaiah wrote this episode in the setting of the Syro-Ephraimite war to express in a dramatic fashion his opposition to King Ahaz’s trust in the king of Assyria rather than in God. His words could be compared to shock therapy. The very fact that the prophet repeats God’s decision to God’s people offers an implicit opportunity to seek God’s mercy. There is a window of opportunity between the sentencing and the execution of judgment. The paradox of this text explains the unchanging nature of divine judgment and, at the same time, God’s desire to save His people. God still hopes for a last-minute conversion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 1, 'This passage presents fulfillments of God’s purposes in spite of the lack of trust by God’s people. The deeper issue of this whole section is related to the historical circumstances of the book. The Syro-Ephraimite coalition threatened the continuity of the Davidic royal line. Later the Assyrian siege threatens the very survival of Jerusalem as the dwelling place of God’s name. The prophet Isaiah’s message was to assure both King Ahaz and later King Hezekiah that God’s purposes cannot be stopped. However, it was expected that both the king and God’s people trust in the Lord’s ability to fulfill His purposes. King Ahaz’s temptation was to appeal to Tiglath-Pileser III for help against the Syro-Ephraimite coalition, at the price of Assyrian control. God had better plans if only His people would trust Him. Isaiah’s ministry was aimed at helping the king make the right choices. The overarching issue is who they would trust. It is a faith issue. In the setting of these verses, however, trusting God is not merely a virtue; it is a life-and-death issue. The fate of the whole nation depends on it. Salvation is found only in the Lord (see 12:2; 30:15; 31:1; 33:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 1, 'This passage presents fulfillments of God’s purposes in spite of the lack of trust by God’s people. The deeper issue of this whole section is related to the historical circumstances of the book. The Syro-Ephraimite coalition threatened the continuity of the Davidic royal line. Later the Assyrian siege threatens the very survival of Jerusalem as the dwelling place of God’s name. The prophet Isaiah’s message was to assure both King Ahaz and later King Hezekiah that God’s purposes cannot be stopped. However, it was expected that both the king and God’s people trust in the Lord’s ability to fulfill His purposes. King Ahaz’s temptation was to appeal to Tiglath-Pileser III for help against the Syro-Ephraimite coalition, at the price of Assyrian control. God had better plans if only His people would trust Him. Isaiah’s ministry was aimed at helping the king make the right choices. The overarching issue is who they would trust. It is a faith issue. In the setting of these verses, however, trusting God is not merely a virtue; it is a life-and-death issue. The fate of the whole nation depends on it. Salvation is found only in the Lord (see 12:2; 30:15; 31:1; 33:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 1, 'An invitation to trust God. Trusting God is a major theme that runs throughout the book of Isaiah (chaps. 25, 30). Trusting God is in fact the cornerstone of Isaiah’s theology. God’s people can only be saved if they turn to God rather than Assyria, Egypt, or the Syro-Ephraimite coalition. In this chapter a new king is challenged to trust God in the threat of defeat. The lack of faith displayed by King Ahaz becomes an opportunity for God to do the extraordinary. Through the current life of the prophet Isaiah, God will provide a sign to show what He can do despite Ahaz’s refusal to ask for one.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 3, 'Shear-Jashub. The name of Isaiah’s son. It literally means “a remnant shall return.” It is a vehicle that captures the importance of the remnant motif (v. 22; 1:9; 4:2–3; 10:20; 37:32). It supposes the threat of exile. However, it also carries the notion of hope in spite of judgment. In the immediate context, this name was in itself a message to stop King Ahaz from relying on Assyrian help against the Syro-Ephraimite threat to the Davidic dynasty. Rezin, king of Syria, and Pekah, king of Israel, wanted to replace Ahaz and choose a king of their liking. Thus, they were indirectly challenging God’s sovereignty and rule over the affairs of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 6, 'Tabel. Means “good for nothing.” In the context it is a word of contempt and a way of saying the plan of the coalition will not stand.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 7, 'If you will not believe. The prophecy that refers to the fall of Samaria is accompanied by a warning to King Ahaz. A play on words is used to make the saying memorable: “If you will not believe, surely you shall not be established.” The words “believe” and “established” come from the same Hebrew root ’mn, the same root as the word “Amen.” In essence, God is telling Ahaz, “If you do not rely on My words as standing firm, you will not stand firm yourself.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 10, 'The Immanuel sign and prophecy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 11, 'Ask a sign. The sign in this context is supposed to be extraordinary, without limit. The text specifies “ask it either in the depth or in the height above.” A sign points beyond itself and ultimately to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 14, 'virgin. The Hebrew word means a young woman of marriageable age. The specific concept of virginity was emphasized later by the Greek translation of the OT and the NT quote of the text in reference to Mary (Matt. 1:23). The identity of the woman to fulfill the sign in Isaiah’s time is uncertain. Immanuel. This designation is found also in 8:8, 10. It literally means “God is with us.” This was a sign fulfilled during Isaiah’s time, possibly by the birth of Isaiah’s own son. However, Jesus’ birth provided the final fulfillment or application of this sign (Matt. 1:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 17, 'The LORD will bring. The Assyrian invasion of Judah is announced. Mention is also made of the tragedy of the split between the northern kingdom of Israel and the southern kingdom of Judah after the death of Solomon in 931 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 18, 'whistle for the fly. This section provides a graphic description of the judgment on God’s people by means of Egypt and Assyria. They, in their rivalry, would be disputing each other over the control of the space between their two countries, which included Judah. Several images refer to humiliation (see v. 20, the mention of shaving the head, the hair of the legs, and the beard), and to desolation signified by the mention of briers and thorns (vv. 19, 23–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 7, 23, 'a thousand shekels. About 25 pounds (11.5 kg).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 1, 'The tone of this passage is that of judgment not only against the Syro-Ephraimite coalition but also against God’s people themselves. The name of Isaiah’s son “Maher-Shalal-Hash-Baz” could literally be translated as “quickly, the plunder; it hurries, the loot.” Or “one hastens to the plunder; one hurries to the loot.” It was given as a sign showing that the coalition against Judah would not succeed. The presence of witnesses served to authenticate the prophecy of Isaiah and reinforce the truth of the prophetic pronouncement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 3, 'I went to the prophetess. This sentence was a euphemism for intimate relations of Isaiah with his wife, who is called a prophetess although we do not have a record of her prophecies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 4, 'the spoil of Samaria. Before the child would be old enough to be able to call for his parents, the Syro-Ephraimite coalition would be defeated. Tiglath-Pileser III conquered Damascus in 732 B.C. The Syrian king Rezin was killed. Isaiah foretold one of the most dramatic events in the history of Israel: the fall of Samaria and the end of the northern kingdom, Israel, in 722 B.C. A significant segment of its population went into exile. Mixing with other populations, this group of exiles eventually became known by NT times as “Samaritans.” The very fact that God sent prophets to His people showed His desire to save them and spare them from the tragedy of conquest with its horrible consequence of loss of freedom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 5, 'The king of Assyria. The Assyrian conquerors are identified. Their conquest spilled over to Judah. A veiled warning is also addressed to Judah so that it heeds the prophetic words and trusts in the Lord. But later history proved that Judah did not learn from this lesson.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 9, 'In the face of predictions of Assyria’s devastating conquest, a note of hope shows that God’s purposes will not be defeated. There are limits to what the enemies of God’s people are allowed to do. The theme of the judgment against the nations, though hinted at here, will receive fuller treatment (chaps. 13–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 11, 'with a strong hand. God forcefully directs the prophet Isaiah to take a stand even if that means distancing himself from the choices of God’s people. The prophet becomes a model of what is expected of God’s people. God’s people should trust God instead of the military might of neighboring nations such as Egypt, Assyria, or Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 13, 'your fear. Isaiah is invited to fear the Lord. In the context, the particular connotation of the fear of the Lord is to consider the Lord as holy. The goal of the covenant and its stipulations was to teach Israel the fear of the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 19, 'This section provides a record of the prophet’s response, which is his determination and resolve to wait on the Lord. He and those who follow God rely on the law and testimony in contrast to those mentioned in v. 19 who consult mediums who consult the dead. who whisper and mutter. God’s people, under the threat of an unknown future, succumbed to the temptation of soliciting mediums who claimed to tell the future. Isaiah stood against that covenant violation. Communication with the dead was a clear violation of God’s prohibition recorded in Deut. 18:9–14. The last verses of this section list the devastating consequences of being involved in spiritualism: distress, darkness, and the “gloom of anguish.” It takes nothing short of drastic liberation from what the NT would later call the powers of this world of darkness (Luke 22:53; Eph. 6:12; Col. 2:15). seek their God. The choice that presented itself to God’s people was either to trust in the law and the testimony—the written record of God’s will—or in false religious practices of predicting the future through communication with demons.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 20, 'this word. The Bible presents at least four tests that can be applied to those who claim to speak for God through prophecy, miracles, or other spiritual gifts. One is agreement with God’s word, the Bible. For others see Deut. 13:1–4; 18:22; Jer. 2:8–9; Matt. 7:20; 1 John 4:1–2. For more on SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 8, 22, 'gloom of anguish. The setting of this prophecy predicts the end of darkness, gloom, anxiety, famine, violence, and war. The reversal of the human predicament is effected by the coming of the Son (chap. 9). His coming brings the needed light that dispels darkness, the honor that cancels shame.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 9, 1, 'The One who will be called “a covenant to the people, as a light to the Gentiles” in 42:6 is introduced in this chapter with several titles that show His royal dignity and supremacy. These titles indicate that the Messiah will be a marvelous Teacher, a powerful God, who will eternally and lovingly care for His children, and one who will bring a reign of peace that nations could not and cannot bring. The fact that the zeal of the Lord will bring about the kingdom of peace stops any credit from going to human agencies (see Zech. 4:6). The titles attributed to the Son express a longing for an ideal king, a wise king with sound judgment, a warrior who can defeat enemies and protect his people, and a peace-making prince who will rule from age to age.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 9, 1, 'This section is one of seventeen passages in Isaiah that proclaims the good news of deliverance by God (see chap. 11; 24:14–25:12; chap. 26; 31:1–32:20; chaps. 33; 35; 40:1–11; 42:1–44:8; chap. 49; 51:1–52:12; 52:13–53:12; chaps. 54; 56; 60; 61; 62).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 9, 1, 'The verse mentions significant geographical locations that are related to the future ministry of the Messiah Jesus: The territories of Zebulun and Naphtali (Josh. 19:10–16, 32–39), which included Galilee, particularly suffered from the Assyrian invasions (2 Kin. 15:29). Other locations mentioned are the way of the sea, beyond the Jordan, and Galilee of the Gentiles or “nations.” All these areas are located within the scope of Jesus’ ministry. What Jesus’ geographical movements show is that He was intentional in bringing the light of His good news to Jews and Gentiles alike.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 9, 2, 'a great light. The coming of the Messiah corresponds with the coming of light that dispels darkness. In Isaiah, the light that the Messiah brings is connected to liberation from captivity and exile (see 42:16; 49:6; 58:8; 60:1, 19–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 9, 3, 'rejoice. These transition verses describe the jubilation of God’s people due to the defeat of their oppressor and to the end of war. They lead to the introduction of the prince of peace who brings God’s kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 9, 6, 'a Son is given. The birth of the Son was both a gift and a sign (see John 3:16; Luke 2:10–11). The titles “Mighty God, Everlasting Father” indicate His divine nature. For more on the divinity of the PERSON OF CHRIST, see also Matt. 1:23; John 1:1; Heb. 1:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 9, 8, 'Isaiah’s primary messages were addressed to Judah; however, occasionally his indictments were against the northern kingdom, clearly designated as Ephraim and Samaria as is the case in this section. This passage depicts the tragedy of not learning from the Assyrian invasion. The northern kingdom was still proud. They were saying, “The bricks have fallen down, but we will rebuild with hewn stones; the sycamores are cut down, but we will replace them with cedars” (v. 10). Ephraim’s problems are listed as pride and arrogance of heart, lack of repentance, ungodliness, wickedness, and false prophets teaching lies. The list continues in the following verses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 10, 1, 'Woe. Begins a court case against those who oppress God’s people. The specific actions that bring about the judgment are the following: unjust laws, oppressive decrees, deprivation of the rights to the poor, refusal to grant justice to the oppressed among God’s people, and abuse of dependent people such as widows and orphans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 10, 5, 'the rod of My anger. Chap. 10 begins with a woe oracle, an accusation against God’s people. Here it shifts to a second woe oracle against Assyria. The overarching theme of this section of chap. 10 is God’s right to judge the whole world, both His covenant people and the nations. Already, God’s sovereign dignity to judge was asserted in 2:4. In chap. 10, Assyria initially is an instrument of God’s judgment against His people (see also 5:26–29; 7:18–20; 8:7–8), but it receives God’s judgment itself when it goes beyond its boundaries. Assyria is judged because of its pride.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 10, 12, 'the arrogant heart. Assyria is judged for its inhumane treatment of God’s people. Their appetite for destruction crosses the boundaries of their assignment and goes on to destroy other nations, far exceeding God’s purposes (see v. 7). They did not understand that the real issue in God’s dealing with His people was to punish them because of their covenant unfaithfulness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 10, 20, 'the remnant of Israel. The believing remnant comprising those who trust God will experience an exodus from Assyria. (For earlier mentions of this motif, see 1:9; 4:2; 6:13; 7:3.) God’s people are encouraged not to fear. In the book of Isaiah, the invitations not to fear are among the most precious and comforting words from God to His people (41:10, 14; 43:1; 44:2). Paul quotes this passage in Rom. 9:27. The judgment of Assyria opens another perspective of God’s dealing with His people. God’s remnant will return to Him. Ultimately, what God desired was to save the repentant remnant among His people. He purposes to secure an unbroken chain for the continuity of His purpose through His people. God’s determination to save a remnant that will carry His purposes was already signaled by Isaiah’s son Shear-Jashub (Is. 7:3). For more on the REMNANT, see Rom. 11:2–5; Rev. 12:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 1, 'Describes the rule of the Messiah and the exodus of a new remnant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 1, 'In this context, the two designations Rod and Branch refer to the Messiah as in 4:2–6 (see also 53:2). The reign of the Messiah is the fulfillment of God’s promise to David that his throne will never lack his descendant upon it (see 2 Sam. 7:11–15). The promise that a Branch will grow out of the root of Jesse indicates that while the Davidic dynasty will suffer heavy blows, it will not be eradicated. As Isaiah’s inaugural vision indicated, a holy seed would be represented by the stump. When all hope seems lost, God acts in an unexpected way to carry out His purposes through His people. An unbroken line is secured among God’s people. God was diligent to weave His purposes throughout the history of His people. The genealogies of the NT, by tracing the genealogy of the Messiah through David, indicate that Jesus has secured and fulfilled the promise God made to David. He fulfills the destiny of the remnant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 1, 'Branch. The NT writers understood that the very place where the Messiah lives is not insignificant or by chance. In His redemptive plan God weaves details that are signs and pointers to His sovereign guidance of human history. The connection with 6:13 leads to the idea that the Messiah secures a remnant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 2, 'The Spirit. References to the Spirit, the Holy Spirit, are very significant in Isaiah (30:1; 32:15; 34:16; 40:13; 42:1; 44:3; 48:16; 59:21; 61:1). Moreover, the relationship between the Messiah and the Spirit mentioned several times in Isaiah anticipates what will be more fully developed in the NT (see Luke 3:22; John 1:32–34). The attributes given the Messiah are indications of the virtues that were expected from the kings among God’s people, which they failed to embody. The Messiah comes to fulfill God’s idea of a righteous king who will be righteous and faithful, living completely in reference to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 3, 'the fear of the LORD. The last characteristic attributed to the Messiah in this section. The Messiah delights in the fear of the Lord. The expression is a synonym for true piety, reverence that contains love and hope rather than dread or fear of punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 4, 'righteousness. Twice used in vv. 4 and 5, this indicates that righteousness is the central element of the Messiah’s reign. His reign will be characterized by justice to the poor and equity to the meek. God’s concern for the well-being of each member of the covenant community finds fulfillment when the Messiah reigns.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 5, 'the belt. The mention of a belt expresses readiness for action. Righteousness and faithfulness will be the motivation of the Messiah’s actions. A belt loosened at the waist meant inaction or a relaxed posture (see 5:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 6, 'This beautiful passage foretells the coming of the Messiah, who will bring the knowledge of God that will cancel all violence and usher in a reign of peace. He will establish universal harmony. This is understood to be the condition for God’s people ultimately on the new earth. For more on the NEW EARTH, see 35:4–10; 65:17, 21–25; Rev. 21:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 6, 'shall dwell with. The Messiah will bring the knowledge of God that will end all violence and usher in a reign of peace. He will establish universal harmony. Compare 35:4–10; 65:17, 21–25; Rev. 21:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 10, 'the Gentiles. The Messiah is the hope of the nations. By means of the expression “in that day” the prophet opens a glorious future marked by what the Messiah brings not only to Israel, the remnant of God’s people (see v. 11), but also to non-Israelites. This verse unveils a remarkable insight into God’s purposes. God cares about His people Israel; He also cares about those who are not part of the covenant people. The Messiah is predicted to be a unifier of Jews and Gentiles. The promise God made to Abraham in Gen. 12, according to which all the families of the earth will be blessed, finds an echo in this verse. Isaiah reveals that the Messiah is a missionary for God who desires to become a connecting point not only for His chosen people but for the whole world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 11, 'the second time. Indicates that the exodus from Egypt, a first time, is far surpassed by an event of a larger scale and scope: the return of a remnant from the whole world. This interpretation is supported by the last sentence of the chapter: “As it was for Israel in the day that he came up from the land of Egypt.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 15, 'His mighty wind. The images of this verse allude to the drying of the river Euphrates. What God did in drying up the “tongue of the Sea” He will do to the River (Euphrates).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 11, 16, 'a highway. The idea of a highway indicates the absence of obstacles or hindrance for God’s people to go home.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 12, 1, 'O LORD. Begins a song of praise for salvation. It is fitting that this section which began with the prophecy about Immanuel, God’s dwelling among His people, would conclude with the celebration of the Holy One of Israel in the midst of His people, having provided salvation for them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 12, 3, 'wells of salvation. Is likely a reference to God’s provision during Israel’s wanderings in the wilderness. God’s provisions were celebrated during major festivals: the Feast of Harvest, Ex. 23:16, and the Feast of Tabernacles, Lev. 23:34.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 12, 6, 'in your midst. The presence of God among His people constitutes the climax of salvation. The same idea occurs in Rev. 22:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 13, 1, 'Oracles against nations other than Israel and Judah are common features in the prophetic writings (Jer. 46–51; Ezek. 25–32; Amos 1–2; Zeph. 2:4–15). In this section, several nations including Israel are accused: Babylon (Is. 13; 14:4–23; 21:1–10), the Philistines (14:28–32), Moab (chaps. 15–16), Damascus (17:1–6), Ethiopia (chap. 18), Egypt (chap. 19), Egypt and Ethiopia (chap. 20), Edom (21:11–12), Arabia (21:13–17), Jerusalem (chap. 22), and Tyre (chap. 23). The nations Isaiah mentions had encounters with Judah. In chaps. 34–35, the restoration of Israel is contrasted with the punishment of Edom. The nations were used to discipline God’s people; they became proud and exceedingly cruel. Therefore, God had to discipline them also; however, in the end the nations also can participate in the blessings enjoyed by God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 13, 1, 'Oracles against nations other than Israel and Judah are common features in the prophetic writings (Jer. 46–51; Ezek. 25–32; Amos 1–2; Zeph. 2:4–15). In this section, several nations including Israel are accused: Babylon (Is. 13; 14:4–23; 21:1–10), the Philistines (14:28–32), Moab (chaps. 15–16), Damascus (17:1–6), Ethiopia (chap. 18), Egypt (chap. 19), Egypt and Ethiopia (chap. 20), Edom (21:11–12), Arabia (21:13–17), Jerusalem (chap. 22), and Tyre (chap. 23). The nations Isaiah mentions had encounters with Judah. In chaps. 34–35, the restoration of Israel is contrasted with the punishment of Edom. The nations were used to discipline God’s people; they became proud and exceedingly cruel. Therefore, God had to discipline them also; however, in the end the nations also can participate in the blessings enjoyed by God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 13, 1, 'Babylon. Like Zion, this name can have several connotations depending on the context. In history it became a great kingdom; during the time of Isaiah it was part of the Assyrian Empire (14:24–27). It became a symbolic name that represents the world powers opposed to God’s kingship, reign, and kingdom (Rev. 14:8; 16:19; 17:1–18:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 13, 6, 'the day of the LORD. See also in vv. 9, 13. This is the day when Babylon is overthrown like Sodom and Gomorrah (v. 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 13, 10, 'the stars of heaven. Sun, moon, and stars stop their assigned function and go back to darkness. Isaiah uses language that describes cosmic upheaval to refer to the day of the Lord, the day of judgment (24:23). The same language is used by later prophets (Ezek. 32:7–8; Joel 2:10, 30–31; Amos 8:9). In the NT the imagery we find in Isaiah is used for signs of Christ’s Second Coming to establish God’s universal kingdom (Matt. 24:29; Mark 13:24–25; Luke 21:25; Rev. 6:12–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 13, 12, 'Ophir. Known in the OT as a source of fine gold, and may have been located on the coast of East Africa (see 2 Chr. 8:17–18; Ps. 45:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 13, 17, 'the Medes. Identified as those who bring about God’s judgment against Babylon. The Medes were an ancient people who lived southwest of the Caspian Sea. They joined force with the Babylonians to defeat the Assyrians and to destroy Nineveh. Historically, Cyrus the king of Persia, later mentioned by name in Isaiah, defeated the Medes and incorporated them into his kingdom. Cyrus, whose mother was Median, conquered Babylon in 539 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 14, 1, 'As do several prophetic sections of the book of Isaiah, this chapter looks forward and prophesies both the historical hardship of the Babylonian captivity and oppression and also predicts its reversal. The reversal of fate can be observed by means of an imaginary description of the underworld, sheol in Hebrew, also translated “hell” in this chapter. The former defeated kings sit on thrones, whereas the tyrant king of Babylon lies on a bed of worms. Several aspects of the text convey the state of utter humiliation. There is no funeral, no honor whatsoever for this tyrant. Moreover, the passage looks beyond historical events to metaphysical realities, specifically the fall of an angel who covets God’s authority. The fall of this proud angel is vividly depicted using dramatic poetic language. This being sets out to usurp God’s status and wants to occupy the highest position above the stars of heaven (angels; see Job 38:7 where the angels are called not only sons of God, but also morning stars), yet God brings this being down.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 14, 12, 'Lucifer. Literally, “shining one, son of dawn.” The name Lucifer comes from the Latin Vulgate, and means “light bearer.” The text seems to be echoed by Jesus in Luke 10:18, where language applied here to the king of Babylon is used of Satan. For further descriptions of Satan and the origin of SIN, see Gen. 3:1–6; Ezek. 28:12–19; Rev. 12:7–9. in your heart. Pride is the essence of sin and represents a self-centeredness which is the root of all sinful actions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 14, 19, 'cast out. In this verse, the contrast between the fallen king of Babylon (the fallen Lucifer) and the Messiah king is reinforced by the designation of the former figure as “an abominable branch.” The Branch that sprung out of the root of Jesse (11:1–5) was honored, whereas this abominable branch is despised because of tyranny, cruelty, and oppression. A broader comparison throughout Scripture shows the following: (1) One is a legitimate king; the other tries to usurp God’s royal prerogatives. (2) They are both called sons. One, in the context of a gift, is introduced as a leader of the kingdom of peace. This Son is the True Light sent to overturn the darkness that overwhelms the world. The other, the son of the morning, tries to usurp the Son’s legitimate prerogatives. (3) One is called the bright morning star, a Rod from the root of Jesse; the other is called the son of the morning. (4) One establishes a kingdom; the other transforms kingdoms into wilderness (14:17). (5) The one upon whom the Spirit of the Lord dwells, the one who is anointed, brings good news to the afflicted. He has been sent to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to captives and freedom to prisoners (61:1). The other one does not allow his prisoners to go home (14:17). (6) They are both designated as “branch.” One brings life and peace; the other is abominable. (7) One bears the people’s burdens to deliver them. The other makes people bear a burden and a yoke. (8) One brings about the reign of peace; the other spreads terror, oppression, and anguish. (9) One intercedes for transgressors; even on behalf of those who have adopted the ways of violence towards Him, He prays, “Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do” (Luke 23:34). The other accuses the faithful day and night. (10) One is uplifted toward heaven; the other one has been cast down to earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 15, 1, 'Oracle or burden against Moab (see also 25:10; and in the writings of other prophets: Jer. 48:1–47; Ezek. 25:8–11; Amos 2:1–3; Zeph. 2:8–11). The origin of Moab is traced back to the story of Lot and his daughters (Gen. 19:30–38; see also Num. 22–25). The antagonism between Moab and Israel is attested in one of the major archaeological discoveries, the so-called Moabite stone (ninth century B.C., discovered in 1868), on which King Mesha celebrates his victory over Israel. Moab is sometimes associated with Edom. The Edomites were descendants of Esau. Israel was not allowed to fight against them during the conquest (see Deut. 2:1–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 15, 1, 'Oracle or burden against Moab (see also 25:10; and in the writings of other prophets: Jer. 48:1–47; Ezek. 25:8–11; Amos 2:1–3; Zeph. 2:8–11). The origin of Moab is traced back to the story of Lot and his daughters (Gen. 19:30–38; see also Num. 22–25). The antagonism between Moab and Israel is attested in one of the major archaeological discoveries, the so-called Moabite stone (ninth century B.C., discovered in 1868), on which King Mesha celebrates his victory over Israel. Moab is sometimes associated with Edom. The Edomites were descendants of Esau. Israel was not allowed to fight against them during the conquest (see Deut. 2:1–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 16, 1, 'This section alludes to the fate of Moabite refugees seeking asylum in Jerusalem. See 2 Kin. 3:4 for lambs as token of tribute.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 16, 5, 'One will sit. The hope and salvation of Moab will depend on the Messiah whose throne will be established (see 9:1–7; 11:1–5; Amos 9:11; Acts 15:16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 16, 6, 'pride. Like Assyria, Moab is accused because of her pride and empty boasting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 16, 12, 'not prevail. The local Moabite god cannot do anything. Prayers to any other god than the Lord are in vain. This was the lesson God taught through the prophet Elijah the Tishbite on Mount Carmel (1 Kin. 18:20–40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 16, 13, 'For another short-term prophecy see also the oracle against Arabia (21:16–17). This prophecy appears to have been fulfilled in 715 B.C., when the Assyrians devastated the land and its territory. The fact that God leaves a remnant not only to Israel but also to the nations speaks in favor of His overarching purpose to save the whole world, Israelites as well as non-Israelites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 17, 1, 'Oracles against Damascus and Ephraim. These oracles are spoken against the nations that formed the Syro-Ephraimite coalition against Judah as attested in 7:1–9 and 8:1–4. The Assyrians defeated Syria in 732 B.C. and Israel in 722 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 18, 1, 'Oracles against Ethiopia. Ethiopia, located south of Egypt, was also called Cush. Cush was a powerful nation during the time of Isaiah. In 740 B.C. at the beginning of Isaiah’s ministry, Piankhy conquered Egypt. His successor was the well-known Shabako (713–698 B.C.). Some assume that he must have reigned until the accession of another well-known Cushite king who ruled Egypt, the famous Taharka in 690 B.C. Shabako attempted to form an anti-Assyrian coalition. The power of Cush, however, could not stop the purposes and control over the nations of the One called the Lord of hosts, the One the Israelites should depend on.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 19, 1, 'Oracles against Egypt. The accusations against Egypt are listed as follows: idolatry, the prevalence of charmers, its mediums and sorcerers, and a spirit of perversion. Judgment comes to Egypt but it is not the last word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 19, 16, 'In that day. In this section, this expression is used five times (19:16–17, 18, 19–22, 23, 24–25). The oracle against Egypt makes a surprising and unusual turn. Egypt experiences liberation and healing. A mighty Savior is sent to liberate them. They are given an opportunity to know the Lord. They worship the God of the covenant with Israel. What began as an oracle against Egypt climaxes into a reunion of enemies all blessed by the Lord: Egypt, Assyria, and Israel. The very notion of God’s people is expanded. Egypt is called by God “My people.” Assyria is called “the work of My hands,” and Israel, “My inheritance.” Moreover, the promise of God to Abraham according to which all the families of the earth shall be blessed through His descendants gains a unique perspective with this prophecy (Gen. 12:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 20, 1, 'The judgment is against Egypt and Ethiopia. However, God was trying to dissuade His people from putting their trust in kingdoms that have no future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 21, 1, 'The fall of Babylon is described with graphic images: a woman in labor, distressed, dismayed, frightened. Mention is made of Babylon’s idolatry. Babylon is conquered while the leaders were having a feast (v. 5; Dan. 5). For other major announcements of the fall of Babylon, see Jer. 51:8 and Rev. 14:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 21, 11, 'Edom is given a symbolic name “Dumah” which means silence. Dumah was also a town in Edom. In Isaiah there are several indictments against Edom (11:14; 34:1–17; 63:1–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 21, 13, 'The people and places mentioned in this oracle are all connected to Arabia or the Arabs: Dedanites (see Ezek. 27:15), the land of Tema, Kedar.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 22, 1, 'the Valley of Vision. Refers to Jerusalem (see v. 10). Jerusalem did not look to God in all its predicaments (see v. 11). This neglect of God prompts the radical announcement that there will be no atonement for this kind of sin (v. 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 22, 15, 'It is prophesied that a high official, Shebna, will be deposed and sent to exile and death because of corruption, whereas Eliakim, whose name means “God will establish,” will be promoted to take his place. Eliakim is portrayed as a father to the inhabitants of Jerusalem (v. 21). Some see this passage as related to and partially fulfilled in the account of chaps. 36 and 37 (36:3, 11, 22; 37:2). “Scribe” (36:3) is a lower position than being “over the house” (22:15). There is no record of Shebna’s death. The key is a symbol of authority. The list of his tasks are taken up in the book of Revelation and applied to the resurrected Christ (Rev. 3:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 22, 23, 'peg … secure place. The root of the Hebrew word “Amen” is used to describe a tent peg affixed to a secure, reliable place. To say “amen” is equivalent to saying “I stake my life on that because it is firm, true and reliable.” In Hebrew, the verb “to believe” essentially means something or someone is reliable, dependable, and trustworthy. The statement that the peg can be unfastened may be a warning to Eliakim himself. Should he betray the Lord’s trust, he too will be removed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 23, 1, 'The fame of Tyre, a great seaport city, goes back in antiquity. The Phoenicians were also famous sailors and builders of the famous North African city Carthage. Tyre is given a different seventy-year prophecy than the one found in Dan. 9 (see Is. 23:15). Tyre has also succumbed to pride. Up until Alexander the Great who devastated the city of Tyre in 332 B.C., all the superpowers from the time of Isaiah on (Assyria, Babylon) would inflict severe blows on Tyre.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 24, 1, 'This section is known as Isaiah’s Apocalypse because of its wide scope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 24, 1, 'This section is known as Isaiah’s Apocalypse because of its wide scope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 24, 1, 'the earth. The judgment moves from the nations listed in the last section of the book to the whole world. The earth is desolate and emptied.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 24, 5, 'defiled. Apostasy among God’s people causes the earth’s desolation. The same motif that shows the relation between ethical behavior and the condition of the earth is also found in Hos. 4. Covenant-breaking results in curse. The defilement of the earth that causes it to mourn is caused by the breaking of what is called the “everlasting covenant.” This expression is contextually understood as a universal covenant between God and humanity similar to the covenant with Noah (Gen. 9:16). It reveals God’s sovereignty over the whole world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 25, 6, 'A feast. The festive language signals a new era, similar to a new covenant and its celebration, like the feast after the covenant at Sinai (see Ex. 24:9–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 25, 8, 'swallow up. Salvation from God will include the end of death. It will be swallowed up. An inseparable part of this salvation is specified in 26:19 with the announcement of the resurrection of the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 25, 9, 'waited. The repeating theme of waiting on the Lord expresses trust and loyalty to God (it occurs 13 times in Isaiah). It is closely associated with the idea of salvation. It occurs again in 26:8. In this setting the reason for this hope in God is beautifully expressed to point out that the desire of the soul is God’s name and the remembrance of God. Moreover, glorious promises are given to those who wait on the Lord: “those who wait on the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings like eagles, they shall run and not be weary, they shall walk and not faint” (40:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 26, 1, 'this song. Shows an underlying motif of trust in God’s salvation. To those who trust God, those whose minds stay on Him, He grants perfect peace (v. 3). The passion for God energizes those who long for the Lord, who seek Him, who wait for Him. Night and day, God is the object of their desire (vv. 8–9). Being saved in this context shows them being focused on God. It is also connected to the resurrection of the dead, clearly affirmed in vv. 18–19. The salvation of God’s people does not occur without deliverance from God’s enemies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 26, 19, 'Asserts the bodily resurrection of the dead. For more on the RESURRECTION in the OT, see Job 19:25–26; Dan. 12:2. In the NT, see 1 Cor. 15:51–55; 1 Thess. 4:13–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 27, 1, 'Leviathan. This mysterious creature is referred to in this setting as the “fleeing serpent,” the reptile that is in the sea, or the sea monster. It occurs in Job 3:8; 41:1; Ps. 74:14; 104:26. This creature may have provided John the background for identifying Satan as a dragon and as an ancient serpent (Rev. 12:9; 20:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 27, 7, 'The passage makes a case that judgment was to purge idolatry from God’s people. The question of v. 7 leads to the affirmation of v. 9 according to which even judgment through exile has a redemptive component.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 27, 12, 'The alternation of judgment and salvation passages so typical and prevalent in Isaiah occurs in this chapter also. The mention of the expression “in that day,” and the sounding of the great trumpet typical of the manifestation of God, shows a decisive action from God to bring about the liberation of His people. Just as the purpose of the exodus from Egypt was to lead the people to worship God, the gathering of God’s people from Assyria and from Egypt make possible the worship of God in the holy mount at Jerusalem. (For the mention of the trumpet with a last-day connotation, see Zech. 9:14; Matt. 24:31; 1 Cor. 15:52; 1 Thess. 4:16; Rev. 11:15.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 1, 'A series of six woe messages gives six warnings that alternate with promises of salvation (28:1; 29:1, 15; 30:1; 31:1; 33:1; see also the six woes in chap. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 1, 'A series of six woe messages gives six warnings that alternate with promises of salvation (28:1; 29:1, 15; 30:1; 31:1; 33:1; see also the six woes in chap. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 1, 'The promises of salvation contain some of the most remarkable designations of God (see vv. 5–6, 14–19a, 23–29; 29:5–8, 17–24; 30:18–24; 31:4–32:8; 32:15–20; 33:17–24). The Lord is introduced as a Savior (28:2), a crown of glory and a diadem of beauty for the remnant of His people (v. 5), a cornerstone, a sure foundation (v. 16), a teacher (v. 26), the Lord of hosts wonderful in counsel and excellent in guidance (v. 29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 1, 'Ephraim. The northern kingdom of Israel, also known as Samaria, is first on the list of the judgment against the nations (see also Amos 6:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 5, 'the remnant. This verse is in reference to the ten tribes of the northern kingdom. The demise of Israel was not meant to end in total destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 7, 'wine. Accusations are directed against priests and prophets who have lost their way because of intoxicating drinks.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 16, 'a stone. The NT writers apply the image of a stone for a foundation to Christ (1 Pet. 2:4–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 17, 'the measuring line. The ideas of justice and righteousness reoccur as the basis for what God expects of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 21, 'awesome work. God’s work of judgment is called awesome and unusual; some versions translate the word as “strange.” What is strange is that God is going to use foreign nations to execute His judgment on His people. The book of the prophet Habakkuk addresses this issue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 28, 24, 'the plowman. The parable of the farmer indicates that if the farmers are providentially guided and work in various ways depending on the need of the field, God who is wonderful in counsel and excellent in guidance knows how to deal with His people to produce righteousness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 29, 1, 'Oracles against Jerusalem. Woe. The second woe describes the paradox of a place called city of God (Ariel) where people turn away from God. They honor God with their lips but their hearts are far from Him (v. 13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 30, 1, 'Woe. The context of this woe against seeking counsel without the Lord may refer to the maneuvering that shows more trust in human abilities than God’s ability to save. Salvation is promised to the deaf who hear the words of the book, the blind who see out of darkness. The humble will increase their joy; the poor will rejoice in the Holy One of Israel. not of Me. The opening verses of this chapter set the tone of the futility of trying to depend on Egypt (v. 7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 30, 9, 'a rebellious people. Self deception and refusal to hear the word of the Lord through His prophets lead to disaster. Rebellion is particularly targeted, and its devastating consequences are to be recorded for future generations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 30, 15, 'In returning. Chap. 30 captures one of the major issues of the book of Isaiah. V. 15 expresses God’s expectation. It indicates the remedy to the predicament of God’s people: repentance and rest. Instead of giving heed to the words of the prophet, God’s people place their trust in international alliances: specifically in Egyptian military might, in the immediate setting, or in Assyria’s power earlier during the Syro-Ephraimite threat. Chap. 31 expands on this indictment, clearly providing evidence of the lack of faith of God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 30, 18, 'gracious to you. As common in the book of Isaiah, a sudden shift in tone characterizes the beginning of this section. It provides a moving expression of God’s longing to show compassion to His people. Furthermore, as a means to appeal to God’s people to trust and give allegiance, a blessing is uttered to those who wait on the Lord. The latter theme: to “wait on the Lord” has deeply informed believers’ identity and sense of loyalty to God. A significant title of God that captures what God is seeking among His people occurs here: “the LORD is a God of justice.” This title provides a rationale for the indictments that occur throughout the book against injustices: the exploitation or lack of care for the poor, the widows, and the orphans (1:17, 23; 10:2; 11:4); the corruption of legal practices in accepting bribes (1:23); accumulation of property and possessions to the detriment of the people (2:7; 3:16–23; 5:8–9; 10:2–3); violence and bloodshed (1:15, 21; 5:7); lies (28:15); oppression (1:17; 3:15; 5:7); and debauchery (5:11–12, 22; 28:1, 7–8; 29:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 30, 19, 'This passage expands on the previous thought about God eagerly waiting to be gracious and to have mercy on His people, according to His character as revealed in Ex. 34:6–7. God promises grace and announces that weeping will cease if idolatry will end. God will heal His people and bind their wounds (Is. 30:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 30, 27, 'His anger. The salvation of God’s people is inseparable from the judgment of their enemies. Assyria is the target of God’s judgment as specified in v. 31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 30, 33, 'Tophet. Or Topheth. A place of burning in the valley of Hinnom to the south of Jerusalem (see Jer. 7:31–32). Biblical history records that it was a place where children were sacrificed to Molech (2 Kin. 23:10; Jer. 7:31–32; 19:6, 11–14). In the context of Is. 30, the language is symbolic. It alludes to the fate of Assyria and its king.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 31, 1, 'Woe. Most of the woe oracles express what displeases God (see v. 1; 3:9, 11; 5:8, 11, 18, 20–22; 10:1; 33:1; 45:9–10). This one taunts those who seek help from Egypt and Assyria. Chap. 31 picks up the recurring theme of trust in the Lord. It also provides the key to God’s people’s success against their enemies. The people were inclined to trust in political alliances and, in particular, the military might of Egypt with its horses. However, the impressive enemy that Assyria was could only fall by divine intervention, not by human power (sword). One of the moving promises is expressed in v. 4: “the LORD of hosts will come down to fight for Mount Zion and for its hill.” This verse echoes Ex. 14:13–14 when the people of Israel were pursued by the Egyptians. Moses told the people: “Do not be afraid. Stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 32, 9, 'Rise up. This passage began as a lawsuit against the complacent women of Jerusalem, similar to the indictment one finds in Amos 4:1–3. They must realize their complacency will be shattered. The text describes the destruction of a city left in utter desolation and forsaken. Only wild animals dwell in it. The only hope of a reversal of fate is when the Holy Spirit is poured from on high (Is. 32:15). Then, what God intends will be fulfilled: justice, righteousness, quiet confidence, and peace. The descent of the Spirit is compared to rain, pouring down water from heaven, resulting in the up-springing of justice, righteousness, peace, and security. The role of the Spirit of God, as a transforming agent, is to bring the blessing that reverses the covenant curses and restores the covenant relationship between God and His people. This same theme is developed in 44:3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 33, 1, 'Woe. The chapter begins with a woe oracle. Although the problem party is not named, Assyria is the likely target.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 33, 2, 'O LORD. A prayer for deliverance. The theme of “waiting on the Lord” occurs here also in this setting. It is associated with a key biblical concept: “the fear of the LORD.” The fear of the Lord has a positive connotation. It is reverent awe and attention to God, which is a key to connecting with God. The fear of the Lord is one of the virtues of the Messiah (11:2–3). It is the attitude God expects from all human beings (Eccl. 12:13). See also Rev. 14:7. The fear of the Lord is compared to the value of a treasure (Is. 33:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 33, 14, 'The sinners. The judgment of God’s people distinguishes between the righteous and the wicked. Sinners and hypocrites are described as dwelling in devouring fire and everlasting burnings. The context of burning specified in vv. 11–13 indicates that reference here to everlasting burnings is a graphic description of total destruction. In other words, the everlasting burning consumes the wicked, with no reference here to being consciously tormented forever.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 33, 15, 'See Ps. 15, in reference to what the Lord expects from His people. The portrait of the righteous is graphically described. He or she walks or behaves righteously, speaks uprightly, despises the gain of oppressions, refuses bribes, and protects his or her ears and eyes from bloodshed and evil (vv. 14–16). Magnificent promises are given that allude to pilgrimage or better yet exodus to Jerusalem, the city of peace. It all climaxes with the cancellation of sickness (see Rev. 21:3–4, 27) and the gift of forgiveness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 34, 1, 'Judgment against the nations and restoration of God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 34, 1, 'you nations. Four terms are used to designate the totality of humanity (nations, people, earth, and world), indicating that the judgment is general. The call is to come hear the sentence of an executive judgment: the Lord’s fury and indignation. The language has apocalyptic (end time/last day) elements: mountains melting, host of heaven dissolved, heavens rolled up like a scroll, the falling of the stars like figs falling from a fig tree.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 34, 5, 'Judgment of Edom. In 25:10–12, Moab was singled out to illustrate God’s indignation against the nations. In the current context, Edom is declared to suffer the “curse” or destruction. V. 8 specifies that God has appointed a day of vengeance and recompense for evil done to Zion (Jerusalem).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 34, 9, 'The destruction of Edom (vv. 5–6) is placed in the context of a destruction that clearly has international overtones (vv. 1–4). Edom illustrates what happens to all who merit God’s judgment. Isaiah here describes the miserable and awful destiny of the wicked, both in his own day and at the end of time. not be quenched. The devastation of Edom is presented as lasting forever, burning day and night, a concept commonly associated with an eternally burning hell. But Edom did not burn forever. In biblical usage such language intends to convey the idea—not that the fire will never go out, but that it cannot be put out by any other means than completely consuming what it is burning until there is nothing left (see 1:31; Jer. 4:4; 17:27; Ezek. 20:48). forever. Literally means an indefinite period of time, the limits of which must be determined by the nature of the person, thing, or circumstance to which it is applied. For Jonah in the belly of the fish, “forever” (Jon. 2:6) was “three days and three nights” (Jon. 1:17). As it relates to humans, the word often means “as long as a person lives” (see Ex. 21:6; 1 Sam. 1:22, 28). The passage provides the clearest example that the language of burning forever should not be taken literally. In other words, this verse demonstrates the metaphoric language of the burning of hell. It simply means total destruction. It would be impossible to have perpetual burning of the place where pelican and porcupine, owl and raven can dwell (Is. 34:11). The text displays a profusion of images to say there will be no human life there. For more on HELL and the punishment of the wicked, see Matt. 10:28; Rev. 14:11; 20:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 35, 1, 'Another shift in subject matter brings a vision of the joy of salvation. The use of several terms expressing joy is remarkable: “be glad,” “rejoice,” “blossom as the rose,” “blossom abundantly,” “rejoice, even with joy and singing” (vv. 1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 35, 4, 'do not fear. The expression “fear not,” which occurs several times in Isaiah, is meant to give God’s people the assurance that God is on their side (see 40:9; 41:10, 14; 43:1, 5; 44:2; 51:7; 54:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 35, 5, 'A song of joy. The reign of God brings a transformation of nature and a healing of the body of sickness and limitations. Salvation and its resulting joy and peace will be the new reality of God’s kingdom. Jesus uses the language of these verses to report to John the Baptist what He was doing as evidence of the dawn of the era of salvation announced by the prophet Isaiah (see Matt. 11:2–4; Luke 7:18–23). The ultimate fulfillment of the prophecies of this chapter will occur at the Second Coming of Christ and on the new earth. For more on the NEW EARTH, see Is. 11:6–9; 65:17, 21–25; Rev. 21:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 36, 1, 'Trust in the Lord and succeed. The encounters between Isaiah and King Hezekiah in these chapters can be compared with those of Isaiah and King Ahaz in chaps. 7 and 8. In both, there was an international crisis that threatened the very survival of the kingdom of Judah, and therefore of the Davidic dynasty. With Ahaz it was the threat of the Syro-Ephraimite coalition against Judah. With Hezekiah, it was the Assyrian siege to destroy Jerusalem. Beyond the literary recurrence of the phrase “the aqueduct from the upper pool, on the highway to the Fuller’s Field” (v. 2; 7:3; 2 Kin. 18:17), the stories share much in common theologically. The issue in both stories is about trusting God rather than political alliances. Ahaz fails to trust; Hezekiah trusts the Lord. He allows God to do in his lifetime a miracle that has marked the consciousness of God’s people second only to the exodus when God judged the Egyptians. The Lord’s protection is more certain than dependence on humans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 36, 1, 'The defeat of the Assyrian army. This episode, in the history of God’s people, is so important that it is recorded three times in Scripture (see 2 Kin. 18–19; 2 Chr. 32). It is one of the greatest miracles in the OT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 36, 1, 'Trust in the Lord and succeed. The encounters between Isaiah and King Hezekiah in these chapters can be compared with those of Isaiah and King Ahaz in chaps. 7 and 8. In both, there was an international crisis that threatened the very survival of the kingdom of Judah, and therefore of the Davidic dynasty. With Ahaz it was the threat of the Syro-Ephraimite coalition against Judah. With Hezekiah, it was the Assyrian siege to destroy Jerusalem. Beyond the literary recurrence of the phrase “the aqueduct from the upper pool, on the highway to the Fuller’s Field” (v. 2; 7:3; 2 Kin. 18:17), the stories share much in common theologically. The issue in both stories is about trusting God rather than political alliances. Ahaz fails to trust; Hezekiah trusts the Lord. He allows God to do in his lifetime a miracle that has marked the consciousness of God’s people second only to the exodus when God judged the Egyptians. The Lord’s protection is more certain than dependence on humans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 36, 1, 'The defeat of the Assyrian army. This episode, in the history of God’s people, is so important that it is recorded three times in Scripture (see 2 Kin. 18–19; 2 Chr. 32). It is one of the greatest miracles in the OT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 37, 14, 'Hezekiah’s prayer for deliverance. This prayer contains the rehearsal of God’s power. He alone is God and Creator. He has the ability to see and hear, which is not the case with idols. The prayer describes the problem and threat: the Assyrians have conquered many peoples and have laid waste their lands, but their gods are made by human hands. In other words, they have no power. They were created. The Lord alone has power to deliver.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 37, 35, 'I will defend. As a response to Hezekiah’s trust in God and his prayer, God assures His people that He will defend the city and save it, but God also has deeper reasons. The phrase “for My own sake and for My servant David’s sake” reminds the reader that God is faithful to His promise to David in 2 Sam. 7:11–16, which states his throne would never lack one of his descendants upon it. What was at stake in the crises of the book of Isaiah went beyond those events to the question of continuity of the “house of David” (Is. 7:2, 13; 22:22), the “throne of David” (9:7), the “city of David” (22:9), and the “tabernacle of David” (16:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 37, 36, 'the camp. Sennacherib invaded Judah in 701 B.C. He boasted of having captured 46 cities and of having shut in Hezekiah like a bird in a cage—a reference to the siege of Jerusalem. God directly intervened and 185,000 Assyrian troops were killed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 38, 9, 'The passage presents Hezekiah’s prayer following his cure, an individual psalm of distress, hope, and thanksgiving. Three sections can be identified, two of them introduced by words connected to discourse: “I said” in vv. 10–11; “what shall I say” in v. 15; and a conclusion. In vv. 10–14 Hezekiah expresses his distress. In vv. 15–19, he foresees hope of restoration. V. 20 is a conclusion that includes the community’s celebration of God’s mighty acts on behalf of Hezekiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 38, 10, 'sheol. Or the grave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 39, 1, 'At that time. Ties the story of Hezekiah’s recovery in chap. 38 to his welcome of the Babylonian ambassadors in chap. 39. God’s gracious healing does not lead Hezekiah to wise action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 39, 3, 'Isaiah in true prophetic fashion rebukes Hezekiah and foretells the final result of Hezekiah’s foolish act of showing Israel’s treasure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 39, 8, 'in my days. Even kings rated as good (38:3; 2 Kin. 20:3) can be selfish and short-sighted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 1, 'The main thrust of this section is God’s salvation granted to God’s repentant people. Chap. 40 begins a section commonly called “the book of consolations” or “the book of comfort.” The whole section presents themes that are related to the concept of salvation and signals the shift in emphasis which begins here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 1, 'The main thrust of this section is God’s salvation granted to God’s repentant people. Chap. 40 begins a section commonly called “the book of consolations” or “the book of comfort.” The whole section presents themes that are related to the concept of salvation and signals the shift in emphasis which begins here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 1, 'A series of questions are asked, followed by declarations. The declarations show the purpose of the previous questions. Identifying the questions in the text helps delineate the real purpose of this section. The main argument is that God can do it. He can make the new exodus happen. He can end the exile. He can defeat the oppressors and liberate His people. All the questions throughout the chapter emphasize the power and self-sufficiency of God. They lead to the declaration that the nations cannot be an obstacle to God’s plan. Chap. 40 provides the language and the concepts that are used to express the decisive acts of God in Jesus Christ. The kingdom of God, His rule, and His sovereignty are announced. The precursor of the Messiah is later identified in the person of John the Baptist. Matthew quotes v. 3 to indicate that John the Baptist was sent to prepare the way of the Lord Jesus (Matt. 3:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 5, 'glory of the LORD. A technical term that means the disclosure of the divine presence and nature through His actions. Glory is connected to the idea of splendor, majesty, sovereignty, light, revelation, and presence of God. God’s glory is inseparable from His goodness, compassion, and grace. Moses requested to see God’s glory (see Ex. 33:18–19). God’s goodness, grace, and compassion are components of His glory. God’s glory was also manifested in the sanctuary (Ex. 40:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 8, 'the word of our God. Points to the enduring reliability of God’s word. God is faithful to His promises. His word will stand in contrast to the word of humans. In short, God can be trusted. This verse is quoted in 1 Pet. 1:24–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 9, 'good tidings. Explained in the whole context of chap. 40. It includes the following aspects: God comforts His people, the war is over, iniquity is pardoned (removed), the time of judgment has expired, the end of the exile has come, the new exodus can finally occur, the revelation of God’s glory is available to contemplate, God keeps His promises, and His word is reliable and permanent contrary to the transitory nature of man and his glory. The time of fear is passed. God’s reign has arrived. The arm of the Lord is identified in 52:10 as His salvation and more specifically as His Messiah who shall reign. The pastoral picture of a Shepherd tending his flock, delicately caring for the nursing lambs, is also one of the portraits of the Messiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 12, 'This passage makes a case for God’s sovereignty. However, the overall purpose is to prove that God is able to deliver His people from their enemies and from the gods of their enemies. It is meant to provide a sense of security and dispel or erode the idea that God does not care. To those who wait on the Lord, restoration and renewal are secured.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 40, 31, 'wait on the LORD. Throughout the book of Isaiah, the concept of waiting on the Lord distinguishes God’s faithful from those who do not trust Him and who take things into their own hands, even if it means sinking into idolatry and spiritualism. See also 8:17; 30:18; 33:2; 49:23; 64:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 41, 1, 'one from the east. An allusion to Cyrus. He is also referred to in v. 25. Explicit references with his name mentioned will come in 44:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 41, 4, 'the first. A reference to God as being the first and the last in the context of His ability to speak of things long before they occur (see 42:9). Such is also the case for the mention of Cyrus. These attributes of God show also the major difference between Him and idols (see 41:21–24, 26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 41, 10, 'Fear not. The first of several occurrences in this chapter of an invitation not to fear (see also vv. 13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 41, 14, 'worm. Despite the love and honor toward Jacob expressed in the previous verses, God nevertheless renders a frank evaluation of their condition as lowly and undesirable as a worm. Redeemer. The concept of redeemer is rich in content. The redeemer or next-of-kin had some power and legal obligations. They include the following: (1) To buy back family land sold by a relative due to financial difficulties (see Lev. 25:23–28). (2) To buy back a family member sold into slavery in payment of a debt (Lev. 25:47–49). An allusion of this reality appears in Is. 50:1. (3) To fulfill the requirements of levirate marriage (Deut. 25:5–10). According to this law, when a man dies without having a son, his brother is to marry the widow and the firstborn son of that union is considered the son and heir of the dead man. This language is echoed in Is. 49:20–21, and again in 54:1, 4–5. (4) To avenge the murder of a kinsman (Num. 35). Calling God “Redeemer” implies He will fill all these needs.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 41, 21, 'A taunt against idols. They are worthless, a delusion, and an abomination.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 42, 1, 'My Servant. Introduces the first of four texts in Isaiah that came to be known as “Servant Songs” (see also 49:1–13; 50:4–11; 52:13–53:12; 61:1–3). In general the term “servant” can designate individuals such as patriarchs and prophets (Abraham, Moses, Elijah, David). It is also at times in Isaiah applied to a group such as Israel/Jacob, a corporate entity God has chosen (41:8; 43:10; 44:1), formed or “created” (44:2, 21), redeemed (48:20), and called to be witnesses (43:10). The corporate servant is also depicted as deaf and blind (42:19). Other passages in Isaiah clearly refer to an individual (vv. 1–4; 49:1–6; 50:4–9; 52:13–53:12). This is evident from the usage of the term servant in 49:5–6, where we are informed that the Servant has the mandate or mission to restore Jacob and to raise up the tribes of Jacob. 42:1–4 is quoted in Matt. 12:18–21 and applied to Jesus. In Acts 8:35, Philip identifies the Messiah Jesus by reference to the servant in Is. 52:13–53:12. The key to understanding the role of the Servant of the Lord is His commitment to bring the nations to join Israel in the everlasting covenant. This everlasting covenant with God’s people will be made available to the nations. The Lord will make righteousness and praise spring up before all nations (61:7–11). The Servant brings justice to the nations. He purposes to establish justice on the earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 42, 2, 'not cry out. The coming of redemption and justice is connected to peace, gentleness, and rejection of violence. 53:7–9 specifies that the Suffering Servant embraced nonviolence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 42, 6, 'have called You. The Servant is called “a covenant to the people” and “a light to the Gentiles.” The messianic fulfillment of this declaration fits with what the writers of the Gospels told us about Jesus Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 42, 10, 'Sing. The work of the Servant calls for universal celebration. The world benefits from the defeat of God’s enemies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 42, 14, 'God’s people are promised deliverance and exodus under the Lord’s guidance. The idolatrous will be ashamed of their so-called gods. These verses are veiled calls to forsake idolatry and to turn to the God who can save.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 42, 18, 'God’s people are accused of being deaf and blind. Negligence of the covenant stipulations and of the law brought on this condition. Nonetheless, chap. 43 begins with words of hope: “But now.” Even under these circumstances the Lord’s covenant faithfulness will not be defeated. He resolves to save His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 43, 1, 'God’s love for Israel and His faithfulness to the covenant are central elements to understanding the paradoxes of the book of Isaiah: oracles of judgment and salvation, threats of destruction, and promises of preservation of a remnant that will carry on the identity and mission of God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 43, 14, 'There are several expressions here that allude to the exodus from Egypt: “a way in the sea,” “a path through the mighty waters,” “chariot and horse,” and “the army.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 43, 19, 'a new thing. A new act of deliverance. If in the previous verses God created a path in the waters, this time in reference to the exodus from Babylon, God creates a path through the wilderness from Babylon to Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 44, 1, 'God comforts His people and promises to bless them. The blessing of the Holy Spirit will qualify God’s people to claim to belong to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 44, 1, 'Yet hear now. Used to show first of all that there is a connection between what precedes and what follows. More importantly, it is an indicator of God’s determination to save His people in spite of their unfaithfulness. Even with evidence of covenant breaches and unfaithfulness, God’s grace will prevail.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 44, 2, 'Jeshurun. Found elsewhere only in Deut. 32:15; 33:5, 26. It means straight or upright and refers to Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 44, 6, 'no God. God’s uniqueness, His unique divine prerogative, sets Him apart to be the only One who qualifies to be a Savior. There is no god besides Him and therefore no Savior besides Him. The designation of God as “Rock” is found several times in Deuteronomy (see Deut. 32:4, 15, 18, 31, 37). Elsewhere in Isaiah, see 17:10; 26:4; 30:29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 44, 9, 'Those who make. In these verses images are drawn from the work of a blacksmith (v. 12) and of a carpenter (vv. 13–20) to show the futility of idol-making and worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 44, 28, 'Cyrus. To name a person like Cyrus before he was born, and to name his specific purpose, is intended to show God’s power. Only God could do such a thing unheard of in ancient history except in the biblical record.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 45, 1, 'Israel’s restoration: the fulfillment of God’s purposes for His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 45, 1, 'I will go. This whole section makes a case for God’s absolute sovereignty. He chooses Cyrus and smoothes the path before him so that he may conquer his enemies and fulfill God’s purposes in letting God’s people go free from their exile and captivity. God’s purpose is that Israel be saved with an everlasting salvation (v. 17). In this chapter and in the next, God’s uniqueness as a just God and a Savior is emphasized in contrast to idols (v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 45, 1, 'His anointed. Expands on the leader Cyrus, this time designated as God’s “anointed” or, in Hebrew, messiah. To be anointed signified the fact of being appointed to an office: it could be a kingly, a priestly, or a prophetic function (see Lev. 4:3, 5, 16; 6:22 of priests; 1 Sam. 12:3, 5 of Saul; 2 Sam. 19:21 of David; Dan. 9:25–26 of the Messiah).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 45, 22, 'Look to Me. Salvation is available to all who look to God, to all whose hope is in the God of Israel. Even when God chooses Cyrus to fulfill His purposes, He introduces Himself to him as the God of Israel (see v. 3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 45, 23, 'every knee. Universal allegiance to God is expressed. That every knee will bow before God and every tongue confess is referred to in the NT in reference to the allegiance due to Jesus Christ (Rom. 14:11; Phil. 2:9–11; Rev. 5:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 46, 1, 'The fall of Babylon. When the Lord reverses the fate of His people in captivity, their captors become captives. The idols are incapable of saving themselves. To move locations they have to be loaded in carriages and carried (46:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 46, 1, 'The fall of Babylon. When the Lord reverses the fate of His people in captivity, their captors become captives. The idols are incapable of saving themselves. To move locations they have to be loaded in carriages and carried (46:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 46, 11, 'bird of prey. An allusion to Cyrus, God’s agent in Babylon’s fall (see 41:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 47, 1, 'daughter of Babylon. Babylon is destitute and humiliated (vv. 1–4) and is judged because of her brutality against God’s people and because of her pride, sorceries, enchantments, and wickedness (vv. 5–11). Her tragedy is that she does not realistically see the future (v. 7). She will be suddenly and swiftly judged and punished (v. 9). Evil, trouble, and desolation (v. 11) will befall her. Vv. 12–15 describe Babylon’s helplessness. Not one of those she used to rely upon is now able to save her: astrologers, stargazers, and prognosticators will be consumed (vv. 13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 47, 13, 'astrologers. One of the many references to Babylonian astrology. One of the purposes of the repeated affirmations of God as the Creator of the heavens and the earth is to counter the deification of the sun, the moon, and the stars (40:12, 22, 26; 42:5; 44:24; 45:12; 48:13; 51:13, 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 48, 1, 'A new exodus, defeat of God’s enemies, and celebration of victory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 48, 1, 'A new exodus, defeat of God’s enemies, and celebration of victory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 48, 8, 'the womb. The mention of the womb and the idea of transgression are a reference to Jacob, who had an inclination, even from birth, to take his brother’s rights (see Gen. 25–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 48, 18, 'heeded. This verse reveals how deeply God wishes the well-being of His people. If only His people would give heed to His commandments, then peace and righteousness would prevail among them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 48, 20, 'Go forth. God’s people are called to come out of Babylon. This theme is repeated again in Rev. 18:1–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 49, 3, 'My servant, O Israel. In this verse, the servant is called Israel and is distinguished from corporate Israel as God’s people. This shows that the word Israel can also be a messianic designation without excluding the fact that in other contexts it is a designation for God’s covenant people Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 49, 5, 'bring Jacob back. The mission of the Servant to restore the survivors of Israel shows that in this context the Servant is a specific individual, the Messiah, rather than a corporate entity. Moreover, the mission of the Servant is to bring light to the Gentiles so that the salvation of God may reach to the ends of the earth (v. 6). Jesus fulfills God’s promise to Abraham according to which all the families of the earth will be blessed through him and through his descendants.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 49, 7, 'chosen You. These verses support further the idea that the Servant of the Lord is an individual in this context. The Servant will be worshiped. The Servant embodies the covenant (see also 42:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 49, 8, 'desolate heights. A new exodus comes with the Servant Messiah as leader and provider for His people. He liberates God’s people (v. 9). As He sustained His people in the desolate wilderness after the exodus, He will sustain them in the desolation of the new exodus. The language is used in Rev. 7:16 to describe God’s Last Day people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 49, 14, 'This passage contains God’s remarkable promise and revelation of His inner feelings towards His people. He compares Himself to a mother who would not forget her nursing child. Actually, even if it happens that she would, God would not. Israel is constantly the object of God’s concern and care (v. 16). God’s liberation of His people from their enemies is based on this faithfulness of God to care for His people. V. 25 specifies that God will contend with whoever contends with His people. He is their Savior, their Redeemer, the Mighty One of Jacob. The undergirding theme of waiting on the Lord occurs in this setting. God promises to those who wait for Him that they will not be ashamed (v. 23). On “waiting,” see also 40:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 50, 1, 'Where … Why? The chapter begins with two questions that show that God has not completely rejected His people. There was no divorce in spite of the exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 50, 4, 'has given Me. The Servant is dedicated to the Lord and deeply depends on His leading. Unlike the people against whom an indictment is directed (see v. 2), the Servant is obedient (see Heb. 5:8–9). The Servant suffers abuse, and is shamed, mocked, and spit upon. His vindication, however, comes from God (Is. 50:7). He will overcome His adversaries (v. 9). This text sets the stage for the teaching of the substitutional suffering of the Servant that will be more fully developed in chaps. 52 and 53.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 50, 10, 'The obedient Servant is to be obeyed as a sign of trust in God. V. 11 describes a reversal of fortune; the wicked that abused the Servant instead of listening to Him will ultimately grieve.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 51, 1, 'Call and comfort to righteousness seekers. The commands given throughout this section can serve as indicators of the issues at stake: “Listen” 51:1, 4, 7; “Awake, awake” 51:9, 17; 52:1; “Stand up” 51:17; “Break forth into joy, sing together” 52:9; “Depart! Depart!” 52:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 51, 1, 'Call and comfort to righteousness seekers. The commands given throughout this section can serve as indicators of the issues at stake: “Listen” 51:1, 4, 7; “Awake, awake” 51:9, 17; 52:1; “Stand up” 51:17; “Break forth into joy, sing together” 52:9; “Depart! Depart!” 52:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 51, 5, 'righteousness. This is a word with several meanings. It can mean salvation as in this verse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 51, 6, 'My salvation. The fundamental issue of the book of Isaiah is encapsulated in this section. A key question is the following: Will salvation occur? And connected to it, Will righteousness prevail? The answer is unequivocal. It is the purpose of the whole book to affirm that salvation will occur. God with His Messiah will establish righteousness. The thought is repeated for emphasis in v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 51, 8, 'the moth will eat them up. Isaiah uses graphic language to refer to the end of the wicked. The righteous, on the other hand, are identified as those whose heart is with God’s law and who will not perish.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 52, 7, 'The gospel is defined in terms of peace, salvation, and the reign of God. V. 8 adds the idea of return from exile; v. 9 adds the ideas of comfort and redemption (see 40:1–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 52, 11, 'Depart. This is a call to the people for a new exodus. The call is to depart from Babylon and not to touch anything unclean. See 2 Cor. 6:17–18 and Rev. 18:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 52, 13, 'This is the final, longest, and most crucial of the four songs (see also 42:1–9; 49:1–13; 50:4–11) about the Servant whom Isaiah sees as central to Israel’s restoration. It is probably the most crucial passage in chaps. 40–60. The NT quotes from this passage more than any other OT passage. The following passages relate Jesus to this Servant song: Mark 15:17; John 10:11; 12:37; Rom. 3:25; 4:25; 2 Cor. 5:21; Phil. 2:9. When Jesus’ teaching about His life and mission is understood, the reason for this passage’s importance is clear. Jews of Jesus’ time expected a kingly messiah bringing political deliverance. Jesus, by contrast, taught that the Messiah would bring salvation through suffering and death on behalf of Israel and the nations. This song directly supports and is fulfilled in Jesus’ teaching. The song has five sections of three verses each (Is. 52:13–15; 53:1–3, 4–6, 7–9, 10–12). The first and last sections speak of the glory and exaltation of the Servant while the middle three sections detail what the Servant experiences in bringing salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 52, 13, 'This introduction to the Servant makes clear that in the end the Servant will be “exalted,” “extolled,” and “very high.” Although He may not appear attractive and has a marred visage, rulers of the nations will in the end be impressed by Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 52, 13, 'My Servant. See note on 42:1–9 for an in-depth discussion of the term in Isaiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 52, 14, 'marred. The disfigured appearance and form of the Servant in the end does not hinder His exaltation and the amazement of Gentile nations and their rulers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 52, 15, 'sprinkle. Probably for cleansing, but could also refer to consecration (see Ex. 29:21; Lev. 8:11, 30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 1, 'This three-sectioned core of the song describes the person and work of the Servant Messiah as He deals with sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 1, 'arm of the LORD. Symbolizes God’s power (see also 51:5, 9). The work of the Servant is a revelation of God’s power and will.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 2, 'The Servant is despised and rejected by people because He is outwardly unimpressive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 4, 'borne our griefs. This seemingly unattractive Servant Messiah surprisingly bears the consequences of our sin (griefs and sorrows) as well as the sin itself (transgressions and iniquities). His suffering for us brings us healing. This is all a divine work of God (“the LORD has laid”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 5, 'stripes. Wounds.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 7, 'The result of all the Servant’s suffering and sin-bearing is His death. This death is undeserved because the Servant is blameless: “He had done no violence, nor was any deceit in His mouth” (v. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 7, 'oppressed … afflicted. This verse along with others, especially v. 5, details the physical and emotional abuse that the Servant receives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 8, 'For the transgressions of My people. Over and over the entire song emphasizes that the horrible things the Servant experienced, as well as His death, were on behalf of others and their sin. All this was substitutionary and undeserved. This was God’s plan. Note the following statements: “He has borne our griefs … carried our sorrows” (v. 4); “He was wounded for our transgressions” (v. 5); “He was bruised for our iniquitites” (v. 5); “The chastisement for our peace was upon Him” (v. 5); “by His stripes we are healed” (v. 5); “the LORD has laid on Him the iniquity of us all” (v. 6); “For the transgressions of My people He was stricken” (v. 8); “You make His soul an offering for sin” (v. 10); “He shall bear their iniquities” (v. 11); “He bore the sin of many” (v. 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 10, 'This final section returns to the theme of the first stanza (52:13–15). The climax of the Servant’s work and ministry is celebrated. The results of many being justified leads to a reward by God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 10, 'see His seed. This refers to offspring or descendants and should be understood in a spiritual sense.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 53, 12, 'portion … spoil. The Servant is rewarded by God as a king would be after a mighty victory. intercession for the transgressors. The Servant not only delivers sinners but, as their Priest, climaxes His work by interceding on their behalf (Heb. 7:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 54, 1, 'A covenant of peace. This chapter uses moving language to depict the relationship between God and His people. The Lord is Creator, Redeemer, Husband, and Teacher. He is One who shows everlasting love or kindness and mercy, who is in a covenant of peace that cannot be removed. He is the protector of His people so much so that “no weapon formed against [them] shall prosper” (v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 55, 1, 'Invitation to an abundant life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 55, 1, 'Come. The exiles are invited to return to a party where even those with no money can buy wine and milk, which are symbols of abundance. The death of the Servant (53:5–9) paid for the cost of the feast.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 55, 3, 'everlasting covenant. Will last just as long as the one with David.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 55, 4, 'people … people. Could also be translated “peoples” (plural), meaning nations, as in v. 5. This divine party is not just for Jews but for the nations as well.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 55, 11, 'My word. God’s promises are sure and can be trusted. This is probably a special reference to vv. 3, 5, 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 55, 12, 'go out with joy. The statement refers to the happiness that comes when exile can be left behind and God’s great coming-home party can be celebrated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 56, 1, 'A universal covenant signified by the Sabbath. The themes of justice and righteousness occur and are connected to Sabbath observance. The benefits of the covenant were not exclusive to Israel: foreigners and even eunuchs, who were by law excluded from the Lord’s assembly, could be integrated into God’s people if they showed faith and allegiance to God by keeping themselves from doing evil and by keeping the Sabbath and refraining from desecrating it. Even the temple that clearly delineated boundaries that no foreigner could cross is here called a “house of prayer for all nations.” This text reiterates a truth found throughout the book of Isaiah, namely, the purposes of God go beyond His covenant people Israel to include non-Israelites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 56, 2, 'Sabbath. As the Sabbath was a sign of the covenant at Sinai, so it is a sign for the new covenant discussed here. See also vv. 4, 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 56, 7, 'holy mountain. Refers to Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 56, 9, 'Indictment against the leaders. The charges are the following: they are ignorant, self-indulgent, greedy, and indifferent to what is right. Leaders bear special responsibility because of their influence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 57, 14, 'Promise of healing and restoration for the humble. Humility and a spirit of repentance are valued. They place believers in a right relation with God, waiting for Him, depending on Him. God will not disappoint their expectation, whereas for the wicked there is no peace (v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 58, 1, 'True dedication: the sign of true Sabbath-keeping. Chap. 58 is based on the premise that true devotion cannot bypass obedience to God’s commandments; otherwise it becomes presumption. Without obedience, fasting avails nothing. Without care for the needy, true Sabbath-keeping does not exist. This chapter reveals the depth of the intent of the law. Separating right from wrong is part of the function of the law. Human beings must learn what is in harmony with God’s will and what He dislikes in order to have a clear idea of God’s regulations. In this chapter, what God dislikes is injustice and oppression. What He values is solidarity and sharing one’s goods with the poor and the foreigner.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 58, 12, 'raise up the foundations of many generations. Both physical and spiritual restoration were lacking. The failure to practice a sincere religion had resulted in a need for revival. Reformation must begin with remembering the true Sabbath (v. 13) and a commitment to honor it. Breach. A chasm had grown because people were not spending quality time in communion with God (such as is especially encouraged by Sabbath-keeping) or living according to His law. Streets to Dwell In. The call continues for those who will choose to honor God and return to right paths in the way they live their lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 58, 13, 'The holy day of the LORD honorable. Restoration can be achieved through a renewed understanding of the Sabbath and a desire to keep holy the day God set apart for communion with Him and remembrance of His work as Creator. God is honored as believers engage specifically in activities that lead them to more deeply understand Him and reflect His love to others. not doing your own ways. Especially on Sabbath, God’s people are called to set aside their common secular pursuits, entertainments, and activities and, instead, worship God and extend acts of kindness which will glorify Him and bring joy both to themselves and to those being served. For more on keeping the SABBATH, see Ex. 20:8–11; Jer. 17:19–27; Matt. 12:12; 24:20; Mark 2:27–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 58, 14, 'delight yourself in the LORD. Sabbath will be a blessing rather than a burden to those who recognize its value as a gift from God—a day leading observers into an even more intimate relationship with Him. high hills of the earth. Those who choose to honor the seventh-day Sabbath for a twenty-four-hour period each week—despite hardship, conflicts, or apparent economic disadvantage—will be sustained and find their needs met with abundant spiritual and material blessings beyond what they can imagine.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 59, 1, 'This chapter provides a summary of the major issue of the book of Isaiah. It begins with God arguing His case that it is not because of a supposed inability on His part to save the people from their plight that exile comes. His people are in darkness, they grope like the blind, they stumble at noon, they are like dead men in desolate places (vv. 9–10); they growl like bears and moan sadly like doves, because there is no justice and salvation is far from them (v. 11). V. 14 specifies what is lacking among God’s people: justice, righteousness, truth, equity. Instead there is abuse of those who depart from evil. God decides to bring His salvation and His righteousness, in spite of the people’s abandonment of the covenant. His salvation comes along with the judgment of His enemies. Ultimately, the nations will also have an opportunity to be exposed to the Lord’s glory. The climax of this section is that the Redeemer will come to Zion for those who repent. The theme of God’s frustrated expectations as He looks at His people is repeated in 63:5. Nevertheless, God is determined to fulfill His purposes. His Messiah, His Anointed One, comes for this very purpose.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 59, 9, 'us … we. The prophet included himself with his people and identifies with them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 60, 1, 'The restoration of the city of God for all people. The city is promised eternal excellence, a joy of many generations, a place where violence shall no longer be heard. Seen in the light of the whole book of Isaiah, this prophecy will be fulfilled at the restoration of all things. God will be the everlasting light of the people who are called righteous. Darkness is a symbol of God’s absence; light is the symbol of God’s presence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 60, 3, 'Gentiles … kings. The wealth of nations, as well as their people, will stream toward a renewed Jerusalem. They will even help build the city (v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 60, 19, 'The sun. Not needed in the city because God is the light. This is part of the picture of a new Jerusalem in Rev. 21:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 61, 1, 'The mission of the Messiah: the proclamation of the good news of salvation. Jesus quotes the first part of this section to apply it to Himself (Luke 4:18–19). The text of Isaiah shows that the everlasting covenant God purposes to make with His people and beyond (the nations are mentioned in Is. 61:9, 11) is to bring righteousness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 61, 2, 'the acceptable year. Understood by many to be either a Sabbatical year (every seventh year; Lev. 25) or a Jubilee year (every 50 years; see also Lev. 25). At such times liberty from debt and slavery was proclaimed, and land was returned to former owners.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 61, 3, 'joy. The theme of joy is also connected to the Messiah’s mission. It occurs in this section and also in passages associated with deliverance as in 35:8–10. Ultimately, the gospel or good news is the reversal of the plight of human existence. It is for the poor, the brokenhearted, the captives, the oppressed, those who mourn and are sad, those who are disfigured and loaded in pain, those whose homes are in ruin, and those whose cities are devastated. righteousness. The Messiah’s coming is ultimately aimed to bring justice and righteousness (for other occurrences of the word “righteousness” in this section, see vv. 10–11). The number of themes that Jesus mentions in the beatitudes (Matt. 5:3–11) that are found in Is. 61 is remarkable: the Spirit, v. 1; the poor, v. 1; comfort to those who mourn, v. 2; inheritance of the earth, righteousness, vv. 3, 7–8, 11; joy, vv. 10–11. Also, the mission of the Messiah is inseparable from the mission of the Holy Spirit (see also 11:2; 42:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 61, 6, 'the priests. In the context of chap. 61 the good news for God’s people Israel will climax in the restoration of Israel’s priestly function, just as initially God set His people to be a kingdom of priests, a holy nation (Ex. 19:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 61, 9, 'blessed. According to Gen. 12:1–3, the covenant with Abraham includes the blessing of his descendants and through them all the families of the earth. The everlasting covenant mentioned in this verse and the mention of the nations who will know the posterity whom the Lord blessed reveals the depth and consistency of God in His purpose to transform the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 61, 10, 'As a bridegroom. Garments of salvation, robes of righteousness, and bride and bridegroom ornaments all refer to the festive nature of the marriage covenant in ancient Israel’s wedding ceremony symbolism.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 62, 1, 'Exuberant marriage announcements. This chapter takes up the theme of marriage mentioned in the last verses of chap. 61. Salvation, restoration, and joy saturate chapter 62. Righteousness as an attribute is used twice in this chapter. It is paired with the word glory, specifically the glory of Jerusalem that the kings will see (v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 62, 1, 'My peace. The theme of God’s silence is repeated several times in Isaiah (42:14; 57:11; 64:12). Here God breaks that silence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 62, 2, 'a new name. A new status (see Rev. 2:17; 3:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 62, 4, 'Forsaken. Instead of being called Azubah (“Forsaken”) and Shemamah (“Desolate”), Jerusalem is now called “Hephzibah” (My delight is in her), and “Beulah” (married). These names, not unknown in Scripture (see 1 Kin. 22:42; 2 Kin. 21:1), signal the reversal of fate for Jerusalem. The covenant will be restored, and the marriage renewed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 62, 6, 'set watchmen. A moving call to God’s watchmen, who are the prophets/intercessors, never to cease pleading Jerusalem’s case before God until He answers their prayers and changes her circumstances.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 62, 8, 'give your grain. In Deut. 28:33, one of the covenant curses was the confiscation of the produce of the land by an enemy nation. God now commits Himself by oath that this will never happen again.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 62, 10, 'Go through. The marriage ceremony ends in a procession describing the homecoming of a holy people, the Redeemed of the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 63, 1, 'Edom … Bozrah. The country of Edom and one of its major cities, Bozrah, symbolizes the world that hates God’s people. God will judge them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 63, 7, 'Covenant prayer. Like the prayer in Dan. 9, this prayer begins with the recounting of God’s covenant relation with His people. God’s covenant loyalty is mentioned first, and then the praises due Him. The prayer proper, in terms of request, comes into focus from v. 15 to the end of the chapter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 63, 16, 'our Father. Though not prevalent, it is not uncommon to find the designation for God as Father explicit in the OT (see 64:8; Deut. 32:6; Jer. 3:4, 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 64, 1, 'rend the heavens. Compares the sky to a tent curtain, which God rips and comes through when He wants.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 64, 6, 'filthy rags. The people’s “righteousness” is compared to filthy rags unable to stand before the holiness of God in the day of judgment. This is probably a reference to the cloths used by a woman during her period. Note that it is not the sins that are likened to filthy rags but the people’s attempts at righteousness. See also 57:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 65, 1, 'Grand conclusion to the book. The main themes of judgment, salvation, and hope are discussed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 65, 1, 'Grand conclusion to the book. The main themes of judgment, salvation, and hope are discussed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 65, 1, 'I was found. God answers Isaiah’s prayer even though Israel had not been truly seeking Him and had sinned obstinately.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 65, 8, 'My servants. A major designation of the remnant of God’s people is the word “servant,” repeated several times in this chapter (vv. 8–9, 13–15). Those who have sought Him are the elect of God. The contrast with the wicked reveals further characteristics of the remnant. They have not forgotten or forsaken the Lord; they answer God’s call. They heard Him and desist from doing evil. They rather choose what God delights in (contrast vv. 11–12). The very word “servant” can mean a worshiper, depending on the configuration that is in view. The remnant can be identified as the true worshipers of God, those not given over to idolatry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 65, 11, 'Gad … Meni. The two Hebrew words refer to gods of fortune and destiny or fate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 65, 16, 'truth. One of God’s attributes. The expression “the God of truth” literally means “the God of Amen,” the Dependable One.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 65, 17, 'new heavens and a new earth. Several times in the book Isaiah talks about newness: “new things,” 42:9; “a new song,” 42:10; “a new thing,” 43:19; “new things,” 48:6; “a new name,” 62:2. In chap. 65 what is new brings a completely new order of joy, fulfillment, and harmony among God’s creatures. The covenant curses will be cancelled forever. That is what the agricultural language of abundance conveys (see Lev. 26:14–17; Deut. 28:30). For more on the NEW EARTH, see Is. 11:6–9; 66:22–23; Rev. 21:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 66, 2, 'trembles at My word. An attitude of reverence toward God’s word and listening as one who can be taught (50:4) is essential to understanding Scripture correctly. For more on the role of the BIBLE in the Christian life, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 66, 20, 'bring all your brethren. Gentiles appear to assist in helping to bring the righteous remnant back to a restored Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 66, 23, 'Sabbath. For the significance of the SABBATH across historical time, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 16:22–30; 20:8–11; Luke 4:16; Acts 13:42; 17:2; Rev. 14:7, 12. The SABBATH will be important in the new covenant era and the restored Jerusalem. For other NEW EARTH insights in Isaiah, see 11:6–9; 35:4–10; 65:17, 21–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Isaías', 66, 24, 'The language used here is related to the historical event of the judgment of the Assyrian army during the reign of Hezekiah (37:21–36; see also 2 Kin. 18:17–19:36). The message the prophet emphasizes is that what happened to the Assyrians will occur on a larger scale to God’s enemies at the end time. The righteous witness the event and look at the dead bodies. The metaphors of worm and fire emphasize not eternal conscious torment in a “hell” but the completeness of the destruction and the disgrace of no burial.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 0, NULL, 'Jeremiah’s prophetic ministry was focused on Judah and Jerusalem, and it coincided with the reigns of the five last kings of Judah. The first of these, King Josiah (641–609 B.C.), was the only one to have a positive influence on the nation. His reign was characterized by attempts at religious reform, to rid the country from the idolatrous practices that had become firmly established during the reigns of the preceding kings. The main event of Josiah’s reign, the discovery of the Book of the Law (consisting at least of Deuteronomy) during temple renovations in 622 B.C. (2 Kin. 22:3–20), served to strengthen Josiah’s commitment. However, these reforms proved to be short-lived as Josiah suffered an untimely death in 609 B.C., when fighting Pharaoh Necho II at the battle of Megiddo (2 Kin. 23:29). The book of Jeremiah contains no reaction to these reforms at the time, but their failure to take root provides the religious background for Jeremiah’s messages and probably also the reason for his call.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 0, NULL, 'The primary message of the book of Jeremiah is that peace and true well-being are found only in a covenant relationship with God. This is true as much for the nation of Judah—in the historical setting of Jeremiah’s work—as for the individual, for whom the new covenant promises a transformation of the heart.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 0, NULL, 'It should be noted that the messages in the book are not presented in chronological order and that only some of them are dated. Where the date is known, this is provided in the');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 1, 'words of Jeremiah. A heading for the book, establishing its prophetic authority. Hilkiah. Probably a descendant of Abiathar (see 1 Kin. 2:26–27). Anathoth. A town 3 miles (5 km) northeast of Jerusalem (see Introduction, “Author and Date”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 2, 'days of Josiah. Reigned 641–609 B.C. (see 2 Kin. 22:1–23:30). thirteenth year. 627 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 3, 'Jehoiakim. Reigned 609–598 B.C. (see 2 Kin. 23:30–37). eleventh year. 586 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 4, 'the word of the LORD came. A formula introducing a prophecy (see Ezek. 1:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 5, 'Before I formed you. Jeremiah is specially chosen for the task. God is the giver of life, even though birth happens by natural means. This gives God the right to Jeremiah’s life and obedience. knew. Intimate knowledge (see Amos 3:2). sanctified. The prophetic office is sacred by nature. ordained. The appointment is made by God alone; the prophetic office is not inherited. nations. Jeremiah has a message for all the nations, not just Judah (see chaps. 46–51).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 6, 'I cannot speak, for I am a youth. Jeremiah feels inadequate for the task, as Moses did (see Ex. 4:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 7, 'Do not say. God counteracts Jeremiah’s objections. Jeremiah is not chosen because of his eloquence; and the message to be spoken is God’s anyway. to all to whom I send you. The audience is chosen by God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 8, 'faces. People. I am with you to deliver you. Jeremiah’s task is daunting, but God promises His protection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 9, 'LORD … touched my mouth. In a vision, to remedy Jeremiah’s feeling of inability to speak (see Is. 6:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 10, 'root out … pull down … destroy … throw down … build … plant. Six terms which reoccur in Jeremiah’s message. Four are negative: Jeremiah’s message will be largely condemnation and doom, but the two last words offer hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 11, 'what do you see? A prophetic vision is introduced (see v. 13). almond tree … ready to perform. A play on two words which in the original language sound almost the same. Jeremiah is only the messenger; God Himself will fulfill His word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 13, 'What do you see? See v. 11. pot. A vessel for cooking found in every home. facing away from the north. That is, facing south.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 14, 'Out of the north. No direct route existed between Mesopotamia and Judah because of the intervening mountains and deserts. Thus enemies came through a northern route, via Syria. The Assyrians came this way a century before Jeremiah to invade the northern kingdom. The Babylonians are now coming this way against Judah. calamity. Literally, the evil. all the inhabitants. Destruction will affect everybody.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 15, 'families. Tribes or clans. kingdoms of the north. Babylonians were a coalition of peoples. This prophecy may also look beyond Jeremiah’s time to the final gathering of nations (see, e.g., Ezek. 38). set his throne. Foreign powers establish themselves in Judah. the gates. The place for administrating justice in Israel, which the Babylonians will take over. walls all around. Jerusalem was well fortified and had previously endured long sieges. cities of Judah. The disaster will be comprehensive, affecting the whole country.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 16, 'wickedness. The reason for judgment. forsaken Me. Broken the covenant. Burned incense. Common feature of idol worship. other gods. Idols Judah adopted from its neighbors. works of their own hands. Idols were human-made (see Is. 44:9–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 17, 'prepare yourself. Literally, gird up your loins, that is, gather and tie the long flowing robes so you can move easily.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 18, 'fortified city … bronze walls. Symbols of indomitable power. kings … princes … priests. The three groups holding administrative, financial, and religious power, and thus most responsible for the judgment (see 2:2, 26). people of the land. The judgment will affect all, even if responsibility is not equally shared.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 1, 19, 'fight against you. Jeremiah’s ministry will experience opposition and hostility. prevail. Jeremiah’s mission is God’s mission and will therefore triumph in the end.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 1, 'Israel’s sin and punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 1, 'Evidence for Israel’s wickedness is drawn from the nation’s history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 1, 'Israel’s sin and punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 1, 'Evidence for Israel’s wickedness is drawn from the nation’s history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 1, 'word of the LORD came. See 1:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 2, 'in the hearing of Jerusalem. Jeremiah’s main ministry was for the people of Judah and mostly took place in its capital. kindness. A vivid word in the original language, also meaning “love,” “devotion,” “faithfulness.” betrothal. The time of Israel’s covenant with God at Sinai. God’s relationship with Israel is frequently compared to marriage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 3, 'holiness. Being set apart for God (see Lev. 11:44). firstfruits. Symbolize Israel’s belonging to God. The firstfruits sacrifice was a reminder of this (see Lev. 23:9–14). All that devour … offend. The covenant afforded Israel God’s special protection. Anyone mistreating Israel would be held guilty before God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 4, 'Hear. Urgent summons, as in a law court.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 5, 'Thus says the LORD. A phrase indicating that a prophet is speaking God’s word on God’s authority. False prophets adopted this phrase to mislead listeners (see 28:2). injustice. The only valid reason for Israel breaking the covenant would have been God doing so first.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 6, 'brought us up out of … Egypt. The exodus is evidence that God had kept His side of the covenant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 7, 'bountiful country. Canaan, the land of “milk and honey” (see Ex. 3:8), opposite of “wilderness” and “deserts” (see Jer. 2:6), was God’s gift to Israel. defiled. Opposite of holiness (v. 3); consequence of idol worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 8, 'Four groups of leaders all failed to guide their people to God. Baal. Canaanite god adopted by some Israelites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 9, 'charges. Legal language (see v. 4). The covenant is likened to a legal treaty whose violator would be taken to court (see v. 35). children’s children. Effects of wrongdoing often last for generations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 10, 'Cyprus. Mediterranean island to the northwest. Kedar. A region in Arabia to the southeast, together representing Israel’s neighbors all around.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 11, 'nation changed its gods. Ironically, the Gentiles were often more loyal to their idol-gods than Israel was to the true God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 12, 'Be astonished, O heavens. The whole universe is appalled by Israel’s heinous apostasy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 13, 'living waters. God is the vital source of life, here and eternally (see John 4:10). cisterns … hold no water. People who prefer idols perish, as all life does without water.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 14, 'Is Israel a servant? Negative answer is expected; Israel was God’s bride (v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 15, 'young lions. Assyrians had “plundered” the northern kingdom a century before. Noph … Tahpanhes. Cities in Egypt. broken the crown. Egyptians had aided the fall of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 17, 'forsaken the LORD. The calamities had occurred because the nation left the protection of God’s covenant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 18, 'Egypt … Assyria. God’s people had frequently sought alliance with other nations, against God’s will, but these, too, were subject to God’s judgment. Sihor. The Nile. the River. Euphrates. Drinking from these cannot substitute for the “living waters” (see v. 13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 19, 'bitter thing. Sin may taste sweet at the time, but it will always turn into bitterness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 20, 'I have broken your yoke and burst your bonds. God broke the bonds of His people at the exodus. They promised to serve Him and then rebelled.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 21, 'I had planted. God planned prosperity for His people. vine. Plant highly valued for its fruit, a symbol for Israel (see Is. 5:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 22, 'lye … soap. Natural cleansing cannot remove guilt; forgiveness is needed (see Ps. 51:2, 7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 23, 'How can you say. Israel denies her sins. dromedary. A she-camel. Israel is like an animal seeking a male in her lust for other gods.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 26, 'ashamed … found out. The nation and its leaders will regret their idol worship when trouble comes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 27, 'tree … my father … stone … gave birth. Mockery of Canaanite idol practices adopted by Israel. trouble. Impending Babylonian invasion. Arise and save. Plea from Israel, but too late.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 29, 'Why will you plead. The nation turns to God when it’s too late.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 30, 'In vain I have chastened. God’s discipline was meant to be redemptive. devoured your prophets. God’s true messengers were often persecuted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 32, 'Can a virgin forget. A “no” answer is expected. It would have been equally unnatural for God to forget Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 34, 'blood of … poor innocents. Ceasing to honor God led to injustices against one’s neighbor, as much a sin as idol worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 2, 35, 'I am innocent. Denial is the first obstacle to repentance. plead My case. God can show, as if in court, that He has kept His covenant promises, while Israel has broken theirs. Judgment will thus be just.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 1, 'If a man divorces … Yet return to Me. Divorce was allowed on account of unfaithfulness, but remarriage to the original partner after another marriage was not (Deut. 24:1–4). God, however, was willing to take Judah back, regardless. lovers. Idols, also Judah’s improper political alliances.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 2, 'polluted the land. An unholy nation defiles its land, for a land and its people are closely connected. harlotries. Idol worship, but this often involved sexual misconduct.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 3, 'showers … withheld. Ironically, Baal was worshiped to guarantee the land’s fertility. In fact, creation depends on its Creator.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 4, 'cry to Me, “My Father.” See 2:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 5, 'angry forever. Upon repentance, forgiveness was always available, even when some consequences of sin were unavoidable (see 34:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 6, 'A plea for Israel’s repentance and a promise of restoration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 6, 'backsliding Israel. The northern kingdom preceded Judah in apostasy as well as punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 8, 'certificate of divorce. Symbolic language for undoing the covenant between God and Israel, His bride (v. 1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 9, 'adultery with stones and trees. See 2:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 10, 'in pretense. Judah pleaded for God’s help when in trouble but did not repent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 11, 'Israel … more righteous. Judah had the advantage of seeing Israel’s fate, but they refused to learn from it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 12, 'north. Where Israel was exiled. I am merciful. God’s main characteristic is abundant and never-failing mercy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 13, 'acknowledge your iniquity. Repentance alone is needed for forgiveness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 14, 'one … two from a family. Hope for at least a remnant of the ten tribes of Israel to return from their exile. Zion. Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 15, 'shepherds. Nation’s leaders who totally failed in their task. according to My heart. Eventually, God Himself will choose leaders who will not fail (see 33:14–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 16, 'shall come to pass. That is, at the end of the days when the Messiah comes (see Introduction, “Content and Themes”). ark of the covenant. Disappeared from the temple before its destruction and has never been recovered. shall not come to mind. At the final restoration the symbols of God’s presence, like the ark, are not needed as God Himself lives among His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 17, 'Jerusalem … The Throne. God’s dwelling place amongst the nations, as the mercy seat of the ark was God’s throne among Israel (see 1 Sam. 4:4). nations shall be gathered. To fulfill God’s plan that all nations should serve Him (see Is. 2:2–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 18, 'In those days. At the final restoration (see v. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 19, 'I. God. “My Father.” The parental relationship well illustrates the intimacy possible between God and His people (see v. 4; Matt. 6:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 21, 'voice was heard … Weeping. Jeremiah personally feels the sins of his people and offers a prayer of repentance on their behalf (see vv. 21–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 23, 'in vain is salvation … from the hills. Idols were served on high places, but at times of crisis no help came from them. LORD … salvation of Israel. God is the only hope for the nation’s survival.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 3, 25, 'we have sinned. Jeremiah confesses the nation’s sins on its behalf.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 1, 'If you will return. Repentance can yet safeguard the nation’s future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 2, 'shall swear … In truth. Repentance has to be heartfelt and genuine, not superficial or pretended. nations shall bless themselves. The covenant with Israel was meant to bring blessings to all peoples (see Gen. 12:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 3, 'men of Judah and Jerusalem. People of Jeremiah’s time are addressed again, with a plea for repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 4, 'Circumcise yourselves. Circumcision was a covenant sign (see Gen. 17:10–14), but it was never intended simply as an outward act; it symbolized the conversion of the heart (see Deut. 10:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 5, 'Announcement of punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 5, 'Blow the trumpet. A ram’s horn, to alert the city to danger. go … fortified cities. The safest places, but eventually even they offered no shelter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 6, 'disaster from the north. See 1:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 7, 'lion. A symbol of Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 8, 'with sackcloth. To show heartfelt mourning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 9, 'princes … priests … prophets. See 2:8, 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 10, 'You … deceived. In shock, seeing the impending doom, Jeremiah blames God for allowing false prophets to lead the people astray. You shall have peace. What the false prophets proclaimed (8:11; 23:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 11, 'At that time. The time of the Babylonian invasion. dry wind. The Babylonian army will blow like a scorching desert wind, which instead of helping the nation to winnow its harvest, will result in the total destruction of the land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 13, 'chariots like a whirlwind. The enemy is unstoppable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 14, 'wash … from wickedness. Only repentance can reverse the nation’s fate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 15, 'Dan … Ephraim. Northern places will see the army approaching before Judah and raise a warning cry (see v. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 16, 'nations. Will witness God’s dealings with Judah. watchers. Enemies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 18, 'wickedness … reaches … heart. Judah itself is responsible for her misfortune.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 19, 'I am pained. Jeremiah is in deep sorrow, having delivered God’s message of doom, knowing it will come true.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 22, 'My people. A covenant term. God laments the loss of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 23, 'without form, and void. The chaos before creation (see Gen. 1:2), which the scale of destruction calls into mind.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 24, 'Natural disasters illustrate the seriousness of Judah’s judgment. These did not necessarily occur at Jeremiah’s time, but symbolize God’s power over the creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 26, 'fruitful land … wilderness. Destruction caused by the Babylonians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 27, 'not … full end. There is hope after judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 28, 'earth mourn. See 3:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 4, 30, 'crimson … gold … paint. Jerusalem, dressed up as a prostitute, hopes to lure the invaders and avert destruction. However, she dies in agony.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 1, 'executes judgment. A search is made for one righteous man to save the city, to avert the judgment, but none is found (see Gen. 18:23–25). Though this is not meant literally, for some remained true to God, it highlights the need for corrective punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 2, 'Pious words are not enough; honest actions must follow. But Judah refuses brazenly to repent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 4, 'poor … foolish … do not know. Burdened with the demands of daily existence, the uneducated poor depended on “the great men,” their leaders, for guidance. Thus they carry the ultimate responsibility.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 6, 'lion … wolf … leopard. The judgment will be like being caught by these fierce animals, which all existed in Palestine in Jeremiah’s time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 7, 'How shall I pardon. The nation’s conduct leaves God no choice but judgment. sworn by … not gods. See 2:27–28. committed adultery. See 3:1–2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 8, 'stallions. See 2:23–24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 9, 'punish … avenge. Punishment is inevitable as the nation sinks into depravity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 10, 'not … complete end. A remnant will survive; the coming judgment will not wipe out the nation. branches. Judah is pruned as if it were a vine (see 2:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 11, 'Israel. The northern kingdom (see 3:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 12, 'not He. People think that God will not punish them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 13, 'prophets become wind. False prophets with their messages of peace are shown to have no substance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 14, 'My words … fire. God’s words spoken by Jeremiah are powerful and will come true.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 15, 'nation … from afar. Babylonians. ancient nation. Much older than Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 16, 'quiver … tomb. Imagery of Babylonian military superiority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 17, 'they shall eat. War brings the evils of famine and devastation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 18, 'See v. 10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 19, 'For choosing to worship foreign gods, Judah will be forced to serve the people of these gods.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 21, 'God has revealed Himself to His people in numerous ways; they have no excuse for not knowing Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 22, 'The creation provides evidence for God’s power as the Creator of the world and the sustainer of life, yet Judah still withholds her respect.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 26, 'My people. God still acknowledges Judah as His chosen nation, although they have erred. snares … trap … catch men. Like a trap-setter catches a cage full of birds, so the lies of the leaders catch people entrapped in their leaders’ deceit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 28, 'fat … sleek. Ruling with dishonesty has benefited the leaders, while the fatherless and needy are neglected.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 29, 'See v. 9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 5, 31, 'prophets … priests. Pervert their calling by doing the opposite of what is expected.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 1, 'Jerusalem is under attack; nowhere is safe. children of Benjamin. Tribe north of Jerusalem. Tekoa … Beth Haccerem. Towns south and west of Jerusalem: thus warnings should be set all around. disaster … north. See 1:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 2, 'daughter of Zion … lovely and delicate. Like a beautiful woman attracts men, foreign leaders are drawn to Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 4, 'Prepare. Literally, consecrate. The enemy undergoes religious rites in readiness for war. noon … night. Not the usual times for attack; underlines the enemy’s urgency and eagerness. thus … LORD … said. God ordered the invasion. build a mound. To overcome the city walls. oppression … wickedness. Idolatry and injustice against the vulnerable were Judah’s main sins against their neighbor and against God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 7, 'Jerusalem is like a productive fountain, but unfortunately only for wickedness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 8, 'Lest My soul depart. Repentance is needed for any hope of avoiding the disaster.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 9, 'thoroughly glean. The enemy will destroy Judah as thoroughly as the gleaners look for the last grapes after the main harvest. grape-gatherer. Jeremiah, who looks for anyone on God’s side. uncircumcised. Jeremiah’s final warnings fall on unrepentant ears.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 11, 'full of … fury. As no righteousness was found, God’s anger will be poured on Judah. Everyone and everything will be affected by judgment. prophet … priest. Leaders will carry special responsibility (see 4:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 14, 'healed … slightly. Meaning the false prophets have not taken sin seriously. “Peace, peace!” False messages lulled the nation into false security, ignoring repentance (see 4:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 15, 'When the basic decency to feel shame for wrongdoing is lost, there is no hope of repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 16, 'in the ways. The nation stands at a crossroads. old paths. The righteous ways of Israel’s ancestors. walk in it. The right choice would guarantee life and prosperity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 17, 'watchmen. God’s prophets who went unheeded. sound of the trumpet. God’s warning messages (see 4:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 18, 'hear, you nations. Other nations are called to witness what happens to Judah, as God’s plan of salvation is universal. Hear, O earth! The creation also witnesses that God’s judgment is just.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 20, 'Religious rituals were popular in Judah, but offerings in themselves bring no salvation. frankincense. A tree resin that was burned with sacrifices to make a pleasing aroma. Sheba. Place in southern Arabia, famous for its incense. sweet cane. A plant used for precious oils.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 22, 'people … from the north. The Babylonians (see 1:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 23, 'Babylonia’s well-equipped military and cruel reputation. daughter of Zion. Jerusalem and her inhabitants.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 26, 'sackcloth … ashes. Funeral attire. mourning … only son. Bitter mourning as parents left with no children.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 27, 'assayer. Tester of metals. among My people. Jeremiah’s final search for the gold of righteousness in Judah. bronze and iron. Only base metals are found.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 6, 29, 'lead. Used in refining silver in ancient times. smelter refines in vain. No gold is found. rejected silver. The nation failed the test.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 1, 'Messages concerning the temple and idolatrous practices replacing true worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 1, 'Jeremiah’s famous Temple Sermon—a key message in the book (see also 26:2–6, 12–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 1, 'Messages concerning the temple and idolatrous practices replacing true worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 1, 'Jeremiah’s famous Temple Sermon—a key message in the book (see also 26:2–6, 12–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 2, 'gate. Between the inner and outer courts of the temple, probably the New Gate (see 26:10). all … Judah who enter … to worship. Worship services were popular; the issue is not neglect of religion but its nature.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 3, 'Amend. Call to changed lives. this place. The promised land. Possessing it depended on loyalty to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 4, 'lying words. False prophets proclaimed that the presence of the temple guaranteed God’s continuing protection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 5, 'execute judgment. Act justly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 6, 'do not oppress … walk after other gods. Explains what justice means in relation to one’s neighbor and in relation to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 7, 'dwell in this place. Possession of Canaan was a covenant promise to Abraham (see Gen. 17:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 9, 'Five of the Ten Commandments are referred to, as representatives of all. Having broken them, the people still expected God to accept their worship. My name. God’s character and presence. abominations. Idolatry and law breaking, incompatible with God’s character.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 11, 'den of thieves. A gathering place for criminals. Jesus used this expression when He threw the money changers out of the temple (Matt. 21:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 12, 'My place … in Shiloh. Where the tabernacle was set up after the conquest of Canaan (Josh. 18:1), but destroyed by the Philistines, after the Israelites had lost the ark to them in battle (see 1 Sam. 4). Similarly, Judah’s evil practices will bring judgment on Jerusalem. rising up early and speaking. Doing so earnestly and continuously.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 14, 'The fate of Shiloh is predicted for Jerusalem’s temple. Ephraim. Israel, the northern kingdom (see 31:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 16, 'do not pray. God says it will be pointless to pray. Even a prayer of intercession will not change His mind.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 18, 'cakes for the queen of heaven. Specially shaped cakes were offered to the goddess Ishtar in the very temple itself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 20, 'My anger … be poured. God is slow to anger, but once judgment takes place nothing escapes the consequences.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 22, 'Sacrifices are not effective in themselves, but only when offered with obedient and dedicated hearts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 24, 'they did not obey. Israel’s history was one of disobedience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 25, 'Since the day. Israel broke the covenant soon after it was first made. sent … My servants. God kept calling His people back through His prophets. daily rising … and sending. See v. 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 27, 'Judah is moving beyond redemption.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 29, 'Cut off your hair. Mourning ritual (see Job 1:20). Jeremiah laments over Judah as if it had already been destroyed (see vv. 29–34). lamentation on … heights. Jeremiah performs a funeral as a prophecy for Judah’s destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 30, 'abominations. Altars to idols, present in the temple at least since Manasseh’s time (2 Kin. 21:1–5). pollute it. Foreign practices destroyed the holiness of God’s house.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 31, 'high places of Tophet. Sites for idolatry near Jerusalem. Valley of … Hinnom. Area outside Jerusalem, where human sacrifices were offered, often by burning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 7, 33, 'Birds and beasts will eat the bodies too numerous to be buried. There is no one to stop such feasting. The sounds of normal human interaction will cease.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 1, 'bring … bones. Desecration of graves was a serious offense and indicates the breakdown of society.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 2, 'Worship of heavenly bodies is mocked. Even in death the people of Judah will come before the deities they worshiped and which could not save them. refuse. Manure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 4, 'Judah’s behavior is unnatural: one who falls naturally rises and goes on, but Judah’s falling into apostasy has become permanent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 6, '“What have I done?” Even when challenged, the people cannot see their guilt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 7, 'Even birds follow their God-given instinct to migrate and thus acknowledge the natural law that governs them, but Judah refuses to acknowledge God’s specific revelation to them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 8, '“We are wise … law … is with us.” Judah lives in self-denial. Having the law does not save; it needs to be obeyed as well. false pen of the scribe. The leaders’ deceitful teaching undermined God’s law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 9, 'Wisdom is found only in dedication to God’s word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 10, 'See 6:12–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 13, 'vine. See 2:21. Judah produces no fruit of righteousness, despite dwelling in a fertile land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 14, 'enter the fortified cities. Terrified peasants flee to cities as the countryside is invaded first. gall to drink. Perhaps a reference to trial by ordeal (see Num. 5:11–31). Judah is found guilty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 15, 'looked for peace. The hope of peace was given by leaders, but war came, as Jeremiah predicted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 16, 'Dan. See 4:15; where invading armies were first seen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 17, 'Vipers which cannot be charmed. The enemy will have no mercy for Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 18, 'Jeremiah grieves his people’s fate, even though he knows judgment is necessary. From a far country. Babylon. Jeremiah sees the exile as if it had already happened. Is not the LORD in Zion? People are bewildered as the unthinkable happens and God’s city and temple are destroyed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 20, 'Harvest, the usual time of plenty, has brought starvation and death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 21, 'For the hurt … I am hurt. Jeremiah loves his people and identifies with them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 8, 22, 'Gilead. A region east of Jordan famous for its medicinal herbs (Gen. 37:25). no physician. Only repentance can cure the sin of disease (see 6:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 1, 'Jeremiah mourns the fate of his people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 2, 'Jeremiah wishes for a refuge in the wilderness, as witnessing Judah’s depravity becomes unbearable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 3, 'lies. Opposed God’s message at every step (see v. 6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 4, 'Loving God and one’s neighbor go together: as God is forgotten, social order also breaks down. People live in fear of their neighbors as each one takes advantage of the other.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 6, 'refuse to know Me. Refusing to make a personal commitment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 7, 'I will refine them. See 6:27–30. God’s judgment is not meant as a punishment, but as an attempt to bring the nation to repentance. God is still merciful, and return is possible.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 9, 'See 5:9, 29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 10, 'Lament in the face of total devastation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 10, 'mountains. Even the land itself suffers the effects of judgment. cattle. Domesticated animals have been slaughtered. birds … beasts. Wild animals have fled.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 11, 'den of jackals. Utter desolation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 12, 'to whom … has spoken. God’s word alone leads to understanding the turmoil Judah is suffering.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 13, 'Jeremiah’s prophecies explain why judgment has fallen on the nation: idol worship and the neglect of God’s law are the reasons.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 15, 'God’s remedy for Judah’s situation will feel bitter. wormwood. Bitter plant. water of gall. See 8:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 16, 'scatter … among the Gentiles. Judah will be exiled in Babylon. Some people will flee to other countries, such as Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 17, 'Judah’s fate is now sure, as judgment had already taken place. Her destruction is mourned. mourning women. Professional wailers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 19, 'Destruction and exile cause great grief.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 20, 'Teach your daughters wailing. The misfortune is so great that everyone is needed for mourning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 21, 'death … through our windows. No precautions help against death. entered our palaces. Nowhere is safe.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 22, 'See 7:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 23, 'Human cunning, power, and wealth have failed to protect the people. The only true reason for boasting is the knowledge of God. lovingkindness, judgment, and righteousness. Key characteristics of God and His dealing with humankind.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 25, 'God is the God of all nations and eventually all will be judged. days are coming. Later than the current crisis.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 9, 26, 'Egypt … Moab. Israel’s and Judah’s neighbors. uncircumcised. Many of Israel’s neighboring countries practiced circumcision, but true circumcision means the conversion of the heart (see 4:4). Thus none of the nations count as circumcised.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 1, 'Hear. See 2:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 2, 'way of the Gentiles. Their beliefs. signs of heaven. Heavenly bodies which Israel’s neighbors worshiped and from whose movements the will of a god was divined.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 3, 'A realistic description of idol manufacturing (see Is. 44:9–20). Idols were often made of wood, then plated with silver and gold. cannot speak … must be carried. Description of the powerlessness of the idols which cannot even stand unaided. do evil … good. Thus idols need not be feared.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 6, 'God is the powerful ruler of all nations. Gods in the ancient world were thought to have their own territories, such as cities, but Israel’s God claimed the whole world as His territory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 8, 'Earthly origins of idols, skillfully made from precious materials, but still powerless. worthless doctrine. Wooden idols cannot impart knowledge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 9, 'Tarshish. A center of commerce in ancient times, which many suggest was located in southern Spain (see Ezek. 27:12). Uphaz. Location unknown.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 10, 'God’s authority is based on His being the Creator. While idols need to be made, God is the Maker of heaven and earth. The power is in the Maker, not in the things He has made, such as the heavenly bodies (v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 14, 'no breath. Idols have no life, no power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 15, 'Idols cannot come to anyone’s aid.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 16, 'Portion of Jacob. Or “inheritance of Jacob,” a title for God suggesting the close relationship He has with the nation and His ability to provide for their needs (see 51:19). Israel is the tribe of His inheritance. The chosen nation, by covenant with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 18, 'I will throw out. Suddenly and violently.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 19, 'Woe is me. Jeremiah speaks, mourning over the community he also represents.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 20, 'tent is plundered. Image from nomadic life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 21, 'shepherds … dull-hearted. Judah’s leaders, who failed in their duty (see 3:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 22, 'commotion out of the north. See 1:14; 6:22. den of jackals. See 9:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 23, 'Left to his own devices, man will always go wrong. way of man is not in himself. Only God can direct a person to righteousness. correct … with justice. Plea for mercy in judgment, as no one can survive God’s anger.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 10, 25, 'Plea for God to remember the covenant even though Judah has broken it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 1, 'Messages concerning the broken covenant and the coming judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 1, 'Messages concerning the broken covenant and the coming judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 2, 'Hear. Urgent summons as Jeremiah will deliver a sermon on the broken covenant (vv. 12–17). words of this covenant. Covenant terms, rights and responsibilities, agreed at Sinai, reaffirmed by Josiah (see 2 Kin. 22:8–23:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 3, 'Cursed … does not obey. The covenant included curses, best understood as consequences of sin, if broken, and blessings when kept (see Deut. 27:15–28:68).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 4, 'brought them out of … Egypt. Exodus is the basis of covenant. iron furnace. Severe conditions in Egypt. Obey. Israel’s covenant responsibility. My people. God promised to make Israel a special nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 5, 'oath … to your fathers. The covenant was first made with the patriarchs, then with Israel at Sinai (Gen. 12:2; 15:18; Ex. 24:7). God redeemed His promises, including giving Israel the land of Canaan. a land flowing with milk and honey. Picturing abundance and fertility (see 2:7). So be it. Literally, amen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 6, 'Judah … Jerusalem. See 2:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 7, 'God recounts His faithfulness. exhorted your fathers. God patiently called Israel back when they disobeyed. rising early and exhorting. See 7:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 8, 'Disobedience brought curses upon the nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 9, 'conspiracy. Both to undo Josiah’s reforms (see v. 2) and to silence Jeremiah as God’s messenger. turned back. To idol worship, after Josiah had rooted it out.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 11, 'I will surely bring calamity. The Babylonian invasion will be God’s judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 12, 'gods … will not save. Idols cannot influence history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 13, 'number … cities … gods. Judah’s idols were many; cities often had their own patron gods (see 2:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 14, 'Intercession is forbidden; see 7:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 15, 'My beloved. Judah (see 2:2). My house. The temple. evil … rejoice. The sin the nation sunk into.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 16, 'Green Olive Tree. Image of the committed Israel, (compare “vine,” 2:21), now to be destroyed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 17, 'doom against … Israel … Judah. See 3:6–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 18, 'The first of Jeremiah’s six Confessions (see Introduction, “Author and Date”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 18, 'LORD gave me knowledge. God revealed to Jeremiah a plot against his life (see 12:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 19, 'docile lamb. Jeremiah was totally unaware of any threat. destroy the tree with its fruit. A sinister plot to kill Jeremiah while he has no children (see 16:2). He would leave no descendants to continue his line and remember him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 20, 'Jeremiah prays for God’s intervention. vengeance. Jeremiah speaks in despair and honesty, rather than angrily. To silence him was to attempt to silence God’s voice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 21, 'Anathoth. Jeremiah’s hometown. The plotters were mainly his kinsmen (see 1:1). Do not prophesy. Jeremiah’s messages disagreed with the deceptive but comforting words of false prophets.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 11, 22, 'Unlike Jeremiah, the plotters will die young and childless. no remnant. No one will remember them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 1, 'Jeremiah’s second Confession (see Introduction, “Author and Date”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 1, 'Why … wicked prosper? Jeremiah does not question God’s righteousness but laments that the innocent suffer while the wicked prosper. To this difficult question posed in Scripture, there is no simple answer (Ps. 37; 73; book of Habakkuk).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 2, 'You have planted them. God is the source of all life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 3, 'tested my heart. Jeremiah’s suffering does not stem from wrongdoing. sheep … slaughter. Image of innocence and helplessness (see Is. 53:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 4, 'How long. It is easier to bear pain when one knows when it will end.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 5, 'God’s answer. run with the footmen … contend with horses. Even harder times are coming. God tells Jeremiah that no human can fathom the mystery of suffering. Jeremiah must trust that God is in charge and that ultimately the wicked will not prosper.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 7, 'God mourns over the state of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 7, 'My house. Judah (see 11:17). heritage. Chosen nation, promised land. dearly beloved. See 2:2. With terms of endearment God shows His love for Judah even as He has to punish her.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 8, 'like a lion. Judah has defied God and is herself responsible for the coming catastrophe.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 9, 'like a speckled vulture. Judah’s proud appearance makes her a target of other nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 10, 'Many rulers. Foreign kings who have plundered Judah. My vineyard. Judah (see 2:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 12, 'plunderers have come. The Babylonian invasion is imminent. sword of the LORD. The Babylonians are God’s agent of judgment for Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 13, 'sown wheat but reaped thorns. Judah’s problem was not lack of religion but its nature: idol worship brings a harvest of destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 14, 'evil neighbors. God allowing other nations to triumph over Judah does not exempt them from judgment in their turn. pluck … Judah. A promise that Judah will be returned to the promised land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 15, 'return and have compassion. There is hope beyond the current judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 12, 16, 'if they will learn carefully the ways of My people. Covenant terms do not change, and the nations are welcomed into them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 1, 'The linen sash is an enacted prophecy (see Introduction, “Content and Themes”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 1, 'linen sash. Wide belt. Linen, used for priestly garments, symbolizes holiness. The sash around the waist symbolized the close, personal relationship God and His people were meant to enjoy (see v. 11; 2:2–3). not … in water. Judah’s pride (see v. 9) cannot be washed away by natural means.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 4, 'Euphrates. For textual reasons, it is unclear whether this refers to the famous river near Babylon or, metaphorically, to a place much closer to Jeremiah’s hometown. Either way, as the sash is ruined by burial near the spring, so the Babylonians, from the famous river, cause Judah’s ruin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 6, 'after many days. Unspecific lengthy time, symbolizing time in captivity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 9, 'pride … great pride. Judah’s arrogance was in not recognizing its dependence on God. Thus the objects of their pride will be destroyed, including Jerusalem and the temple itself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 10, 'profitable for nothing. No longer fit to represent God. renown … glory. God’s intention for His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 12, '“Every bottle shall be filled with wine.” A proverb stating a self-evident truth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 13, 'drunkenness. God’s anger. Judah should have been full of God’s goodness and holiness; instead it is so filled with God’s anger that no one will escape.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 14, 'dash. Break. Judgment will leave the nation devastated. Like broken bottles cannot hold wine, Judah was unfit for its purpose of representing God (see vv. 10–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 16, 'Give glory to the LORD your God. Last minute plea for Judah to recognize God, in order to avert the Babylonian invasion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 17, 'My soul will weep. Jeremiah mourns his nation’s fate. taken captive. Exiled in Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 18, 'king … queen mother. Leaders, headed by the royalty, are ultimately responsible for the nation’s fate. Humble yourselves. Recognize God as the origin and sustainer of the nation’s existence (see v. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 19, 'South. Negev desert, the edge of Judah’s kingdom. shall be shut. Judgment will affect even the farthest reaches of the kingdom. carried away captive. See v. 17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 20, 'who come from the north. See 1:14. flock … given to you. Leaders are the nation’s shepherds (see 3:15; 10:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 21, 'Various images are used to illustrate Judah’s sinfulness and fate. skirts … uncovered. Public humiliation. Ethiopian change his skin … leopard its spots. Evil has become as inseparable as one’s skin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 24, 'scatter … stubble. Judah will suffer great confusion on its way to exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 13, 25, 'falsehood. False prophecies of peace (see 6:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 1, 'concerning the droughts. Jeremiah’s message, concerning a drought brought on by the nation’s disobedience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 2, 'Judah mourns. Drought is causing suffering and death. gates. Cities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 3, 'nobles. Not even money and power can obtain relief. covered their heads. Sign of mourning (see 2 Sam. 15:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 4, 'plowmen were ashamed. All agriculture ceases because of lack of rain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 5, 'All creation is affected. deer … left. Animals will abandon their young only in the most desperate circumstances.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 7, 'our iniquities testify. The prophet prays on behalf of the people who do not recognize their sin themselves. Your name’s sake. An appeal to God’s mercy, but also suggesting that God’s reputation is at stake if His people perish (see Ezek. 20:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 8, 'Hope of Israel. God as Deliverer (see 17:3). Why. Suffering makes people look for its cause.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 9, 'called by Your name. See v. 14; 7:10–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 10, 'God’s answer, vv. 10–12. loved to wander. Judah suffers, not innocently, but for its own sins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 11, 'Jeremiah’s intercession is forbidden (see 7:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 12, 'fast … offer. Outward forms of religion will not save the nation (see v. 9), as repentance is needed (6:20). sword … famine … pestilence. The three powerful destroyers of life in the ancient world, also covenant curses (see Lev. 26:25–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 13, 'peace. False prophets contradicted Jeremiah’s message of war and exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 14, 'in My name. See 23:25. False prophets attributed their messages to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 15, 'Sword and famine shall not be. The prophets’ deception. They will therefore be killed by the very sword whose coming they denied.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 16, 'no one to bury them. A dreaded and shameful fate in the ancient world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 17, 'eyes flow with tears. God’s deep grief over His nation’s inevitable fate. virgin daughter. Term of affection (see 2:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 19, 'utterly rejected … no healing. The people still do not understand that their sins have brought the calamity on them, not rejection by God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 20, 'We acknowledge. Jeremiah confesses sins on the nation’s behalf.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 14, 21, 'Your name’s sake. See v. 9. throne of Your glory. The mercy seat on the ark of the covenant. God’s glory departed from the temple before its destruction (see Ezek. 10:18). do not break Your covenant. The covenant had been broken, but by Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 1, 'Moses and Samuel. Intercession is of no avail. Even Israel’s spiritual giants would fail, for Judah’s fate is sealed. let them go. The command God gave to Pharaoh for Israel’s liberation (see Ex. 5:1), but now for return to bondage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 2, '“Where should we go?” Unlike at the exodus, no divine guidance is provided.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 3, 'dogs … birds … beasts. Scavengers eating decaying matter left after the destruction (see 7:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 4, 'Manasseh. The king of Judah responsible for much of the idol worship in Judah (see 2 Kin. 21:1–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 5, 'who will have pity. God laments over His people’s fate. Judah’s enemies will not pity her, and she can only blame herself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 7, 'winnow. Separating grain from husks at harvest time; also an image for judgment (see 51:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 8, 'widows … more than the sand. Reversing the promise to Abraham of posterity as numerous as sand (Gen. 22:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 9, 'remnant. Often those who are spared. Here even they are delivered to the sword.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 10, 'Jeremiah’s third Confession and God’s response (see Introduction, “Author and Date”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 10, 'Woe … my mother … borne me. Jeremiah’s depths of despair. The heavy burden of his ministry makes him lament his birth and call (see 1:5). man of strife … of contention. Jeremiah was called (see 1:4–19) to preach devastating and unpopular messages, frequently bringing persecution on him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 11, 'God encourages Jeremiah. enemy. Those persecuting Jeremiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 12, 'northern iron … bronze. The Babylonians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 13, 'Your wealth … treasures … as plunder. The object of God’s prediction switches from Jeremiah to Judah. Both personal and national wealth, such as the temple vessels and furnishings, will be taken (see 52:17–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 15, 'take vengeance. In despair Jeremiah pleads for his life and the victory of God’s cause, for which he is suffering (see 11:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 16, 'Your words … I ate them. Jeremiah has honored his calling (1:4–19). joy and rejoicing. God’s word is an eternal fountain of wisdom and comfort. I did not sit in the assembly of the mockers. Jeremiah’s suffering is no fault of his own (see 15:10). sat alone. Jeremiah was not married and his family turned against him (see 11:18–21; 16:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 18, 'pain perpetual … wound incurable. See 17:14. unreliable stream. Jeremiah reminds God of His promises at his call (1:18–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 19, 'If you return … bring you back. This call for Jeremiah to return is an implied rebuke, but also a promise of protection. be as My mouth. Invitation for Jeremiah to renew his commitment to his call.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 20, 'fortified bronze wall. See 1:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 15, 21, 'I will deliver. God’s promise to save Jeremiah’s life stands firm.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 1, 'Messages of disaster as well as hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 1, 'Messages of disaster as well as hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 2, 'not take a wife. An unusual command, as family life was seen as a great blessing and being unmarried carried no virtue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 4, 'die gruesome deaths. Jeremiah’s non-marriage enacts a prophecy heralding the devastation families will experience. The loss will be worse than not having the comfort of a family in the first place. not be lamented … buried. The dead are too numerous to mourn and bury (see 7:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 5, 'house of mourning. All normal human interaction will cease (see 7:34). peace. Not just absence of war, but general well-being.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 6, 'cut … make themselves bald. Mourning rituals, though prohibited, were sometimes practiced (see Deut. 14:1). break bread … cup of consolation. Customs to support those in mourning will cease.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 8, 'voice of mirth. No joyful occasions will occur (see 7:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 10, 'Why … what is our sin. The people search, though hardly sincerely, for a reason for the threatened destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 11, 'The answer is obvious, but not pleasing. Judah has forsaken God and broken the covenant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 12, 'you have done worse. The people blame their ancestors, but their generation is worse than their fathers’. They have the benefit of Jeremiah’s messages, but they fail to heed them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 13, 'not show you favor. Their special status as the chosen nation will end.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 14, 'days are coming. A phrase often referring to a future messianic era (see 23:5), but here introduces a promise of restoration of Jews to Judah (see 29:10). no more be said. As the second exodus will surpass the first in glory. brought … Israel from … Egypt. The exodus, the event that made Israel a nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 15, 'land of the north. Babylon. Foretells a new exodus, this time from Babylon. all the lands. Other countries the people had fled to or were taken to.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 16, 'Judgment is pronounced again and Judah’s sins are condemned. fishermen … hunters. The Babylonians will lead the people away as fish are caught on hooks or prey is carried by huntsmen. repay double. Destruction will be catastrophic. defiled My land. See 3:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 19, 'strength … fortress … refuge. Metaphors for God, in whom a believer finds shelter (see Ps. 18:2–3). Gentiles shall come. To be instructed by God (see Is. 2:2–4). inherited lies. Idols are discovered to be worthless.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 20, 'make gods. See 10:10–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 16, 21, 'shall know … My name. Foresees the conversion of the nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 1, 'iron … diamond. Tools for engraving permanent records (see Job 19:24). heart. The true seat of worship. horns of your altars. Normally associated with atonement and protection (see Ex. 29:12; 1 Kin. 1:50–51). Here they carry the record of Judah’s deeply engraved sins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 3, 'The land will be devastated, treasures taken, and the people taken to captivity (see 15:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 5, 'Life outside God’s covenant is as barren as a withering shrub, while the righteous person, rooted in God’s goodness, flourishes (see Ps. 1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 9, 'heart is deceitful. The same Hebrew word from which comes the name Jacob. desperately wicked. Literally, “incurable,” that is, without any ability to heal its own evil (13:23; 30:12–13). Who can know it? A proverb. God knows everything and rewards everyone fairly, but people struggle to understand their own hearts (see 17:5–8). For more on the perversion of the human heart, see Titus 1:15. For more on the results of SIN, see Gen. 3:1–6; Ps. 51:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 11, 'Like a partridge brooding over another bird’s chicks loses the young, so unrighteous gains vanish.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 12, 'glorious high throne. The mercy seat on the ark, where God’s glory dwelled (see 3:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 13, 'hope of Israel. The covenant God. Those who forsake Him cut themselves off from life. living waters. See 2:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 14, 'Jeremiah’s fourth Confession at a time of personal trial (see Introduction, “Author and Date”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 14, 'Heal me. Incessant opposition had given Jeremiah an incurable wound (see 15:18), but he trusts in God’s power to heal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 15, 'Where is the word. Jeremiah prophesied war. While peace prevailed, he was mocked (see 20:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 16, 'not hurried away. Jeremiah is committed to his ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 17, 'terror. Even the righteous are shaken by God’s awesome power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 18, 'who persecute me. See 12:6; 18:18. Bring … day of doom. See 11:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 19, 'The Sabbath commandment is singled out for special attention. While all the commandments had been broken (see 7:9), the Sabbath had special significance as a covenant sign (see Ex. 31:12–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 19, 'gate of the children of the people. One that both ordinary people and royalty used, as the Sabbath message was for everyone.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 21, 'bear no burden. Refers particularly to bringing goods to the city for selling them on the Sabbath.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 22, 'nor do any work. The Sabbath is the day of rest. hallow. Keep separate. I commanded. See Ex. 20:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 23, 'made their neck stiff. Describes their stubbornness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 24, 'if you heed Me. Covenant blessings are conditional on obedience. Faithful Sabbath observance here represents faithful obedience to the whole law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 25, 'The blessings promised are far-reaching, looking also to God’s eternal rule (see 23:6). The people will be restored to Judah, to be ruled by their own king. Jerusalem will be the center of worship for all as God had intended.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 17, 27, 'Disobedience will bring destruction. fire … not be quenched. By human effort. God’s judgment will take its course.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 1, 'Lessons from the potter’s house.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 1, 'Lessons from the potter’s house.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 1, 'A parable arises from Jeremiah’s visit to a potter. The setting is familiar to Jeremiah’s audience, as most everyday objects at the time were made of clay.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 2, 'go down. See 19:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 3, 'wheel. Literally, two stones. The lower was spun to rotate the upper stone, on which the clay was shaped.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 4, 'vessel … marred. Ruined (see 13:7). another vessel. If the clay was not right for the potter’s purpose, he would use it for another, perhaps humbler vessel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 6, 'as the clay … so are you. God is the Potter; if His chosen do not yield to their intended purpose, God chooses another destiny for them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 7, 'Highlights the conditional nature of predictive prophecy. A striking illustration of this important principle can be found in the story of Jonah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 7, 'a nation. God has destinies planned for all nations. pluck … pull down … destroy. Destruction is planned (see 1:10). turns from its evil. Even on the brink of judgment, repentance may save a nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 9, 'build … plant. Prosperity is planned. does not obey. Likewise disobedience will destroy a nation’s prosperity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 11, 'Now therefore. A lesson is drawn for Judah: judgment is planned for it, and a final call for repentance is sounded.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 12, 'That is hopeless. Persuading Judah is useless; she prefers her own wicked ways.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 13, 'Ask … Gentiles. Judah’s disloyalty to God shocks even idolaters (see 2:10–13). virgin of Israel. A virgin keeps herself pure for her husband, but God’s betrothed chooses adultery.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 14, 'snow water … strange waters. No one exchanges pure mountain waters for an unpredictable source. Yet Israel has done just that by forsaking God for idols.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 15, 'ancient paths … pathways. Israel forsook the righteous ways of the patriarchs for the ways that made them stumble.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 16, 'desolate. War will devastate Judah. perpetual hissing. Strangers passing Judah’s ruins will whistle in mockery. shake his head. Show disbelief.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 17, 'east wind. Hot desert wind, symbolizing Babylonian armies (see 4:11). show them the back. Sign of disrespect. Here God disrespects Judah, who had shown Him disrespect.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 18, 'devise plans. Plot is made to kill Jeremiah. law shall not perish … word from the prophet. Jeremiah’s opponents believed that God spoke through their own leaders, and rejected Jeremiah’s messages. attack … with the tongue. Accuse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 19, 'Jeremiah’s fifth Confession. (See Introduction, “Author and Date”). He brings his cause to God as his enemies bring a case against him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 20, 'evil … for good … stood before You. Jeremiah interceded for Judah to spare the nation (see 7:16). Would his reward be death?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 22, 'dug a pit. Perhaps as in 38:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 18, 23, 'You know. Jeremiah leaves his case to God’s superior wisdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 1, 'An enacted prophecy of the broken flask.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 1, 'earthen flask. Perhaps a clay water bottle. take … elders. Some of the nation’s leaders were to witness Jeremiah’s action (see v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 2, 'Valley of … Hinnom. See 7:31. Potsherd Gate. So named because broken pottery was cast outside it, a suitable place for Jeremiah’s demonstration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 3, 'Hear the word. See 11:2. kings … inhabitants. Everybody, from royalty to ordinary people, is invited to hear Jeremiah’s message. catastrophe. Destruction of Judah and Jerusalem. ears will tingle. Expresses shock on hearing terrible news (1 Sam. 3:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 4, 'alien place. The reasons for judgment are enumerated: Jerusalem no longer belongs to God. other gods. See 1:16; 11:3. blood of the innocents. Righteous people (children, perhaps), victims of the Baal cult which practiced human sacrifices. nor … come into My mind. These practices were particularly reprehensible to God. Metaphorically, God had not even imagined such a thing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 5, 'See 7:31–32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 6, 'Valley of Slaughter. Renaming predicts the scale of death that will take place as Jerusalem falls.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 7, 'corpses … birds … beasts. See 7:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 8, 'desolate and a hissing. See 18:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 9, 'eat the flesh. During an extreme famine such as took place while Jerusalem was under Babylonian siege, some people resorted to cannibalism.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 10, 'break the flask. Literally, smash the flask. The deliberate breaking symbolizes God’s imminent judgment of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 11, 'say to them. Particularly to the elders witnessing the event. cannot be made whole. Just as the broken flask cannot be mended, once God’s judgment falls on Judah, life is thrown into irreversible chaos. defiled like … Tophet. See 7:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 19, 14, 'court of the Lord’s house. The temple court. Jeremiah repeats the message of warning for all to hear.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 1, 'Jeremiah imprisoned.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 1, 'chief governor. Pashhur’s title suggests one in charge of keeping order within the temple grounds (see 29:26). Jeremiah’s words (see 19:14–15) may well have caused a commotion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 2, 'struck. Was beaten. prophet. The title emphasizes Jeremiah’s high authority and therefore the magnitude of Pashhur’s offense. stocks. A restraint enforcing a painful crouching position. gate of Benjamin. Different from the one in 37:13 and 38:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 3, 'Magor-Missabib. Literally, “terror all around.” The name change indicates the fate of both Pashhur and Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 4, 'A prophecy against Pashhur: he will cause his allies’ destruction, besides his own. They will be taken to Babylon and executed there. The nation will be taken captive and its treasures looted (see 27:10–22; 28:1–4). prophesied lies. The reason for Pashhur’s judgment. By opposing Jeremiah Pashhur becomes an agent for deception. He may also have claimed to be a prophet himself (see 14:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 7, 'Jeremiah’s sixth Confession as he struggles with his calling (see Introduction, “Author and Date”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 7, 'induced. Seduced, enticed. Jeremiah complains that God overpowered him at his call, thus not giving him a genuine choice. derision daily. Jeremiah feels people’s mockery keenly: this may be when he was put in stocks by Pashhur (v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 8, '“Violence and plunder!” Jeremiah prophesied doom, but hoped for national repentance. Instead, while peace prevailed, people mocked him (see 17:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 9, 'not make mention. Jeremiah confesses thinking of not proclaiming God’s word to save himself from suffering, but his calling is stronger. fire … in my bones. Holding back God’s word will consume its bearer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 10, '“Fear on every side!” Literally, Magor-Missabib (see v. 3). A mocking nickname apparently given to Jeremiah, deriding him with the very name given to Pashhur in v. 3, because of his messages of doom. report it. Jeremiah’s words, often interpreted as disloyalty (see 21:9). All my acquaintances. Instead of support from his community Jeremiah received persecution. revenge. For the shame Jeremiah brought on the community.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 11, 'mighty, awesome One. faith, even when persecuted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 12, 'see Your vengeance. A plea to see God’s cause vindicated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 13, 'Sing to the LORD! Having poured out his heart, Jeremiah takes hold of God’s promises in faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 20, 14, 'Jeremiah’s despair over his calling. In vivid language he wishes to undo his birth and the joy others felt at it (see 15:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 1, 'A message to King Zedekiah and his house.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 1, 'King Zedekiah. The last king of Judah (see Introduction, “Author and Date”). Pashhur the son of Melchiah. Not the same Pashhur as in 20:1 (see 38:1–13). Zephaniah the son of Maaseiah. A priest sympathetic to Jeremiah (see 29:25–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 2, 'inquire of the LORD. Kings frequently asked God’s prophets for the will of God, but they often ignored the advice, preferring their own court prophets whose messages echoed the king’s policies (see 1 Kin. 22:6–7). Nebuchadnezzar … makes war. The third Babylonian attack, sparked by Zedekiah’s rebellion. wonderful works. God had previously rescued Jerusalem from the hands of enemies (see 2 Kin. 19:35–36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 4, 'turn back the weapons. All Zedekiah’s military efforts will fail if he rebels against Nebuchadnezzar, who is God’s agent for Judah’s judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 5, 'I Myself will fight. God is not on Judah’s side but Nebuchadnezzar’s (see 43:10). outstretched hand … strong arm. A common metaphor in Scripture representing God’s power on Israel’s behalf, but now on behalf of Judah’s enemy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 6, 'man and beast. Judgment is comprehensive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 7, 'Message to Zedekiah personally: his fate is sealed and his stubbornness has endangered the nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 8, 'way of life … of death. The common people, unlike the leaders, can still choose their destiny (see Deut. 30:15–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 9, 'who remains … shall die … who goes out … shall live. The Babylonian conquest is inevitable. Jeremiah advises defection, which would undermine Zedekiah’s obstinate refusal to submit to God’s will. Not surprisingly Jeremiah was accused of disloyalty (see 26:8), but his first loyalty is to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 10, 'set My face … for adversity. God’s countenance, often an image of blessing (see Num. 6:25), has become a threat.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 12, 'Execute judgment. Practice justice. The house of David had failed in its duty to protect and bring justice to the vulnerable. It will be judged for that as much as for idolatry. like fire. Total destruction; also, a purification.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 13, 'I am against you. God Himself is fighting Judah. Who … against us? Judah felt safe because of the temple and God’s covenant promises, but they had forgotten their own obligations (see 7:4–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 21, 14, 'fruit of your doings. Judah reaps what it has sown.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 2, 'king of Judah. Probably Zedekiah (see 21:1). throne of David. Reminder of the dynasty’s illustrious beginning and prophetic significance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 3, 'Execute judgment. See 21:12. stranger … fatherless … widow. The three most vulnerable groups of people in society, often exploited by the rich (see 21:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 4, 'if you indeed do … kings. Protecting the weak was a covenant obligation, the observance of which brought great blessings (see Ex. 22:21–27), including the security of the royal line.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 5, 'not hear … desolation. Dishonoring the obligations brought misery.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 6, 'Gilead … Lebanon. Beautiful places famous for their cedar forests used in building the Jerusalem temple (see 1 Kin. 5:1–10). wilderness … not inhabited. As excessive wood cutting can desolate the forests, so the Babylonians would demolish life and property in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 8, 'Why has the LORD done so. The judgment was God’s, although executed by Babylonians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 9, 'forsaken the covenant. The reason for the nation’s suffering.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 10, 'the dead … who goes away. The dead, free from hardship, are more fortunate than those taken to exile. Shallum. Also known as King Jehoahaz. He endured captivity in Egypt, while his father, the good king Josiah, died in battle and ended any suffering (see 2 Kin. 23:29–35).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 12, 'Jehoahaz died in captivity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 13, 'builds … by unrighteousness. The poor and powerless suffered injustice as the rich and powerful thirsted for luxury. wide house … vermilion. Contemporary luxuries.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 15, 'reign … in cedar. Having a palace does not make a king. your father. Josiah, who enjoyed life but also administered justice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 17, 'covetousness. Jehoahaz took advantage of the poor and weak.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 18, 'not lament. A bad king’s death brings relief to his subjects.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 19, 'burial of a donkey. Animals were normally left to decay, rather than buried. For a person, such a fate was a great humiliation. At his death, Jehoiakim was left exposed (see 36:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 20, 'Lebanon … Bashan … Abarim. Mountain regions north and south of Judah. Messages delivered from such heights would metaphorically be heard everywhere. your lovers. Judah’s political allies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 21, 'in your prosperity. Israel lost their God-given prosperity as they forgot its source. your youth. Israel’s early history (see 2:14–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 22, 'your rulers. Two of Judah’s last three kings, Coniah and Zedekiah, were taken to Babylon; the third, Jehoiakim, died a shameful death (see v. 19). lovers. Judah’s political allies were also conquered.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 23, 'Lebanon. Jerusalem, full of Lebanese cedar.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 24, 'Coniah. Also known as Jehoiachin. signet. A precious and personal ring. Precious as the house of David is to God, it will nevertheless be cast aside.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 25, 'whose face you fear. Nebuchadnezzar was universally feared in Coniah’s day.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 26, 'cast you … your mother … another country. Prophecy as to how Coniah and his family, particularly the Queen Mother, will be affected. They were taken captive in 597 B.C. (see 29:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 27, 'Coniah/Jehoiachin never returned to Judah (see 52:31–34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 22, 30, 'Write … as childless. None of Jehoiachin’s sons succeeded him to the throne.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 1, 'shepherds. Leaders, kings in particular, are chastised for their failure to care for Judah, their flock. You have scattered. By the end of Jeremiah’s ministry most Judeans lived in exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 3, 'I will gather. God will take charge of restoring His people. remnant. Only a portion of the nation survived Nebuchadnezzar’s attacks. Even fewer returned to Judah. fruitful and increase. The covenant promises are reactivated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 4, 'God will appoint new faithful leaders.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 5, 'days are coming. Unspecific future time when an ideal king will reign in Jerusalem. I will raise to David. The Davidic dynasty harbored the promise of the Messiah who would one day descend from it. Branch of righteousness. Or “righteous Branch.” Literally, “shoot” (see Is. 11:1). David’s family tree appears dead but will yet sprout new life. King shall reign … execute judgment. The promised king is none other than the Messiah, whose rule in OT times was expected on earth and whose government would succeed where Judah’s kings had failed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 6, 'In His days. A new era will dawn. Judah will be saved … Israel will dwell safely. Restored from Babylon and Assyria, the chosen nation will be reunited in Palestine (see Ezek. 37:15–22). Never literally fulfilled, this prophecy now applies to the spiritual Israel at the end of time. THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS. A messianic title, underlining the nature of God’s rule.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 7, 'See 16:14–15. A new exodus is promised, surpassing even the exodus from Egypt in glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 9, 'heart … is broken … bones shake. Jeremiah feels great pain as God’s expectations stand in stark contrast with the nation’s sin. adulterers. See 3:1–2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 11, 'prophet and priest. Spiritual leaders (see 2:8, 26). profane. Ungodly. in My house. The temple. wickedness. Idolatry flourished in the temple itself (see 7:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 12, 'slippery ways. Apostasy invariably leads to destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 13, 'prophets of Samaria. Court prophets, serving the king rather than God, aided the nation’s ruin (see 27:15). Idolatry took root in the northern kingdom first. Baal. See 2:8. My people. See 4:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 14, 'horrible thing. Idolatry, injustice. prophets of Jerusalem. False court prophets. strengthen the hands. Encourage the evildoers, instead of rebuking them. Sodom … Gomorrah. A byword for wickedness and swift judgment (see Gen. 19:6, 28–29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 15, 'wormwood … gall. See 9:15. False prophets will taste the bitterness of God’s punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 16, 'vision … own heart. Not from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 17, '“You shall have peace.” The false prophets’ optimistic message, when Jeremiah foresaw the Babylonian invasion (see 4:10; 8:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 18, 'stood in the counsel. God’s prophets had the privilege of intimate communication with Him (see Gen. 18:22–33; Amos 3:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 19, 'violent whirlwind. God’s swift punishment (see 30:23–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 20, 'you will understand. Punishment was meant to make people understand how sin leads to death, and to seek God in repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 22, 'My counsel. See v. 18. would have turned. Prophets had enormous influence either for good or for the nation’s detriment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 23, 'God sees everything; hiding is futile (see Ps. 139:7–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 25, 'lies in My name. See 2:5; 28:2. Condemnation of false prophets for speaking without God’s commission and misleading the nation (vv. 25–32). I have dreamed. Dreams were a way of God revealing Himself, but they are never in themselves evidence of divine communication.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 26, 'God’s name is used to make people forget God!');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 28, 'chaff … wheat. Fulfillment reveals the character of God’s true word and reveals false dreams as lies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 29, 'fire … hammer. God’s powerful word can create but also destroy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 30, 'I am against. The repetition of this statement emphasizes God condemnation of false prophets.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 33, 'oracle. A play on two meanings of the original word: “prophecy” and “burden.” When Jeremiah is asked what God’s oracle/burden is, his answer can also be translated: “you are the burden.” forsake. Cast off. God will let go of the burdensome nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 34, 'Those prophesying without commission will be punished.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 23, 38, 'perpetual shame. There is severe punishment for those who falsely assume God’s authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 1, 'LORD showed me. See 1:11–16. two baskets of figs set before the temple. Perhaps as an offering. Jeconiah. King Jehoiachin. The vision occurs in the aftermath of Nebuchadnezzar’s second conquest of Jerusalem in 597 B.C. princes … craftsmen … smiths. The skilled and influential people were taken to captivity first (see 29:2; Dan. 1:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 2, 'good figs … first ripe. First fruit, a delicacy. bad figs … not be eaten. Perhaps rotten.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 5, 'carried away … for their own good. The good figs are the Judeans now in Babylonian exile, there to see how their sins led to punishment and to return to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 6, 'back to this land. God promises restoration to Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 7, 'heart to know Me. True worship and righteous life has only ever arisen from a converted heart. God promises that even after much disobedience He can create a new heart for anyone repentant (see 31:31–34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 8, 'bad figs. Those left in Judah or who have escaped to Egypt. Their judgment will be either exile or death in war or by illness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 9, 'reproach … curse. See 44:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 24, 10, 'sword … pestilence. See 14:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 1, 'fourth year. 605 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 3, 'thirteenth year. 627 B.C., the year of Jeremiah’s call (see 1:2). twenty-third year. The time Jeremiah had been calling Judah to repentance, with no response.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 4, 'See 7:25–26. not listened. True prophets were usually rejected.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 5, 'Repent. Calling people to repentance was the prophet’s main task. dwell in the land. Conditional on obedience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 6, 'other gods. Israel pledged to worship God alone (see Ex. 24:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 7, 'works of your hands. Idols. own hurt. Idolatry was the nation’s downfall.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 9, 'families of the north. Babylonians and their allies (see 1:14–15). My servant. See 27:6. nations all around. God is in charge of the destinies of all nations (see vv. 15–38).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 10, 'voice of mirth. See 16:8–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 11, 'seventy years. Time till the fall of Babylon and Cyrus’s decree of restoration (see Ezra 1:1–4; 7:8–9), counted from Jerusalem’s fall in 605 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 12, 'punish … the land. Babylon, too, will face judgment (see chaps. 50–51). This was fulfilled in 539 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 13, 'in this book. A scroll probably contained vv. 15–38 and chaps. 46–51. The book of Jeremiah is made of several “books” (see 30:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 14, 'nations … be served. By the subjugated Babylonians. according to their deeds. Such as excessive cruelty. Each nation is judged according to what they understood of God’s will (see Amos 1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 15, 'wine cup of fury. Symbolizes God’s anger (see 49:12; 51:7). Judgment on foreign nations follows, vv. 15–29. Fear of judgment intoxicates like wine. all the nations. God’s rule is universal. Jeremiah was a prophet to the nations (1:10). Vv. 15–26 enumerate the nations Judah had dealings with.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 16, 'stagger and go mad. From fear. Drunkenness also symbolizes evil (see v. 27). sword. People will fall dead, like they fell down drunk.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 17, 'made … nations drink. God gives nations no choice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 18, 'See vv. 3–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 19, 'Egypt. See chap. 46.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 20, 'mixed multitude. Foreign settlers, particularly in Egypt (see v. 24). Uz. See Job 1:1. Philistines. See chap. 47. Ashkelon, Gaza, Ekron. Philistine cities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 21, 'See 27:3–5. Edom, Moab … Ammon. See 48:1–49:22. Tyre … Sidon. See 47:4. coastlands. Phoenician territories.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 23, 'Dedan, Tema. See 49:7–8. Buz. Arabian tribe.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 24, 'Arabia. Syrian and Arabian desert.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 25, 'Elam. See 49:34–39. Medes. See 51:11, 28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 26, 'kings of the north. See 1:15. all the kingdoms. Judgment becomes universal. Sheshach. Possibly a cryptic name for Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 27, 'God’s judgment is unavoidable and cannot be refused.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 29, 'city … called by My name. Jerusalem. Where judgment starts, other nations will follow.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 30, 'LORD will roar. For all to hear. Universal judgment follows, vv. 30–38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 31, 'plead His case. As in court. God’s judgment is never arbitrary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 32, 'The scale of the catastrophe anticipates the final battle with God’s enemies (see 1:15). not … buried. See 7:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 34, 'shepherds. See 3:15. The Lord has plundered their pasture and devastated their land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 25, 38, 'like the lion. Symbol of fierce majesty (see v. 30; Amos 3:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 1, 'beginning of the reign. Ca. 609–605 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 2, 'See 7:1–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 2, 'court. See 19:14. Do not diminish. This message was unacceptable to the nation, yet it could not be compromised as the nation’s salvation depended on it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 3, 'turn from his evil … I may relent. Judah’s fate was not sealed yet. God holds out His mercy as long as possible.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 4, 'walk in My law. Obedience means devotion to God and justice to one’s neighbor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 5, 'My servants the prophets. God chooses His messengers. Jeremiah’s opponents spoke in God’s name without His authorization.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 6, 'Shiloh. See 7:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 7, 'Ordinary worshipers and their religious leaders heard Jeremiah’s message.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 8, 'You will surely die. Jeremiah contradicted the leaders’ promises of peace and was accused of treason, punishable by death (see 21:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 9, 'Ironically, the temple was regarded as safe, while God’s laws were constantly violated in it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 10, 'princes. Here taking the role of judges. sat down. Court was held. New Gate. See 7:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 11, 'The priests and false prophets appear as prosecution witnesses and demand death for Jeremiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 12, 'LORD sent me. Jeremiah’s defense is that he is God’s spokesman.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 13, 'amend … will relent. Jeremiah’s core message is a call to repentance and the promise of God’s mercy; destruction follows only from not heeding that message (see 7:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 14, 'in your hand. In reality, Jeremiah had trusted his life in God’s hand (see 1:4–19). Nothing happened to him without God allowing it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 15, 'innocent blood. A warning against adding to the already large number of the people’s sins (see Ex. 23:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 16, 'princes and all the people. Jeremiah’s defense convinces all but the religious leaders. not deserve to die. Some fear of God and sense of justice is still left.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 18, 'Micah of Moresheth. The prophet Micah’s experience shows a king respecting God’s message and messenger even when he prophesied judgment (see Mic. 3:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 20, 'Urijah. Nothing more is known of this prophet’s ministry, but he concurred with Jeremiah’s message. Urijah’s flight may have put him under more suspicion. killed him. Many servants of God had to give their life for their ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 26, 24, 'Ahikam. King Josiah’s official who supported his reforms (see 2 Kin. 22:12–14). Through his powerful position in Jehoiakim’s court he protected Jeremiah, preventing people from taking mob action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 1, 'beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim. Could also mean Zedekiah, 593 B.C. (see 28:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 2, 'bonds and yokes. Such as oxen wear. This is an enacted prophecy. Jeremiah wears a yoke to predict Nebuchadnezzar’s subjugation of Judah and neighboring nations who were planning a coalition against him. send them. The neighboring kings, whom Jeremiah, as the prophet to the nations (see 1:5), informs of God’s message through their ambassadors.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 4, 'I have made. God, the Creator, is sovereign over all nations and has a plan for them. My outstretched arm. Symbol of God’s power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 6, 'I have given … lands. God is in charge of all human history. My servant. Nebuchadnezzar, God’s agent for judgment of Judah and her neighbors.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 7, 'him … his son’s son. The Babylonian rule will last three generations (see 25:11). until the time. Babylon in turn will be judged. many nations … serve them. The Babylonians were enslaved by the Medes, Persians, and their allies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 8, 'will not serve. See v. 2. Slavery to Nebuchadnezzar is sure, ordained by God. Resistance will only increase suffering. sword … famine … pestilence. Three agents of death (see 14:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 9, 'prophets … sorcerers. Each nation had people claiming to see the future. Such messages rose from people’s imaginations, and the practices were forbidden in Israel (see Lev. 19:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 11, 'nations … under the yoke. Submitting to God’s judgment will lessen suffering.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 12, 'under the yoke. See v. 11. The message of submission is now given specifically to Zedekiah, vv. 12–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 13, 'See v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 14, 'prophets. False prophets, who contradicted Jeremiah, but misleadingly spoke in God’s name (see 2:5). You shall not serve. Optimistic, false message lulling the nation into complacency. lie. See v. 10; 7:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 15, 'I have not sent. Many of the king’s advisors claimed the prophetic gift, although God had not called them. lie in My name. Thus confusing the people (see 2:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 16, 'vessels. Taken during Nebuchadnezzar’s two previous conquests (see v. 20; Dan. 1:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 17, 'serve … live. See v. 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 18, 'if. A challenge to all claiming God’s revelation to join forces with Jeremiah in intercession and keep the remaining vessels in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 19, 'A list of temple furnishings not yet looted, but to be taken to Babylon at its final capture (see v. 22; 52:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 27, 21, 'bring them up and restore. The people of Judah, and the vessels, were restored by Cyrus (see Ezra 1:7–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 1, 'same year. 593 B.C. (see 27:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 2, 'Thus speaks the LORD. Hananiah spoke falsehood in God’s name. broken the yoke. Contradicts God’s message through Jeremiah (see 27:9–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 3, 'See 27:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 6, 'Amen. See 11:5. LORD do so. Jeremiah replies with irony. It was his hope more than anyone’s that Judah would prosper, but he knew that this could be achieved only through obedience to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 7, 'Nevertheless. Jeremiah poses a test for prophets from Israel’s history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 8, 'prophets … before. God had called prophets to turn His nation back to Him. If the nation did not repent, the predicted doom followed. The responsibility was on the prophets predicting peace to show that their prophecies had ever been fulfilled. Fulfilled prophecy is one of at least four tests (see 1 Thess. 5:19–21) to determine a prophet’s genuineness. For the four tests, see Is. 8:20; Matt. 7:20; 1 John 4:1–2. Also see Jer. 18:7–10 on the conditional nature of predictive prophecy. For more on SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 10, 'Hananiah … took the yoke. Which Jeremiah was still wearing (see 27:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 11, 'break the yoke. A symbolic action on Hananiah’s part to illustrate his words. two full years. See v. 3. This did not happen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 13, 'yokes of wood … of iron. Hananiah’s words and impetuous action backfire. Nebuchadnezzar’s imminent conquest is confirmed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 15, 'not sent you. Hananiah had spoken without God’s authority, misleading the nation, and would suffer dire consequences.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 28, 16, 'you shall die. Hananiah’s fate is the opposite of his preaching: death instead of life and peace. seventh month. He dies within two months of confronting Jeremiah, thus not surviving even the two years of his prophecy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 1, 'elders. Jeremiah writes to exiled elders, showing that his mission includes those in captivity. carried away captive. In 597 B.C., Nebuchadnezzar’s second conquest of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 2, 'Jeconiah. Jehoiachin. queen mother. Nehushta (see 2 Kin. 24:8). eunuchs. Officials (see 34:19). craftsmen … smiths. See 24:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 3, 'Elasah … Gemariah. Judean diplomatic envoys, probably supporters of Jeremiah, carried the letter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 4, 'I have caused. God was responsible for Judah’s exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 5, 'Build … eat. Settle down.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 6, 'Take wives and beget. Life in exile should go on as normal. you may be increased. A covenant promise, God’s original plan, is reaffirmed (see Gen. 17:4–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 7, 'seek the peace. By accepting the exile as God’s plan and being an influence for good.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 8, 'prophets. False prophets. diviners … dreams. Forbidden magical practices continued in exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 9, 'prophesy … in My name. See v. 31; 23:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 10, 'God’s plan for Judah is revealed: exile offers a new beginning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 10, 'After seventy years. See 25:11–12. perform My … word. Restore the Jews to Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 11, 'peace. Literally, “shalom,” welfare, wellbeing, what God wishes for all people. not of evil. Judgment was for correction, not for vengeance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 12, 'pray … I will listen. Neglecting prayer deprived people of God’s blessings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 13, 'seek … find. God’s promises are available for those seeking Him. with all your heart. Sincerely, honestly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 14, 'bring you back. Restoration to Judah is promised. from all the nations. Where war and captivity had dispersed God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 15, 'See v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 16, 'Message to people left in Judah. Judgment will befall them if they ignore God’s warnings (see 21:8–10). king. Zedekiah. this city. Jerusalem. sword … pestilence. See 14:12. rotten figs. See 24:8. hissing … reproach. See 18:16. My servants. God’s prophets, as opposed to false prophets.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 21, 'Ahab … Zedekiah. False prophets among the exiles (see v. 8). prophesy a lie. They persist in denying Jerusalem’s impending fall into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 22, 'The fate of the prophets becomes an example, illustrating the consequences for opposing God’s plans. fire. A method of execution in Babylon (see Dan. 3:6, 20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 23, 'committed adultery. Thus causing a scandal by their immorality.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 24, 'Shemaiah. Another false prophet (see v. 31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 25, 'sent letters. Probably to counteract the message of Jeremiah (see v. 1), whom Shemaiah wants to have silenced. Zephaniah. See 21:1; 37:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 26, 'Jehoiada. Mentioned only here. officers. Of the chief priest, in charge of temple security, and therefore trouble-makers (see 52:24). demented … prophet. A derogatory reference to Jeremiah, casting him as a trouble-maker. However, enacted prophecies may have been seen as abnormal behavior (see 27:2–3; Ezek. 4:1–5:12). stocks. See 20:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 27, 'Shemaiah rebukes Zephaniah for not denying Jeremiah’s divine call. Anathoth. See 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 28, 'Jeremiah predicted 70 years of captivity (see v. 10); false prophets foretold its swift end. build … plant. See v. 5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 29, 30, 'Shemaiah is condemned for his false message. As punishment his family line will face extinction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 1, 'word that came. See 1:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 2, 'Write in a book. A scroll, probably written by Baruch (see 32:12; 36:2–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 3, 'days are coming. In chaps. 30–33, prophecies foretelling the literal restoration of Judah overlap with those looking to a future messianic kingdom (see 16:14; 23:5). Israel. The northern kingdom. Its restoration from Assyria was intended, but the prophecies were conditional, and this never happened.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 5, 'voice of trembling. Panic reigns in Judah, as enemy armies approach. man … in labor. In their anguish, the people appear to be troubled, gesturing like a woman with labor pains.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 7, 'that day is great. Day of judgment. Jacob’s trouble. See Gen. 32:24–26. God assures that the nation, too, will experience salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 8, 'that day. Day of deliverance. break his yoke. Foreign rule will end.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 9, 'serve the LORD. God’s people will return to obedience. David their king. Ultimately the Messiah (see 23:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 10, 'do not fear. God can turn even the most desperate circumstances into triumph. My servant Jacob. Israel’s task was to bring salvation to other nations, but now only the Messiah can fulfill that (see Gen. 12:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 11, 'full end of all nations. The enemies of God’s people will be judged in their turn (see 25:15–38; 46–51). not … complete end. Judah will not lose its identity in Babylon, and they will return to Palestine. correct … in justice. God’s fair discipline was aimed at turning Judah away from destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 12, 'affliction is incurable. See 17:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 13, 'no one to plead. Judah’s allies abandoned her. no … medicines. See 8:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 14, 'lovers have forgotten you. Neither political allies nor idols can save Judah from destruction (see 3:1; 22:20). wound of an enemy. God had used Babylon to deliver His judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 16, 'devour you. See 5:17. shall be devoured. See 2:2. your adversaries. See 30:11. all who prey. Although God allowed the capture of His people, all nations will bear responsibility for their actions, including excessive violence in war.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 17, 'restore health. Restored national existence, as well as true well-being, which is only found in connection with God. heal … your wounds. Judah’s incurable wounds for which she found no medicine (see vv. 12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 18, 'Jacob’s tents. Israel’s tribes. Their fortunes will be restored. built upon … mound. Cities will be rebuilt on their ruined sites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 19, 'thanksgiving … make merry. Joy and gratitude are meant to accompany life in service of God. multiply them. The promises to Abraham included progeny (see Gen. 12:2). Ultimately, multitudes from all nations will seek God (see Matt. 28:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 20, 'children … as before. When Israel entered the covenant (see 2:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 21, 'nobles … governor. Israel’s leaders usually failed in their duty to represent God to their people. The Messiah will accomplish this.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 22, 'My people. God’s covenant people (see Ex. 6:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 30, 23, 'See 23:19–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 1, 'same time. Continues the prophecies from chap. 30. Some were fulfilled, if partially, in Judah’s restoration (see 25:11–12). Some will only be fulfilled at the end of time. all the families. All 12 tribes (see 30:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 2, 'survived the sword … grace in the wilderness. A remnant will survive captivity and, like the people who sojourned on Sinai after the exodus, will be delivered by God (see Is. 40:3–4, 10–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 3, 'lovingkindness. Characteristic of God (see 2:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 4, 'rebuilt. Land desolated by war will be made habitable. virgin. Israel, when committed to God (see 2:2). tambourines … dances. An image of rejoicing for life’s goodness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 5, 'vines. Their fruit, a valued delicacy, will be abundantly available. mountains of Samaria. The prophecies include northern Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 6, 'cry … go up to Zion. Joyful summons will call people to worship God in Jerusalem (see 30:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 7, 'Jacob … chief of the nations. Israel; a term used particularly in the northern kingdom (see Amos 6:1), whose restoration is seen in Jer. 31:7–14. This never received a literal fulfillment (see 30:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 8, 'north country. Assyria, where Israel was exiled in 722 B.C. (see 3:18). ends of the earth. Where Israel was scattered. In the final gathering, God’s followers will come from everywhere. blind … lame. They will not be chosen because of physical strength. great throng. The saved will be many.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 9, 'weeping. With relief and gratitude (see Ezra 3:12–13). straight way. Prepared by God (see Is. 40:3–4). Father. See 3:19. Ephraim. See 7:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 10, 'Hear … O nations. The nations witnessed God punishing His people; now they are summoned to see the return of their fortunes (see 6:18–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 11, 'redeemed … ransomed. God, like a kinsman redeemer (see Ruth 2:20), rescued Israel from their captivity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 12, 'Rejoicing will accompany the redemption, now from Babylon. A feast is laid in celebration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 13, 'virgin … young men … the old. Rejoicing is as universal as sorrow was before.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 14, 'priests with abundance. Abundant sacrifices will again be offered, of which priests had their portion (see Lev. 7:31–34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 15, 'Ramah. See 40:1. Lamentation. The route to captivity went through Ramah. Rachel weeps for the children perishing in the process. Rachel. Mother of Joseph and Benjamin, mother of nation Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 16, 'Refrain … from weeping … children shall come back. God comforts Rachel, for fortunes will be reversed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 18, 'Ephraim. Joseph’s second son, and the name used for northern Israel. I was chastised. Envisages Israel’s sorrow. untrained bull. Israel, when uncontrolled, needs decisive discipline. Restore me. A plea that will never go unheard, no matter how great the sin (see Ps. 51). struck … the thigh. A gesture of shame and sorrow.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 20, 'My dear son. See 3:19. I earnestly remember. Israel’s condition has grieved God. have mercy. Forgiveness is available upon repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 21, 'signposts. For the way back from captivity. backsliding daughter. Unrepentant Israel (see 14:17). created a new thing. Restoration from Babylon is a powerful act of God, even more so the final restoration (see Is. 42:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 23, 'use this speech. People will praise God again, at the restoration of Judah. home of justice. Jerusalem. mountain of holiness. Temple mount.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 24, 'Judah will again be populated. People can live ordinary lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 26, 'awoke. From a prophetic dream.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 27, 'sow … seed of man … beast. The land will again be populated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 28, 'pluck … afflict. Judah and Israel have suffered destruction as God’s judgment (see 1:10). build … plant. Now God will rebuild the nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 29, 'fathers have eaten … set on edge. A proverb echoing the current generation’s belief that they were judged for the previous generations’ sins (see Ezek. 18:2). own iniquity. People will realize that the responsibility is their own.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 31, 'The new covenant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 31, 'days are coming. Messianic age. new covenant. A covenant to be ratified by the blood of Christ. For more on the new COVENANT, see Matt. 22:36–40; Luke 22:20; 2 Cor. 3:3; Heb. 8:8–13; 13:20. Israel … Judah. With the whole nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 32, 'covenant … with their fathers. The covenant at Sinai, in which animal sacrifices typified the death of the Redeemer. they broke. God’s people were taken to captivity because of breaking the covenant (see 7:25). husband. See 2:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 33, 'I will put. Through the Holy Spirit. My law … on their hearts. Where it was always meant to be (see Deut. 30:5–6; Mic. 6:6–8), to enable a life in harmony with God’s will. their God … My people. The covenant formula (see 7:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 34, 'No more … teach his neighbor. For every one will love and obey God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 35, 'Israel is assured of the reliability of God’s promises: as the laws of nature do not alter, so God will not forsake Israel. As no one can measure the heavens, so God cannot forget His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 31, 38, 'Jerusalem will be rebuilt, as will the New Jerusalem at the end of time. Places around the city are enumerated to highlight the completeness of rebuilding. valley. Refers to the Valley of Hinnom, which will be cleansed (see 7:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 1, 'tenth year. 587 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 2, 'besieged Jerusalem. Nebuchadnezzar’s final campaign against Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 3, 'Jeremiah had frequently prophesied the imminent Babylonian victory and even the personal fate of Zedekiah (see 21:3–7; 34:2–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 7, 'Hanamel. Jeremiah’s cousin. Buy my field. No reason for selling is given. In any case the Babylonians will occupy it. Anathoth. See 1:1. right of redemption. As next of kin Jeremiah had priority for such a purchase (see Lev. 25:25–31; Ruth 4:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 8, 'I knew. God had told Jeremiah to buy the field (see v. 25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 9, 'I bought. As a symbolic action heralding a return to normal life in Judah (see vv. 15, 44). weighed out … money. Silver pieces, as there were no coins. seventeen shekels. About 7 ounces (200 g).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 10, 'Details of official transaction of land purchase.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 12, 'gave … to Baruch. Jeremiah’s scribe (see 36:4); given for safe-keeping.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 14, 'earthen vessel. The best way of preserving documents.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 15, 'possessed again. See vv. 9, 44.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 16, 'I prayed. Vv. 15–25. Jeremiah is puzzled by God’s order to buy the field, while destruction is imminent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 17, 'Jeremiah enumerates God’s mighty acts and goodness in the past.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 18, 'lovingkindness to thousands. God desires good for all people. repay the iniquity. Demanded by God’s justice (see Ex. 20:5–6). fathers … children. See 2:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 20, 'signs and wonders. God’s actions on Israel’s behalf, from the plagues in Egypt to the conquest of Canaan, which fulfilled the promise to Abraham (see Gen. 17:8). milk and honey. Abundance (see 11:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 23, 'took possession … not obeyed. Israel’s history of breaking the covenant despite enjoying its blessings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 24, 'What You have spoken has happened. God’s messages, disbelieved by many, were coming true.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 25, 'Buy the field … yet. Having obeyed God, Jeremiah struggles to see how the prophecy can come true.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 26, 'God’s reply to Jeremiah: He has power to judge Judah (vv. 27–35) but also to restore her (vv. 36–44).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 27, 'God of all flesh. The Creator-God is in charge of all life. too hard. Nothing is impossible for the omnipotent God (see v. 17; Gen. 18:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 28, 'give this city … Chaldeans. The Babylonians are God’s agent for judgment (see 21:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 29, 'set fire. Jerusalem’s destruction is prophesied (see 52:12–13). offered incense to Baal. Idol worship. Reasons for judgment are listed. only evil … from their youth. From soon after the covenant was made on Sinai (see Ex. 32:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 31, 'city … provocation. Idolatry in God’s dwelling place.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 32, 'kings … prophets. A total failure of leadership is responsible for Judah’s fate (see 1:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 33, 'turned … the back. Showed contempt to God; see 18:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 34, 'See 7:30–31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 36, 'See v. 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 37, 'I will gather. The dispersed people will be brought back to their own land. out of all countries. See 16:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 38, 'My people … their God. Covenant formula (see 7:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 39, 'one heart. With God’s law in it. Everyone will obey God (see 31:33). fear Me. Respect God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 40, 'everlasting covenant. The new covenant (see 31:33) will never be broken. For more on the everlasting COVENANT, see Gen. 17:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 41, 'I will rejoice. God always seeks the good of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 32, 42, 'Answer to Jeremiah’s apprehension in v. 25: as surely as God is judging Judah, He will ensure His people’s return to their land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 1, 'second time. See 30:1. court of the prison. See 32:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 2, 'LORD who formed. God’s credentials for any task; He is all-powerful. LORD is His name. The name was first revealed to Moses (see Ex. 3:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 3, 'Call … I will answer. God is always accessible to anyone turning to Him. show … mighty things. Acts of creation and works in history (see 32:17–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 4, 'houses … pulled down to fortify. Bricks from buildings in Jerusalem were needed to mend siege damage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 5, 'dead bodies. People dying in defense of the city. God’s instruction had been to surrender (see 27:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 6, 'health and healing. See 30:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 7, 'return … rebuild. Reoccupation of the land after captivity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 8, 'cleanse … pardon. More important than the return of earthly fortunes is the people’s inner purification, achieved only by repentance and God’s forgiveness (see 31:33–34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 9, 'name of joy. Israel’s original purpose was to represent God among the nations (see Gen. 12:3). fear and tremble. Nations will credit Israel’s prosperity to its powerful God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 10, 'desolate, without man … beast. What Jerusalem becomes when destroyed and de-populated (see 7:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 11, 'joy … gladness … bridegroom … bride. The joys of life’s ordinary interactions and family occasions return, a reversal of 7:34. bring the sacrifice. A return to worship of the true God, a reversal of 7:16–19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 12, 'See v. 10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 13, 'cities. All Judah will be repopulated. flocks shall again pass. Restored animal husbandry is a sign of normality.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 14, 'days are coming. See 30:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 15, 'See 23:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 17, 'never lack a man … on the throne. A promise first given to David (see 2 Sam. 7:12–16). Kings of Judah descended from him as well as the final Heir to the throne, the Messiah (see Luke 1:32–33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 18, 'Levites, lack a man. The Levitical priesthood was similarly assured of permanence (see Num. 25:13). Jesus’ high priesthood would eventually fulfill this prophecy (see Heb. 7:11–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 20, 'covenant with the day … with David. God’s promises to David cannot be broken any more than God’s laws of nature (see 31:35–36). with the Levites. To ensure that God is always worshiped.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 22, 'multiply the descendants. As Abraham was, so David’s line was promised numerous descendants (see Gen. 22:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 24, 'people have spoken. Enemies or some among the Judeans deriding the fate of God’s people. two families. Israel and Judah, or specifically the house of David.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 25, 'See v. 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 33, 26, 'descendants of Jacob. Israel. David My servant. David is promised an Heir who will rule all Israel (see 23:15–16; 30:9–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 1, 'The word which came. See 1:4. A warning to Zedekiah follows vv. 1–5. kingdoms … under his dominion … fought. Nebuchadnezzar’s empire was vast (see Ezek. 26:7; Dan. 3:2–4), and nations already subjected were drafted into his army. Jerusalem is besieged for the final time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 2, 'Zedekiah. See Introduction, “Author and Date.” I will give this city. Zedekiah is warned that Jerusalem’s fate is now sealed; resistance is futile and will simply increase suffering. burn it with fire. Total destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 3, 'not escape. Zedekiah himself will be captured, but his life will be spared (see v. 4). your eyes shall see … speak … face to face. Zedekiah is predicted to meet Nebuchadnezzar, as then happened (see 32:4; 39:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 4, 'not die by the sword. See 38:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 5, 'die in peace. Not violently. ceremonies of … kings. Zedekiah died in Babylon, and presumably received proper funeral customs (see 52:11). “Alas, lord!” Words of mourning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 6, 'Lachish and Azekah. Fortified cities west of Jerusalem. When Nebuchadnezzar invaded Judah, these were the only cities that did not succumb immediately.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 8, 'covenant. Binding agreement between two parties, not only with God. proclaim liberty. Motive for this act is not made explicit. V. 14 suggests that the nation was in violation of the law on slavery. With war threatening, this may have been an attempt to show God a reformed spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 9, 'slave. Bonded labor of fellow Israelites was permitted at the time, but strictly governed by Mosaic law (see Deut. 15:12–18). Hebrew. Original name for the patriarchs and their descendants (see Gen. 14:13). Jewish brother. Laws of bondage were different for native Israelites and foreigners.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 10, 'all the princes … people. The king’s covenant was with the whole nation. obeyed … let them go. Initially the agreement was honored.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 11, 'afterward. When Babylonian armies withdrew their siege of Jerusalem temporarily (see v. 21). changed their minds. The nation’s change of heart was temporary, and they became covenant-breakers once again.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 13, 'I made a covenant. At Sinai (see 11:2). house of bondage. Egypt (see Ex. 13:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 14, 'See Deut. 15:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 15, 'did what was right. Here by freeing the slaves. Similarly, Israel initially committed itself to the Sinai covenant (see Ex. 24:3). house … called by My name. See 7:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 16, 'turned around and profaned My name. By again enslaving their brothers the nation broke their covenant with the king, as they had done repeatedly with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 17, 'I proclaim liberty … to the sword. Punishment fits the crime. Causing oppression to their own people delivers the nation to the oppression by the enemy. sword … famine. See 14:12. among all the kingdoms. See 9:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 18, 'cut … passed. Ritual at covenant-making, see Gen. 15:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 19, 'eunuchs. A term for an official; does not necessarily imply castration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 20, 'meat for the birds. See 7:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 34, 21, 'Surrender to the Babylonians becomes inevitable as the people cannot reform themselves. army … gone back. Relief from the Babylonians was temporary. return … burn it with fire. Jerusalem fell in 586 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 1, 'The Rechabites keep their faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 1, 'in the days of Jehoiakim. Ca. 605 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 2, 'house. Household. Rechabites. A family who abstained from wine and planting vineyards and adhered to a nomadic lifestyle in obedience to their forefather’s commands (see vv. 7–8). house of the LORD … chambers. Living quarters attached to the temple. give them wine. The Rechabites’ obedience becomes an object lesson and a rebuke to the covenant-breaking Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 3, 'Jaazaniah. The current head of household.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 4, 'sons. Perhaps disciples (see 2 Kin. 2:3). man of God. Prophet (see 1 Kin. 12:22). keeper of the door. An overseer of guardians of the temple entrance (see Jer. 52:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 5, 'bowls. Large vessels, set to tempt the Rechabites. “Drink wine.” The Rechabites are urged to break their faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 6, 'We will drink no wine. The Rechabites remain resolute in their obedience to their forefather Jonadab.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 7, 'The Rechabites have kept their faith since the time of Jonadab, who lived over 200 years earlier (see 2 Kin. 10:15–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 7, 'dwell in tents. Perhaps on the model of the patriarchs.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 11, 'The Babylonian invasion drove the Rechabites to seek refuge within the city walls where they presumably pitched their tents.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 13, 'receive instruction. The Rechabites’ faith is an object lesson used to invite the nation to repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 14, 'words of Jonadab … are performed. Jonadab, a mere man, is obeyed by his descendants. I have spoken … you did not obey. God’s divine instruction goes unheeded.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 15, 'See 25:4–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 16, 'The obedience of the Rechabites condemns the faithless Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 17, 'Refusing God’s repeated pleas will inevitably result in judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 35, 18, 'shall not lack … before Me. For their faithfulness, the Rechabites are blessed with service in the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 1, 'Jeremiah’s scroll.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 1, 'fourth year. 605 B.C. Jehoiakim. See Introduction, “Author and Date.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 2, 'scroll. Papyrus or leather (see 30:2). write … words that I have spoken. Jeremiah’s messages must be preserved for future generations. Israel … Judah … nations. See 1:10. days of Josiah. See 11:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 3, 'may be … turn from his evil way. The sole purpose of Jeremiah’s preaching was to turn people back to God. I may forgive. Judgment is still avoidable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 4, 'Baruch. See 32:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 5, 'I am confined. He was either barred from the temple because of his former messages, or imprisoned (see 7:2–15; 20:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 6, 'Baruch becomes Jeremiah’s spokesman. day of fasting. Declared because of Nebuchadnezzar’s attack (see Dan. 1:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 7, 'See v. 3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 9, 'fifth year. A year later than v. 1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 10, 'Baruch read. Second reading of the scroll. chamber. Living quarters overlooking the temple court where people were gathered. Gemariah. Supporter of Jeremiah, as his father had been of Josiah’s reform (see 2 Kin. 22:10). New Gate. See Jer. 26:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 11, 'Michaiah … went. Gemariah’s son is trusted with the urgent message. princes were sitting. A meeting was probably called, along with a fast, because of the national crisis.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 14, 'Baruch is invited to read the scroll to the princes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 16, 'looked in fear. Exact contents of the scroll are unknown, but it predicted Jerusalem’s downfall (see v. 29). tell the king. Because of the vital national importance of the message.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 17, 'at his instruction. Baruch is asked to establish the authenticity of the words as Jeremiah’s.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 18, 'He proclaimed … I wrote. A rare insight into the recording of a divine message. ink. Made of gum arabic or soot.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 19, 'Go and hide. The princes anticipated the king’s hostile reaction and were concerned with Jeremiah’s safety.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 20, 'stored the scroll. Perhaps for safe-keeping, but in vain, see v. 23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 21, 'The scroll is read to the king.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 22, 'winter house. Perhaps a separate wing of the palace. hearth. A brazier or a depression in the floor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 23, 'king cut … cast it into the fire. The king shows his dismay, but will learn that a prophecy cannot be stopped by burning the scroll.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 24, 'they were not afraid. Showed no repentance. Some 20 years before, Jehoiakim’s father Josiah tore his robe in repentance as God’s word was read to him (see 2 Kin. 22:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 25, 'implored … not to burn. The princes manifest a more reverent spirit to God’s word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 26, 'king’s son. Here, an official. seize Baruch … Jeremiah. The princes had anticipated the king’s reaction correctly, see v. 19. LORD hid. Through His providence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 28, 'another scroll. The prophecies are rewritten.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 29, 'Why have you written. Jehoiakim refused to understand the divine origin of Jeremiah’s message.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 30, 'no one to sit. Jehoiakim’s punishment is the extinction of his royal line (see 2 Kin. 24:6–20). body … cast out. Jehoiakim personally would experience a shameful death (see Jer. 22:18–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 31, 'The entire nation suffers because of the king’s action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 36, 32, 'added … similar words. The record of God’s word grows, instead of diminishing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 1, 'Jeremiah is imprisoned.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 1, 'Zedekiah … reigned instead of Coniah. Nebuchadnezzar made Zedekiah his puppet king when deposing Coniah (Jehoiachin) (2 Kin. 24:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 2, 'neither … gave heed. Zedekiah, although sympathetic towards Jeremiah, was a weak king, often led by his officials (see v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 3, 'Jehucal. Later turned against Jeremiah (see 38:1, 4). “Pray … for us.” To save Jerusalem from the Babylonians besieging the city (see 37:5). God had intervened before on Judah’s behalf (see 2 Kin. 19:32–37).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 4, 'not yet … in prison. Once arrested, Jeremiah remained imprisoned until the fall of Jerusalem (see 38:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 5, 'Pharaoh’s army. Hophra came to Zedekiah’s aid ca. 588 B.C. (see v. 7). Chaldeans … departed. To deal with the Egyptian threat.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 7, 'Pharaoh’s army … will return. That is, defeated, as then happened (see Ezek. 30:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 8, 'Chaldeans shall come back. For the final assault against Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 9, 'Do not deceive. The temporary lifting of the siege should not lull people into false security.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 10, 'only wounded men. As God’s agents, the Babylonians could conquer Jerusalem even severely depleted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 11, 'Chaldeans left. Temporarily, see v. 5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 12, 'claim his property. Jeremiah takes advantage of the more peaceful conditions to attend to financial matters.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 13, 'Gate of Benjamin. Leading north towards the Benjamite territory. You are defecting. Jeremiah was under suspicion as he had urged others to defect (see 21:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 14, 'False! Literally, a lie! Jeremiah continuously dealt with lies given as prophecies (see 7:4), but now he is personally lied about.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 15, 'princes were angry. Many officials were particularly antagonistic towards Jeremiah, as he undermined their power. struck him. Jeremiah was beaten, illegally, as no offense had been proven. house of Jonathan. An overflow prison or a particularly secure one.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 16, 'dungeon. Probably an underground empty water cistern (see 38:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 17, 'king asked him secretly. Fearing his own officials. any word. As reply to his request (see v. 3). You shall be delivered. God’s message had not changed (see 34:3). Jeremiah speaks the truth despite his own vulnerable position.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 18, 'What offense. Jeremiah’s treatment had been against the strict guidelines of Mosaic law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 19, 'your prophets. False prophets predicting peace were now being proved wrong (see 4:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 20, 'my petition. Like the king, Jeremiah had a request. lest I die. Jeremiah must have guessed why he was held in such a sinister place.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 37, 21, 'court of the prison. See 32:2. The king grants Jeremiah’s request as Jeremiah had granted his.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 1, 'Jeremiah in the cistern.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 1, 'Jucal. Jehucal; see 37:3. Pashhur. See 20:1. Officials leading the persecution of Jeremiah. Jeremiah had spoken. Jeremiah was still preaching despite his confinement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 2, 'who goes over … shall live. Jeremiah urged cooperation with the Babylonians as they were God’s agent for Jerusalem’s fall (see 21:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 4, 'put to death. For perceived treason. weakens the hands. This was true, but then resistance was futile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 5, 'king can do nothing. Betrays Zedekiah’s fear of his officials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 6, 'dungeon. See 37:16. king’s son. See 36:26. mire. Mud.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 7, 'Ebed-Melech. Literally, “king’s servant.” Ethiopian. Israel had had contacts with Ethiopia since early times. eunuchs. A high official (see 34:19). in the king’s house. Indicates Ebed-Melech’s trusted position, which explains his easy access to the king. king was sitting at the Gate. The usual place to administer justice. of Benjamin. See 37:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 9, 'men have done evil. It takes a foreigner to prick the king’s conscience. no more bread. Perhaps the plan was to starve Jeremiah to death (see 37:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 10, 'thirty men. To counter any opposition by the officials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 11, 'clothes … rags … ropes. The cistern is awkwardly shaped with a narrow neck, so the rescue takes some ingenuity. court of the prison. Where Jeremiah had been previously imprisoned (see 37:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 14, 'third entrance. Perhaps the king’s private entrance. something … nothing. Literally, word; that is, word from the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 15, 'put me to death … not listen to me. Jeremiah already knew that for all his apparent willingness, Zedekiah was too weak to change the nation’s policy of resistance and perhaps even to protect Jeremiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 16, 'swore secretly. Unknown to his officials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 17, 'See vv. 2–3; 34:2–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 19, 'Jews … defected. Who might blame Zedekiah for not surrendering earlier.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 20, 'obey … it shall be well. Obedience would have guaranteed God’s protection all along.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 21, 'Disobedience, in turn, will have serious consequences.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 22, 'women … shall be surrendered. The conquering king would impose his power by taking over Zedekiah’s many wives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 23, 'you shall cause. The king’s responsibility is huge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 24, 'Let no one know. For fear of the king’s officials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 25, 'if the princes hear. Zedekiah asks Jeremiah to tell a partial truth, if his consultation with Jeremiah becomes known.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 38, 28, 'Jerusalem was taken. The third and final capture, 586 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 1, 'Fall of Jerusalem (see 52:1–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 1, 'ninth year. 588 B.C., final siege against Jerusalem began.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 2, 'eleventh year … fourth month … ninth day. July 18, 586 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 3, 'princes … sat. Babylonian captors impose their authority as predicted (see 1:15), taking Zedekiah’s place (see 38:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 4, 'they fled. Although Jeremiah had predicted that there was no escape (see 34:3). gate between … walls. Presumably the king’s secret entrance, as it was left unguarded. plain. Jordan Valley, east of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 5, 'Jericho. 18 miles (30 km) northeast of Jerusalem. Riblah. Nebuchadnezzar’s headquarters on the Orontes River. Hamath. Area in Syria called by its main city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 6, 'killed the sons. As punishment for rebellion (see 2 Kin. 24:17–20) and to prevent them from claiming the throne later.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 7, 'put out Zedekiah’s eyes. Cruel fate for Zedekiah, whose last images were the execution of his sons. fetters. Chains. carry him off. As predicted (see 32:4–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 8, 'Final destruction of Jerusalem; see 52:12–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 8, 'burned … with fire. Including the temple (see 52:13), as predicted (see 34:22). broke down the walls. Well-attested by archaeology.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 9, 'Nebuzaradan. See vv. 10–11, 13. remnant. People left from the two previous deportations and spared from war, famine, and disease.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 10, 'left … poor people. Mainly peasants, to retain the land as governable and to prevent wild beasts from overrunning it (see 2 Kin. 17:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 11, 'charge concerning Jeremiah … do him no harm. Jeremiah’s advice to Zedekiah not to resist must have been known to Nebuchadnezzar, perhaps through defectors.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 14, 'court of the prison. Where Jeremiah still was (see 38:28). Gedaliah. Appointed as governor of Judah (see 40:5), a protector of Jeremiah. son of Ahikam. See 26:24. home. Governor’s residence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 16, 'Go and speak. Send a message, as Jeremiah was still confined. Ebed-Melech. See 38:7. My words. God’s many prophecies concerning Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 17, 'But … deliver you. As Ebed-Melech had stood for justice and protected Jeremiah. men … afraid. Presumably those persecuting Jeremiah (see 38:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 39, 18, 'life … as a prize. As promised to those obeying Jeremiah’s advice (see 21:9). trust. Opposite of what Zedekiah had shown.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 1, 'Nebuzaradan. See 39:9. Ramah. A town 5 miles (8 km) north of Jerusalem, it was a staging post for captives bound for Babylon. How vv. 1–6 relate to 39:14 is not clear. Jeremiah may have been taken to Nebuzaradan in Ramah to be officially freed, or perhaps Jeremiah had been rearrested by mistake and therefore freed twice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 2, 'LORD your God has pronounced … brought it. A Babylonian soldier acknowledges the genuineness of Jeremiah’s prophecies and the Babylonian victory as the act of Jeremiah’s God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 4, 'Jeremiah receives better treatment from Nebuzaradan than he had from his own king’s officials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 5, 'Jeremiah’s commitment is to his own land. Gedaliah. See 39:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 7, 'Gedaliah’s assassination.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 7, 'captains … their men. Judean soldiers who had managed to hide from the Babylonian army. poorest of the land. See 39:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 8, 'Mizpah. A town 8 miles (12 km) north of Jerusalem; the seat of Gedaliah’s government as Jerusalem was in ruins. Ishmael. A military leader of noble birth (see 2 Kin. 25:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 9, 'took an oath. Gedaliah solemnly assures the men that they can dwell in safety as long as they do not rebel against the Babylonians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 10, 'cities that you have taken. The guerrilla army had occupied these since the Babylonian departure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 11, 'Moab … Ammonites … Edom. Many Jews had fled to neighboring countries during the fighting and now returned to Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 13, 'Johanan … the captains. The soldiers trusted Gedaliah and became his supporters, except for Ishmael.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 14, 'Ammonites. Nation east of Judah, descendants of Lot (see Gen. 19:38), and Judah’s former allies against the Babylonians (see 27:3). murder you. Baalis’s motive is not made clear, but Judah’s end was an opportunity for him to extend his own borders.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 40, 16, 'you speak falsely. Gedaliah refused to believe ill of a man of noble birth. Ishmael. See v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 1, 'seventh month. October of presumably the same year, 586 B.C. Gedaliah survived as the governor only for three months (see 40:5; 2 Kin. 25:25). Ishmael. See 40:8. ate bread together. The obligations of hospitality should have ensured the safety of both parties.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 2, 'struck Gedaliah … with the sword. As a cowardly act of treachery. made governor. See 40:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 3, 'struck down … men of war. Ishmael’s gaining the upper hand may be explained by the others being unarmed in good faith while sharing a meal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 4, 'Ishmael’s acts of treachery.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 5, 'Shechem … Shiloh … Samaria. Traditional worship sites in northern Israel. beards shaved … clothes torn … cut themselves. Signs of mourning, here because of the destruction of the temple. Cutting oneself and shaving were prohibited in Mosaic law, but sometimes practiced (see Lev. 19:27–28). offerings and incense. The men were on pilgrimage to Jerusalem, despite its destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 6, 'Ishmael … went … weeping. Treacherously masquerading himself as a fellow mourner to entice the pilgrims to their deaths. cast them into … a pit. To shame his victims by denying them a proper burial and to cover up his crime.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 8, 'we have treasures. Ten men appeal to Ishmael’s greed and need of provisions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 9, 'Asa. See 1 Kin. 15:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 10, 'king’s daughters. Women from Zedekiah’s court. carried … captive … to the Ammonites. Ishmael had been persuaded to betray his own people for a foreign king, presumably for personal gain (see 40:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 11, 'Johanan. See 40:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 12, 'all the men. Those loyal to Gedaliah. pool. Perhaps the one in 2 Sam. 2:13. Gibeon. A town 6 miles (9 km) north of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 13, 'people … were glad. The captives, anticipating rescue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 15, 'Ishmael … escaped. Nothing more is known of him. eight men. Perhaps two had been killed (see v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 17, 'Chimham. Exact location is uncertain. Egypt. Where some Judeans had already escaped and established communities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 41, 18, 'they were afraid. Thinking that they might be tarnished by Gedaliah’s murder.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 1, 'Flight to Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 1, 'captains. See 40:8; 41:11. Johanan. See 41:11. Jezaniah. Probably the same as in 40:8. least to the greatest. Everyone.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 2, 'said to Jeremiah. Jeremiah had either stayed in Mizpah (see 40:6) or was among those rescued (see 41:13–14). pray … to the LORD. Whether the intended flight to Egypt is God’s will (see v. 17; 41:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 4, 'keep nothing back. Jeremiah will tell the truth, even if it displeases the inquirers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 5, 'LORD … witness. The people make a show of extreme sincerity, but this turns out to be hypocritical (see v. 20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 7, 'after ten days. An answer to prayer is not always immediate, even when God’s true prophet is interceding.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 8, 'least … to the greatest. See v. 1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 10, 'If you … remain. The planned flight to Egypt is not God’s will; instead He is ready to restore the people’s fortunes in their own land, if they are obedient. build … not pluck. See 1:10. I relent. If the people change, God’s plan for them will change too.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 11, 'Do not be afraid … I am with you. Assurance that people committed to God need not fear any other power, not even Nebuchadnezzar’s power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 12, 'return to your own land. The people could dwell safely in Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 13, 'We will not dwell … we will go. The people consulted God and God answered; disobedience will now have its consequences.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 16, 'sword … shall overtake you. While God promised the people safety in Judah, the attacks they thought they were fleeing from would materialize in Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 17, 'sword … famine … pestilence. See 14:12. none … shall remain. The people who had escaped two deportations (see 39:9–10; 41:10–16) and various threats on their lives (see 41:3), now choose death instead of life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 18, 'enter Egypt. Egypt will become a place of judgment, not of help, for those going there against God’s will (see 44:11–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 19, 'Do not go. The answer to prayer (see vv. 2–3) comes with extraordinary clarity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 20, 'hypocrites. They had only pretended sincerity when they sought God’s will, see vv. 5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 21, 'have not obeyed. Implies that the people’s minds were made up even before they approached Jeremiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 42, 22, 'you shall die … in the place. The judgment for the mock sincerity takes place, ironically, where people sought life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 1, 'Jeremiah is taken to Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 2, 'You speak falsely! When the people received an answer to their prayer (see 42:2–3) they did not want, they accused the messenger of falsehood. This is what Jeremiah’s opponents, not Jeremiah, had proclaimed all along (see 7:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 3, 'Baruch … set you against us. The scribe (see 32:12) is credited with influence beyond his station, as it is perhaps safer to accuse him than Jeremiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 4, 'would not obey. Israel’s history keeps repeating itself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 5, 'remnant … who had returned … from all nations. People had escaped the Babylonians to neighboring countries, but they returned once the armies had departed (see 40:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 6, 'king’s daughters. See 41:10. Jeremiah. Taken perhaps with the mistaken belief that the prophet’s presence would ward off judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 7, 'Tahpanhes. A city in the Nile Delta.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 8, 'Symbolic prophecy of foundation stones.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 8, 'word of the LORD came. See 1:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 9, 'Take large stones … hide them. As foundations for Nebuchadnezzar’s throne; see v. 10. at the entrance. Nebuchadnezzar will set his seat of power opposite Pharaoh’s. Pharaoh’s house. Not the main palace, but one for Pharaoh’s visits and his officials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 10, 'I will send and bring. God will judge Egypt, and as with Judah, the Babylonians will be His agent. Nebuchadnezzar … My servant. See 25:9. throne … royal pavilion. Symbols of power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 11, 'he shall strike … Egypt. As then happened; see 46:13. deliver to death those appointed for death … captivity … sword. Nebuchadnezzar will deliver the God-appointed judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 12, 'kindle a fire. Utterly destroy. houses of the gods. Foreign conquerors routinely destroyed local holy objects and places to emphasize the powerlessness of the local gods to protect their people. array himself. Conquer. as a shepherd puts on his garment. With ease. go out … in peace. Unchallenged.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 43, 13, 'sacred pillars. Standing stones central to sun worship. Beth Shemesh. Literally, house of the sun (god).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 1, 'Jews … in … Egypt. A message of judgment to those who had fled (see 43:5–7) or been deported there (2 Kin. 23:34). Migdol … Noph. Cities in northern Egypt (see 2:16). Pathros. Southern Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 2, 'calamity. Recent destruction of Judah and Jerusalem (see 39:1–2; 52:4–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 3, 'Details reasons for Judah’s punishment. wickedness. See 1:16; 7:9–10. My servants. See 7:25. My anger. See 7:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 7, 'Why … commit … evil. Jerusalem’s destruction had not changed the minds of Jews in Egypt. man … infant. Everyone.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 8, 'works … burning incense. See 1:16. curse and a reproach. The people’s comprehensive demise would become a proverb (see 24:9; 42:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 9, 'wickedness of the kings … their wives. Royal marriages to foreigners had led to idolatry since Solomon’s time (see 1 Kin. 11:4–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 10, 'not been humbled. Previous generations had also refused to acknowledge their sins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 11, 'God’s judgment on the Jews in Egypt (see 42:17–18). set My face. See 21:10; Ezek. 6:2. those who escape. A remnant will survive and return to Judah (see v. 28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 15, 'wives … women. Women, in particular, worshiped fertility idols (see v. 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 16, 'not listen. Open refusal to obey God’s word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 17, 'The Jews are committed to idolatry. queen of heaven. A fertility goddess, particularly condemned by Jeremiah (see 7:18). plenty of food. Idols are credited for God-given prosperity. since we stopped. The people fail to understand that idolatry is the cause of their misfortune.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 19, 'cakes. See 7:18. our husbands’ permission. The women quickly point out that vows were taken with their husbands’ consent (see v. 25; Num. 30:7–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 20, 'The true cause of Judah’s destruction is idolatry. curse. See v. 8. law … testimonies. Covenant terms (see 11:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 25, 'surely keep your vows. Spoken ironically (see 7:21). perform. Even as God will perform His word (see 1:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 26, 'My great name. See 22:5. no more be named. Because people call to idols and the community perishes in Nebuchadnezzar’s invasion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 27, 'sword and by famine. As predicted when they escaped to Egypt (see 42:16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 28, 'number … shall return. A remnant will be spared and return to Judah (see v. 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 29, 'sign. Defeat of Hophra. punish you in this place. Nebuchadnezzar’s invasion of Egypt is foretold.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 44, 30, 'Hophra. The pharaoh who helped Zedekiah (see 37:5) will suffer a fate similar to his. gave Zedekiah. See 39:5–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 45, 1, 'Baruch. Jeremiah’s faithful scribe and spokesman (see 36:4, 32). fourth year. 605 B.C. Follows chap. 36, where Jeremiah was imprisoned (see 36:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 45, 2, 'to you. A message of encouragement to Baruch.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 45, 3, 'Woe. Announces a lament (see 15:10). grief … sorrow … no rest. Baruch’s anguish as he shared the burden of Jeremiah’s difficult ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 45, 4, 'built … break down … planted … pluck up. God, too, had experienced grief when His plan for Judah had been rejected (see 1:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 45, 5, 'seek great things for yourself. Baruch, an educated man whose brother was a high-ranking official to Zedekiah (see 32:12; 51:59), may have had similar ambitions. life … as a prize. See 31:18. Baruch is assured of God’s protection, wherever he goes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 1, 'A heading for Jeremiah’s prophecies against the nations (see also Is. 13–23; Ezek. 25–32; Amos 1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 2, 'Pharaoh Necho. Ruled Egypt 610–595 B.C. Carchemish. A town on the Euphrates in Syria. It was the scene of a decisive battle, in 605 B.C. The Egyptian defeat opened a way for the Babylonians to invade Judah. fourth year. 605 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 3, 'Battle commands are shouted, but mockingly. horses. Egypt was famous for them (see 1 Kin. 10:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 5, 'Why … turned back? Surprise! The Egyptian might is beaten!');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 7, 'flood … waters. Egyptian army resurges, like Nile’s annual flooding overwhelms its surroundings. Ethiopians … Lydians. Mercenaries fighting for the Egyptian army.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 10, 'day of vengeance. God is Egypt’s real enemy. avenge Himself. The reason may be the cruelty Egypt had inflicted on Judah (see 2 Kin. 23:29–30, 33–35). sword shall devour. As if it had a mouth. sacrifice. Defeat depicted as a feast where God offers Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 11, 'Gilead … balm. See 8:22. virgin … daughter. Inhabitants. In vain. Egypt is beyond cure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 12, 'your shame. As a superpower is defeated. stumbled … fallen. Pictures two soldiers both falling in combat with one another; the carnage is great.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 13, 'strike … Egypt. Happened in 568 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 14, 'Declare … Proclaim. Egypt needs to prepare for invasion, instead of attack (see v. 3). Migdol … Noph … Tahpanhes. Egyptian cities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 15, 'valiant men. Warriors. LORD drove them away. God Himself fights the Egyptians through the Babylonians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 16, 'to our own people. No victory in sight, the mercenaries desert (see v. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 17, 'Pharaoh … a noise. The boastful pharaoh is uncovered as a loudmouth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 18, 'the King. God, the real King. Tabor … Carmel. Mountains in northern Israel. The creation witnesses to the truth of God’s words. he shall come. Nebuchadnezzar, to invade Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 19, 'Prepare. For the consequences of defeat. go into captivity. The fate that Egypt had inflicted on others (see 23:10–13) awaits them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 20, 'Animal imagery illustrates Egypt’s plight.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 20, 'heifer. Perhaps a sarcastic reference to Egyptian bull-worship. destruction. May mean “gadfly,” a cattle pest: Egyptian splendor succumbs to a fly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 21, 'fat bulls. Well-fed mercenaries escape from adversity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 22, 'noise … like a serpent. All Egypt can do is hiss. army … with axes. Like a felled forest, the Babylonian devastation leaves no hiding place. grasshoppers. Like a locust swarm, the Babylonians are innumerable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 25, 'bring punishment. Trusting in powerless gods is futile and inevitably ruins a nation. Amon. Egyptian god. No. Thebes. A city in southern Egypt. Nebuchadnezzar. The Babylonians are God’s agent of judgment. Afterward … inhabited. The nation will not be totally destroyed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 46, 27, 'See 30:10–11. God, who rules the nations, remembers Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 47, 1, 'Philistines. Israel’s archenemies from earliest times (1 Sam. 4). Their territory was on the Mediterranean coast. Pharaoh. Possibly Necho (see 46:2). Gaza. A Philistine city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 47, 2, 'waters rise. See 46:7–8. out of the north … overflow. Babylonian invasion, possibly in 604 B.C. cry … wail. In pain and panic.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 47, 3, 'horses … chariots. War imagery (see 46:4). fathers … children. Chaos and fear are such that parents even neglect their children.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 47, 4, 'Tyre and Sidon. Phoenician cities, northern neighbors of the Philistines and apparently in alliance with them. Caphtor. Crete, the apparent homeland of the Philistines (see Gen. 10:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 47, 5, 'Baldness … cut yourself. Signs of mourning (see 16:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 47, 6, 'sword. See 46:10. Put … scabbard. It is useless to resist God’s plan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 47, 7, 'Ashkelon. A coastal Philistine city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 1, 'Moab. Descendants of Lot (see Gen. 19:36–37) whose territory is east of the Dead Sea. Moab’s destruction is predicted, as well as its stronghold shamed. This was fulfilled probably around 582 B.C. (see 52:30). Nebo … Kirjathaim. Reubenite cities the Moabites occupied, north of the Arnon River.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 2, 'Heshbon. An Amorite, then Israelite city, now under Moabite control; northeast of the Dead Sea (see Num. 21:25–26; 32:37). devised evil. A plot for Moab’s destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 3, 'voice of crying. The inhabitants are distressed as destruction spreads.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 6, 'juniper. Symbol of desolation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 7, 'trusted … treasures. Self-sufficiency and complacency are reasons for judgment. Chemosh … into captivity. Moabite chief god (see 1 Kin. 11:7), powerless when facing the true God. priests … princes. The retinue carrying Chemosh.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 8, 'plunderer. Probably Nebuchadnezzar. valley … plain. Moab’s main geographical features; stands for the whole land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 10, 'Cursed. Agents of destruction must work with vigor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 11, 'settled on his dregs. Moab’s complacency is likened to maturing wine from its famous vineyards.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 12, 'days are coming. Of judgment. tip him over. Moab’s destruction is likened to destroying clay wine jars.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 13, 'ashamed of Chemosh … of Bethel. Chemosh cannot help Moab anymore than the golden calves helped Israel (see 1 Kin. 12:26–29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 15, 'the King. God, Moab’s true enemy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 17, 'Bemoan. Nations around are invited to mourn the fallen nation (see 27:3). staff … rod. Symbols of authority; Moab’s greatness has passed (see Gen. 49:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 18, 'daughter. Inhabitants. Dibon. Town north of the Arnon River (see Num. 32:34). Come down … sit. The ruler is demoted. strongholds. Cities. Destruction moves from city to city, vv. 18–28. Some locations are unknown.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 19, 'Aroer. Town north of the Arnon River.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 20, 'Arnon. Originally the northern border river of Moab.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 21, 'Holon and Jahzah and Mephaath. Occupied Reubenite towns.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 22, 'Dibon. See v. 18. Nebo. See v. 1. Beth Diblathaim. Town north of the Arnon River.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 23, 'Kirjathaim. See v. 1. Beth Gamul and Beth Meon. Towns north of the Arnon River.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 24, 'Kerioth. Perhaps the same as Kir (see Amos 2:2). Bozrah. Town north of the Arnon River.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 25, 'horn … arm. Symbols of strength.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 26, 'Make him drunk. From God’s cup of wrath, for setting its own god against God (see 25:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 27, 'Israel a derision. Moab had rejoiced over Israel’s misfortune. Was he found among thieves? Should have a negative answer; Israel had no reason for shame.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 28, 'dwell in the rock. Desert, the rational place of escape.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 29, 'pride. The cause of Moab’s downfall (see v. 11; Is. 16:6; 25:10–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 30, 'I know. God is in charge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 31, 'I will wail. Lament over Moab, seen as already destroyed, vv. 29–39. Kir Heres. Where king Mesha sacrificed his son, in southern Moab (see 2 Kin. 3:25–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 32, 'vine. See note on v. 11. Sibmah. Town near Heshbon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 33, 'Joy … taken. Harvest, the traditional time of enjoyment, turns to grief.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 34, 'Places around Moab: destruction will touch every part of the nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 35, 'sacrifices. Idol worship, the cause of judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 37, 'See 41:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 38, 'housetops … streets. Lamentation is heard everywhere. broken … vessel. Moab lies in pieces. shame … derision. See v. 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 40, 'eagle. The most ferocious bird of prey, understood here to be Nebuchadnezzar. See Ezek. 17:3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 41, 'Kerioth. See v. 24. mighty men’s … birth pangs. Image of helplessness and pain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 42, 'destroyed as a people. The nation will cease to exist. exalted himself. Reason for judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 43, 'Escape is futile; judgment is unavoidable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 45, 'Heshbon. See v. 2. fire. Destruction. Sihon. An early king, and Heshbon was his capital (see Num. 21:26–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 46, 'people of Chemosh perish. The idol could not protect its worshipers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 48, 47, 'bring back … latter days. Hope for future (see 46:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 1, 'Ammonites. Descendants of Lot (see Gen. 19:38) with territory northeast of Moab. They were hostile to Israel from early times (see Judg. 3:13) and commissioned Gedaliah’s assassination (see 40:13–41:15). Milcom. Molech, chief deity. inherit Gad. The Ammonites are judged because they annexed Israelite territory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 2, 'days are coming. See 48:12. Rabbah. Ammonite capital. desolate mound. Ancient cities often rebuilt on the same location for centuries, leaving a mound when destroyed. Israel shall take possession. Fulfillment is undocumented.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 3, 'Heshbon. See 48:2. Under Ammonite control. Ai. Location unknown; not the one in Josh. 7:3. Milcom … into captivity. The national god is proven powerless.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 4, 'trusted … treasures. Pride, self-sufficiency, reason for judgment (see 48:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 6, 'afterward … bring back. Hope for future (see 48:47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 7, 'Edom. Descendants of Esau (see Gen. 36:1, 9), in frequent conflict with Israel, with territory southeast of the Dead Sea. wisdom no more. The Edomites were famous for wisdom (see Job 2:11). Teman. An Edomite town, personifies the nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 8, 'Dedan. Oasis town in Arabia. calamity of Esau. Judgment on Edom. The time. See 48:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 9, 'made Esau bare. Harvesters and thieves left some grapes, but God’s judgment destroys Edom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 11, 'fatherless … widows. Hope is extended to the poor and weak.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 12, 'drink of the cup. God’s cup of fury, judgment of Edom (see 25:15, 28–29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 13, 'sworn by Myself. Guaranteed to be true. Bozrah. Edomite capital, not the Bozrah of 48:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 14, 'See Obad. 1–4. small among nations. As if all nations loomed over Edom. dwell … rock. Edom’s famous rocky fortresses, such as Petra, now offer no shelter (2 Kin. 14:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 17, 'Sodom and Gomorrah. See 23:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 19, 'like a lion. Fierceness of God fighting Edom. floodplain. The thickets by Jordan, where lions hid. chosen man. Instrument used by God Himself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 22, 'eagle. See 48:40. spread His wings. After God used Nebuchadnezzar, Arab tribes overran the area.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 23, 'Damascus. Main Syrian city; indicted for cruelty in Amos 1:3. Hamath and Arpad. Syrian city-states allied to Damascus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 24, 'Panic seizes the once proud Damascus, as judgment approaches.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 27, 'kindle a fire. For total destruction, also purification (see Amos 1:4). Ben-Hadad. Literally, Son of Hadad, the chief Aramean god. Name of several kings (see 1 Kin. 15:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 28, 'Kedar. Arabian tribes, descendants of Ishmael (see Gen. 25:13); a territory in the Arabian desert. Hazor. Location unknown; may mean “unwalled villages,” in reference to the nomad’s lifestyle. Nebuchadnezzar … shall strike. Probably in 599 B.C. Arise. The Babylonians are summoned.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 29, 'tents … camels. Their nomadic lifestyle and wealth provided booty for the enemy (see v. 32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 30, 'depths. Caves, for shelter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 31, 'wealthy. Carefree.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 32, 'See v. 29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 34, 'Elam. Country east of Babylon, one-time ally, later under its control. beginning … reign. Ca. 597 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 35, 'break the bow. Of the famous Elamite archers (see Is. 22:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 36, 'four winds. Image of military might (see Ezek. 37:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 37, 'I will bring disaster. God is Elam’s real enemy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 38, 'set My throne. The conquering king assumes authority (see 43:8–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 49, 39, 'See 49:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 1, 'word … against Babylon. Prophecies of judgment. Jeremiah the prophet. See 1:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 2, 'Babylon is taken. Fulfilled in 539 B.C., by the Medo-Persians. Bel. Literally, “lord.” Merodach. Marduk, Babylon’s chief god (see Is. 46:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 3, 'out of the north. The Medo-Persians were located north and east of Babylon. This is a traditional direction for enemy approach (see 1:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 4, 'In those days. Time of restoration. With continual weeping. The purpose of captivity was to lead Judah to repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 5, 'ask the way. God can still be found. perpetual covenant. See 31:31–33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 6, 'My people. Covenant term (see 30:3). lost sheep. Misled people. shepherds. Leaders (see 3:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 7, 'devoured. Without God’s protection Israel became prey to other nations. not offended … they have sinned. God’s allowing other nations to discipline His people does not excuse the sins of those other nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 8, 'Go out. A new exodus is called for (see 16:14–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 9, 'assembly of great nations. An alliance to attack Babylon (see 51:27–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 11, 'were glad … rejoiced. Babylon had gleefully taken advantage of Judah. My heritage. Judah was still God’s people, even when judged. grown fat. By unfair means.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 12, 'Your mother. The city of Babylon. ashamed. For losing her glory. wilderness … desert. Desolation following destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 15, 'vengeance of the LORD. God holds Babylon responsible for its sins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 17, 'Israel. The reunited nation. Her return is heralded in vv. 17–20. sheep. Vulnerable. lions. Enemy armies. Assyria conquered northern Israel in 722 B.C. Babylon has broken his bones. Similarly, Babylon captured Judah, 605–586 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 18, 'I will punish. Assyria fell to the Babylonians; Babylon will fall to the Medo-Persians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 19, 'bring back Israel. Judah’s return was permitted in 537 B.C. Unification with the northern kingdom never happened (see 23:6). Carmel … Gilead. Fertile areas of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 20, 'In those days. Restoration (see 30:3). iniquity … shall be none. For God has forgiven His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 23, 'hammer … broken. God’s agent of destruction is destroyed (see 51:20–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 25, 'God goes to war against Babylon. Looting and destroying follows.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 28, 'Declares in Zion. Jerusalem receives the message of Babylon’s fall.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 29, 'War preparations continue. proud against the LORD. Babylon is indicted for its arrogant failure to recognize God’s superiority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 33, 'Israel … oppressed. Egyptian slavery is recalled as Babylon holds fast to its captives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 34, 'Redeemer is strong. God Himself will liberate His people. plead their case. That conditions for return have been met (see 25:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 35, 'sword is against. See 46:10. The battle is announced.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 38, 'Babylon will become desolate, a place for desert animals. carved images. Wanton idolatry brought Babylon punishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 40, 'Sodom and Gomorrah. See 23:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 42, 'Report of invasion causes fear in Babylon; even the king loses heart.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 50, 44, 'See 49:19–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 1, 'Leb Kamai. Cryptic term for Chaldea. destroying wind … winnowers. Images of judgment (see 4:11; 15:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 3, 'Total destruction by military means.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 5, 'Israel is not forsaken. God has not forgotten His people. Though … filled with sin. Forgiveness is possible. Holy One of Israel. See Is. 1:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 6, 'Flee. God’s people are called out of Babylon. LORD’s vengeance. See 50:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 7, 'golden cup. Of God’s fury, enacted through Babylon (see 25:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 8, 'suddenly fallen. Like all nations, drunk with God’s fury (see 25:16; Is. 21:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 9, 'not healed. Babylon missed any opportunity for renewal. go … own country. Babylon’s fall announces release for exiled nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 11, 'raised up … Medes. The Medo-Persians, under Cyrus, destroy Babylon and liberate Judah. vengeance … temple. For its destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 13, 'by many waters. The Euphrates, its tributaries and canals, dominated Babylon scenery. treasures. Babylonian wealth, much of it acquired as booty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 14, 'sworn by Himself. See 49:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 15, 'A hymn praising God, the Creator (see 10:12–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 20, 'My battle-ax. See 50:23. break … in pieces. Babylon was God’s weapon for the judgment of nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 24, 'repay Babylon. The battle-ax will be broken. evil … done in Zion. The reason for judgment is their unnecessary ferocity against Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 25, 'destroying mountain. Powerful kingdom (see Dan. 2:35, 44–45).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 26, 'not … a stone. The empire will not be rebuilt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 27, 'Prepare the nations. A coalition from Asia-Minor is summoned against Babylon. Ararat. See Gen. 8:4. Ashkenaz. Probably the Scythians (see Gen. 10:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 28, 'Medes. See v. 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 29, 'The creation shudders as God pursues war. ceased fighting. Babylon’s military might crumbles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 31, 'runner. Carrying battle news.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 34, 'devoured me. Judah speaks of how Babylonian greed destroyed them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 36, 'plead your case. See 25:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 37, 'See 50:13, 39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 39, 'drunk. See v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 41, 'Sheshach. See 25:26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 42, 'sea … waves. Enemies will roll over Babylon and leave it desolate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 44, 'punish Bel. See 50:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 45, 'My people, go out. A new exodus for the covenant people, who must trust God and not rumors (see 50:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 47, 'days are coming. See 48:12. carved images. See 50:38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 48, 'sing joyously. Liberation for the exiles has come.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 49, 'See 25:26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 50, 'Get away. See v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 51, 'strangers … sanctuaries. The Babylonians defiled and destroyed the Jerusalem temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 52, 'See v. 47.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 54, 'LORD is plundering. God is Babylon’s enemy. recompense. All will bear responsibility for their deeds.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 58, 'broad walls. Fortifications did not deter the enemy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 59, 'Seraiah. Possibly Baruch’s brother (see 45:5). fourth year. 593 B.C. in a book. A scroll. evil … upon Babylon. Jeremiah sends Babylonians the prophecies against them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 51, 61, 'Instructions to enact a prophecy on Jeremiah’s behalf. Babylon shall sink. Like the stone with its scroll. Thus far … words of Jeremiah. Chap. 52 is based on 2 Kin. 24:18–25:30 and was added to the book of Jeremiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 1, 'A historical epilogue, based on 2 Kin. 24:18–25:30, which it closely parallels. See notes there. It was probably included here to show the fulfillment of many of Jeremiah’s prophecies. Some discrepancies in numbers exist between the two accounts, which may be explained as copying errors or different ways of counting (see vv. 12, 22, 28–29, 31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 1, 'Zedekiah. Unlike Jehoiakim, not hostile to Jeremiah, but led by his courtiers. Zedekiah consulted Jeremiah, and Jeremiah asked him to save his life. Jeremiah prophesied Zedekiah’s fate (see 34:1–7; 38:14–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 2, 'A summary of the reign of a bad king after his death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 4, 'Summarized in 39:1–10. See notes there.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 6, 'famine. A devastating feature of Jerusalem’s 18-month siege (see 14:12; 38:2, 8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 17, 'The looting of the magnificent temple furnishings (see 1 Kin. 7:15–37). For their restoration, see Ezra 1:7–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 21, 'eighteen cubits. About 27 feet (8 m). twelve cubits. About 18 feet (5.4 m). four fingers. About 3 inches (7.5 cm).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 22, 'five cubits. About 7 1/2 feet (2.3 m).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 24, 'Of Judah’s leaders, some were executed, some taken to captivity. The reasons for the choices are not given, but those executed must have been held especially responsible for resistance against the Babylonians. Seraiah. See 51:59.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 29, 'The number of people exiled is difficult to calculate. The figure given (“four thousand six hundred”) may only include heads of household.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jeremias', 52, 31, 'See 2 Kin. 25:27–30.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 0, NULL, 'The first word of the book of Lamentations in Hebrew is ‘ekah, or “How!” This word is used in the Hebrew Bible as the name of the book of Lamentations. The question, with the same word, is repeatedly asked: How? A look at these “how” questions helps discern the real issues of the book. The English title “Lamentations” means “mourning song” or “dirge.” The book’s content fits this title, as it reflects on the tragic fall of Jerusalem (4:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 0, NULL, 'Scholars are united in the belief that Lamentations was written in the days of Jeremiah. There are, moreover, several striking parallels of phraseology and subject matter between Jeremiah’s prophecy and Lamentations, which point to him as its author. Without definitive evidence that he was not the author, it is reasonable to accept the ancient belief of the Jews that Jeremiah wrote the book of Lamentations.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 0, NULL, 'More than a century before the fall of Jerusalem, the prophet Micah had foretold its destruction, because the leaders of Judah “build up Zion with bloodshed, and Jerusalem with iniquity” (Mic. 3:10). For forty years Jeremiah urged the people of Judah to repent; he sought to strengthen the hands of Josiah and his sons toward just government at home and a wise and honest policy abroad; and above all, he warned Judah of the certainty of destruction to come if she persisted in her evil ways.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 0, NULL, 'The literary structure of Lamentations reflects its theme. The book is made up of five poems, corresponding to the five chapters in our modern Bibles (see');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 0, NULL, 'I.      First poem: a weeping city (1:1–22)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 1, 'The unspeakable devastation and desertion of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 1, 'How. Hebrew ’ekah. The first word of the book is an exclamation that gives the pulse of disaster. It is used in the setting of a funeral (see 2 Sam. 1:19, 25, 27; Is. 1:21; 14:4; Jer. 48:17; Ezek. 26:17) and is a short way of saying “How could this be?” The word expresses disbelief and shock in the face of inexplicable tragedy. The reasons for this astonishment are loneliness, loss of a spouse, desertion of a city, destitution from greatness, and deprivation of sovereignty. the city. Jerusalem is described as conquered, depleted of her population, and dominated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 2, 'The pain of Jerusalem’s suffering is vividly described, as grief turns into bitter tears. The verse speaks to the unfaithfulness of God’s people, as they have trusted more in foreign political powers than in God. Those whose favors they have courted have become enemies. They were so all along. Political alliances without God’s endorsement end up detrimental to the very freedom of God’s people. The tragedy is heightened by the absence of comfort. What Isaiah proclaimed to comfort God’s people has not yet found fulfillment (Is. 40:1). God’s people are still in the midst of tragedy. The reality of tears and weeping occurs repeatedly in the book (Lam. 1:16; 2:11, 18; 3:48).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 3, 'Judah … captivity. Pictures servitude and distress (Jer. 52:28–30). The promise of rest from all enemies as expressed in Deut. 12:10 is reversed. The covenant provides a background for the tragedy that befell God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 4, 'The set feasts have ceased. Pilgrimage is no longer relevant. What made the roads busy—the festivals—are taken away. There is a disintegration of religious life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 6, 'daughter of Zion. A personification of Jerusalem. The image of a helpless deer portrays the vulnerability of Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 9, 'Defilement because of unfaithfulness is a common motif of the eighth-century prophets.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 10, 'The desecration of God’s people could not be portrayed more dramatically. The sacred space, the sanctuary that delineated separation of the holy and the profane, has lost its function. There are no more boundaries. Not only have the nations entered into God’s assembly, but they also enter into what symbolizes the very center of God’s presence among His people—the sanctuary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 11, 'Covenant curse of famine (see Deut. 28:17, 38–42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 1, 12, 'Engaging God in time of suffering. The city interprets her fate as the Lord’s punishment because of her rebellion. The Lord is referred to as righteous, that is, justified in His dealings with His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 2, 1, 'The attribution in this chapter of Judah’s tragedy to God’s anger and actions should be understood in context. Israel has rebelled against God and broken the covenant. God, in order to be a covenant keeper Himself, needed to enact the covenant curses (see Deut. 28:15). The core cause of Israel’s problem was her unfaithfulness, not God’s anger. Such passages must be balanced with the constant OT reference to God’s love and willingness to forgive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 2, 6, 'The whole cultic system is affected, including (1) the worship places: the tabernacle (v. 6), the place of assembly (v. 6), the altar (v. 7), the sanctuary (v. 7), the house of the LORD (v. 7); (2) the worship times: appointed feasts and Sabbaths (v. 6); and (3) the worship personnel: the priest (v. 6). The same applies to the political realm: the king (v. 6) and the palaces (v. 7). The demise of the city is obvious in the destruction of the gates, the walls, and fallen ramparts. The city has become defenseless.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 2, 9, 'The collapse of the religious and political structures is extended to the fact that the Law does not assume its function any longer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 2, 10, 'Utter desolation and unspeakable suffering portray the desperation and helplessness that spread from the elders to the infants.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 2, 14, 'Your prophets. An accusation against false prophets who spread lies, encouraging delusions among God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 2, 20, 'See, O LORD. God is asked to consider how intolerable the situation is to the point of inhumanity. Women eat their children.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 1, 'The focus shifts from the city to the prophet. In spite of terrible suffering, the message of the prophet is to invite God’s people to patience and humility.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 1, 'I am the man. The prophet described himself as ambushed and chained, unable to escape. Not even his prayers can pierce through the roof.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 14, 'The prophet Jeremiah went through incalculable trials, tribulations, and humiliations. He faces a fierce opposition from family, the people, and the leadership (see Jer. 17:14–18; 18:23; 20:7–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 19, 'Remember. In the midst of incalculable grief, remembrance brings hope; specifically the consciousness that the Lord’s gracious love and kindnesses are not exhausted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 22, 'mercies. The word is correctly translated as plural. It conveys several related ideas: covenant love, grace, goodness, kindness, compassion; each one of these connotations can be expressed with other words. compassions. Another plural word that expresses a deep feeling of empathy. It is connected to the word “womb” when it is related to a mother’s deep attachment to her child. The whole verse can literally be translated as follows: “The kindnesses of the Lord never cease; His compassion (feelings of solidarity) never fails.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 23, 'Great is Your faithfulness. These memorable words are uttered in the midst of the most difficult situation: loss of freedom, provision, support, comfort, and tangible evidence of God’s presence. They testify to the triumph of hope in the most unpredictable and dire circumstances. The word “faithfulness” comes from the root that is used for the word “Amen.” It expresses the idea of firmness, therefore reliability and confidence. In Hebrew the word “belief” is of the same root. In the face of the collapse of the entire social, economic, and religious structure, hope in God is standing on still-solid ground. These are words of faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 24, 'The affirmation of vv. 22–24 is the numerical, theological, and literary heart of the book. When all is lacking, God can finally become the supreme value. portion. The prophet confesses that God is his portion, his part. The tribes of Levi did not have an inheritance among the tribes of Israel; of them it was said that the Lord is their “portion,” their inheritance (Deut. 10:9; Josh. 13:14). The Lord said this to Aaron as well (Num. 18:20), and David acknowledged the Lord as his portion (Ps. 16:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 25, 'An exhortation to wait on the Lord, to whom salvation belongs. This is a deliberate dependence on God in an attitude of trust—a trust that expresses that life’s ultimate solutions are the result of God’s grace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 31, 'Words of hope and justification. The knowledge of God as compassionate and loving provides the assurance that we can always hope in His grace. The understanding that God does not willingly afflict or grieve anyone leads to the conviction that God’s character is good. He is not capricious and vengeful. He has other purposes for humanity than to bring destruction. Beyond words of comfort, this section is also a theodicy, which is a justification of God by showing that God is righteous in judging His people. With these two ideas—comfort and justification of God’s judgment—the logical conclusion which the following verses develop is that God’s people’s responsibility is to repent and turn back to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 3, 40, 'Repentance and a call on God to vindicate His people from their enemies. The section describes the distress of the prophet, who embodied the plight of his people in the grip of tragedy. He also shows the need of liberation from enemies, who hunt them down like birds and silence their life in the pit (vv. 52–53). It is therefore fitting that God be presented as arbiter of a dispute between His people and their captors, and as liberator, defender, and judge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 4, 1, 'The loss of the value of previously precious items is exemplified by the fact that the stones of the sanctuary were considered most precious; however, under the devastation of the city they are reduced to being common stones. Likewise the Nazirites lose their distinction and prestige. Because of their dedication to God they were considered holy people (see Num. 6:1–21); now nothing sets them apart.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 4, 4, 'tongue of the infant clings to the roof of its mouth. A way of saying they have nothing to eat. Famine brings the most horrible scenes, especially when it affects children and infants.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 4, 9, 'better off. When death is seen as more fortunate than living, it shows how extreme the circumstances of the destruction of Jerusalem were.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 4, 12, 'The inviolability of Jerusalem was not just a belief among the Jews (see Jer. 7); even the enemies of God’s people seem to have accepted this idea. The previous defeat of the powerful Assyrian army in 701 B.C. may have played a role in this.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 4, 13, 'Those appointed to guarantee justice and righteousness and conformity to the covenant stipulations are the same ones who break them. The covenant curse of exile from God’s land becomes their fate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 4, 17, 'These verses essentially signify that when God’s covenant curses are unleashed, no external help can remedy the situation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 4, 21, 'daughter of Edom. The antagonism of the Edomites against God’s people, already mentioned in the book of Amos at the time of the Assyrian crisis, shows up again during the Babylonian crisis. For this and other sins, Edom will be punished. The whole book of Obadiah expands on this issue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 5, 1, 'A time of assessment, regret, repentance, and prayer. The gloomy picture of loss of freedom and its implications in living under precarious conditions led to deep soul-searching and confession of sins (see v. 7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 5, 16, 'The corporate representative of God’s people takes responsibility for the catastrophe. This terrible tragedy could not have happened if they had remained loyal to God’s covenant. God is vindicated through their remorse, but God will also be vindicated in another way in the last verses of the book. The tragedy of the destruction of Jerusalem and of the destruction of its temple will not be the end of the story of God’s relation with His people. Already the covenant curses were accompanied with a clause according to which repentance and restoration were never out of the question.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lamentações', 5, 19, 'The book ends with an affirmation of faith, the mention of God’s eternity, His sovereignty, and His everlasting rule and reign. This confession of faith is all the more remarkable in the context of the tragic experience of the transitory nature of human kingdoms, including the kingdom of Judah and its center, Jerusalem. What is stated implies the impossibility that God would forget His people. In the book of Isaiah, God pleaded with His people and challenged their thinking: “Zion said, ‘the LORD has forsaken me, and my Lord had forgotten me. Can a woman forget her nursing child, and not have compassion on the son of her womb? Surely they may forget, yet I will not forget you’ ” (Is. 49:14–15). Despite the tragedy and conquest of Jerusalem and the unspeakable suffering the city experienced, God is not inaccessible. The prayer to God itself is a testimony to the possibility that God will be gracious to His people.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 0, NULL, 'The title of the book is the name of its author, Ezekiel, which means in Hebrew, “God strengthens.” The book is placed third among the writings of the Latter Prophets (Isaiah-Malachi).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 0, NULL, 'As a priest, Ezekiel’s style is often heavy, repetitive, and very detailed, as he expresses ideas with precision of thought and terminology. His writing is mostly prophetic prose (33 of the 48 chapters contain no poetry) and is largely autobiographical.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 0, NULL, 'The theology of the book emerges from its literary structure. The major foci of Ezekiel are judgment and restoration, grounded in Day of Atonement themes. As on the Day of Atonement there was both judgment and restoration/cleansing (see notes on Lev. 16), so in the opening and closing sections of Ezekiel (members A and A′ in the');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 0, NULL, 'As noted above, this outline demonstrates the orderly, symmetrical (chiastic) arrangement of the book of Ezekiel.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 1, 'Ezekiel’s prophetic ministry begins with a vision of the glory of God: God comes in His heavenly storm-cloud chariot accompanied by four cherubim. As the storm cloud draws nearer, Ezekiel discerns more details of the cherubim and the wheels of the chariot, hears the deafening sound of the cherubim’s wings, and finally sees the sapphire throne occupied by a dazzling human-like form.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 1, 'thirtieth year. Probably the age of Ezekiel when called to the prophetic ministry (the normal age a priest began serving; Num. 4:3). fourth month … fifth year. July 21, 592 B.C., five years after Ezekiel went into exile with King Jehoiachin in 597 B.C. (see Introduction).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 3, 'River Chebar. Modern Shatt-en-Nil, an irrigation canal bringing the water of the Euphrates River in a loop southeast from Babylon via the city of Nippur and back near Uruk (Erech). hand of the LORD was upon him. Appearing seven times in the book, a phrase emphasizing God’s sovereign control over Ezekiel’s life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 4, 'north. The direction from which judgment often came upon Israel (e.g., Jer. 1:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 5, 'four living creatures. Called cherubim in chap. 10; throne-attending angels (see Gen. 3:24; Ex. 25–26; 36–37; 1 Kin. 6–8; 1 Chr. 28:18; 2 Chr. 3; Ps. 18:10; Heb. 9:5; Rev. 4:7). While Babylonian karubu (“cherubs”) generally took the body shape of animals, the cherubim Ezekiel saw had a human form (“likeness of a man”).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 14, 'lightning. Traveling at great speed; the destination remains unstated until 8:1 (see note on 8:1–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 16, 'wheels. Implies a movable throne (see v. 26 and Dan. 7:9), probably with intersecting wheels, making possible movement in every direction. May represent the complicated play of human events, which are under divine control (symbolized by the hands under the cherubim in v. 8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 18, 'full of eyes. Symbolizes the all-seeing nature of God (Ps. 139:1–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 22, 'firmament. A crystal platform, upon which was the divine throne (see v. 26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 24, 'noise. Since sound travels slower than light, Ezekiel hears the deafening sound of their wings some time after seeing the living creatures.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 1, 26, 'throne. The chariot gets close enough for Ezekiel to see what the living creatures and wheels were supporting: a moveable throne (see Dan. 7:9). sapphire. Or lapis lazuli, one of the most precious stones of ancient times. appearance of a man. The focal point of the vision has finally been reached: the dazzling human-like figure on the throne (identifiable as the pre-incarnate Christ; see the similar picture of Jesus in Rev. 1:13–16). rainbow. Symbolizes divine mercy (Gen. 9:13). appearance of the likeness. Ezekiel gives only an approximation of what he sees; words fail him to describe the scene adequately. fell on my face. The consistent human reaction of humility and a sense of unworthiness when God appears in His glory (see Gen. 28:16–17; Ex. 34:8; Josh. 5:14; Job 42:5–6; Dan. 10:5–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 1, 'Audible continuation of the first vision. Chap. 1 describes what Ezekiel saw, and chap. 2 articulates especially what the prophet heard.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 1, 'Son of man. The first expression of 93 occurrences addressing the prophet, stressing the humanity and mortality of an individual, in sharp contrast to the greatness and eternity of God. It is pronounced by God, never by humans. This term is not identical with the Son of man of Dan. 7:13 (= the heavenly divine being).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 2, 'When God speaks, He sends His Spirit, who gives new strength. When God’s Spirit and His word come together, life occurs (like in creation, see Gen. 1:1–3). The Spirit lifts up (see also 3:12, 24; 11:5, 24) and even transports Ezekiel (3:14; 8:3; 11:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 3, 'I am sending you. God’s commission to the prophet is stated twice for reinforcement. Thus says the Lord GOD. A solemn prophetic formula for delivering a revealed message, with the double usage of God’s name (very frequent in the book; e.g., 3:11, 27; 5:5, 7–8; 6:3, 11) in order to magnify the Lord’s sovereignty and the authority of the spoken word (3:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 5, 'prophet. Hebrew nabi’, a spokesperson for God (33:33) called by Him to deliver His message to people. Fulfillment of the prophetic work will confirm that God spoke through Ezekiel, His prophet. About false prophets, see especially chap. 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 6, 'His task will be difficult, because he will meet with opposition. People will stand stubbornly against him, rebel against God and His message, behaving like briers, thorns, and scorpions, but he should not be afraid (three times stressed in v. 6; plus once in 3:9), and should deliver the message, because God promises to strengthen him (3:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 8, 'Do not be rebellious. The prophet himself is warned and called to act in a directly opposite way from the rebellious people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 2, 9, 'when I looked … He said to me. “Seeing” and “hearing” mentioned in the same vision is a specific literary feature for interpreting biblical prophetic material (see Dan. 7:7–18; 8:1–14; Rev. 5:5–6; 7:1–9), because these two different modes of receiving messages refer to the same reality described from different angles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 3, 1, 'eat this scroll … like honey in sweetness. Digest the content of the scroll; the focus is on acceptance and identification with the message (v. 10). God’s words are sweet (Ps. 19:10). A similar experience is described in Rev. 10:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 3, 7, 'they will not listen to Me. Attention is on the personal relationship with the Lord, and on the contrast between foreign nations, who would listen if Ezekiel were sent to them, and God’s people, who are not willing to follow the Lord’s instruction (2:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 3, 14, 'I went in bitterness, in the heat of my spirit. Ezekiel’s passionate reaction to his difficult task. He is greatly disturbed and needed seven days to accept his call. A seven-day period was a customary period for mourning (Gen. 50:10; 1 Sam. 31:13; Job 2:13). Ezekiel was grieving for his rebellious nation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 3, 17, 'watchman. Ezekiel is called to be a watchman to warn people and call them to repentance before the destruction of Jerusalem. He is responsible to God for the lives of his fellow citizens, because everyone needs to hear the last appeal before impending judgment strikes. He needs to speak to the wicked; and if in spite of the warning they will continue stubbornly in their ways, they will die, but Ezekiel will no longer be accountable for their behavior. However, if someone repents, that person will live. On the other hand, if a righteous person does wrong things, and Ezekiel does not warn him, the prophet will be held accountable for his death. But if the prophet gives right advice and the sinner repents, then he saves him from destruction. Everyone is individually responsible to God, but believers have a special task to warn unbelievers of the consequences of their wrong choices. A call for repentance is implied. See also 33:2–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 3, 26, 'you shall be mute … but … I will open your mouth. The first of 12 sign-actions. People could hear Ezekiel only when he delivered God’s direct message. His muteness was lifted after the fall of Jerusalem (see 24:25–27 and 33:21–22). He who hears, let him hear. People decide if they will listen and obey, or if they will refuse to follow God’s appeals. Similar wording appears in Rev. 2:7, 11, 17, etc.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 4, 1, 'The second symbolic action portrays the siege and fall of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 4, 4, 'Lie also on your left side … lie again on your right side. The third and fourth sign-actions. The prophet symbolically bears the iniquity of his people day after day. He can only reveal their guilt but can do nothing to remove it or relieve them of it. For each year of iniquity, Ezekiel needs to lie one day on a side, altogether for 430 days (390+40=430). The time period reminds us of the 430 years that God’s people were in Egypt (Ex. 12:40–41). The period of 390 years is a puzzling number for scholars as it does not fit any precise known period of time. It may roughly refer to a period of iniquity from Solomon’s unfaithfulness to the fall of Jerusalem in 586 B.C. Forty years may point to the wicked life of King Manasseh who on a massive scale was a model of iniquity in Judah (2 Kin. 21:1–18; 23:26–27; 24:3–4) before his conversion (2 Chr. 33:10–20). Forty years reminds also of the period of wandering in the desert (Num. 14:33–34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 4, 9, 'The fifth sign-action. The food that should have been cooked over human excrement (but then changed for cow manure; commonly used as a fuel for baking) was a symbol of Judah’s spiritual uncleanness. The porridge of mixed grains and beans signified famine in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 4, 10, 'twenty shekels. About 8 ounces (about 0.2 kg).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 4, 11, 'one-sixth of a hin. About 2/3 quart (0.6 l).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 5, 1, 'barber’s razor. The sixth sign-action, symbolizing the sword that will come against Jerusalem (Is. 7:20). The instruction does not contradict Lev. 21:5, for the Levitical law allows the priest to shave the hair and beard, but forbids him to shave his head bald and the corners of his beard. weigh and divide. Implies the process of evaluation and the justice of God’s imminent judgment upon Jerusalem. one-third. Three fates for the inhabitants of Israel in the impending judgment (v. 12): (1) death in the pestilence/famine that overtakes the city; (2) death in the battle; and (3) exile. small number. A remnant of Israel would remain in the land; many of these would also perish (Jer. 40–42; 52:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 5, 5, 'Explanation of the sign-action, in the form of a covenant lawsuit (e.g., Deut. 32; Mic. 6): (1) preamble, introducing the overlord/judge (“the Lord GOD,” v. 5a); (2) historical prologue, summarizing the past blessings of the ruler to the subject nation (“I have set her in the midst of the nations,” v. 5b); (3) indictments or charges against the unfaithful nation (“She has rebelled,” vv. 6–7); (4) verdict of guilt (“therefore … I … will execute judgments,” vv. 8–9); (5) sentence of receiving the curses for covenant disloyalty (predicted in Deut. 28 and Lev. 26; “fathers shall eat their sons,” etc., Ezek. 10–17); and (6) the witnesses (in Israel often the mountains, which in this case are also condemned since they are the location of the idolatrous fertility cult ceremonies, 6:1–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 6, 9, 'I was crushed. Expresses God’s grief at being abandoned by His adulterous wife, Israel. idols. The Hebrew uses the vowels for a word meaning “detested thing” and the consonants for a word meaning “pellet of dung.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 6, 11, 'Pound your fists. Literally, “Strike your hands.” It is a gesture indicating joy/celebration, gloating over another’s misfortune, ridicule, or anger. Here it probably expresses God’s anger (see 21:14, 17; 22:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 6, 14, 'Diblah. Only mention in Scripture (see the dual form in Num. 33:46–47; Jer. 48:22); location uncertain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 7, 2, 'end. Also in vv. 3, 6. See Amos 8:2 for play on this word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 7, 3, 'will judge/repay. Also in vv. 4, 8–9, 27. For the law of lex talionis, just retribution, see note on Ps. 28:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 7, 10, 'day. Echoing the “day of the LORD” language of Amos 5:18–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 7, 17, 'weak as water. Or “wet with urine.” They pass urine in fright.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 7, 23, 'chain. For transporting the exiles to Babylon (see 2 Kin. 25:7; Jer. 40:1; Nah. 3:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 7, 26, 'No help from the recognized channels of authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 8, 1, 'The Spirit takes Ezekiel in vision some 700 miles (1,100 km) across the Arabian desert to Jerusalem’s temple courtyard, to be a witness in God’s legal case against Judah. Each of the four scenes reveals sins more abominable than the previous. All involve false (counterfeit) worship; see the parallels in Rev. 7 and 12–14, where false worship will be the issue in the final conflict; again there will be three displays of counterfeit worship (Rev. 16:13), climaxed by counterfeit worship paralleling the worship of the sun and identified as the mark of the beast (see note on Rev. 13:16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 8, 1, 'sixth year … fifth day. Sept. 7, 591 B.C., fourteen months after the glory of God had first appeared to Ezekiel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 8, 3, 'image of jealousy. I.e., the idol of a foreign god that provoked God to jealousy (see 2 Kin. 16:10–16; 21:7; 2 Chr. 33:7, 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 8, 4, 'glory. The God of Israel had taken up residence in the Jerusalem temple (see 9:3) to conduct legal proceedings (trial judgment) against Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 8, 7, 'Animal worship, probably part of a spiritualistic cult mixing many practices and originating in Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 8, 14, 'Tammuz. Dumuzi, a Sumerian vegetation god, who was thought to die and come back to life annually. The god was thus a counterfeit of the coming Messiah, who would die and be resurrected.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 8, 16, 'The climax of abominations, sun worship (see 2 Kin. 23:5, 11). branch to their nose. An insulting physical gesture in sun worship, not attested elsewhere. After showing Ezekiel the last abomination, God announces the close of Judah’s probation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 9, 1, 'The investigative (trial) phase of the judgment is over; the professed people of God are separated into two groups: the faithful remnant who are marked on their foreheads, and those who have embraced counterfeit worship. God leaves the Most Holy Place and announces the executive phase of the judgment. The historical fulfillment of this passage in the days of Ezekiel typologically represents the ultimate fulfillment in the book of Revelation that describes the trial (investigative) judgment before the Second Coming (Rev. 14:7), the sealing of God’s faithful people prior to the close of probation for the world (Rev. 7:2–3; 14:1), and the executive judgment upon the wicked (Rev. 14:17–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 9, 1, 'six men. Executioners of God’s legal sentence of judgment. writer’s inkhorn. A scribe’s writing kit, used by the man dressed in linen (the dress of priests and angels, Ex. 28:29–42; Dan. 10:5; 12:6–7) to mark God’s faithful remnant (Ezek. 9:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 9, 3, 'cherub. In the singular, here and 10:4, the word describes the static golden cherub beside the ark in the Most Holy Place of the Jerusalem temple, in contrast to the living cherubim (the four living creatures) elsewhere in chap. 10, depicted with the plural. The glory of God leaves the Most Holy Place, and pauses at the threshold of the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 9, 4, 'mark. In Hebrew, the word tau, the name of the last letter of the Hebrew alphabet; in Ezekiel’s day it was shaped like a cross. It was a symbol of the last or remaining ones, God’s faithful remnant who “sigh and cry” for the abominations done in Jerusalem. It parallels the “seal of God” marked in the forehead (mind or character) of His end-time remnant (Rev. 7; 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 9, 6, 'begin at My sanctuary. Where the sins had been concentrated, among Judah’s elders (chap. 8). Compare the echo in 1 Pet. 4:17: God will begin His end-time judgment at “the house of God.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 10, 1, 'The glory of the Lord departs slowly from the temple in His chariot-throne, leaves the city to the east, and pauses over the Mount of Olives, as if loathe to close the investigative phase of the judgment and execute the sentence upon His people, if there was even one more who would “turn and live” (see 18:30–32). The scene is reminiscent of Jesus’ pausing on the same Mount of Olives 600 years later, weeping over His beloved but doomed city (Luke 19:41–44); it points forward ultimately to God’s longsuffering at the end of time, not wanting any to perish (2 Pet. 3:9–13). The executive judgment upon Judah is announced (Ezek. 11:1–13), and a message of hope and future restoration is given to those who face exile (vv. 14–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 10, 1, 'The glory of the Lord departs slowly from the temple in His chariot-throne, leaves the city to the east, and pauses over the Mount of Olives, as if loathe to close the investigative phase of the judgment and execute the sentence upon His people, if there was even one more who would “turn and live” (see 18:30–32). The scene is reminiscent of Jesus’ pausing on the same Mount of Olives 600 years later, weeping over His beloved but doomed city (Luke 19:41–44); it points forward ultimately to God’s longsuffering at the end of time, not wanting any to perish (2 Pet. 3:9–13). The executive judgment upon Judah is announced (Ezek. 11:1–13), and a message of hope and future restoration is given to those who face exile (vv. 14–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 10, 1, 'cherubim. Plural, referring to the living creatures described in chap. 1 (see notes on 9:3 and 1:5). The singular form of the term in 10:2a was probably originally a plural (as in all of the ancient versions; scribes inadvertently omitted the plural ym ending due to its similarity to the start of the next word wml’). coals of fire. Symbolic of the impending destruction of Jerusalem.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 10, 14, 'face of a cherub. Replaces the face of an ox in 1:10; probably refers to the fact that in Babylon cherubs usually had the appearance of animals such as oxen, so use of the name “cherub” would connote an ox or similar animal.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 11, NULL, '11:1, 13 Pelatiah. The name means “The LORD saves/delivers.” The sudden death of Pelatiah (v. 13) gave away his lie that Jerusalem was a place of safety and deliverance.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 11, 16, 'little sanctuary. Or “sanctuary for a little while.” God still cares for, and is present with, His people, even while they are exiles and the temple is in ruins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 11, 17, 'Summary of what will be developed in the second half of the book: God will restore His exiled people to their land, remove their idols, give them a new spirit and heart of flesh, empower them to obey Him, and restore the covenant relationship with them. My people … their God. A statement of the heart of the covenant relationship between God and His people (14:11; 36:28; 37:27; see Ex. 6:7; Jer. 7:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 11, 22, 'In His cherubim-surrounded chariot-throne, God moves away from the midst of the city, and pauses on the Mount of Olives. Although Ezekiel is not shown His final destination, the implication is clear that the Lord has departed from the city, leaving it to its fate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 11, 23, 'Chaldea. Babylon. Ezekiel comes out of the vision (see 8:1), and relates to his fellow captives what he has seen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 12, 1, 'The seventh sign-action. In order to help people think about their destiny, Ezekiel needs to demonstrate packing for captivity, which is certain to happen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 12, 2, 'A rebellious attitude remains. To have ears and refuse to hear, and to have eyes and not to want to see, is an act of the will, a deliberate decision, stubbornness, and a sin strongly condemned (2:4–5; Ex. 13:15; Deut. 21:18–21; Judg. 2:19; Ps. 78:8; 81:12; Jer. 6:28; Hos. 4:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 12, 17, 'The eighth symbolic action. Ezekiel needs to eat with trembling, which symbolizes the fear and anxiety of the people when God’s judgment falls upon them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 12, 28, 'God stresses that His warnings are serious. Ezekiel is not talking about a distant future, but the present situation. There will be no more delays. Impending punishments will soon be fulfilled.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 13, 1, 'Condemnation of false prophets, who are lying (giving comforting, reassuring, but deceptive and cheap messages), prophesying out of their own imagination, and not speaking the word of God (see Jer. 23:9–40; 27–28; 29:20–28). They are compared to foxes (Ezek. 13:4), animals associated with ruins and dead flesh. They lead people to destruction, prophesy peace when there is no peace (v. 16), and give empty hopes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 13, 5, 'the day of the LORD. Refers to God’s intervention in history, and more specifically to the Second Coming of Christ. Seven prophets use this expression: Isaiah (Is. 13:6, 9), Ezekiel (here and Ezek. 30:3); Joel (Joel 1:15; 2:1, 11, 31; 3:14), Amos (Amos 5:18, 20), Obadiah (Obad. 15); Zephaniah (Zeph. 1:7, 14), and Malachi (Mal. 4:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 13, 10, 'mortar. Hebrew tafel, “plaster” or “whitewash,” even “deceptive visions” (Lam. 2:14). They build flimsy walls, easily destroyed by rain and wind, daubing them with plaster. They fix things externally, and create disaster (Ezek. 13:10–12). God Himself will be against them, which will result in their exclusion from society (v. 9). The punishment for idolatry was death (Deut. 13:6–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 13, 17, 'Against false prophetesses who practiced divination, employed wristbands and veils as part of their magic charms, and were paid for their practices (vv. 18–19). They are strongly condemned (vv. 20–23). People needed to be freed from their snares as birds from the traps. On the other hand, there is a rich biblical tradition of female prophets (Ex. 15:20–21; Judg. 4–5; 2 Kin. 22:14–20; Neh. 6:14; Is. 8:1–4; Luke 2:36; Acts 21:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 14, 1, 'Judgments upon elders associated with idolatry and upon false prophets; a continuation of chap. 13 in the form of case law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 14, 3, 'set up their idols in their hearts. People looked to different idols and fetishes (Hebrew gillul, literally, “dung pellets or balls,” a term used in Ezekiel 39 times, e.g., 6:4, but only 9 times elsewhere in the OT), but they are not able to help and have no worth. God will respond personally to such misleading and horrible practices. The worst was that the people in Babylonian captivity still had the same sinful attitudes and idolatry in their hearts even though they were taken away from their idols (6:9). They did not learn their lesson and needed to go through a deep conversion process.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 14, 6, 'Repent. A call for a change of thinking, attitudes, and practice. Three explicit appeals for repentance appear at crucial points in the book (here; 18:30; 33:11–12; but see also 3:19–20; 6:9; 20:43; 36:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 14, 7, 'strangers. Aliens or foreigners who joined the community of faith to live with the people of God (Ex. 12:19; Lev. 16:29; 17:8–16; 18:26; Deut. 16:11; 26:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 14, 11, 'they may be My people and I may be their God. Covenant formula. The people are encouraged to stay faithful to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 14, 14, 'Noah, Daniel, and Job. These three great men (two ancient and one contemporary model) among God’s people were known for their relationship with the Lord, wisdom, and righteousness. But even these devoted exemplary people could not save other persons (vv. 16–20; 28:3). Everyone stands for oneself before God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 15, 1, 'First parable: the parable of the vine, a symbol of God’s people (see Ps. 80:8–13; Is. 5:1–7; John 15:1–17); the vine also symbolizes prosperity, richness, and prolific growth. Its only purpose is to bear fruit, but if the vine does not produce fruit, it is useless, only good for fuel (and after burning, good for nothing). Thus, disaster follows; the vine needs to be burnt down (vv. 7–8), so Israel will go through a great calamity, because they are unfaithful, spiritually dead, and ready for the fire.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 16, 1, 'Second parable: God’s love song. His people living in Jerusalem are symbolized by a beautiful woman (two images are intertwined—she is an adopted daughter but also a wife). When she was born she was doomed to die, but God took care of her and made her to prosper, making her famous and rich. Unfortunately, she became unfaithful to Him, lived wildly in promiscuity, and behaved like the worst prostitute, worse than Sodom and her daughters (v. 48). She not only worshiped idols but offered her own children to foreign gods (vv. 20–21, 36; 20:26, 31; 23:37, 39). Her ingratitude and forgetfulness of God’s love and care is surprising and unbelievable. Yet, God is always for her—forgiving, faithful, and showing grace. The parable ends with the hope that God will again intervene for her and reestablish an everlasting covenant with her (v. 60), and even though she has broken it repeatedly (v. 59) she will repent (vv. 61, 63). In this way it is a magnificent story of a prodigal daughter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 16, 59, 'The integral part of establishing the covenant is the covenant blessings for the obedient and curses for the disobedient (Lev. 26; Deut. 27–30). Instead of fulfilling the covenant curses, God in His grace renews the covenant with His unfaithful wife.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 16, 63, 'when I provide you an atonement for all you have done. God makes an atonement for His people, who do not deserve grace but only punishment. provide … atonement. From the Hebrew root kafar, a technical word for making atonement, covering sins, providing forgiveness (Ex. 29:36; 30:10; Lev. 16:6, 16, 30–34; 17:11; Ps. 65:3; 79:9; Ezek. 43:20, 26; 45:15, 17, 20). It ultimately points to Jesus Christ’s death on the cross, where He made atonement for us (Rom. 3:25; Heb. 9:28; 1 John 4:10). When God provides atonement and forgives, then His people should remember His goodness and change their attitude and behavior. God forgives all the past and makes everything new (Ps. 32:1–2; 51:1–2, 10–12; Is. 1:18; Mic. 7:18–19; Rom. 8:1; 2 Cor. 5:17; 1 John 1:9). Only God’s mercy and people’s grateful appreciation for it lead them back to Him, to true repentance, and to a new life (see Rom. 2:4; 12:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 17, 1, 'The third parable is about two eagles and a vine and refers to a political situation. It foretells especially to Zedekiah what will be his fate (and that of his army) if he will not obey God’s word (vv. 16–21). The first great eagle represents King Nebuchadnezzar, who appointed Zedekiah (“seed” that becomes a “vine,” vv. 5–6) as king in Jerusalem (symbolized by “Lebanon,” v. 3). The “cedar” points to David’s dynasty and its “highest branch” is Jehoiachin, whom Nebuchadnezzar carried as a prisoner to a “city of merchants,” identified as Babylon (v. 12). Unfortunately, the last king of Judah, Zedekiah, rebelled and allied with Egypt whose pharaoh (Psammetichus II [595–589 B.C.] or Hophra [589–570 B.C.]) is the second “eagle” (v. 7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 17, 22, 'a tender one. A twig, branch. This is a messianic prophecy. In a totally new and unexpected way, a shoot grows up from David’s house, and a new King and His kingdom will expand. The mountain represents Jerusalem. This figure of the Davidic king points ultimately to Jesus the Messiah (Is. 11:1–5; Jer. 23:5–6; 33:15–16; Zech. 3:8; 6:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 18, 1, 'A unique chapter about the responsibility of each person for his or her own actions, it disputes the view that the present punishment is a result of the sins of past generations (quoted as a proverb in v. 2). In the background is a misunderstanding of the biblical passages about punishments going into the third or even fourth generations (see Ex. 20:5–6; 34:7; Deut. 5:9–10). Three generations are mentioned in this chapter—a righteous man (Ezek. 18:5), his wicked son (v. 10), and the wicked son’s repentant son (v. 14)—in order to demonstrate that every one is responsible for his own life, accountable to God, and judged by Him according to one’s own work. These principles are explained in vv. 21–24 (see also 33:12–20). Our actual behavior reveals our choices, values, and attitudes, thus our character.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 18, 32, 'turn and live! The core of God’s message to people is to repent—to return to Him and follow His instructions. His desire for humans is that all live (see note on 14:6). In order to live a new, meaningful, and different life, God offers believers a new heart and a new Spirit, but they need to personally accept these gifts (18:31) and let Him work in them (11:19–20; 36:26–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 19, 1, 'A lament in the form of parable or allegory over the queen mother Hamutal (symbolized by a lioness in v. 2 and by a vine in v. 10). Her two sons who became kings in Judah (2 Kin. 23:31; 24:18) are used as an illustration of the principle of individual moral responsibility. The first young lion, identified with Jehoahaz, was removed from his throne by the Egyptian pharaoh Necho II after his three-month reign and was brought in chains to Egypt (2 Kin. 23:29–33). The second young lion was the disobedient, rebellious King Zedekiah, whom Nebuchadnezzar deported to Babylon (2 Kin. 25:1–7; this prediction was so certain that it was told in the past tense—so-called “prophetic perfect”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 20, 1, 'This key chapter (the year is 590 B.C.) recounts God’s goodness and faithfulness in the midst of Israel’s rebellion and provides the basis for God’s judgment. The stress is on God’s people’s notorious devotion to idolatry instead of to the Lord. The history of God’s grace and the attitudes of His people has a transparent structure:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 20, 11, 'Three times it is stated that the person who obeys God’s given laws will live by them (vv. 11, 13, 21). It echoes Lev. 18:5.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 20, 25, 'statutes that were not good. Does not refer to the Decalogue or other laws of God, which were good (Ps. 19:7–11; 119; Rom. 7:12) and were given to the first generation (see the outline in note on 20:1–49). The statutes referred to in this verse are human-made laws (see also Ezek. 20:18) and refer to the unlawful practice of child sacrifices (vv. 26, 31, 39; 2 Kin. 17:17; 21:6; 23:10, 13; 2 Chr. 28:3; Jer. 7:31; 32:35) and to the misuse of laws related to the principle of the firstborn—that everything that opens the womb belongs to the Lord (Ex. 13:2; 22:29; Num. 18:15). Ezek. 20:25 expresses God’s sarcasm in relationship to the stubbornness of God’s people (letting them become defiled through their sinful practices) which in the midst of their idolatry was an ultimate divine measure to shake the people’s consciousness and lead them to repentance and back to Himself (like in v. 39; Ps. 81:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 21, 6, 'The ninth symbolic action (for the first eight, see notes on 3:26–27; 4:1–3; 4:4–8; 4:9–17; 5:1–4; 12:1–6; 12:17–20). Ezekiel has to groan with a broken heart and bitter grief because of the great calamity relating to the fall of Jerusalem, which is definitely coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 21, 18, 'The tenth sign-action. The prophet needs to mark two roads with signposts: one leading to Rabbah, the capital of Ammon (the Ammonites conspire against Babylon, too), and the other to Jerusalem. Nebuchadnezzar, the Babylonian king will choose (using divination and magic as mentioned in v. 21; magic practices are nowhere described in the biblical text, and in this way a safe distance is kept from them) to go against Judah and take God’s stubborn people into captivity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 21, 25, 'wicked prince of Israel. King Zedekiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 21, 27, 'Until He comes whose right it is. A messianic prophecy in view of Gen. 49:10. Only the Messiah will set up the righteous kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 21, 28, 'God’s judgments will fall also upon the Ammonites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 22, 1, 'A continuation of the countdown that leads to the destruction of Jerusalem. The prophet presents a series of short scenes which describe why the fall is inevitable. A long list of sins is enumerated (vv. 1–14) as reason for their terrible doom (v. 15). God’s people are like a worthless waste without substance (v. 18) and will melt inside a furnace of coming calamities (vv. 19–22). All went astray—princes, priests, officials, prophets, and people (vv. 25–29)—and lived without acknowledging God and respecting His law. There was no one who would stand in the gap between God and His people (v. 30; Jer. 5:1); therefore judgment will strike them all.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 22, 26, 'distinguished between the holy and unholy. The main task of priests; see 44:23; Lev. 10:10–11. Because of the lack of the true knowledge of God, the people perish (Ezek. 18:32; Jer. 7:24; Hos. 4:6; Joel 2:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 23, 1, 'A parable about two adulterous sisters: Oholah represented Samaria, and Oholibah was identified with Jerusalem. In spite of having wonderful husbands and lacking nothing, they became prostitutes, lusted after political alliances with foreign nations, and had even worse behavior than that of the foreign nations (idolatry being like religious prostitution). With rich colorful metaphors and allusions, the dark history of God’s people is explained and God’s judgment is shown to be unavoidable and inescapable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 23, 10, 'A reference to the fall of Samaria in 722 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 23, 27, 'A warning against lifting up one’s eyes to idols (18:12; Ps. 121:1) in contrast to lifting up one’s eyes to the Lord (Ps. 2:4; 11:4; 115:3, 16; 123:2; Is. 66:1). God shows His mercy to those who bow humbly before Him (Is. 57:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 23, 35, 'you have forgotten Me. Forgetfulness is a key element in disrespecting God and His covenant and is often mentioned as the sin of God’s people (Deut. 4:23; 8:11, 14, 19; 9:7; 2 Kin. 17:38; Prov. 3:1; Is. 65:11; Jer. 23:27). God is always faithful and does not forget them (Deut. 4:31; Is. 49:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 24, 1, 'The siege of Jerusalem started on January 15, 588 B.C. (2 Kin. 25:1). The parable (allegory) of the cooking pot signifies that the destruction of Jerusalem is inevitable; the time of judgment has arrived (Ezek. 24:14). The cooking pot represents Jerusalem, and meat stands for the inhabitants of Jerusalem (11:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 24, 15, 'with one stroke. The eleventh and culminating sign-action (all 11 are negative) before the fall of Jerusalem, which should shock and shake the people into repentance. This is the ultimatum for them. Ezekiel’s wife, the “desire of [his] eyes,” suddenly dies, symbolizing that the sanctuary, the “desire” and “delight” of the people, will be destroyed. The greatest catastrophe in national history is going to happen: God’s people will lose the temple—their center of worship will be burnt; Jerusalem, the capital city, will be destroyed; national freedom will be lost; homes and land will be confiscated; many people will be killed or injured; and the majority will be taken into Babylonian captivity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 25, 1, 'After Jerusalem is besieged, thus sealing the nation’s doom, God calls Ezekiel’s attention to the surrounding nations, who also will be judged. The Lord, “Creator/Possessor of heaven and earth” (Gen. 14:19, 22), is King over all nations (Ps. 96:10), assigning and controlling their territories and boundaries (Deut. 32:8; 2 Kin. 10:32; Is. 10:13), directing their migration (Amos 9:7), raising them up and putting them down (Is. 40:22–23; Jer. 1:10; 27:5; Dan. 2:21). The entire earth is under an “everlasting covenant” (Is. 24:5), an international law or code of human standards in which all nations have ethical duties of civility and humaneness (Amos 1–2). God in His universal sovereignty is the guarantor of justice and decency among the nations in their treatment of each other, and those nations who violate the universal norms of proper behavior receive divine sanctions for their crimes. Numerous other prophecies concerning foreign nations in Scripture address their crimes and the divine sanctions against them: Num. 24:17–24; Is. 13–24; Jer. 46–51; Amos 1–2; Obadiah; Jonah; Nahum; Hab. 2:4–20; Zeph. 2. For the most part these prophecies may be classified as divine lawsuits (God’s legal proceedings against various nations). Ezekiel’s lawsuits against the seven nations surrounding Judah generally follow the same structure: (1) indictments or legal charges against the nation (introduced by “Because”); (2) the verdict/sentence (introduced by “Therefore”); and (3) the ultimate purpose of the lawsuit in vindicating God’s character and course of action (“know that I am the LORD”). At this stage of history Babylon was filling the role of God’s agent of judgment against other nations, and thus is not judged herself (Deut. 28:32–57; 2 Chr. 36:17; Jer. 25:9; Hab. 1:5–11). Oracles in this section are not arranged in chronological order, but in a literary pattern called block parallelism, with the first half matching the second half (see chart below). In the center of this section, and of the whole book, the oracle shifts to the universal level, and the judgment of the fallen cherub, who is ultimately responsible for evil in the world (see notes on Ezek. 28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 25, 1, 'After Jerusalem is besieged, thus sealing the nation’s doom, God calls Ezekiel’s attention to the surrounding nations, who also will be judged. The Lord, “Creator/Possessor of heaven and earth” (Gen. 14:19, 22), is King over all nations (Ps. 96:10), assigning and controlling their territories and boundaries (Deut. 32:8; 2 Kin. 10:32; Is. 10:13), directing their migration (Amos 9:7), raising them up and putting them down (Is. 40:22–23; Jer. 1:10; 27:5; Dan. 2:21). The entire earth is under an “everlasting covenant” (Is. 24:5), an international law or code of human standards in which all nations have ethical duties of civility and humaneness (Amos 1–2). God in His universal sovereignty is the guarantor of justice and decency among the nations in their treatment of each other, and those nations who violate the universal norms of proper behavior receive divine sanctions for their crimes. Numerous other prophecies concerning foreign nations in Scripture address their crimes and the divine sanctions against them: Num. 24:17–24; Is. 13–24; Jer. 46–51; Amos 1–2; Obadiah; Jonah; Nahum; Hab. 2:4–20; Zeph. 2. For the most part these prophecies may be classified as divine lawsuits (God’s legal proceedings against various nations). Ezekiel’s lawsuits against the seven nations surrounding Judah generally follow the same structure: (1) indictments or legal charges against the nation (introduced by “Because”); (2) the verdict/sentence (introduced by “Therefore”); and (3) the ultimate purpose of the lawsuit in vindicating God’s character and course of action (“know that I am the LORD”). At this stage of history Babylon was filling the role of God’s agent of judgment against other nations, and thus is not judged herself (Deut. 28:32–57; 2 Chr. 36:17; Jer. 25:9; Hab. 1:5–11). Oracles in this section are not arranged in chronological order, but in a literary pattern called block parallelism, with the first half matching the second half (see chart below). In the center of this section, and of the whole book, the oracle shifts to the universal level, and the judgment of the fallen cherub, who is ultimately responsible for evil in the world (see notes on Ezek. 28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 25, 1, 'Undated prophecy. Ammonites. Located east of Judah, across the Jordan River. Descendants of Lot by his younger daughter (Gen. 19:38), with a history of hostility toward Israel (Judg. 3:13; 11:12–15, 32–33; 1 Sam. 11:1–11; 2 Sam. 10:6–14; Amos 1:13–15). Here God condemns Ammon for rejoicing over Israel’s misfortunes (Ezek. 25:3, 6–7). men of the East. Nomadic tribes of the desert east of Ammon and Moab, comprised largely of the descendants of Abraham through Ishmael, Amalek, and Midian (Gen. 29:1; Judg. 6:3, 33; 7:12; 8:10; 1 Kin. 4:30; Job 1:3; Jer. 49:28). Rabbah. Capital of Ammon; modern Amman (capital of Jordan).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 25, 8, 'Moab. South of Ammon, east of the Dead Sea. Descendants of Lot through his older daughter (Gen. 19:37). Other oracles against Moab include Is. 15–16; Jer. 48; and Zeph. 2:8–9. Seir. Edom (see note on Ezek. 25:12). Moab is indicted for losing its awe of the Lord and not taking Him seriously. Beth Jeshimoth. City in Moab’s plains (Josh. 12:3; 13:20). Baal Meon (Num. 32:38; Josh. 13:17; 1 Chr. 5:8) and Kirjathaim (Num. 32:37; Josh. 13:19; 1 Chr. 6:76) are cities mentioned in the inscribed monument of Mesha, king of Moab, discovered in A.D. 1868 in Dhiban, Jordan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 25, 12, 'Edom. Located south of Moab and the Dead Sea; descendants of Esau, Jacob’s brother (Gen. 25:30). They were condemned by God for taking vengeance against Judah, probably when they urged on the Babylonians in their attack upon Jerusalem (Ps. 137:7) and refused to protect Judah’s refugees after the fall of Jerusalem (Obad. 11–14). Teman. District near Petra in central Edom (Jer. 49:7, 20; Amos 1:12; Obad. 9; Hab. 3:3). Dedan. Territory and tribe in southern Edom (Ezek. 27:20; 38:13; Is. 21:13; Jer. 49:8; see also Gen. 10:7; 25:3).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 25, 15, 'Philistines. Lived west of Judah in the coastal plain; continually vexed Israel until David subdued them (2 Sam. 21). For origins, see Amos 9:7 and notes on Gen. 10:14; 20:1. The Philistines continued their hostility toward Israel (Is. 14:29–31; Jer. 47;');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 26, 1, 'Oracles against Tyre, the main seaport of the Phoenicians (in modern Lebanon). Old Tyre was located on the mainland, and New Tyre was built on an island a half mile from shore; both were fortified. The prophet probably takes so much space to describe the character of Tyre because it so closely resembles the character of her ultimate leader, the fallen cherub (see note on 28:12–19). Many of the descriptions of Tyre and her punishment in chaps. 26–28 are alluded to in the judgment of mystical Babylon (Rev. 17–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 26, 1, 'eleventh year … first day. (Feb. 13) 586 B.C. The month is not given, but probably should read “eleventh month.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 26, 2, 'Predicts a sequential destruction of the city, involving at least two powers (vv. 1–14; note the alternation between “he” [referring to Nebuchadnezzar, vv. 7–11], and “they,” vv. 4–5, 12–13). The historical events strikingly fulfilled this prophecy, first, as Nebuchadnezzar managed to take the mainland of Old Tyre following a 13-year siege (585–572 B.C.; vv. 7–11; 29:18). More than 200 years later, the troops of Alexander the Great (the “they” of 26:4–5, 12–13) literally fulfilled another part of the prophecy (vv. 4, 12) as they scraped into the sea the remains of the previously-destroyed mainland city and built a causeway out to the off-shore island city of New Tyre, to complete the conquest, after a seven-month siege in 332 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 26, 14, 'never be rebuilt. Predicts the total and permanent destruction of the city of Tyre; the largely-deserted site of ancient Tyre is still used by fishermen for drying their nets. This does not necessarily mean there will be a total lack of buildings on the site forever; the Hebrew banah (“re-build”) is used for the predicted restoration of the city of Jerusalem (Dan. 9:25 and Is. 44:28) but according to 2 Kin. 25:9 and Jer. 52:13, Nebuchadnezzar did not destroy all the buildings when he conquered Jerusalem, but only “the houses of the great.” The ancient city of Tyre with its culture and civilization are gone forever, whatever modern architecture may appear on the ancient sites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 26, 19, 'Pit. A poetic term for the grave. The city is personified and pictured as going to the grave, where she “shall be no more” (v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 27, 1, 'lamentation. Hebrew qinah, a funeral dirge/song. The literary form of the lament usually is 3:2, i.e., a long poetic line (three accented syllables) followed by a shorter line (two accented syllables). This chapter portrays Tyre as a gallant merchant ship conducting a prosperous trade among the major countries of the Mediterranean world but at last shipwrecked on the high seas.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 1, 'This chapter begins with an oracle against the “prince” (Hebrew nagid) of Tyre; i.e., the earthly ruler of the city. In v. 12 the scene shifts to the cosmic realm and focuses upon the one really in charge, the “king” (melek) of Tyre, i.e., the supernatural being that ruled from behind the scenes (see notes on vv. 12–19). This parallels the usage elsewhere regarding Israel: the earthly ruler was originally called “prince” (nagid, 1 Sam. 9:16; see also Ezek. 13:14; 2 Sam. 7:8) with God as the real king (Hebrew mlk; 1 Sam. 12:12; see also 1 Sam. 8:7). Ezekiel employs the same contrast with regard to Judah: the earthly ruler is often called “prince” (nasi, e.g., 12:10, 12; 19:1; 22:6) and God is the King (20:33). See Daniel’s description of angel mentors of earthly rulers (Dan. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 1, 'The earthly ruler of Tyre, probably Ithobaal III (ca. 591–573 B.C.), was noted for his wisdom (vv. 3–5), riches in trading (vv. 4–5), pride (vv. 2, 5), and his claim to be divine (vv. 2, 5–6, 9). God judges this proud ruler, sentencing him to death and descent to the Pit (a poetic term for the grave, v. 8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 12, 'king of Tyre. The language of this section is no longer applicable to an earthly ruler. The focus shifts to the cosmic realm, emphasizing the supernatural being behind the scenes, the angelic mentor of the human pawn on the earthly throne. The passage describes the cosmic origin of SIN and rebellion against God (vv. 15–17); it is parallel to Is. 14:12–14, where the supernatural being responsible for evil is named Lucifer, and to Rev. 12:4, 7–9, where this being after his fall is called Satan and the devil. This central and climactic section of Ezekiel’s book is arranged in a symmetrical (chiastic) pattern:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 12, 'seal of perfection. This being was not at first sinful; he was “perfect” (tamim) in his ways from the day he was created (v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 13, 'Eden. Not the earthly Eden, but the heavenly “garden of God,” on the holy mountain of God (v. 14). Every precious stone … timbrels and pipes. The stones recall the precious stones of the anointed high priest, and the timbrels and pipes the music of sanctuary worship; this description hints at the special status, and musical ability, of this heavenly being.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 14, 'anointed cherub who covers. Not human, but a supernatural being, a throne-attending angel (in the Most Holy Place of the heavenly sanctuary; see note on 1:6, 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 15, 'iniquity. Literally, “injustice.” Apparently this heavenly cherub harbored in his heart a false belief that God was unjust.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 16, 'trading. Literally, “to go about from one to another trading, either in goods, or in words [slander].” The meaning of trading material goods fits the context of this word in v. 5, referring to the earthly king of Tyre, a city known for its merchandising (see notes on chap. 27); but in the cosmic context, the meaning “slander” fits best: the guardian cherub slanders God by accusing Him of injustice. violence. The slander grows into violent rebellion (as described in Rev. 12:7–8). destroyed. Literally “to [treat as] profane,” best translated in the sense of “cast out, expel” (Rev. 12:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 17, 'lifted up. The cherub became proud of his beauty and wisdom (see Is. 14:13–14). sanctuaries. On earth, the fallen cherub is portrayed as possessing rival sanctuaries to that of the Most High, which he defiles by his iniquities. trading. After his expulsion to the earth, he continues his slander (trading in slanderous words; see previous note). no more forever. The final and lasting destruction of the fallen cherub is predicted in the Hebrew “prophetic perfect”—the past tense used to depict a future event as already having happened because it is so certain to come to pass.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 20, 'Sidon. A Phoenician city about 25 miles (40 km) north of Tyre; usually mentioned together with Tyre (Is. 23:1–4; Jer. 47:4; Joel 3:4; Zech. 9:2). No specific sins are mentioned, but Sidon was one of the nations whose land God was giving over to Nebuchadnezzar (Jer. 27:1–8).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 23, 'By the sword. As predicted, Sidon was destroyed by the Persians (ca. 345 B.C.), and after being rebuilt, was later conquered by Alexander the Great (333 B.C.) and the Roman general Pompey (first century B.C.).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 24, 'pricking brier. The destruction of Sidon eliminated the last of Israel’s neighboring enemies, called “pricking briers” and “painful thorns” (Num. 33:55), thus constituting an implicit message of salvation for Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 28, 25, 'gathered. The promise of Israel’s restoration to her homeland after the Babylonian exile, a theme more fully developed in the second half of the book (e.g., 34:13; 36:24; 37:21; 38:8; 39:27). The gathering prophecies for historical Israel are ultimately fulfilled with the “gathering” of God’s people during the NT era and at the Second');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 1, 'Seven oracles against Egypt, most coming during the final years before the destruction of Jerusalem, when Pharaoh Hophra (Apries for the Greeks, 589–570 B.C.; see Jer. 44:30), was urging King Zedekiah to rebel against Nebuchadnezzar (Jer. 37:5–8; Ezek. 17:15). All but one of the oracles are dated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 1, 'tenth year … twelfth day. Jan. 7, 587 B.C. Perhaps given shortly after the Babylonian siege of Jerusalem was temporarily lifted when Pharaoh Hophra approached the city (Jer. 37:5, 11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 3, 'great monster. The Nile crocodile, but also carrying mythological connotations (Ps. 87:4; 89:10; Is. 51:9). I have made it. See also Ezek. 29:9. Or “I have made myself.” This is a claim of divine kingship on the part of Pharaoh Hophra; the Greek historian Herodotus (ii.170) describes the arrogant boasting of Apries in claiming not even a god could unseat him from power. The pharaohs of this 26th Dynasty dared to dream that they could rebuild the old Egyptian Empire in Asia, and challenged the emerging Neo-Babylonian Empire.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 6, 'staff of reed. The inhabitants of Judah placed false hopes in Hophra, that their city could be spared by relying on him (see note on v. 1; see also Is. 36:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 8, 'Migdol … Ethiopia. The territorial boundaries of Egypt. forty years. God announces the punishment of a generation of Egyptians that dared to interfere with His plans for Babylon and Israel (Jer. 46:13–26). The number 40 is reminiscent of the punishment of the wilderness generation of Israel for their rebellion against the Lord. Egypt fell to the Babylonians in 568 B.C.; Nebuchadnezzar attacked the pharaoh’s palace at Taphanes, burned the temples of the Egyptian gods, and smashed the obelisks of Heliopolis (Jer. 43–44; see notes on Ezek. 30:13–18). No record is preserved of a 40-year exile of the Egyptian people, followed by a return (under the Persians), but such is not improbable. lowly kingdom. Egypt would never again regain its past glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 17, 'twenty-seventh year … first day. April 16, 570 B.C. The latest dated message of Ezekiel. Commentators have speculated as to why, in the series of six dated oracles against Egypt, only this oracle (29:17–30:19) appears out of chronological sequence—actually coming some 16 years after the fall of Jerusalem and shortly before Nebuchadnezzar’s conquest of Egypt (568 B.C.). But a ready reason is at hand; while Babylon is briefly mentioned in the other oracles against Egypt, this oracle highlights Babylon as the agent of divine judgment. Its placement parallels the spotlight upon Babylon in the sequence of Tyre oracles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 18, 'labor strenuously. Refers to the 13-year siege of Tyre by Nebuchadnezzar, that was only partially successful (see note on 26:2–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 20, 'worked for Me. See note on 26:2–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 29, 21, 'In that day. A common formula often indicating eschatological time and/or the messianic age (e.g., 38:19; 39:11; occurs over 40 times in Isaiah). horn … spring forth. Predicts the coming of the Messiah, echoing the prophetic prayer of Hannah (see note on 1 Sam. 2:10; see also Ps. 132:17; 148:14). It is one of five messianic prophecies in Ezekiel (see Introduction, “Title, Author, and Date”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 1, 'Continuation of the theme begun in the previous section: Nebuchadnezzar is God’s agent of judgment against Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 3, 'day of the LORD. See note on 7:1–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 5, 'Ethiopia. Egypt’s southern neighbor on the Nile (Gen. 10:6). Libya. In North Africa. Lydia. In Asia Minor; Lydians served as mercenaries for Egypt. mingled people. Probably foreign mercenaries from various lands (see Jer. 25:20; 50:37). Chub. May refer to a particular region of Libya. men … allied. Literally “sons of the land” (the Septuagint reads “My covenant with them”): probably the Jewish hired soldiers and colonies in Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 6, 'Migdol to Syene. See note on 29:8–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 13, 'Noph. Ancient Memphis, former capital of Egypt; 15 miles (24 km) south of modern Cairo.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 14, 'Pathros. Upper (southern) Egypt. Zoan. City in the delta region of NE Egypt; also called Rameses (Gen. 47:11; Ex. 1:11), Avaris, and Tanis (Is. 19:11, 13; 30:4). No. Ancient Thebes, capital of Upper Egypt; modern Luxor/Karnak.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 15, 'Sin. Probably ancient Pelusium, frontier town in the eastern delta region.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 17, 'Aven. Ancient On, Heliopolis; six miles (10 km) NE of modern Cairo. Pi Beseth. Bubastis. 40 miles (65 km) NE of modern Cairo; former capital of Lower (northern) Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 18, 'Tehaphnehes. Tahpanhes, 10 miles (16 km) west of Pelusium (Jer. 2:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 30, 20, 'eleventh year … seventh day. April 19, 586 B.C. A restatement of previous oracles against Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 31, 1, 'Highly symbolic picture of the fall of the proud Pharaoh (Hophra) and his multitude, compared to the cutting down of a majestic tree (which first represented Assyria, and then Egypt; vv. 2–3, 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 31, 1, 'eleventh year … first day. June 11, 586 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 31, 15, 'hell. A poetic term for the grave. Pit. Another poetic term for the grave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 32, 1, 'A lamentation (see note on 27:1–36) for Pharaoh (Hophra), using the figure of a dragon (or crocodile) being captured and slain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 32, 1, 'twelfth year … first day. March 3, 585 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 32, 10, 'Babylon is God’s agent of judgment (see note on 26:2–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 32, 17, 'twelfth year … fifteenth day. March 17, 585 B.C. (No month is given, but assumed to be the 12th as in the previous oracle.) The seventh of Ezekiel’s oracles against Egypt. the Pit … hell. See note on 31:15–18. Highly symbolic prophecy, in which dead nations and their leaders are personified, and represented as speaking out of the Pit or Sheol (poetic references to the grave). Far from supporting continued survival after death in some kind of shadowy existence in the underworld, this symbolic oracle emphasizes that those great nations which terrorized the land of the living will one day be destroyed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 32, 24, 'Elam. Country east of Assyria, in modern Iran; see Is. 11:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 32, 26, 'Meshech and Tubal. See 38:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 32, 30, 'princes of the north. See 38:6, 15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 1, 'Ezekiel’s ministry now takes a new direction. After divine judgment comes God’s grace and promise of the restoration of His people in the messianic age (see Introduction). God’s program is primarily about healing and rebuilding healthy relationships as well as about renovating places and giving blessings. It is a message of hope, and Ezekiel also plays a pastoral role to comfort God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 1, 'Ezekiel’s ministry now takes a new direction. After divine judgment comes God’s grace and promise of the restoration of His people in the messianic age (see Introduction). God’s program is primarily about healing and rebuilding healthy relationships as well as about renovating places and giving blessings. It is a message of hope, and Ezekiel also plays a pastoral role to comfort God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 1, 'watchman. See note on 3:17. Ezekiel’s role as a watchman is repeated and intensified (see the triple usage of the term here). He must call people to prepare for the return of God’s presence and His glory. This section concludes with a double appeal for repentance. Restoration starts with God’s call for a change of thinking, of being, and of doing things. Responsibility does not lie only upon the prophet, but also upon his audience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 13, 'Righteousness is a gift from God and needs to be always freshly accepted and lived. Self-centeredness and prideful confidence lead to destruction. God never punishes the innocent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 21, 'The first fugitive from Jerusalem announces the fall of Jerusalem after his arrival on January 8, 585 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 25, 'Dietary laws, idolatry, murder (violence), and adultery are enumerated on the same level as serious moral offenses to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 29, 'all their abominations. All their detestable things; a summary of unacceptable practices (18:13) which are a serious offense to God. The list includes idolatry (5:11; 7:20; 11:18, 21; 33:26), adultery (22:11), and violence (8:17). Outside of Ezekiel the list includes child sacrifice (Deut. 12:31; 2 Kin. 16:3), eating of unclean food (Deut. 14:3), misuse of power (Prov. 16:12), false weights (Deut. 25:13–15; Prov. 11:1), wages of prostitution (Deut. 23:18), pride of heart (Prov. 16:5), and the prayer of a lawbreaker (Prov. 28:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 33, 31, 'Here is the reason why God’s people had to go through the exile: theirs was a fake and superficial religion that did not change the heart.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 34, 1, 'Charges against selfish and irresponsible shepherds (leaders) and misbehavior of sheep (people); God will judge all, and promises to put His Shepherd over His flock.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 34, 23, 'I will establish one shepherd over them. A passage of messianic hope; a prediction about the new Davidic Shepherd who will take care of God’s people, pasture them, and rule over them as a Prince. This good Shepherd will make a covenant of peace with them, and provide a land, safety, and blessing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 34, 30, 'Unique expressions of the covenant relationship between God and His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 35, 1, 'A prophecy against Edom, who is a type (symbol) of a long adversary of God’s people. God will defend His followers; and Seir (a synonymous word for Edom), because of his hatred, violence, and pride, will be destroyed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 1, 'God’s blessing will fall upon His people, their land will be restored, and waste places will be rebuilt and repopulated, while judgment will fall on the nations (vv. 1–7). This section is a counterpart to chap. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 1, 'mountains of Israel. Representation of the land of Israel. Restoration of the land invokes the creation theme and covenant blessings of Lev. 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 22, 'Theological core of the entire book which enumerates God’s actions for His people. It is an expansion on 11:16–21 and is similar to many thoughts found in Ps. 51 (see notes there).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 23, 'when I am hallowed in you. When God’s holiness is demonstrated through His people before other nations (20:41; 28:22, 25; 39:27; Lev. 22:32); for the negative aspect of vindication, see Ezek. 38:16, where the identical phrase is used; only the plural “you” is changed to singular. God is seen as holy and just when His people live according to His word. Only in this way can they live to His glory, be good witnesses for Him, and unbelievers can see that faith works (Gal. 5:6). For the opposite, on profaning God’s name, see Ezek. 36:20–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 24, 'See 11:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 25, 'God will cleanse them; it means He will forgive their iniquities (Ps. 32:1–2; 51:1–2, 7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 26, 'See 11:19; 18:31. A heart transplant is needed. This change can be accomplished only by God’s miraculous action, by the endowment of His Spirit (Ps. 51:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 27, 'I will … cause you to walk in My statutes. God will move His followers to obedience through the power of His Spirit. This is a unique declaration about obedience as a result of God’s working in humans through the Holy Spirit. Thus, obedience is not our achievement or performance, but a consequence of letting God work in us. Alone, we are not able to follow Him (Josh. 24:19). Power to overcome evil and live in harmony with His commandments comes from a source outside of us. Only the Spirit of God can transform hearts and enable people to observe His laws and instructions (see Ps. 51:11–12). What God requires He also provides. On COVENANT obedience to God’s commandments, see Ex. 24:3–8; Deut. 28; 29; John 14:15; 1 John 2:3–6; 5:2–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 28, 'The covenant relationship between God and His people will be maintained, and God will provide His blessing (11:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 36, 31, 'God’s amazing grace leads to repentance (see Rom. 2:4). you will remember … you will loathe yourselves … Be ashamed and confounded for your own ways. Expressions for being aware of one’s own fragility and sinful condition, and being called to a life of daily repentance (see 16:61–63; 18:23, 30–31; 20:43; Ps. 51:5, 17; 130:4, 8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 37, 1, 'Ezekiel’s third vision, though the term is not used here as it is for three other visions (1:1; 8:3; 40:2), describes a resurrected nation. God’s word and breath (Spirit) brings life to the dry bones (37:4–5). It is the imagery about God who will resurrect the dead, exiled nation (v. 11) from the grave into life, giving them their land (v. 12), and a new spiritual life through His word and Spirit (vv. 12, 14; Titus 3:4–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 37, 15, 'The twelfth, the last, and the only positive sign-action (see note on 24:15–27), and the only one in the second section of the book (chaps. 25–48) regarding one nation (two sticks put together) under one Davidic King, the Messiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 37, 23, 'God will deliver His people from idolatry and cleanse them. they shall be My people, and I will be their God. The covenant formula and the heart of the special relationship between God and His people (11:20; 14:11; 34:30; 36:28; Gen. 17:7–8; Ex. 6:7; 29:45; Lev. 26:45; Jer. 7:23; 24:7; 31:33; 32:38; Zech. 8:8; 10:6; 2 Cor. 6:16; Heb. 8:10; Rev. 21:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 37, 24, 'David My servant shall be king over them. The fifth and the last messianic prophecy where the Davidic King, the Messiah, is called also the Shepherd and Prince (v. 25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 37, 26, 'covenant of peace. See 34:25. The purpose of God’s covenant is to establish peace. peace. It is more than absence of conflict; it is a comprehensive well-being of a person, a nation, or a country. everlasting covenant. God’s covenant of grace is one and eternal. For more on God’s everlasting COVENANT, see Is. 55:3; 61:8; Jer. 32:40; Heb. 13:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 37, 27, 'My tabernacle also shall be with them. God’s climactic and very last promise of what He will do for His people as the fulfillment of the covenantal blessing of Lev. 26:11, followed by the final vision of the New Temple and the New City (Ezek. 40–48), but interrupted by Gog’s prophecy in chaps. 38–39. It is stated twice that the sanctuary will be “in their midst forevermore” (37:26, 28). For the covenant formula, see v. 23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 1, 'These chapters form a literary unit and contain a prophecy about Gog, of the land of Magog. The principal message is transparent: In the future (38:8, 16) Gog will launch, from the far north (38:6, 15; 39:2), his antagonistic campaign with his allies (38:2–8) against Israel after the Jews have returned from the Assyrian/Babylonian captivity (38:8, 12; 39:23, 25, 27–28) and are living securely in their land (38:8, 14; 39:26). Then the divine judgment (38:18–22; 39:2–6, 17–20) will destroy Gog and his confederacy upon the mountains of Israel (39:4, 15); thus Israel (39:7, 22, 28) and all nations will know that the Lord is God (38:16, 23; 39:6b, 7, 13b, 21, 28) and His holiness will be vindicated (38:16b, 23; 39:7, 27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 1, 'These chapters form a literary unit and contain a prophecy about Gog, of the land of Magog. The principal message is transparent: In the future (38:8, 16) Gog will launch, from the far north (38:6, 15; 39:2), his antagonistic campaign with his allies (38:2–8) against Israel after the Jews have returned from the Assyrian/Babylonian captivity (38:8, 12; 39:23, 25, 27–28) and are living securely in their land (38:8, 14; 39:26). Then the divine judgment (38:18–22; 39:2–6, 17–20) will destroy Gog and his confederacy upon the mountains of Israel (39:4, 15); thus Israel (39:7, 22, 28) and all nations will know that the Lord is God (38:16, 23; 39:6b, 7, 13b, 21, 28) and His holiness will be vindicated (38:16b, 23; 39:7, 27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 2, 'against Gog. Gog is a mysterious enemy of God and His people, whose identity is unclear probably by plan. The prediction of attack and defeat of Gog and his allies, however, never occurred in history, even though it could have happened under different circumstances. But this potential historical situation serves as a type (symbol) for a threefold fulfillment:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 3, 'Thus says the Lord GOD. The statement appears seven times (vv. 3, 10, 14, 17; 39:1, 17, 25), thus dividing chaps. 38–39 into seven parts after the introduction (38:1–2): (1) presentation of Gog and his allies (38:3–9); (2) Gog’s pride and plot (38:10–13); (3) Gog coming against Israel (38:14–16); (4) destruction of Gog’s forces by God’s judgment (38:17–23); (5) destruction and burial of Gog and his allies (39:1–16); (6a) Gog’s confederation at God’s sacrificial feast (39:17–22); (6b) Israel in exile for their sins (39:23–24); (7) restoration of the fortunes of Israel after captivity (39:25–29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 4, 'What is first mentioned as God’s action (38:4–16; 39:2) turns into a description of Gog’s own evil device (38:10). He deliberately planned to destroy Israel (vv. 10–12), and his pride led to his own destruction (see the stress on “I” in vv. 11–12). Gog’s pride and destruction closely reflects Lucifer’s pride and fall as described in Is. 14:12–15 (compare with Ezek. 28:17–19). It is also similar to the hardening of Pharaoh’s heart, which is attributed to both Pharaoh (plagues 1–5) and God (plagues 6–10; see note on Ex. 4:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 5, 'There are five nations listed as Gog’s allies: Persia, Ethiopia, Libya, Gomer, and Togarmah (representing the northern extreme of the world known to Israel), besides Meshech and Tubal (the southern extreme) already mentioned in v. 2. Thus, there are altogether seven nations in confederation whose names are derived from Gen. 10:2–3, 6, and Ezek. 27:10, 13–14. The number seven plays a significant role in this prophecy and the entire book of Ezekiel, symbolizing completeness or totality, and in this specific context points to a universal conspiracy, a world plot against Israel. The phrase “Thus says the Lord GOD” is used seven times; “for seven years” (39:9) the weapons of defeated Gog’s army will be used for fuel; the seven months are needed for burying the bodies (39:12, 14). Previously in the book, there are prophecies against the seven foreign nations (chaps. 25–32); the prophet utters seven oracles against Egypt (29:1–16, 17–21; 30:1–19, 20–26; 31:1–18; 32:1–16, 17–32); and there are seven woes expressed in the book (13:3, 18; 16:23 [twice]; 24:6, 9; 34:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 8, 'Gog and his allies attack a land (see also vv. 11, 16), the mountains of Israel (see 39:2), “My people Israel” (v. 16), and the “land of Israel” (v. 18). After many days … In the latter years. Ezekiel uses three time expressions—“after many days” (v. 8); “in the latter years” (v. 8); and “in the latter days” (v. 16)—which give a general future-time framework to this prophecy.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 38, 16, 'in the latter days. This time expression is used 12 additional times in the Hebrew portion of the OT—Gen. 49:1; Num. 24:14; Deut. 4:40; 31:29; Is. 2:2; Jer. 23:20; 30:24; 48:47; 49:39; Dan. 10:14; Hos. 3:5; Mic. 4:1. It is also used once in the Aramaic portion of the book of Daniel (Dan. 2:28) and basically means “in the after(wards) of the days,” “in the following time,” “in the future,” “in days to come,” “in those future days,” or “in the latter days.” It is without any specific time reference to the time of the end. The meaning depends on the application of the context. The prophecy about Gog’s invasion against God’s people is to be fulfilled in the future (as seen from the perspective of the prophet Ezekiel) after Israel returns from Babylonian captivity (39:23, 25, 27). “Gog and');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 39, 20, 'The idea that the slain will become a sacrificial meal is also found in Is. 34:5–11; Jer. 46:10; Lam. 2:21–22; Zeph. 1:7–8; and Rev. 19:17–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 40, 1, 'The final vision of Ezekiel’s book, matching chaps. 1–11 (see Introduction, “Outline,” and chart below). Whereas chaps. 1–11 highlighted the trial judgment aspect of the Day of Atonement, the vision of chaps. 40–48, given on the very Day of Atonement (see note on 40:1), highlights the companion theme—restoration/cleansing of the sanctuary. The vision has three main divisions: (1) New Temple (40:1–43:11); (2) New Temple Torah (43:12–46:24); and (3) new land surrounding the temple (47:1–48:35). The close connection between chaps. 1–11 and chaps. 40–48 is seen in the literary pattern of block parallelism, in which items in the first section match items in the second in the same order.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 40, 1, 'The final vision of Ezekiel’s book, matching chaps. 1–11 (see Introduction, “Outline,” and chart below). Whereas chaps. 1–11 highlighted the trial judgment aspect of the Day of Atonement, the vision of chaps. 40–48, given on the very Day of Atonement (see note on 40:1), highlights the companion theme—restoration/cleansing of the sanctuary. The vision has three main divisions: (1) New Temple (40:1–43:11); (2) New Temple Torah (43:12–46:24); and (3) new land surrounding the temple (47:1–48:35). The close connection between chaps. 1–11 and chaps. 40–48 is seen in the literary pattern of block parallelism, in which items in the first section match items in the second in the same order.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 40, 1, 'twenty-fifth year. October 22, 573 B.C. beginning of the year. Rosh Hashana. The only appearance of this term in the Hebrew Bible. Based upon a fall-to-fall calendar for calculating the civil year (see Introduction, “Title, Author, and Date”), it indicates the seventh month. In later Jewish practice, and perhaps already in Ezekiel’s day, the term Rosh Hashana denoted the first day of the seventh Jewish month, New Year’s day. tenth day. The tenth day of the seventh month is the Day of Atonement: Ezekiel received the vision of a cleansed/restored sanctuary on the very day that the sanctuary was annually cleansed (Lev. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 40, 2, 'Ezekiel, as a new Moses (see note on 43:12–46:24) is shown a vision of the “pattern” of the New Temple. He views: (1) exterior features of the temple compound (40:5–27), (2) interior features of the compound (40:28–46), and (3) dimensions of sacred space (40:27–42:20). Then Ezekiel is shown the return of the Lord to the temple (43:1–9) and is told to describe its pattern to the people of Israel (43:10–11).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 40, 2, 'very high mountain. Reminiscent of Mount Zion (see 17:22–23; 20:40), the heavenly mountain of God (28:14), and Mount Sinai (43:12; Ex. 25:9–18; Is. 2:2–4). See Rev. 21:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 40, 5, 'cubit and a handbreadth. The shorter cubit was about 1 1/2 feet (0.5 m). The long (royal) cubit (consisting of a cubit and a handbreadth) was about 20 1/2 inches (52 cm), which was the more ancient standard of measurement (see 2 Chr. 3:3). In chaps. 40–48');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 41, 1, 'The Holy Place and Most Holy Place have the same inner dimensions as in the Solomonic temple (see 1 Kin. 6:16–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 41, 22, 'altar … of wood. Either the altar of incense (see 23:41), or the table for the Bread of the Presence (see Ex. 25:23, 30; Lev. 24:6). The fact that other items of furniture are not mentioned is no indication that they were missing; the vision highlights things that are somewhat different than in the Solomonic temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 43, 2, 'The high point of Ezekiel’s last vision; the Lord is returning to His temple. east. God returns from the same direction to which He left (see 10:18–19; 11:1, 23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 43, 5, 'filled the temple. See Ex. 40:34–35; 1 Kin. 8:10–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 43, 10, 'pattern. Meaning “perfection, ideal” (only found elsewhere in 28:12), and implying a type of temple that is beautiful and exemplary in appearance. ashamed. Or “humiliated.” The purpose for showing the people the plans for the glorious New Temple was not for them to celebrate the return of the Lord, but in order for them to acknowledge their responsibility in causing His departure, to realize that they are sinners, invited into God’s presence as an act of divine grace. A balanced focus on the sanctuary always leads to spiritual awareness of sin and grace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 43, 12, 'the law of the temple. The New Temple “Torah.” This section deals with the various rituals involved in the restored temple worship, focusing especially upon modifications from the Mosaic ritual prescribed in the Pentateuch. The return from exile is a New Exodus (20:32–44), and Ezekiel functions as a New Moses, being shown the “pattern” for the building of the sanctuary (see Ex. 25:9, 40) and receiving legislation concerning various needed additions and modifications in the temple ritual and personnel for the returned exiles. The major apparent contradictions between Ezekiel’s and Moses’ “Torah,” as vigorously discussed by later Jewish rabbis, are found in Ezek. 44:31; 45:18–20; and 46:6–7 (see notes on these passages).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 43, 13, 'cubit. About 20 1/2 inches (52 cm). The long cubit is a handbreadth longer than the Hebrew common or Egyptian cubit, about 17 1/2 inches (45 cm).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 43, 15, 'altar hearth. Literally, “Lion of God” or “mountain of God.” The altar was over 20 feet (6 m) high, including horns, made up of three slabs stacked in decreasing size, resembling a “mountain” (or an Egyptian pyramid or Babylonian ziggurat).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 44, 3, 'prince. Civil ruler of the future Israelite kingdom, using language of leadership from Sinai (Ex. 22:28; 34:31; 35:27; Lev. 4:22; and over 60 times in Numbers). Not a specific reference to the coming Messiah here, since the prince would offer a sin offering for himself (Ezek. 45:22) and would have sons (46:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 44, 9, 'See Ex. 12:43–51.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 44, 18, 'sweat. Very practical legislation for the comfort of the priests as they ministered.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 44, 22, 'The ordinary priest could not take a widow for a wife, a further restriction from Levitical law (Lev. 21:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 44, 31, 'Prohibitions that in Ex. 22:31 and Deut. 14:21 applied to all Israelites have been regarded here as restricted only to the priests, but this general prohibition is probably here given special emphasis with regard to the priests, as in Lev. 22:8, without limiting it to them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 45, 18, 'This ritual was probably part of the one-time inauguration services of the sanctuary (see 43:18–26; see Ex. 29:35–36; 40), not a monthly or yearly ritual contradicting Num. 28:11 or Lev. 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 45, 21, 'Passover and the “feast” (of Tabernacles) are mentioned here probably because there is a special added sacrifice specified for the prince during these feasts that was not included in the Mosaic legislation. The other annual feasts presumably resumed without substantial change.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 46, 3, 'Worship services on the Sabbaths and New Moons explicitly involve the congregation as well as the sanctuary personnel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 46, 6, 'Different number of animals than specified in the Mosaic law (Num. 28:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 46, 17, 'year of liberty. Jubilee (see Lev. 25:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 47, 1, 'Describes the new land surrounding the temple. There are many parallels between this section and the description of the New Jerusalem in Rev. 21–22 (see Introduction).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 47, 1, 'Describes the new land surrounding the temple. There are many parallels between this section and the description of the New Jerusalem in Rev. 21–22 (see Introduction).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 47, 1, 'The description implies a future last-day dimension of the entire vision, with a supernatural transformation of nature that exceeds historical possibilities. It is spiritually fulfilled in Christ and the NT church with the blessings of the new covenant flowing out to the world (John 7:37–38); literally it is fulfilled in the New Earth (Rev. 22:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 47, 1, 'east. Orientation toward the east, as with the Mosaic sanctuary and Solomonic temple (Ex. 36:20–30; 1 Kin. 7:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 47, 10, 'En Gedi. A spring midway along the west side of the Dead Sea. En Eglaim. Location uncertain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 47, 13, 'Approximates the borders listed in Num. 34:1–12, and those at the time of David and Solomon (except nothing east of the Jordan is included).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 47, 21, 'Note the inclusion of strangers in the inheritance, in harmony with the universality proclaimed by prophets like Isaiah (Is. 56:3–8), and fulfilled in the NT (Eph. 2:12–14; Col. 3:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 48, 1, 'The allotment of the land is divided into 13 equal and parallel strips: one portion to each tribe plus a sacred district at the center with the New Temple and the New City. Seven tribes are located above the sacred portion and five below that district. The description of each tribe’s location goes from Dan to Gad and territorially from the north to the south. The emphasis is on each neighbor’s borders from the east to the west. The New Holy Land would stretch from approximately the entrance of Hamath above Tyre and Sidon, as the northern border, to the Wadi of Egypt in the south, and from the Jordan River (including the Sea of Galilee and the Dead Sea), which would form the eastern border, to the Mediterranean Sea as the western border (similar to Num. 34). For details see Ezek. 47:13–23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 48, 9, 'The Lord’s special presence will be at the very center of the most holy district allotted for the priests in the new sanctuary of the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 48, 11, 'the priests of the sons of Zadok. Described as consecrated and faithful (see 40:46; 43:19; 44:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 48, 15, 'The New City will be in the center of the southern strip of the holy district, thus separated from the New Temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 48, 28, 'the Great Sea. The Mediterranean Sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 48, 31, 'The 12 gates of the New City (3 on each side) have the names of the 12 tribes (see Rev. 21:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ezequiel', 48, 35, 'The name for the New City is not New Jerusalem (as expected) but THE LORD IS THERE, which is not only a new name for the capital but also the theme and the climax of the whole book. The book begins with the vision of the holy God coming to and being present in Jerusalem and the temple (1:4, 28; 8:1–4). After engaging in a trial judgment over the people of God, the temple, and Jerusalem (chaps. 8–11), the Lord left the temple and Jerusalem (8:6; 10:18; 11:23–24), but was with His people in Babylon. In the final section of the book, the Lord comes back to the New Temple (43:3–5) and stays with His people in the New Capital and the New Holy Land forever.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 0, NULL, 'Daniel, whose name means “God is my judge” (a theme of the book), was taken captive to Babylon in 605 B.C., when that kingdom overpowered his nation of Judah. He and his Jewish friends remained loyal to God, who blessed them with special wisdom so that they rose to high positions in the Babylonian government. Daniel continued as a top administrator into the beginning of Medo-Persian rule, after Babylon was conquered in 539 B.C.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 0, NULL, 'The Lord used Daniel and his friends, and the miracles associated with them, to impress upon a series of kings the fact that He was in charge and they were accountable to Him. Central to the book is the theme that God is sovereign over the nations, even when His people are oppressed, and He ultimately delivers those who are loyal to Him. This theme is explicitly introduced in 2:20–23 and emphasized in chapters 4–5 by repetition of the concept: “the Most High rules in the kingdom of men …” (4:17; compare vv. 25, 32; 5:21). Chapters 4–5 form the center of a symmetrical (chiastic) structure:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 0, NULL, 'Many scholars maintain that the book was written in Daniel’s name by someone living much later. They think it was composed in the second century B.C., in response to persecution of the Jews by the Seleucid (Greek) King Antiochus IV Epiphanes, who reigned from 175 B.C. to 164 B.C. Such scholars regard the “little horn” symbol of Daniel 7–8 as referring to Antiochus. This idea was already implied by the apocryphal book of 1 Maccabees, which used the language of Daniel predicting an action by the “little horn” (Dan. 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) to describe what Antiochus did (1 Macc. 1:54). Those who accept the late authorship of Daniel believe that the accurate predictions in the book were really history written after the events had happened, as though it were prophecy, so that Jews in the second century B.C. would believe that the author had received messages from God. In this way, scholars try to avoid the biblical teaching that God knows the far distant future and has revealed it to human beings in Scripture. They also avoid identification of the evil “little horn” power as Roman, coming after the Greek/Macedonian Empire that followed Medo-Persia, which was preceded by Babylon (see notes on Dan. 2; 7–8).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 0, NULL, 'I.      Narratives of Jewish exiles and Gentile kings (1:1–6:28)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 1, 'Interactions among Daniel and his friends and with their God demonstrated to proud monarchs that the Lord rules the world and delivers His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 1, 'third year of the reign of Jehoiakim. 605 B.C. Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon. Nebuchadnezzar’s father, Nabopolassar, died in July or early August, 605 B.C. So Daniel was probably taken captive while Nebuchadnezzar was still the crown prince. His first official year of reign began in the spring of 604 B.C., in the fourth year of Jehoiakim (compare Jer. 25:1). Regarding events around this time, see 2 Kin. 23:36–24:6; 2 Chr. 36:5–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 2, 'some of the articles of the house of God. The temple remained standing until 586 B.C. But Nebuchadnezzar took some of its valuable items and deposited them in the treasury of his own deity. This introduces conflict between the true God of heaven and human power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 7, 'he gave Daniel the name Belteshazzar. Nebuchadnezzar intended to absorb the young men into his culture by giving them Babylonian identities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 8, 'he would not defile himself. Because Daniel resolved to remain loyal to the Lord, he could not allow himself to be absorbed into Babylonian culture in ways that conflicted with holiness, including eating the meat of “unclean” species of animals (Lev. 11; Deut. 14; compare Gen. 7:2, 8–9, 20). There were probably additional problems with the Babylonian diet: meat may have been slaughtered without properly draining its blood (Gen. 9:4; Lev. 17:10–12; compare Acts 15:20, 29), and food and drink may have been offered to idols (compare Num. 25:2; Acts 15:20, 29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 12, 'vegetables … water. This vegetarian diet would avoid all religious problems (see note on v. 8). It would also be noticeably healthier, which is why Daniel and his friends were allowed to continue it (v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 17, 'Daniel had understanding in all visions and dreams. Daniel was rewarded for his faithfulness to holiness by receiving special access to knowledge from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 1, 20, 'ten times better. An expression meaning “very much better.” than all the magicians and astrologers. Daniel and his friends were trained as wise, scholarly counselors to the king alongside various kinds of experts in the use of supernatural forces that were believed to affect human beings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 1, 'second year of Nebuchadnezzar’s reign. This could have been just after Daniel and his friends completed their three years of training (1:5, 18). Their training would have started before the first official year of Nebuchadnezzar’s reign began in the spring of 604 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 4, 'Here the original language changes from Hebrew to Aramaic.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 28, 'what will be in the latter days. Compare Num. 24:14; Ezek. 38:16. Nebuchadnezzar’s dream moved forward to the time when God Himself would set up His kingdom on earth (Dan. 2:37–44).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 31, 'a great image. Appropriate for representing a succession of temporary human governments.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 38, 'you are this head of gold. The fact that Daniel identified the first segment of the image as Nebuchadnezzar’s Neo-Babylonian kingdom (605–539 B.C.), of which he was the current monarch, provides a historical anchor point for identifying the following kingdoms (compare 8:20–21).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 39, 'after you shall arise another kingdom. The fact that a kingdom came after Nebuchadnezzar’s head of gold indicates that the head also represented a kingdom, which included Nebuchadnezzar (605–562 B.C.) and several lesser Neo-Babylonian rulers who followed him (562–539 B.C.). inferior to yours. This inferiority, represented by the decreasing value of metals, could not refer to geographical extent, military might (note increasing hardness of metals; compare v. 40; 7:7), or overall wealth, which increased from one empire to another. However, it could refer to the glory of its capital city (Babylon is legendary), culture, and especially moral worth, as human rulers became progressively more corrupt, arrogant, and rebellious against God. a third kingdom of bronze. Alexander the Great (336–323 B.C.) conquered Medo-Persia and set up a Macedonian/Greek Empire (330–30 B.C.; see 8:20–22). which shall rule over all the earth. Alexander’s empire was notable for its vast extent. But “all the earth” does not literally refer to the totality of planet Earth. Nor does it mean “the then-known world.” This and all other empires had limits, and those inside knew about peoples outside, with whom they warred or traded. There were great empires elsewhere, such as a Chinese empire that rivaled the Roman Empire in extent and power. But the empires in Dan. 2 were those that controlled the land of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 40, 'the fourth kingdom shall be as strong as iron. Between 168 and 30 B.C., Rome took over the Greek kingdoms into which Alexander’s empire was divided (compare 8:8, 22). Rome was the strongest and lasted the longest of the four kingdoms represented in Daniel’s image.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 41, 'the kingdom shall be divided. The Roman emperor Constantine divided the empire into western and eastern parts (A.D. 326). Then the western part fell to barbarians and was divided into a number of European countries, which have varied in strength. The eastern part of the Roman Empire continued as the Byzantine Empire, which was conquered by Muslims in A.D. 1453.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 43, 'they will not adhere to one another. The remnants of the Roman Empire have never been successfully reunited, despite major efforts for centuries, through warfare and political marriages (“the seed of men”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 44, 'in the days of these kings the God of heaven. Sometime during the current period of divided powers, God will suddenly intervene (as represented by the stone smashing the image; vv. 34–35, 45) and set up His eternal kingdom throughout the whole world. The NT identifies Christ’s Second Coming as the cause of this sudden and universally recognized end of human civilization as we know it (Matt. 24:23–51; Rev. 19:11–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 2, 46, 'present an offering and incense to him. Apparently Nebuchadnezzar tried to treat Daniel as divine (compare Acts 14:11–18), although he praised Daniel’s God (Dan. 2:47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 1, 'height … sixty cubits and its width six cubits. About 90 feet (27 m) high and 9 feet (2.7 m) wide.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 2, 'dedication of the image. Consecration of the statue as a sacred object (vv. 12, 14, 28; compare dedication of the Lord’s temple in Ezra 6:16–18). Apparently it represented Nebuchadnezzar, the “head of gold” (Dan. 2:38), and/or his gods (3:12). Rebelling against God’s power, it seems that he twisted his dream (chap. 2) to serve his own ambition.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 3, 'So the satraps … all the officials. The list gives an atmosphere of pomp and prolongs the story to heighten suspense.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 5, 'with all kinds of music. Compare the use of instruments for Israelite worship (2 Sam. 6:5, 15; 1 Chr. 25) or for religious signals (Num. 10:10). Nebuchadnezzar had an entire ancient symphony orchestra. The names of three of the instruments in Dan. 3 are Greek and the others are Persian. This is not surprising because Babylonia was in contact with other cultures.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 6, 'burning fiery furnace. Perhaps it was like a kiln for baking bricks. The fact that Nebuchadnezzar threatened such punishment could imply that he doubted whether everyone was sufficiently loyal to him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 13, 'in rage and fury. Understandable because Nebuchadnezzar was trying to stage a 100 percent show of absolute personal loyalty to himself from his subjects.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 15, 'who is the god who will deliver you …? Intended as a rhetorical question with the answer: no god. But the young Jews had a different answer (v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 18, 'But if not. They would trust God, even if He allowed them to be killed (compare Job 13:15; Matt. 26:39; Acts 7:59).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 25, 'the Son of God. Or “a son of the gods,” that is, a divine being. This was from Nebuchadnezzar’s religious perspective. The being was Christ, the pre-incarnate. He literally fulfilled God’s promise to be with His people in order to deliver them: “When you walk through the fire, you shall not be burned, nor shall the flame scorch you” (Is. 43:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 3, 28, 'Blessed be the God. The king realized that the young men’s disobedience was due to their amazing loyalty to their unique deity, not to rebellion against his human rule of Babylon. So he protected the honor of their God and promoted them (vv. 29–30). who sent His Angel. The word “angel” means “messenger” and can be divine (Judg. 6:11–13—“Angel of the Lord”). It is not restricted to created beings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 1, 'To all peoples, nations, and languages. This chapter begins as a letter by Nebuchadnezzar to the inhabitants of his kingdom. He expresses interest in the well-being (“peace”) of his subjects and acknowledges the supremacy of the Lord’s power (compare chap. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 4, 'I, Nebuchadnezzar. The king recounts the experience that led to his praise for Daniel’s God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 8, 'in him is the Spirit of the Holy God. Compare Gen. 41:38; Num. 27:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 13, 'a watcher, a holy one. A supernatural being (angel) serving God.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 27, 'let my advice be acceptable to you. Daniel hoped the divine sentence could be put off if the king would walk humbly with God by doing justice and loving mercy (compare Mic. 6:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 30, 'great Babylon, that I have built. Accurate from a human perspective. Nebuchadnezzar was an incredible builder and made Babylon, with its hanging gardens, walls, palaces, and temples into a wonder of the ancient world. But pride led him to arrogantly overlook his debt and accountability to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 32, 'seven times … his nails like birds’ claws. Such growth implies a considerable length of time, implying that the “seven times” of Nebuchadnezzar’s madness were seven years. The Aramaic word for “time” also means year.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 4, 37, 'praise and extol and honor the King of heaven. The message of God’s sovereignty finally sank in when Nebuchadnezzar was first removed from his throne and then restored (compare 2 Chr. 33:11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 1, 'Belshazzar the king. Belshazzar co-reigned with his father, Nabonidus (555–539 B.C.), during the last ten years of his reign. Because only Nabonidus was mentioned in later Greek historical writings as the last king of Babylon, scholars regarded Daniel as inaccurate and unreliable. However, discovery of Babylonian texts has confirmed the existence and kingship of Belshazzar. So Daniel records accurate information that was lost for many centuries. This helps scholars establish an understanding of when the book of Daniel was written. great feast. Belshazzar and his subjects thought they were secure within the immense fortifications of their city. By hosting a feast, Belshazzar apparently tried to maintain his people’s morale at a time of crisis, while the Medo-Persian army was approaching.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 2, 'command to bring the gold and silver vessels. Under the influence of alcohol that clouded his judgment (compare Lev. 10:9–10; Matt. 14:6–11), Belshazzar commanded improper use of the Lord’s temple vessels, apparently to boost confidence by showing that the Babylonian gods were superior.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 5, 'fingers of a man’s hand appeared and wrote. The Babylonians would likely have viewed this as a deity recording their fate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 10, 'The queen. This could be the queen-mother, Belshazzar’s grandmother, who may have belonged to Nebuchadnezzar’s harem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 11, 'King Nebuchadnezzar your father. Compare v. 22. Here “father” may mean “grandfather,” “step-grandfather,” or simply “previous king of Babylon.” We do not know the precise relationship between Belshazzar and Nebuchadnezzar.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 23, 'the God who holds your breath in His hand. God’s creative power continually sustains His creatures (compare Job 12:10; Ps. 104:14–15; 145:15–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 25, 'MENE, MENE, TEKEL, UPHARSIN. These words are in Aramaic, but share some roots with the Babylonian language. They refer to units of weight that were used for money: “mina, mina, shekel (1/60 of a mina), and half-minas [or half-shekels].” The handwriting on the wall was a riddle that would sound to us something like “dollar, dollar, penny, and half-dollars.” Weights were appropriate in this context because they were weighed in balances (v. 27). Money was appropriate because all the wealth of Babylon could not ransom it from destruction because it was morally bankrupt. Beyond the initial impact of the riddle, the underlying roots of the words for weights refer to “numbering,” “weighing,” and “cutting/dividing.” Daniel explained how God applied these actions to the ruler and kingdom that were under divine judgment (vv. 26–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 29, 'third ruler. Why not the second ruler, which is what Pharaoh made Joseph (Gen. 41:40)? Perhaps because Belshazzar himself was second ruler to his father Nabonidus, with whom he was co-reigning. So this verse may imply the existence of Nabonidus, although the book of Daniel does not directly mention him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 5, 31, 'Darius the Mede received the kingdom. Cyrus II the Great (reigned 559–530 B.C.; see also 1:21; 6:28; 10:1), the Persian who founded the Achaemenid Medo-Persian Empire, conquered Babylon in 539 B.C. “Darius the Mede” is not mentioned, at least not by this name, in ancient writings outside the Bible that have been discovered so far. This could be a throne name for Gaubaruwa (Babylonian Gubaru/Ugbaru; Greek Gobryas), the general who took Babylon for Cyrus and then assisted Cyrus by ruling Mesopotamia for a short time until he died.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 6, 7, 'whoever petitions any god or man for thirty days, except you. The plotters against Daniel appealed to the king’s ego in order to trap Daniel. den of lions. We know that some ancient Near Eastern monarchs kept zoos of exotic animals. In this case the animals would be used for a terrifying form of execution.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 6, 8, 'law of the Medes and Persians, which does not alter. The notion that human laws are infallible and cannot pass away is contrary to the teaching of Daniel that only God’s government is eternal (chap. 2). Human laws are invalid when they conflict with divine law (compare Acts 5:29), because only God possesses total knowledge, wisdom, and power. Notice that the Medes and Persians were already a combined empire with one set of laws (see also Esth. 1:19) before they conquered Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 6, 10, 'toward Jerusalem. Daniel prayed toward Jerusalem, the place of the temple and its altar (compare 1 Kin. 8), even though they had been destroyed. Today there is no equivalent holy place of divine-human mediation on earth. Rather, Christians are to pray directly to God in heaven, where Christ, our High Priest, is mediating for us (Heb. 4:14–16). as was his custom. Daniel changed nothing about his daily practice of worshiping and praising God, even though he knew that it directly violated the new law. Like his friends when they refused to worship Nebuchadnezzar’s image (chap. 3), he ignored the human consequences of putting loyalty to God first.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 6, 14, 'the king … was greatly displeased. He regretted having bound himself to a commitment that he could not change. Compare Esth. 3:12–15; 8:8 (irrevocable decree to destroy the Jews) and the problem of binding oneself through an oath (Judg. 11:31; 1 Sam. 14:24; Matt. 5:33–37; 14:6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 6, 24, 'they brought those men who had accused Daniel. Unlike those who accused his friends on the basis of accurate information (3:8–12), Daniel’s accusers conspired to trick the king into destroying him, even though he was innocent of any wrongdoing, simply because they were jealous. So they were really false accusers who deserved the punishment that they intended for Daniel (compare Deut. 19:16–21). their children, and their wives. Horrifying as this appears today, destruction of family members was part of the punishment on the guilty men (compare divine retributive justice in Num. 16:27, 31–33). This is partly due to the communal group view of life prevalent at that time and a measure to prevent retaliation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 6, 25, 'Then King Darius wrote. Compare proclamations of Nebuchadnezzar regarding the true God (3:29; 4:1–3, 34–37).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 1, 'These chapters record several visions and explanations of future history, which Daniel himself received.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 1, 'These chapters record several visions and explanations of future history, which Daniel himself received.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 1, 'In the first year of Belshazzar. Earlier than the events recorded in chaps. 5 and 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 2, 'four winds. This expression can refer to the four directions of the compass (compare 8:8; Jer. 49:36; Zech. 2:6). The winds are stirring up the sea from all directions to create turbulence, presumably representing strife (e.g., Rev. 7:1). Great Sea. The Mediterranean (e.g., Num. 34:6–7; Josh. 1:4; Ezek. 47:10, 15, 19–20), representing the turbulent human environment from which the human empires arise (compare Is. 17:12; Rev. 17:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 3, 'four great beasts. These carnivorous animals symbolize powers that reach the top of political strength and authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 4, 'lion, and had eagle’s wings. Just as Babylon was earlier represented by gold, the most noble metal (chap. 2), it is symbolized here by a combination of the most noble carnivorous animal and bird: the lion and the eagle. a man’s heart was given to it. This depiction of Babylon receiving characteristics of a human being and becoming less like a wild animal of prey could reflect the experience of oppressive Nebuchadnezzar, who became humane and more intelligent when he humbled himself before God (chap. 4). Compare Ezek. 11:19; 36:26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 5, 'bear … raised up on one side … three ribs. This lopsided stance reflects the unequal power between the older Median and younger but stronger Persian sides of the Medo-Persian Empire (compare 8:3, 20). The three ribs seem to represent the bear’s victims (such as Lydia, Babylon, and Egypt, which Medo-Persia conquered).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 6, 'leopard … four wings of a bird … four heads. Four parts reflect the fact that Alexander the Great’s Macedonian/Greek Empire divided into four kingdoms after he died (Macedonia, Pergamum, Egypt, Syria; compare 8:8, 21–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 7, 'a fourth beast, dreadful and terrible. This monster is not like any species of animal that Daniel could identify. Its teeth are of iron, the strong and crushing metal that symbolized the fourth kingdom in chap. 2 (v. 40): Rome. ten horns. In symbolic usage in the Bible, horns represent power against enemies (Deut. 33:17; 1 Sam. 2:1, 10; 2 Sam. 22:3, etc.). Here the ten horns are connected to Rome. In Dan. 8 we will see that horns on a symbolic animal represent powers that make up an empire (8:3, 20) or parts into which the empire can split (8:8, 21–22). The vast Roman Empire was made of many parts, into which it divided after the city of Rome fell to the barbarians in A.D. 476, as Dan. 7:24 predicted (compare 2:41–43).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 8, 'a little one. As the youngest horn, it starts out small, but grows greater than the other horns (v. 20; compare 8:9—literally, “a horn from smallness”). before whom three of the first horns were plucked out. The rise of the little horn power after the breakup of the Roman Empire involves the fall of three other post-Roman powers (see 7:24). eyes like the eyes of a man, and a mouth speaking. Discernment and communication like those of a human being (compare v. 4). pompous words. Boastful speech against the Most High God Himself (compare v. 25; for “Most High” as God, compare 3:26; 4:2, etc.). The little horn power is not only proud; it has a strong religious agenda and is blasphemous.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 9, 'Ancient of Days. Appearing only in this chapter in the entire Bible, this unique expression is a title for the Most High God, who lives forever and whose kingdom is everlasting (compare 4:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 10, 'The court was seated, and the books were opened. This heavenly court, over which God Himself presides, responds to the challenge posed by the “little horn.” Books are records that are relevant for determining the verdict and indicate that each case is carefully investigated. See vv. 22, 26–27 for two more glimpses of this judgment scene. The Bible touches upon books used in JUDGMENT in Mal. 3:16–18; Luke 10:20; Rev. 3:5; 20:12; see also Ex. 32:33; Phil. 4:3. For the type of evidence used in the JUDGMENT, see Matt. 7:24–27; 16:27; Rom. 2:6, 13; James 2:12. For the various stages of God’s JUDGMENT, see note on Rom. 14:10, 12. For the JUDGMENT in the heavenly sanctuary, see note on Dan. 8:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 11, 'the beast was slain. The heavenly judgment condemns the horn, along with the Roman beast from which it has arisen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 12, 'dominion taken away, yet their lives were prolonged. Peoples who composed the earlier empires remain, even though the empires are gone.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 13, 'One like the Son of Man. A person like a human being approaching the throne of God in heaven to receive God’s eternal kingdom on planet Earth (compare 2:44). This must be Christ, the divine Son of God (Matt. 26:63–64; 27:54; Mark 1:1), who also spoke of Himself as the Son of Man and is coming to earth again to set up His eternal kingdom (Matt. 16:27; 19:28). By itself the expression “son of man” can refer to an ordinary human being (Ezek. 2:1, 3, 6, 8), but the person in Dan. 7 is special. When Christ comes to earth a second time, His kingdom will already have been determined in terms of the people who belong to it (Matt. 25:31–46; Rev. 22:11–12). So the judgment in heaven that determines human destinies (Dan. 7:9–14) is before Christ’s Second Coming (compare Rev. 14:6–7). Notice that Dan. 7:13 distinguishes between two divine personalities belonging to the holy Trinity: Christ the Son approaches the “Ancient of Days,” who must be God the Father (compare Matt. 6:9; 7:21; 28:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 7, 22, 'judgment was made in favor of the saints of the Most High. Two opposing');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 3, 'a ram which had two horns. Representing the Medo-Persian Empire, with two unequal horns representing the Median and Persian concentrations of power (v. 20; see note on 7:5). Daniel saw this during the reign of Belshazzar (8:1; compare chap. 5), but Babylon was not represented in the vision because its time was almost finished. In this chapter the biblical writer returns to using the Hebrew language (from Aramaic in 2:4–7:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 4, 'pushing westward, northward, and southward. Medo-Persia came from the east (of Palestine) and expanded in these directions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 5, 'a male goat came from the west. Symbolizing the Macedonian/Greek Empire, with a single large horn representing its first king (v. 21), namely, Alexander the Great. without touching the ground. Depicting the great speed (compare 7:6—leopard with wings) of Alexander’s conquests.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 7, 'rage. The Greeks wanted revenge for what the Medo-Persian Empire had done to them, including the unsuccessful invasion of their territory by Xerxes in 480–479 B.C. (compare 11:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 8, 'the male goat grew very great. Compare 2:39, where the third kingdom of Nebuchadnezzar’s image, representing Macedonia/Greece, “shall rule over all the earth.” when he became strong, the large horn was broken. Alexander died at the height of his power in 323 B.C., when he was not quite 33 years old. four notable ones came up. After a power struggle, Alexander’s empire divided into four Greek kingdoms: Antigonid Macedonia, Attalid Pergamum, Ptolemaic Egypt, and Seleucid Syria.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 9, 'out of one of them. Out of one of the four directions, represented by the “four winds of heaven” (see note on 7:2), just mentioned at the end of 8:8. These are the directions toward which Alexander’s empire was divided. a little horn. The next actor on the stage of history, even greater than the empires of Medo-Persia and Alexander the Great. It would become exceedingly great. The horn is not simply another Greek power or ruler (such as Antiochus IV Epiphanes), but one that takes over all the Greek kingdoms. It is not part of an animal, which would be impossible for a literal horn in real life. However, the vision is symbolic. The little horn is the same as the power symbolized in chap. 7. In chap. 8, however, the horn first expands horizontally from the northwest toward the south, east, and the “Glorious Land” (land of Israel; compare 8:9; 11:16). These are the directions toward which Rome expanded to build its empire, taking over one after another of the Greek kingdoms. Dan. 8:10–12 finds the horn growing vertically against heaven in a religious attack on God, His people, and His truth. Thus the little horn of Dan. 8 has both a secular and religious phase.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 10, 'host of heaven. Elsewhere this expression can refer to the vast array of heavenly bodies that shine in the sky (Deut. 4:19; Neh. 9:6) or to the Lord’s heavenly attendants (1 Kin. 22:19). Dan. 8 explains that those of the “host of heaven” and “stars” whom the little horn casts down and tramples are “the mighty, and also the holy people” (v. 24). So these are humans on earth who belong to God and therefore are regarded as belonging to heaven (compare 12:3). Because they are on earth, they are vulnerable to attack (compare 7:21, 25; Rev. 13:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 11, 'the Prince of the host. The Commander of the armies of heaven is the same being as the Son of Man in 7:13. When Joshua met and worshiped the Prince/Commander (same Hebrew word) of the Lord’s army, this Being told Joshua to take off his sandals because he was standing at a holy place (Josh. 5:13–15). This is what the Lord commanded Moses at the burning bush (Ex. 3:5). So the Commander is clearly divine, the Lord Himself. Created angels do not allow themselves to be worshiped in this way (Rev. 22:8–9). By exalting himself as high as the Lord, the little horn shares the aspirations of Lucifer, who wants to exalt his throne above the stars of God and “be like the Most High” (Is. 14:12–14). and by him the daily sacrifices were taken away. Meaning, “and from Him (the Prince of the host) he (the little horn) removed the regularity/the daily” (compare 11:31; 12:11). The word “sacrifices” is often supplied by translators but is not in the original text (see also “sacrifice,” “offering,” or “burnt offering” in other translations). In the context of the earthly sanctuary/temple, the Hebrew term for “regularity” (sometimes referred to as the “continual” or “daily”), applied to a variety or system of regular rituals (lamps, burnt offerings, incense, placing bread) that were performed daily (Ex. 27:20; 29:38; 30:7–8) or weekly (Lev. 24:8). It designated the daily service of the priest in the court and inside the Holy Place of the tabernacle. It is used here to refer to the mediation of the Prince of the hosts in the heavenly sanctuary (see Heb. 7:25). The horizontal phase of the little horn represented by the Roman Empire extended beyond the destruction of the Jerusalem temple in A.D. 70. The religious phase of the little horn interfered with the daily ministration of Christ for us in the heavenly temple (see Rev. 13:6). the place of His sanctuary was cast down. Compare Rev. 11:2, where the court of God’s temple, where His earthly people come to worship, is trampled by the nations/Gentiles for 42 months (= 1,260 days = 3 1/2 years or “times”). This is the period of domination and persecution by the little horn of Dan. 7:25, during which the mediation of Christ in the heavenly temple was obscured through a human system of mediation (see also Rev. 13:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 8, 12, 'Because of transgression. The little horn here casts God’s truth to the ground and opposes God’s sacred provisions ministered out to sinners by our High Priest through');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 1, 'In the first year of Darius. At the beginning of Medo-Persian rule.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 2, 'Jeremiah … seventy years. Daniel was studying the prophecies of Jeremiah, his older contemporary. Jeremiah recorded God’s word that the land of Judah would lie desolate under Babylonian domination for 70 years until Babylon would be punished, and then the Jews could return from exile (Jer. 25:11–12; 29:10). Now Babylon had been punished and a new empire had just taken over (Dan. 5–6). So according to what Jeremiah had said, the end of the 70 years had come and it was time for the Jewish captives to be set free. So Daniel should have been overjoyed!');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 3, 'fasting, sackcloth, and ashes. Rather than rejoicing, Daniel was mourning (compare Esth. 4:1, 3; Ps. 35:13; Is. 58:5; Jer. 6:26). His problem was the vision of Dan. 8, which showed that there would be a long time of difficulty for God’s people before the sanctuary would be restored. It is clear that he did not understand the 2,300 days (8:14) to be literal days (less than 6 1/2 years), or he would not have been upset. He naturally would have assumed that the sanctuary was the temple in Jerusalem and associated its justification with restoration after his people would be released from exile. So he would have concluded that because of their sins, God may have decided to postpone the release from Babylonian captivity until far in the future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 4, 'prayed … made confession. At the end of the covenant blessings and curses in Lev. 26, God had promised that if His exiled people would humble themselves, repent, and confess their sins and those of their ancestors in this way, He would restore them from exile (Lev. 26:40–45).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 5, 'we have sinned. Not “they have sinned.” Daniel was a righteous man and the Bible does not record any sin that he committed, but he identified himself with his people in this prayer of intercession.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 17, 'Your sanctuary … Your city and Your people are called by Your name. Like Moses (Ex. 32:11–13; Num. 14:13–19), Daniel interceded with the Lord on behalf of His people by appealing to God’s character of mercy and need to preserve His reputation in the world. Daniel was also concerned for their city of Jerusalem and God’s sanctuary there on its “holy mountain” (Mount Zion), where He had put His name/identity (compare Deut. 12:5, 11, 21). Concern for the people and sanctuary links Daniel’s prayer to his vision of Dan. 8, where the little horn power oppresses the people and acts against the sanctuary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 21, 'evening offering. Or “evening grain offering” (2 Kin. 16:15; Ezra 9:4–5). The angel Gabriel (compare Luke 1:19, 26) appeared to Daniel in human form about the time when the last regular sacrifice of the day would have been performed if the temple had been functioning (compare Ex. 29:39–42; Lev. 6:20). This was an appropriate time, as the psalmist said: “Let my prayer be set before You as incense, the lifting up of my hands as the evening sacrifice” (Ps. 141:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 23, 'understand the vision. There is no vision in chap. 9, so this refers to the vision in chap. 8. More precisely, the Hebrew word for “vision” here is the one used in 8:26 for the conversation that speaks of the 2,300 days (8:13–14), which had particularly perplexed Daniel (8:27). Gabriel had come to explain this time element in order to relieve his distress over the apparent conflict between the 70 years of Jeremiah and the 2,300 days of his own vision.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 9, 24, 'Seventy weeks. The word for “week” means “group of seven,” which can be a cycle of days or of years. Here it is obviously years because the events span a much longer period than 490 (70 times 7) literal days. Such matters, for example, as rebuilding Jerusalem could never be fulfilled in 70 weeks or days (less than 1 1/2 years). Thus it is understood as “weeks of years.” For cycles of seven years, see also the sabbatical year periods in Lev. 25. The number “seventy” links this time unit to the 70 years prophesied by Jeremiah (see note on Dan. 9:2). Just as the nation of Judah suffered 70 years of exile as a result of rebellion and sin, it would now have 70 sabbatical year cycles (490 years) at the end of which rebellion will finish and there will be an end of sins. determined. This word appears only here in the Hebrew Bible. But in rabbinic writings its basic meaning is to “cut off” from something longer, and this can be an expression for “determined.” In this verse, both the basic and extended meanings would apply: The 490 years are “cut off” from the longer unit of 2,300 “days” = years (8:14), and this period of 490 years is “determined” for the Jewish people and the holy city of Jerusalem. “Determined” means that it applies especially to them. Daniel was concerned about the fate of his people, their city, and the temple during the 2,300 “days,” and here is the answer. To finish the transgression. The Hebrew word for “transgression” means rebellious sin (compare the verb “rebel” from the same root in 1 Kin. 12:19; 2 Kin. 1:1; 3:5). For the combination of terms for “transgression,” “sin,” and “iniquity”/culpability (but with “iniquity” listed first), see also Ex. 34:7; Lev. 16:21. The 490 years were to be a time for resolving the problems of moral faults that had plagued God’s people throughout their history as a nation. This was to take place through the Messiah. Along with remedies for the three evils, three good things were to come about: lasting righteousness, sealing up (confirmation of) prophecy, and anointing the Most Holy. to anoint the Most Holy. Consecrating a sanctuary to God for its sacred function (compare Lev. 8:10–12). This consecration would take place centuries after that of the Second Temple, which was built and dedicated soon after the return from exile (Ezra 6). So this must be consecration of another temple (see note on Dan. 9:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 10, 1, 'Daniel’s final great vision traces the story of God’s people down to the end of time. We should not get bogged down in details (like chap. 11) but remember that the purpose was to encourage God’s people with the fact that God is leading and that their final salvation is sure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 10, 1, 'third year of Cyrus. After the writing of at least chap. 1, which ends with the note that “Daniel continued until the first year of King Cyrus.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 10, 2, 'mourning. Daniel was mourning and humbling himself by practicing self-denial (compare v. 12; Lev. 16:29; Ezra 8:21; Ps. 35:13–14) through a partial fast and by abstaining from putting oil as lotion on his skin. He did this because he sought further understanding from God (Dan. 10:12), no doubt regarding the fate of his people (compare v. 14; chap. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 10, 5, 'man clothed in linen. An awesome heavenly being (compare Rev. 1:13–16, of the glorified Christ) appearing in human form, who came to enlighten Daniel as Gabriel had previously done (Dan. 9:20–27). In Ezek. 9:2–3, 11; 10:2, 6–7 a servant of God, apparently an angel, similarly wears linen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 10, 13, 'prince of the kingdom of Persia. This could be Cambyses, the son of Cyrus (559–530 B.C.), who may have been resisting God’s decision to restore His people. However, the fact that an angel from God would need to fight against this “prince” (v. 20) suggests spiritual warfare against an evil supernatural being (compare Eph. 6:12). If so, there must be demons (evil angels) struggling to influence and control human governments (compare Dan. 10:20—“prince of Greece”; John 12:31—“ruler of this world”) in order to counter the purposes of God. The Lord revealed this battle between good and evil near the beginning of rule by the Persians, whose Zoroastrian religion emphasized conflict between good and evil supernatural forces. Michael, one of the chief princes. Michael (meaning “Who is like God?”) was the name of various men in the OT (Num. 13:13; 1 Chr. 5:13; etc.). But it is also the name of an exalted heavenly person, who is a chief prince (Dan. 10:13). Elsewhere he is the great guardian of Daniel and his people (v. 21; 12:1), “the archangel” who disputed with the devil (Jude 9), and the commander of God’s angels, who defeated Satan and his angels and drove them out of heaven (Rev. 12:7–9). So it appears that this Michael is “the Prince of the host,” who is Christ (see note on Dan. 8:11). Christ Himself could serve as a messenger = angel (not necessarily a created being) from God (compare note on Judg. 6:11—“the Angel of the LORD,” who is the Lord Himself).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 10, 14, 'latter days. The revelation about to be given (chaps. 11–12) reaches into the distant future (compare note on 2:28), like the prophecies of chaps. 2, 7–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 10, 16, 'touched my lips. Compare Is. 6:5–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 11, 1, 'Daniel 11 is one of the most difficult chapters in the Bible to interpret in detail. Events described are often condensed and vague, which leads to many differing interpretations. There is a majority agreement on the first part of the chapter, but the further one goes in the chapter, the greater becomes the disagreement even among those of the same theological view. While the details are not all clear, the basic message is that God is the Lord of history and in spite of earthly rulers and their actions, God wins over evil in the end and saves His people. Daniel 11 has many parallels with Daniel 8 and 9 (see chart of similarities on p. 1134).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 11, 2, 'three more kings will arise in Persia. Unlike the presentations in chaps. 2, 7, and 8, this prophecy consists of literal language covering future history in much greater detail, with no symbolic representation of powers by an image, animals, or other such items. Daniel received the prophecy during the reign of Cyrus (10:1) and the next three Medo-Persian kings were Cambyses (reigned 530–522 B.C.), Gaumata (“false Bardiya/Smerdis”; 522), and Darius I (522–486). Daniel 11 describes rulers without naming them, so we must identify them by their descriptions. While the first part of the chapter is fairly clear, as things move on it is progressively harder to identify the exact people and events referred to. he shall stir up all against the realm of Greece. The fourth king after Cyrus was Xerxes I (486–465 B.C.), who mounted a massive invasion of Greece that failed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 11, 3, 'Then a mighty king shall arise. This is Alexander the Great (336–323 B.C.), whose empire was divided into four Greek kingdoms (v. 4; compare 8:8, 21–22). Notice that once Persia under Xerxes loses to Greece (11:2), chap. 11 skips over all the remaining Persian kings (from 465 B.C. on) and goes directly to the Greek Empire (beginning in 330).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 11, 5, 'king of the South. Ptolemy I Soter (305–285 B.C.) of the Ptolemaic Greek dynasty that ruled Egypt, to the south of Palestine. one of his princes. Seleucus I Nicator (305–281 B.C.), a general of Alexander who became ruler of Babylon. He was driven out and fled to Egypt, but Ptolemy helped him to regain Babylon. Seleucus greatly expanded his domain and founded the Seleucid dynasty with a capital in Syria, to the north of Palestine. So members of this dynasty were kings of the north in chap. 11. The fact that Seleucus was under Ptolemy for a time explains why he is called “one of his princes.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 11, 6, 'they shall join forces. Antiochus II Theos (261–246 B.C.) and Ptolemy II Philadelphus (285–246) made an alliance, sealed through the marriage of Antiochus to Berenice, the daughter of Ptolemy II. king of the North. Refers to the Seleucus territory located north of Israel. Note that God’s people were stuck in the middle between North and South.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 11, 9, 'shall come to the kingdom of the king of the South. In 242 B.C., Seleucus II Callinicus (246–225) tried but failed to get revenge for the invasion of Ptolemy III.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 11, 10, 'his sons shall stir up strife. The sons of Seleucus II, who waged war against Egypt, were Seleucus III (225–223 B.C.) and Antiochus III the Great (223–187).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 12, 1, 'Michael shall stand up. Michael (probably Christ; see note on 10:13), the heavenly guardian of God’s people, stands up when the apostate power and its allies are intent on destroying many. This implies that their target is God’s people. Shortly before Christ’s Second Coming, God’s end-time people will threaten the powers that be (compare Rev. 13) by proclaiming a gospel message of allegiance to the Creator God and accountability to Him during the time of His judgment (Rev. 14:6–12). time of trouble. Compare Rev. 15–16. written in the book. Christ’s “Book of Life,” recording the names of those who receive eternal life (compare Rev. 3:5; 13:8; 17:8; 20:12, 15; 21:27). By the time Michael stands up, the judgment process of decision involving books/records (Dan. 7:10) has ended and all that remains is to administer positive and negative verdicts (compare 12:2; Matt. 25:31–46; Rev. 22:11–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 12, 2, 'those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake. This is the resurrection at Christ’s Second Coming (1 Thess. 4:15–17; but see Rev 1:7), which further indicates that Michael is Christ. For more on the RESURRECTION in the OT, see Job 19:25–26; Is. 26:19. In the NT, see Acts 24:15; 1 Cor. 15:51–55; 1 Thess. 4:13–18. On DEATH described as a sleep, see John 11:11–14; Acts 7:60; 1 Thess. 4:13–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 12, 4, 'shut up the words, and seal the book until the time of the end. Daniel mentions that only two parts of his prophecy would be sealed until the time of the end: (1) the 1,260-day prophecy of 12:7, 9; 7:25 and (2) the 2,300-day prophecy of 8:26, 14. Both of those prophecies are featured in Rev. 11–14 after the little book of Rev. 10 is opened. shall run to and fro. Many will search the Scriptures diligently in order to understand the prophecies of Daniel. knowledge shall increase. In the end-time there would be increased knowledge so that the prophecy could be understood.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 12, 7, 'a time, times, and half a time. See note on 7:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 12, 11, 'The two time periods mentioned here are related to but slightly different from the time period in 7:25 and 12:7, indicating that they begin, end, and overlap in approximately the same era.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Daniel', 12, 13, 'will arise to your inheritance. Daniel was given the wonderful assurance that he would enjoy ultimate salvation (compare 2 Tim. 4:7–8).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 0, NULL, 'Hosea’s name means “salvation” or “God has saved.” The name may be a shortened form of Hoshea, or Joshua, meaning “Yahweh has saved” (as in Num. 13:8). The corresponding Greek form of that name is Jesus. Thus Joshua, Hoshea, Hosea, and Jesus are four variants of the same name. The name of the last king of Israel’s northern kingdom was also Hoshea.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 0, NULL, 'The theme of Hosea is “the anguish of steadfast love.” The book is about Hosea’s love life and God’s steadfast love. If a person’s love is temporary and fickle, then unreturned love, or rejection, presents no major problem. Weak love can simply seek another object. However, when love is steadfast, rejection and unfaithfulness cause great anguish. Since love goes on while its object no longer returns any love, tremendous suffering results. Israel’s unfaithfulness causes unspeakable anguish to God’s heart of steadfast love. Hosea’s own experience of having an unfaithful wife teaches him the pain in God’s heart over Israel. Hosea’s heart is broken because his wife breaks their marriage covenant. God’s heart is broken because Israel breaks their covenant with Him.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 0, NULL, 'Superscription (1:1)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 1, 'Hosea. The name (“God has saved”) captures a surprising outcome of a book that predicted the tragic fall of the northern kingdom of Israel in 722 B.C. The kings mentioned are Uzziah (792–740), Jotham (750–732), Ahaz (735–716), and Hezekiah (716–687), all of the southern kingdom, and Jeroboam (793–753), from the northern kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 2, 'For God’s covenant relationship with Israel understood in terms of marriage see also Is. 43; 49; 51; 54:5–8; 62; 63; Jer. 2; 3:1–3; 31:32; Ezek. 16; 23. In the NT, the Apostle Paul uses marriage to describe the relationship between Christ and His church (Eph. 5:25–33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 2, 'Hosea’s broken family is an illustration of God’s relationship with Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 2, 'a wife of harlotry … children of harlotry. These expressions and the phrase “the land has committed great harlotry” suggest that the message of the book of Hosea is woven into the story of the prophet’s family life. Harlotry here is a symbol of unfaithfulness. Unfaithfulness is failure to fulfill one’s covenant obligations. Some see the experience of Hosea as not literal, but the realism of the story suggests that it is not mere allegory. The betrayal of Hosea by his wife mirrors the apostasy of Israel. The identification of idolatry with harlotry is already present in Ex. 34:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 4, 'Like the names of Isaiah’s children, the names of Hosea’s children are connected to the subject matter of the book (see Is. 7:3; 8:1–3, 18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 4, 'Jezreel. The space that separated Galilee from Samaria, a place associated with Jehu’s extermination of Omri’s dynasty. He killed Jezebel and Ahab’s seventy sons (2 Kin. 9–10) here. The name Jezreel, which means “God scatters,” was an anticipation of the end of the reigning dynasty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 6, 'Lo-Ruhamah. The name of the second child means “no mercy” and is symbolic of the relationship between God and Israel, especially through the experience of exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 7, 'I will have mercy. A prediction of the miraculous deliverance of Judah in 701 B.C. from the hands of Sennacherib and from the siege of the Assyrian army (see 2 Kin. 19:32–36; Is. 37:29). Tragically, Judah will not have learned the lesson. Less than a hundred years later Judah will be defeated by King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon in 605 B.C. and the leadership of the population exiled. Later (586 B.C.) the temple will be destroyed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 9, 'Lo-Ammi. Means “not my people.” The name of the third son heightened the seriousness of Israel’s condition and seems to cancel the covenant relation. It was, however, a shock therapy to foster repentance and return to the Lord. The next section illustrates this feature.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 1, 10, 'Yet. This section begins with the conjunction which connects it to what precedes. It is the reversal of God’s distancing from His people in v. 9. God’s people will be called “sons of the living God.” This time it is another reversal, that of the children’s names. Beyond the reversals, the promise to Abraham in Gen. 22:17 may be echoed here. This text, moreover, points to a time certainly after the collapse of the northern kingdom, and possibly after that of the southern kingdom, when God’s people will live up to their identity: from “children of Israel” to the promotion of “sons of the living God.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 2, 'Bring charges. God makes a case by giving the reason why Israel is to be punished. Harlotry and adultery will bring drought and famine. Forgetfulness of God and idolatry call for judgment. Nevertheless, when the execution of the judgment is expected to be more specific, the text shifts to courting romantic language (v. 14). Against all expectations, God uses the language of love to woo His people back to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 8, 'Baal. Can mean “lord,” “master,” “owner,” or “husband.” Baal worship was a constant temptation for the Israelites when they settled in Canaan. As early as the time of the judges, Baal worship was attested among the Israelites (Judg. 8:33). Baal was the Canaanite god of storm and fertility. Because of the region’s dependence on rain, unlike river-based cultures like Egypt and Mesopotamia, the worship of Baal was prominent. He was believed to provide rain. To restore the truth about who the real provider was, God makes a case that all the resources Israel enjoys come from Him (see vv. 8, 22–23; 6:3; 14:5–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 11, 'New Moons, her Sabbaths. Festivals celebrated the first day of each month (Num. 10:10). Interpretations differ on whether this is a reference to the weekly Sabbath, to other sabbaths or holy days in the Jewish festival year, or to both.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 13, 'punish her. Baal worship is spiritual adultery, unfaithfulness to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 14, 'allure her. A significant shift from the expectation of punishment to a beautiful declaration of love occurs in this verse. The verbs of the previous verses were all verbs of separation: “take back” v. 9; “uncover” v. 10; “will also cause … to cease” v. 11; “will destroy” v. 12; “will punish” v. 13. Then, beyond all expectations, God promises to allure, to speak comfort, and to restore.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 15, 'the Valley of Achor. Near Jericho. It literally means “the valley of trouble” but it becomes a door of hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 16, 'that day. Short for “day of the Lord” and is found in several prophetic writings with various nuances in reference to its meaning. In this setting it has a positive connotation. It will be a day of restoration of the covenant of creation, a covenant of peace and safety.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 2, 19, 'righteousness and justice, in lovingkindness and mercy. These attributes echo the revelation of God to Moses (Ex. 34:6–7). All the attributes are used to describe a faithful husband. As a result of God’s revelation, His people are led to know Him. The word lovingkindness is one of the key concepts that characterizes God’s fundamental feelings towards His people. It fundamentally expresses the idea of a bond, a commitment. In the secular domain it could designate friendship, solidarity, or loyalty. When used in reference to God, it designates His faithfulness to His covenant and to the goodness of the outcome. It can legitimately be translated as love, or covenant love.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 3, 1, 'This section expresses the depth of God’s love for His people. Since love cannot be forced, only those who return to God will benefit from God’s covenant blessings. Meanwhile, the time of purging Israel’s unfaithfulness is referred to as being “many days.” The expression “children of Israel” in the last verse pictures a last-day group beyond the existence of political Israel. These are individuals who express their allegiance to God and to the Messiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 3, 1, 'Go again. Hosea is called to go back to Gomer just as God takes Israel back after spiritual adultery. The raisin cakes played a role in Canaanite idol worship as well as in Israelite culture, although the connotation in this text is negative (see 2 Sam. 6:19; 1 Chr. 16:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 3, 2, 'fifteen shekels. About 6 ounces (170 g). one and one-half homers. About 10 bushels (330 l).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 3, 3, 'toward you. Gomer’s “stay with [Hosea] many days” was a parable of the exile of God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 4, 1, 'The choice of words to designate the group accused, namely “the inhabitants of the land,” and the choice of words to describe the root cause that justifies the lawsuit, namely a lack of faithfulness, all reflect the covenant background. The backsliding of God’s people away from the terms of the covenant results in a reversal of the privileges of election. Even the land and all that dwells in it suffer the curses of the covenant. Furthermore, the land suffers a kind of de-creation. It mourns (v. 3). The beasts of the field, the birds of the air, even the fish of the sea perish. (For similar lawsuits see Is. 3:13; Mic. 6:2.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 4, 1, 'There is no. Three virtues are listed. They are “truth” or faithfulness, in the sense of being truthful to our relationship with God; “mercy” or covenant love (see note on 2:19–20); and “knowledge of God.” To know God is ultimately to enjoy an intimate relationship with Him. It means embracing and participating in God’s way in all areas, aspects, and domains of life, whether it is in relating to people or to things. For a similar clear explanation of the centrality of these virtues, see Jer. 9:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 4, 2, 'The list of sins (swearing, lying, killing, stealing, and committing adultery) are all prohibited in the fundamental text of divine covenant guidelines, the Ten Commandments (Ex. 20:1–17; Deut. 5:7–21). The breaking of all restraint shows a deep lawlessness and impiety which perpetuates a cycle of violence and disdain for the sacredness of life leading to bloodshed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 4, 4, 'This section shows the depth of the problem God addressed through His prophet Hosea. The institutions that God established among His people to mediate His leadership are lacking the essential part that justifies their existence: the knowledge of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 4, 12, 'These verses specify idolatrous rites. A spirit of prostitution has caused them to turn to divination sacrifices on high places. These practices are unequivocally condemned in the covenant (divination in Deut. 18:10; 1 Sam. 15:23, Jer. 14:14; Ezek. 13:23; and worship on high places and under green trees in Deut. 12:2; see also 1 Kin. 3:3; 12:31–33; 13:32; 14:23; Is. 65:7; Jer. 2:20; 19:5; Ezek. 6:3, 6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 1, 'Religious and political leaders are indicted, and so is the nation as a whole. The accusation of prostitution is reiterated here, as are lack of knowledge of God and pride. As a consequence, God Himself experiences an exile from His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 1, 'Religious and political leaders are indicted, and so is the nation as a whole. The accusation of prostitution is reiterated here, as are lack of knowledge of God and pride. As a consequence, God Himself experiences an exile from His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 1, 'house of Israel. This expression and references to places like Mizpah and Tabor indicate that the prophet Hosea directed his messages mainly to the northern kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 3, 'defiled. The word often describes a person unfit to worship God. It is the opposite of the Hebrew word which means “clean” or “pure.” In the context of Hosea, it refers to moral uncleanness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 5, 'Judah … stumbles. Judah is not excluded from the indictments, as several mentions of Judah in this section show (vv. 5, 10, 12, 14; 6:4). V. 8 mentions Benjamin, often paired with Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 8, 'the ram’s horn. Or shofar. This horn was used to give a signal to warriors of the time to begin an attack or conquest (see Judg. 3:27; 7:20). Beth Aven. Means “house of wickedness.” It is used to replace the name Bethel which means “house of God.” Beth Aven is thus a derogatory designation, showing a failed destiny. The house of God, supposed to be showing righteousness and justice, has become a place for wickedness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 13, 'Assyria. Israel’s political alliances and maneuvering, such as those denounced in the book of Isaiah, occur also in the book of Hosea. The prophet is unequivocal: rescue and healing can only come from God, not from Assyria.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 5, 15, 'seek Me. God is portrayed waiting for His people to repent and search for Him. God is in exile away from His people. It illustrates that God dwells with the repentant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 6, 1, 'The desire to “return” to God seems acceptable. The people say the right words. God would even revive them in three days, they say. He will come to them like rain, most likely with showers of blessings. However, God’s response comparing their faithfulness to a morning cloud or like early dew that goes away shows a deeper problem. What God desires is mercy or covenant fidelity, not sacrifice; He desires knowledge of God more than burnt offerings. Jesus cited this text when confronting religious hypocrisy (Matt. 9:13; 12:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 6, 7, 'like men. The original text can also be rendered as “like Adam.” These verses expand on the charges God makes in relation to the transgression of the covenant. Priests supposed to be mediators of life became murderers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 6, 10, 'I have seen. What God saw among His people is labeled as a “horrible thing,” which is the same word used to describe shocking behavior (Jer. 5:30; 23:14; 29:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 7, 1, 'Expanding on the list of sins, the charge of prostitution and adultery are repeated, along with self-indulgent wine drinking and ignorance of the real issues that were undermining their very existence (v. 9). No wonder Ephraim is compared to a silly dove in v. 11. Lack of discernment makes them look for the wrong solutions. They rely on Egypt and Assyria instead of counting and waiting on the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 7, 7, 'their kings. Four of the six last kings of Israel were murdered (see Introduction, “Author and Date,” and 2 Kin. 15:8–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 8, 1, 'an eagle. The Assyrian invasion is compared to an eagle over the house of Israel. The metaphor of an eagle here is the same used in the covenant curse of conquest by an enemy in Deut. 28:49–50. The figure of attacking birds representing enemies is also used in Jer. 4:13; Lam. 4:18–19; Hab. 1:6–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 8, 4, 'not by Me. God’s disapproval of the setting up of kings shows that not all things that happened among God’s people bore His approval.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 8, 8, 'swallowed up. The exile is not only predicted but also pictured. God’s people lost their dignity. By choosing to trust in the Assyrians they end up serving them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 9, 3, 'the LORD’s land. The concept that the land belongs to the Lord is central to the Israelite worldview. In Lev. 25:23 the Lord explicitly stated that the land is His.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 9, 4, 'bread of mourners. Signifies that Ephraim’s religious sacrifices could not be accepted by God. They were unclean (see Num. 19:14; Deut. 26:14; Jer. 16:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 9, 9, 'Gibeah. See Judg. 19–20. The tribe of Benjamin was responsible for a civil war that stemmed from an event at Gibeah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 9, 10, 'Baal Peor. Refers to a dramatic episode of idol worship prior to the entrance into Canaan (Num. 25:1–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 10, 6, 'carried. This deportation prophecy was fulfilled. The Assyrian king Tiglath-Pileser conquered Israel and exiled part of the population to Assyria (2 Kin. 15:27–29). In 722 B.C., which marked the end of the northern kingdom, Shalmaneser V captured Samaria.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 10, 15, 'The name “Bethel,” also alluded to in 4:15, was a memorable place associated with the patriarch Jacob and the revelation of God to him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 11, 1, 'a child. Description of an exemplary father’s amazing care and the prodigal son’s waywardness. God’s love for His people contrasts with their lack of appropriate response to His solicitude, depicting a tragic and beautiful story at the same time. out of Egypt. Refers to Israel’s exodus from Egypt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 11, 8, 'Admah … Zeboiim. The two cities were located east of the Dead Sea and were overthrown when Sodom and Gomorrah were destroyed (Gen. 19:24–25). Here is one of the most amazing descriptions of God’s inner struggle in the Scriptures. churns. What happened within God’s heart is compared to an inner earthquake. The same word is used to refer to the destruction of the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah in Gen. 19:25. It is as if God experiences within His heart what Ephraim experiences in exile. God’s attachment to His people is that strong. The word translated “sympathy” can be translated “compassion,” a key virtue in God’s dealing with sinful and frail humanity. Paul calls God the “Father of mercies and … all comfort” (2 Cor. 1:3). The Gospels mention Jesus, the revelation of God’s heart, moved with compassion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 11, 10, 'after the LORD. Like many other passages in the prophetic writings, the voice suddenly shifts and the possibility of hope emerges as if to express God’s deep desire: the repentance of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 11, 12, 'Ephraim. The northern kingdom as distinguished from Judah, the southern kingdom, who was still walking with God. The episode referred to in this passage might correspond to the reign of Hezekiah. The seeking of international alliances fits that period in the history of God’s people. The expression “Ephraim feeds on the wind” is an allusion to worthless political moves. Only the Lord is faithful, reliable, and dependable. The word translated as “faithful” contains all these nuances. It is from the same root as the word “amen.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 12, 2, 'a charge. The similarities of Judah’s and Jacob’s sins of deceitfulness and distrust are brought to light. In the book of Hosea, the names of patriarchs or places are not chosen by chance. Jacob’s experience is alluded to both for his sin and repentance. God’s touching aim is to invite people to move on from Jacob’s initial sinful choices to his later experience of repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 12, 6, 'wait. Another refrain woven throughout the book of Hosea. People are called to patiently wait on God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 12, 7, 'Deceitful. Dishonest scales were considered an abomination to the Lord (Prov. 11:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 12, 8, 'rich. Ephraim’s self-sufficiency and self-deception will serve as a model for Laodicea in Rev. 3:17 when she says “I am rich, have become wealthy, and have need of nothing.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 13, 1, 'Ephraim. The largest tribe and leader of the northern kingdom, so much so it became a synonym of the northern kingdom itself. Famous Israelite leaders came from the tribe of Ephraim: Joshua (Num. 13:8; Josh. 24:29–30) and Jeroboam I (1 Kin. 11:26; 12:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 13, 4, 'These verses shift back to the inevitable judgment of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 13, 4, 'like a lion. The uses of images in the book of Hosea reflect the theology of the book. They are illustrations of the issues discussed. In this section God is referred to as a Shepherd caring for His flock in the desert. Then the images shift to that of a lion, a leopard, a bear robbed of her cubs, and a wild beast. This shift expresses God’s frustration at being ignored by the people He saved, led, and nurtured.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 13, 4, 'your God. In the midst of a serious accusation appear the amazing words of deliverance. God declares His intention to deliver.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 14, 1, 'This last chapter reviews all the major denouncements in the book and puts them in the setting of repentance and renunciation of the following: idolatry, reliance on military alliances rather than God, and apostasy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 14, 4, 'I will heal. It is fitting that a book that clearly announces the threat of judgment would finish with a note of hope, more specifically with an image signifying protection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 14, 5, 'The image of “dew” is connected to the idea of blessing (Deut. 33:13; Mic. 5:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 14, 7, 'his shadow. In restoration, God’s land stands as God’s shadow for His people and the nations. The last image pictures God as a green tree.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Oséias', 14, 9, 'The standards for righteousness are the ways of the Lord. How we relate to them determines if we are considered righteous or wicked.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 0, NULL, 'This book contains the prophetic message of Joel. The name Joel is common in the Old Testament, identifying about a dozen different people. It means “the Lord is God.” Other than the name of his father, Pethuel, we know nothing about the prophet besides what can be inferred from his book. The fact that we know so little about Joel means that the primary focus remains not on his person, but on his prophetic message—and that is just the way it should be for any prophet of God.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 0, NULL, 'The message of Joel, like that of his fellow prophet Zephaniah, is dominated by a single theme—the day of the Lord, a time of God’s visitation. However, the prophet unfolds his message in his own unique way. A terrible plague of locusts has devastated Judah’s agricultural output (1:4, 7, 11–12), leaving the people and cattle lacking food (1:16–18) and the temple lacking offerings (1:9). Such a disaster reminds Joel of the looming and imminent day of the Lord and prompts him to call for repentance (1:13–15).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 0, NULL, 'I.      Reflection on judgment past: the locust invasion foreshadows the day of the Lord (1:1–20)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 1, 'The word of the LORD. Though the book is usually known by the name of its author, the prophet Joel, the very first phrase in the Hebrew text more accurately describes the contents, highlighting the fact that though these are human words, they are also the words of the living God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 2, 'elders. Prominent members of society who played an important leadership role in Joel’s time (2:16) and are also mentioned in some other biblical books (2 Kin. 23:1; Jer. 26:17). the land. The southern kingdom of Judah (3:1, 20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 4, 'Four different Hebrew words are used to refer to locusts in this verse. However, it is unknown whether these words denote four different species of locusts, four different stages of development for one kind of locust (that is, from pupa to full-grown), or are synonyms simply used for variety. In any case, the picture is the same—total destruction of the food supply.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 6, 'The “nation” that is “without number” is a reference to the innumerable locusts that have invaded the land. Through metaphorical language likening the locusts to lions, the prophet refers to the locusts’ ability to devour vegetation and strip the trees bare of their bark.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 8, 'sackcloth. A coarse, dark cloth worn to express penitence (Jon. 3:5–8) or in times of mourning (Lam. 2:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 11, 'These verses reveal the extent of the damage wrought by the locusts, highlighting the destruction of two types of grain (“the wheat and the barley”), as well as five kinds of fruit (grapes from “the vine,” “the fig tree,” “the pomegranate tree”; dates from “the palm tree,” and “the apple tree”). The picture is of total agricultural devastation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 13, 'This “natural disaster” of the locust plague is in reality a spiritual crisis. The priests are challenged to exert spiritual leadership by humbling themselves before the Lord in self-denial and repentance, and by summoning all the people to the temple to “cry out to the LORD.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 1, 15, 'The destructive plague of locusts serves as a foreshadowing of the soon-coming day of the Lord, which is the main theme of Joel’s prophecy. As Joel and other prophets indicate (Amos 5:18–20; Zeph. 3:8–13), it will be a time of destruction for all who refuse to repent and a time of salvation for everyone who “calls on the name of the LORD” (2:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 1, 'trumpet. The shofar, or ram’s horn, was sounded by a watchman to warn the people of approaching danger (Jer. 4:5; Ezek. 33:3). My holy mountain. Refers to the hill on which the temple was located (Ps. 15:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 2, 'Joel’s description of the day of the Lord as “a day of darkness and gloominess, a day of clouds and thick darkness” is the same as Zephaniah’s (Zeph. 1:15). Darkness and clouds are often associated with the Lord’s presence (Deut. 4:11; Ps. 18:9, 11) and signify the judgment which is coming. A people come, great and strong, the like of whom has never been. Since the judgment described in chap. 2 is future and the locust plague of chap. 1 is past, Joel is probably prophesying about an invading enemy army, perhaps the Babylonians (depending on when Joel’s prophecy is dated). The parallels between the two chapters are appropriate, because in the ancient Near East the locust symbol was used in ancient artwork to represent soldiers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 6, 'The fear portrayed here is often described as present when people face divine judgment (Is. 13:8; Zech. 14:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 10, 'The sun and moon grow dark, and the stars diminish their brightness. Diminished light from the heavenly bodies is a characteristic feature of the day of the Lord (Is. 13:10; Zech. 14:6–7; Rev. 6:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 11, 'For the day of the LORD is great and very terrible; who can endure it? This rhetorical question is echoed in the searching query in the Bible’s last book: “For the great day of His wrath has come, and who is able to stand?” (Rev. 6:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 12, 'Turn to Me with all your heart. Divine judgment—or even the threat of it—is intended to prompt the Lord’s people to return to Him (Amos 4:6–11). The return is not to be merely external in nature, as indicated by “So rend your heart, and not your garments” (Joel 2:13). Rather, it is to be a sincere and wholehearted repentance and turning to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 13, 'He is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness. These words recall the Lord’s self-description in Ex. 34:6, a description stated after the sin of the golden calf and repeated elsewhere in Scripture to highlight the nature of Israel’s gracious God (Neh. 9:17; Ps. 103:8; Jon. 4:2). Since the Lord is full of love and mercy, there is reason for hope if they will but turn to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 14, 'Who knows if He will turn and relent. This question parallels that of the king of Nineveh in Jon. 3:9. It preserves divine sovereignty and indicates that God is not obligated to relent from sending punishment. As in Jonah (Jon. 3:10), so in Joel also (the verbs in Joel 2:18–19a are past tense verbs); sincere repentance leads to divine forgiveness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 20, 'the northern army. Mesopotamian armies, though their home was to the east of Judah, tended to attack from the north so as to avoid the difficulties of traveling across the huge desert located east of Judah. eastern sea. Probably denotes the Dead Sea, which is southeast of Judah. western sea. Refers to the Mediterranean Sea, which is to Judah’s west.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 23, 'The former rain, and the latter rain. The success of Judah’s agriculture was dependent on the autumn rains, occurring in September and October, and the spring rains in March and April.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 25, 'A complete reversal of the previous judgment takes place, resulting in praise to God and recognition of His incomparable nature.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 28, 'afterward. Indicates the end times. God promises to “pour out” His Spirit in abundant supply, not limiting the gift by gender (“your sons and your daughters”), age (“your old men,” “your young men”), or status (“menservants … maidservants”), thus fulfilling Moses’ prayer of long ago that all of God’s people would have the gift of the Spirit (Num. 11:29). Peter quotes Joel directly (Acts 2:16–21) at Pentecost, but that was not the ultimate, climactic, exhaustive fulfillment when the Spirit is poured out and people call on the Lord’s name in order to be saved. The signs of the end of the world in Joel 2:30–31 make it evident that its ultimate fulfillment will be at the end of earth’s history. It will include both an outpouring of the spiritual gifts in general and the gift of prophecy in particular (see Rev. 12:17; 19:10). For more on SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 2, 32, 'calls on the name of the LORD. Means more than merely articulating the right syllables or words. It refers to an attitude of humility and reverence towards God. As is the case elsewhere in Scripture, a “remnant” survives the purging judgment and becomes the nucleus for the new people of God (Zeph. 3:10–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 1, 'in those days and at that time. This phrase denotes the last days—the same time period indicated by “afterward” in 2:28. The Lord’s promise to “bring back the captives,” translated in some versions as “restore the fortunes,” is a commitment to fulfill the promise given centuries earlier through Moses (Deut. 30:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 2, 'the Valley of Jehoshaphat. This place name occurs in the OT only here and in v. 12. It is uncertain whether a location was known by that name in Joel’s time. Here the Lord will intervene on behalf of His covenant people and judge the nations for treating them with cruelty. This judgment is a fitting event for this locale, because Jehoshaphat means “the Lord judges.” He will do so, declares Joel, in abundant measure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 4, 'Tyre and Sidon … Philistia. All were involved in expressing hostile words or actions at the time of Jerusalem’s destruction by the Babylonians (Ezek. 25:15; 26:2; 28:21, 24). The Lord identifies with His people. What the nations have done to His people and His land has been done to Him, and He will punish those nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 8, 'The punishment will fit the crime, as those who sold people from Judah into slavery will themselves be sold to the Sabeans, an Arabian tribe known for being merchants (Ezek. 27:22–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 13, 'Using imagery that is later echoed in the last book of Scripture, the judgment of the wicked is likened to reaping the harvest with a sickle and treading grapes in a winepress (Rev. 14:15–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 14, 'the valley of decision. Was previously called “the Valley of Jehoshaphat” (vv. 2, 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 15, 'As earlier indicated, the heavenly bodies will be darkened on the day of the Lord (2:10, 31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 16, 'As is the case in other prophets, the judgment on the day of the Lord is described as a purging judgment that leaves behind a purified remnant who seek refuge in God (Is. 4:2–6; Zeph. 3:8–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Joel', 3, 21, 'For the LORD dwells in Zion. With this thrilling conclusion, which is similar to Ezekiel’s climactic phrase, “THE LORD IS THERE” (Ezek. 48:35), Joel brings down the curtain on his book with a declaration that signals bright hope for Judah’s future, notwithstanding their bleak present in the aftermath of the locust invasion.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 0, NULL, 'Amos came from Tekoa, a village in Judah located about ten miles south of Jerusalem and six miles south of Bethlehem (Amos 1:1). We can better understand how Amos’s ministry focused on the northern kingdom if we remember that the border of Israel and the sanctuary at Bethel were only about fifteen miles from Amos’s farm home in Tekoa. Samaria, the northern capital, was only a day’s journey away using the transportation methods of that day. Amos, by profession (according to Amos 1:1), was a “sheepbreeder.” This word can refer to a keeper of sheep and/or goats. In a later passage (7:14), Amos also calls himself “a tender of sycamore fruit.”
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 0, NULL, 'Most chapters are messages of judgment. The first two are known as oracles of judgment against the nations. Successively indicted are Damascus, Gaza, Tyre, Edom, Ammon, and Moab. Judgment does not stop far beyond the borders of God’s people. It is coming closer. Judah is next on the list. But Amos is in the north, up to this point; his audience may have appreciated God’s judgment against their enemies, foreign people groups. They may not even have been disturbed by the judgment against Judah, the southern sister nation. But Amos is driving his message home to these northerners, and they are totally taken by surprise when he mentions them. No wonder they complain to King Jeroboam through their representative, the official priest, who accuses Amos: “Amos has conspired against you in the midst of the house of Israel. The land is not able to bear all his words” (7:10).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 0, NULL, 'Introduction (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 1, 'Amos is introduced as a shepherd and a farmer (v. 1 and 7:14–15). He was from Tekoa, 10 miles (16 km) south of Jerusalem. He was not a professional prophet, but nonetheless he was called to a delicate task. He was to confront a priest and a king and the leadership mainly of Israel but also of Judah. (See the mention of Zion in 6:1.) The kings mentioned in the superscription are well known in the history of Judah and Israel. Both had long and prosperous reigns (see Introduction). The earthquake mentioned is referred to in Zech. 14:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 2, 'roars. God renders judgment from Jerusalem. The repercussions are felt up north in the shepherds’ pastures and on the green garden of Mount Carmel. Right from the start this verse signals disaster. The fact that God roars like a lion should strike fear in the hearers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 3, 'One by one, Israel’s neighbor nations are singled out for various sins. Israel is happy about judgment on these enemy nations, but in the end she herself is condemned.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 3, 'Damascus. Indicted for cruelty in warfare for “they have threshed Gilead with implements of iron.” Unlike Israel, these nations are not condemned for breaking a covenant of law. Unlike Israel, they were not a special covenant people. They are all condemned for their harsh behavior to others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 6, 'Gaza. Indicted for forced migration and slavery.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 9, 'Tyre. Indicted for their practice of forced migration and slavery of captive or conquered populations. God shows that there are basic expectations in human relations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 11, 'Edom. Mentioned twice for receiving deported populations as slaves and is portrayed as stifling all compassion. He indulges in anger and wrath and relentlessly pursues his brother for slaughter. The Edomites were descendants of Esau, Jacob’s brother.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 1, 13, 'the people of Ammon. Accused because of their unbelievable cruelty. They ripped open pregnant women in Gilead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 2, 1, 'Moab. Committed acts of desecration in burning the bones of the king of Edom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 2, 4, 'Judah. Judah’s condemnation is based not simply on the expectations of a universal covenant similar to that of Noah, but rather by the special covenant between God and His chosen people. Judah is indicted for having despised the law of the Lord and for not having kept His commandments.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 2, 6, 'This longest oracle is reserved for Israel, the northern kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 3, 1, 'children of Israel. Three ideas appear in this section: election, covenant, and judgment. The mention of the exodus and the whole of God’s people serve as a reminder of Israel’s covenantal relationship with the Lord. Even though the focus of the indictment is the northern kingdom, Israel, north and south are viewed as a historical unit in the typical covenantal manner. Special covenant privilege implies ethical responsibility.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 3, 3, 'A series of questions meant to justify the necessity for Amos to prophesy (see also 7:13–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 3, 7, 'prophets. The content of this verse shows God sends His servants the prophets to get His people back to the covenant before it is too late. Eighth-century B.C. prophets such as Amos, Hosea, Isaiah, and Micah were sent as God’s lawyers to bring a charge against God’s people and the nations before catastrophe struck. The Assyrian threat was looming on the horizon. Amos was sent nearly 40 years before the Assyrian captivity. God wanted to spare His people, as He specified in 2 Chr. 36. He further sent Hosea in 750 B.C., Isaiah in 740, and Micah in 725. In spite of His calls to His people to come back to covenant faithfulness and righteousness and social justice, His people did not listen. 722 B.C. marked the end of the northern kingdom of Israel, after a little over two hundred years of existence since the schism between the north and the south in 931.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 3, 12, 'a piece. Indicating the insignificance of the remnant that survives the judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 3, 14, 'altars. Idolatry and prosperity characterized the northern kingdom’s religious and social life. Bethel. The main sanctuary of the northern kingdom (see 4:4; 5:5; 7:10–13; Gen. 12:8; 28:19; 35:7; 1 Kin. 12:26–33; 13:1–5, 10; 2 Kin. 23:15; Hos. 4:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 4, 1, 'cows of Bashan. The sarcastic tone and the comparison of the leading women of Samaria to cows of Bashan, a region rich in pastures and cattle, was meant to shake their complacency. The comparison bears on the fact that these women oppress the poor and crush the needy like the cows press and crush the grass (see Ps. 22:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 4, 4, 'Bethel. Had a long history as a sacred place, beginning with the patriarch Jacob (Gen. 28:22), whereas Gilgal was the first site of the central sanctuary after the conquest under the leadership of Joshua. In the context of Amos 7, Amaziah the priest makes a case that Bethel is the sanctuary of the king.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 4, 6, 'The divine teaching that was meant to draw people back to a covenant relation with God did not produce the expected repentance. The refrain “Yet you have not returned to Me” (vv. 6, 8–11) leads to a dramatic climax, a judgment warning “Prepare to meet your God” (v. 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 4, 12, 'The seven calamities listed in the previous verses culminate in divine judgment all the more terrifying in that its nature is not specified except to say “Prepare to meet your God.” The particles “thus” and “this” are supposed to indicate what God plans to do. Meeting God in judgment is more dreadful than so-called natural calamities. Moreover, the judgment is not so much for what God’s people do as what they do not do—repent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 1, 'This chapter, along with chap. 6, is a lament on the condition of Israel and the devastation prophesied through upcoming war and subsequent lamentations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 2, 'The expression “virgin of Israel” along with the expression “chief nation” (or “head of the peoples”) in 6:1 were favorite designations in circulation among the Israelites to express their pride and sense of superiority. The meaning of the phrase “as you have spoken” in v. 14 implies this attitude among the people. Amos uses all these terms to challenge the connotations they carry. In his mouth they become words of derision. The reality of a fallen virgin, having no one to raise her up, and forsaken on her own land, signifies unfulfilled destiny.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 4, 'Seek Me. There is only one way out of the tragedy of conquest, deportation, and exile that loom on the near horizon. The pressing invitation is to seek God and God Himself, not through illegitimate religious shrines such as Beersheba, Gilgal, or Bethel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 7, 'turn justice. The core undergirding charge against God’s people is that they have turned justice into wormwood and laid righteousness to rest (see also 6:12). Justice and righteousness represent the essence of the covenant that Israel has neglected. The lack of conformity to these essential covenant stipulations is basically due to neglect of God’s law (2:4) and moral character. This neglect has resulted in the following: an idolatrous worshiping community (2:4; 5:26; 8:14), a society that exploits the destitute (2:6–8), an oppressive and abusive system (3:9; 4:1; 5:11; 8:4, 6), an unjust juridical structure (5:10–12), and a disregard of the Sabbath (8:5). They profaned God’s holy name by indulging in sexual immorality (2:7), counteracting God’s initiatives by repressing the signs of holiness (i.e., the pressure on the Nazirites to betray their vow in 2:12). They even attempted to silence the prophet of God (2:12). Unrighteousness can also be seen in the lack of repentance (4:6–11), in a complacent attitude (6:1; 9:10), carelessness (6:4–6), and arrogance (6:8). Justice is therefore connected to how one treats fellow human beings. It means granting others their due. Righteousness is also moral rectitude. It is life rightly oriented towards God in absolute allegiance and commitment to upholding His holiness. It is conformity to God’s moral character. God is a just God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 8, 'He made. The heart of the book of Amos presents God as Creator and Judge. The center of the book of Amos is the sentence “The LORD is His name.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 15, 'the remnant of Joseph. A repentant northern kingdom of Israel. Ephraim, the largest northern tribe, and a metaphor for the whole northern kingdom, was named for Joseph’s son.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 16, '‘Alas! Alas!’ The use of this particle is for emphasis on the reality of the mourning because of affliction. It is again used in v. 18 and 6:1 (“woe”) as an indication of the reality of conquest and exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 18, 'the day of the LORD. In the setting of Amos, it is a day when the expectations of the people are frustrated. It is a paradox that God’s people are in such denial about their true condition that they desire the day of the Lord. It will therefore be a day of darkness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 21, 'feast days. Festivals, sacred assemblies, worship rituals, and offerings, without justice and righteousness, are abominable to God. It is not frequent in Scripture that God is so explicit about what He hates. Justice and righteousness are core values of the whole structure of biblical faith. Without them religion turns into meaningless formalism and hypocrisy. The centrality of these two core values not only in the OT but also in the whole of Scripture is akin to the centrality of love in the NT. They are all divine attributes God shares with His loyal covenant partners.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 5, 27, 'beyond Damascus. Without justice and righteousness, exile is the outcome. The last verse of this chapter announces the certainty of the exile of idolaters.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 6, 1, 'Woe. The particle “alas,” or “woe,” can at times be used to get people’s attention. In this setting there is a connection between the uses of repeated woes (5:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 6, 4, 'beds of ivory. Clear example of unnecessary luxury.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 7, 1, 'the Lord GOD showed me. There are three visions which concern judgment: (1) locusts (vv. 1–3); (2) fire (vv. 4–6); and (3) a plumb line (vv. 7–9). Amos interceded for Israel and “the LORD relented” (vv. 3, 6), delaying the execution of judgment. This makes it clear that prophetic intercession or prayer makes a difference in God’s response to people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 7, 10, 'Historical interlude: confrontation with Amaziah (see Introduction, “Outline”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 8, 1, 'The vision of summer fruit tells of the end of God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 8, 4, 'The charges in these verses relate to social injustice against the poor and needy. Fraud and idolatry are connected to the trembling of the land and the mourning of the inhabitants (v. 8) and lead to covenant curses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 8, 11, 'famine. Comes because God proclaims it. That famine will be for hearing God’s word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 9, 1, 'The end is predicted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 9, 8, 'the house of Jacob. God will not allow the complete destruction of Israel. There will also be a remnant, one group of people from all the nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 9, 9, 'Those promised destruction are unrepentant, arrogant, and self-confident sinners. They pretend that the calamity will not overtake or confront them. They feel safe and secure like those on Mount Zion and in Samaria as reported in 6:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 9, 11, 'raise up its ruins. This refers to the ruins of the house of David and the promise of the Messiah in David’s line. The book of Amos ends with words of restoration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Amós', 9, 13, 'Using rich agricultural metaphors of prosperity, we are given one of the most beautiful prophetic visions of ultimate restoration.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Obadias', 0, NULL, 'The name Obadiah means “servant of Yahweh” or “worshiper of Yahweh” (Obed, servant; iah, a shortened form of the divine name Yahweh). Some have suggested that this name is like the name Malachi (meaning “My messenger”) in that it is really a title rather than a name. This is not likely, since thirteen different Obadiahs are mentioned in the Old Testament from Davidic to postexilic times. In addition, there are six persons, including David’s grandfather, who bear the name Obed, which is probably a shortened form of the same name.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Obadias', 0, NULL, 'The text explains that judgment is coming to the Edomites because of their actions, their pride (v. 3), and their false sense of security because of their physically strategic, lofty position in the mountains. But God promises that He will bring the nation down, no matter how lofty and impregnable they think their fortress is (v. 4).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Obadias', 0, NULL, 'I.      Title and introduction (v. 1)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 0, NULL, 'The book of Jonah is named after its chief character, who is portrayed as a prophet. The book does not specifically identify its author and speaks of Jonah in the third person, but tradition has ascribed authorship of the book to him.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 0, NULL, 'Jonah is the greatest missionary book of the Old Testament. The book clearly shows God as One who cares deeply about nations beyond His special people, Israel. Although the book begins with a proclamation of judgment on Nineveh, it ends with grace and salvation for that great city.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 1, 1, 'the word of the LORD came. The same beginning as found in the books of Hosea, Joel, Micah, Zephaniah, Haggai, and Zechariah. This verse connects the main character of the book with the person who prophesied during the reign of Jeroboam II (793–753 B.C.) (2 Kin. 14:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 1, 2, 'Nineveh. Located east of the Tigris River in today’s northern Iraq, near the current city of Mosul. The fact that their wickedness came up before God reveals that God is a universal Judge and that nations are accountable to Him. He is guarantor of a moral world order and if a nation trespasses the boundaries of good by doing evil, He intervenes. The book of Jonah reveals a God who intervenes in human affairs and cares about the nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 1, 3, 'Tarshish. The location of Tarshish is not certain but seems to be as far away from Nineveh as was possible in Jonah’s world. Somehow Jonah believes that God is localized and can be escaped by travel. Joppa. Was Judea’s main port and is today named Jaffa.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 1, 7, 'lots. The casting of lots was a common practice in the ancient world. It was supposed to discover the will of the gods. Though not frequently referenced in Scripture, lots are mentioned, for example, in Num. 26:55; Josh. 18:6–10; Neh. 10:34; Prov. 16:33; and Acts 1:24–26. Usually marked sticks or rocks were drawn from a container.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 1, 9, 'I am a Hebrew. An ethnic identification (Gen. 40:15; Ex. 1:19; 3:18; 10:3). I fear the LORD. A shortcut expression that means “I believe in the Lord God and live according to His stipulations.” This is strange because Jonah was trying to flee from the God who is the Creator of heaven and earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 1, 14, 'cried out. The sailors’ plea to save Jonah’s life contrasts with Jonah’s indifference or, perhaps more surprisingly, his unyielding desire and passion to see the destruction of the Ninevites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 1, 17, 'three days and three nights. Jesus referred to this expression to illustrate His own forthcoming experience (Matt. 12:38–41; 16:4; Luke 11:29–32). He mentions the sign of Jonah not only in relation to His death but also in reference to Jonah’s preaching.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 2, 1, 'prayed. This remarkable prayer narrates the history of the deliverance of Jonah. Jonah is unquestionably a man of prayer, whether in anguish or in anger (2:1; 4:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 2, 3, 'You cast. The poetic language of this section describes a person drowning, sinking in the depths of the sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 2, 6, 'moorings. The image of the roots of the mountains with bars dramatizes the confinement, helplessness, and life-threatening experience Jonah went through in the sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 2, 9, 'Salvation is of the LORD. The core of this prayer celebrates God’s miraculous intervention to rescue Jonah by a fish. At the end of Jonah’s prayer God spoke to the fish, which saved Jonah from drowning and vomited him up. Creatures obey the voice of God. Humans are uniquely endowed with the prerogative to say no.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 3, 2, 'great city. According to archaeological exploration, Nineveh was about eight miles (13 km) in circumference with a population of 120,000. This number is mentioned in 4:11. This is large for an ancient city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 3, 5, 'believed God. Repentance of the Assyrians stopped the planned judgment. This repentance did not have lasting effects, since Nineveh would later be destroyed by the Medes and Babylonians in 612 B.C. During the time of Jonah, however, God’s warning was taken seriously.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 3, 10, 'God relented. The prophets often refer to God changing planned judgment in response to human pleas or repentance. See Jer. 18:7–10; Amos 7:1–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 4, 1, 'displeased Jonah exceedingly … angry. Jonah’s anger shows his deep nationalistic pride. The missionary God had a prophet with a non-missionary heart.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 4, 2, 'I know. Jonah recognized the longsuffering nature of God’s character, and especially His desire to forgive. God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger, and abounding in covenant love (Ex. 34:6; Num. 14:18; Neh. 9:17; Ps. 103:8; Joel 2:13). God forgives and relents from punitive judgment. Jonah can appreciate this from his own experience. What disturbed him and destroyed his peace is that God extends the grace even to enemies. Salvation outside the confines of God’s people was inconceivable. But the God of the Bible is different, and nationalism is not part of His agenda. He purposes to save and to bless all the religions and families of the earth as He said to Abraham. God could have destroyed the Ninevites without warning in the first place. The very fact that He sends Jonah shows that God was open to the possibility of reversing His verdict against the Ninevites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 4, 5, 'Jonah sat outside the city waiting to witness its destruction. This shows his struggle to let God be God and forgive. At this point Jonah refused to engage in a conversation. God had to reach him through an object lesson which would help Jonah to feel the way God feels.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Jonas', 4, 6, 'God mobilizes agents from His creation—the plant, the worm, the sun, and a scorching east wind—to get Jonah to talk again. The strategy works. God can now express His rationale for forgiving the Ninevites: it is about compassion. If Jonah can have compassion for a plant, how much more is it justified for God to have compassion for the Ninevites, and even for the cattle in the city? This book reveals God’s missionary passion to save lives.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 0, NULL, 'Direct information about the man Micah is scant. His name is probably a shortened form of “Micaiah,” which means “Who is like Yahweh?” He is not, however, to be equated with Micaiah the son of Imlah, who was a prophet in the northern kingdom at the time of Ahab, ca. 874–853 B.C.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 0, NULL, 'Micah’s basic message is clear: God hates and condemns sin—whether it be religious, economic, or social. Because of their sin, God must judge and punish His people. Doom is about to fall. On the other hand, there is hope, because God is merciful, compassionate, and forgiving.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 0, NULL, 'I.      Heading and introduction (1:1)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 1, 1, 'Micah. The name is an abbreviated form of Micaiah (“Who is like Yahweh?”). He was from Moresheth, also called Moresheth Gath in 1:14, most likely near the Philistine city Gath. God revealed to him in a vision the future fate of the northern and southern kingdoms of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 1, 2, 'Judgment and deliverance heeding.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 1, 2, 'Hear. The call to witnesses is typical of covenant lawsuits, already encountered in earlier prophetic books (Amos, Hosea, and Isaiah). It introduces the indictment against God’s people, both Israel with Samaria as capital city, and Judah with Jerusalem as capital city. holy temple. Ps. 11:4 specifies that the Lord has His throne in the temple in heaven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 1, 3, 'coming out. The reason given for the manifestation of God (theophany) is to judge His people who have sunk into idolatry. The destruction of Samaria is predicted by means of what happens after destruction: “a heap of ruins” and stones as signs of desolation and abandonment of a site.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 1, 8, 'wail. The prophet embodied his message, illustrating the lamentation and shame God’s people were to experience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 1, 9, 'wounds. For the use of the images of wounds to describe the deplorable condition of God’s people, see Is. 1:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 1, 10, 'Tell. The prophet describes the advance of the Assyrian army through several cities near his own hometown to the very gate of Jerusalem. This device heightens the tragedy of the situation and the seriousness of the judgment about to befall God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 2, 1, 'Those who make others destitute among God’s people will be stripped of what they usurp and they will receive no portion during land allocation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 2, 6, 'you say. This is an allusion to the resistance to Micah’s message of rebuke. He, like Isaiah and later Jeremiah, had to take a stand and not follow the example of prophets who falsely prophesied good things, encouraging complacency.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 2, 9, 'cast out. When people are abused, it is an attack against God’s glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 2, 12, 'God will lead His people again. He will guide them. Exile is not the last word. The images of gathering and leading evoke the work of a shepherd.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 3, 1, 'False leaders and a righteous ruler.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 3, 1, 'False leaders and a righteous ruler.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 3, 1, 'Injustice characterizes the deeds of the rulers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 3, 5, 'prophets. There was no lack of prophets prophesying “Peace”; however, God had nothing to do with it. On the contrary, Micah, God’s legitimate prophet, announces disaster because of the moral bankruptcy of leaders, prophets, and priests. There is a sense of false security among the people of Jerusalem. They believe that nothing bad will happen to them because God is in their midst. Therefore, the words that Zion shall be plowed like a field is shocking. That Jerusalem shall become heaps of ruins and the mountain of the temple like the bare hills of a forest must have stunned Micah’s audience. They had a lasting effect. Later, the leaders of Judah during the time of Jeremiah remembered Micah’s words (Jer. 26:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 3, 12, 'Micah predicts the destruction of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 4, 1, 'Typical to prophetic writings are the sudden shifts of perspectives and subject matter. Micah moves from a bleak coming judgment to a glorious distant future. When the circumstances seem most desperate and hope appears a luxury, the deep confidence that God’s purposes cannot ultimately be defeated brings about the most encouraging perspectives in the prophetic writings. Micah offers an expanded and almost identical version of what Isaiah prophesied (see Is. 2:1–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 4, 1, 'in the latter days. The phrase takes the reader to Last Day times when God will establish His universal reign. What history has not witnessed, God fulfills. Peace between nations has been the historical missing link. Wars have characterized international relations to this day. However, God through His prophet gives hope that it will not always be like that. Wars will cease. The use of weapons will be obsolete. The pervasive curse of fear will be eradicated from human hearts. Peace will be the reality of the day. This prophecy is so central to the prophetic writing that God affixed His signature to it: “the LORD of hosts has spoken” (v. 4). What Israel as a people was supposed to mediate by drawing all nations to come to learn from God’s people, the Messiah assumes. Jesus is the new center on whom converge the desires of the nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 4, 4, 'under his vine. The restoration is personal, as well as national and international.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 4, 10, 'you shall go. The exile to Babylon was predicted nearly two centuries before the event. The Babylonian captivity was carried through several deportations, beginning in 605 B.C. Ezekiel was exiled in 597 B.C. The captivity was sealed in 586 B.C. with the destruction of the temple of Jerusalem. Predictive prophecy is a component of the reliability of the Bible.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 4, 11, 'The restoration of God’s people requires judgment on their enemies and rescue from their hands.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 5, 2, 'Bethlehem. Literally, “house of bread”; David’s birthplace. Ephrathah. Mentioned because it distinguishes this Bethlehem from another Bethlehem in Zebulun (Josh. 19:15). It also refers to the Ephrathite clan living there (Ruth 1:2) and ties the city more closely to David. The Shepherd to come who shall be great to the ends of the earth goes beyond the scope of David’s influence. The chief priests and the teachers of the law understood that the Messiah was to be born in Bethlehem of Judea (Matt. 2:3–6). John 7:42 takes it for granted that the Messiah comes from the seed of David and from the town of Bethlehem. The Messiah goes forth also from the days of eternity which precede the beginning of creation. In a nutshell the Messiah is both human and divine. The idea of the Messiah as divine is also found in Micah’s contemporary, Isaiah (see Is. 9:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 6, 1, 'Arise, plead. God’s people on trial and God’s sorrow. The same Hebrew word that Hosea uses (Hos. 4:1) to describe a court trial is used here. Just as in Isaiah when the heavens and earth are called as witnesses (1:2), and also in Amos when the neighboring nations are called to observe the behavior of God’s people (3:9), here the mountains and the hills are called to hear God’s people’s defense and the Lord’s accusation. One of the meanings of the name Israel is “one who contends with God” in order to get a blessing, and now God contends with Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 6, 3, 'what have I done. In these moving verses God appeals to His people’s reason and memory. He summons them to answer Him. God asked the question “What have I done to you?” He reminds His people about what is variously translated as “righteousness,” “righteous acts,” or “saving acts.” The word “righteousness” can refer to God’s acts, His saving acts, or His just actions even in judgment (see Judg. 5:11). It is also an indication of God’s holiness, His moral character. He is a righteous God. When used in reference to people, it signifies conformity to God’s character and to God’s law. Its use becomes a way of saying that a person is in a sound relationship with God and acts with ethical rightness with fellow human beings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 6, 6, 'come before the LORD. The question is “How are we to worship?” burnt offerings … calves … thousands of rams. Not the whole answer. They can be only forms. Even child sacrifice (v. 7) is not the answer. Instead God asks for three things: (1) “do justly,” which means literally to act with justice toward other people. The term is ethical and relational; (2) “love mercy,” or to love being merciful; and (3) “walk humbly with your God,” which means to have an attitude of humble wisdom in response to God. While the first two principles have to do with the horizontal human-to-human relationship, this final one deals with the vertical human-to-God relationship. These three principles are the heart of worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 6, 9, 'When people frustrate justice, accountability and judgment come to reverse their fate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 6, 12, 'lies. A deceitful tongue is a symptom of moral corruption.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 6, 16, 'Omri. The name of the northern kingdom’s dynasty of which Ahab was a part.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 1, 'Micah speaks about his loneliness due to the rarity of righteous people. The situation is so bad that there is no relief wherever one looks.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 3, 'Looking up to princes, judges, notables, and others who are corrupt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 5, 'The sacred bonds of family provide no safe zone. Spouse and children are not reliable. The prophet comes to the terrible conclusion that “a man’s enemies” are those of his own household.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 7, 'I will look. There is still one place left. One can look to the Lord and wait for Him. The prophet says the Lord is the God of salvation. One can speak to Him, and He hears.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 8, 'light to me. One of the gems of the book of Micah is hope in the righteousness of God. For one who bears God’s judgment, there is still hope. Recognition of the justice of God’s judgment and acceptance of one’s responsibility can turn God into an ally. God pleads the case of those who repent of their sins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 10, 'Then. Earlier in the book the nations were eager to see Zion defiled (4:11). Now there is a reversal of fate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 11, 'your walls. The verse describes the return to Jerusalem and the rebuilding of the city. The remnant will return as God promised (see the various references throughout the book: 2:12–13; 4:1–4, 6–8; 5:3, 7–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Miquéias', 7, 18, 'Neither Micah nor his contemporary prophets like Amos, Hosea, or Isaiah were unconditional prophets of doom. Messages of salvation were inseparably woven with the proclamation of judgment. God will forgive the remnant of their iniquity and will cast their sins into the depth of the sea. History will climax in the fulfillment of God’s promises to the patriarchs.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 0, NULL, 'The title of the book is the name of the prophet who was its author, Nahum. In Hebrew it means “comforted,” or “the one comforted.” The name occurs only here in the Old Testament, although it is related to the name Nehemiah (“The Lord has comforted”) and Menahem (“comforter”).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 0, NULL, 'Nahum has been unfairly accused by some of being a narrow-minded nationalist, full of hate and vengeance. However, he opens a window to the revelation of God’s sovereignty to judge the nations. When nations cross the boundaries beyond which they betray human dignity, God steps in to stop the madness. Assyria, with its powerful military machine that subdued countless nations, city-states, and peoples, crossed that boundary. Prophecies against nations other than God’s covenant people were not Nahum’s innovation (see Is. 13–23; Jer. 46–51; Amos 1–2; and Obadiah). Nahum uses a rich imagery to describe both the destruction of Nineveh and the liberation of those who were under her power. The fall of Assyria was the best news for several people groups who were suffering under the empire’s heavy rule.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 0, NULL, 'I.      Superscription (1:1)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 1, 1, 'The book. A unique feature in this superscript is that the totality of Nahum’s speeches is called a book. It is also designated as a “burden” or a vision. The vision is the fall of Nineveh. “Nahum” means “comfort.” The same Hebrew root is used in 3:7 to say there will be no comforter for Nineveh when the city faces destruction. On the other hand there is a message of comfort to Judah (1:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 1, 2, 'The coming of God as Judge is described by means of His sovereignty over nature. He masters the elements and uses them as His agents for judgment. The image of God as warrior is a message of hope. God intervenes to restore justice. What human powers could not achieve on their own, the God of justice fulfills to stop cruelty and abuse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 1, 14, 'your gods. Nineveh was guilty of idolatry. They had apparently forgotten what they had learned from Jonah’s reform.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 1, 15, 'good tidings. One of the occurrences of the idea of good news and peace in a book filled with gloom and doom. The message is for Judah. The one who will not be able to pass through Judah is called “the wicked one,” a name that is later used as a designation for Satan (2 Cor. 6:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 2, 1, 'This graphic chapter describes a city that is about to be attacked and that prepares itself accordingly for defense. It will be in vain, declares the prophet, for ultimately Nineveh is plundered, emptied, desolated, and wasted (v. 10). Courage is lacking. The ones who made others tremble now have their own hearts melt, their own knees shake, and their own faces anxious.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 2, 1, 'Man the fort! The tone is sarcastic as the prophet tells the Assyrian leaders and their people what they should do, even while the prophet knows it is not going to work. The Assyrians will not be able to resist the One who scatters.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 2, 7, 'voice of doves. The decree of the end of Nineveh. The one who used to exile others is exiled. The voice of doves and the image of beating of breasts signify mourning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 2, 8, 'flee away. For fugitives running for their lives, nothing is of value but life itself. They leave behind plunder for their attackers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 3, 1, 'bloody city. The first section of this chapter exposes the barbaric behavior of Assyrian conquerors. Nineveh is exposed as a city where victims are always present. Violence and murder are common reality. Her cruelty has no bounds, so much so that on the day of her disaster, no one bemoans her. No one is found to comfort. It is rather a relief that she is devastated and meets her end. Her punitive judgment is at the same time liberation for those she oppressed continually.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 3, 3, 'slain. The mention of a “multitude,” the “great number of bodies,” and “countless corpses” gives a hint of the legendary Assyrian cruelty. It was customary for Assyrian kings to boast about an impressive number of corpses piled at the gates of their conquered enemies. The Assyrian king Shalmaneser III, for example, boasted to have erected a pyramid of chopped-off heads at the gate of a conquered city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 3, 8, 'No Amon. Thebes, the capital of Upper Egypt. It was located 450 miles (724 km) south of Cairo. Known today by the names of Karnak and Luxor, it was in ancient times connected to a famous Egyptian deity, Amon. It was destroyed by the Assyrians in 663 B.C. In essence, what Assyria had done to others, such as Thebes, will be done to her.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 3, 15, 'locust. The economic and military power of the Assyrian Empire will be destroyed as though by the ravages caused by locust invasion. Locusts were known for swift devastation and then disappearance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 3, 18, 'The helplessness of the Assyrian king and his subjects, nobles, and people alike contrasts with their previous might now neutralized with no way to recover. Assyria is described as incurably wounded. Death of the empire is the only outcome.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Naum', 3, 19, 'All who hear. The last word of the book is given to those victims of the Assyrian abusive power. They celebrate. They can now clap their hands and dare speak out about Assyria as a tyrant. upon whom … continually. Gives an idea of the extent of the oppressive Assyrian power.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 0, NULL, 'The book of Habakkuk records the prophetic messages of the man whose name appears as the title of the book. We know little about him. We are not sure of the meaning of Habakkuk’s name.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 0, NULL, 'Most prophetic books record divine admonitions and rebukes which the Lord has given to the prophet to share with the people. Habakkuk takes a different approach. Instead of proclaiming the Lord’s word to the people, Habakkuk addresses the people’s concerns to God.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 0, NULL, 'I.      Dialogue one: Habakkuk’s first complaint and God’s response (1:1–11)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 1, 'burden. Sometimes translated “oracle,” the Hebrew word is regularly used to denote a divine message proclaimed by a prophet. The word “saw” is used by the prophets to indicate a visionary experience (Is. 1:1; Ezek. 12:27; Mic. 1:1). Prophets were sometimes called “seers.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 2, 'I cry. Evidently, Habakkuk has for some time been expressing concern to God about the wickedness prevalent in his society over a period of time, but nothing has changed, and injustice continues to abound. The prophet concludes that God is either not listening to his concern or is indifferent toward it, so his frustration erupts in this striking complaint given in the traditional format of the lament. Though some are surprised that a prophet would speak to God in this way, this passage, as well as others in Scripture (Ps. 13; 22; Jer. 20:7–18), indicates that God’s servants sometimes make honest and heartfelt statements of concern about His fairness and justice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 5, 'Look among the nations. God is not just addressing the prophet but all the people when He responds to the prophet’s complaint by telling him that the divine response to the problem of injustice will be seen internationally. Though it may seem amazing that the Lord will use Babylon as His instrument of punishment, it should not be so surprising because the Lord previously used Assyria, calling it “the rod of My anger” (Is. 10:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 6, 'Chaldeans. Master race in ancient Babylon (Is. 47:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 7, 'themselves. In their work of conquest the Babylonians recognize no authority outside of themselves.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 9, 'Their faces are set like the east wind. A difficult phrase to interpret, it may refer to the scorching winds that would sometimes come from the eastern desert and devastate the vegetation. They gather captives like sand. Like the Assyrians before them, the Babylonians would carry out an intentional policy of deportation and exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 10, 'They scoff at kings. Sometimes the Babylonians would make a public spectacle of defeated rulers, as when they slaughtered Zedekiah’s sons before him, put out his eyes, and then took him to Babylon in chains (2 Kin. 25:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 11, 'Ascribing this power to his god. Can also be translated so as to indicate that the Babylonians are guilty of idolatry because they have made a god out of their military power. Marduk or Bel was Babylon’s chief god.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 12, 'You. The address to the Lord indicates a change in speaker from God back to the prophet, thus beginning Habakkuk’s part in the second dialogue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 13, 'Why. In his opening comment Habakkuk inquired why God caused him to look upon evil (v. 3). Now he questions how God, being righteous and holy, can look on while wicked Babylon defeats “righteous” Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 1, 14, 'fish. Mesopotamian artwork portrays defeated enemies being captured in fishnets.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 1, 'my watch. Using the vivid imagery, familiar in Habakkuk’s time, of a sentinel posted on a watchtower eagerly awaiting information to share with his people (2 Sam. 18:24; Is. 21:6; Ezek. 33:7), the prophet declares that he will await God’s answer to his complaint.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 2, 'This verse highlights the fact that God’s revelation is to be both preserved (“Write the vision”) and publicized (“That he may run who reads it,” referring to a herald who would proclaim the message after learning of it).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 3, 'appointed time. The Lord reminds the prophet that sometimes He asks His people to wait before they see the fulfillment of His word. However, His word is certain and there is no doubt it will be fulfilled in accordance with His own plan and on His schedule (Matt. 24:35).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 4, 'the just shall live by his faith. This declaration is quoted three times in the NT (Rom. 1:17; Gal. 3:11; Heb. 10:38), summarizing the great biblical doctrine of righteousness by faith. This verse makes a fitting summary because it contrasts the proud and self-sufficient Babylonians, who are headed for destruction, with those among Judah who have an abiding trust in the Lord while they live lives of integrity. shall live. Implies experiencing the blessings, abundance, and security offered by God to those who are faithful to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 5, 'hell. From the Hebrew sheol, referring to the grave. Just as the grave ultimately devours everyone, so Babylon has an insatiable appetite and desires to conquer all nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 6, 'a proverb. The peoples whom Babylon has defeated are described as taunting Babylon with five woes or taunt songs (vv. 6–8, 9–11, 12–13, 15–17, 18–19), emphasizing the wickedness and ultimate downfall of their erstwhile conqueror. Woe. Used repeatedly in the prophetic books (Is. 3:11; Jer. 48:1; Amos 6:1) to begin a declaration of judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 8, 'plunder you. Babylon, having plundered others, will be plundered itself. The punishment fits the crime.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 14, 'the glory. A ray of light penetrates the darkness of the five woes. Though Babylon in all its power and splendor is headed for destruction, there is a bright future in store for the world. Habakkuk, echoing Isaiah’s earlier prophecy (Is. 11:9), proclaims that the glory of the Lord will cover the earth as fully as the waters cover the bottom of the sea. The future belongs to God and His kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 16, 'exposed as uncircumcised. Means to be worthy of scorn and derision. The cup of the LORD’s right hand. As is the case in other prophetic books (Is. 51:17–23; Jer. 25:15–17), this is a symbol of divine judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 18, 'the image. There is no help to be had from idols of wood or stone. Being lifeless, they cannot speak or hear.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 2, 20, 'But. Introduces a purposeful contrast with the preceding verses. In contrast to the lifeless gods of wood and stone, the living God reigns from His holy temple in heaven (Ps. 11:4; Mic. 1:2–3). The whole earth is called to a worshipful silence in His presence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 1, 'Shigionoth. Probably a musical term that gives some information for the use of the psalm in temple liturgy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 2, 'In the midst of the years. Means “in this time.” At the outset of his prayer, Habakkuk requests that the Lord demonstrate a fresh manifestation of His saving work right then and to temper His judgments with mercy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 3, 'God came. Similar to the way that Job had an encounter with God after his complaint about divine justice, so Habakkuk comes face to face with God’s mighty acts after his complaint. He highlights the Lord’s workings at the time of the exodus, at Mount Sinai, and at the conquest of the promised land, and anticipates that God will work again on behalf of His people in the future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 3, 'Mount Paran. Was probably on the Sinai Peninsula. Elsewhere in Scripture (Deut. 33:2) it is associated with the giving of the law at Sinai and is probably meant to recall that event here. Selah. Appearing two other times in this chapter and elsewhere only in Psalms (71 times), this is probably a musical term but is of uncertain meaning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 7, 'Cushan … Midian. Two tribes that lived near Edom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 11, 'The sun and moon stood still. Reminiscent of the extension of daylight which God granted, enabling Joshua to defeat the Amorites during the conquest of the promised land (Josh. 10:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 13, 'Anointed. From the Hebrew term sometimes rendered “Messiah.” It is used in the OT to refer to a person and never to the nation of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Habacuque', 3, 17, 'Though. In a ringing climax to the book, Habakkuk announces that though all the natural means of sustenance should fail, he will rejoice in God anyway, even as Paul did while awaiting a decision in a Roman jail cell (Phil. 4:4). Evidently, this is due to a continuous trust in God, regardless of external circumstances, an attitude which Habakkuk needs right now as he contemplates Babylon’s impending judgment upon Judah. Likewise, our relationship with the Lord should not be dependent on what is happening in our lives at any given moment.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 0, NULL, 'The first verse of the book attributes the message it contains to the prophet Zephaniah, whose name means “the LORD has hidden” or “the LORD has sheltered.” We know little about him. According to this verse, he was the great-great-grandson of a man named Hezekiah. Since no other prophet traces his genealogy back four generations, it is likely that this is a reference to the famous King Hezekiah, thus making Zephaniah part of the royal lineage. This is of interest since one of the groups upon which he announces coming judgment is “the king’s children” (1:8), probably his cousins. Though perhaps a contemporary, the prophet Zephaniah is a different person and should not be confused with the priest of the same name who is mentioned several times in Jeremiah (Jer. 21:1; 29:25, 29; 37:3; 52:24).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 0, NULL, 'Zephaniah’s message emphasizes a single theme: the coming day of the Lord. This theme, perhaps first proclaimed by the prophet Amos (Amos 5:18–20), becomes a prominent theme in the prophetic books, with two prophets—Zephaniah and Joel—focusing almost exclusively on it.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction (1:1)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 1, 'The word of the LORD. The first two words in the Hebrew accurately describe the contents of the book and indicate that the message is of divine origin and has divine authority. Hezekiah. Zephaniah is the only prophet to trace his ancestry back four generations, making it likely that the prophet’s great-great-grandfather was the famous king of Judah who brought about spiritual reformation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 2, 'The destruction announced here is more sweeping than that of the flood because it also includes “the fish of the sea.” The different life forms destroyed are listed in the opposite order that they were created (Gen. 1:20, 24, 26), indicating that the judgment announced by Zephaniah is the undoing of creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 3, 'stumbling blocks. Possibly a reference to idols; the metaphor is appropriate because the people of Judah repeatedly stumbled over the sin of idolatry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 5, 'Milcom. A variant spelling of the Ammonite god Molech, to whom some Israelite parents offered their children as human sacrifices (Jer. 32:35).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 8, 'foreign apparel. Wearing foreign clothing probably signified identification with foreign values and practices, including idolatrous worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 9, 'leap over the threshold. A likely reference to the superstitious practice of not stepping on the threshold when entering an idolatrous temple (1 Sam. 5:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 11, 'Maktesh. This term denotes the market district of Jerusalem, which is singled out as a place where divine judgment would take place on the merchants.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 1, 13, 'The nature of the divine judgment is in the form of the covenant curses (Deut. 28:30), thus fulfilling the Lord’s warning to Israel of long ago.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 2, 'Before. The threefold repetition of “before” emphasizes the urgency of the prophetic summons and warns that soon it may be too late to repent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 3, 'Seek the LORD. The prophet’s announcement of coming judgment is not meant to lead to despair but to repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 4, 'Judgment is pronounced on four of the five major cities of the Philistines, listed in geographical order from south to north.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 5, 'Cherethites. Literally “Cretans.” This term refers to the Philistines and links them with their geographical origins on the island of Crete.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 7, 'Land once held by their enemy will become pastures for the remnant of Judah who are graciously saved by the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 9, 'As was the case in v. 7, the remnant will possess land formerly occupied by those who despised them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 11, 'Elimination of the false gods paves the way for worldwide worship of the true God, an important event that is proclaimed by other prophets as well (Mal. 1:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 2, 13, 'Nineveh, an especially large and powerful city of the ancient world, with an inner wall 50 feet (15 m) thick and 100 feet (30.5 m) high, was conquered in 612 B.C. by a coalition of Babylonians and Medes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 1, 'The identity of “the oppressing city” being addressed is not revealed at first, perhaps to heighten suspense. But in the following verses it becomes clear that another city than Nineveh noted for its wickedness is also hurtling towards judgment: Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 3, 'As is often the case, a failure of leadership on the part of princes, judges, prophets, and priests leads to moral breakdown among God’s people. The judges are described as “evening wolves that leave not a bone till morning,” suggesting that they have a voracious appetite for violence and oppression and that they thoroughly devour their victims. In Ezek. 22:27 Jerusalem’s princes are also depicted as ravenous wolves.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 6, 'The prophet quotes the Lord’s hope that Jerusalem, having observed divine judgment on other cities and nations, would repent of its wickedness and turn to Him; but alas, their response was more corruption and rebellion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 8, 'Having indicted both Jerusalem and the other nations of various acts of wickedness, the prophet declares the Lord’s intention to gather the nations for a universal judgment in which “all the earth shall be devoured.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 9, 'Zephaniah has already indicated that judgment does not result in annihilation (2:3, 11). a pure language. Literally, “a pure lip.” Lips that are unclean, probably due to expressions of worship to idols, render people unfit to worship the true God (Is. 6:5). But in a stunning reversal of their previous condition, the Lord transforms their lips, thus enabling them to call on His name in worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 10, 'My worshipers, the daughter of My dispersed ones. When viewed in its context, this phrase continues the reference to those whom the Lord saves from the other nations. Having had His children dispersed since the Tower of Babel (Gen. 11), God is bringing them home. offering. The nature is not stated. However, by bringing it to the Lord, the foreigners are submitting to His lordship and giving homage to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 11, 'These verses emphasize the fact that the judgment on the day of the Lord is a purging judgment. The reason why “you shall not be shamed” is that the blot of wickedness will be removed from Jerusalem. The “remnant of Israel,” that is, those who are saved from the judgment, pattern their lives after the Lord whom they serve (v. 5). They live humbly, trustingly, and righteously under the watchful care of their God. This passage makes a contribution to the great remnant theme of Scripture that culminates in the last book of the Bible (Rev. 12:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 14, 'Now beyond the cleansing judgment and having received salvation, with the Lord dwelling in their midst, the people of God are exhorted to sing and shout with joy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Sofonias', 3, 17, 'The word “you” appears here as the object of the verb “quiet,” but it is not in the Hebrew. Thus we could also read the phrase as “He will be quiet in His love.” Though it is a stunning thought to consider, the text likely portrays a God so thrilled with the people that He has saved that He is in quiet contemplation as He savors His love for them.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 0, NULL, 'The little book of Haggai (only Obadiah is shorter among the OT books) contains the messages of the prophet after whom the book is named. Haggai, whose name means “festival,” ministered to the Jewish community after their return from exile in Babylon, exhorting the political and spiritual leadership and attempting to awaken the people from their spiritual lethargy and inspire them to rebuild the temple.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 0, NULL, 'Haggai’s message is emphatic and direct, packing a powerful spiritual message into a short book of two chapters. After rebuking the people for wrong priorities in focusing on beautifying their own homes rather than the Lord’s and pointing out the results of their mistaken choice (1:4–6), the prophet challenges the people to get to work and rebuild the temple (1:8). After they respond positively (1:12, 14), Haggai speaks of the benefits and blessing that will result from their obedience, including future glory for the temple (2:9), agricultural abundance for the people (2:19), and a special exalted role for Zerubbabel (2:23). It is clear that Haggai sees the restoration of the temple, a visible symbol of God’s dwelling in the midst of His people (Ex. 25:8), as necessary if the people wish to experience the Lord’s blessing in the future.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 0, NULL, 'I.      Message One (August 29, 520 B.C.): time to rebuild the temple (1:1–15)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 1, 'The Darius mentioned here is Darius I (also known as Darius the Great or Darius Hystaspes), who reigned from 522 to 486 B.C. His second year began in the spring of 520. The precise day of Haggai’s first prophetic message has been identified as August 29, 520 B.C. the sixth month, on the first day of the month. The day of the New Moon festival and thus a holy day (see Amos 8:5), so the prophet may have been sharing his message in the midst of ongoing religious observances. Speaking to the governor and the high priest, Haggai is addressing the political and the religious leadership of the nation. The names of Zerubbabel, the governor of the post-exilic community, and Joshua, the high priest of that era, appear together elsewhere in the books of Ezra and Nehemiah, and they are both mentioned in Zechariah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 2, 'Instead of a direct refusal to rebuild the temple, the people voiced an interesting rationalization, stating that it was not yet the right time to build the Lord’s house.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 4, 'paneled houses. The Hebrew term can mean either that the houses are covered over with a roof or that the houses are adorned with some type of luxurious paneling. In either case, the net result is the same. The people are giving priority of focus to their own homes while ignoring the Lord’s house (see also v. 9), a dereliction of duty that earned the prophet’s indictment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 5, 'thus says the LORD. This phrase appears more than 350 times in the prophetic books and identifies the prophet’s most basic and essential task of serving as a spokesperson for God. Consider your ways. Twice in this chapter (see also v. 7) the prophet calls the people to do some introspection and reflect on their actions in light of the divinely revealed message. This type of spiritual reflection is healthy for the people of God in any era.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 6, 'Ironically, far from leaving them satisfied and content, the people’s focus on their own priorities leaves them hungry, thirsty, and penniless. The implication is that they are experiencing divine judgment for their disobedience (see Deut. 28:38–40), just as their ancestors had who were taken into exile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 8, 'This verse summarizes the main task with which Haggai challenges the people: namely, to get busy with the work of rebuilding the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 10, 'the dew. In arid climates like that of Israel, dew can be rather heavy during the growing season and is important to the development of the crops.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 11, 'the grain and the new wine and the oil. Herein are referenced the three major types of produce from ancient Israelite agriculture: various grains, grapes, and olives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 12, 'To obey the words of Haggai is to obey the voice of the Lord. There is no difference between the message of the prophet and the word of the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 13, 'The phrases “the LORD’s messenger,” “the LORD’s message,” and “says the LORD” underscore the divine authority behind Haggai’s message three times in one verse, something Haggai repeatedly emphasizes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 14, 'The leaders are mentioned first among those who respond positively to the prophet’s message, perhaps because their example helps inspire the people to obedience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 1, 15, 'That the people started work on rebuilding the temple “on the twenty-fourth day of the sixth month” indicates a delay of 23 days between Haggai’s initial proclamation and the obedient response of the people. Perhaps this gap allowed for the people to do the work of the harvest, as well as for some time for the Spirit to work on the hearts of the people. This date corresponds with September 21, 520 B.C. The temple would be completed a little over four years later in early 515 B.C. (see Ezra 6:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 1, 'Haggai’s second message is received several weeks after the activity of the previous verse and on the last ordinary day of the Feast of Tabernacles, a day before the solemn rest day that marked the feast’s conclusion (see Lev. 23:39). The date was October 17, 520 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 3, 'former glory. Some of the elderly members of the community who had seen Solomon’s temple are evidently discouraged as they compare the meager structure under construction, a depiction that has other biblical support (see Ezra 3:12; Zech. 4:10). Perhaps their discouragement threatened to paralyze the community from continuing the work of rebuilding, thus prompting a message from Haggai predicting the new temple’s future glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 4, 'be strong … for I am with you. Just as an earlier Joshua was encouraged to “be strong” (Josh. 1:6–7, 18) and was assured of the Lord’s ongoing presence (Josh. 1:5), now the high priest Joshua, along with Zerubbabel and the “people of the land,” receive the same exhortation and the same promise.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 7, 'the Desire of All Nations. A phrase that appears only here in all of Scripture, is translated and understood in various ways. Two of the most common are (1) a reference to the coming Messiah who, as God incarnate, will bring glory to the temple by His personal presence there (this understanding is supported by this translation of the phrase, which is capitalized); (2) a reference to gifts that will be offered by the Gentiles to beautify the temple as they give homage to the Lord, and usually translated as “the treasures of all nations” or “the wealth of all nations.” This understanding has some support both in the immediate context (v. 8) and elsewhere in the prophetic books (Is. 60:5–7, 11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 9, 'The promise of greater glory for this temple than Solomon’s temple was fulfilled in two ways. First, Herod the Great and his successors greatly beautified and expanded the temple complex, making it one of the wonders of the ancient world. Second, Jesus, One greater than the temple (see Matt. 12:6), would later walk in the precincts of this temple, bringing the presence of God in bodily form. in this place I will give peace. An appropriate promise regarding the place where the “Prince of Peace” (Is. 9:6) would someday walk. The prophets declare that international peace will be one of the blessings of the Messiah’s reign (see Zech. 9:9–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 10, 'the second year. This prophetic message can be dated to December 18, 520 B.C., about two months after the previous message, during which time the prophet Zechariah also had begun his prophetic ministry (Zech. 1:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 11, 'Haggai consults the priests for an official ruling regarding the transmission of ritual holiness and ritual defilement. The essence of their answer is that ritual defilement is more easily transferable than ritual holiness, contaminating everything which the defiled object contacts (see Lev. 6:25–27; 22:4–7). The prophet then applies this principle to the worship of the people, suggesting that their sacrifices are defiled because they come from unclean hearts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 18, 'this day. The obedient response of the people in working on the temple will bring the Lord’s blessing on their lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 20, 'again. A second message is given to Haggai on the same day, December 18, 520 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 21, 'This final message of Haggai, unlike the previous ones, is addressed only to Zerubbabel, the governor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Ageu', 2, 23, 'The title “My servant,” along with the word “chosen,” are terms that echo messianic terminology appearing in earlier prophets, especially Isaiah (Is. 42:1–9; 49:1–7; 50:4–9; 52:13–53:12) and Ezekiel (Ezek. 34:23–24; 37:24–25). Some think that in comparing Zerubbabel to a “signet ring,” the prophet is reversing the curse earlier pronounced on Zerubbabel’s ancestor, King Jehoiachin (see Jer. 22:24–30). The signet ring, a sign of ownership and royal authority, functioned like a signature in the ancient world. With this comparison, an exalted role for Zerubbabel is promised. As a descendant of David and in the royal line, Zerubbabel foreshadows and typifies the Messiah, who will be in his line of descendants (Matt. 1:12–13).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, NULL, 'The visions and prophetic sayings of the prophet Zechariah are recorded in the book which bears his name. The book presents him as the son of Berechiah and the grandson of Iddo (1:1). There is no contradiction between this and the twice-repeated statement in Ezra that he was “the son of Iddo” (Ezra 5:1; 6:14), because the Hebrew word for “son” can also be rendered “descendant.” Moreover, it is possible that Zechariah’s father met a premature death, leaving him to be raised by his grandfather.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, NULL, 'The book of Zechariah has a rich variety of themes and covers a broad array of topics, making it challenging to summarize the book’s overall message. However, certain themes are especially prominent.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction: a call to repentance (1:1–6)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 1, 'In the eighth month of the second year of Darius. This is Darius I, also known as Darius the Great or Darius Hystaspes (not to be confused with Darius the Mede, who is mentioned several times in Daniel), king of the Persian Empire from 522 to 486 B.C. Zechariah’s first prophetic declaration can be dated to sometime in October or November of 520 B.C. the word of the LORD came to Zechariah. As is the case with other prophets (Jer. 1:2; Hos. 1:1; Joel 1:1), God takes the initiative in seeing to it that His word is proclaimed. Zechariah the son of Berechiah, the son of Iddo. This does not contradict the statement in Ezra 5:1; 6:14 that Zechariah was “the son of Iddo,” because the Hebrew word “son” also means “descendant.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 3, 'Return to Me. The summons to repentance is not, first and foremost, a call to be obedient to the law, though that would be included. It is primarily God’s call for His people to return to Him. A restoration of a personal relationship with the Lord is at the heart of biblical repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 4, 'the former prophets. Refers to the prophets before the Babylonian exile, like Hosea, Amos, Jeremiah, and others, who repeatedly called the covenant people to leave their wickedness and return to God (Jer. 3:12–13; Hos. 14:1; Amos 5:4–6), but their call was rebuffed (Jer. 17:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 7, 'This verse introduces Zechariah’s series of eight visions, which set forth God’s future plans for Judah and Jerusalem and the nations who have attacked them. These visions apparently were given in one night and can be dated to February 15, 519 B.C. This was a momentous time, just five months after the rebuilding of the temple resumed (Hag. 1:14–15), about three months after Zechariah’s first prophetic declaration (Zech. 1:1), and two months to the day after Haggai’s final prophecy (Hag. 2:10, 20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 8, 'The first of Zechariah’s eight visions teaches that the Lord is deeply interested in His covenant people and will bless them tremendously, and that Jerusalem and the temple will be rebuilt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 8, 'man riding on a red horse. The leader of the patrol of horsemen. He is later identified as “the Angel of the LORD” (v. 11) and is to be distinguished from the interpreting angel (v. 9) who appears repeatedly in this series of visions. sorrel. Translated in other versions as “brown” or “speckled.” Since the word only appears once in the Hebrew OT, the exact color intended is uncertain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 9, 'The fact that Zechariah asks questions (1:9; 2:2) and makes statements (3:5; 4:2) during these encounters underscores that these are not ordinary dreams given while he is sleeping. Rather, they are visions communicated by divine revelation to the prophet while he is awake.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 11, 'Is it good news or bad news that “all the earth is resting quietly”? The context suggests that it is bad news, because Persia and other nations are looking after their own selfish interests and are uninterested in Judah’s welfare or in rebuilding the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 12, 'seventy years. The prophecy of the 70-year captivity given by Jeremiah (Jer. 25:11–12; 29:10) can be understood in various ways: (1) It could be the period of Babylonian control in Palestine, extending from Nebuchadnezzar’s assertion of power by winning the Battle of Carchemish in 605 B.C. (the year in which the prophecy was first given [Jer. 25:1]), after which he took captives from Judah, including Daniel (Dan. 1:1–6), until the release of Jewish exiles by Cyrus about 537 B.C., which is explicitly stated to be the fulfillment of Jeremiah’s prophecy (2 Chr. 36:22; Ezra 1:1; see also Dan. 9:2). (2) Alternatively, Zechariah seems to see the 70-year period as still unfinished and to understand it as the span of time beginning with the destruction of Jerusalem and the temple in 586 B.C. (Zech. 1:12; 7:5) and perhaps ending with the rededication of the temple in 515 B.C. (3) Additionally, some interpreters understand the 70 years as a symbolic number representing the lifespan of a generation (Ps. 90:10), indicating that those taken captive will not live to be involved in the return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 14, 'zealous. God is not passive towards His people but is constantly seeking to restore them to a relationship with Himself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 15, 'with evil intent. Can be rendered “they made the disaster worse.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 18, 'Zechariah’s second vision expresses the Lord’s intention to defeat the powers that previously oppressed Judah and continued to threaten their survival and well-being.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 18, 'four horns. The horn, a powerful appendage on various animals such as the bull, is a fitting symbol of strength and can represent the power of a nation or a nation’s king, which is the epitome of its power (Ps. 75:10; Dan. 7:24; Mic. 4:13; Rev. 17:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 1, 20, 'four craftsmen. Represent the powers that God is using to defeat the nations that have previously oppressed His covenant people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 1, 'Zechariah’s third vision (vv. 1–5), with its accompanying prophetic message (vv. 6–13), prophesies that the restored city of Jerusalem will be abundantly populated and that it will be well defended—even without walls—because the Lord Himself will be its sure defense and provide miraculous protection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 4, 'this young man. Refers to either the man doing the surveying work, who is being recalled to be informed of the Lord’s plan to bless Jerusalem, or to Zechariah himself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 5, 'The glorious presence of God dwelling in Jerusalem fulfills Ezekiel’s prophecy depicting the return of the Lord’s glory to the temple (Ezek. 43:2–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 6, 'The Jews in exile who have thus far chosen not to come back to their homeland are urged to do so. Not long after Zechariah’s time, they will come under a death decree during the time of Esther. the apple of His eye. Literally “the opening of His eye.” The same metaphor appears in Deut. 32:10, where it speaks of God’s special care over His people. It may refer to the pupil, which is a sensitive and important part of the eye, quite susceptible to injury and therefore most in need of protection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 9, 'Just as an earlier generation of God’s people plundered those who had oppressed them (Ex. 12:35–36), so Judah’s oppressors will be defeated and plundered.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 11, 'This is the first of several instances in Zechariah where the great biblical theme of salvation for the nations appears, where people from other nations are portrayed as worshiping the Lord and being embraced as His people (8:20–23; 14:16). Other prophets emphasize this theme as well (Is. 2:2–4; 19:18–25; Zeph. 3:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 12, 'the Holy Land. This is the only use of this exact phrase in all of Scripture. Many Christians use this term to refer to the land of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 2, 13, 'Making use of the same Hebrew word found in Habakkuk (2:20) and Zephaniah (1:7), Zechariah calls all people to “Be silent” in reverent awe, in light of what the Lord is about to do.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 1, 'Zechariah’s fourth vision portrays Joshua, the high priest, being cleansed from sin and restored to his priestly function. Joshua is then exhorted to obey the Lord and informed that he is a sign of the coming Messiah, who will provide the ultimate cleansing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 1, 'The scene shown in this vision probably took place somewhere in the temple courts. Joshua, the Hebrew equivalent of the name Jesus, served as high priest in the time of Haggai and Zechariah (Hag. 1:1). He appears before “the Angel of the LORD,” who serves as Judge in this courtroom-like setting. The prosecutor (“to oppose” can be rendered “to accuse”) is identified as “Satan,” which is simply a transliteration of a Hebrew noun meaning “adversary.” The fact that it has the definite article indicates that it is not a proper name here. However, the role played by the adversary here and elsewhere in Scripture (Job 1:6–12; 2:1–7) paves the way for the most significant personal adversary of God to be called by the name “Satan,” who is later described as “the accuser of our brethren” (Rev. 12:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 2, 'the LORD. “The Angel of the LORD” from v. 1 is now specified as God Himself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 3, 'The garments of the high priest are a matter of great importance (Ex. 28), and for the high priest to be improperly attired is a serious infraction. Since Joshua as high priest is the nation’s representative, his “filthy garments” symbolize the nation’s sin. The Lord’s decision to remove his dirty attire and clothe him in “rich robes” and “a clean turban” are not based on his words or works, for he says and does nothing. Rather, it is based on God’s gracious choice to save His people from the fires of judgment (Zech. 3:2). The cleansing of Joshua and his restoration to the priestly office symbolize the Lord’s purpose to restore the entire nation to its divinely ordained role of reconciling the world to its Creator God (Israel is described in Ex. 19:6 as “a kingdom of priests”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 7, 'walk in My ways. After God forgives and restores His people, He expects them to live in accordance with His principles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 8, 'Joshua and his priestly companions who bear the iniquities of the people foreshadow the coming future Messiah, here called “My Servant the BRANCH.” Both Servant (Is. 42:1–4; 49:1–7; 50:4–9; 52:13–53:12) and Branch (Is. 11:1; Jer. 23:5; 33:15; Zech. 6:12–13) are important messianic terms. They describe the coming Branch of David, that is, the Davidic descendant who will function as God’s ultimate Servant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 9, 'remove. The announcement that these seven eyes are connected with the final removal of sin highlights the messianic context of the verse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 3, 10, 'Under his vine and under his fig tree. A biblical expression connoting the blessings of contentment and prosperity (1 Kin. 4:25; Mic. 4:4). According to Zechariah, these blessings are shared with others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 1, 'Zechariah’s fifth vision provides assurance that the temple will be rebuilt and God’s purposes will be accomplished through the power of His Spirit working through His two chosen servants: Zerubbabel, the governor; and Joshua, the high priest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 2, 'a lampstand. What is notable about this lampstand—and different from the lampstands in the tabernacle and the temple—is that no priest is needed to replenish the oil. Instead, the Lord is constantly renewing its oil supply through the agency of the two olive trees that flank it, the bowl suspended above it, and the seven pipes coming into the lamps.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 6, 'Zerubbabel is assured that the temple will be rebuilt (v. 9) and God’s purposes will be accomplished, not by human strength or military might, but by the inspiration, guidance, and empowerment provided by the Spirit of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 7, 'capstone. Though some think this is the foundation stone, it is more likely the completion stone. Putting it in place would signify the completion of the temple and result in celebration and rejoicing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 10, 'Those who “despised the day of small things” may be the older Jews who had seen Solomon’s temple and were unimpressed with what they were seeing as the temple was being rebuilt (Ezra 3:12; Hag. 2:3). plumb line. A builder’s tool used to determine if a wall is vertically straight. However, the Hebrew word is of uncertain translation and may be another reference to the “capstone” (Zech. 4:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 4, 14, 'The implication is that the two olive trees represent Joshua and Zerubbabel. As high priest and governor, respectively, they are set apart by God for the fulfillment of His purposes for the postexilic community. Said to be “the two anointed ones,” they foreshadow the coming Anointed One, or Messiah, who will be the ultimate priest and king.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 1, 'Zechariah’s sixth vision personifies the word of God (v. 4), emphasizes its inherent power to accomplish its intended purpose (Is. 55:11), and teaches that commandment-breakers will be punished for their disobedience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 2, 'Its length is twenty cubits and its width ten cubits. About 30 feet (9 m) long and 15 feet (4.5 m) wide. The dimensions of the “flying scroll” are unusual. It is only twice as long as it is wide and is thus depicted like a huge billboard or placard floating in the sky, located in full view so that its message can be read.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 3, 'Like the two tablets with Ten Commandments (Ex. 32:15), this scroll has writing on both sides. Though violators of the eighth and the third of the Ten Commandments are singled out (the “perjurer” probably refers to the person who takes the Lord’s name in vain), they probably represent all who will disobey any of the covenant stipulations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 5, 'The seventh vision of Zechariah represents the cleansing of the restored covenant community by portraying the removal of sin from the land of Judah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 6, 'basket. Sometimes translated as “ephah,” this basket was a common barrel or basket used for measuring, which normally held approximately 2/3 bushel (22 l).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 7, 'Wickedness is personified as a woman, not for the purpose of denigrating women, but because the Hebrew word for wickedness is a feminine gender noun. Likewise, wisdom, also a feminine gender noun, is personified as a woman (Prov. 1:20–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 5, 11, 'Shinar. Refers to Babylon (Gen. 11:2, 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 1, 'The eighth and final vision is reminiscent of the first one, with horses of different colors fulfilling a divine commission. It teaches the sovereignty of God over all powers, as evil is defeated and the Spirit is proclaimed to be at rest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 5, 'The “spirits,” or “winds” (the Hebrew word is the same), are said elsewhere (Ps. 104:4) to be the messengers of the Lord to fulfill His purpose.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 6, 'the north country. Denotes Babylon. the south country. Refers to Egypt, the locations from which most attacks had come to the covenant people in Israel and Judah and where some of the exiled people still resided. The point is that even the most hostile enemies—indeed, all the earth (v. 7)—are under the sovereignty of the God of Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 8, 'have given rest to My Spirit in the north country. Implies that the divine work of cleansing and judgment is accomplished and that a new era is dawning for the covenant people. If God’s Spirit has found rest in the north, a place which has been the source of so much difficulty for God’s people (Jer. 1:13–15), then by implication all opposition to the divine plan has been subdued.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 12, 'BRANCH. See note on 3:8. “BRANCH” is an important messianic title (Is. 11:1; Jer. 23:5; 33:15; Zech. 3:8). By being crowned and called “the BRANCH,” the high priest Joshua is designated as a symbol of the coming Messiah, which is appropriate since “Joshua” is the OT equivalent of the NT name “Jesus.” This is the clearest statement in the OT that the Messiah will be both priest and king. According to the NT, Jesus fulfills this prophecy as the ultimate Priest and King (Heb. 4:14; Rev. 19:16), thus bringing into harmony the royal and priestly roles and embodying in Himself the last phrase: “And the counsel of peace shall be between them both” (Zech. 6:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 6, 15, 'if. One of the clearest expressions in the prophetic books of the conditional nature of the divine promises.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 1, 'The date is December 7, 518 B.C., a little more than two years after Zechariah’s first prophetic message (1:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 2, 'sent. A delegation, perhaps from Babylon, though the text is unclear, has been asked to inquire of the priests and the prophets if the covenant community should still engage in fasting and mourning to commemorate what happened in their history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 7, 5, 'The prophet reiterates a point emphasized by earlier prophets about religious rituals. Unless feast days, sacrifices, and fasts are accompanied by humility and obedience, they are external decorations done for selfish purposes and are offensive to God (Is. 1:11; 58:1–12; Amos 5:21–24). Jesus offers the same rebuke of His own contemporaries (Matt. 23:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 4, 'The restored Jerusalem is described as a place of security and serenity for both the aged and the young, those groups most susceptible to the ravages of disease or the attack of an enemy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 10, 'In the early years after coming back to Judah, the returned exiles experienced grinding poverty and constant threat. However, now that the people are turning from their disobedience and working on the temple, God promises to bless them (vv. 11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 13, 'The phrases “just as you were a curse” and “you shall be a blessing” signal the reversal of Israel’s recent history and the fulfillment of God’s foundational promise to Abraham that through him and his descendants blessing would come to all the earth (Gen. 12:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 16, 'Give judgment in your gates. In OT times important matters, such as court cases, were decided at the city gates (Ruth 4:1–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 19, 'The fast of the fourth month. Commemorated the breaching of Jerusalem’s walls by Nebuchadnezzar’s army on July 18, 586 B.C. (Jer. 39:2), shortly before the destruction of the city. the fast of the tenth. (Tenth month). Commemorated the beginning of the siege of Jerusalem by the forces of Nebuchadnezzar on January 15, 588 B.C. Instead of simply doing away with their fast days, which were not done with proper motives and attitudes, anyway (7:5–6), the Lord has an even better plan. He promises to transform their days of fasting into days of feasting, due to the abundant blessings and joy they will experience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 20, 'As the context shows, the “inhabitants of many cities” (v. 20) are clearly non-Israelites, for they are later identified as “many peoples and strong nations” (v. 22) and “ten men from every language of the nations” (v. 23). This striking passage, which echoes the earlier promise of Zechariah that “many nations” will be incorporated into the Lord’s people (2:11), and foreshadows the same point in the book’s conclusion (14:16), is one of the grand OT passages that portrays foreigners coming to Jerusalem to seek and worship the true God (Is. 2:2–4; 66:18–21; Mic. 4:1–4). Thus, blessing comes to all the earth, and the great foundational promise to Abraham is fulfilled (Gen. 12:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 8, 23, 'ten men. In the Bible the number ten sometimes represents the number of completion or totality (Gen. 31:7; Ruth 4:2), so all the nations are represented here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 1, 'burden. See note on Mal. 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 2, 'Tyre was renowned for both its wisdom and its wealth (Ezek. 28:4–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 4, 'destroy. Tyre would ultimately be destroyed by the forces of Alexander the Great in 332 B.C.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 9, 'Rather than the fear and dread of judgment which other places will experience (v. 5), Jerusalem’s inhabitants can rejoice because of their coming King. Instead of riding on a war horse (v. 10), He comes in righteousness and humility, bearing salvation, mounted on a donkey. His purpose is to bring about worldwide peace (v. 10). According to the NT, this prophecy is fulfilled in Jesus’ triumphal entry into Jerusalem, an event recorded in all four Gospels (Matt. 21:1–11; Mark 11:1–10; Luke 19:29–44; John 12:12–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 10, 'peace to the nations. The kingdom of the Messiah will be a worldwide kingdom characterized by complete peace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 11, 'the waterless pit. Sometimes empty cisterns were used to hold captives or prisoners (Gen. 37:24). The term may be used here as a metaphor for the exile in Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 12, 'prisoners of hope. Israelites who may be held captive but who have hope, due to their expectation of their coming King (v. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 9, 13, 'Judah and Ephraim are reunited to accomplish the Lord’s purposes, thus undoing the great tragedy of schism that occurred in the history of Israel after the death of Solomon (1 Kin. 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 1, 'Ask the LORD. Rain comes from the Lord, not from idols, such as the Canaanite god Baal, who was claimed by his worshipers to be the giver of fertility. latter rain. Refers to the springtime rains that were vital for the ripening of the crops.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 3, 'The term “shepherd” was commonly used as a metaphor for a ruler in the ancient Near Eastern world. As in Ezek. 34, the Lord punishes the evil shepherds and intervenes on behalf of His people, the sheep.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 10, 'In fulfillment of the prophecy of Moses (Deut. 30:3–5), the Lord will bring His covenant people home. The restored community will be so large that there will be no room left in the land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 10, 11, 'Just as the Lord made a path in the Red Sea to deliver His people from Egypt (Ex. 14:21–22), so He will work in the same way in the future to bring His people home. The nations of Assyria and Egypt represent all the enemies of God’s people. scepter. A rod held in the hand of the king; serves as a symbol of royal authority (Esth. 5:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 4, 'In a passage that is very difficult to interpret, the prophet is involved in an allegory dealing with the leadership of the people. The flock (the people of Israel) is under judgment from God and suffers mistreatment from its leaders and from one another (vv. 4–6). Next, the flock rejects the good shepherd (vv. 7–14), and he is then replaced by a worthless shepherd (vv. 15–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 7, 'Beauty. Or “Grace.” The staff represents God’s gracious care for His people. Bonds. Or “Unity.” This staff denotes the union of the northern kingdom of Israel and the southern kingdom of Judah (Ezek. 37:15–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 8, 'More than 40 different interpretations have been offered for the “three shepherds,” many of them identifying the three with specific historical figures. What can be said with confidence is that these three represent bad leaders.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 11, 15, 'In the last part of the allegory, the prophet takes the role of a “foolish shepherd,” representing a leader who is morally hardened and evil and mistreats his people. When people reject leaders selected by God, they will get the type of leaders they deserve.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 1, 'against Israel. Meaning “concerning Israel,” because, as the succeeding verses describe, the Lord will intervene on behalf of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 2, 'The two metaphors used for Jerusalem (“a cup of drunkenness” and “a very heavy stone”) suggest the helplessness and the injury that will ensue when all the nations attack the city in order to destroy it. They fight against God’s people to their own harm.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 4, 'In that day. Appears repeatedly in chaps. 12–14 and refers to the day of the Lord. For a summary description of the major events associated with the day of the Lord, see the “Content and Themes” sections in the Introductions to Joel and Zephaniah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 6, 'The metaphors used for Judah’s leaders (“a firepan in the woodpile” and “a fiery torch in the sheaves”) make clear that they are delivered, while their enemies are destroyed in the conflagration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 11, 'The phrase “like the mourning at Hadad Rimmon in the plain of Megiddo” may refer to the intense sorrow that took place when Josiah, the last godly king of Judah, was slain in the valley of Megiddo, an event that was lamented with sadness for many years afterward (2 Chr. 20–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 12, 12, 'The mourning over the killing that has taken place will be widespread, intense, and sincere, not just an outward show of sadness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 1, 'It is unfortunate that a new chapter begins here, because the phrase “In that day” connects the verse with what comes before. The piercing of the Lord (12:10) and the ensuing repentance will somehow lead to cleansing from sin. The forgiveness that was prophesied by earlier prophets (Jer. 31:34; Ezek. 36:25) and depicted in one of Zechariah’s visions (Zech. 3:4–5, 9) will now come to pass.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 4, 'Prophets were sometimes identified by their distinctive attire (2 Kin. 1:8; Matt. 3:4), but the prophetic garb will be avoided because false prophecy has brought the prophetic office into such disrepute.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 6, 'When confronted about the “wounds” on his torso (“between your arms” probably refers to the chest or back), scars which probably resulted from self-mutilation while participating in idolatrous rituals (1 Kin. 18:28), the false prophet lies about how he received them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 7, 'In what seems to be a startling turn of events, the Lord calls for the sword to attack “My Shepherd” and also calls Him “My Companion.” The latter term only appears elsewhere in Leviticus and refers to a near neighbor or a close associate (Lev. 6:2; 18:20). In other words, the killing of the divinely appointed Shepherd is somehow part of the Lord’s plan (Is. 53:10). Jesus quotes from this verse in reference to the scattering of the apostles at the time of His trial and crucifixion (Matt. 26:31; Mark 14:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 13, 8, 'The traumatic process of testing and trial will result in the remnant’s being established in a special relationship with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 1, 'As do other prophets (Joel 1:15–20; Zeph. 1:14–18), Zechariah declares that “the day of the LORD” will bring severe judgment to God’s people as “all the nations” unite and inflict a severe defeat on Jerusalem, resulting in plunder and rape.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 3, 'will go forth. The Lord intervenes personally in order to deliver a remnant. The “earthquake” during Uzziah’s reign occurred around 760 B.C. and must have been a significant event, being used by Amos as a chronological marker for his prophecy (Amos 1:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 6, 'no light. Other OT prophets also speak of a supernatural darkness on the day of the Lord as the celestial bodies no longer give their light (Is. 13:10; Joel 2:10, 30–31; 3:15). However, foreshadowing a description in Revelation (Rev. 21:25; 22:5), ultimately night itself will be eliminated as darkness is replaced by an ongoing and constant supply of light.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 8, 'living waters. Refers to a flowing river or an active spring. eastern sea. The Dead Sea. the western sea. The Mediterranean.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 9, 'All the land surrounding Jerusalem is leveled “into a plain,” while the city itself is elevated into a place of commanding prominence, as befits the capital of the One who is “King over all the earth.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 12, 'the plague. Giving further elaboration to the previously mentioned divine intervention on behalf of Jerusalem and against the foreign nations (v. 3; 12:2–9), Zechariah describes both the plague and the panic that will overtake them, resulting in their utter defeat.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 16, 'to worship. In a striking echo of his earlier description of foreigners worshiping the Lord at Jerusalem (8:20–23), Zechariah announces that the survivors among Judah’s enemies will come annually “to worship the King, the LORD of hosts, and to keep the Feast of Tabernacles.” This feast was used to express thanksgiving for the bounties of the harvest (Deut. 16:13–15) and to provide time for instruction in the word of God (Neh. 8:14–18). This portrayal of true worship coming from foreigners indicates that the Lord’s ultimate plan for the other nations, as well as for Judah, is not judgment resulting in destruction but restoration to a relationship with their Creator God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Zacarias', 14, 20, 'HOLINESS. Consecration to the Lord is pervasive in the new restored Jerusalem and the distinction between the sacred and the common seems to have disappeared, because even common objects, such as “the bells of the horses” along with all cooking pots used in the city, will bear the same words of dedication engraved on the gold plate fastened to the high priest’s turban (Ex. 28:36–37).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 0, NULL, 'The last book of the Old Testament indicates in the very first verse that it contains the prophetic sayings of Malachi, whose name means “My messenger.” We know nothing about him other than what can be inferred from his book. Some interpreters suggest that “Malachi” is not a proper name but a title that is used as a heading for the collected sayings of an anonymous prophet. However, this discussion views “Malachi” as a proper name, identifying a specific individual who delivered the prophetic messages found in this book.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 0, NULL, 'The message of Malachi rebukes the spiritual deafness of the postexilic community, calling them to live in faithful obedience to the covenant the Lord made with them. Though the open idolatry condemned by the preexilic prophets seems no longer to be practiced, the people are nonetheless falling short of living up to the expectations of the covenant. After responding positively to the message of Haggai and Zechariah and completing the rebuilding of the temple in 515 B.C. (Hag. 1:12–15; Ezra 6:14–15), the people, along with their spiritual leaders, have sunk back into a state of spiritual indifference. They are going through the motions of religious observance by offering their sacrifices, but their religion is a dry formalism and heartfelt commitment is lacking.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 0, NULL, 'I.      Disputation #1: the Lord’s love for Israel (1:1–5)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 1, 'burden. Translated thus because it comes from a Hebrew root meaning “to carry.” An alternate translation is “oracle” (see NKJV translation note), meaning simply a divine communication through a prophet.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 2, 'Jacob I have loved; but Esau I have hated. Refers to the Lord’s sovereign choice of Jacob and his descendants to play a key role in the plan of salvation and, by implication, his rejection of Esau and his descendants (the Edomites) for that role. It does not refer to personal animosity toward Esau; in fact, God prohibited this type of hatred toward the Edomites (Deut. 23:7). For other texts of Scripture which use the word “hate” in a comparative way, see Luke 14:26; 16:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 2, '“I have loved you.” Not a general statement of divine love for the human race as a whole, but the Lord’s declaration of love for His covenant people whom He has chosen. Reminiscent of other declarations of God’s love for His people in the prophetic books (Is. 43:4; Jer. 31:3; Hos. 11:1), it is a reminder that love was the foundation of the covenant with Israel from its very inception (Deut. 7:7–8) and that it called for a response of loving obedience on the part of God’s people (Deut. 6:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 3, 'This declaration of defeat and perpetual judgment against the Edomites was fulfilled during the period 550–400 B.C., as the Nabateans gradually defeated the Edomites and chased them into southern Palestine to an area later called Idumea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 6, 'The name of the Lord, which is to be exalted on a worldwide basis (Mal. 1:11), is being despised by the priests who are treating “the table of the Lord” (possibly a reference to the altar of burnt offering) with contempt by offering defective sacrifices. Malachi points out that their earthly governor would never accept such a flawed gift and neither does God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 6, 'This verse joins other prophetic texts (Is. 64:8; Hos. 11:1) in describing the Lord as the “father” of His people, thus anticipating the way Jesus opens the Lord’s Prayer with the words “Our Father.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 10, 'shut the doors. Probably refers to the doors at the entrance of the court of the priests in the temple complex. If these doors should be shut, no sacrifices could be made, and that would be better than the defective sacrifices the people are offering.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 11, 'Malachi emphasizes the universal theme so often present in the NT, thus joining with other prophets who also foresee a time when peoples all over the world will engage in worship of the true God (Is. 45:22–24; 49:6; Zeph. 2:11; 3:8–10; Zech. 14:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 2, 3, 'refuse. Refers to the dung or feces from the entrails of the sacrificial animals, which were supposed to be removed from the sanctuary and burned (Lev. 4:11–12). To have this smeared on his face would be terribly humiliating and would disqualify a priest from service. Because the priests have defiled the Lord (Mal. 1:7), the Lord will defile them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 2, 5, 'fear. Denotes a respect and reverence for God that results in obedience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 2, 6, 'A faithful priest, like any good spiritual leader, exemplifies in his own life those principles which he is teaching others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 2, 10, 'Father. As indicated by the parallel found in the following line, this refers to God as Israel’s father (Ex. 4:22; Hos. 11:1), not to Abraham, as some have suggested.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 2, 11, 'the daughter of a foreign god. Refers to a non-Israelite woman who worships idols. The men of Israel were expressly forbidden to marry such women so as not to be turned away from God (Ex. 34:11–16; Deut. 7:3–4). This verse indicates that the main reason for the prohibition of such intermarriages is not racial or ethnic, but to preserve the religious purity of the people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 2, 14, 'Malachi indicates that marriage is much more than a civil contract and that it involves a permanent covenant between husband and wife with the Lord as witness. Thus, God hates divorce, detesting the fact that the men of Israel are ending their marriages in order to marry foreign women. This is the strongest statement in the OT about the Lord’s feelings regarding divorce.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 3, 1, 'My messenger. Though this is the same Hebrew word as the name “Malachi” in 1:1, it is best not to translate it as a proper noun here, since it refers to someone coming in the future to prepare the way for the Lord. the Lord … temple. Just as the glory of God filled both the tabernacle of Moses (Ex. 40:34–35) and the temple of Solomon (1 Kin. 8:10–11) in earlier times, Malachi prophesies that the restored temple will be glorified by being visited by the Lord Himself in fulfillment of the prophetic word (Ezek. 43:1–5; Zech. 8:3), thus bringing it greater glory than that enjoyed by the former temple (Hag. 2:9). the Messenger of the covenant. This phrase is used only here in the OT. As this translation correctly indicates, and as supported by the parallelism of the text, this figure is identical with “the Lord” and is not the same person as “My messenger” earlier in the verse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 3, 6, 'Affirms the unchanging character of God and His unshakable love for Israel (Is. 54:10; Jer. 31:3). The verse is not asserting that the Lord never revises or modifies His interaction with His people based on their response in a given situation (Jer. 18:7–10; Jon. 3:9–10). God’s character is unchanging, but His specific way of dealing with His people may change depending on their response.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 3, 8, 'Since the temple is the Lord’s house, refusing to support the temple service by not bringing tithes and offerings is stealing from God. The tithe, one-tenth of both produce and livestock (Lev. 27:30, 32), had been designated for the Levites, due to their service at the tabernacle (Num. 18:21). The practice of tithing extended all the way back to Abram (Gen. 14:20) and Jacob (Gen. 28:22), but was not being carried out faithfully by the community that returned from exile, as seen here and in Nehemiah (Neh. 13:10–13). the windows of heaven. God’s abundant blessings follow our faithfulness to His instructions on tithing which, in turn, is part of our response to God’s blessings on us. For more on tithing as an expression of STEWARDSHIP, see Gen. 14:20; Lev. 27:30–33; Matt. 23:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 3, 16, 'book of remembrance. Just as the Persian king Ahasuerus maintained a book chronicling the good deeds of his subjects in order to reward them, so God has a permanent record of those who respect and reverence Him and reflect on His character. As the next verses will point out, God will differentiate between those who follow Him and those who do not.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 4, 1, 'Burning like an oven. A vivid picture of the complete destruction of the wicked in the judgment meted out on the coming day of the Lord. So thorough is the destruction that “neither root nor branch” remains, but only ashes (see v. 3). This is a word picture commonly associated with the concept of HELL. See Is. 34:9–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 4, 2, 'Presents a contrast rich in meaning with the previous verse. While the wicked were consumed from intense heat, the righteous receive healing from the same source of heat, described as “the Sun of Righteousness,” a phrase that appears only here in Scripture. Even as the rays of the sun revitalize and nourish plant and animal life, so God’s people are renewed and restored by their connection with Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 4, 3, 'ashes. The wicked, in all their aspects, are completely destroyed on the day of the Lord, and are not granted eternal life as disembodied spirits suffering continuous torment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 4, 4, 'the Law of Moses. With Malachi’s strong and repeated emphasis on the importance of faithfulness to the covenant, it is fitting that before his book concludes, he echoes the repeated reminders of Deuteronomy that God’s people “remember” the divinely revealed instructions, and challenges the people of his own day to “remember” the laws that were given to Moses. The Hebrew word “remember” implies not only a mental recollection but also taking appropriate external actions in harmony with inward thoughts (Ex. 20:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 4, 5, 'Elijah the prophet. Once again (see 3:1) there is a reference to the messenger who will prepare others for the day of the Lord, this time designated as Elijah. Though Elijah was taken to heaven without dying (2 Kin. 2:11) and would later appear with Jesus at the transfiguration (Matt. 17:3), this is not a reference to the person of Elijah himself, which is probably what John the Baptist was denying in John 1:21. Rather, according to both the angel Gabriel and Jesus Himself, this prophecy anticipates John the Baptist—one like Elijah—who would come “in the spirit and power of Elijah” (Luke 1:17; see Matt. 11:14; 17:12–13) and help prepare the way for Jesus. So Malachi concludes his book both by emphasizing God’s great revelation in the past (“Remember the Law of Moses,” v. 4) and by looking forward to the great future day of the Lord—that is, both reaching back and pointing forward. There is no more fitting way for the OT to end!
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 1, '(2) Pre-fleshly State of Christ');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 1, '(3) Genealogy of Jesus Christ');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 1, '3:23–38
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 5, '7 or 6 B.C.
(2) Announcement of the Birth of Jesus to the Virgin
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 26, '5 B.C.
(3) Song of Elizabeth to Mary
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 39, '(4) Mary’s Song of Praise');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 46, '(5) Birth, Infancy, and Purpose for the Future of John the Baptist
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 57, '(6) Announcement of Jesus’ Birth to Joseph
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 18, '5–4 B.C.
(7) Birth of Jesus Christ
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Malaquias', 1, 24, '2:1–7');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 0, NULL, 'As the first of the Gospels and the first of the documents of the New Testament, the Gospel of Matthew has been, through the centuries, a favorite among the New Testament books. When multiple Gospels share the same material, Matthew’s stories about Jesus have often been preferred. Examples of these are the temptations of Jesus (4:1–11), the Sermon on the Mount (chaps. 5–7), and the Lord’s Prayer (6:9–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 0, NULL, 'The authors of the four Gospels do not identify themselves by name. The titles “The Gospel According to Matthew,” “According to Mark,” and so on, were added later, reflecting the widespread opinion about who wrote them. By the end of the second century, the disciple and former tax collector Levi Matthew was believed to be the author of this book, because this is the only Gospel that identifies him as a tax collector (9:9). Matthew, like the other Gospel writers, seemed not only to draw on memory but to use material that was already in written form or was circulating orally (see Luke 1:1–4).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 0, NULL, 'The Gospel writers were inspired pastors who were intent on presenting a unique message that would address the particular needs of their congregations. It is generally accepted that Mark and Luke wrote to Gentile audiences. Matthew, while written in Greek, seems to be addressed to a Jewish audience. A number of features suggest this:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 0, NULL, 'The text of the Gospel of Matthew does not provide much evidence to identify when it was written. Many Bible students are of the opinion that Matthew was written in Palestine before the fall of Jerusalem in A.D. 70.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 0, NULL, 'Matthew wants his readers to recognize that Jesus is the Messiah, as promised in the Old Testament Scriptures. To do so, he parallels Jesus with a number of important Old Testament characters. First he traces Jesus’ genealogy back to Abraham, unlike Luke, who traces it back to Adam (due to Luke’s more universal focus). This emphasis on Abraham is vital to Matthew’s message, because, for Jews, Abraham is the father of the nation.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 0, NULL, 'I.      Prologue: the genealogy (1:1–17)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 1, 1, 'genealogy. Matthew’s genealogy (unlike Luke’s, which goes back to Adam with a focus on Jesus as the universal Savior) aims to show that Jesus is in the direct line of Abraham and David, and thus is the Jewish Messiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 1, 3, 'Tamar. The first of four women, besides Mary, mentioned in the genealogy (Gen. 38:1–30). The other three are: Rahab from Jericho (Josh. 2:1–21; Heb. 11:31), Ruth the Moabitess (Ruth 4:12–22), and Bathsheba the wife of a Hittite (2 Sam. 11:1–27). At least three of these women are Gentiles, affirming the OT witness that God works with and through both men and women, Gentiles, and people of diverse ethnicity, and, like many of the men, even ones whose behavior had been questionable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 1, 16, 'Jesus. Means “the Lord is salvation.” It is the Greek transliteration of the Hebrew name “Joshua.” Christ. Means “Anointed One,” the Greek translation of the Hebrew word “Messiah.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 1, 17, 'fourteen. Matthew selected three groups of fourteen generations, not for historical purposes, but because in Hebrew the numerical value of David’s name is fourteen. Matthew uses different techniques to convince his readers (who would only catch this nuance if they were Jewish) that Jesus is the Messiah-King in the line of David. He does so genealogically and theologically.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 1, 19, 'put her away. Divorce her. Though marriage was not fully consummated during the betrothal period, the couple was viewed as husband and wife. The relationship was binding and could only be dissolved by a certificate of divorce. Because of the suspicion surrounding Mary’s pregnancy, Jewish law demanded stoning (Deut. 22:23–24). However, Joseph acted nobly and proposed a private divorce.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 1, 21, 'Jesus Christ is central to biblical history. For His unique divine/human nature, see note on 1:23. The WORK OF CHRIST includes many roles, such as Creator (John 1:3), Sacrificial Lamb (John 1:29), Judge (John 5:22), Returning Savior (John 14:1–3), Revealer of the Father’s character (John 14:9), Sender of the Holy Spirit (John 15:26), Savior (Rom. 3:21–25), Giver of Eternal Life (Rom. 6:23), Risen Lord (1 Cor. 15:1–4), Substitute for Sinners (2 Cor. 5:21), Curse Bearer (Gal. 3:10–12), High Priest (Heb. 8:1–2), Sinner’s Advocate (1 John 2:1), Conqueror of Satan (Rev. 12:9–11), and Coming King (Rev. 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 1, 23, 'virgin. Translation of the Hebrew word for “young maiden.” The NT uses this translation of Is. 7:14 to emphasize God’s incarnational act of being with us by means of a virgin, but conceived by the Holy Spirit (Luke 1:35). The incarnated Christ possessed two natures. He was both fully divine (Is. 9:6; Matt. 28:19; John 1:1, 14; 8:58; 10:30; Titus 2:13–14; Heb. 1:8) and fully human (John 1:14; Phil. 2:5–8; Heb. 4:14–16). As God incarnate in human flesh, He was designated “that Holy One” at His birth (Luke 1:35). See note on Matt. 1:21 for the work of Christ. For more on the title Son referring to the PERSON OF CHRIST, see Is. 9:6; Luke 1:35; Heb. 1:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 2, 1, 'Bethlehem of Judea. There were two places in Palestine called Bethlehem. The one referenced here is a village approximately 5 miles (8 km) S of Jerusalem. The other was the town of Bethlehem approximately 7 miles (11 km) NW of Nazareth in Galilee.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 2, 2, 'King of the Jews. An indication that the Magi were Gentiles and that Matthew desired to make the gospel and worship inclusive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 2, 11, 'gold, frankincense, and myrrh. Some of the most valuable and transportable gifts available, they would doubtless be essential for financially supporting Joseph’s family on the trip into Egypt. Frankincense was a perfume that was widely used in religious rites (Ex. 30:34); and “myrrh,” a tree sap native to northeastern Africa, was a popular ointment used for burial preparation, as well as the main ingredient for the anointing oil (Ex. 30:23–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 2, 'kingdom of heaven. Matthew mostly uses “heaven” instead of “God,” in deference to the Jewish practice of avoiding the divine name. Mark and Luke, who were writing to more non-Jewish audiences, use “kingdom of God” quite frequently.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 3, 'The voice. Matthew quotes Is. 40:3. Just as John the Baptist was a voice declaring Prepare the way of the LORD at Jesus’ first coming, so God’s followers today should be at work to prepare for the Second Coming (see Matt. 28:19–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 4, 'John’s simple clothing and diet signified simplicity and poverty (similar to the Essenes community at nearby Qumran). This contrasted with the wealth and self-indulgence of the elite who came out to see him. locusts. There is a long history of scholarly debate over whether this term refers to an insect (listed as a clean food in Lev. 11:21–22) or to the carob bean.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 7, 'Pharisees and Sadducees. Two major Jewish parties. For more on the Pharisees, see Mark 2:16; 12:13; on the Sadducees, see Mark 12:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 9, 'Abraham as our father. John is warning his audience, and Matthew is warning his readers, that religious family background does not assure salvation. It is not a birthright.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 11, 'baptize … with water. Also translated “baptize in water.” Baptism by immersion was a common practice in Judaism for non-Jews who wished to become part of the Jewish religion. The Essenes, a Jewish sect in Qumran (by the Dead Sea and Jordan River), practiced it as a symbolic act by which one’s impurities were washed away. Thus it was appropriate for baptism to take place in the flowing river.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 12, 'unquenchable fire. A fire that cannot be put out until it runs out of fuel. For more on HELL and the hell fire, see 13:42; 25:41; Mark 9:43; Jude 7; and note on Is. 34:9–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 3, 16, 'came up. On the NT mode of BAPTISM as immersion, see Acts 8:38–39; Col. 2:12–13; Rom. 6:3–4. See also John 3:3, 5 on the meaning of BAPTISM. Spirit. The one common feature that all four Gospels record in the telling of this event is the descent of the Spirit. This anointing with the Spirit (Luke 4:18; Acts 10:38) equips Jesus for ministry. It is also a precursor of the Spirit baptism that separates Christian baptism from previous Jewish baptism (John 3:5; Acts 19:1–6). Note that all three persons of the Godhead or Trinity are present at the baptism (Jesus is baptized; the Father speaks; the Spirit descends). Matthew concludes his Gospel with a similar emphasis on the presence of all three members of the TRINITY in the baptism of all believers (see 28:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 4, 2, 'fasted forty days and forty nights. Only Matthew mentions the fasting. He wishes to parallel Moses’ fasting for 40 days and nights on Mount Sinai in the desert (Deut. 9:9–18). It also parallels the 40 years that the Israelites wandered in the desert beset by temptations and testing (Deut. 8:2–3). See also Introduction to Matthew, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 4, 3, 'stones become bread. The tempter first attacks Jesus at the point of His greatest immediate need. In similar ways we are tempted at our greatest vulnerability—e.g., materialism in the more developed world, and power (both religious and political) in the developing world. The temptations of materialism and power are the two big temptations of Jesus according to Matthew (see v. 8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 4, 8, 'kingdoms of the world. Matthew places this temptation last. In Greek what comes last or first is most important. For Matthew, Jesus’ kingship is key to his Gospel (see Introduction, “Message”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 4, 23, 'teaching … preaching … healing. Jesus’ mission and evangelism was holistic—it encompassed proclamation, as well as social and physical concerns.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 1, 'mountain. Matthew continues to parallel Jesus with Moses (see Introduction, “Message”). Matthew wishes to portray Jesus as the new Moses giving the new law on the “new Sinai.” Each author, under the guidance and inspiration of the Holy Spirit, selects his material to reach his audience. Matthew wanted his Jewish audience to read all these parallels and think of Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 3, 'Blessed. Some have translated this as “happy.” But it is doubtful that Jesus is speaking of the subjective state of happiness. The objective state of blessedness is independent of outward circumstances. If the beatitudes are divided into ten (one for each verse), they can be divided in similar fashion as the two tables of the law given on Mount Sinai: the first four deal with one’s relationship with God, the last six with fellow human beings (compare Ex. 20:1–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 3, 'poor in spirit. In Matthew Jesus includes the phrase “in spirit” to make the poor part of the spiritual community. The poor in spirit can also refer to all who depend on God, no matter their social or economic status.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 13, 'salt. Salt was used for flavoring and preserving (in a society without refrigeration). The metaphor is an evangelistic call to intermingle with the world and transform it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 14, 'light. Lamps were mostly small clay pots with wicks burning olive oil. They were put on stands to maximize their benefit. The Christian’s evangelistic outreach can be very effective if it is strategically positioned in the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 17, 'For more on Jesus’ understanding of the heart of the LAW, see 22:36–40. For other important NT insights on the LAW, see John 14:15; Rom. 3:20, 31; 7:12, 14; James 2:10, 12; Rev. 12:17; 14:12. For more on LAW, see Ex. 20:1–17; Heb. 8:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 17, 'Law … Prophets. The first five books of the OT and all the rest of the ancient Scripture. Jesus is not here making a distinction between ceremonial, civil, or moral laws. He is affirming all of God’s will as recorded in the Hebrew Scriptures, and showing its continuity. Though the will of God as defined in the OT Scriptures continues, Jesus takes it a step further: He fulfills it; i.e., He “fills it up”—He gives the Scriptures their fuller meaning (as illustrated in the six contrasting statements in 5:21–48; see Jer. 31:33; Ezek. 36:26–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 18, 'jot … tittle. The smallest Greek letter (the iota) and the smallest projection on a Hebrew letter (like the bottom of a j). Jesus emphasizes the permanence of Scripture (here the OT) even in the new era that He is inaugurating.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 20, 'scribes and Pharisees. They were exacting and scrupulous in following the 613 laws found in the Torah. But the righteousness that Jesus demanded was one that was demonstrated in more than keeping the external particularities of the law. It went to the heart of motive. He gives six examples in vv. 21–48.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 21, 'The issues addressed in these verses have been traditionally called the six antitheses (or contrasting statements), because they each begin with You have heard that it was said … but I say to you. Jesus takes a teaching or understanding of a scriptural passage and gives it a fuller, deeper, and Christian understanding (v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 21, 'murder. In the Torah, breaking the sixth commandment was limited to taking life. Jesus expands that understanding to include the motives, thoughts, and actions that lead in some instances to acts of killing. One such is anger or not reconciling with one who has something against you.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 26, 'penny. Smallest Roman copper coin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 27, 'The previous understanding of adultery was the overt physical sexual act with another person’s wife. Jesus expands the sin to include lustful thoughts and desires.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 31, 'divorce. Two rabbinic schools interpreted Deut. 24:1 to allow for divorce: Hillel allowed it for any reason; Shammai allowed it for only adultery. Jesus is closer to Shammai and allows divorce for sexual immorality. Jesus’ point here is not so much the exception (see 19:3–9; Mark 10:11–12; Luke 16:18). Rather, He corrects the misuse of a biblical passage by accenting the importance and permanence of marriage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 33, 'swear. The focus is on truthfulness, not on making vows in general or taking an oath in a legal setting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 38, 'Some have seen this as a command for absolute pacifism. But if it is understood in the shame-honor society of Jesus, we might grasp the idea that while Jesus is ruling out physical retaliation, he supports other expressions of dealing with evil. To give the person your cloak (an undergarment) also is to stand before them naked. To look on a naked person is a shame. Thus, the Christian gently invites the abuser to experience shame for the deed. To insist on going the second mile after you have fulfilled the legal requirement of taking the Roman soldier’s load the first mile is to be in charge. Jesus is teaching an active pacifism. He rejects violent vengeance and retaliation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 41, 'one mile. The Roman mile was about 4,855 feet (1,480 m).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 5, 48, 'perfect. Picturing maturity; not sinless perfection. This is in the context of this last contrasting statement: God demonstrates His perfection by loving His enemies, and by treating good and bad persons alike in terms of good gifts. When we show love and mercy to friend and foe alike, our righteousness exceeds that of the scribes and Pharisees (v. 20) and we attain this kind of godly perfection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 1, 'charitable deeds. Jesus here focuses on three religious acts: generosity to the needy, prayer, and fasting. He is not contradicting His statement in 5:14–16 where He encourages His hearers to let their light shine and let their good deeds be seen. He is not condemning public prayer, fasting, and charity; rather He is condemning the self-centered nature of public religiosity (in 5:14–16 the acts are God-centered).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 9, 'Our Father. The use of “our” (and the use of plural pronouns throughout) demonstrates that Jesus is not against public prayer (see verses above). The Lord’s Prayer is intended to be a public prayer. The intimate address, “Father” (translation from Abba, “daddy”), indicates the personal relation with God that is prevalent in the NT. But the phrase in heaven and subsequent language about hallowed be Your name and the coming of the kingdom speak to the majesty and otherness of God. God is both near us and approachable and at the same time majestically beyond us.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 11, 'Give us. The prayer starts with God and His concerns, and only then moves on to our requests and desires. The English word “prayer” comes from the Latin precarious, which means “to beg.” Thus requests dominate our prayers, rather than being similar to a conversation between a parent and a child.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 16, 'Jesus rejected the fasting that involved a mournful and sad countenance, placing ashes on one’s head, and general unhappiness. He actually goes in the opposite direction and recommends an outward look that was reserved for joyous occasions—washing the face and anointing the head. Jesus was making the practice of fasting a discipline that differed from the ritualistic practice of the Pharisees who religiously did it on Mondays and Thursdays. Note that Is. 58:3–9—the most extensive biblical passage on fasting—speaks of fasting not as a ritual, but in terms of outreach to the poor and needy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 21, 'For STEWARDSHIP of material possessions, see Gen. 1:28; Deut. 8:18; Ps. 24:1. On the principle of giving, see Acts 20:35; 2 Cor. 9:7–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 24, 'mammon. An Aramaic word for “wealth.” In the first century A.D., those who had many possessions were often obsessed with accumulating more and not with sharing (Luke 12:13–21). Wealth and material things were the god and master of their lives. Little has changed since then. Today in our materialistic society, selfish prosperity has become our ultimate goal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 25, 'worry. Both here and in Luke 12:13–34 the teaching on worry is tied into instructions and parables on rich people who have made wealth and material things their god, and are worried and obsessed with getting more. Thus, in a primary sense this instruction on worry is meant for the well-off in the times of Jesus. It is not for those who don’t have, as much as for those who have and are not satisfied. Those who travel around the world today may observe that poor Christians in the developing world are happy, joyous, and trust in Jesus much more than Christians who live in material comfort. Jesus’ counsel on worry, however, can be applied to all situations by all persons. This passage teaches that worry is useless and pagan, demonstrating a lack of trust in God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 6, 33, 'seek first. This is the punch line for the teaching beginning with v. 19. Money, material treasures, and prosperity should not be the priority. God’s kingdom and His righteousness (which can also be translated “justice,” and which would make sense in the first-century oppressive social and economic setting) should be the priority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 1, 'Judge. This is not a blanket statement against critiquing and condemning bad behavior. It is against hypocritical criticism that aims to tear down another while building up oneself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 6, 'dogs … swine. Dogs and pigs were both detestable and unclean scavengers in first-century Palestine.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 7, 'Ask. This is not an unconditional promise that God will answer all requests positively if we pray with tenacity. This verse must be viewed in light of the entire Sermon on the Mount which emphasizes God’s grace and particularly Jesus’ teaching on worry (6:25–34). This passage encourages us to be persistent in pursuing God, and He will give us the gifts that He knows that we need.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 12, 'The “Golden Rule” has been stated in both its negative and positive forms in many cultures such as Hinduism, Buddhism, and Confucianism, and in Greek and Roman ethical teachings. One hundred years before Jesus, the famous Jewish teacher, Rabbi Hillel, said it in its negative form to a Gentile inquirer: “What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbor; that is the whole Torah, while the rest is commentary thereon; go and learn it” (Shabbath 31a). Jesus frames the idea positively, and puts it in the unique context of the Sermon on the Mount in which grace and love are prominent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 13, 'gate … way. The image of two ways was common in first-century Jewish and non-Jewish philosophy. Jesus’ description of a narrow gate and road is meant to show that the countercultural new law that He presented in the Sermon on the Mount is not easy or popular to live and practice. Jesus’ teachings on religious works and worship, violence and relations to one’s enemies, and relation to material goods all went against the popular cultural norms of the day.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 20, 'A prophet’s “fruit” (the impact of his or her life and work) is one of the four tests (see 1 Thess. 5:19–21) to determine the genuineness of the gift. For other tests, see Is. 8:20; Jer. 28:9; 1 John 4:1–2. For more on SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 24, 'does them. On works being used as evidence in God’s JUDGMENT, see 16:27; Rom. 2:6, 13; James 2:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 24, 'rock. The emphasis is not on the composition of the building, but the foundation. The rock foundation is the teachings that Jesus just presented on the mount. As the children of Israel covenanted to do all the words from God that Moses received on Sinai, so Jesus challenged His hearers to build on a rock by hearing and obeying this new law of the kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 7, 28, 'Matthew uses the words in these verses, or similar phrases, to indicate the close of each of the five major sections of the Gospel (see 11:1; 13:53; 19:1; 26:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 8, 1, 'The second major section of Matthew begins with a series of actions by Jesus. The Synoptic Gospel writers portray Jesus not simply as a preacher and teacher, but also as One who is concerned about the whole person through His actions. Words and actions, proclamation and demonstration, are equally important.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 8, 2, 'leper. Leprosy in the Bible is not exclusively modern Hansen’s disease. It included several skin disorders referred to by the same name. Leprosy was the most dreaded disease in Bible times both because of its physical discomfort and the isolation that it brought. The lepers were excluded from society; they had to wear torn clothes, bare their heads, and cry, “Unclean, unclean” (Lev. 13:35). No person would jeopardize his or her ritual purity or health by coming in close contact with them, much less touch them. But Jesus not only healed the leper, He touched him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 8, 11, 'sit down with Abraham. Writing to Jews, Matthew is here emphasizing the universal nature of the gospel. OT prophets proclaimed it (Is. 56:3, 6–8; 66:12, 19; Mal. 1:11), but some in Jesus’ day chose to ignore it. For Jesus to picture unclean Gentiles eating together with the holy patriarchs was astonishing and scandalous. Note that at formal meals it was customary for the participants to recline on couches or pillows with feet extended away from the table, rather than sitting. Eating together demonstrated fellowship, friendship, and solidarity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 8, 19, 'These verses emphasize the cost of discipleship. Following Jesus is not always easy and comfortable. Jesus’ and the disciples’ itinerant life contrasted with the settled life of the Jewish leaders and the powerful in society. Jesus’ command to Follow Me is a clarion call that takes precedence over all other duties and relationships.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 8, 22, 'let the dead bury their own dead. It is possible that the disciple was not asking permission to go to his father’s funeral, but wished to wait until his elderly father died before following Jesus. But Jesus did not want any conditions that would interfere with full commitment, attention, and total loyalty to the mission. Thus the strong words “let the spiritual dead bury the physical dead” (or ironically, “let those who have been dead for a long time bury the recent dead”). The demands of discipleship are radical. Jesus is not afraid to overturn accepted social and religious obligations in calling His disciples to a mission that involves total commitment to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 8, 27, 'obey Him. Matthew 8 focuses on the complete authority of Jesus: He has authority over leprosy, paralysis, fever, demon possession, and even the elements that caused the storm. The contrast is stark: this humble, homeless, itinerant preacher has power and authority over all the forces of evil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 6, 'In the Sermon on the Mount, Jesus shows His authority over the law; in chap. 8 He has authority over physical ailments, demons, and creation; here His authority extends to sin. The Jewish view of illness was that it was caused because of some sin that was committed (John 9:2). For the onlookers and antagonists to believe that Jesus truly had the authority to forgive sins, the act of healing had to follow the pronouncement of forgiveness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 9, 'Matthew’s acceptance of Jesus’ call to discipleship opened the door for other tax collectors, outcasts, and sinners (i.e., all who did not follow the scruples and traditions of the Pharisees) to be part of Jesus’ circle. This, however, led to harsh criticism of Jesus. For the Pharisees, a respectable Jew, much more a leader, would be concerned with appearance and image. Those we associate with says much about who we are. But for Jesus the “kingdom” life was not based on image, but on helping and saving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 14, 'Jesus supports the discipline of fasting. However, He denies that it is necessary for His followers at this time. The principle laid out here is that spiritual disciplines, though biblical and necessary, all have their time and place.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 17, 'wineskins. In ancient Palestine, wine was kept in goatskin containers. The new grape juice would ferment over time and stretch the containers. An old bag would not be able to withstand the pressure and would burst; thus a new bag was recommended for new juices. Jesus used this illustration to emphasize that His way was new, and would not fit into the old, discredited ways of the Pharisees, or even within some of the limitations of the Mosaic laws.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 20, 'touched. This woman, who was suffering from a menstrual disorder and had been hemorrhaging for 12 years, was considered ritually unclean and was excluded from social and religious activities. She could not touch or be touched by anyone. In her desperation she was prepared to ignore all the taboos and restrictions and reach out for salvation from the master healer. Here Matthew also wishes to show that Jesus’ power and authority over sickness needs only a simple touch. As it was with Peter’s mother-in-law (8:15) and the dead girl (9:25), so it was here: it only took a touch to effect deliverance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 22, 'made well. The same word in Greek for “to save.” When Jesus physically heals He also saves holistically.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 24, 'sleeping. A biblical metaphor for death (Dan. 12:2; John 11:11; 1 Thess. 4:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 9, 25, 'took her by the hand. Whoever touches or is in the room with a corpse becomes ritually unclean (Num. 19:14–16). Jesus breaks those laws to bring wholeness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 1, 'Falls within the second of the five major blocks of teaching in Matthew (see Introduction, “Outline”). This is often called the Missionary Discourse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 2, 'apostles. This is the only time that Matthew calls the disciples “apostles.” The word means “sent ones,” and thus fits the context of this discourse. In the letters of Paul the designation is more common, and Paul includes himself as one. In those letters the designation primarily refers to one who has personally seen the Lord and who has been directly commissioned by Christ to proclaim His resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 4, 'Cananite. The Aramaic word is “Zealot.” Simon the Zealot could either be a religious zealot, or more than likely, he belonged to (or previously had membership in) a radical, revolutionary group that was committed to the violent overthrow of the Roman government and all those who worked for them or supported them. They (particularly the extremist group called “Sicarii”) resorted to guerilla tactics, mostly waylaying soldiers or tax collectors and assassinating them with daggers on deserted roads. The composition of this band of apostles demonstrates the inclusiveness of Jesus. That He would have a zealot (who might have been supportive of the very violence that Jesus condemned in the Sermon on the Mount) and Matthew the tax collector (the nemesis of the zealot) demonstrates the grace and openness of Jesus and His desire to make the Christian community unified.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 5, 'Jesus commissions the apostles, laying out four aspects of their mission that parallel His mission as laid out by Matthew in the previous chapters: (1) go first to the Jews, the primary audience of Matthew’s Gospel (vv. 5–6); (2) preach and perform miracles (vv. 7–8); (3) travel as simply as possible (vv. 9–10); and (4) prepare for success and failure, with blessings for those who receive the missionaries and curses for those who reject them (vv. 11–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 5, 'Samaritans. These were the offspring of intermarriage between the northern Israelite tribes left behind in Samaria after the Assyrian invasion in 722 B.C., and other peoples that were imported by the Assyrians (2 Kin. 17:1–24). The Samaritans were seen as unclean and not pure Jews. After the Jews returned from the Babylonian captivity, the tensions grew and were intense during the time of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 14, 'shake off the dust. Pharisaical Jews would often shake off the dust from their feet when leaving a Gentile town as a symbolic act when they departed that ritually unclean place. Here Jesus instructed the apostles to do the same to Jewish cities and towns if the inhabitants refused to receive them or hear their message. The symbolic gesture here signifies separation, rejection, and divine judgment for those who made the wrong choice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 19, 'what you should speak. This is not an admonition against careful preparation when preaching or presenting the gospel. The point is about “worrying”: Don’t stop preparing; stop worrying! The Christian can depend on the Spirit to say the appropriate thing at the right time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 23, 'flee. Jesus makes it clear in this verse that martyrdom is not to be sought. Run from persecution!');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 25, 'Beelzebub. The meaning of the name is uncertain. Many scholars believe that it means “Lord of the flies.” In the time of Christ it was a popular name for Satan, the “prince of demons.” In 12:24 the Pharisees accused Jesus of casting out demons with the power of Satan. Jesus is telling His apostles that if He, their master, is thus accused, they should not expect any less.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 28, 'soul. Jesus is not making or supporting the Greek dichotomy between soul and body as separate entities. Jewish teaching did not think of persons as having souls separate from the body as Greek philosophy taught. Thus, here Jesus is using the term “soul” to mean “eternal life”: Do not fear those who can take you from this present life (body), but fear Him who can also take away eternal life (soul). hell. Jesus not only taught the reality of HELL (see Matt. 13:42 and note on 25:41), but He also indicated that it would lead to the total destruction of the entire person (compare Mal. 4:1, 3; Rom. 6:23; 2 Pet. 3:12; Rev. 20:14). Since the wicked do not have immortality (see 1 Tim. 6:16 and notes on 1 Cor. 15:51–55), it is impossible for them to live perpetually in fire. On the placement of HELL and hell fire in the flow of history, see 2 Pet. 3:12–13; Rev. 20:9–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 29, 'copper coin. Worth about 1/16 of a day’s wage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 10, 34, 'sword. In this passage Jesus seems to be contradicting His teaching on nonviolence and loving enemies and family. Rather than contradicting Himself, He is making two points: First, He is saying that the gospel at times engenders conflict because of the deep differences and disagreements that arise by those who accept it and those who don’t. Second, family and its place in society are redefined in its popular social understanding. The blood family is no longer at the top of the hierarchy in the setting of the gospel. Acceptance of, and love for, Jesus must be primary. To be sure, the Scripture teaches the importance of the family bond; but not at the expense of following Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 11, 3, 'Are You the Coming One. Herod imprisoned John the Baptist because John had publicly rebuked him for marrying his brother Philip’s wife (14:3–5). While in prison John began to doubt whether Jesus was truly the coming Messiah, especially the type of Messiah of whom Mal. 3 speaks, or he himself predicted (3:10–12)—one whose mission is that of fiery judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 11, 4, 'Go and tell John. Jesus alters the traditional understanding of the Messiah’s mission in His response to John’s messengers. Instead of a political messiah who will bring judgment on the enemies of the Jews and break the yoke of Roman imperialism, Jesus is the Messiah who is socially, spiritually, and personally conscious of the needy: He heals, resurrects, and proclaims good news to the poor. Matthew is intent on demonstrating that the mission of Jesus is more than giving a new law, and more than dying on the cross. Those are most important; but not to the exclusion of social action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 11, 28, 'rest. When God met with Moses in the wilderness, He told him that He would bring rest to the children of Israel through Moses’ leadership (Ex. 33:14). Here, Jesus, the new Moses as pictured by Matthew, promises rest to those who labor and are burdened down.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 11, 29, 'yoke. A heavy wooden harness placed around the neck of a beast of burden (particularly an ox). It was attached to a vehicle used to carry heavy loads. The metaphorical yokes that Jesus’ audience were carrying were many: the oppressive legal demands of the religious leaders of the day based on their interpretation of the Mosaic Law; the economic oppression brought on by the wealthy, particularly that of debts and related legal cases; and the oppressive Roman taxation system. Jesus invites these burdened ones to take His yoke. This is not a less stringent interpretation of the law, as He makes very clear in the Sermon on the Mount. Rather it is the lightness of the yoke that He emphasizes. The lightness consists of meekness and humility in heart. These characteristics make the fuller demands of Jesus much easier to bear than even that of scribes and Pharisees. When the Christian takes on Jesus’ yoke, he or she will find true refreshment (or rest) for the soul.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 12, 2, 'not lawful to do on the Sabbath. The fourth commandment (Ex. 20:8–11; Deut. 5:12–15) commanded rest from work on the Sabbath, and Ex. 34:21 even specified that there should be no harvesting. The expanded laws, as found in the Jewish Mishnah (see note on Mark 7:8), list 39 categories of work that were prohibited on the Sabbath. Harvesting grain was one. Some rabbis even discouraged walking through a grainfield if the grain was even at ankle height; if one’s ankle accidentally knocked off the grain by stepping on it, it would be considered threshing. Jesus and His disciples not only deliberately walked through the grainfield, but they harvested, threshed, and ate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 12, 8, 'Lord even of the Sabbath. At issue in this debate with the Pharisees is not whether the Sabbath should be observed or abolished. Nowhere in the NT is there any statement that Jesus abolished the observance of the seventh-day Sabbath. The issue at hand is how the Sabbath should be observed, and who has the authority to make that determination. For the Pharisees, the traditions spelled out by their rabbis were the authority. Jesus claimed ultimate authority, for He is the Creator of the Sabbath. He is Lord of the Sabbath; thus it is the Lord’s Day. For Jesus (as illustrated in His actions), mercy, relief of pain and suffering, and filling basic needs determined the legitimacy of appropriate Sabbath work. The OT Sabbath commandments all focused on creation and redemption (Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11; 21:2–3; 31:12–17; Lev. 25:1–22; Deut. 5:12–15; 15:1–6). Sabbaths were not meant to be burdensome; rather they were to be restorative—a back-to-Eden restoration. For more on Christ and the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Mark 2:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 12, 12, 'lawful to do good. For more on how to keep the SABBATH, see Ex. 20:8–11; Lev. 23:32; Neh. 13:15–22; Is. 58:13–14; Jer. 17:19–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 12, 24, 'Beelzebub. Exorcists in the time of Jesus used a variety of methods to cast out demons. Among them were incantations that included calling the names of a long list of ancient figures, the use of herbs and various potions, the use of nose rings to draw the spirit out, etc. That Jesus never used those methods led His antagonists to accuse Him of casting out demons by Satan himself. Practicing black magic was punishable by stoning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 12, 31, 'blasphemy against the Spirit. This is traditionally known as the “unpardonable sin.” The context of this statement (11:24, 28, 30) suggests that the unpardonable sin that the Pharisees were committing was that they were attributing to Satan (Beelzebub), rather than to the Holy Spirit, the power by which Jesus did His miraculous works. From this we can extrapolate that the essence of this sin is the deliberate refusal to acknowledge the working of God’s Spirit. This deliberate and obstinate stance ultimately leads to a hardening of the heart, and a total rejection of the Spirit and thus Jesus Himself. Under such circumstances salvation through Jesus is impossible. Compare the sin that leads to death (1 John 5:16–17). The refusal to heed the Spirit’s warning stems from a sinful, perverted heart (Jer. 17:9; Titus 1:15). It is important to note, then, that if one is concerned as to whether he or she has committed the unpardonable sin, it is almost certain that that stage has not been reached. Such a smiting of the conscience means that the Holy Spirit is still at work in the individual. For more on the results of SIN, see Rom. 3:23; 6:23. On its remedy, see 1 John 1:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 12, 36, 'idle word. In this context Jesus is addressing the fact that the Pharisees refused to acknowledge His work as that of God and the Holy Spirit. In v. 34 He calls them a “brood of vipers,” who do not speak good things. It is these words that Jesus refers to as idle, not casual expressions or humorous talk.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 12, 42, 'queen of the South. She is the same as the queen of Sheba, who came to visit Solomon and acknowledged his wisdom (see notes on 1 Kin. 10:1–13; 2 Chr. 9:1–12). Jesus rebukes the scribes and Pharisees for not accepting Him, though He is greater than Solomon. Compare with previous statements: Jesus claims to be “greater than the temple” (v. 6) and “greater than Jonah” (v. 41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 13, 3, 'parables. The use of parables was widespread during the time of Jesus, especially by the rabbis. A parable is often defined as “an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.” They can also be thought of as short or long metaphors (or comparisons) used to illustrate a greater point. They are not meant to be interpreted literally. Jesus’ parables differed from those of the rabbis in a number of significant ways. Unlike the rabbis, whose parables were often obscure and were used to prove theological points from the Mosaic Law, Jesus used everyday incidents and common objects as a foundation. His parables were also more vivid and had elements of shock and surprise, such as a punch line at the end.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 13, 24, 'This parable is unique to Matthew, and it illustrates the mixed character of those in the church. Explicit is the warning that Christ’s disciples should not anticipate the judgment by excluding members who are considered sinners. This parable rejects the strictures of the Pharisees and the Essenes that clearly separated the righteous from the unrighteous in their communities. tares. Probably “darnel,” a poisonous weed, which when young is hard to differentiate from wheat, but is quite distinguishable when it is mature.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 13, 31, 'mustard seed. In its cultivated form, the mustard seed was probably the smallest seed used by the farmers in Jesus’ audience. Under the right conditions it could grow to about 10 feet (3 m), which is significant growth for a shrub from a seed that small. The point Jesus makes is that the kingdom, even though it has an insignificant beginning, will have a glorious ending, producing great results, and making a significant impact in the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 13, 33, 'leaven. Yeast. In other passages of Scripture yeast is used metaphorically for sin and uncleanness (16:5–12; Ex. 12:15–20; 23:18; 34:25; Lev. 2:11; 6:17; 1 Cor. 5:6–8; Gal. 5:9). Here Jesus uses it positively for growth (see also Lev. 7:13–14; 23:17). The yeast fungus, though small, seemingly insignificant, and dormant, has the most powerful effect when used for making bread. three measures. About 36 quarts (39 l). The three measures of meal or flour made about the largest amount of dough a woman was able to knead at one time. Such an amount was able to feed about 100 people. As in the mustard seed parable, the kingdom, though small at first and even seemingly invisible, will grow and have a great impact in and on the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 13, 42, 'gnashing of teeth. Jesus uses this expression several times in Matthew’s gospel and once in Luke’s. It is a metaphor for the punishment of the wicked, and is usually related to the concept of being cast into outer darkness or fire. For more on HELL and the punishment of the wicked, see 10:28; 25:41, 46.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 13, 44, 'The parables of the treasure hidden and beautiful pearls both make the point that the kingdom is of such great value that one should be prepared to do whatever it takes to gain it. Of course, Jesus is not teaching that His grace can be bought or worked for.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 13, 47, 'The parable of the dragnet makes the same point as the parable of the wheat and tares (vv. 24–30). They are both unique to Matthew, they both teach the mixed character of the church, and they both emphasize that it is the Lord who separates the righteous from the unrighteous at the end of the age. We cannot and should not try to determine who will be saved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 14, 1, 'Herod the tetrarch. Herod Antipas was the son of Herod the Great, who ordered the killing of the babies in Bethlehem (2:16). Herod Antipas was one of the three rulers over the three districts of Palestine during the times of Jesus. He was also the Herod who heard the case against Jesus before His crucifixion (Luke 23:6–12). His half-brother’s (Philip who lived in Rome) wife, Herodias, left her husband to live with Herod Antipas. She was also his niece. John the Baptist condemned this incestuous relationship (Lev. 18:16; 20:21). Herodias sought revenge by demanding the beheading of John.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 14, 13, 'This miracle of “feeding the five thousand” is the only miracle that is recorded in all four Gospels. It is, therefore, possibly the most important miracle that Jesus performed. It recalls the miracle of manna that fed the Israelites in the wilderness, the miracle of Elisha and the widow’s oil (2 Kin. 4:1–7), and the feeding of the 100 men by Elisha with 20 loaves of barley bread and some newly ripened grain (2 Kin. 4:42–44). It also anticipates the Last Supper. The Gospel of John does not have a description of the Last Supper with the theological interpretation of the meaning of the drinking of Jesus’ blood and eating of His flesh. That discussion is placed in Jesus’ discourse following the feeding of the 5,000 (John 6:1–58). The miracle also anticipates the great eschatological banquet at the end of the age—a banquet that was widely taught in both Jewish and Christian apocalyptic writings. The miracle also demonstrated Jesus’ holistic ministry that recognized the physical and economic needs of His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 14, 21, 'five thousand men. Women and children were not counted because in patriarchal societies they were the property of their husbands and fathers and had no standing on their own. Also, in public men and women were not to be seen together, associate with each other, or eat together. Most likely then, the women and children ate separately, and only the men were counted. Because Jesus fed everyone that day, it seems that the number fed would be closer to fifteen or twenty thousand.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 14, 25, 'fourth watch. About 3:00 to 6:00 a.m. Romans had four night watches (6:00–9:00 p.m.; 9:00–midnight; midnight–3:00 a.m., and 3:00–6:00 a.m.). Jews had three night watches (sunset-10:00 p.m., 10:00 p.m.–2:00 a.m., and 2:00 a.m.-sunrise). These are approximate times because time-keeping devices were not widespread in the ancient world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 15, 2, 'tradition of the elders. After the Babylonian exile, the Jews, in an attempt to keep the Torah perfectly (so that the exile experience would not be repeated), began developing meticulous rules and regulations that were expansions of the 613 laws found in the books of Moses. These were handed down orally from generation to generation until about A.D. 200, when they were put into writing in a book called the Mishnah. The question of the scribes and Pharisees as to why Jesus’ disciples did not wash their hands was not a question of inquiry into their personal hygiene; rather it was about ritual and ceremonial purity. The purpose of hand washing was to remove the defilement that was brought on the pious Jew by contact with ceremonially unclean persons or things. The developers of these traditions based them on Ex. 30:17–21, where God commanded the priests to wash their hands and feet before entering the tabernacle. This was expanded to everyday life. By the time the Mishnah was written, the number of things that caused defilement was so great that an entire section (or book, called Yadaim or “Hands”) was devoted to it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 15, 5, 'a gift to God. This was the practice of Corban. To circumvent one’s duty to support his parent, a Jew could take the Corban vow and dedicate to the temple the money that would have been used to take care of his parents.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 15, 11, 'defiles. By saying that it is not what goes into a person that defiles a person, Jesus is not making all foods permissible or healthy. Neither is He talking about literal hand washing (v. 20). Jesus uses literal outward eating and hand washing as a parable or symbol of external acts as contrasted with the internal state of the heart. Jesus turned the focus of the teachers of the law inside out: they were obsessed with the externals, while Jesus emphasized the internal and moral actions. For Him, sin was rooted inside of one’s being—the heart. We find it easier to maintain the external façade of being a Christian by what we do or do not do. However, while human beings look on the outward appearance, God looks on the heart. On God’s plan regarding the diet of humans, see Gen. 1:29–30; Lev. 11:2–23; Acts 10; 1 Cor. 10:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 15, 21, 'In this region of Tyre and Sidon Jesus encounters a predominantly Gentile community. The scene that Matthew records is one in which much prejudice and many barriers abound: religious, cultural, ethnic, and political.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 15, 26, 'little dogs. Is Jesus being harsh when He implies that the Gentile communities to which this woman belongs are dogs? A few would say yes, since dogs in that region were viewed as unclean, disgusting, and scavengers. Others point out that the Greek word indicates a puppy, therefore, a pet, which would take the edge off His words. It has also been suggested that Jesus’ words were accompanied by facial expressions and body language that cannot be captured in the text of the Gospel. The woman caught on that Jesus’ words were not meant to be final and she was willing to show Jesus that He can still help without giving up the primacy of His mission to the Jews (v. 24; 10:6; Acts 1:8; Rom. 1:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 1, 'sign. The Pharisees and Sadducees, who differed on much theologically, united here to test Jesus. They wanted Jesus to work a miracle to verify His legitimacy. But signs never convince skeptical persons. Jesus had performed major miracles: healings, raising the dead, feeding thousands; but none of that was convincing. And even the sign of the prophet Jonah (i.e., the three days and three nights in the fish, or, in Christ’s case, in the grave) would still not convince them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 13, 'Caesarea Philippi. This city is not to be confused with the city on the Mediterranean coast that Herod the Great built. The city of this passage was located several miles north of the Sea of Galilee. There was a strong Greco-Roman cultural influence in the city, which boasted many temples and idols.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 18, 'Peter … rock. The Greek text of Matthew plays on these two words: petros and petra, respectively. The interpretation with the strongest biblical support, and most widely held since the Protestant Reformation in the sixteenth century, is that petros is Peter, and petra refers to Christ who is the foundation and chief cornerstone of the church (1 Cor. 3:11; Eph. 2:20; 1 Pet. 2:4–7). church. In the Gospels, only here and in 18:17 is the term “church” used. The Greek word ekklesia means “assembly.” In the Septuagint translation of the OT, it refers to the children of Israel assembled together in worship. In the Greco-Roman world it is an exclusive civic or political assembly of the elite, but Jesus uses it in the OT sense for an inclusive assembly of believers and thus closely connects the NT church with Israel (compare Acts 7:38 which also uses the term ekklesia). On the relationship between the CHURCH on earth and Christ, see Eph. 1:22–23; 2:19–20; 5:23–27. Hades. In Greek it is the place of the dead, equivalent to the Hebrew sheol. The “gates of Hades” most likely has reference to the powers of death; that is, the forces of evil that are opposed to the kingdom of Christ. Compare Rev. 1:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 19, 'keys. Authority. The granting of the metaphorical keys cannot be interpreted as a special ecclesiastical power that church authorities hold over members. In chap. 18 Jesus stresses childlikeness and humility for those who lead and a communal ministry for believers. The power of binding and loosing is granted to the church as a whole in 18:18, rather than to any specific individual. There is also no evidence in this passage (or in the NT) that the giving of the keys includes the power to forgive sins and grant absolution by humans or church authority. will be bound. A literal reading of the Greek is “shall have been bound in heaven,” meaning that the church on earth will require only what heaven requires and will prohibit only what heaven prohibits.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 20, 'tell no one. Jesus commanded His disciples not to publicize His messiahship because of the false understanding of the role of the coming messiah. They expected an imperial messiah who would overthrow Roman rule and inaugurate a new age for the children of Abraham. In vv. 21–26 Jesus explains what type of messiah He is, and that His kingdom includes cross bearing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 23, 'Get behind Me, Satan! Satan is a Hebrew word meaning “accuser” or “adversary.” Peter’s rebuke, and seeming refusal to accept that Jesus’ mission involved suffering and death, was an attempt by Satan to deflect attention from the course that was appointed for the Messiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 27, 'works. On works being used as evidence in God’s JUDGMENT, see 7:24–27; 25:31–46; Rom. 2:13, 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 16, 28, 'Son of Man coming. Various attempts have been made to interpret this prophecy. Among them are these: (1) It was fulfilled at the resurrection and ascension of Jesus; (2) Jesus expected to return during the lifetime of some of His disciples, but His coming was delayed; thus this conditional prophecy was not fulfilled; (3) the prophecy was fulfilled at Pentecost and with the rapid spread of the gospel as recorded in the book of Acts; or (4) the fulfillment of the prophecy is found in the subsequent verses that report the transfiguration that happened a week later. Three of His disciples saw Jesus changed into a glorious being similar to how He will be at the Second Coming. This last interpretation is probably what Jesus had in mind.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 17, 1, 'The appearance of Moses and Elijah has significance at a number of levels: (1) They represent the Law and the Prophets, respectively. Jesus said that He did not come to abolish the Law or the Prophets but to fulfill them (5:17). (2) Moses and Elijah, like Jesus, performed miracles, were rejected, and encountered God on a mountain (Sinai and Horeb/Sinai [Ex. 24:12–18, 1 Kin. 19:8–19] respectively). (3) In Judaism these men also had last-day significance. In Deut. 18:15–18 Moses prophesied that God would raise up a prophet like himself. It was believed this would take place in the last days. In Mal. 4:5–6 it was also prophesied that Elijah would appear “before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD.” (4) Finally, both these figures were believed to be in heaven, having been resurrected (Moses, Jude 9) or translated without seeing death (Elijah, 2 Kin. 2:11–12). They became representative of those who will be saved at the Second Coming—both those who died in the Lord and those who are alive when Jesus returns (1 Thess. 4:16–17). The scene, portraying the transfigured Jesus with Moses and Elijah, is a type of the final last-day event with the glorified Jesus and all the saved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 17, 20, 'faith as a mustard seed. The mustard seed was the smallest seed known in ancient Palestine. The disciples in their failure to be able to cast out the demon lacked even that smallest amount of faith. With the smallest amount of faith Jesus’ followers should be able to do the seemingly impossible, like moving a mountain. People back then thought that mountains were pillars that held up the sky and kept in place the disc of the earth over the subterranean waters. For those pillars to be moved around would be the work of the gods. In Jesus’ theology even the tiniest faith can lead to the accomplishment of what is generally thought as impossible. This teaching of Jesus, of course, must be understood in the light of the rest of Scripture and not taken without understanding. Faith has to be in accord with God’s will and to His glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 17, 24, 'temple tax. Every Jewish male 20 years old and older was required to pay an annual temple tax. The Greek double drachma was worth the Hebrew half a shekel, which was the equivalent of two days’ wages. This tax was designated for the upkeep of the temple (Ex. 30:11–16). The temple tax collectors set up booths to collect these taxes. Only Matthew mentions this incident, possibly because he was a tax collector before he accepted Jesus’ invitation to become a disciple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 17, 27, 'piece of money. Large Greek silver coin equal to four drachmas.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 3, 'children. This is not a call to be “childish.” That was how the disciples were acting in their fight to be the greatest. Jesus’ call is to be “childlike”—having the humility, innocence, and dependence that is easily displayed by children. In Bible times children and women had no rights and in many instances were objects of oppression (esp. if they were fatherless; see James 1:27). They were weak and dependent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 6, 'millstone. One of two large circular stones that were used to grind grain as a donkey turned them. It was much heavier than the millstone women used each morning in their home for grinding grain (24:41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 8, 'cut it off. As with the reference of the millstone (v. 6), Jesus is not being literal here. He is emphasizing the seriousness of leading a weak member astray. Unfortunately, some Christians over the centuries have taken Jesus’ teaching literally and have mutilated themselves in what they see as literal obedience to Jesus’ command in this and similar verses (5:29; 19:12). The most famous example is the third-century church father Origen who castrated himself because of his lustful thoughts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 15, 'brother sins against you. This passage is dealing with the exercise of church discipline when issues appear between church members. In the process of reconciliation the wronged person takes the initiative.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 17, 'heathen and a tax collector. Jewish society generally did not socialize with Gentiles or tax collectors. Removal from church membership is the last step in the process meant to bring individuals to repentance and reconciliation if they will not listen to the one they wronged, or to the church as a body. Jesus’ instruction is especially strengthened by v. 18, which speaks about binding and loosing, understood to include the power of the local congregation to accept and dismiss members. Nevertheless, the terms used by Jesus remind us of His example in treating sinners and tax collectors (9:9–11; 11:19). His loving care and forgiveness demonstrate how the church should treat those who are disfellowshipped, seeking the ultimate restoration of all sinners.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 18, 'bind … loose. The language of legally binding decisions (see notes on v. 17 and 16:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 21, 'seven times. Judaism taught that forgiving a person three times demonstrated that one had a true spirit of forgiveness. Some rabbis made it clear that a fourth time was not required. Peter’s question went beyond that and, being generous, suggested the perfect number.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 22, 'seventy times seven. Or, seventy-seven times, according to some Greek manuscripts. Jesus’ response is that forgiveness does not have a terminus point.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 24, 'ten thousand talents. About 60 million days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 18, 28, 'a hundred denarii. About 100 days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 19, 3, 'divorce. See note on 5:31–32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 19, 8, 'hardness of your hearts. Although Jesus allowed an exception for sexual immorality (meaning “unchastity” in the Greek), His ideal was that there be no divorce. He takes His hearers back to Eden where there was no intent of divorce. He agrees that Moses allowed divorce. But it was not God’s ideal; it was because of the people’s hard hearts. See Matt. 5:31–32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 19, 10, 'See 1 Cor. 7:7–8, 26, 32–35; also see note on Matt. 18:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 19, 14, 'Let the little children come. Some have taken this as authorization for infant baptism. However, nothing in the context suggests this. Baptism in the NT is based on personal repentance, which can take place meaningfully only when the individual is old enough to understand clearly what it means.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 19, 24, 'eye of a needle. Centuries ago a legend developed that the “eye of a needle” was a small gate in Jerusalem which, with some effort, a camel could go through on its knees. Jesus, however, meant His statement to be more rigorous than later Christians wished it to be. He was speaking about the impossibility of a selfish wealthy person to enter the kingdom. To illustrate, He chose the largest animal and the smallest hole. Zacchaeus was an example of a wealthy person who shared with the poor, and thus salvation came to his house (Luke 19:8–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 20, 1, 'This parable of the vineyard workers turns traditional values upside-down. It is also a clear teaching that entrance into the kingdom is by a willingness to accept God’s grace alone and not by merit or the quantity or quality of work. The parable further teaches that God makes the determination of who enters the kingdom. Some we don’t expect to be there will be.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 20, 28, 'ransom. The Greek word was used commonly for the payment for the release of a slave from servitude.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 21, 2, 'donkey. A political or imperial personality in the time of Jesus would make such an entrance not on a donkey but on a warhorse, demonstrating greatness, intimidation, domination, and power. Jesus rides a donkey, the beast of burden, demonstrating meekness, gentleness, humility, and peace (18:1–5; 20:20–28; see also Zech. 9:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 21, 9, 'Hosanna. Originally in the Hebrew this could be translated, “Grant us salvation, we pray, O Lord.” But it evolved into an exclamation of praise.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 21, 12, 'bought and sold. This was not simply for secular profit but for enabling the worshiper to participate correctly in the temple worship. The marketplace covered the large outer court of the Gentiles. There the worshipers could purchase the various animals and birds for the sacrifices and exchange secular monies for money that was acceptable in the temple. This, however, was used as an opportunity to charge extremely high prices and oppress the poor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 21, 14, 'the blind and the lame … in the temple. Jesus not only overturned the tables, but He turned the worship system upside-down. Persons with infirmities were not allowed in the temple. Only those who were deemed absolutely pure and well were given access to the temple. Jesus not only accepted and welcomed these unacceptable ones into the temple, but healed them as well.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 21, 28, 'As the conflict between the religious leaders and Jesus intensifies, Jesus tells a series of parables that attacks their arrogance, greed, and self-righteousness. These confrontational parables illustrate the great reversal of the expected response to the coming Messiah: the leaders of Israel reject Him; and the outcasts and unacceptable accept Him. It is the classic Great Reversal and upside-down theme that is pervasive throughout the Gospels: those who are expected to be saved are lost, and the “sinners” are saved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 4, 'dinner. Social gatherings involving large quantities of food were common in Palestine in the time of Jesus, as in most non-Western cultures. It was, therefore, natural for Jesus to center many of His parables and teachings around this practice. Jesus Himself and His disciples seemed to be involved numerous times in settings with food and feasting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 16, 'The Herodians were Jews who supported the Roman rule of the Herods, in contrast to the Pharisees who were nationalists wanting independence from the Romans. Here the two groups worked together to trap Jesus, discredit Him, and perhaps accuse Him of treason. If He said yes to the question as to whether it was lawful to pay taxes to Caesar, then the Pharisees would denounce Him as a disloyal Jew; if He said no, the Herodians would accuse Him of incitement to rebellion and treason and demand His execution.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 19, 'a denarius. Common silver coin of the Roman Empire, the daily wage of a laborer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 23, 'Sadducees. This group now tried to trap Jesus after the failed attempt by the Pharisees and Herodians. The Sadducees used their denial of resurrection to accomplish the same purpose. The Sadducees accepted only the first five books of Scripture (the Pentateuch) as authoritative, and they found no direct teaching of the resurrection there.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 24, 'brother shall marry his wife. This is the levirate law of Deut. 25:5–6 that protected women who were left alone because of the death of their husbands. The man’s brother was required to marry and care for her, and thus also guarantee the continuance of his brother’s lineage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 30, 'like angels of God. In addition to the resurrection, the Sadducees also denied the existence of angels.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 36, 'For more on Jesus’ understanding of the LAW, see 5:17–19. See also Rom. 13:8–10; Gal. 5:14; 1 John 5:2–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 22, 40, 'these two commandments. Jesus profoundly summarizes the breadth and depth of God’s desires for His people: love God (Deut. 6:5) and love others (Lev. 19:18). These are the two great principles of the Decalogue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 2, 'Moses’ seat. This was possibly the metaphorical seat of authority that the teachers of the law (the scribes) and the Pharisees claimed. Based on the interchanges in the previous chapter and Jesus’ discourse in this one, Matthew here wishes to highlight that Jesus is the new Moses and He is the true authority who sits in Moses’ seat.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 4, 'heavy burdens. See note on 11:29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 5, 'phylacteries. These were small leather boxes containing parchments on which passages of Scripture were written. They were worn on the left forearm and the forehead (Ex. 13:9, 16; Deut. 6:8; 11:18) during prayer. The scribes and Pharisees widened these boxes and lengthened the borders of their garments (the four tassels at each corner of the men’s cloak) to show their piety.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 15, 'proselyte. Gentiles who were converted to Judaism were converted to the legalistic regulations of circumcision and the Mosaic Law and not to a personal relationship with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 23, 'Jesus’ bitter condemnation of the scribes and Pharisees in these famous woes holds those leaders accountable for the fate of the nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 23, 'tithe. Jesus is not abolishing the practice of giving one-tenth of one’s income to God. Just the opposite, Jesus’ comments reaffirm the permanency of the law of tithing and place it in perspective in relation to what is still more important (weightier): justice and mercy and faith. The scribes and Pharisees had extended the law of tithing on the produce of the land (Lev. 27:30; Deut. 14:22–29) to the smallest herbs such as mint, anise, and cummin (spices that were indigenous to that region of the world). Jesus affirmed this practice. But He condemned the hypocrisy of keeping the letter of the law in small matters while engaging in the exploitation and oppression that the prophets railed against (Is. 1:16–18; Hos. 6:6; Mic. 6:8). For more on tithing as an expression of STEWARDSHIP, see Gen. 14:20; Mal. 3:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 24, 'gnat … camel. Jesus compares the scribes and Pharisees to those obsessed with straining out the smallest unclean insect (a gnat) from their drinking water, yet they would eat (most likely figuratively) the camel, the largest unclean animal in Palestine (Lev. 11:4, 41–45).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 23, 37, 'Jesus’ lament over Jerusalem at the end of chap. 23, and His prediction of the destruction of the temple at the beginning of chap. 24, ties the final eschatological (last day) discourse (chaps. 24 and 25) with the indictment on Jerusalem’s religious leadership. In both passages Jerusalem is left desolate, without one stone left upon another.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 3, 'when will these things be? And what will be the sign of Your coming, and of the end of the age? The disciples were apparently making a connection between the destruction of Jerusalem and the end of time. As is seen at the end of the chapter (v. 50), because no one knows the precise time of the end, the disciples wished to have some signs that would give them a clue. In this chapter Jesus lists a number of events that have commonly been interpreted as signs or indicators that the SECOND COMING is near. These include turmoil (religious, political, military), natural disasters, hostility towards the disciples, apostasy, dissipation, and the proclamation of the gospel. Jesus, however, said that most of these are not indicators that the end has arrived (v. 6). These events are not intended to tell us the specific time of the SECOND COMING, but to remind us of the reality of that coming. Two events could be seen as more precise signs: the gospel preached in the entire world (v. 14) and the actual Second Coming, which is the only event specifically called a “sign” (v. 30). Matt. 24–25 is Christ’s fullest explanation of the SECOND COMING. For other important statements on His SECOND COMING, see Mark 13:1–37; Luke 21:5–36; John 14:1–3; Acts 1:6–11; 1 Thess. 4:13–18; 2 Pet. 3:10–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 13, 'endures. Compare Rev. 14:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 14, 'The great call—or commission—to proclaim the gospel to the world is given repeatedly in the NT (see 28:19–20; Mark 16:15; Luke 24:47; John 20:21). This worldwide proclamation is a clear sign that the coming of the Lord is about to occur. All humanity will be given an opportunity to respond to Christ. Only then will the cosmic conflict come to an end. On the Great Commission, see also Matt. 28:19–20; Acts 1:8; Rev. 10:11; 14:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 15, 'abomination of desolation. The clear context of the next five verses (vv. 16–20) is the destruction of Jerusalem that took place in A.D. 70. Therefore, this prophecy of Daniel (Dan. 9:26–27) points to, among other events, the destruction of Jerusalem by Rome.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 21, 'On last-day world conditions before the SECOND COMING, see vv. 23–25, 37–39; Luke 21:25–27; 1 Thess. 5:3; 2 Tim. 3:1–5; 2 Pet. 3:3–4. After the fall of Jerusalem and before the Second Coming of Christ the church was to go through oppression and persecution, as predicted in Dan. 7:21–26. Jesus promised that God in His sovereignty and love would limit the duration of that experience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 24, 'The existence of false prophets indicates that true ones are also to be expected at the end of time. For more on prophecy in the end time, see Joel 2:28–31; Rev. 12:17. On other SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 26, 'Among the many false christs, the use of the singular suggests that shortly before the coming of Christ a specific false christ will appear but not in the same way Jesus will come. On the manner of the SECOND COMING, see also 25:31; 1 Thess. 4:16–17; 2 Thess. 1:8; Rev. 1:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 29, 'Jesus described the darkening of the sun and moon and the falling of the stars as occurring at the end of the tribulation (see vv. 21–22). See note on Rev. 6:12–14. This correlates with the close of the 1,260-day prophetic period in A.D. 1798 (see Dan. 7:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 31, 'For a vivid portrayal of the RESURRECTION at the Second Coming, see 1 Thess. 4:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 32, 'On the practical meaning of the knowledge of the nearness of the SECOND COMING for the Christian life, see 1 Thess. 5:1–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 34, 'this generation. This has been difficult for Bible students to interpret over the centuries. Is the reference to the disciples’ generation, and thus to the destruction of Jerusalem? Or is Jesus referring to the Second Coming, the topic of the immediately preceding verses? Evidence suggests the former because the phrase “these things” is used in this chapter to refer to events leading up to the destruction of Jerusalem (vv. 3, 33). Also, in Matthew, the expression “this generation” is always a negative verdict by Jesus on those who were rejecting Him then. See 11:16; 12:41–42, 45; 23:36.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 36, 'Compare vv. 42–44 for what this means for the Christian life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 37, 'On what the world will be like at the end of time before the SECOND COMING, see vv. 21–25; Luke 21:25–27; 1 Thess. 5:3; 2 Tim. 3:1–5; 2 Pet. 3:3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 40, 'one taken … the other left. This is understood by some as a secret rapture. However, the taking of saved people to heaven at a time prior to the Second Coming contradicts 1 Thess. 4:16–17 and Heb. 11:39–40 which teach that none will precede the others there. A resurrection of the faithful dead at the Second Coming is followed by a gathering of all the saved to meet the Lord in the air in a manner that is far from secret. The phrase simply means that some will not be ready at the Second Coming of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 42, 'Watch. This is the basic point of the entire discourse of Matt. 24–25. No one knows exactly when Jesus will return, not even the angels (v. 36). We are instructed to know when it is near (vv. 32–33) but, in another sense, He will come unexpectedly as a thief in the night. Therefore, be prepared!');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 24, 44, 'For Christ’s advice on how to be ready, see the parables in 24:45–51; 25:1–13, 14–30, 31–46.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 1, 'to meet the bridegroom. Anticipating Christ’s return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 5, 'delayed. The concern here is not in the delay itself, but in the unpreparedness of the foolish ones. They failed to take into account the possibility of a delay. This, like the next two parables, gives one explanation of what it means to “watch.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 15, 'This parable, like the other two in this chapter, focuses on what Christians should be doing between now and the Second Coming—another description of what it means to “watch.” Here the emphasis is on the fact that Christians have various levels of abilities. But all are expected to use their gifts, resources, and talents to the maximum of their abilities, demonstrating faithful stewardship of their aptitudes. For more on STEWARDSHIP, see Gen. 1:28; Deut. 8:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 15, 'talents. A talent was about 6,000 days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 31, 'On caring for others and the heart of true religion, see Mic. 6:8; James 1:27. The parable demonstrates that our actions are important in the Judgment. Christianity is to make a difference in our lives. On works being used as evidence in God’s JUDGMENT at Christ’s SECOND COMING, see 7:24–27; 25:31–46; Rom. 2:6, 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 32, 'separate … sheep from the goats. The separation and judgment is based on how Christ’s followers treat the least and the marginal while they wait for Jesus’ return. This parable is not teaching salvation by legalistic works, which Paul rejects (Rom. 3:27). It articulates the ethical and social works that characterize the ministry of Jesus Himself, and that later the Apostle James requires of Christ’s followers (James 2:14–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 41, 'On the reality of HELL, see v. 46; 13:42; 2 Thess. 1:9; 2 Pet. 3:7, For more on HELL and hell fire, see Matt. 3:12; Mark 9:43; Jude 7; and note on Is. 34:9–10. For more on the destruction of the wicked, see note on Matt. 10:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 25, 46, 'On HELL and eternal punishment, see note on v. 41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 15, 'thirty pieces of silver. About 120 days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 17, 'Passover. This annual meal celebrated the deliverance of the children of Israel from Egyptian bondage (Ex. 12:1–30). With this supper, Jesus inaugurated a new institution that celebrated deliverance from sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 26, 'For other passages on the LORD’S SUPPER, see also Mark 14:22–25; Luke 22:19–24. For a fuller meaning of the symbolism (1) of the bread, see John 6:35, 48–59; 1 Cor. 10:16–17; and (2) of the entire service, see 1 Cor. 11:23–28. For the service that accompanied the Lord’s Supper, see John 13:1–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 28, 'new covenant. Jesus’ words here possibly are in reference to Ex. 24:8 which speak of the “blood of the [old] covenant” that God made with the children of Israel. That covenant involved the forgiveness of sins via the sacrificial system. Under this new covenant, the remission of sins for the world comes through the shedding of Jesus’ blood on the cross. For more on the new COVENANT, see Jer. 31:31–34; Luke 22:20; 1 Cor. 11:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 29, 'Father’s kingdom. The LORD’S SUPPER not only celebrates the death on the cross, but also points forward to when we will all join with Christ and all the saved around the heavenly banquet table. We do injustice to the Communion service if we celebrate it only with somber and sad hearts and actions. Like the ancient Passover, it is a celebration of what God has done in delivering His people from the state of sin and what He will do in delivering us from the world of sin. The Christian should, therefore, participate in the service with awe, reverence, and joy. See note on vv. 26–30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 39, 'cup. A symbol of bitter suffering which Jesus is now beginning to experience (see 20:22). Both Jesus’ humanity and His dependence on His Father are demonstrated in this passage. He suffered as any human in a sinful world, and wished that the agony would go away. But His trust in God’s plan was absolute (see 26:42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 54, 'Scriptures be fulfilled. Probably a reference to Zech. 13:7, especially in light of the disciples’ desertion in Matt. 26:56.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 56, 'Scriptures of the prophets. Likely passages would be Ps. 22; Is. 53; and Zech. 12–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 65, 'tore his clothes. The high priest was forbidden to tear his garments (Lev. 10:6; 21:10). But here the high priest used this extreme gesture of dismay and distress (see 2 Kin. 18:37–19:3; Acts. 14:14) to show his horror at Jesus’ messianic claim (see Matt. 26:64).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 26, 74, 'swear. This is not foul language. It is swearing in the sense of taking an oath in a court of law setting. In addition, Peter seemed to be “cursing” in the sense of invoking a curse upon himself if his words were not true (see 5:34–37).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 27, 3, 'thirty pieces of silver. About 120 days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 27, 35, 'crucified. Crucifixion was the worst punishment of that time, and it was reserved for slaves and foreigners. It was both physically painful and emotionally shameful. The nails normally went through the wrists and ankles, and the victims hung on the cross immobile, breathing with difficulty. They hung in shame because they were usually stripped of their clothes. Death came slowly, often after many days, because none of their vital organs were affected. Jesus’ death was unusual, in that it took only a matter of hours for Him to expire. The most reasonable explanation for this is that He died from a broken heart.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 27, 51, 'veil of the temple. This most likely was the inner veil that separated the Holy Place from the Most Holy Place. Only the high priest could enter into the Most Holy Place to speak to God on behalf of the people. The rending of this curtain signified that the old order of ceremonies and sacrifices in an earthly sanctuary were over. Jesus’ sacrifice on the cross replaced those activities. It also began a new era when people could approach God directly through Christ in the heavenly sanctuary (Heb. 10:19–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 27, 54, '“Truly this was the Son of God!” See note on Mark 15:39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 28, 1, 'the first day of the week. Matthew’s account makes it clear that the first day of the week followed the Sabbath, which followed the Day of Preparation (27:62) which was Friday. This makes it clear that decades after the resurrection, when Matthew wrote this Gospel, Sunday was still the first day of the week (and had not been changed to the “Sabbath”), and that the seventh day was still being observed as the Sabbath according to the commandments and ancient biblical practice since Creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 28, 7, 'go … tell His disciples. Women were the first ones to tell the good news (the gospel) of the resurrection. Matthew wishes his Jewish readers to be aware that Jesus’ disciples encompass persons beyond the traditional male Jewry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Mateus', 28, 19, 'make disciples of all the nations. Although Matthew’s specific audience was Jewish, he intends to show that the good news of Jesus Christ is universal, international, and inclusive. He begins his Gospel by including women, both non-Jewish and ones with stains on their character, in his genealogy (see note on 1:3), and he ends by not only highlighting the sending of women with the good news (28:5–8), but by commissioning the disciples to take this good news to all the nations and all peoples and make them disciples by means of baptism in the singular name of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. When this work is done He will return the second time (24:14). baptizing. See notes on 3:11, 16–17. Compare BAPTISM as the command of Christ with Acts 10:47–48. On the relationship of BAPTISM with belief, see Acts 2:38; 16:30–33. Father … Son … Holy Spirit. The three persons who make up the Divine Trinity or Godhead. There is one God: Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, a unity of three Persons who have existed together through all time. Just as all three were present at Jesus’ own baptism (see 3:16–17), so believers are to be baptized in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. For the plurality of the Godhead indicated from the first chapter of the Bible, see Gen. 1:26. For statements about the oneness or unity of the TRINITY, see Deut. 6:4; Eph. 4:4–6. For more on the TRINITY working together in unity of purpose, see 2 Cor. 13:14; 1 Pet. 1:2.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 0, NULL, 'The Gospel of Mark is the second of the three Synoptic Gospels (Matthew, Mark, Luke; the term “synoptic” comes from the Greek word meaning “see together”) at the beginning of the New Testament, though it may have been the first to be written. While it is also the shortest, it is more action-packed than any of the other Gospels. Mark is brief on teaching and full of the miraculous activities of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 0, NULL, 'The authors of the four Gospels do not identify themselves by name (see Introduction to Matthew, “Author”). The earliest tradition that identifies an author for this Gospel is that of the church leader Papias, who lived during the first part of the second century. He was quoted much later by the historian Eusebius as saying that the Gospel was written by Mark, a follower of Peter. Later church leaders like Augustine were not aware of this tradition and suggested other authors.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 0, NULL, 'The text of Mark’s Gospel provides no clear evidence as to when it was written. Many students of the Gospels believe that it was written before Matthew and Luke (see Introduction to Matthew, “Author”) and therefore place it before the destruction of Jerusalem in A.D. 70. Some suggest a date as early as the 50s. If it was written for Roman Christians during the persecution by Nero, a date in the 60s is most probable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 0, NULL, 'Much internal evidence supports the suggestion that the Gospel was written for a non-Jewish audience. Mark tends to explain Jewish customs (7:24; 15:42) and is careful when utilizing Aramaic (the language Jews of Palestine used during the first century A.D.) words and phrases to translate them to Greek (3:17; 5:41; 7:11, 34; 15:22).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 0, NULL, 'The Gospel of Mark has been called a Passion story (Jesus’ suffering and death) with an extended introduction. Almost half of Mark is devoted to Jesus’ teachings on the suffering Messiah (and the suffering that the disciples must undergo); and the final third is devoted to the last week of Jesus’ life on earth. The first half (which comes to a climax with Peter’s acknowledgement that Jesus was the Christ in 8:29) rushes along at a rapid pace (notice the frequent use of the adverb “immediately”), with very little teaching and many miracles. Jesus is presented as a “man of action” whose greatest action is His death on the cross.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 0, NULL, 'I.      Prologue: preparation for mission (1:1–13)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 1, 'gospel. Literally, “good news,” normally in reference to the oral report of positive news. Centuries later it became a designation for the type of writings that focused on the story of Jesus’ life and ministry. Mark uses the term as a statement that what follows in his book is good news. Christ. From the Hebrew word translated “Messiah” and the Greek word Christos, both meaning “Anointed One.” Son of God. Mark goes against Jewish expectation that the Messiah would be a human savior. It is clear at the outset that Jesus is divine.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 2, 'The quotation is an apparent combination of Ex. 23:20; Is. 40:3; and Mal. 3:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 4, 'baptism of repentance. Baptism of Gentile converts was widely practiced among Jews. But only among the Essenes (a small community of Jews living near the Dead Sea) do we find baptism connected with repentance and confession of sins. It is significant that so many Jews from Judea and Jerusalem went to be baptized by John, repenting and confessing their sins.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 5, 'in the Jordan River. Baptism was by immersion. See note on Matt. 3:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 6, 'camel’s hair. John’s dress resembled that of Elijah (2 Kin. 1:8) and other ancient prophets who wore garments of coarse hair (Zech. 13:4). locusts and wild honey. See note on Matt. 3:4');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 10, 'Spirit descending. Jesus’ baptism signals a new dimension for the baptism event—baptism of the Spirit. The descent of the Spirit is a symbolic act of ordination to ministry. Christian baptism involves water and the Spirit—water symbolizing the cleansing of sins, and reception of the Spirit symbolizing the separation and empowerment for ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 12, 'Immediately. Distinctive of Mark’s style (used almost 50 times). It is key to his fast-paced narrative. The first part of his Gospel is moving rapidly to the climactic suffering of Jesus in the second part.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 13, 'tempted. The Greek word can be translated “test” or “tempt.” Here Mark seems to want to emphasize the aspect of “putting to the test” rather than “enticement to sin.” All other uses of the word in the Gospel seem to have the former meaning (8:11; 10:2; 12:15). wild beasts. Mark’s mention of beasts and Satan in the wilderness, as well as the ministry of angels, is to highlight the cosmic struggle that Jesus would face throughout His ministry by heavenly, demonic, and earthly powers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 15, 'kingdom … is at hand. The Greek perfect tense used in this verse is meant to express the fact that the kingdom, meaning God’s kingly presence, had arrived in Jesus’ ministry and the effects of its arrival would continue throughout Jesus’ time of ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 17, 'Follow Me. A major focus of Mark is discipleship. Jesus’ first act of ministry is to call ordinary persons to leave the normal routines of life and dedicate themselves actively to His service.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 21, 'synagogue. During the Babylonian captivity the Jews instituted the synagogue as a center for Judaism in the local community. Wherever there were 10 men, a synagogue could be established. Besides being a community center (see note on James 2:2), it was where worship took place on the seventh-day Sabbath. Jesus worshiped regularly at the synagogue on the Sabbath.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 22, 'scribes. The authorized interpreters of the oral traditions and the biblical laws. They were more like lawyers than secretaries. See also 8:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 23, 'unclean spirit. In these initial miracles Mark emphasizes Jesus’ authority over demonic powers. See note on v. 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 25, 'Jesus’ method of casting out demons differed from the popular practice that involved a complex invocation of many names of God and prophets and a variety of prescribed formulae. Jesus’ exorcism was simple, without ritual, but with absolute authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 31, 'she served them. The first miracle of healing involves a woman who responded to Jesus’ healing touch by “serving” (the same Greek word used for the angels “ministering” to Jesus in v. 13). In Mark the model of a true disciple is lowly service, as is demonstrated in Jesus’ life and ministry and in that of a number of women (12:41–44; 14:3–9; 15:40–41; 16:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 32, 'when the sun had set. People wait until the Sabbath ends at sunset to bring the sick. For more on the time to begin and end the SABBATH, see Lev. 23:32. For more on Christ and the SABBATH, see Mark 2:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 40, 'leper. Not necessarily the modern Hansen’s disease; the word applies to a variety of skin disorders (see note on Matt. 8:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 41, 'touched. The ancient law prohibited touching or having any contact with a leper (Lev. 13). Jesus’ compassion for the suffering person superseded the ceremonial requirements of the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 1, 44, 'say nothing. See Introduction, “Message,” for comments on the “messianic secret.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 4, 'roof. Roofs on such first-century houses were typically made of thatch and clay mud, placed across wooden beams going from wall to wall. A stairway led to the roof, a place sometimes used for rest and relaxation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 5, 'sins are forgiven you. Jesus here correlates sin and sickness. In John 9:1–3 He denies that sin and sickness had any correlation in the case of the man who was blind from birth. These two passages suggest that some illnesses are due to personal sins, but not all are. Some are simply due to evil in the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 7, 'blasphemies. For Jews not even the coming Messiah could forgive sins, since they expected a human from the lineage of David. Anyone claiming that prerogative was blasphemously assuming divinity. Here Jesus authoritatively demonstrated His divinity by words and actions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 10, 'power … to forgive sins. While any believer may announce God’s forgiveness of sin, only God can forgive sin. Jesus’ claim to be able to forgive sins presented His argument for His divinity. See note on Matt. 1:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 14, '“Follow Me.” Jesus here expands the discipleship call to include even the outcast tax collector. First it was to the humble fishermen (1:16–18), and now He calls a notorious sinner in the eyes of the people. The call to discipleship crosses status, gender, and moral imperfection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 15, 'tax collectors and sinners. Jesus is portrayed as associating with the morally and ritually imperfect. Because of the tax collectors’ close interaction with the Roman government and their extortion of their own Jewish people, they were not only viewed as ritually unclean but placed in the same category as murderers and adulterers. The term “sinners” included more than the morally imperfect. Anyone who did not adhere to the rigid standard of ritual purity was a sinner. Thus all the poor and common people were classified as “sinners.” These Jesus associated with; these He called to be His disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 16, 'Pharisees. A party within Judaism that accepted not only the books of the OT but followed the oral traditions that were developed after the Babylonian captivity. They strove to keep perfectly all 613 laws as found in the books of Moses. Ritual perfection was their highest goal. Not all Pharisees were hypocrites. But those portrayed in the Gospels were the hypocritical segments who were opponents of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 18, 'fasting. The Mosaic Law required fasting only on the Day of Atonement (Lev. 16:29, 31; 23:27–32; Num. 29:7), and then after the Babylonian captivity there were four annual fast days (Zech. 8:19). By the time of Jesus, tradition had extended to every Monday and Thursday for those who wished to keep the law perfectly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 22, 'wineskins. See note on Matt. 9:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 24, 'not lawful on the Sabbath. See note on Matt.12:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 2, 27, 'The Sabbath was made for man. The traditions and legal requirements regarding Sabbath keeping had expanded by the first century to emphasize the mechanics of observing the day. In the process, the purpose of the Sabbath had been lost—the spiritual and physical restoration of people. Instead, the Sabbath had become a burden. Jesus wishes to recapture the joyful dimension of the Sabbath as God’s gift to His children. The passage also implicitly teaches that the seventh-day Sabbath is not only a Jewish institution, but one for all humanity, given before there was a specific Jewish nation. For more on the purpose of the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11; 31:12–17. Lord of the Sabbath. Another claim to divinity (see Mark 2:7), and an implicit claim that He was the one who instituted the SABBATH at creation (Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11). By claiming to be Lord of the Sabbath, Jesus is also asserting that it is His day. Therefore, the seventh-day SABBATH is the only “Lord’s Day.” See also Matt. 12:8; Rev. 1:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 4, 'lawful on the Sabbath to do good. The Jewish oral traditions, as later recorded in the Mishnah, noted numerous medical conditions that could not be alleviated on the Sabbath. (Thus the crowds waited until after the Sabbath to come for healing in 1:32–34.) Jesus cleared away these human commands. Because it was “made for man” (2:27), doing good by meeting pressing human needs was entirely appropriate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 6, 'Herodians. See Matt. 22:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 12, 'they should not make Him known. See Introduction, “Message,” for discussion of the “messianic secret.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 14, 'appointed. Literally, “made.” Some translations interpret this as “ordained.” twelve. Some Greek manuscripts add “apostles.” Jesus begins the new era with 12 apostles just as the old era began with the 12 tribes of Israel. Twelve is symbolic of the completeness of God’s church and His people. send them out. The possible reason why “apostles” was added in some manuscripts is because the definition of an apostle is “one who is sent.” The mission of the twelve included preaching, but also healing and freeing people oppressed by demons. Evangelism involves the restoration of the whole person.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 15, 'Jesus calls a diverse group of people to follow Him, a variety mirrored in the church throughout the ages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 21, '“He is out of His mind.” Even Jesus’ family thinks that He has gone mad. Mark highlights this by beginning this passage (vv. 20–35) with this charge of insanity; ending it with a statement of who really is Jesus’ family; inserting in the middle a discussion in which the Jews charge Him with being possessed by Beelzebub, the prince of demons; and making a statement about the unpardonable sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 22, 'Beelzebub. The original Greek reads “Beelzebul.” The variant name Beelzebub (“Lord of the flies”) was a Canaanite divinity (see 2 Kin. 1:2). In Jesus’ time it was applied to the ruler of demons, Satan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 3, 28, 'never has forgiveness. Note that all sins and blasphemies are forgivable. The only sin that is unpardonable is blasphemy against the Holy Spirit. In the context of this passage, this is done by attributing to Satan the saving work of the Holy Spirit. Such a sin would naturally be committed by one whose heart is hardened and cannot respond any longer to the pleading of the Spirit. (See also note on Matt. 12:31.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 4, 2, 'parables. Parables in Bible times included more than “heavenly stories with an earthly meaning.” Jesus often told such stories. In many instances, though, parables were simple statements of comparison introduced with the phrase “it is like,” or the word “as.” In other instances, a parable is a statement of fact that draws a comparison or teaches a lesson (as in the case of Jesus’ response to the Beelzebub charge in 3:23). See note on Matt. 13:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 4, 3, 'sower went out to sow. In ancient Palestine famers scattered the seed by hand, many times scattering it before plowing the ground. Only the seeds that fell on the productive ground that was plowed and tended would produce a harvest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 4, 8, 'seed fell on good ground … increased and produced. When interpreting narrative parables, what is said last is usually the key to understanding the parable. It is called the “end stress.” The point of the parable is that the kingdom of God will have an extraordinary triumph, despite attempts to hinder its success. This is clear in light of the context of the previous chapter: first Jesus’ opponents were watching to see if He would heal on the Sabbath so they could accuse Him; second, they think He has not only gone insane, but is an operative of Satan; third, even His mother and brother had doubts. But the parable teaches that the kingdom of Jesus will have extraordinary success. A good harvest in Palestine would be tenfold. Thirty, sixty, and a hundredfold would be extraordinary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 4, 9, 'He who has ears to hear. These words at the end of the parable, and the subsequent interpretation in vv. 13–20, suggest that the parable has a double message. Not only is it a response to the interchanges of chap. 3 (see note on 4:8), but it is an exhortation to listen, hear, and correctly respond to the teachings of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 4, 31, 'mustard seed. As with the parable of the sower, this parable emphasizes the comparison between the small beginning of the kingdom of Jesus and its extraordinary ending. What makes it extraordinary is not only that it grows disproportionately large compared to its beginnings, but that it is expansive enough to bring in non-Jews to be part of the movement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 4, 37, 'a great windstorm. The Sea of Galilee is a lake over 600 feet (180 m) below sea level, situated in a basin surrounded by hills. The cold air from the mountains in the north combining with the warm and humid air over the lake and from the lowlands would occasionally produce violent and unexpected storms.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 4, 39, 'rebuked the wind, and said to the sea, “Peace, be still!” This miracle demonstrated that Jesus has authority over nature. He rebuked the storm in the same manner He rebuked the unclean spirit in 1:25. The words of command were meant to “muzzle” the storm. It was a command for the storm to be silent. A more forceful translation would be, “Shut up! Be calm!”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 2, 'tombs. Most burial places were in caves. At times the poor and homeless as well as the deranged used these burial places as a temporary home and shelter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 9, 'My name is Legion. A legion was the largest unit of the Roman army, consisting of up to 6,000 men. This was possibly the greatest demonic force that Jesus encountered and over which He demonstrated His authority. This seems to be Mark’s point, because he gives so much space to the story (even though the first half of this Gospel is noted for its brevity). We see that Jesus encountered a legion of Satan’s army, and not only had authority over them, but put them to rout. Even more, they admitted Jesus’ divinity when they acknowledged that He was the “Son of the Most High God” (v. 7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 11, 'swine. Lev. 11:7 lists swine among the unclean animals that should not be eaten or even touched. The Gadarenes region where this incident took place was a Gentile area at the southeast corner of the Sea of Galilee.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 19, 'Go … tell. See Introduction, “Message,” on the “messianic secret.” Here, by contrast, Jesus commands this new disciple to not keep what Jesus had done a secret. The earlier commands not to tell who Jesus really was and what He did (e.g., 1:44; 3:12; 5:43) were for Jews and Jewish regions, where there was a misunderstanding of who the Messiah was and of His mission. This healed demoniac was sent to 10 Gentile towns (Decapolis) where such misconceptions did not exist.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 22, 'one of the rulers of the synagogue. A layman (not necessarily a Levite) who was in charge of the administration of the synagogue and supervised the worship services and other activities held there.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 25, 'flow of blood. Women with this condition were considered unclean and were prohibited from entering the temple, and coming in contact with people or things. Any thing or person that she touched became unclean (Lev. 15:19–30; see also Lev. 12). She was in the same category as a leper. That this woman would press through the crowd, thus defiling many, and intentionally touch Jesus, shows her desperation, faith, and hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 26, 'physicians. The Jewish Talmud records treatments and medicines that were prescribed for such conditions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 28, 'be made well … healed. The same Greek word for “well” and “healed” can also be translated “saved.” Jesus’ healing miracles were both physically and spiritually therapeutic.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 38, 'those who wept and wailed loudly. Not members of the family or friends, but most likely professional mourners as was customary in the East.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 39, 'not dead, but sleeping. Scripture teaches that death is a “sleep” (John 11:11–14; 1 Cor. 15:6; 1 Thess. 4:13–18). The Greek philosophy that when one dies the soul goes to heaven or hell and the body remains in the grave is not supported in Scripture. Death is but a sleep until the life giver comes to resurrect the whole person, as Jesus raised Jairus’s daughter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 41, '“Talitha, cumi.” Mark includes the original Aramaic words. This was the common language spoken in Palestine during the time of Jesus, and one that Jesus used. The fact that Mark gave the translation indicates that his readers were not from Palestine.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 5, 43, 'no one should know it. See Introduction, “Message,” for more on the “messianic secret.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 3, 'carpenter. Only here in Scripture is Jesus identified as a carpenter. The Greek word can apply not only to a carpenter in its modern understanding, but to masons, smiths, and builders in general. Because much construction was done with stone in Palestine, it is possible that Jesus was more than a wood-working carpenter. brother of … His sisters. Over the centuries it has been debated whether these siblings of Jesus were Joseph’s before his marriage to Mary, or Jesus’ younger brothers and sisters by both his parents. Scripture does not give us an answer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 7, 'two by two. They are sent out in pairs to provide mutual support in their missionary endeavor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 8, 'take nothing. They should be totally dependent on God for their provisions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 11, 'shake off the dust. A symbolic act of censure to one who showed a lack of hospitality—an act of shaming in Eastern societies. It was also an act that pious Jews did when leaving a Gentile town to demonstrate their separation from that ritually unclean place. Here Jesus does not discriminate between Gentile and Jew. If the disciples are not welcomed or their words rejected, they should simply give the sign of censure and move on. A principle here for today is that we are not responsible for those who reject Jesus or His message. We are responsible to invite, proclaim, and demonstrate the love and healing/saving power of Jesus. We are not responsible for the results.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 13, 'anointed with oil. In Eastern societies, olive oil was widely used for medicinal purposes. But there was also a spiritual dimension to healing. They did not make the distinctions that we make in the West between the physical and the spiritual. The oil of anointing worked both physically and spiritually (see James 5:14). healed. The Greek word sozo means both “heal” and “save.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 14, 'The various opinions about who Jesus was, as listed here, will come to a climax when His identity is affirmed by Peter in 8:27–30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 14, 'Herod. See note on Matt. 14:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 30, 'apostles. From the Greek word, meaning “one who is sent,” this is possibly the only time Mark uses the term (some Greek manuscripts have it in 3:14). After Jesus’ resurrection the disciples are normally known as apostles. But Mark’s focus is on discipleship, and he only uses the word “apostle” in the context of the disciples being sent on a missionary journey.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 37, '“You give them something to eat.” Jesus demonstrated that evangelism should not be only proclamation but should involve social action. One should not only minister to the “soul” but also to the “body” (James 2:14–17). The Bible teaches that they are inseparable. two hundred denarii. One denarius was equal to one day’s wages for the ordinary working man, so this was the equivalent of about 200 days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 38, '“Five, and two fish.” Barley loaves and fish were the staple of the poor. The loaves were small and flat and the fish were small sardine-size. It would normally take a lot of these to fill one person.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 40, 'ranks, in hundreds and in fifties. Similar to the ordering of the Israelites in the wilderness (Ex. 18:21). The word translated “ranks” has the sense of a “garden plot.” Mark is being picturesque: the crowd is set up in groups like orderly garden plots on the green grass (see Mark 6:39). Besides demonstrating that Jesus is a master of order, the reader should sense that the author wishes to highlight the idea of the wilderness being transformed into a scenic garden where the shepherd feeds his flock.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 48, 'fourth watch. See note on Matt. 14:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 6, 50, 'It is I. Literally, “I am.” This is the name of God revealed to Moses at the burning bush (Ex. 3:14). The English transliteration of the Hebrew verb approximates “Yahweh” (Jehovah). When Jesus says, “It is I,” or “I am” (John 8:24, 28, 58; 13:19; 18:4–6), He is identifying Himself as the Yahweh of the OT. This explains why the Jews attempted to stone Him when He said, “Before Abraham was, I AM” (John 8:58).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 1, 'Pharisees. See 2:16; 12:13. scribes. See 8:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 2, 'unwashed hands. The traditions regarding hand washing were not for hygienic purposes, but had to do with ritual purity. There was no biblical injunction regarding the ritual washing of hands before eating. The washing of hands, eating utensils, and foods were all purity behaviors developed by pious Jews as a fence around the law. Such practices were intended to keep them from inadvertently breaking any of the prescribed 613 Mosaic laws.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 4, 'marketplace. In the public square Jews would come in contact with ritually impure Gentiles (and their goods), who did not follow the ceremonial laws or the traditions that developed around them. Such contact required that they symbolically purify themselves by washing their hands (similar to the requirement in Lev. 15:11 that demanded washing after any bodily discharge) and cleansing the goods. washing of cups, pitchers, copper vessels, and couches. Mark gives a detailed explanation of these rituals because he apparently was writing to a non-Jewish audience who were not acquainted with the practices.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 8, 'commandment of God. Jesus makes it clear that He is upholding the laws of God while rejecting the additions imposed by scribes and Pharisees. These oral additions (known as the halachah, and “tradition of the elders” in the Gospels) were written down 200 years after Christ in a collection of writings called the Mishnah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 11, 'Corban. Jesus illustrates how the religious leaders in actuality placed the laws of the Ten Commandments below the requirements of their traditions. He used the fifth commandment (Ex. 20:12) to illustrate His point: A son could by an oath dedicate an offering to God by giving it to the temple or continuing to use the dedicated funds for himself. This is Corban. These oaths could be made even to deny one’s parents their rightful support. By doing that, the breaking of the commandment to honor one’s father and mother was overlooked. The tradition of Corban could supersede it. This oath was also taken to avoid obeying the commandment to take care of the poor or those falling on hard times (Lev. 25:35–43).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 15, 'In these verses, Jesus is not declaring that a person can eat anything. Some foods and liquids are life-destroying, and some are plainly unhealthy. See Lev. 11:2–23 for lists of animals, birds, fish, and insects that were not only deemed ritually unclean but physiologically unhealthy. What Jesus is attacking is the belief that if pious Jews simply observed the ritual purity laws, they would thereby be morally clean. On the contrary, no food in and of itself can defile a person’s character. What ethically defiles are the evil thoughts (which are realized in outward actions), which originate inside of a person. See note on Matt. 15:11. For the issue of clean and unclean in the NT, see Acts 10:9–16; 15:19–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 26, 'The woman was a Greek, a Syro-Phoenician. In the thinking of first-century Palestine this woman had a number of inabilities. She was a Gentile, a woman, and perhaps a single parent. But none of those factors kept her from approaching Jesus for help, or from engaging in a challenging conversation with Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 27, 'little dogs. See note on Matt. 15:26–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 34, 'Ephphatha. For Mark’s use of Aramaic words in his Gospel see note on 5:41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 7, 36, 'tell no one. See Introduction, “Message,” for more on the “messianic secret.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 11, 'testing Him. See note on 1:13. As Jesus moves toward the cross, His path is met with tests by Satan, the Pharisees, and the Herodians—by human and superhuman powers (10:2; 12:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 15, 'leaven. Yeast. With the exception of Matt. 13:33 (see note) where Jesus uses leaven positively for the growth of His kingdom, throughout Scripture leaven is used as a symbol of sin, corruption, and uncleanness (see Ex. 12:15–20; 23:18; 34:25; Lev. 2:11; 6:17; Matt. 16:5–12; 1 Cor. 5:6–8; Gal. 5:9). In this context, the leaven of the Pharisees and Herod Antipas refers to their evil attempts to test Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 23, 'spit on his eyes. Jesus first uses a common home remedy used in the ancient world, and it gives partial healing. The act of Jesus then putting His hands on the man’s eyes, without reference to the spit, was the second stage of the healing/saving. This story of two-stage healing is unique to Mark. This miracle is an example of the combination of the medical and spiritual used together to accomplish healing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 26, 'nor tell anyone. See Introduction, “Message,” for discussion on the “messianic secret.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 27, 'Caesarea Philippi. A predominantly Gentile city northeast of Galilee. This episode marks the turning point of Jesus’ ministry in Mark, and the transition between the first and second parts of the Gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 28, 'John the Baptist … Elijah. See 6:14–15, where Herod and others had similar questions about who Jesus was.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 29, 'Christ. From the Hebrew word for Messiah, the Greek Christos means “Anointed One.” In Mark’s Gospel Jesus seldom used the title in reference to Himself. This is possibly because the Jews at that time expected a Messiah who was coming as a political and national leader to deliver the nation from Roman oppression. Jesus did not wish to perpetuate that interpretation of the Messiah for His mission.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 31, 'Son of Man. Jesus uses this designation in reference to Himself more than any other title. must suffer. The Greek verb dei indicates necessity. The plan of salvation made it imperative that He suffer. It was not optional. It was central in His coming as Messiah. rejected by the elders and chief priests and scribes. These are members of the Sanhedrin, the highest legal, religious, and political body of Judaism. The elders were the lay members; the chief priests were at that time Caiaphas (the high priest), Annas (the former high priest), and other members of the high priest’s family (all Sadducees); and the scribes were the legal interpreters of the Torah (the laws found in the five books of Moses) and the oral traditions that were developed during the period between the OT and the NT. The scribes were like religious lawyers and many opposed Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 32, 'He spoke this word openly. This passage begins the second half of the Gospel of Mark. Jesus now begins to reveal His messiahship. No longer does He command anyone not to say who He was. There is no messianic secret from here on. Peter … began to rebuke Him. Even though Peter was the one to acknowledge that Jesus was the Messiah in v. 29, he still held the popular expectation of the Messiah: one who would come as a David-like king and bring political redemption to the Jewish people (see note on v. 29). Jesus rejected this traditional understanding. In Peter’s view, such inconceivable teaching by Jesus demanded censure and rebuke.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 33, 'Get behind Me, Satan! It is not completely clear whether Jesus was metaphorically identifying Peter with Satan, or was literally rebuking Satan himself (as He did in the temptation in the wilderness—Matt. 4:10). Jesus’ rebuke may be bringing together all the forces (human and nonhuman) that were united to tempt and test Him and to dissuade Him from His mission (see notes on Mark 1:13; 8:11). In one stroke He rebukes them all by rebuking Satan who is the mastermind behind it all.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 34, 'deny himself. This is not a call to be an ascetic—to deny oneself of all material things and live a solitary life. Neither is it a call to self-hatred. It is a call to cease to make one’s self the center and object of one’s thoughts and actions. take up his cross. Death on a cross was the most shameful method of execution in the ancient Greco-Roman world. Adding to the shame was the practice of demanding that the prisoner carry his own cross to the place of execution. This act signified the prisoner’s total submission to the Roman power. follow Me. The call to discipleship demands cross-carrying submission.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 8, 36, 'soul. A metaphor for eternal life (see note on Matt. 10:28). Jesus challenges the disciple who intends to follow Him to not focus on the present life and the suffering that comes with it. Loss of this life is nothing compared to the loss of eternal life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 1, 'some standing here. See note on Matt. 16:28 for a number of interpretations of this prophecy. It seems most likely that the reference is to the following text that describes the transfiguration, when three of the disciples witnessed Jesus transfigured before them. The glorious transformation of Jesus into a divine manifestation was a powerful demonstration of the kingdom of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 2, 'transfigured. A transformation of Jesus into a glorious, divine, other-worldly figure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 3, 'exceedingly white. White clothing denoted heavenly status (16:5) or purity and worthiness (Rev. 3:4, 18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 4, 'Elijah … Moses. See note on Matt. 17:1–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 5, 'three tabernacles. In Greek, “three tents.” Peter may have been thinking of the Feast of Tabernacles when the Jews lived in booths annually for a week (Lev. 23:39–43). But he might have been reflecting on Ex. 29:42, which speaks of God meeting and communicating with His people from the tabernacle (see also Ex. 40:34–35). “Could it be happening again?” he might have thought.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 9, 'tell no one. This “messianic secret” differs from the ones in the first half of the Gospel (see Introduction, “Message”). This has a time limit: after the resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 10, 'what the rising from the dead meant. Jewish theology did not envision a suffering Messiah, much less one who would die. There was no place for a resurrected Son of Man.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 11, 'Elijah must come first. After seeing Elijah on the mountain, Peter, James, and John were a bit confused. According to the teachings of the rabbis, the prophet Malachi (Mal. 4:5–6) predicted that Elijah was to come first, before the Messiah. Jesus, however, seems to teach that John the Baptist was the fulfillment of that prophecy (see Mark 1:6; 6:14–29). And furthermore, Jesus would be rejected and suffer in the same manner as John (the promised Elijah) suffered and was treated with contempt.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 17, 'mute spirit. The symptoms as indicated by the recounting of the same incident in Matthew (Matt. 17:14–18) may indicate a severe case of epilepsy. However, in ancient times the illness often was viewed not only as the result of a physiological defect, but also as a result of spiritual and demonic possession. Life was viewed more holistically: the separation between the spiritual and physical was not viewed in the same way that Western philosophy divides and dichotomizes them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 28, '“Why could we not cast it out?” The disciples had cast out spirits on their missionary journey (6:13). Why not now? In the next verse Jesus makes it clear that not all miracles are equal. Some need an extra unction of the Spirit that is obtained by prayer and fasting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 36, 'took a little child. Children (like women and the poor) were held in low esteem in first-century Jewish society. In such shame and honor societies, to associate with such lowly persons would bring no honor to a person. But Jesus identifies with the lowly and marginal, and thereby shows that seeking high rank and status while demeaning others has no place in His kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 39, 'Do not forbid him. Jesus’ vision of discipleship was broader and more inclusive than the seemingly narrow sectarian view of His disciples. Because the other exorcist was not part of Jesus’ inner circle of disciples does not mean that he is not a supporter and disciple. Discipleship includes all who faithfully profess Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 42, 'millstone. A tool used to grind grain (see note on Matt. 18:6). Jesus is not teaching violence against sinners. He is emphasizing the absolute importance of protecting the opportunity of the weak to experience the blessing of discipleship in the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 43, 'cut it off. As with the millstone, Jesus is not being literal here (see note on Matt. 18:8). hell. The Greek term refers to the garbage dump outside Jerusalem (the Valley of Hinnom), which was always burning, and thus became symbolic of the final fires of hell and eternal punishment. For more on HELL and hell fire, see text and notes on Is. 34:9–10; Matt. 3:12; 13:42; 25:41; and Jude 7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 9, 50, 'Salt. Used both for preserving foods and for flavoring. Without its saltiness, salt is useless. To have the “salt” or flavor of the gospel is a call to live in peace (and accepting of all disciples as illustrated in vv. 39–42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 2, 'Is it lawful. In Greek, “Is it proper?” or “Is it permissible?” divorce. Jews accepted divorce as lawful, based on Deut. 24:1–4. The debate within Judaism was the grounds on which divorce could take place. The rabbinic school of Hillel taught that a woman could be divorced for any cause; and the school of Shammai said it was only for adultery committed by the woman (see note on Matt. 5:31–32). testing Him. See notes on Mark 1:13 and 8:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 5, 'hardness of your heart. Divorce was never God’s ideal. The Mosaic allowance (Deut. 24:1) was not a command to divorce. It was a concession God made to regulate the practice in the context of ritual defilement and property rights. Jesus states clearly here that the reason why God moved away from His original creation ideal was to accommodate human weakness due to the evil hardness of their hearts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 9, 'what God has joined together. Here (as well as in Luke 16:18) Jesus makes it plain that marriage is a lifelong contract that should not be broken by divorce. Mark does not include an exceptive clause such as is in Matt. 5:32 and 19:9 (for the case of adultery), and 1 Cor. 7:15 (where Paul allows such separation for the unbelieving spouse who wishes to divorce). Jesus strongly emphasized God’s ideal of the permanence of marriage. Thus, this can be understood as Jesus reacting against an approach to marriage and divorce as a mere business transaction where women were treated as property that could be acquired or discarded at will. Jesus taught an Edenic ideal, where it was God who did the joining (Gen. 2:24), and only He could separate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 11, 'Whoever divorces his wife … if a woman divorces her husband. Jewish custom dictated that only the husband could initiate a divorce, because divorce was part of the laws of property rights. Law courts and authorities were not involved. However, only here in Mark do we find a reference to a woman being able to initiate a divorce. This may be another indication that Mark’s audience is non-Jewish, and possibly very much Roman. In Roman law, the woman also had the prerogative to divorce her husband.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 14, 'Let the little children come to Me. This is not an authorization for infant baptism (see note on Matt. 19:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 16, 'He took them up in His arms. This expression can either mean that He picked them up or He placed His arms around them. We do not know the size of the children. The Greek word is too imprecise to give us a clue as to their ages. The point of the text is that even the little ones can be Jesus’ disciples. The Gospel of Mark emphasizes the fact that Jesus was constantly criticized for associating and spending too much time with the wrong kind of people—the marginal: the tax collectors, the sinners, and the children—instead of with the people of status and honor. In this episode, the disciples joined Jesus’ detractors and rebuked those who were bringing these little disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 17, 'one came running. Mark does not identify the person. Luke calls him a ruler (Luke 18:18), and Matthew identifies him as young (Matt. 19:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 20, 'all these things I have kept. A pious Jew faithfully observed all 613 laws as listed in the first five books of Scripture, the Pentateuch. The Apostle Paul, who was a Pharisee, said that he was blameless in the observation of these legal requirements, until he found Jesus; then they were but rubbish in comparison to the gift of righteousness that he received from Jesus (Phil. 3:4–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 25, 'camel to go through the eye of a needle. A proverb that expresses impossibility. See note on Matt. 19:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 37, 'Grant us that we may sit. Jesus had just now, for the third time (8:31; 9:31; 10:33–34) since Peter’s confession at Caesarea Philippi (8:29), told the disciples the true nature of His messiahship. It would not be, as generally expected, one of political and kingly power; but one of suffering and death. Yet James and John were requesting places of honor and high status in the kingdom—a request that renewed the dispute among the other disciples that occurred after the second prediction of His death (9:33–34; 10:41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 39, '“We are able.” Jesus’ question to James and John—if they were able to endure the suffering that He was going to go through—elicited a positive response from them. James was the first disciple to die as a martyr (Acts 12:2), and John was exiled on the island of Patmos (Rev. 1:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 45, 'to serve. James and John wanted great and prestigious positions in the reign of Jesus. But Jesus told them that at the heart of true greatness is service. ransom. The Greek word was used commonly for the payment for the release of a slave from servitude. Through the giving of Jesus’ life, we have eternal life. In bearing the punishment for sin, Christ pays the debt owed by the sinner. For more on SALVATION through Christ’s substitutionary atonement, see Rom. 3:21–26; 2 Cor. 5:21; Gal. 3:13; Eph. 1:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 10, 51, 'Rabboni. “Rabbi” can be translated “master,” and this would be “dear master.” It is the same term of endearment that Mary used when she addressed Jesus after His resurrection (John 20:16). Bartimaeus recognized Jesus as more than just a miracle worker. He desired a close relationship with Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 11, 2, 'colt. See note on Matt. 21:2–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 11, 9, 'Hosanna! See note on Matt. 21:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 11, 14, '“Let no one eat fruit from you ever again.” Jesus is not having a fit of anger over the lack of figs. Rather the episode is a symbolic show of displeasure over the temple and its leadership. It is an enacted parable of judgment upon the Jerusalem establishment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 11, 15, 'bought and sold. See note on Matt. 21:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 11, 24, 'whatever things you ask when you pray. This is not a blanket assurance that God will give us whatever we pray for if we have faith. Jesus prayed that He would not have to go through the suffering of the cross, but it was not God’s will to remove that pain (13:36). The context of Jesus’ statement about “whatever you ask” is the fig tree incident, and the opposition of the Jerusalem leaders (see 11:27–33). It may be that even though Jesus’ comments, properly understood, have meaning outside their immediate context, He may have primarily been assuring His disciples that even though they may have opposition, God is on their side and will give them success.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 1, 'parables. See note on Matt. 13:3. A man planted a vineyard. This parable uses the language of the song in Is. 5:1–2. There, as well as here, the vineyard clearly symbolized Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 8, 'killed him and cast him out. The parable describes the Jewish leaders’ rejection of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 13, 'the Pharisees and the Herodians. The Pharisees were religious nationalists who were opposed to the Roman domination of the Jewish people. The Herodians were probably a Jewish political group that approved of Herod the Great and his successors’ accommodation to Rome. Both the Pharisees and the Herodians were opposed to Jesus and saw Him as a threat—the former, because His messianic authority did not match their expectation; the latter could have been afraid that He could overtake the political establishment through a destabilization of the Roman rule. See note on Matt. 22:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 14, 'taxes. The “head,” poll, or census tax. It was a contentious issue at that time. The Herodians supported it; the Pharisees didn’t like it, but paid it; it was unpopular among the common people; the Zealots refused to pay it. The efforts of the Zealots ultimately led to the destruction of Jerusalem in A.D. 70. If Jesus said “yes” to the question, He would offend the crowds and would lose His popularity. If He said “no,” He could be accused of treason against the imperial power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 15, 'a denarius. A common silver coin of the Roman Empire equal to an ordinary laborer’s daily wage. On the coin was the image of the Roman emperor Tiberius.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 18, 'Sadducees. An aristocratic party in Judaism whose members were wealthy, always occupied the position of high priest, accepted only the five books of Moses (Genesis–Deuteronomy: the Pentateuch), and did not believe in the resurrection or in angels.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 19, 'brother should take his wife. See note on Matt. 22:24 for the practice of levirate marriage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 25, 'neither marry nor are given in marriage. See note on Luke 20:35 for the question of marriage and sexual relations after the resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 28, '“Which is the first commandment of all?” Jewish rabbis counted 613 commandments in the first five books of the OT—248 positive commandments and 365 prohibitions. To capture the essence of a rabbi’s thinking, an inquirer would pose a question such as the scribe asked Jesus. A Gentile challenged the famous rabbi Hillel (ca. 40 B.C.–A.D. 10) to teach him the whole law while standing on one foot. Hillel replied, “What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbor. That is the whole law; the rest is commentary.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 29, 'Hear, O Israel. The opening words of the Jewish confession of faith called the Shema (Deut. 6:4). The pious Jew repeated this every morning and evening, affirming his or her belief in only one God (monotheism). Jesus affirms this.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 30, 'with all your mind. Jesus expands the text from Deut. 6:5 to include the mind. He calls His disciples to a holistic religion: the heart and soul (emotional), the mind (intellectual), the strength (physical).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 31, 'love your neighbor as yourself. Jesus speaks of three objects of love: God, neighbor, and self. If one does not have a positive sense of self-worth, the love for the neighbor will be far from the ideal that God requires.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 35, 'Christ. See 8:29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 36, 'The LORD said to my Lord. This quotation from Ps. 110:1 is meant to make it clear that the Messiah was not merely a descendant of David, but He is his Lord. He is pre-David in origin, and divine. Yet, paradoxically, He is the human Son of David. This is Mark’s presentation of the theology of God becoming man that John has in a different form in the prologue of his Gospel (John 1:1–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 38, 'See Matt. 13 for a more extensive denunciation of the scribes and Pharisees. Jesus’ challenging and harsh words are reserved for those who pride themselves in piety and at the same time oppress the marginal, like the poor and defenseless widows.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 42, 'two mites. The mite was the smallest coin in circulation in Palestine at that time. Two of these made a quadrans, the smallest Roman copper coin—possibly close to a cent. Sixty-four quadrans (128 mites) equaled one denarius (a day’s wages for the typical day laborer).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 12, 44, 'all that she had. Jesus is contrasting this woman’s giving with both the rich who were putting in less, percentage-wise, than she was, and the scribes (whom He spoke of earlier in v. 40) who were oppressing her.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 1, 'This chapter in Mark, like its parallels (Matt. 24; Luke 21), has been a challenge for interpreters over the centuries. Is Jesus speaking about the fall of Jerusalem in A.D. 70, or the events preceding the Second Coming, or a combination of the two? Many students of the Gospels believe the passage combines the two events, constituting the events surrounding the fall of Jerusalem into a prefiguration of what will happen at the return of Christ. For more on Christ’s SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24–25; Luke 21:5–36; John 14:1–3; Acts 1:6–11; 1 Thess. 4:13–18; 2 Pet. 3:10–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 4, 'what will be the sign. See note on Matt. 24:3. Jesus makes it clear that turmoils like wars and disasters are not the “signs” or indicators as to when He will return. They do not tell when He is coming, but that He will be returning. The Christian should beware of anyone who proclaims that such events reveal when the end is about to occur. Jesus said that when these things occur “the end is not yet” (Mark 13:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 5, 'Take heed. Watch out! Be alert! Be on guard! A major focus of this chapter. There is the danger of deception and the danger of complacency.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 10, 'the gospel must be first preached to all the nations. Turmoil and disaster will occur until Jesus returns. But none of these are an indicator that He is just about to arrive. The only such indicator is that the gospel is preached to all the nations. But only God will know when this sign is fulfilled. Following Jesus means that we will preach the gospel even though we do not know when the task will be done.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 14, 'abomination of desolation. See note on Matt. 24:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 19, 'there will be tribulation. This and the immediately preceding verses seem to have reference to the fall of Jerusalem in A.D. 70. However, the persecution of God’s people continues in the tribulation of the 1,260-year period (Rev. 12:13–14) until the time of the Second Coming (Mark 13:24–27). Just before Christ’s arrival, evil will be at its worst, as in the days of Noah (Gen. 6:1–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 24, 'after that tribulation. See note on Matt. 24:29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 26, 'see the Son of Man coming. This is the sign of Jesus’ Return. Matt. 24:30 actually uses the phrase “the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 30, 'this generation will by no means pass away. See note on Matt. 24:34.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 32, 'of that day and hour no one knows … only the Father. Attempts to determine with precision the time of Christ’s return are futile because even the heavenly, sinless beings—and Jesus Himself as He speaks here to His followers—have no idea of the day or the hour.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 13, 37, 'Watch! Because we do not know when Jesus will return, we are to be faithful and constantly alert, ready to meet Him at any time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 1, 'Passover and the Feast of Unleavened Bread. These were celebrated as one. The lamb for the Passover was killed on the fourteenth of the month Nisan (March–April), and the meal, which included bread made without yeast, was eaten that evening, the fifteenth of Nisan. In Scripture, the new day begins at sundown. The Passover was the annual celebration of the children of Israel’s deliverance from slavery in Egypt. The night that they were delivered, the Lord “passed over” the houses of the Israelites who had blood on the doorposts, and so their firstborn sons were not slain (Ex. 12:1–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 2, 'uproar of the people. During the week-long celebration of the Feast, there would be at times several hundred thousand Jews (mostly males 12 years and older). The Jewish leaders would be taking a risk of inciting the visiting throngs if they tried to arrest Jesus during this period.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 3, 'sat. This refers to a manner of reclining, which was the customary posture for dining. alabaster flask. A long-necked jar used for special ointments. spikenard. A very expensive aromatic oil that is extracted from an herb that grows mostly in India. poured it on His head. When a guest arrived at a feast, it was the custom for the host to anoint the invitee’s head with perfume. Simon seems not to have done this for Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 5, 'three hundred denarii. Equivalent to one year’s wages for a typical day laborer. given to the poor. It was customary to give gifts to the poor on Passover eve.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 7, 'you have the poor with you always. This is not an injunction to neglect the poor. This citation is from Deut. 15:11. In that context God told the children of Israel that if they obeyed His commandments there would be no poor among them. But if there are poor, the people should stretch out their hands to help. Throughout the Gospels Jesus is on the side of the poor. But this time, in terms of priority, the woman did the right thing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 8, 'anoint My body for burial. Besides being used for anointing the head and hair, spikenard was used to anoint the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 13, 'a man … carrying a pitcher of water. Usually the task of a woman; thus this person would be easily identified. However, the Greek word used here could refer to either male or female.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 18, 'sat and ate. Originally the Passover meal was eaten while standing. But in the time of Jesus the customary posture of reclining was also used for this meal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 22, 'For more on the LORD’S SUPPER, see notes on Matt. 26:26–29; John 13:1–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 23, 'given thanks. The centuries-old name for this meal (Eucharist, “Thanksgiving”) comes from the root of the Greek word for thanksgiving. The meal is also known as “communion,” because Jesus communed with His disciples. Another title is “The Lord’s Supper,” because Jesus transformed the Passover supper into a new celebration of deliverance (see 1 Cor. 11:23–29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 26, 'hymn. Most likely from Ps. 115–118, which were traditionally sung at the Passover meal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 36, 'Abba. An Aramaic term of endearment used by a child for its father. cup. Jesus did not dread the physical suffering and death. It was the weight of the sins of the world that He had to carry that was the bitter cup. not what I will, but what You will. A complete surrender of the human will of Jesus to the divine will of His Father.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 38, 'spirit … is willing … flesh is weak. This explains why the disciples were so eager to follow Jesus, but so quick to flee when danger appeared.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 45, 'kissed. An Eastern greeting, similar to the Western handshake. But it was (esp. in the case of the particular Greek word used here) also a sign of mutual trust and affection. In this case, Judas used it as a sign of betrayal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 51, 'a linen cloth … around his naked body. Fine linen was worn by well-to-do persons. That this man lacked undergarments suggests that he had dressed hastily to follow Jesus. Tradition says that this young man may have been John Mark. This is the only place in the Gospels that we find this incident.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 55, 'council. The Sanhedrin. The political and religious ruling body in Judaism. It consisted of 71 members (see note on 8:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 63, 'tore his clothes. A sign of grief, anger, dismay, or shock. Here the high priest tore his garments as a judicial act in response to what he perceived as blasphemy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 14, 71, 'curse and swear. See note on Matt. 26:74.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 1, 'council. The Sanhedrin (see notes on 8:31 and 14:55).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 7, 'Barabbas. It is possible that he was a member of the violent Jewish party called the Zealots. The rebellion mentioned in Mark is unknown in other sources, but apparently it was well known in Palestine.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 21, 'Simon a Cyrenian. Probably an observant Jew from modern-day Libya in North Africa.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 22, 'Golgotha. Called Place of a Skull, possibly because of its shape, or because it was a place of execution.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 23, 'myrrh. A spice from the Arabian Desert that, when mixed with wine, was used to deaden pain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 24, 'crucified. The Romans’ extreme method of execution, reserved for the worst non-Roman criminals such as insurrectionists. The victim often was flogged with a whip made of leather strips, the ends of which had bones and lead embedded in them. He had to carry a beam of his own cross, weighing between 30 and 40 pounds (14–18 kg). The shame (which included hanging naked on the cross) was enormous; and the physical pain was excruciating. Heavy wrought-iron nails were driven through the wrist and heel or ankle bone. Breathing was difficult, the heart had difficulty pumping blood, and many times the victim developed a fever. Death came slowly, sometimes taking two to three days.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 25, 'third hour. 9:00 a.m.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 33, 'sixth hour … ninth hour. 12:00 noon and 3:00 p.m.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 34, 'Eloi … sabachthani. Aramaic. See note on 5:41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 37, 'breathed His last. The death of Jesus came faster than a typical crucifixion would cause.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 38, 'veil of the temple. See note on Matt. 27:51.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 39, '“Truly this Man was the Son of God!” This is a culminating point in Mark’s story. For the first time in the Gospel, someone other than Jesus Himself—and a Gentile at that—correctly identifies Jesus as the Son of God as announced to readers from the start (1:1). It is only in the light of the cross that the true identity of Jesus may be understood: He is the Messiah, the royal Son of God, the Savior of all.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 15, 42, 'Preparation Day. Friday was the day that God ordained for His people to get ready for the Sabbath day (see Ex. 16:22–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 16, 1, 'when the Sabbath was past. After sundown Saturday. anoint Him. Jews did not practice embalming. Anointing was like taking flowers to a grave in the modern Western world. It was a sign of love and respect.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 16, 2, 'first day of the week. Sunday. See note on Matt. 28:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 16, 9, 'There is some considerable scholarly debate over whether these verses were original with Mark’s Gospel. Credible evidence can be lined up on either side of the question. Thus, we should be cautious about building any specific teaching only on this passage. We may, however, use this valuable summary insofar as it is confirmed by other passages of Scripture.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Marcos', 16, 18, 'drink anything deadly. There is no account of anyone drinking deadly poison in the NT.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 0, NULL, 'Although the');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 0, NULL, 'Like the other Gospels, the name of the author is not mentioned within the Gospel of Luke. It is clear, however, that the same author also wrote Acts, for they are alike in their style, themes, and introductions (Luke 1:1–4; Acts 1:1–2). In Acts a clue is given to authorship, for the author often uses the pronoun “we” in telling the story of Paul’s travels, indicating that he himself was a traveling companion of Paul (see Introduction to Acts, “Author”). Elsewhere in the New Testament, a doctor named Luke is identified as accompanying Paul on such occasions (Col. 4:10–11, 14; 2 Tim. 4:11; Philem. 24).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 0, NULL, 'The Gospel of Luke is dedicated to “most excellent Theophilus,” a man who had already been taught the Christian faith (1:3–4). Theophilus, meaning literally “lover of God,” was a common Greek and Jewish name of Luke’s time. Other than in the similar dedication at the beginning of Acts (1:1), Theophilus is not mentioned again in the New Testament. The honorary words “most excellent” were generally used to address high-ranking officials and aristocrats, although they could also be used of other individuals in order to show particular respect. Possibly Theophilus was a patron who used his wealth and influence to assist Luke in writing, copying, and passing on the Gospel to others with whom Luke also wanted to share the good news about Jesus.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 0, NULL, 'It is possible that Luke wrote from Caesarea or from Rome during Paul’s imprisonments, which would place the writing around A.D. 60, though he may have written later from any number of places.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 0, NULL, 'The purpose of the Gospel of Luke is directly stated in the opening verses. Luke writes, he tells Theophilus, “that you may know the certainty” of the things you were taught (1:4). From the beginning the story is crafted to demonstrate that Jesus is indeed God’s own Son and promised Messiah, sent to save the world (2:11; “Christ” in Greek is the same as the Hebrew word for “Messiah”). In fact, Luke insists that what happened to Jesus, from the humble circumstances of His birth to the events of His crucifixion, was not a terrible accident but the fulfillment of God’s promises to His people in the Old Testament (1:30–35, 68–70; 4:17–21; 24:25–27). Through these events, God’s forgiveness and salvation are shown to be offered to all who repent and believe, so that everyone, no matter who they are or what they have done, has the opportunity to be a part of the people of God (e.g., 7:36–50; 15:11–32; 18:9–14; 19:1–10).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 0, NULL, 'I.      Prologue (1:1–4)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 1, 'An elegant introduction of the type often used to begin historical works in Luke’s day.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 1, 'fulfilled. God’s promises to Israel are shown to be fulfilled in Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 2, 'eyewitnesses and ministers. The apostles who witnessed and shared the good news about Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 3, 'perfect understanding. Refers to Luke’s careful research into the events of Jesus’ life. Theophilus. See Introduction, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 4, 'things in which you were instructed. Luke’s purpose is to give assurance that what they had learned about Jesus is true.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 5, 'Luke opens with narratives of the heaven-sent prophecies regarding the births of John, the prophet, and of Jesus, whose way John was sent to prepare. The narratives are written in the style of the OT, to signal the continuation of God’s work of salvation portrayed there.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 5, 'Herod. The mention of Herod the Great (ruled 37–4 B.C.) would tie the incredible story of Jesus to a known historical figure from the recent past. Aaron. Both parents were of priestly families.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 6, 'both righteous. In contrast with the murderous Herod, a righteous remnant remained in Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 8, 'division. Each of twenty-four divisions of priests served for two week-long periods each year in the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 10, 'praying. A side point to the story, emphasizing the importance of prayer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 12, 'fear. Awe at the sight of an angel, and the angel’s reassurance, are common in the OT (e.g., Gen. 15:1; Dan. 10:7–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 13, 'a son. A son promised to a barren couple follows the pattern of OT leaders like Isaac (Gen. 16:1; 17:15–16) and Samson (Judg. 13:3–24; see also Samuel, 1 Sam. 1:1–20; Acts 3:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 15, 'neither wine nor strong drink. Like Samson (Judg. 13:4–7) and the men and women who took the Nazirite vow (Num. 6:1–5), John’s life was set apart as holy to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 17, 'Elijah. John was to turn people back to God as Elijah had done (1 Kin. 17–21), and as Malachi prophesied would happen again just before the Lord’s coming (Mal. 4:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 19, 'Gabriel. The sending of God’s official emissary (Dan. 8:16) shows that God is behind what is about to happen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 24, 'hid herself. Apparently for reasons of her own, as no known Jewish custom required seclusion during pregnancy (see v. 80).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 26, 'a city. Luke uses the term “city” to refer to towns of all sizes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 27, 'virgin. Mary had never had sexual relations with a man, as v. 34 makes clear. betrothed. Engaged to be married.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 28, 'favored one. In that she has been chosen by God, despite her low status as a poor (2:24; see also Lev. 12:8), unmarried woman.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 32, 'throne of His father David. David had been promised a mighty descendant whose kingdom would have no end (2 Sam. 7:12–13; Jer. 23:5; compare to Dan. 7:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 34, 'How can this be.… Mary sought understanding, while Zacharias expressed doubt by demanding proof (vv. 18–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 35, 'Son of God. Israel (Ex. 4:22) and the angels (Job 1:6) are spoken of as sons of God, but Jesus is fully God’s Son conceived by the agency of the Holy Spirit. For more on the title “Son” referring to the PERSON OF CHRIST, see Is. 9:6; Matt. 1:23; Heb. 1:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 37, 'nothing will be impossible. As God had long ago shown Sarah, another childless woman (Gen. 18:14; 21:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 38, 'Let it be. Zacharias and Mary receive similar messages from the angel, yet in contrast with Zacharias’s doubt Mary gives a shining example of submission, even though she would be looked on negatively for bearing a child out of wedlock (e.g., Matt. 1:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 41, 'the babe leaped. The Holy Spirit already acts through John to announce the Lord’s coming (vv. 15, 17). Holy Spirit. The Holy Spirit’s work is highlighted from the beginning to the end of Luke’s writings (v. 15; Acts 28:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 43, 'the mother of my Lord. Only God, through the Holy Spirit, could have told Elizabeth this.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 46, 'Mary’s poetic hymn of praise shows how Jesus’ coming would carry forward God’s work of mercy and justice recorded in the OT (e.g., 1 Sam. 2:1–10; Ps. 107; cf. Luke 6:20–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 50, 'those who fear Him … the proud. The reason behind the reversals described in vv. 52–53 (compare to Job 5:11–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 53, 'the hungry … the rich. This can be understood both in a spiritual and a physical sense (e.g., 16:19–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 59, 'circumcise. The Law of Moses required that children be circumcised on the eighth day, a symbol of their admission into covenant relationship with God (Gen. 17:12–13; Lev. 12:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 68, 'Zacharias’s poetic prophecy answers the hearers’ question in v. 66, “What kind of child will this be?” (vv. 76–77).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 69, 'horn of salvation. The horn of an animal symbolizes strength and power (Deut. 33:17). It is often used prophetically to symbolize God’s anointed leaders (1 Sam. 2:10; Ps. 132:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 1, 70, 'mouth of His holy prophets. Voices the prophetic hope of God’s people (e.g., Ps. 106:10; Is. 62:8–11), which would now begin to be fulfilled—not through earthly co');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 1, 'Augustus. Ruler of the Roman Empire, which included Galilee and Judea. registered. For an official list of inhabitants for taxation, governmental, and military purposes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 5, 'betrothed. Engaged to be married.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 7, 'swaddling cloths. Strips of cloth wrapped snugly around a baby to keep the limbs straight. manger. A feeding trough for animals.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 8, 'shepherds. Considered a lowly occupation. Yet David had been a shepherd (1 Sam. 17:15), and Jesus compared His work with that of a shepherd (Luke 15:4–7; John 10:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 9, 'afraid. Natural in the presence of angels (1:13) and the brilliance of God’s glory (Is. 6:1–5). But God’s purpose here is to bring joy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 11, 'Savior. Caesar Augustus was praised in Roman religion as a savior who brought peace, but this babe is God’s long-promised Christ. Christ. Comes from the Hebrew, “Messiah” which means “Anointed One.” OT leaders, such as kings, were anointed to indicate their selection and empowerment by God (1 Sam. 2:10; 16:13; Ps. 2:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 12, 'the sign. Prepares the shepherds in advance for the lowly place they would find their Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 14, 'peace. Emphasizes Zacharias’s prophecy (1:79) that Jesus would lead His people “into the way of peace.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 17, 'made widely known. God arranged many witnesses to attest to this marvelous story.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 19, 'kept … pondered. As a faithful follower of God, Mary treasured God’s works in her life though, like the others, she did not yet fully understand.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 21, 'circumcision … purification. Jesus’ family were faithful keepers of God’s law (see note on 1:59; see also Lev. 12:1–8). to present Him. After God delivered Israel’s firstborn sons at the exodus, He directed that all future firstborn sons be set apart to Him and the blood of a lamb be paid to redeem them (Ex. 12:3–13; 13:11–15; see also Num. 8:13–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 25, 'Consolation. The Promised One who would bring comfort and pardon to Israel (Is. 40:1–4; 61:1–2; Luke 4:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 28, 'blessed. Praised.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 30, 'Your salvation. Salvation is personified in the Babe in Simeon’s arms whom God had sent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 32, 'the Gentiles. All those who are not descendants of Abraham and thus not a part of the Jewish people, God’s covenant people of the OT (Gen. 15). God had a love and a plan for the Gentiles through the ministry of the Jews (Is. 11:10; 49:6; Acts 9:15; 11:18). the glory of … Israel. The Savior whom God had promised to send through them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 34, 'fall and rising. While people were looking for the Messiah to set up God’s kingdom, Simeon warns that difficult times of decision were first to come as people choose whether to accept or reject Jesus. sign. Jesus’ coming is like a banner signaling God’s intentions (11:29–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 36, 'Anna. As Luke portrays an angelic visitation to both Zacharias the priest and the woman Mary (1:11, 28), so prophecies are here given by both a godly man (Simeon, v. 25) and woman (Anna). from her virginity. After she became a married woman.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 40, 'wisdom. Demonstrated in the story which follows. grace. Favor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 41, 'Passover. Every Israelite was commanded to observe this annual commemoration of Israel’s deliverance from Egypt (Ex. 12:3–27; Deut. 16:1–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 42, 'twelve. At the age of twelve boys were considered to be passing into manhood, and thus becoming responsible with regard to the Law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 49, 'I must be about My Father’s business. Jesus recognizes His first allegiance is to the will of His heavenly Father.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 2, 51, 'subject to them. Despite Jesus’ growing recognition of His destiny, He subjected Himself to His parents during His youth, in harmony with the Law of Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 1, 'A grand historical introduction marks the opening of this pivotal stage in God’s plan of salvation. Here people and positions of worldly importance are bypassed in favor of an unknown youth in the wilderness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 3, 'baptism. Immersion in water. Water immersion had long been used by many cultures to represent spiritual cleansing (e.g., 2 Kin. 5:14). In the NT baptism is a physical act indicating one’s inner repentance (e.g., Acts 22:16). repentance. A turning away from sin in both heart and action (Luke 3:7–9; Acts 26:20). remission. Forgiveness, which is freely given to those who believe and repent (Acts 5:31; 1 John 1:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 4, 'Prepare the way of the LORD. John fulfilled this prophecy (Is. 40:1–3) by calling for repentance in preparation for the Mighty One to come (Luke 3:3, 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 7, 'Brood of vipers! Shocking words used to awaken the crowds to their desperate need for forgiveness in the face of coming judgment (see 3:9, 17; Acts 17:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 8, 'fruits worthy of repentance. The repentance God calls for will result in a change of behavior that is ongoing in one’s life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 10, 'These verses describe the kind of repentance God requires.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 16, 'Holy Spirit and fire. Jesus pours out the Holy Spirit upon His followers following His ascension (Acts 2:1–4; also Acts 1:5; 2:33). The fire may symbolize both God’s judgment (Gen. 19:13, 24) and His refining of the saints (Zech. 13:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 20, 'in prison. Foreshadows the mistreatment Jesus Himself would undergo (see 9:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 21, 'Jesus also was baptized. From the beginning Jesus unites Himself with His people, walking their road with them. while He prayed. Luke repeatedly portrays Jesus in prayer at crucial transition points in His life (6:12–13; 9:29; 22:40–43).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 22, 'My beloved Son. Affirms Jesus’ already declared Sonship (1:35) as He begins His work, equipped by the Holy Spirit.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 3, 38, 'the son of Adam, the son of God. Luke traces Jesus’ connections to God’s work in history, and shows His common ancestry with the whole human race.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 1, 'The Holy Spirit had descended on Jesus at the baptism and remained with Him, filling and leading Him. the wilderness. See note on 1:80.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 2, 'tempted for forty days. Perhaps a symbolic relationship to the forty years the children of Israel spent in the wilderness being humbled and tested (Deut. 8:2; Acts 7:36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 3, 'Son of God. The devil recognized Jesus’ true identity (1:35; 3:22, 38; 4:41), and tempted Jesus to use this power to make His own way easier instead of following God’s leading in His life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 4, 'It is written. Deut. 8:2–3 describes how God struggled forty years in the wilderness to teach His people to trust Him. In contrast, Jesus shows full trust and submission, triumphing over each temptation by immediately turning to God’s word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 7, 'worship before me. It would have been much easier for Jesus to accept Satan’s authority and the kingdom of the world rather than to continue on the difficult path set out for Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 8, 'worship the LORD. Quoting again from Moses’ instruction to God’s people on the border of the promised land (Deut. 6:13), Jesus declares His full submission to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 9, 'throw Yourself down … bear you up. This would have provided miraculous evidence of messiahship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 12, 'You shall not tempt the LORD. Again quoting from Moses’ words to God’s people (Deut. 6:16), Jesus refuses to do as they had done in attempting to force God’s hand (e.g., Ex. 17:1–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 13, 'While Israel fell to their temptations in the wilderness, Jesus successfully resists.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 14, 'Jesus’ ministry is outlined in a sermon that foreshadows how His work will ultimately be received.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 14, 'in the power of the Spirit. Declares from the beginning that Jesus’ ministry is done in the power of the Spirit (3:21; Acts 10:38).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 15, 'synagogues. Jewish meeting places. glorified. Models the appropriate reaction to Jesus’ words and works (e.g., 4:36; 5:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 16, 'as His custom was. Jesus regularly participated in worship services on the Sabbath (v. 31; 6:6; 13:10). on the Sabbath day. Stories about Jesus and the SABBATH play an important role in Luke (see 4:31–41; 6:1–5; 6:6–11; 13:10–17; 14:1–24; 23:50–56). In this story (4:16–30), Jesus chooses the Sabbath to announce His ministry of preaching, liberty, and healing. The later Sabbath stories show Him performing this ministry on the Sabbath. The Sabbath is, in a very special sense, His day. For more on Jesus’ relationship to the SABBATH, see Mark 2:27–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 18, 'Quoted from Is. 61:1–2. He has anointed Me. The coming of the Spirit at Jesus’ baptism is His anointing (Luke 3:21; Acts 4:27; 10:38; compare to 1 Sam. 16:13). “Christ” (in Greek) and “Messiah” (in Hebrew) mean “Anointed One.” to the poor. Luke’s Gospel portrays Jesus bringing God’s compassion (Deut. 24:19; 1 Sam. 2:8) to the poor and suffering, both spiritually (Luke 5:20; 7:6, 10) and physically (e.g., 7:22; 14:13; 16:20–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 24, 'no prophet is accepted. Though the response is at first positive, Jesus warns of how difficult it will be for His own people to accept His prophetic message.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 25, 'Elijah … Elisha. Jesus emphasizes the inclusive nature of His ministry by describing the great prophets’ ministry to Gentiles instead of to the Jews. These prophets’ miracles were not just for the people at home but for unlikely Gentiles: one a lowly widow (1 Kin. 17:9–16), the other an enemy soldier (2 Kin. 5:1–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 28, 'filled with wrath. An early indication of the reaction many would have to Jesus’ inclusive ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 31, 'Perhaps a typical Sabbath day in the ministry of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 31, 'on the Sabbaths. See note on v. 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 35, 'rebuked him. The demons knew that announcing Jesus’ identity in this way would only do Him harm. The demons’ confession may have been a reflexive acknowledgement of Jesus’ lordship or a calculated (thoughtful) statement to cause Him harm before His audience. Either way, Jesus demanded their silence on this point. come out of him. Jesus releases this man from captivity, an illustration of what was promised in v. 18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 4, 43, 'The kingdom of God refers to the long awaited renewal of God’s reign on this earth, which Jesus fulfilled through His words and works of mercy and power (see 11:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 1, 'the multitude. Evidence of Jesus’ growing popularity. Lake of Gennesaret. Another name for the Sea of Galilee.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 4, 'Simon. Simon Peter (6:14; see also Matt. 4:18) had seen Jesus heal his mother-in-law (Luke 4:38–39), but he doubted Jesus’ ability to exert authority over nature on this scale. Nevertheless, Simon obeys Him as Master.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 8, 'Depart from me. An encounter with divine power brings one face to face with one’s own sinfulness and unworthiness (Job 42:6; Is. 6:1–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 10, 'you will catch men. Only after Simon has seen Jesus’ authority and recognized his own sinfulness does Jesus call him to join in bringing people into the kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 11, 'forsook all. At the moment of significant economic success, following Jesus becomes the one thing important for His disciples. See also 5:28; 10:27; 14:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 12, 'leprosy. Any of several disfiguring skin diseases that may or may not include what is today called modern Hansen’s disease. if You are willing. Like Christ in Gethsemane (22:42), the leper submits his request to God’s will. See also Matt. 8:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 13, 'touched. A person with leprosy was excluded from social and religious life. One who touched a leper was also considered to be ritually unclean (Lev. 13–14). Jesus was willing to do this even while the miracle could have occurred without the touch.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 16, 'withdrew into the wilderness. Luke here summarizes Jesus’ habit of leaving inhabited places to be alone with God (e.g., 4:1–2; 9:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 17, 'Pharisees. A large religious group who pursued holiness before God through the careful keeping of biblical and traditional laws. See also Mark 2:16; 12:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 20, 'their faith. The fervent hope of the paralytic and his friends is demonstrated in determined action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 21, 'Blasphemy is an attack on God’s majesty, demonstrated here in apparently usurping a right believed to belong to God alone. (God did at times delegate to humans the task of announcing God’s forgiveness, e.g., 2 Sam. 12:13.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 24, 'that you may know. The implied question is: Would God give power to make a man whole to one who was acting in a blasphemous way?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 28, 'he left all. Like Simon and his partners, Levi leaves all (meaning his profession and way of life). He did not become immediately poor. Rather, his home and resources were dedicated to Jesus’ service (v. 29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 30, 'tax collectors. Widely viewed as dishonest and, by Jews, as collaborators with the Roman occupation. sinners. Those who committed obvious, socially disapproved sins. Dining with someone was understood to place oneself on the same social level with them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 32, 'to call … sinners. Peter, in v. 8, has modeled the recognition of sinfulness Jesus is looking for.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 36, 'new … old. Jesus does not just bring an adjustment to the traditions of the Pharisees, but a completely different way of understanding.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 5, 39, 'old wine. It is difficult for those who are used to the old traditions to consider and accept what is new.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 1, 'On Sabbath stories in Luke, see note on 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 1, 'rubbing. To remove the husks.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 2, 'not lawful. Many traditional laws and restrictive traditions had been devised to explain and enforce the original Sabbath rest commanded by Scripture from the time of creation (Gen. 2:2–3; Ex. 20:8–11; Deut. 23:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 3, 'what David did. Interpreting Scripture with Scripture, Jesus points out how David, whom the Pharisees revered, gave consideration to pressing human need in his interpretation of the law (1 Sam. 21:1–6; see also Lev. 24:5–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 5, 'Lord of the Sabbath. Jesus recognizes the Sabbath as an ongoing institution, and declares His authority to interpret how it is to be kept. He implies that He is the One who instituted the Sabbath at creation (Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 6, 'On Sabbath stories in Luke, see note on 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 8, 'He knew … and said. Although knowing the danger, Jesus openly demonstrates His refusal to be ruled by human laws that contravene God’s higher principles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 9, 'good or … evil. Suggests only two options: One who refuses the opportunity to do good, does evil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 12, 'to pray. See note on 3:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 13, 'apostles. Literally, “sent ones” delegated with a task. They were appointed by Jesus as official eyewitnesses to His life, death, and resurrection (Acts 1:21–22), and as ambassadors in proclaiming God’s kingdom (Luke 9:1–2; 10:1, 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 20, 'toward His disciples. This sermon shows a whole crowd of disciples (v. 17) what it means to live as a disciple. (The first steps in becoming a disciple are addressed in more detail elsewhere, e.g., 3:3; 7:50; Acts 16:31; 26:18). poor. Jesus was anointed in order to preach good news to the spiritually and physically poor. See note on 4:18–19. kingdom. Received by all who recognize their need and turn to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 21, 'hunger … weep. In both a spiritual (7:44–50; Ps. 107:9; Ezek. 9:4–6) and physical (Luke 7:12–14; Ps. 146:7; Jer. 33:10–11) sense.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 24, 'woe. A lament, as well as a warning of something to come. rich. God may bless a person with wealth (Gen. 24:35), but this wealth is to be spent to aid those in need (Luke 1:50–53; 6:30; 12:21, 33; Prov. 28:11; Is. 58:1–10; Acts 2:44–45). Those who pride themselves in their spiritual wealth are also the recipients of this woe (e.g., Luke 18:10–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 25, 'full … laugh. Jesus deplores the taking of comfort and ease with no concern for the wickedness and tragedy going on around, and within, oneself (e.g., 12:15–21; 16:19–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 27, 'In place of seeking wealth and comfort, Jesus’ command to love your enemies reveals the radical principle underlying God’s kingdom—sacrificial love even for those apparently most undeserving of that love.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 29, 'Jesus expands on the meaning of this command with examples that provoke ongoing consideration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 29, 'offer the other. Jesus, in His own trials, exemplifies this peaceful yet unintimidated response to attack (22:50–53; see also Acts 16:22–28). At times He also recognized and acted on the need to remove Himself from the scene of danger (Luke 4:28–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 35, 'The children of the Most High will show the mercy their Father shows, instead of judging and condemning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 39, 'Can the blind lead the blind? Before attempting to lead or correct others, a disciple must submit to Jesus’ teaching.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 42, 'Hypocrite! Literally, an actor in a play who pretends to be something they are not. remove the plank. Recognize and act on the truth Jesus has taught.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 43, 'A disciple who truly follows Jesus will bear good fruit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 45, 'good treasure of his heart. Jesus’ teachings treasured in a disciple’s heart and life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 6, 47, 'comes … hears … does. Those who first come to Jesus, then hear and act on these challenging words He has taught, will be unshakable disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 2, 'centurion’s. A military officer in charge of ca. 80 to 100 soldiers. dear. Highly valued either for reasons of work or personal emotional attachment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 6, 'not worthy. Like Peter (5:8), this Gentile recognized the greatness of Jesus. He is also aware that Jesus believed that entering the house of a Gentile like him would make a Jew ceremonially unclean (e.g., Acts 11:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 10, 'found the servant well. Such a healing gives hope to readers of Luke’s Gospel, who cannot come into Jesus’ physical presence to be healed. It also recalls the healing of the Gentile, Naaman, that Jesus refers to in 4:27 (compare to 2 Kin. 5:1–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 12, 'A widow would have been dependent on her son to supply her daily needs, and now she had no one. As a woman and a widow she would have had little status in ancient society, yet Jesus has compassion for her.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 14, 'touched the open coffin. A violation of the Jewish rules of ritual purity (Num. 19:16), yet instead of Jesus receiving defilement, the man receives Jesus’ power. This recalls the raising of the widow’s son that Jesus refers to in Luke 4:25–26 (1 Kin. 17:20–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 16, 'A great prophet. Recalls Moses’ prophecy in Deut. 18:18–19 (see also Acts 3:19–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 19, 'the Coming One. John had earlier predicted the coming of “One mightier than I” (3:16), but Jesus’ actions were not meeting his expectations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 22, 'the blind see. Jesus quotes Isaiah (35:4–6) to show that He is indeed fulfilling OT prophecy, as He had promised (Luke 4:18–19; Is. 61:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 23, 'is not offended. Does not turn away from Jesus because He does not conform to their preconceived ideas.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 25, 'luxury. God’s chosen messenger did not seek luxury (6:24), but lived a simple wilderness life (1:80).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 26, 'more than a prophet. John was unique in that he was given the singular honor of preparing the way for the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 28, 'Ironically, John never got to witness Jesus’ mighty works or participate in the triumphs of His resurrection and work through the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 30, 'rejected the will of God. In rejecting the prophet’s message (3:3, 7–18, 21) they rejected God’s will for them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 32, 'Children play games that are changeable at their own whim, and are disappointed when others will not play.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 35, 'justified by all her children. Wisdom’s children are those who recognize God’s wisdom and declare it to be just and true (see v. 29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 37, 'alabaster. A costly stone that is soft enough to carve.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 38, 'stood at His feet behind Him. The diners reclined on couches propped on one elbow, with their feet extended away from the table. tears. Emphasizes her heartfelt response to His ministry. anointed … with … oil. An expression of honor. (Normally the head is anointed, but she may have felt unworthy.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 39, 'if He were a prophet. Jesus ironically demonstrates that He is indeed a prophet, by knowing and responding to Simon’s silent criticism (v. 40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 41, 'two debtors. Both owed a significant debt they were unable to repay. five hundred denarii. About 500 days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 44, 'water … kiss … oil. Traditional means of showing hospitality.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 47, 'for she loved much. The loving acts of the woman are an enactment of her faith (see vv. 48, 50; 5:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 48, 'Your sins are forgiven. In the eyes of heaven she is no longer a sinner, for forgiveness has already been granted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 7, 49, 'Who is this …? The question is left unanswered for Luke’s audience to ponder (see 5:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 1, 'kingdom of God. See note on 4:43.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 2, 'certain women. Assistance given by women to rabbis (Jewish teachers) was not uncommon, but women traveling with a rabbi was unheard of. Luke, however, often pairs stories of men and of women receiving and participating in Jesus’ ministry (2:22–38; 4:31–39; 7:1–17, 36–50; 24:1–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 5, 'A sower walking back and forth on a field scattering seed was a common sight. Inevitably not every place the seed fell was adequate soil for growing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 10, 'To you. The disciples had willingly heard and followed Jesus’ words (e.g., 5:1–11). And hearing they may not understand. Jesus’ further explanation makes clear that everyone has a choice whether they will hear God’s word with an open responsive heart, and so be given understanding (see vv. 18, 21; Ps. 81:10–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 15, 'keep it. Hold to it firmly as something precious so that the seed of God’s word can bear fruit in their lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 16, 'the light. God’s word may also be compared to light that illuminates all things.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 18, 'take heed how you hear. States the main point of vv. 3–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 21, 'My mother and My brothers. Not a rejection of His own mother and brothers but an invitation to all to be included in His family by hearing and doing God’s word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 25, 'Who can this be, whom even the forces of nature obey? This and the previous question are left open for Luke’s audience to answer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 26, 'country of the Gadarenes. The exact location on the eastern shore of the Sea of Galilee is uncertain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 28, 'torment. By sending him to the “abyss” (v. 31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 30, 'Legion. A Roman military unit of 4,000 to 6,000 soldiers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 31, 'abyss. Represents a place where the forces of evil are imprisoned until their ultimate destruction (Rev. 17:8; 20:1–3; see note on Rev. 20:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 33, 'drowned. The loss of the swine resulted in many hearing what Jesus did for the demon-possessed man (v. 34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 37, 'asked Him to depart. As in the parable of the sower, Jesus’ enactment of God’s word results in rejection by many, but discipleship for some (vv. 38–39).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 39, 'Return … and tell. While some are called like Peter to travel far from home (5:10; Acts 1:8), this new Gentile follower receives the task of telling His own people what he has witnessed of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 41, 'ruler of the synagogue. An influential community member in charge of the services of the synagogue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 42, 'Jesus has just delivered a man from demons; now He will help two women.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 43, 'flow of blood. A continuous flow which made this woman ritually impure, cutting her off from other people and from the temple of God (Lev. 15:25–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 44, 'touched … His garment. Though impure her touch does not defile Jesus; instead it cleanses the woman.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 52, 'sleeping. Jesus compares death with sleep, rather than comforting her parents with the assurance that she is now in heaven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 53, 'ridiculed. As Jesus had suggested would happen to all who profess his name (6:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 8, 55, 'spirit. From the Greek word pneuma which means “wind” or “breath.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 1, 'power and authority. The apostles are empowered to continue the very work Jesus had been doing (4:36–40; 8:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 3, 'Take nothing. Jesus gave different instructions later, in 22:36.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 5, 'shake off the … dust. Done formerly when leaving a pagan country to symbolize one’s separation from the ways of those contrary to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 7, 'tetrarch. A ruler appointed by Rome.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 8, 'Elijah. One like Elijah was expected before the day of the Lord (Mal. 4:5). This was fulfilled in John the Baptist (Luke 1:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 10, 'For the miracle of feeding the 5,000, see note on Matt. 14:13–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 17, 'were filled. As God had long before provided bread for the children of Israel in “a deserted place” (v. 10; Ex. 16:4). fragments … taken up. God gives in abundance and His followers do not waste the overflow.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 18, 'praying. As Jesus prepares to inform the apostles of the difficult events to come, He turns, as always, to God in prayer (see note on 3:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 19, 'Elijah. See note on v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 20, 'The Christ. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 21, 'tell … no one. Calling Him “Christ” would raise expectations and opposition that would only hinder His ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 22, 'must suffer. The disciples’ first warning that the Messiah would be different from the conquering Messiah they had hoped for.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 23, 'take up his cross daily. Like Jesus, be daily prepared to face powerful opposition while following Him. (Listeners at the time would well remember the many horrible crosses in Galilee after the last revolt against the Romans.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 24, 'will lose it. One’s life cannot be saved by one’s own efforts no matter how selfishly it is guarded. will save it. Only by surrendering one’s life to God can it be saved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 26, 'when He comes. Following Jesus (v. 23) is the best possible preparation for His Return, a day of great glory. On the theme of the Second Coming in Luke, see Introduction, “Message,” and 17:20–37; 19:11–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 27, 'see the kingdom of God. This mysterious saying may refer to the preview of His kingdom glory seen at the transfiguration (9:32; 2 Pet. 1:16–17), His ascension into heaven to sit at God’s right hand (Acts 2:33), or the coming of the Spirit at Pentecost (Acts 2:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 28, 'to pray. See note on 3:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 30, 'Moses and Elijah. Elijah was taken to heaven without seeing death (2 Kin. 2:11), while Moses is traditionally understood to have been taken to heaven following his death (Jude 9). The two represent the Law and the Prophets in the OT, both of which witness to Christ (e.g., Deut. 18:15; Is. 9:6; Luke 24:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 31, 'decease. The Greek word is exodos, literally meaning “departure.” Like Israel’s exodus from slavery in Egypt under Moses’ leadership, Jesus’ death, resurrection, and ascension would save many from slavery and bring them to the true promised land.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 33, 'tabernacles. Tent dwellings. In the OT the tabernacle was the place where God dwelt among people and was worshiped. not knowing what he said. Peter still does not recognize that Moses and Elijah were not of equal status with Jesus, to be worshiped.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 35, 'Hear Him! Deut. 18:17–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 41, 'perverse. Literally used of a distorted vessel on a potter’s wheel. Apparently it refers to the disciples’ failure to heal the boy (see 9:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 48, 'receives this … child. Cares for the unimportant and vulnerable ones rather than seeking one’s own advancement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 50, 'Do not forbid. Though they must distinguish truth from error (21:8), humans do not control the giving of God’s grace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 51, 'set His face. To the agony and triumph He knows is ahead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 52, 'Samaritans. See note on 10:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 53, 'did not receive Him. Jerusalem was seen as a rival to Mount Gerizim, their own place of worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 54, 'command fire. Still the disciples seek to exercise coercive power instead of loving care (vv. 46–50).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 9, 60, 'the dead. Anyone separated from God (15:24; 18:18, 22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 1, 'appointed seventy others. Like the twelve (9:1), these are empowered to continue Jesus’ work of kingdom preaching and healing (10:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 2, 'The harvest. Those ready to hear and follow the message of God’s kingdom (vv. 1, 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 3, 'as lambs. Apparently vulnerable and dependent on others (vv. 3–9), but in reality backed by God’s power (vv. 11–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 4, 'greet no one. Lengthy customary greetings may have distracted from their mission.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 5, 'Peace. The customary Jewish greeting, “Shalom,” wishes peace and wholeness upon the hearer. This blessing becomes reality in the coming of Jesus (1:79; 2:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 6, 'son of peace. One open to receiving peace from Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 11, 'dust. See note on 9:5. kingdom of God has come near. See note on 4:43.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 12, 'Sodom. Wicked city destroyed by fire from God (Gen. 19:1–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 13, 'Woe. See note on 6:24. Chorazin … Bethsaida. Jewish towns, whereas Tyre and Sidon were Gentile cities near Galilee. repented. The essential human response to God’s correction (3:3; 13:3; Ezek. 18:30–31; Jon. 3:4–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 15, 'Hades. The place of the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 18, 'fall like lightning. See Is. 14:12–15 (compare to Rev. 12:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 19, 'serpents and scorpions. Symbolic of evil (Gen. 3:1–5; Deut. 8:15; Ps. 140:1–3). the enemy. Satan. Nothing has power to permanently harm those whom Jesus sends.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 20, 'written. On the books to be used in the JUDGMENT, see Dan. 7:10; Mal. 3:16–18; Phil. 4:3; Rev. 3:5; 20:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 21, 'Jesus recognizes God’s justice in what He has done. hidden. Those considered wise were not willing to hear, so God has sought out the lowly (see 1:51–52; note on 8:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 25, 'lawyer. An expert in the Law of Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 28, 'answered rightly. Love is the nucleus of the law and the standard by which God’s people are called to live.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 29, 'to justify himself. To look wise in the eyes of the onlookers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 30, 'Jerusalem to Jericho. Descends about 3,000 feet (900 m) in 18 miles (29 km) through rocky desert.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 32, 'Levite. Levites assisted the priests in temple duties. By helping the victim, both risked another attack and the time-consuming ritual cleansing for touching a potentially dead man (Lev. 21:1–4; Num. 19:11–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 33, 'Samaritan. Samaritans were of mixed Jewish and Gentile ancestry. Jews and Samaritans had a long history of hostility toward each other, beginning soon after the Jews’ return from exile in Babylon (Ezra 4:1–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 35, 'two denarii. About two days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 36, 'which … was neighbor. Turns the lawyer’s original question, “Who is my neighbor?” into the more important question, “Am I a good neighbor?”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 39, 'at Jesus’ feet. The position of a disciple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 10, 42, 'that good part. Though Jesus sympathizes with Martha’s desire to serve, she had allowed it to distract her from what must be primary and would have brought her peace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 1, 'teach us to pray. Disciples may still sit at Jesus’ feet (10:39, 42) through prayer. as John also taught. Recognized rabbis/teachers taught their disciples prayers that illustrated their key teachings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 2, 'A model prayer, seeking first the things of God, then one’s daily needs.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 2, 'Father. An unusually simple and personal opening, used by Jesus Himself (10:21). Hallowed. Honored as holy. Your kingdom come. Seeks the soon-coming of God’s eternal kingdom (see 10:9; Dan. 2:44; 7:13–14), as well as His reign in our lives at the present moment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 3, 'daily bread. Calls for simple food, as needed for each day.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 4, 'For we also. God’s forgiveness is free to all who repent (2 Chr. 7:14; Ps. 32:1–5; Acts 5:31), and calls for forgiveness of others in response (Luke 17:3; Matt. 18:21–35). temptation. Acknowledges one’s need for God’s continual leading (see 22:40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 6, 'a friend. This refers to prayer for the sake of another.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 9, 'In contrast to the sleepy friend (v. 8), God provides mercifully for the needs of His friends, though not always with an immediate “yes” (11:2; 22:42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 13, 'the Holy Spirit. The greatest and most essential of gifts. The Spirit brings close the guidance and power of God (Acts 5:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 16, 'from heaven. Unsatisfied with Jesus’ healings, they demanded even more spectacular evidence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 19, 'your sons. If they claim that their kinsmen cast out demons by God’s power, there is no grounds for claiming Jesus does it by Beelzebub.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 20, 'has come. God’s reign is present in Christ, and the demons are powerless. See 4:43.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 21, 'a strong man. Referring to Satan (vv. 15, 18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 22, 'a stronger. Jesus, who has overcome Satan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 23, 'not with Me. There is no neutral ground.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 26, 'they … dwell there. There is no safety from Satan unless God reigns within (v. 23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 27, 'Those who hear and do God’s word enter a relationship with Him deeper even than one’s birth family (8:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 29, 'sign of Jonah. After spending three days and three nights in the “belly of the great fish” (Matt. 12:40), Jonah’s preaching became a sign to Nineveh, and Nineveh responded (v. 32; Jon. 3:1–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 31, 'queen of the South. The queen of Sheba, who longed to hear wisdom from God (1 Kin. 10:1–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 33, 'lamp. Jesus, who is sent to shed light upon all (see 8:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 34, 'the lamp of the body. Anciently, a properly functioning eye was seen to bring light into the body.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 35, 'take heed. Jesus calls His hearers to allow the full light of His presence into their hearts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 38, 'had not first washed. For symbolic, not physical cleanliness. This was a tradition built on the OT command for priests to wash before approaching God (Ex. 30:19–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 41, 'give alms. Care for those in need.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 42, 'woe. See note on 6:24. tithe. A tenth, required by God as His portion of what He has given (Lev. 27:30; Mal. 3:8–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 44, 'hypocrites. See note on 6:42. Luke 11:39–43 give the reasons for this charge. graves … not seen. Touching a grave made one ritually unclean (Num. 19:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 46, 'burdens hard to bear. Over the centuries, many additional rules and regulations had been added to God’s law given to Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 48, 'you approve. Jesus claimed that the tombs they built were actually memorials to their fathers’ killing rather than to the prophets, for the lawyers themselves were about to kill Him, the One of whom the prophets prophesied, and also sought to kill His apostles and prophets (vv. 49–51; Acts 7:52, 60; 12:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 11, 52, 'key of knowledge. The knowledge of salvation (1:77; Matt. 23:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 1, 'innumerable multitude. Evidence of Jesus’ growing influence. leaven. Dough from a previous batch of bread that, mixed with a new batch, causes it to rise. Hypocrisy (see note on 6:42) likewise influences and deceives many.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 4, 'those who kill the body. The scribes and Pharisees of 11:53–54, as well as many future opponents.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 5, 'Fear Him. Reverent fear of God liberates from all other fears (v. 7). hell. From Hebrew Hinnom; literally, a ravine outside Jerusalem where trash was burned, and which became a symbol of final judgment (Jer. 7:30–32; 19:1–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 6, 'two copper coins. Worth about one-eighth of a day’s wage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 8, 'confesses. Declares allegiance to.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 10, 'blasphemes. Speaks against. will not be forgiven. Many opposed Jesus during His earthly life and were later forgiven (Acts 2:36–38), but those who fully resist the Holy Spirit’s work in their hearts have cut themselves off from what God wants to do for them (see John 16:13; Acts 7:51; Rom. 8:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 12, 'will teach. This is evidenced, for example, by Peter’s speech to the rulers and elders in Acts 4:8, 31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 15, 'covetousness. Greed. A warning illustrated in the parable which follows.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 18, 'build greater. He already had full barns to supply his need.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 19, 'soul. The Greek word, pneuma, refers to a person’s life as a whole (Prov. 10:3; Acts 2:41), but at times specifically of one’s inner life and thought (Luke 10:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 21, 'rich toward God. To use one’s possessions as God has required (see 3:11; 21:1–4; Is. 58:6–7; Mal. 3:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 29, 'seek. Give all one’s attention to.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 33, 'Sell what you have and give alms. Share one’s surplus with those in need (11:41), an instruction obeyed by early believers (Acts 2:44–45; 4:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 35, 'girded. Robes tied with a belt to keep them out of the way when working.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 37, 'watching. Looking forward to the master’s coming, while living as He has just described (chaps. 10–12). serve them. A master serving His servants is a startling picture of Jesus’ way of leadership.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 38, 'watch. Night was divided into four “watches” by the Romans, three by the Jews.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 39, 'The parable shifts to a different illustration where the master of the house is the believer waiting for Christ’s return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 42, 'steward. The slave assigned to manage the household, representing those given responsibility to care for God’s people until He returns.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 46, 'cut him in two. The strong reaction of this earthly master conveys the anguish God feels toward any who abuse those they are sent to serve.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 48, 'few. The master is least severe to the one who did wrong in ignorance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 49, 'to send fire. Fire in the OT refines and purifies. It is used in reference to God’s people (Zech. 13:9) and also of the ultimate destruction of the wicked (Gen. 19:24) and cleansing of the earth (Zech. 3:8). The Spirit arrives in Acts 2:3 with tongues of fire.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 50, 'a baptism. Like His literal water baptism, Jesus’ death and resurrection would be a transition to a new phase in His ministry (9:22; Acts 2:33; compare to Rom. 6:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 51, 'to give peace. Not peace, but fire (v. 49). Though peace was promised (1:79) and is now available within (7:50), its complete enactment is delayed until His coming (19:42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 56, 'Hypocrites! See note on 6:42. The multitudes (12:54), though claiming to be God’s people and flocking to see Jesus, do not accept Him for who He really is.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 58, 'the magistrate. Recalls the judge mentioned at the beginning of this teaching (v. 14). The verse may allude to making things right with God before it is too late.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 12, 59, 'mite. Small bronze Jewish coin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 2, 'worse sinners. It was often wrongly assumed that anytime a person suffered, it was because of their own wrongdoing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 3, 'repent. See note on 3:4–6. In the final judgment, God freely saves those who have repented of their sins (15:10; Acts 26:30–31; Rom. 6:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 4, 'tower. Likely a tower on the city wall near the pool of Siloam in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 6, 'fig tree. Often used to represent Israel and Judah (Jer. 8:13; Hos. 9:10; see also Luke 3:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 8, 'this year also. Underlines the urgency of Jesus’ counsel to repent (vv. 3, 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 10, 'On Sabbath stories in Luke, see note on 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 11, 'spirit of infirmity. Verse 16 identifies Satan as the cause of her illness. Jesus similarly heals an unnamed man on the Sabbath in 14:1–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 14, 'ruler. See note on 8:41. not on the Sabbath. See note on 6:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 16, 'on the Sabbath. Jesus repeatedly sought to demonstrate that proper Sabbath-keeping embraces acts of compassion and restoration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 17, 'put to shame. They could match neither Jesus’ logic nor the powerful compassion He showed for the people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 19, 'mustard seed. A tiny seed whose ultimate growth in size is startling.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 21, 'leaven. See note on 12:1. Both the seed planted by a man (vv. 18–19), and the leaven added by a woman (vv. 20–21), assure hearers that the much-opposed kingdom proclaimed by Jesus would, by its very nature, grow and spread greatly. three measures. About 36 quarts (39 l).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 23, 'few … saved. A debate of the time asked: Would the saved be only the righteous within Israel? All Israel? Or a few worthy Gentiles as well?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 24, 'Strive. Used of athletes pressing forward to victory. narrow gate. Entrance into the kingdom (v. 28) gained by believing in Jesus (12:31; Acts 16:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 27, 'I do not know you. These are not recognized as disciples of Jesus and thus members of God’s kingdom (see note on v. 24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 28, 'gnashing of teeth. An expression of rage (Ps. 35:16; Acts 7:54).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 29, 'from the east. Gentiles, and others, whom they least expected.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 32, 'third day. Alludes to His death and resurrection. shall be perfected. Will complete His mission.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 34, 'a hen. The picture of a mother hen drawing her chicks close conveys the truth of God’s tender concern for His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 13, 35, 'Blessed is He. One day every knee will bow before Jesus (Is. 45:23; Phil. 2:10–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 1, 'On Sabbath stories in Luke, see note on 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 1, 'Pharisees. See note on 5:17. There is no record of Jesus’ refusing an invitation to share a meal, whether with Pharisees or with the most despised of sinners. watched Him. See 6:7, 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 2, 'dropsy. A swelling of the body due to excess fluids.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 4, 'kept silent. Their reason for silence is suggested in v. 6; 13:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 7, 'best places. Dining couches were often arranged in a U-shape along three walls of the room. The host reclined in the center with the most important guests at his right and left and the least important at the far ends.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 9, 'with shame. Honor was of utmost importance in ancient culture. Jesus, however, tells people to start at the very foot of the table (v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 11, 'humbles himself. Greco-Roman thinkers warned against exalting oneself too highly, but unlike the testimony of Jesus they also considered humility something to be avoided.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 12, 'repaid. The primary motivation for doing something for a non-family member was, as often today, in order to receive a favor in return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 13, 'the poor. An instruction already given in the Law of Moses (Deut. 14:29; 16:14; compare to Luke 4:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 14, 'resurrection of the just. See Dan. 12:2; Acts 24:15; 1 Thess. 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 17, 'sent his servant. It was a custom for the host to send a servant to summon guests as the event is beginning (see Esth. 6:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 18, 'excuses. The casual nature of these excuses reveals the guests’ lack of interest in attending the supper.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 23, 'compel. Strongly urge. Represents God’s desire that all, even the most distant or lowly, may be part of the great banquet in His kingdom (Is. 25:6–9; Rev. 19:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 24, 'none of those. Jesus’ point is unmistakable. His invitation to God’s kingdom banquet is being similarly spurned by the Jewish establishment, and He will turn to others (see note on v. 23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 26, 'hate. In Jesus’ world, overstatement was often used to emphasize a point. An attitude of hatred is not called for (see 6:27), but rather putting Jesus first, which risks an accusation of hating one’s family.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 27, 'his cross. See note on 9:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 28, 'tower. For protection of crops, a home, or a city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 32, 'a delegation. As with the tower, the goal is safety from approaching danger.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 33, 'forsake. All our possessions are to be put at God’s disposal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 14, 34, 'lost its flavor. Like a disciple who does not count the cost, and gives up total commitment to Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 1, 'Three parables showing the joy in heaven when one sinner repents.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 1, 'the tax collectors and the sinners. See note on 5:30. Only these outcasts answer the call Jesus has just made (14:35).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 2, 'Pharisees and scribes. See note on 5:17. Jesus responds to their complaint with three vivid parables showing how God treats sinners.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 4, 'go after the one. God takes the initiative to seek and to find, even just one (Ezek. 34:11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 7, 'joy … over one sinner. In contrast to Jesus’ critics, who rejected those they saw as sinners (v. 2; see also Ezek. 34:16–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 8, 'ten silver coins. Drachmas—a drachma was a day’s wage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 9, 'God’s deep concern for His own is again pictured, this time through a woman’s actions and within her own house.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 11, 'The third parable in this set moves from focus on God’s action to consider the action of the sinner himself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 12, 'falls to me. The younger of two sons would inherit one-third of the property; however, it was an insult to demand it while his father was still living.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 15, 'feed swine. Working for a Gentile feeding unclean animals (Lev. 11:7) was one of the most degrading jobs imaginable for a Jew.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 17, 'came to himself. Admitted the truth about himself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 18, 'I have sinned. An example of the repentance God desires (vv. 7, 10; 13:2–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 20, 'ran. Authority figures in that culture did not run. Here, the father abandoned his dignity to show his deep love and forgiveness, even before his son can speak.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 22, 'The robe, ring, and sandals demonstrate his return to high status and authority—above slaves and other servants.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 23, 'fatted calf. Specially cared for and fed on grain in anticipation of a future celebration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 25, 'older son. Holds up a mirror to Jesus’ critics who, like the older son, saw themselves as obedient, yet they lacked love (v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 28, 'pleaded. The father felt compassion also for his older son despite his resentful attitude.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 29, 'serving you. His anger evidences that his obedience was not out of love, but only for the purpose of getting a good return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 15, 30, 'son of yours. Admits no relationship to his own brother. Whether the older brother will repent and welcome the lost brother is left up to those “older brothers” who would hear Jesus’ words.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 1, 'The disciples are the recipients of this parable. steward. Managed the business affairs of a wealthy person.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 6, 'A hundred measures. About 1,000 gallons (4 kl). write fifty. So they would owe him a favor. It appears the steward was again misusing his master’s belongings, although possibly the portion he discounted was the fee he was to receive for his services.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 7, 'A hundred measures. About 1,100 bushels (40 kl).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 8, 'the unjust steward. Though the steward’s master is impressed by how he cares for his own future needs, Jesus identifies him as unjust.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 9, 'make friends. Though this steward was unjust, he sets an example for the “sons of light” in the diligence with which he used possessions to secure his future. unrighteous mammon. Possessions, which are of no heavenly value unless they are used for heavenly purposes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 11, 'true riches. Things of eternal value.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 12, 'another man’s. Earthly possessions belong to God and are to be used, as He requires, to care for one another (12:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 15, 'justify yourselves. Seek human approval (see 7:29; 12:1). an abomination. Detestable, disgusting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 16, 'pressing into it. May mean either trying hard, or being urged to enter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 17, 'tittle. A small marking on a Hebrew letter. to fail. Jesus’ preaching of love only reinforces God’s law, reorienting it again around its proper center in the love of God and love of neighbor (10:27; Lev. 19:18; Deut. 6:5; 15:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 18, 'commits adultery. Illustrates the ongoing validity of the law, showing how the law against adultery extends also over the man-made institution of divorce (Deut. 24:1–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 19, 'This parable teaches the lesson that worldly wealth is no guarantee of eternal bliss, a lesson already to be found in Moses and the Prophets. Jesus uses a popular yet mythical story about Abraham to give force to this main point. After all, if people did not believe Moses and the Prophets, they would not believe even if someone did rise from the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 23, 'saw Abraham … and said. A mythical description marking this as an imaginary story, built on popular folk tales and told to illustrate Jesus’ point about sharing one’s wealth (see 6:20, 24). Given the figurative nature of this parable, the details should not be misread to determine what happens to the saved and the lost at death. The Bible makes it clear that, at death, all rest in the grave (see Ps. 146:4; Eccl. 9:5; John 11:11, 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 23, 'Hades. Technically both Lazarus and the rich man go to Hades, the place of the dead. On the Bible’s teaching on HELL and the punishment of the wicked, see notes on Is. 34:9–10; Matt. 10:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 16, 31, 'neither will they be persuaded. Demonstrated in the continued opposition to Jesus even after the raising of the widow’s son (7:12–15), the ruler’s daughter (8:52–55), and Jesus Himself (24:6; Acts 4:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 1, 'offenses. Temptations to act or believe wrongly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 2, 'these little ones. Probably refers to the lowly and suffering ones like Lazarus just spoken of in 16:20–23 (see also 10:21; 12:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 3, 'rebuke. In addition to avoiding being a cause of sin (vv. 1–2), Jesus calls on disciples to respectfully reprove and forgive those who do sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 4, 'seven times in a day. Not a maximum number; rather it illustrates the unlimited nature of the forgiveness Jesus calls for.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 5, 'Increase our faith. Possibly a response to the difficult instruction Jesus has just given.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 6, 'as a mustard seed. Even a little faith is enough to do the impossible. (Jesus is not here calling for spectacular transplanting tricks, but illustrating the power already available to His disciples to follow His leading.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 10, 'unprofitable servants. Disciples are not to seek praise or preference for following the Master’s instructions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 12, 'lepers. See notes on 5:12–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 15, 'glorified God. A despised Samaritan (see note on 10:33) models grateful faith for the disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 20, 'Jesus teaches about His Second Coming. See also the Introduction, “Message,” and the notes on 21:5–36 and 21:25–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 20, 'with observation. They would see at this time no signs of the conquering kingdom they were looking for.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 21, 'within you. Through the ministry of Jesus, God’s reign is already present in the hearts of His followers. The phrase may also mean “among you,” referring to Jesus’ presence among believers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 22, 'you will desire. A warning to the disciples that they would experience a time without His physical presence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 24, 'as the lightning. Unlike the false messiahs that have appeared since Jesus’ time, Jesus’ coming will be sudden and widely visible (21:27; Rev. 1:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 27, 'ate … drank … married … were given in marriage. The usual events of human life can become so absorbing that we forget that life’s great purpose is to prepare for Christ’s Return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 31, 'housetop. A good location to catch cooling breezes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 32, 'Lot’s wife. Looking back to what she had left resulted in the loss of her life (Gen. 19:17, 26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 33, 'save his life. See note on 9:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 34, 'one will be taken. The choice to be ready for His coming is a choice that must be made by each individual. Vv. 34–36 are not talking about a secret rapture, since according to v. 24, Christ’s coming will be a very public event. It is at that time that the angels will gather His people from the four winds of the earth (Matt. 24:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 17, 37, '“Where, Lord?” The disciples wish to know to what location the Son of Man would come (see v. 24). The proverb suggests it will be as obvious as a gathering of vultures around a dead animal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 1, 'pray and not lose heart. What disciples are to do while waiting for His coming (v. 8; 17:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 2, 'regard man. Care what people thought of him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 3, 'widow. A woman without a husband had few rights or resources. came. She kept coming repeatedly (see v. 5; Deut. 27:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 5, 'avenge. A legal term for the granting of justice. weary me. Literally, “give me a black eye.” (Jesus tells the story with humor.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 7, 'avenge His own elect. In contrast to the unjust judge God answers the call for justice from His elect. bears long. He is patient but will not delay longer than necessary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 8, 'speedily. May refer to His diligent work to bring all things to completion, or to the sudden nature of His coming, when complete justice will be accomplished (2 Pet. 3:9). find faith. Meaning the kind of faith exemplified by the widow, who continued her requests despite the delay.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 11, 'not like other men. The Pharisee judges others, and ignores his own sinfulness, which Jesus has clearly pointed out in 7:30; 11:42–44.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 12, 'fast … give tithes. The Pharisee fasted and tithed more than the norm.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 13, 'a sinner. Both actions and words show his recognition of his undeserving state.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 14, 'justified. God counted him as righteous, not on the basis of his good behavior but because he called on God recognizing his need.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 15, 'touch. An act of blessing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 17, 'as a little child. Like the tax collector (vv. 13–14), babies depend fully on the caregiver, with their only claim being their great need.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 19, 'No one. Jesus gives the glory to God, and leaves His own relation to Him open for the ruler to ponder.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 22, 'Sell all. While every person is called to commit everything they have and are to follow Jesus in His way of love (12:15–33; 14:16–33), Jesus saw that this ruler needed to literally give away all his possessions in order to be fully committed to God (see 3:10; 19:8–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 23, 'very sorrowful. The ruler had come to love his possessions more than he loved God (compare to Ex. 20:3), yet Jesus extended God’s invitation to him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 24, 'How hard. Because riches distract from God’s kingdom (8:14; 12:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 26, 'Who then. The rich were considered to be especially favored by God. If they couldn’t make it, who could?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 27, 'impossible with men. God alone is able to save.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 30, 'present time. Jesus’ followers do not need to wait until they get to heaven to begin receiving His blessings, for some may be received even now.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 33, 'kill Him. As Jesus had just challenged His followers to give everything in their lives over to Him (vv. 29–30; 14:16–33), so He was willing to give up all for them (22:19; Is. 53:3–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 18, 42, 'Receive your sight. The disciples, too, had just been shown to be spiritually blind (vv. 31–34), but they would receive understanding through Jesus’ intervention (24:27, 45–47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 2, 'he was rich. This is the hopeful finale in a series of rich man stories (12:16–21; 16:19–24; 18:18–27; see also 1:53; 6:24), answering the crowd’s earlier question, “Who then can be saved?” (18:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 3, 'sought to see. Not satisfied with hearsay, Zacchaeus was so thirsty to see Jesus that he sacrificed his dignity and climbed a tree.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 5, 'I must stay. Jesus was determined not just to see Zacchaeus but to enter into a personal relationship with him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 8, 'I give. Repentance is demonstrated in his commitment: (1) to care for the poor as Jesus had instructed (12:33); and (2) to repay those he had cheated, acting according to the strictest standards of the law (Ex. 22:1; Lev. 6:1–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 9, 'salvation has come. Salvation arrives at the moment of Zacchaeus’ repentance (5:30–32; Acts 26:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 11, 'On the theme of the Second Coming in Luke, see Introduction, “Message,” and notes on 21:5–36 and 21:25–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 11, 'they thought. Although His followers are still blind to what is happening, Jesus continues to prepare them with teachings they would later understand (see 12:35–40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 13, 'minas. One mina was worth 100 days’ wages. Do business. Jesus left His followers a much more important task: the proclamation of the gospel (24:46–48; Acts 1:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 21, 'feared you. If he had truly feared him for this reason, he would have used the mina more wisely.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 26, 'does not have. The servant had no gain to return to the master, only because he chose to do nothing with what he was given.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 27, 'slay them. Jesus, too, will return to set up His kingdom, but His judgment will be founded on justice, in contrast to the desire for personal gain and power evidenced by earthly kings (Ps. 9:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 30, 'a colt. It was prophesied that a king would enter Jerusalem on a colt, bringing salvation and peace to all the earth (Zech. 9:9–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 37, 'Traditionally the leaders of a city would go out to welcome a dignitary with great acclamation. Here it was left to Jesus’ followers, who celebrate His arrival with joy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 39, 'rebuke Your disciples. Instead of welcoming the king, these Pharisees scold Him (vv. 14, 27, 47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 41, 'wept. Jesus was not just fulfilling a duty but acting out of the deepest love for His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 42, 'hidden. Jesus had brought the good news for all the people, but most had refused to see it (see 4:43; 10:13; 12:54–56), so it was too late to see it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 43, 'enemies will. Fulfilled when Rome destroyed Jerusalem in A.D. 70.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 44, 'visitation. A term used of the coming of God for good (Gen. 50:24), as here in the person of Jesus the Messiah, but also, when not heeded, for judgment (Ex. 32:33–34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 19, 47, 'sought to destroy Him. The Lord came to His temple as prophesied (Mal. 3:1), but Jerusalem’s leaders greet Him with rejection instead of celebration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 1, 'the gospel. Though in this section Luke mainly reports on the attempts of the leaders to entrap Him, Jesus continued to preach the good news to the people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 2, 'by what authority. Jesus has acted with authority not only in His teaching and healing (4:32, 36), but now also in His actions in the temple (19:45–46), where the priests expected to have complete control.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 3, 'Jesus avoids a direct answer to the question about His authority, but His own question in reply hints at the answer, for John had announced that Jesus was the Mighty One and Judge (3:15–17; see also 7:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 9, 'a vineyard. Isaiah tells a similar parable of a vineyard, which represented Israel (Is. 5:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 10, 'a servant. Jesus has spoken earlier of God’s servants the prophets, whom Israel’s leaders had mistreated (11:49; 13:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 13, 'beloved son. Jesus is the beloved of the Father (3:22; 9:35). Jesus hereby answers the leaders’ question (20:2) indirectly, showing that His authority comes from the Father.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 14, 'kill him. Israel’s leaders were already plotting to kill Jesus (19:47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 16, 'to others. To those who honor the Father and His Son and servants. Certainly not! The people recognize the implications of the parable and reject the possibility that God, the vineyard owner, would ever destroy them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 17, 'The stone. Represents Jesus (Is. 28:16; Acts 4:10–12; 1 Pet. 2:5–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 18, 'grind him. Jesus is the Cornerstone of salvation, but will also return as a judging power (Is. 8:14; Dan. 2:44–45).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 20, 'to deliver Him. Ironically, the religious leaders immediately act to fulfill the prophecy of vv. 14–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 22, 'Is it lawful. A “yes” would get Jesus in trouble with those who believed that paying taxes to Caesar was disloyal to God; a “no” would make it possible for Jesus to be accused of treason.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 24, 'a denarius. A common silver coin of the Roman Empire.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 25, 'to Caesar … to God. Let Caesar have his likeness back (Rom. 13:1–7), but remember that God rules over all and claims our ultimate allegiance (Ps. 24:1; Dan. 4:32; Acts 5:29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 27, 'Sadducees. An influential religious party (Acts 5:17) which claimed to accept only the five books of Moses as authoritative Scripture. See also Mark 12:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 28, 'Moses wrote. See Deut. 25:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 35, 'neither marry nor are given in marriage. Marriage as we know it will be replaced with something better, which cannot yet be known (1 Cor. 2:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 38, 'live to Him. Demonstrates that God has the power of life, even for the dead (8:52; John 11:11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 41, 'Christ … Son of David. Jesus has repeatedly been recognized as Christ (see note on 2:11; see also 4:41; 9:20) and Son of David (1:32, 69; 18:38).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 42, 'David … said. Ps. 110:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 44, 'how is He then his Son? The unavoidable answer is that the Christ is more than just a son of David. Jesus thus again indirectly answers the priests’ question, “By what authority are You doing these things?” (v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 46, 'Beware. Jesus is concerned that His disciples not fall under the influence of these temptations (see also 11:39–44; 12:1–3; 14:1–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 20, 47, 'devour widows’ houses. Use their influence to gain property from the poor dishonestly (e.g., 18:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 2, 'mites. The smallest Jewish coins then in use.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 4, 'put in all. Note the contrast with the selfish scribes described in 20:46–47.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 5, 'For more on the theme of the Second Coming in Luke, see Introduction, “Message,” and 9:26; 17:20–37; 19:11–27. For more on Christ’s SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24–25; Mark 13:1–37; John 14:1–3; Acts 1:6–11; 1 Thess. 4:13–18; 2 Pet. 3:10–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 6, 'thrown down. The majestic temple, the center and pride of the Jewish world, was destroyed by the Romans about 40 years later in A.D. 70.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 7, 'these things. The destruction of the temple and the hoped-for setting up of God’s kingdom were perceived to be connected events.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 8, 'Jesus answers the question generally at first, alerting His hearers not to expect the end whenever a claim is made or a catastrophic event occurs.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 8, 'in My name. History records many false messiahs, including Theudas mentioned in Acts 5:36.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 9, 'commotions. Revolts, riots. the end. Refers most directly here to the destruction of Jerusalem (vv. 6, 20; see also Dan. 9:26), but more broadly also to the final end of the world (Luke 21:27; Dan. 2:44; 12:13; Joel 3:9–18; Rev. 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 11, 'earthquakes … famines. Before the fall of Jerusalem (Acts 11:28; 16:26) as well as ongoing until the final end (Luke 21:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 12, 'before all these things. Signals the beginning of a new section. Here Jesus warns of what the disciples would face immediately—persecution, which would become the opportunity for Spirit-inspired testimony (e.g., Acts 4:1–10; 26:1–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 18, 'not a hair. Jesus has just warned that some would be put to death (v. 16); thus, this promise must look beyond to the ultimate certainty of eternal life with God (see 12:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 19, 'patience. Endurance (see 8:15). possess your souls. Save your lives. See note on 12:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 20, 'Jesus next turns His attention specifically to the destruction of the temple (v. 6), giving a sign by which His hearers could escape the city’s destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 22, 'days of vengeance. The ancient prophets prophesied disaster for those who refused to accept God’s Anointed One (13:34–35; 19:44; see also Deut. 18:18–19; 32:43; Dan. 9:26–27; Zech. 13:7–14:2) and to live according to His word (Is. 61:2; Jer. 5:28–29; Mic. 3:8–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 24, 'trampled by Gentiles. Compare Rev. 11:2 where “the Gentiles” will “tread the holy city underfoot for forty-two months” and where “the holy city” is to be understood as representing the faithful people of God. until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled. Until the whole earth has had the opportunity to receive salvation (2:32; 3:6; Is. 49:6; Matt. 24:14; Acts 15:16–17; 28:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 25, 'Jesus now briefly points forward to signs which must take place before His return in glory. For other passages indicating world conditions before the SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24:21–25, 37–39; 1 Thess. 5:3; 2 Tim. 3:1–5; 2 Pet. 3:3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 27, 'they will see. Christ’s glorious coming will be publicly witnessed (see also Rev. 1:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 28, 'lift up your heads. Jesus’ followers can view these frightening signs (vv. 7, 11, 25) with confidence and joy, knowing that their Savior is coming for them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 32, 'this generation. See Matt. 24:34.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 33, 'On the permanence of God’s promises, compare 1 Pet. 1:23. For more on the BIBLE being the Word of God, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17; 2 Pet. 1:20–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 34, 'Compare with God’s ideal regarding human HEALTH and wellbeing in 1 Cor. 10:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 21, 36, 'Watch … and pray. The trustworthy means that Jesus provides for escaping the events and distractions He has just described (see 22:40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 1, 'Passover. See note on 2:41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 3, 'Satan had been awaiting the opportunity to get back at Jesus (4:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 7, 'the Passover must be killed. The Passover lamb, sacrificed on the night of Israel’s deliverance from Egypt. Its blood, placed on the doorpost, caused the plague of death to pass safely over the household (Ex. 12:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 8, 'prepare the Passover. The Passover meal, which originally included a sacrificed lamb, unleavened bread, and bitter herbs (Ex. 12:8; Num. 9:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 16, 'until it is fulfilled. The symbolism of Passover (see notes on v. 7; 2:41) looks forward to the ultimate deliverance from death and evil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 19, 'For more on the LORD’S SUPPER, see Matt. 26:26–29; John 13:1–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 19, 'for you. Jesus’ death is not just a cruel happenstance, but is entered into on behalf of sinners (5:32; 1 John 4:9–10). do this in remembrance. As eating the Passover meal reminded Israel of their deliverance from Egypt, so the sharing of the bread and the cup are to remind Jesus’ followers of their own deliverance through His death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 20, 'new covenant. Like the covenant at Sinai, this covenant set apart a people for God (Ex. 19:5) and was made effective with blood (Luke 24:8), but this time with the blood of Christ. It promised that God’s law would be written on the hearts of His forgiven people. For more on the new COVENANT, see Jer. 31:31–34; 1 Cor. 11:25; Heb. 8:8–10; 9:15, 20–22; 10:11–18. Blood represented life. In the sacrificial system, blood poured out on the altar brought atonement, or reconciliation, with God (Lev. 17:10–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 22, 'but woe. Judas’s betrayal accomplished God’s plan; nevertheless he must face the responsibility for his own choices (see Acts 2:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 25, 'benefactors. Powerful men who gained honor and influence through public good works.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 27, 'as the One who serves. Jesus set the ultimate example in humble servanthood by giving His body and blood for humans (vv. 19–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 30, 'sit on thrones. A reassurance that those who faithfully serve Him will have a special place in His future kingdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 31, 'asked for you. Satan desired power over Jesus’ disciples, just as he once sought power over Job (Job 1:6–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 32, 'when you have returned. Jesus knew already of Peter’s future betrayal, yet instead of anger or rejection, His concern was to pray for him and give him assurance for the future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 36, 'sword. Jesus’ followers would face difficult times for which they must be prepared, and Jesus speaks strongly to warn them (see 14:26). Though He has previously counseled peace in place of violence (6:27), His disciples, who have been waiting impatiently to fight for the kingdom, take Him literally (v. 51).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 37, 'numbered with the transgressors. See Is. 53:12; see also Luke 23:32. Jesus applies to Himself the prophecy in Is. 53 of a Suffering Servant who “bore the sins of many.” an end. The prophecies will be fulfilled.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 38, 'enough. Jesus neither affirms nor argues against the disciples’ possession of two swords. See notes on vv. 36, 51.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 40, 'Pray. A call to disciples to do as He Himself has always done in challenging times. See note on 3:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 42, 'if it is Your will. Even Jesus, in His extreme suffering, submits in prayer to the will of the Father. Here God answers by strengthening Him (v. 43), though His specific petition must be denied.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 44, 'drops of blood. A known medical condition in which, under extreme stress, a person’s sweat becomes mixed with blood.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 46, 'temptation. Jesus twice (vv. 40, 46) represents prayer as the disciples’ primary protection against temptation. Yet they slept.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 47, 'kiss. A traditional greeting between close friends and family. See also Mark 14:45.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 51, 'Permit even this. Even an attack on their Lord did not justify the literal use of a sword. See notes on vv. 36, 38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 53, 'power of darkness. More than the cover of night, this phrase refers to the satanic powers that were working with the religious leaders (see vv. 3, 31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 61, 'the Lord … looked at Peter. Even now, Jesus reaches out to Peter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 62, 'wept bitterly. An evidence of repentance, upon remembering Jesus’ accurate warning and his own foolish self-confidence (vv. 31–34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 63, 'mocked. Viciously fulfilling Jesus’ own prophecy (18:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 66, 'day. To be lawful, the trial must take place during daylight hours.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 69, 'the Son of Man will. Applies to Himself the prophecy of Ps. 110:1 (see also Acts 2:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 71, 'heard it ourselves. The leaders choose to understand Jesus’ statement as blasphemy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 2, 'accuse Him. With lies and half-truths. perverting. Could be interpreted by Pilate as either misleading the nation, or inciting revolt. taxes. See 20:22–25. Christ, a King. Although others recognized this, Jesus never makes this claim in His teachings before the people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 3, '“It is as you say.” Literally, “You say,” a way of suggesting the truth of a statement without saying it directly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 5, 'He stirs up the people. A more intense charge hinting at unrest and even revolt, a possibility Pilate could not ignore. Galilee. Home to many revolutionaries.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 7, 'Herod’s jurisdiction. Herod Antipas was ruler of Galilee. Pilate hoped Herod would take this “problem” off his hands.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 9, 'answered him nothing. Jesus refused to gratify the curiosity of the one who ordered the death of John the Baptist (9:9; see also 11:29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 14, 'no fault. Two official witnesses (Deut. 19:15), Pilate and Herod, affirm Jesus’ innocence (see Luke 23:4, 22; Ps. 2:1–2; Acts 4:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 18, 'they all. For the first time, the people join their leaders in opposing Jesus (v. 13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 19, 'rebellion. Ironically, those who have accused the Innocent One of sedition ask for the release of a revolutionary in place of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 20, 'wishing to release. Pilate was caught between the truth he recognized and his desire to protect his own position.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 21, 'Crucify. One of the most horrible forms of execution, used for slaves and the worst of criminals. See also Matt. 27:35; Mark 15:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 26, 'bear it after Jesus. Doing literally what Jesus had called each of His disciples to do. See note on 9:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 29, 'barren. Normally considered a curse in ancient times (1:7, 25), childlessness would spare one from terrible grief at the fall of Jerusalem (see 19:41–44; 21:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 31, 'green wood … dry. A riddle which may refer to the judgment of destruction soon to come upon Jerusalem (Is. 10:16–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 32, 'criminals. As prophesied, Jesus is numbered with transgressors in His death (Is. 53:12; Luke 22:37; see also 23:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 33, 'Calvary. The Greek word means “skull.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 34, 'forgive. Jesus practices His own counsel to love one’s enemies (6:29, 35; see also Is. 53:12). Thus salvation remains available even to those who kill Him (Acts 2:36–38). divided His garments. Ps. 22:18 comes true in the experience of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 35, 'save Himself. Three times (vv. 35, 37, 39) Jesus is tempted to use His power for His own advantage at His crucifixion, just as He had been tempted by the devil at the beginning of His ministry (4:3–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 22, 36, 'sour wine. The wine drunk by soldiers. Thus Ps. 69:21 comes true in the experience of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 38, 'KING OF THE JEWS. An inscription identifying the “crime” was standard at executions. This one, though written in mockery, spoke the truest of truths.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 42, 'Lord … Your kingdom. A common criminal recognizes the truth the religious leaders denied. These are the only words of faith Jesus heard at His crucifixion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 43, 'today. In English translations, the placement of a comma can make a great difference in meaning. The Greek in which this passage originally was written did not use modern forms of punctuation. By His own explicit testimony, Jesus did not ascend to His Father until after His resurrection (John 20:17). Thus, this text must be understood as, “I say to you today, you will be with Me in Paradise” (see Deut. 30:18; Acts 20:26). Jesus was making the remarkable claim that even on that day, at the very darkest moment of His own existence, He could still confidently assert that belief in Him was the means by which the dying thief could receive eternal life. The unmistakable but often overlooked witness of Scripture is that the resurrection and reward of the faithful, including this dying thief, takes place in the future, at Christ’s Second Coming (Acts 24:15; 1 Cor. 15:22–23; 1 Thess. 4:16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 44, 'darkness. Supernatural happenings were understood to mark events of cosmic significance (Amos 8:9; Joel 2:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 45, 'veil of the temple. Opening the way into God’s presence in the Most Holy Place (see 1:9–10), formerly open only to priests with the sacrifice of animals. It symbolizes Jesus’ opening the way for all to have direct access to God through Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 46, 'My spirit. Breath, which represents life, was understood to return to God when one died (Gen. 2:7; Ezek. 37:5, 9; see also Ps. 31:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 47, 'a righteous Man. A Gentile, an impartial observer, affirms Jesus’ innocence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 48, 'beat their breasts. After viewing the events of Jesus’ crucifixion, the people now evidence mourning (see vv. 13–14, 23, 27; Zech. 12:10; Acts 2:36, 41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 50, 'This final Sabbath story in Luke should be read with the earlier ones. See note on 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 50, 'a council member. Not all the leaders rejected Jesus. Many would later come to believe in Him (Acts 6:7; 15:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 54, 'the Preparation. Friday, the day of preparation for the Sabbath.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 55, 'the women. Women were faithful witnesses of His ministry (8:2–3) and of the reality of His death and burial (23:49).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 23, 56, 'spices and fragrant oils. To prepare the body for burial. according to the commandment. Even on the eve of Jesus’ resurrection, Luke continues to emphasize the SABBATH rest as a command of God. This should come as no surprise since his later');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 2, 'the stone. A heavy entrance cover (1–3 tons), meant to keep out unwanted intruders.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 4, 'men … in shining garments. V. 23 identifies them as angels.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 5, 'afraid. The appearance of angels often causes humans to fear (e.g., Dan. 8:17–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 6, 'spoke to you. These women were among the disciples to whom Jesus spoke (v. 8; 8:2–3; 9:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 10, 'women. Had followed Him since Galilee (v. 6; 8:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 11, 'idle tales. The words of women were given little attention by men of the day, yet God gave women the honor of being the first messengers of Jesus’ resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 13, 'seven miles. Or 11 km.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 16, 'their eyes were restrained. They were to listen carefully to Jesus’ teachings from Scripture before being distracted by the joy of His resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 18, 'the only stranger. The only visitor who does not know of these events. This attests to the widespread knowledge of Jesus’ death, in the face of later critics who claimed He had not really died.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 21, 'redeem Israel. Jesus’ disciples still hoped He would deliver Israel from the Romans and set up an earthly kingdom (Acts 1:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 27, 'things concerning Himself. See, e.g., Deut. 18:15–19; Ps. 2:1–2, 7; 16:8–11; 110:1; Is. 53:7–8, 12. Scripture, which accurately provides the basis for the disciples’ understanding of God’s will and plan, receives its clearest interpretation through Jesus Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 28, 'would have gone farther. Jesus forces Himself on no one. Their offer of hospitality, however, brought them great blessing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 31, 'they knew Him. Sharing meals with Jesus, where He “took bread, blessed and broke it” (v. 30), was a precious part of their memories of time with Him (see 9:16; 22:19; compare to Acts 2:42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 33, 'that very hour. Encounter with the risen Lord brings joy that must be shared.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 36, '“Peace to you.” A standard greeting, given meaning and power on the lips of the great Peace-giver in the face of the disciples’ terror (1:79; Is. 9:6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 37, 'terrified. Emotionally exhausted at the death of their Lord and from fear for their own safety, the disciples don’t know what to think.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 39, 'Jesus demonstrates, through sound, sight, and touch, that He has truly and physically risen from the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 44, 'Moses … the Prophets … the Psalms. The three main sections of the Hebrew Scriptures, which tell us about Christ. For more on the role of the BIBLE in the Christian life, see John 20:31; Rom. 15:4; 2 Tim. 3:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 45, 'opened their understanding. See note on v. 27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 47, 'repentance and remission of sins. Repentance and forgiveness is the center of the Scripturally-mandated message Jesus’ followers were to carry to the world. Acts, Luke’s sequel, shows them busy with this very task (e.g., Acts 2:38; 5:31; 26:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 49, 'the Promise of My Father. The Holy Spirit (3:16; Acts 1:4–5) who is also called the “power of the Highest” (Luke 1:35; compare 4:14; Acts 1:8; 10:38).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Lucas', 24, 51, 'carried up into heaven. More detail is given in Acts 1:9–11.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 0, NULL, 'The authorship of the Gospel is left unstated, possibly because of the implication that the Holy Spirit is the true Author (16:13; the title “According to John” was added later). There is, however, repeated mention of a beloved disciple who is also identified as the one who authored the Gospel (13:23–24; 19:35; 21:7, 20, 24). Early church tradition identifies this beloved disciple as John the son of Zebedee and brother of James. The tradition is supported by the fact that this unnamed disciple was a frequent companion of Peter in the Gospel (13:23–24; 20:1–9; 21:15–23), while John was Peter’s companion in the early chapters of Acts (Acts 3:1, 11; 4:13; see also Gal. 2:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 0, NULL, 'The purpose of the Fourth Gospel is to enable its readers to “believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing you may have life in His name” (20:30–31). This purpose statement was written at the transition point between the generation who knew Jesus personally and the second generation, who knew Him only through the testimony of others. John’s audience is this second generation of believers.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 0, NULL, 'The Fourth Gospel is widely considered to be the last book of the New Testament that was written (A.D. 95–100). The occasion of writing is the impending death of the beloved disciple (21:20–24). The Gospel was written in Ephesus, probably after John’s return from Patmos in A.D. 96.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 0, NULL, 'Comparison of the Gospel of John with the Synoptic Gospels (Matthew, Mark, and Luke) reveals a unique picture of Jesus with the following features:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 0, NULL, 'I.      Prologue: the mission of Jesus (1:1–18)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 1, 'The prologue to the Gospel of John. Summarizes the main themes of the Gospel. Views the Jesus who walked this earth from the perspective of eternity (the Word).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 1, 'In the beginning. Recalls Gen. 1:1. was. Expresses ongoing action in the past. Jesus existed from eternity. the Word. Used for a divine mediator figure in Greek philosophy, this would have appealed to Greek readers. John is the only Bible writer to explicitly use this title for Christ (see, e.g., 1 John 1:1; Rev. 19:13). the Word was with God. Distinct from the Father, but in close relationship (see John 1:18). the Word was God. As God, Jesus was fully equal with the Father. The divinity of Jesus, seen also in the other Gospels (Matt. 1:23; 28:18, 20; Mark 4:39–41; 12:36–37; Luke 1:35), is especially emphasized in John (John 1:3, 18; 5:21–23; 8:58; 14:9; 20:28). For more on the divinity of the PERSON OF CHRIST, see Is. 9:6; Heb. 1:8. See also note on Matt. 1:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 3, 'All things … through Him. Jesus created everything. He Himself, therefore, was not created. For more on Christ and CREATION, see Eph. 3:9; Col. 1:16; Heb. 1:3; also see note on Gen. 1:3. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21. was made. Translates a Greek word used often in the Septuagint translation of Gen. 1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 4, 'life. As the Creator, Jesus is the Author of both physical and spiritual life. See Introduction, “Message.” light. A metaphor for revelation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 6, 'John. John the Baptist, not the author of the Gospel (see Matt. 3:13–17; 14:2–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 7, 'witness. Legal language. The Baptist was one of the earliest witnesses to who Jesus is. believe. See note on v. 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 9, 'coming into the world. Unclear whether this refers to “man” or back to “Light” (see translation note). world. See note on 3:16. Here it means “the planet” or “humanity.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 10, 'the world. See note on 3:16. did not know Him. The Gentiles did not know their Creator.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 11, 'His own … His own. The Greek uses different phrasing to convey slightly different meanings (see translation notes). The first is neuter plural and the second is masculine plural. His own … Him. A preview of rejection by His chosen people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 12, 'received. A point in the past. Contrast with v. 11. those who believe. The verb here means “those who are continuing to believe.” It indicates constant action over time, and not a one-time event at a particular moment. Thus, it does not in any sense support a “once saved, always saved” understanding of salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 14, 'became. Same word as “was made” in v. 3. flesh. In the incarnation, Christ, who was God from eternity, took human nature upon Himself. On the humanity of the PERSON OF CHRIST, see also Matt. 1:23; Heb. 4:14–16. dwelt. Literally, “tented.” Recalls the OT sanctuary, through which God provided a way to dwell with His people (Ex. 25:8). glory. Recalls the Shekinah presence of God in the OT sanctuary. Jesus is greater than the sanctuary. only begotten. Understood as “unique” or “one and only.” See John 1:18; 3:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 15, 'He was before me. John the Baptist affirms Jesus’ pre-existence. See vv. 1–2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 17, 'grace and truth. See John 1:14. Abstract ideas in OT, they are embodied in the person of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 18, 'No one … time. Moses came closest (Ex. 33:18–34:8). only begotten. See note on John 1:14. in the bosom. In contrast to Moses, Jesus has a face-to-face relationship with God. The same phrase describes the relationship between the beloved disciple and Jesus (13:23). declared. Translates the Greek word for “exegesis.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 19, 'John the Baptist testifies that he is not the Messiah, but one who prepares the way for the Messiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 19, 'the Jews. Used often in this Gospel for the religious leaders who opposed Jesus. Levites. Descendants of Levi, the third son of Jacob; the priestly tribe (Num. 1:47–53).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 20, 'the Christ. The Greek equivalent of the Hebrew “the Messiah.” Both words mean “the Anointed One.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 21, 'Elijah. See Mal. 4:5. the Prophet. See Deut. 18:15–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 24, 'Pharisees. A strict Jewish sect that often opposed Jesus. See also Mark 2:16; 12:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 26, 'baptize. Completely immerse in water.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 27, 'preferred before me. See v. 15. whose sandal … loose. A slave’s task. The Baptist was a very humble man (3:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 29, 'The Lamb of God. The lamb in Scripture appears in several roles: the sanctuary lamb of Leviticus, the conquering lamb of Rev. 5, the submissive lamb of Is. 53, or the Passover lamb of Ex. 12 (see John 19:31–37). See also 2 Cor. 5:21; Gal. 3:10–13; Heb. 9:22–23, 26; 10:12; Rev. 5:6, 9, 12. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 30, 'See v. 15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 31, 'but that … Israel. The Baptist’s mission from God was to identify the Messiah. It is through baptism that one’s identity as a Christian is established.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 32, 'witness. See note on v. 7. the Spirit … dove. The Spirit identified Jesus to John the Baptist.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 35, 'two … disciples. See v. 40.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 36, 'the Lamb of God. See note on v. 29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 37, 'they followed Jesus. John the Baptist got the Jesus movement started.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 40, 'One of the two. The other unnamed disciple is probably John, later called the disciple whom Jesus loved (see note on 19:35).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 1, 41, 'Messiah … Christ. See note on v. 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 1, 'The wedding took place about 50 days after the baptism of Jesus mentioned in chap. 1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 1, 'third day. Jesus’ first miracle involved grapes which were created on the third day of creation (Gen. 1:13). For more on the meaning of “third day” in Scripture, see Esth. 5:1; Luke 24:7, 21, 46. Cana. See note on John 1:46.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 3, 'wine. Could mean either fresh or fermented. If the wedding was in late autumn (likely), the “wine” would be fresh grape juice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 4, 'Woman. A respectful title for one’s mother in the ancient world. See 19:25–27. what … Me. Jesus rejects His mother’s encouragement to prematurely promote Himself. hour. An expression in John for the suffering and death of Jesus. Jesus avoided actions that would unnecessarily hasten the time of His final suffering. See note on 12:27–28. See also 7:30; 8:20; 12:23–24; 17:1. Jesus’ experience at Cana was a foretaste of the cross (note references to hour, glory, woman, third day, wine [blood]; see note on 2:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 6, 'waterpots of stone. Probably water cisterns, too large to carry. The washing rituals of religion are inferior to the “wine” (blood) of Jesus. twenty or thirty gallons. About 80 or 120 l.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 8, 'master of the feast. Like a modern-day organizer of a wedding reception.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 10, 'You … now. Though Jesus is relatively late on the scene of earth’s history, He is the best that God has for us.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 11, 'beginning. Could be translated “first.” See 4:54. signs. In the Gospel of John the stories always have deeper meanings; they are real-life parables. The signs give miraculous evidence for Jesus’ authority. See note on 11:45. glory. The glory of Jesus is His divine character. In John, this is most clearly manifested at the cross. See 12:23–24, 37–41. See notes on 2:4; 12:27–28. believed. His disciples’ first glimpse of the true greatness of Jesus. See note on 1:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 12, 'Capernaum. A sizeable town on the NW shore of the Sea of Galilee. Peter’s house there became Jesus’ base of operations in Galilee (see Mark 1:29; 2:1; 9:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 13, 'Passover. First of the three annual pilgrimage festivals (Deut. 16:16). It was held on the 15th day of the first Jewish month, in early spring, and celebrated Israel’s deliverance from Egypt. See Ex. 12:1–51. In John, it is always associated with references to Jesus’ death on the cross and to the Lord’s Supper (John 2:19–22; 6:32–59; 12:23–24; 13:1ff.; see note on 2:4). Jerusalem. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 14, 'temple. Trading in the courtyard around the temple began not long before this. Jesus may have cleansed the temple twice. See Matt. 21:12–17; Mark 11:15–19; and Luke 19:45–48.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 18, 'the Jews. See note on 1:19. sign. Miraculous evidence for authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 19, 'Destroy … up. Reference to Jesus’ death and resurrection (see v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 20, 'forty-six years. Temple construction began in the 18th year of Herod the Great (see Matt. 2:1–20). So this event occurred in the spring of A.D. 28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 23, 'Passover. See note on v. 13. signs. See note on v. 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 2, 25, 'He knew … man. Jesus knows the inner thoughts and feelings of everyone. He understands us to the core.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 1, 'Closely connected to 2:13–25. Nicodemus is a living illustration of the inadequate faith described in 2:23–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 1, 'ruler. Member of the Sanhedrin, the Jewish leadership council. the Jews. See note on 1:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 2, 'we. Refers back to the “many” of 2:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 3, 'again. The Greek word can mean “from above.” kingdom of God. A common phrase in Mark and Luke, rare in John. The kingdom was promised in the OT (Dan. 2:44; 7:14, 18, 27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 5, 'water. The water of baptism (v. 22). See Ezek. 36:25–27. On water BAPTISM, see notes on Matt. 3:16; Acts 8:36; Rom. 6:3–4. For its spiritual aspects, see Acts 10:47–48; 16:30–33; 22:16; 1 Cor. 12:12–13; Gal. 3:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 7, 'You. Plural. Parallels the “we” of v. 2. born again. Can mean “from above.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 8, 'wind … Spirit. Same word in Greek.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 9, 'How … be? Answered in vv. 13–15. The new birth flows out from the Son (v. 13), the cross (v. 14), and faith (v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 11, 'you. Plural. See notes on vv. 2, 7. testify … witness. Verb and noun form of same Greek word; legal language.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 14, 'Moses … wilderness. See Num. 21:5–9. lifted up. See note on John 8:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 16, 'world. Depending on the context, can mean the universe, the planet, humanity, or humanity in opposition to God. Here it identifies humanity as the object of God’s love. gave. The Bible pictures the Father as a sort of “director of activities” in the Godhead or Trinity. For more on GOD THE FATHER, see 1 Cor. 15:27–28; 1 Pet. 1:2; 1 John 4:8–10. only begotten. See note on John 1:14. believes. See note on 1:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 17, 'The primary purpose of Jesus’ ministry was salvation. But those who reject that salvation judge or condemn themselves. So every call to faith brings judgment along with it. One cannot be casual about the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 17, 'condemn. Literally, “judge.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 18, 'believes. See note on 1:12. The decisive element in the judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 19, 'light … world. See note on 1:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 20, 'Explains the motivations for rejecting the gospel. To receive the gospel includes admission of wrong, which the sinful nature resists.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 21, 'Accepting the truth about Jesus brings honesty and openness into our lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 22, 'John’s disciples are upset because the crowds are shifting from John the Baptist to Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 22, 'baptized. But see 4:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 24, 'thrown into prison. See Matt. 11:1–19; 14:1–12; Luke 7:18–34.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 25, 'the Jews. Used here in the general sense. purification. See 2:6. Here the concern is baptism.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 28, 'See 1:19–27 and notes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 29, 'bridegroom. The focal point of the “wedding” is Jesus. friend of the bridegroom. The one who arranges the “wedding” is John the Baptist. fulfilled. The Baptist is content with his role.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 30, 'Jesus must increase because of His heavenly origin (v. 31), teaching (vv. 32–34), and role (vv. 35–36). See also 1:1–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 31, 'John the Baptist contrasts himself with Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 32, 'what … heard. Jesus speaks from divine experience. See 1:1–18. no one … testimony. See 1:10–11; 6:44.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 33, 'certified. Literally, “sealed.” Indicates ownership or personal guarantee.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 34, 'God … measure. The Spirit resides in Christ in full measure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 35, 'given … hand. See 5:21–22, 26–29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 3, 36, 'believes. See v. 16; note on 1:12. everlasting life. On the Christian ASSURANCE of present salvation, see 5:24; 1 John 4:17; 5:13. See also Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 1, 'Jacob’s well was in the valley between two mountains, Gerizim and Ebal, close to ancient Shechem. The area was the location of significant OT events (Gen. 12:6; 33:18–34:31; Deut. 11:29; 27:4; Josh. 8:30–35; 24:32). The Samaritans were the descendants of foreigners who had adopted a form of Israelite religion (2 Kin. 17:24–41). The Jews of that time hated the Samaritans and felt they defiled everything they touched, so it was remarkable that Jesus even spoke with the woman in this story.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 1, 'Therefore. Jesus chose to avoid controversy by leaving the area where 3:22–30 took place. Pharisees. See note on 1:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 3, 'again. The first tour of Galilee is described in 1:43–2:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 4, 'Samaria. Mountainous area north of Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 5, 'Joseph. See Josh. 24:32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 6, 'sixth hour. Noon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 9, 'Jews … Samaritans. See note on vv. 1–42.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 10, 'living water. See 7:37–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 12, 'See note on vv. 1–42.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 13, 'water. Does not represent Jesus here. Perhaps it represents salvation (Is. 12:2–6) or the Holy Spirit (Is. 44:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 16, 'husband … five husbands. Jesus’ request precipitates a real-life parable of judgment. He exposes the woman’s sin to see how she will respond to Him. See note on 3:17–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 19, 'The woman changes the subject to avoid confrontation. this mountain. The Samaritans worshiped on Mount Gerizim, where they once had a rival temple to the one in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 22, 'the Jews. Used positively here (compare note on 1:19). The Messiah would be a Jew.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 23, 'the hour … now is. Compare v. 21. The coming of Jesus has introduced a decisive change in how people relate to God. spirit. An attitude of mind and heart rather than external rituals. truth. Matthew, Mark, and Luke make little use of the word “truth.” In John truth is the revelation of the will and character of God in the words (1:17; 3:34) and actions (8:32, 36) of Jesus and in the ongoing ministry of the Holy Spirit (16:13). All other paths to truth are deficient or counterfeit (14:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 25, 'When … things. The Samaritans believed that the Messiah would be a reformer who would teach them a better way of worship. I … He. Jesus reinforces that belief and accepts the role.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 27, 'talked with a woman. Jewish religious teachers rarely talked with women in public.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 29, 'see … did. See note on 2:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 32, 'I … know. See v. 34. Jesus relied on His Father for both physical and spiritual food. See Matt. 4:1–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 34, 'My … work. Jesus’ spiritual nourishment was to both know and do God’s will.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 35, 'lift … harvest. As the Samaritans approach (v. 30), Jesus points them out as an opportunity for the disciples to obtain “spiritual food” (see note on v. 34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 36, 'sows … reaps. Jesus sowed (vv. 7–26) and now the disciples will help Him reap (vv. 39–42). See v. 38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 38, 'others. Probably refers to John the Baptist and his disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 40, 'He … days. This was a remarkable thing in light of Jewish prejudices against the Samaritans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 42, 'the Christ. See note on 1:20. the Savior … world. The Samaritan story shows Jesus extending salvation to people from every race, gender, social status, or religious background (“the world”; see note on 3:16). Jesus offended many with His openness and His acceptance of outcasts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 43, 'Galilee. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 44, 'no honor … received. Although the reception in v. 45 seemed genuine, Jesus was not honored by it. See note on 2:25. having seen … the feast. See 2:13–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 46, 'Jesus … wine. See 2:1–11. nobleman. Literally, a royal officer. Capernaum. 16 miles (25 km) away. See note on 2:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 47, 'implored Him. No mention of faith at this point.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 48, 'Unless … believe. Contrast 20:29. The purpose of “signs” is to produce faith, but the best faith is not based on signs. The nobleman’s belief went only so far as thinking that Jesus had to be physically present to heal the man’s son, not that Jesus could simply speak the words. He was a real-life parable of 4:44.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 50, 'believed. Jesus’ command awakened genuine faith in the nobleman. See note on 1:49–50. He believed without seeing (20:29). He was a representative of the second generation, who would have to believe without seeing. See Introduction, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 52, 'seventh hour. One o’clock in the afternoon. The man could have made it home the same day.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 53, 'believed. The timely healing further deepened the nobleman’s faith in Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 4, 54, 'second … Galilee. See 1:43; 2:1–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 1, 'feast. Not named. Perhaps the Feast of Trumpets, which featured creation (vv. 17, 26, 28–29) and judgment (vv. 22, 27). Jerusalem. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 2, 'Sheep Gate. Close to the temple. Sheep for sacrifice were brought through it. pool … Bethesda. The name means “house of mercy.” A large, double pool with colonnades (porches) all around and between the two pools, it may have been a Gentile place of healing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 3, 'waiting … he had. Not found in earliest known manuscripts of John.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 5, 'thirty-eight years. For nearly a lifetime, this great center of healing had been no help.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 6, 'When Jesus saw. A spontaneous decision on Jesus’ part.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 7, 'I have no man. The paralyzed man was completely abandoned. He doesn’t yet realize that it is Jesus, and not another man or the pool, who is all he needs for healing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 9, 'walked. This act demonstrated that Jesus was the Messiah. See Is. 35:5–6; Matt. 11:2–6. the Sabbath. See note on Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 10, 'The Jews. See note on 1:19. not lawful. The man did not break the law of God here. He violated only the traditions of the Pharisees, which had grown up around the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 13, 'did not know. The man was healed without an expression of faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 14, 'Sin no more. Literally, “stop sinning.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 16, 'the Jews. See note on 1:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 17, 'working. As sustainers of creation, Jesus and His Father are constantly “working.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 18, 'broke the Sabbath. “The Jews” in John’s Gospel are unreliable witnesses. This charge is false (see note on v. 10). making … God. The “making” part of this charge is also false. The Prologue (1:1–18) demonstrates that Jesus was fully God from eternity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 19, 'Jesus responds to the charges in v. 18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 19, 'See v. 30. do nothing. Jesus affirms unity of action with His Father. They both agree on Jesus’ mission and on how to keep the Sabbath.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 20, 'greater works. Would include raising the dead (vv. 21, 26) and end-time judgment (vv. 22, 27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 22, 'committed … Son. God alone has the right to judge, so this demonstrates the divinity of Jesus (see v. 23). On the various stages of God’s JUDGMENT, see note on Rom. 14:10, 12. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 23, 'To question the divinity of Jesus does not honor God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 24, 'believes. See note on 1:12. has everlasting life. Present tense. The quality of spiritual life Jesus gives now is a foretaste of eternity. See also Eph. 2:8; 1 John 5:13. On the Christian ASSURANCE of present salvation, see John 3:36; 1 John 4:17; 5:13; and note on Rom. 8:31–39. shall … life. The final judgment ratifies the decisions made in this life. Believers do not need to live in fear of the judgment. Judgment means here “condemnation,” as the word is translated in v. 29. For more on JUDGMENT, see Dan. 7:10–14; Rom. 14:10–12. death. Used here in a spiritual sense. The spiritually dead suffer a relatively miserable existence in this life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 25, 'now is. See note on v. 24. life in Himself. Only God has life in Himself.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 27, 'See v. 22. because … Man. Jesus’ human experience makes Him a gracious and compassionate judge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 28, 'graves. The dead are asleep in the grave until Jesus calls them to life. For more on DEATH, see 11:11–14; Ps. 6:5; Eccl. 12:7; Dan. 12:2. come forth. The physical resurrection at the end of time. resurrection of life … resurrection of condemnation. According to Rev. 20:4–10, these two resurrections are a thousand years apart. Bible writers often grouped future events without a clear distinction of time. For more on the RESURRECTION in the OT, see Job 19:25–26; Is. 26:19. In the NT, see Acts 24:15; 1 Cor. 15:51–55; 1 Thess. 4:13–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 30, 'See v. 19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 31, 'In the Jewish legal system evidence was not accepted unless based on at least two or three witnesses (Deut. 19:15; Heb. 10:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 35, 'He. John the Baptist. See 1:19–36; 3:22–30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 36, 'works. Their unusual nature testified to Jesus’ divine origin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 37, 'While few have heard God’s voice audibly, we all have an inner witness to the truth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 39, 'the Scriptures. The religious leaders of Jesus’ day searched the OT, yet did not recognize the Messiah when He came. It is possible to read the Bible without benefit, if we read with the wrong motives. Note that Jesus believes that the OT is not obsolete, but bears witness to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 42, 'love of God. Love “for” God, not love “from” God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 45, 'Moses. For the Jews of Jesus’ day, Moses was the ultimate witness. trust. Literally, “hope.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 5, 46, 'he wrote about Me. Moses wrote about the Messiah in the promises to Adam (Gen. 3:15) and the patriarchs (Gen. 12:3; 22:18; 49:10), in the story of the exodus (Ex. 12–15; 1 Cor. 5:7; 10:1–4), in the sanctuary service (Leviticus; Heb. 10:1–14), and in his prediction of a prophet like himself (Deut. 18:15–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 1, 'Galilee. See Introduction, “Message.” Sea of Tiberias. The Roman name for the Sea of Galilee. An indication that John was written with non-Jews in mind.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 2, 'signs. See 2:23–25 and note on 2:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 3, 'See Matt. 5:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 4, 'Passover. See note on 2:13. Depending on the identity of the unnamed feast of 5:1, it was at least six months after the events of chap. 5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 5, 'The only miracle found in all four Gospels. A demonstration of Jesus’ creative power and deity. See 1:1–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 7, 'Two hundred denarii. About 200 days’ wages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 9, 'barley loaves. The inexpensive food of the common people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 10, 'the men. Literally, “males” or “husbands,” so the total crowd may have been much larger than 5,000.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 11, 'And Jesus … disciples. Similar language to the Lord’s Supper. See Matt. 26:26; Mark 14:22; Luke 22:19. See note on John 21:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 12, 'fragments. See Matt. 14:20; Mark 6:43; Luke 9:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 14, 'the Prophet. See Deut. 18:15–18. See notes on John 1:20–21. The multiplication of bread reminded them of Moses and the manna (see 6:31; Ex. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 15, 'make Him king. They hoped He would deliver them from the Romans, like Moses delivered the Israelites from Egypt. See note on 12:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 17, 'Capernaum. See note on 2:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 19, 'Only God could walk on water, calm the storm, and miraculously shift the boat.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 19, 'about three or four miles. About 4.5 or 5.5 km.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 20, 'It is I. Literally, “I am.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 22, 'Still in the context of Passover (v. 4), so there are further symbolic references to the Lord’s Supper (see vv. 1–14, 25–59). See also note on 2:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 24, 'Capernaum. See note on 2:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 26, 'signs. See note on 2:11. Jesus’ miracles were pointing to spiritual realities, but the people were thinking at a material level. In John’s Gospel, bodily needs like bread and water become metaphors for spiritual need. will give you. We do not earn eternal life; it is a gift.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 31, 'manna. See Ex. 16. There was a tradition that when the Messiah would come, He would make manna fall again on a Passover.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 35, 'I am. The unique expression in Greek recalls Ex. 3:14–15. It is used in three ways in John: (1) simple self-identification at a human level (see note on 6:20); (2) with metaphors that point to the spiritual life Jesus offers (e.g., I am the bread of life, or the light, door, Good Shepherd, etc.); and (3) as a direct claim to deity (8:28, 58; 13:19). bread of life. A revelation of who Jesus is (6:35–50) and a reference to the Lord’s Supper in vv. 50–59. Jesus is the “bread” that leads to eternal life (see 17:3). For more on the LORD’S SUPPER, see Matt. 26:26–29; John 13:1–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 36, 'you have seen … believe. Contrast 20:29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 37, 'All … gives Me. Understood by some to mean that some are predestined to be saved and others to be lost. But there is a clear balance in this chapter between God’s initiative (vv. 37, 44, 65) and human response (vv. 40, 47, 63–64). See also note on 9:39–41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 38, 'See 5:19, 30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 39, 'I should lose nothing. Not “once saved always saved.” God’s goal is that all be saved, but no one is forced to respond positively. See 17:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 44, 'the Father … draws him. See note on v. 37. the last day. The resurrection at the Second Coming of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 46, 'seen the Father. See 1:18; 14:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 47, 'believes. See note on 1:12. everlasting life. See note on 5:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 48, 'bread of life. See note on v. 35. Also see Matt. 26:26–29; 1 Cor. 11:23–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 50, 'die. In the spiritual sense. See note on 5:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 51, 'bread … flesh. See note on v. 53.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 52, 'See note on vv. 26–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 53, 'flesh … blood. The cross is the basis for eternal life. Jesus uses Lord’s Supper language in vv. 50–59 to describe the benefits of His sacrifice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 54, 'Repeats vv. 39–40.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 56, 'abides. A major word in the farewell discourse of John 13–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 59, 'Capernaum. See note on 2:12. Evidently the entire scene of 6:22–71 took place in the synagogue of Capernaum.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 60, 'disciples. Jesus’ speech divided even His disciples. This … saying. Referring to vv. 50–59. Jesus wasn’t conforming to their Messianic expectations. Whenever Jesus is rightly understood, He goes against the grain of the common culture.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 61, 'the Son … before. Would they still be offended if they truly understood Jesus’ heavenly origin? The Prologue (1:1–18) is crucial to understanding what Jesus is saying in this chapter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 63, 'flesh profits nothing. Jesus’ concerns are clearly about spiritual things. Success in this world is relatively unimportant in contrast to eternity. The words … life. Jesus’ word is what really matters, not miracles. spirit. See notes on 4:23–24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 64, 'See 2:23–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 65, 'granted … Father. See vv. 37, 44 and notes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 6, 66, 'The larger group of followers beyond the twelve was significantly diminished at this time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 1, 'Galilee. Jesus remains for a time in the same location as in chap. 6. Judea. The province centering on Jerusalem. the Jews. See note on 1:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 2, 'Feast of Tabernacles. Last of the three annual pilgrimage festivals. It took place in the fall, the time of fruit harvest and grain planting, because it is the end of the dry season and the occasional winter rains could be expected (Palestine has a semi-arid Mediterranean climate). It celebrated the Israelites’ wandering in the wilderness after the exodus from Egypt (Lev. 23:33–43). During the feast people lived in makeshift shelters made from branches and leaves. The main themes of the feast were the water (Ex. 17:1–7; Num. 20:1–11) and light (Ex. 13:21–22; Neh. 9:12, 19) God provided for the Israelites in the wilderness. See note on John 9:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 3, 'brothers. Actually half-brothers. See Mark 3:20–35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 5, 'not believe. The brothers’ advice in vv. 3–4 was worldly and self-serving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 6, 'time. Literally, “hour.” See note on 2:4. It is God who determines the timing of Jesus’ actions. His brothers were tempting Him to deviate from God’s plan for His life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 7, 'The world … you. Because His brothers were on “the world’s” side. world. Here means humanity in opposition to God. See note on 3:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 8, 'time. See notes on v. 6; 2:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 9, 'Galilee. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 10, 'the feast. Feast of Tabernacles. See note on v. 2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 13, 'the Jews. See note on 1:19. Here you have Jews (7:11) fearing “the Jews.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 14, 'the middle … feast. The fourth day of the seven-day feast (see note on v. 2). Jesus secluded Himself for the first half and then began to teach openly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 15, 'having never studied. Jesus apparently attended no formal school.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 16, 'not Mine, but His. Jesus didn’t get His teaching from rabbinical schools, nor did He make it up; He got it from His Father. See 1:1–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 17, 'wills to do His will. God will reveal truth through His word to the degree that we are willing to obey what we learn.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 18, 'The true teacher is the one who delivers God’s message, not his or her own.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 19, 'none … law. They honored the Law of Moses with their words but not their deeds.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 20, 'You have a demon. See 8:48–52.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 22, 'not … fathers. Circumcision actually began with Abraham (Gen. 17:10). Moses regulated the practice. See Lev. 12:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 23, 'I made … Sabbath. Referring back to the charge of Sabbath-breaking (5:18), Jesus here denies He broke the Sabbath. In healing the paralytic (5:1–9), He was obeying the higher principles of the Sabbath. Circumcision was surgery on a body part; Jesus was concerned for the whole person.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 25, 'they seek to kill. Contrast vv. 19–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 26, 'the Christ. See note on 1:20. when … from. These residents of Jerusalem seem to have believed that the Messiah would appear out of nowhere. Since they knew where Jesus was from (Nazareth), they reasoned He could not be the Messiah. But see 7:41–42; 9:29; Mic. 5:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 29, 'He sent Me. The crucial piece about Jesus’ identity is that He comes from the Father and represents—and is Himself—the essence of what God is (14:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 30, 'take Him. Arrest Him. hour. See note on 2:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 31, 'many … Him. In contrast to their leaders. the Christ. See note on 1:20. signs. See notes on 2:11; 6:26–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 32, 'The Pharisees. See note on 1:24. take Him. Arrest Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 33, 'A reference to His ascension. See 16:16–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 35, 'the Dispersion. Over the centuries, a large percentage of the Jews had scattered around the Mediterranean and beyond.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 37, 'the feast. See note on v. 2. Jesus may have interrupted the high point of the water ceremony at the Feast of Tabernacles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 38, 'the Scripture … water. Not an exact quote from the OT. Jesus is referring to an overall emphasis in texts like Prov. 18:4; Is. 58:11; Ezek. 47:1–12; and Zech. 14:8, 16–19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 39, 'the Spirit. The Holy Spirit satisfies our spiritual thirst (longings and needs). the Holy Spirit … glorified. In John the glorification of Jesus is the cross (see 12:23–24, 37–41), so this is a reference to Pentecost. See notes on Acts 2:1–21. Jesus prepared His disciples for Pentecost in John 14:16–20, 25–27; 15:26–27; and 16:7–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 40, 'the Prophet. See Deut. 18:15–18. the Christ. See note on John 1:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 42, 'Bethlehem. See Mic. 5:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 44, 'take Him. Arrest Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 47, 'the Pharisees. See note on 1:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 49, 'accursed. The Pharisees believed that ignorance of Scripture is the greatest sin. But they betrayed their own ignorance of Scripture by rejecting the Messiah. See 5:39–40.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 50, 'Nicodemus. See 3:1–15; 19:38–42.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 52, 'no prophet … Galilee. Based on the assumption that Jesus was born in Nazareth (see vv. 41–42). Actually Jonah and possibly other prophets were from Galilee, so this comment further betrays the leaders’ ignorance of Scripture. See note on v. 49.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 7, 53, 'This story is not found in the earliest known Greek manuscripts. Various later manuscripts of Luke and John include it in different ways.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 3, 'Pharisees. See note on 1:24. adultery. Sexual relations outside of marriage, a violation of the seventh commandment. See Ex. 20:14. Jesus’ opponents were also violating the law, since it required that in the case of adultery both partners should be executed (Lev. 20:10; Deut. 22:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 5, 'Moses … stoned. See Deut. 22:22–24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 6, 'testing Him. If Jesus told them to let her go, it would violate the Law of Moses. If He said to stone her, He would be in trouble with the Romans, who did not allow Jews to stage executions. See 18:31. wrote on the ground. Jesus was possibly writing the secret sins of His accusers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 9, 'convicted. A good illustration of Jesus’ inner knowledge of the thoughts and actions of others. See note on 2:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 11, 'Neither … you. Jesus knew all about her and her accusers (see note on v. 9), yet forgave her sin. Jesus upheld the legal penalty for adultery, while highlighting the importance of compassion and forgiveness. sin no more. Forgiveness does not condone sin, but leads to positive life change.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 12, 'I am … world. See 1:4–5; Is. 60:1–3. As the light of the world, Jesus exposes sin (John 8:1–11) and gives physical and spiritual sight (see 9:1–7, 39–41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 13, 'Pharisees. See note on 1:24. witness. The main theme of vv. 12–20. See 5:31–37 and note on 1:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 15, 'according to the flesh. Could mean either judging by appearances or by human standards. I judge no one. See note on 3:17–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 17, 'the testimony … men. See note on 5:31–33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 20, 'hour. See note on 2:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 23, 'The Light of the World (v. 12 and note) exposes the sin of His accusers. See note on 3:17–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 23, 'I am from above. See 1:1–18. The key to Jesus’ identity is His heavenly origin. See also 8:25–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 24, 'I am. See note on v. 28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 25, 'See notes on vv. 23–24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 28, 'lift up. The words have a multiple meaning in John. They describe the literal act of Jesus’ crucifixion and of His ascension, but they also carry the deeper sense that Jesus’ character is glorified (“lifted up”) by His self-sacrifice on the cross (3:14; 12:32–34; note on 2:11). I am. Example of His direct claim to deity. See note on 6:35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 30, 'believed. See notes on 1:12; 2:25. In this case the “believing” seems to have been shallow and self-serving, as the following verses show.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 32, 'the truth. See note on 4:23–24. make you free. The truth brings freedom from the addictive power of sin (8:34–36), enabling people to reach their full potential in Christ (10:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 33, 'A strong parallel to the denunciation of the Pharisees in Matt. 23. The whole section is held together by the theme of Abraham. Both Jesus and His opponents claim to be the true descendants of Abraham.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 33, 'never … bondage. A puzzling statement for a people in bondage to Egypt, Babylon, and now Rome.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 34, 'whoever … sin. Those enslaved by sin are no better off than Gentiles, even if they are physical descendants of Abraham.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 36, 'the Son … indeed. Those in Christ are no longer slaves (see note on v. 32); Jesus extends the freedom of “sonship” to all who believe in Him. We become members of God’s family.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 37, 'They were literal heirs of Abraham, yet they were rejecting the emissary of Abraham’s God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 39, 'Abraham is our father. In the literal, physical sense. the works of Abraham. The true children of Abraham behave the way Abraham behaved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 41, 'the deeds of your father. See v. 44. born of fornication. Probably a sneering reference to Jesus’ mysterious birth. See Matt. 1:18–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 44, 'the devil. The behavior of Jesus’ opponents (vv. 37, 40, 43) marks them as children of the evil one, who was a murderer and a liar from the beginning (see Gen. 3 and 4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 45, 'Contrast to v. 44. Jesus’ truthfulness and gentleness bears witness that He is the true Son of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 48, 'the Jews. See note on 1:19. Samaritan. Response to Jesus’ assertion in 8:39–41. See note on 4:1–42. demon. One of Satan’s angels (Luke 10:17–20). This is a response to Jesus’ assertion in John 8:44.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 49, 'I do not … demon. Jesus does not disavow the charge of being a Samaritan. He is the Savior of the world (4:42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 50, 'One. God the Father.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 51, 'My word. Concerning who Jesus is. shall never see death. Not to be taken as physical death, as the Jews do in v. 52. By “death” here Jesus means the “second death.” See note on Rev. 20:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 52, 'Jesus’ opponents clearly misunderstood His spiritual intent in v. 51.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 53, 'greater … Abraham. Jesus ironically admits the charge in vv. 56 and 58. He is greater than Abraham.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 56, 'Abraham rejoiced. Looking forward to the fulfillment of God’s promises to him. See Gen. 12:1–3; 22:18; Gal. 3:8–9, 13–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 8, 58, 'I AM. Here a direct claim to deity. See notes on v. 28; 6:35. See also Ex. 3:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 1, 'Jesus acts out the “light of the world” concept (8:12; 9:5) in real life. See note');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 1, 'Jesus acts out the “light of the world” concept (8:12; 9:5) in real life. See note on 8:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 1, 'Jesus acts out His claim to be the light of the world (v. 5) by healing a blind man.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 1, 'passed by. Continuing the scene of 8:12–59, Jesus is still in the vicinity of the temple during the Feast of Tabernacles. See note on 7:2. man … birth. Represented the spiritual need of his countrymen for Christ. See 8:31–47.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 2, 'who sinned. The popular theology of the time was that every illness was the direct result of a specific sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 3, 'Neither … sinned. Jesus rejects the popular theology expressed in v. 2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 4, 'night is coming. When the “light of the world” will be taken away by death and ascension (v. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 5, 'the light of the world. See note on 8:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 6, 'These actions violated some of the Sabbath-keeping rules of the Pharisees.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 7, 'pool of Siloam. From which the ceremonial waters of the Feast of Tabernacles were drawn and carried up to the temple. See note on 7:2. But these waters heal only at the command of Jesus. The healing happens 1,300 yards (1,200 m) away from Jesus, so His word is as good as His presence. Sent. See v. 4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 8, 'Similar to 5:9–13. The man born blind is also healed without an expression of faith, but he comes to faith later (9:35–38).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 13, 'Pharisees. See note on 1:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 14, 'Sabbath. Jesus’ violation of their Sabbath rules brought the man into conflict with the religious authorities. See vv. 6–7, 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 15, 'Throughout the story the man grows in faith (vv. 11, 17, 27, 33, 38) and the Pharisees grow in “blindness” (vv. 15–17, 24, 27, 29, 34, 39–41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 17, 'prophet. The man’s faith in Jesus grows.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 18, 'That a miracle had occurred was no longer in dispute. The question was what that miracle said about Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 22, 'put out … synagogue. To be thrown out of the faith. This was fearful, because no one was allowed to do business with an excommunicated person.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 24, 'We know … sinner. The false confidence of the spiritually blind. They were trying to put words into the healed man’s mouth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 27, 'Do you … disciples. The healed man waxes bolder and bolder in sarcastic defiance of the religious leaders.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 28, 'we … disciples. The religious leaders try to frame the discussion in terms of Jesus against Moses. Readers of John already know this is a false dichotomy (1:17; 5:45–47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 29, 'do not know where He is from. Contradicts 7:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 30, 'this is a marvelous thing. The unbelief of the religious leaders was absolutely incredible to the healed man.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 34, 'You … sins. By admitting that the man was born with the “consequences of sin” (blindness), the religious leaders admitted that they knew the healing was valid. Their opposition was motivated by something other than a passion for truth. cast him out. Excommunicated him from the synagogue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 35, 'found him. Sets the stage for Jesus’ Good Shepherd discourse (10:1–21). Jesus takes care of the outcasts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 36, 'Who … Him. Some people are more naturally trusting than others, but saving faith has an object, the person of Jesus Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 38, 'believe. See note on 1:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 39, 'There is a balance in this passage between God’s control of events (v. 41; 12:38–40) and human choice (9:39; 12:42–43). Faith is an attitude humans must adopt for themselves (3:18; 6:63–64; 7:17; 15:22), yet it is not possible without God’s drawing power (6:37, 44–45). Here is the balance: When we look to the past it is clear we owe all spiritual progress to the mighty working of God in our lives, yet when we look to the future it is clear we have decisions to make and actions to take. See also note on 6:37.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 9, 39, 'judgment. See note on 3:17–21. Jesus spiritualizes the healing of 9:1–7. People who see with literal eyes may yet be blind to the truth about Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 1, 'Elaborates the story of 9:1–41. The healed man is a sheep that recognizes the voice of the Shepherd and gladly follows Him. The Good Shepherd, in turn, cares for all the sheep, including those who are cast out by others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 1, 'Most … you. This opening phrase links chap. 10 with the previous chapter (see 6:26, 47; 8:34, 51; 10:7). Jesus is still in the vicinity of the temple during the Feast of Tabernacles. See note on 7:2. sheepfold. Sheepfolds in ancient Palestine were usually natural caves or fieldstone enclosures, with an opening at one end just big enough for the shepherd to block with his body while sleeping. some other way. The Pharisees wanted to be God’s sheep without accepting Jesus. thief … robber. Reference to the religious leaders of chaps. 7–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 3, 'doorkeeper. The shepherd’s assistant or under-shepherd. In this illustration the under-shepherd would represent the disciples of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 4, 'brings out. Same Greek word as “cast out” in 9:34–35. they know his voice. Spiritualizes 9:35–38. The man born blind had never seen Jesus, yet recognized Him by His voice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 6, 'illustration. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 8, 'thieves and robbers. See note on v. 1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 10, 'steal … kill … destroy. An indictment of the religious leaders who opposed Jesus in chaps. 7–9. more abundantly. The life Jesus brings is not just eternal—it is of higher quality and it begins in this life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 11, 'good shepherd. See note on v. 1. The shepherd and the door are often the same thing. gives His life. See also v. 15. As opposed to the “thief” who takes lives (v. 8). A reference to the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 12, 'hireling. Does not own the sheep and has no personal concern for them. Another illustration of the religious leaders of chaps. 7–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 14, 'known. See vv. 3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 16, 'other sheep. Gentiles. See 7:35; 12:20–21. one flock. Union of Jews and Gentiles in the church (Eph. 2:11–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 17, 'lay down … again. Death and resurrection of Jesus. See note on v. 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 18, 'No one … Myself. Jesus’ death on the cross was a voluntary act, a measure of His self-sacrifice and His love for the human race.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 19, 'division. See 9:15–16. the Jews. See note on 1:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 20, 'demon. See 8:37–52 and note on 8:48.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 21, 'open … blind. See 9:1–7, 24–34.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 22, 'Two months after the Feast of Tabernacles events that run from 7:1 to 10:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 22, 'Feast of Dedication. Called Hanukkah today. Celebrates the re-dedication of the temple in 165 B.C., after its defilement by Antiochus Epiphanes, king of Syria. While not an OT feast, the concept of dedication occurs frequently in the Bible (Ex. 40:34–35; Num. 7:1–11; 1 Kin. 8:1–11, 62–65; Ezra 6:13–18; Heb. 10:19–20). winter. The feast occurs in December.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 23, 'Solomon’s porch. The warmest, most sheltered part of the temple grounds at that time of year.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 24, 'the Jews. See note on 1:19. the Christ. See note on 1:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 25, 'I told you. See 5:31–37; 8:12–20, 37–52. The works. Jesus has already answered their question in the healing of the blind man (9:1–41; 10:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 26, 'My sheep. See vv. 3–4, 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 28, 'eternal life. See note on 5:24. never perish. See note on 11:26. snatch … hand. Those who believe in Jesus are secure, unless they choose to rebel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 30, 'Understood as a claim to equality with God (vv. 31–33, compare 1:1). See note on 5:18. For more on the divinity of the PERSON OF CHRIST, see 1:1; Is. 9:6; Matt. 1:23; Heb. 1:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 31, 'the Jews. See note on 1:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 33, 'make Yourself God. See note on v. 30. Jesus responds to the charge in vv. 34–38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 34, 'written … law. See Ps. 82:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 35, 'Jesus’ argument notes that Scripture itself uses the term “god” for a human being. Thus it would be even more appropriate to use that term when that human being has the pre-history that Jesus has (1:1–2, 18). In other words, Jesus’ claims do not exceed what Scripture allows.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 36, 'sanctified. Jesus’ works are as holy and “set apart” as those done in the temple. See note on v. 22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 39, 'again. See 7:30, 44–46; 8:20, 59.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 40, 'beyond the Jordan. See Matt. 19:1; Mark 10:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 10, 41, 'John … true. The ministry of the Baptist was still respected in that region, so Jesus got a more positive hearing there than in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 1, 'The setting for this story is Jesus’ withdrawal from Jerusalem (10:40–42) after a series of battles with the religious leadership there (see 7:1–10:42). The raising of Lazarus from the dead precipitates the events that lead to Jesus’ death on the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 1, 'Bethany. A small village on the eastern side of the Mount of Olives. See v. 18. Mary … Martha. See also Luke 10:38–42.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 2, 'anointed. See 12:1–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 4, 'glorified. While the raising of Lazarus brings glory to God in itself (12:12–18), the greatest glory comes from the fact that it precipitates the arrest, suffering, and death of Jesus. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 6, 'stayed … days. Jesus’ delay will bring glory to God (v. 4) and spiritual growth to His disciples (v. 15). God’s timing can be frustrating to us now, but it is ultimately the best.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 7, 'Judea. The province in which Jerusalem is located. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 8, 'the Jews … You. See 10:31–33. The disciples sense that a return to Jerusalem will be fatal (11:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 9, 'the light of this world. See note on 8:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 11, 'sleeps. See note on v. 26. wake him up. From the dead (vv. 13–14). For more on DEATH as a sleep, see Dan. 12:2; Acts 7:60; 1 Thess. 4:13–14. See also Ps. 6:5; 115:17; 146:4. For DEATH as the reversal of life, see Eccl. 12:7 and note on Gen. 2:7. On the fact that there is a second DEATH from which there is no resurrection, see Rom. 6:23; Rev. 20:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 15, 'that you may believe. A reason for the two-day delay of v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 16, 'Twin. Meaning of Thomas in Hebrew. Let us … Him. Contrast Thomas’s resolute faith here with his doubts in 20:24–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 17, 'four days. In contemporary Jewish belief, resuscitation was possible for three days after death. So Jesus’ delay (v. 6) sets the stage for an indisputable miracle.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 18, 'Bethany. See note on v. 1. two miles. About 3 km.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 21, 'Lord … died. See v. 32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 22, 'In contrast with Luke 10:38–42, Martha exhibits more faith than Mary (see also John 11:24, 27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 25, 'I am. See note on 6:35. resurrection. For those who believe and will die before Christ’s return. For more on the RESURRECTION, see 1 Cor. 15:51–55; 1 Thess. 4:13–18. life. For those who believe and have not yet died. Since life is imparted by the words of Jesus (4:50; 5:8, 24; 6:63), we today are at no disadvantage compared with those who walked with Him (see Introduction, “Audience”). die. Here defined physically and temporarily (see note on 11:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 26, 'lives and believes. It is spiritual life and death that are in view here. shall never die. For Jesus, the physical death that believers die is not ultimately death, or the death that really matters. Though believers will sleep like Lazarus (vv. 11–16), they will not die in the ultimate sense. For more on DEATH as sleep, see Dan. 12:2; Acts 7:60; 1 Thess. 4:13–14. For the ultimate or second DEATH, see Rom. 6:23; Rev. 20:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 30, 'the place … Him. See v. 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 32, 'Lord … died. See v. 21. No doubt the women expressed this thought to each other as they waited for Jesus to come.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 37, 'They misinterpreted Jesus’ tears as powerlessness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 38, 'cave. Commonly used for burial by well-to-do families.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 39, 'stench. Although Martha’s faith shone brightly in vv. 21, 24, 27, she failed to apply it to the situation at hand. four days. See note on v. 17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 40, 'the glory of God. See note on v. 4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 44, 'A real-life parable of 5:28–29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 45, 'many … believed. Every miracle Jesus performed in the Gospel of John produced two equal and opposite results. Some came to faith as they perceived the significance of the “sign” (see also note on 2:11). The same miracle produced increased opposition in others (5:16–18; 9:14–34; 11:46–53). the Jews. Used in a more general and neutral sense here (contrast note on 1:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 46, 'The raising of Lazarus was the final act that provoked Jesus’ opponents to carry out their plan to kill Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 46, 'the Pharisees. See note on 1:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 47, 'chief priests. See notes on 18:13, 15, 24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 48, 'The death of Jesus produced the very two things the religious leaders feared: widespread belief in Jesus and the destruction of their “place and nation” by the Romans in A.D. 70. See Luke 13:34–35; 20:9–19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 49, 'Caiaphas. Official high priest (A.D. 18–36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 51, 'prophesied. God used Caiaphas to explain the meaning of Jesus’ death, even though Caiaphas did not know what he was doing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 52, 'John extended Caiaphas’s “prophecy” about the meaning of Jesus’ death to the Gentiles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 54, 'Being aware of the council’s plans, Jesus suspended His public ministry for a time and withdrew to a remote area of Judea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 55, 'Passover. See note on 2:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 11, 57, 'the Pharisees. See note on 1:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 1, 'Transition between the public ministry of Jesus and the “hour” (v. 23) of His suffering and death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 1, 'six days … Passover. Saturday night of the last week before the crucifixion. Passover. See note on 2:13. Bethany. See note on 11:1. Lazarus. See 11:1–44.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 2, 'Martha. See 11:19–30, 39–40; Luke 10:38–42.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 3, 'a pound. The Roman pound was around 12 ounces (330 g). very costly. See note on v. 5. spikenard. Perfume from the root of the nard plant. anointed the feet. But see Matt. 26:7; Mark 14:3. Anointing the head was an act of honor. Anointing the feet was an act of humble devotion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 4, 'Judas Iscariot. See notes on 6:70–71. See also 13:18–30; 18:3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 5, 'denarii. A denarius represented a day’s wage for a laborer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 7, 'Jesus here reads the motives of both Judas and Mary. See note on 2:25. My burial. Anointing was part of the burial process. See 19:39–40.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 9, 'the Jews. See note on 1:19. Used in a more general and neutral sense here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 10, 'put Lazarus to death. The religious leaders were no longer operating rationally. The chief priests were Sadducees, who did not believe in the resurrection of the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 12, 'The next day. Sunday before the crucifixion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 13, 'took branches … Him. Technical language for a royal procession after victory in battle; this was a way of proclaiming that Jesus is the Messiah. See 6:14–15. Jesus has been anointed (12:7) and will receive a crown (19:2), but He is a different kind of king than they expect.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 15, 'The nature of Jesus’ kingship is clarified in Zech. 9:9–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 16, 'did not understand. Until after Jesus’ death. glorified. See note on 2:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 17, 'witness. See note on 1:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 18, 'sign. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 19, 'the world. See note on 3:16; here means humanity as a whole. See also 11:48.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 20, 'Greeks. Converts to Judaism; a fulfillment of v. 19; 10:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 23, 'hour. See note on 2:4. The arrival of the Greeks somehow convinced Jesus that the hour of His suffering and death had come. glorified. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 25, 'To follow Jesus is to disown self-centeredness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 26, 'if anyone … also. We follow Jesus in the way of the cross (vv. 23–25), which Jesus acted out in real life in 13:2–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 27, 'The hour and the glory of Jesus are always associated with the cross in the Gospel of John. See notes on v. 23; 2:4, 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 27, 'My soul is troubled. Similar to Jesus’ experience in Gethsemane. See Matt. 26:36–45; Mark 14:32–42.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 31, 'judgment of this world. At the cross, Satan was dethroned (Rev. 12:11–12) and the sin of the whole human race was judged in the person of Jesus (see Rom. 8:3; 1 Pet. 2:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 32, 'lifted up. See note on 8:28. It is the cross that draws true believers to Jesus, not miracles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 34, 'the Christ remains forever. Probably based on Ps. 110:4; Is. 9:7; Ezek. 37:25. They ignored Scriptures like Is. 53:5–9. lifted up. See note on 8:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 35, 'Jesus ignores their question and warns them that time is running out for them to grasp His mission. This marks the end of Jesus’ public ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 37, 'signs. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 39, 'could not believe. See note on 6:37.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 41, 'Isaiah … glory. On the throne in Is. 6 and on the cross in Is. 53. Note quotations from these chapters in John 12:38, 40.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 42, 'rulers. Members of the ruling council (the Sanhedrin), like Nicodemus. believed. May have been genuine, but see 2:23–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 44, 'Brief summary of key elements of Jesus’ message.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 45, 'See 14:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 46, 'See vv. 35–36; 1:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 47, 'Strongly reflects Deut. 18:18–19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 47, 'See 3:17. Judgment was not the purpose of Jesus’ first coming, but His very presence caused people to pass judgment on themselves. See 3:18–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 12, 48, 'in the last day. See 5:27–29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 1, 'Jesus moves here from public action to secluded instruction of the disciples. From chaps. 13 to 17 the shadow of the cross hangs over the room as the disciples realize Jesus is about to leave them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 1, 'For more on the LORD’S SUPPER, see Matt. 26:26–29; 1 Cor. 11:23–26. For heart preparation for the service, see 1 Cor. 11:27–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 1, 'A summary of Jesus’ mindset in chaps. 13–17. before … Passover. In this account, Passover began 24 hours later (18:28; 19:14). when Jesus knew … end. Jesus looks past His own immediate suffering to contemplate the disciples’ future suffering and prepare them for it. hour. See note on 2:4. depart. The ascension (20:17; Luke 24:50–51; Acts 1:9–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 2, 'Judas Iscariot. See note on 12:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 3, 'knowing. Jesus knows both who He is and the character of those He is ministering to (vv. 2, 6–9, 18–30). The footwashing demonstrates the regard God has for sinful, even degenerate humanity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 4, 'girded Himself. For the task. He would have looked like a slave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 5, 'wash … feet. People generally bathed before attending a feast and would only need their feet washed from the dust of the journey to the feast. A ceremony of hospitality was normally performed by a servant or slave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 8, 'never. Strongest possible negative language. Peter probably thought he was protecting Jesus from humiliation, but he was actually protecting himself from admitting his need of Jesus’ ministry to the lost (no part with Me).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 10, 'He who … feet. The full-body bath represents the full cleansing from sin at baptism. The footwashing represents the ongoing cleansing needed after baptism. The good news of the footwashing is that believers are not in and out of God’s grace; they are secure as long as they do not choose to turn away (10:27–29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 11, 'not all clean. Judas was not clean in the “full-body” sense (see note on v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 13, 'Teacher and Lord. Titles of respect given to a rabbi.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 14, 'wash one another’s feet. Represents a willingness to forgive the daily irritations and transgressions that threatened the unity in love Jesus purposed for us (vv. 34–35).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 15, 'do … done. Continue practicing the footwashing as a reminder of the kind of love Jesus has for us and that which we are to have for each other (vv. 34–35). Three times (vv. 14, 15, 17) Jesus commands and encourages the disciples to continue footwashing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 18, 'the Scripture. Ps. 41:9. eats bread with Me. The bread and wine of the Lord’s Supper do not explicitly appear in John 13, but this is a hint of the Lord’s Supper context of the footwashing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 19, 'Divine predictions are most clearly understood at the time of fulfillment, not before. I am. See note on 6:35. Here is a direct claim to deity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 21, 'troubled in spirit. See also 11:33; 12:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 23, 'leaning … bosom. See note on 1:18. People generally ate reclining on the left elbow with feet behind and head toward a low table. Jesus would have been right behind John (see 13:25). whom Jesus loved. See note on 1:40.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 26, 'Judas Iscariot. See note on 6:70–71.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 27, 'Satan entered him. To carry out the plan in his mind.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 28, 'The disciples never suspect Judas, having trusted him to care for their money (see also 12:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 30, 'night. Unlike Nicodemus, Judas moves from light into darkness (3:2, 19–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 31, 'Jesus’ “Farewell Discourse” to His disciples, seeking to prepare them for earthly life without His physical presence. Jesus repeats Himself continually, yet His disciples fail to understand. Rather than an organized speech, this often reads like a collection of “sound bites.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 31, 'So, when he had gone out. A turning point. The departure of Judas enables Jesus to speak freely to the others. glorified. See note on 2:11. God is glorified in Him. Jesus is the revelation of His Father’s character to the disciples (14:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 33, '‘Where … come.’ A constant theme of the Farewell Discourse (see note on 13:31–16:33) is Jesus’ going away, where they cannot follow. It is a reference to His death and/or ascension.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 34, 'new commandment … another. Jesus’ mission is described in terms of glory (vv. 31–32), and the disciples’ mission is described in terms of love. The love commandment is new in that it draws its power and its clarity from the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 35, 'Self-sacrificing love is the strongest argument that God is present in a Christian community.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 36, '“Where … afterward.” Death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 13, 38, 'rooster … three times. See 18:15–18, 25–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 1, 'Presents 13:33 in more positive terms.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 1, 'For Jesus’ major teaching on the SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24–25; Mark 13:1–37; Luke 17:22–37; 21:5–36. See also Rev. 19. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 1, 'troubled. By Judas’s betrayal (13:21), Jesus’ departure (13:33), and/or Peter’s denial (13:38).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 2, 'mansions. Literally, “residences.” See “home” in v. 23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 3, 'a place. In heaven, where Jesus lived before He came to earth. I will come again. There is a double meaning in John: Jesus will come (1) spiritually through the ministry of the Spirit (vv. 16–18); and (2) literally, at the Second Coming. where … also. At the Second Coming the saved ascend to heaven with Jesus, to return to earth only at the end of the Millennium. See Rev. 20:1–15. For more on the MILLENNIUM, see Rev. 20:4–6; 21:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 4, 'A riddle designed to draw out questions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 6, 'Jesus is the path to God, the revelation of what God is like (v. 9), and the source of life (1:4–5; 10:10; 20:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 8, 'Philip misses the point of vv. 6–7, asking for a visible manifestation of the Father.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 9, 'He who … Father. A summary of what the reader already knows from 1:1–18. For more on the WORK OF CHRIST revealing the Father, see Heb. 1:1–3. For more on the representation of GOD THE FATHER, see Ex. 34:6–7. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 10, 'words … works. The two ways the Father communicated to the disciples through Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 12, 'greater works. While on earth, Jesus was subject to human limitations. When Jesus went to the Father His disciples, empowered by prayer (vv. 13–14), obedience (v. 15), and the Spirit (vv. 16–17), extended the work of Jesus on a magnitude not possible before. We too can participate in these greater works.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 13, 'whatever … will do. Prayers to extend Jesus’ work on earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 15, 'Prayers (vv. 13–14) are empowered by obedience. Love to Jesus is expressed through obedience. For more on obedience to God’s LAW, see v. 21; 15:10; Matt. 5:19; 22:35–40.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 16, 'Jesus (15:26) and the Father sent the Spirit, just as the Father sent the Son (3:16). The Spirit is also a divine member of the Trinity (Matt. 28:19; Acts 5:3–4; 2 Cor. 3:18). As such He was active in the creation of the earth (Gen. 1:2). The Spirit’s involvement in the creation establishes His deity, but Scripture also portrays the Spirit as participating in other divine activities: regeneration (John 3:5–8), saving (Titus 3:5), inspiring the Bible (2 Tim. 3:16), and equal association with the Father and Son in evangelism (Matt. 28:19).For some of the functions of the HOLY SPIRIT, see John 14:26; 15:26–27; 16:8–11, 13; Acts 1:8; 10:38; 1 Pet. 1:2; 2 Pet. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 16, 'I … Father … Helper. All three persons of the Trinity. another. Jesus is the original Helper. Helper. Literally, “comforter” or “advocate.” The Holy Spirit (see vv. 17, 26) takes Jesus’ place on earth after His return to heaven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 17, 'the world. Here humanity in opposition to God. See note on 3:16. it neither … Him. The acts of the Spirit are unnoticed by the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 18, 'I will come to you. Spiritually at Pentecost and literally at the Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 19, 'the world. See note on v. 17. The world only saw Jesus in the flesh; the disciples would “see” Him spiritually through the Spirit. Because … also. Reference to resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 21, 'commandments. See note on v. 15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 23, 'The Father shows Himself to the obedient. Through them His presence is extended throughout the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 26, 'teach you all things. In Jesus’ absence. remembrance. Of what Jesus taught them while on earth. Validates the accuracy of John’s Gospel, based on his memory of what Jesus said. For the role of the HOLY SPIRIT, see 15:26–27; 16:8–11, 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 27, 'Peace. A customary Jewish greeting and farewell. troubled. See v. 18 and note on v. 1. The Spirit brings peace and comfort in the absence of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 28, 'My Father is greater than I. While Jesus is equal with the Father by nature (see notes on 1:1), in His humanity He is related to the Father from our standpoint.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 29, 'See note on 13:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 14, 30, 'ruler of this world. Satan. he has … Me. Indicates Jesus’ complete sinlessness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 1, 'Jesus repeats the themes of 13:31–14:31 over and over, but now with little interruption from the disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 1, 'Jesus repeats the themes of 13:31–14:31 over and over, but now with little interruption from the disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 1, 'Jesus uses the OT illustration of a vine to describe the relationship between Himself and the disciples. See Is. 5:1–7; Jer. 2:21; Ezek. 15:1–5. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 2, 'A grapevine has only two uses: either it produces food and drink or it must be cut down to provide fuel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 3, 'clean. From the same Greek root word as “prunes” in v. 2. The words of Jesus nurtured the disciples (v. 7), but also “pruned out” Judas from among them. See 13:10–11, 18–30. The presence of Jesus brings judgment (3:18–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 4, 'Abide. Remain, continue, stay. No one can snatch us out of Jesus’ hand, but we are free to leave if we wish.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 5, 'branches. In the immediate situation, the disciples of Jesus who were with Him, those being nourished spiritually by His words. fruit. The church, which would be nourished spiritually by the words of the gospel. See note on 17:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 7, 'My words abide. When we pray in harmony with God’s word and will, He will respond accordingly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 8, 'By this. See 13:35. “Bearing fruit” here parallels “love” there. bear much fruit. As with fruit, so with believers; spiritual growth is a process.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 9, 'Jesus expands the illustration of the vine to explain what it means to “abide in the vine.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 9, 'abide … love. Jesus’ love will cause the disciples to live and love the way Jesus loved them. See 13:34–35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 10, 'keep My commandments. See note on 14:15. just as … love. The disciples were to do what Jesus did, not just what He said.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 12, 'love … you. See 13:34–35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 13, 'friends. Jesus’ friends are those who obey Him (v. 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 15, 'servants … friends. Jesus calls on His disciples to envision God as a friend rather than a taskmaster. Compare 12:26; 13:13, 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 16, 'bear fruit. See notes on vv. 1–8. whatever you ask. In relation to fruit-bearing. Genuine church growth is grounded in prayer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 17, 'See 13:34–35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 18, 'world. See note on 3:16. Here humanity in opposition to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 19, 'because … hates you. Opposition to the disciples arises because they do not conform to the ways of the world. The “world” does not lightly tolerate threats to its control.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 20, 'A servant … master. See 13:16. Those in intimate relationship with Jesus should expect the same opposition from the world as Jesus experienced (see 11:47–53; 18:1–3, 6, 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 22, 'no excuse. See 3:18–21 and notes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 15, 26, 'Jesus sent the Spirit to continue His work on earth. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21. He will testify of Me. The Holy Spirit’s testimony comes in the context of the world’s hatred for Jesus and His disciples (John 15:18–25; 16:1–4). For other roles of the HOLY SPIRIT, see 14:16–18, 26; 16:13. testify. Language of the law court.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 1, 'Continues the theme of the world’s hatred (15:18–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 2, 'time is coming. See 5:25, 28; 16:26, 32; and note on 2:4. offers God service. Literally, priestly, sacrificial service.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 4, 'you may … them. See 13:19; 14:26, 29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 6, 'See 13:19; 14:29. sorrow … heart. Because not only will the disciples miss the physical presence of Jesus (14:18), they will face the opposition of the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 7, 'Jesus goes away, despite the disciples’ sorrow (v. 6), because the benefits of the Spirit will far outweigh the grief and loss caused by His absence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 8, 'For other functions of the HOLY SPIRIT, see notes on 14:16–18; Acts 1:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 8, 'world. See note on 3:16. Here the world is humanity as a whole, both righteous and wicked.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 9, 'Sin is deeper than bad actions; the primary sin is a failure to believe in Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 10, 'because … Father. Jesus’ intercession in the heavenly sanctuary brings the assurance of righteousness to His persecuted disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 11, 'Because Satan was overthrown by Christ’s death on the cross (12:31), those who side with Christ are freed from Satan’s control.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 12, 'you cannot … now. Jesus graciously gives His disciples only as much information as they can handle at the time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 13, 'the Spirit of truth. Called the Helper earlier. For more on the role of the HOLY SPIRIT, see 14:16, 26; 15:26; 16:7. On truth, see note on 4:23–24. things to come. The HOLY SPIRIT guides our understanding of Bible prophecies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 14, 'He will glorify Me. The Spirit’s focus is always on Jesus (see v. 9; 14:26; 15:26). He not only reveals what Jesus is doing in the present, but also what He will do in the future (16:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 16, 'A riddle with a double meaning: (1) His death and resurrection; and (2) His ascension and ultimate return (see note on 14:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 20, 'you will weep … rejoice. The world in opposition to Jesus (see note on 3:16) will rejoice at His death, while the disciples are made sad. sorrow … joy. The previous emotions are reversed at the resurrection and ultimately at the Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 23, 'in My name. We pray to the Father in Jesus’ name.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 24, 'Ask … full. The sorrow the disciples experience in the absence of Jesus is lessened by their joy in a new kind of relationship with Jesus and the Father through prayer (14:13–14; 15:7, 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 25, 'time is coming. See v. 2 and note.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 27, 'the Father Himself loves you. The Father is just like Jesus. He does not have an unwilling ear when we pray.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 28, 'world. See note on 3:16. Here means the physical planet.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 29, 'The disciples suddenly profess a foolish confidence that they have understood Jesus’ meaning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 31, 'Jesus challenges the disciples’ newfound confidence with the warning that they are about to abandon Him. hour is coming. See note on v. 2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 16, 33, 'in Me … peace. Peace is grounded in relationship with Jesus (14:27). tribulation. Literally, “pressure.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 1, 'The farewell get-together of Jesus and His disciples closes with an intercessory prayer in three parts: (1) Jesus prays for Himself (vv. 1–5); (2) then for His disciples (vv. 6–19); and (3) then for those who come to faith through the disciples’ words (v. 20). He desires unity in love for the latter two groups (vv. 21–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 1, 'hour. See note on 2:4. Glorify. See note on 2:11. glorify You. At the cross Jesus revealed the character of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 2, 'eternal life … given Him. See note on 6:37.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 3, 'eternal life. See note on 3:16. Available through relationship with Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 4, 'glorified. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 5, 'before the world was. See 1:1–5. world. See note on 3:16. Here means the planet.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 6, 'the men … Me. Jesus’ disciples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 9, 'for them. The bulk of Jesus’ prayers are for His disciples, since they will have to remain in the world when He returns to His Father. world. See note on 3:16. Here humanity in opposition to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 12, 'son of perdition. Judas. See note on 6:70–71. Scripture … fulfilled. See note on 13:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 13, 'Jesus prays that the disciples will be joyful in spite of His departure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 14, 'hated. See 15:18–25; 16:1–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 17, 'Sanctify. Set them apart to represent Jesus in the world. Your word is truth. The Bible is God’s word. Christ declares it to be truth. For more on the BIBLE being the word of God, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17; 2 Pet. 1:20–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 20, 'for those … word. The second generation, who will come to believe without physical contact with Jesus, through the testimony of the disciples. See Introduction, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 21, 'all. Both the disciples (vv. 6–19) and those they bring to faith in Jesus (v. 20). one. Jesus’ prayer is answered whenever a church in loving unity draws new believers out from the world. A divided church will not draw believers to Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 24, 'glory. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 17, 26, 'name. In the Hebrew context, a name signifies character.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 1, 'The passion of Jesus begins (18:1) and ends (19:41) in a garden. It falls naturally into three parts: (1) The betrayal, arrest, and indictment of Jesus (18:1–27); (2) the trial before Pilate (18:28–19:16a); and (3) the crucifixion and burial of Jesus (19:16b–42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 1, 'The passion of Jesus begins (18:1) and ends (19:41) in a garden. It falls naturally into three parts: (1) The betrayal, arrest, and indictment of Jesus (18:1–27); (2) the trial before Pilate (18:28–19:16a); and (3) the crucifixion and burial of Jesus (19:16b–42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 1, 'the Brook Kidron. Between Jerusalem and the Mount of Olives. garden. Called Gethsemane in the other Gospels (Matt. 26:36; Mark 14:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 2, 'Judas. See note on 6:70–71. knew the place. If Jesus had wanted to avoid arrest, He could have gone somewhere else. See notes on 18:4, 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 3, 'detachment. 200 to 600 Roman soldiers. officers. Chiefs of the temple police.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 4, 'knowing all things. Jesus could have escaped, but chose not to.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 6, 'I am He. See note on 6:35. When used by Jesus, the phrase had great power. fell to the ground. Jesus was in full control of the situation (see 10:17–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 8, 'let these go. In the midst of His own great crisis, Jesus still puts others first.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 9, 'saying … fulfilled. See 17:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 10, 'Although Jesus is fully in control of the situation, Peter sees things as totally out of control. See also Matt. 26:51–54; Mark 14:47; Luke 22:49–51.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 11, 'Put … sheath. Peter’s actions to get control of the situation would have moved things truly out of control.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 12, 'detachment. See note on v. 3. captain. Head of the detachment. officers. See note on v. 3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 13, 'Annas. A previous high priest, deposed by the Romans. Since the Jews considered the office lifelong (see Ex. 29:9; Num. 35:25, 28), he still carried great authority with the people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 14, 'advised. See 11:50–52.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 15, 'another disciple. Thought to be John, the author of the Gospel. the high priest. Annas, not Caiaphas. See note on v. 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 17, 'I am not. The first of three denials predicted in 13:38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 18, 'officers. See note on v. 3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 19, 'the high priest. See note on v. 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 20, 'Jesus has no secret agenda (Matt. 26:55; Mark 14:49; Luke 22:53).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 22, 'struck Jesus. Illegal act before conviction, according to Jewish law. the high priest. See note on v. 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 23, 'Jesus does not follow an extreme interpretation of Matt. 5:39. He protests the abuse of authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 24, 'the high priest. See note on v. 15. Roman policy had created multiple high priests, called the “chief priests” in this Gospel (see 11:47 and note on 12:10). Annas had greater spiritual authority, but Caiaphas (see note on 11:49) had the political authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 27, 'rooster crowed. See 13:37–38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 28, 'the Praetorium. Official residence of the governor, near the temple. defiled. During Passover and the Feast of Unleavened Bread, Jews were not allowed to enter a house that might contain leaven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 29, 'Pilate. Has a much more central and detailed role than in Matthew, Mark, and Luke. What accusation. A formal question at the onset of the trial.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 30, 'An evasive response.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 31, 'not lawful. By Roman decree, as an occupying power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 32, 'saying of Jesus. See 12:32–34. The Jews executed by stoning, the Romans by crucifixion. See note on 19:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 33, '“Are You … Jews?” The chief priests must have formulated the charge in terms of a rival kingship with Rome.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 34, 'you … others. Pilate would be thinking of a political king; the Jews would be thinking of the Messiah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 35, 'Pilate denies personal interest; he was asking a legal question.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 36, 'Response to the legal question: Jesus’ kingship is no political threat to Rome. As evidence, Jesus notes the behavior of His followers at the time of His arrest. Peter’s foolish action (vv. 10–11) could have undermined Jesus’ legal argument.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 37, 'the truth. See 1:7, 14; 14:6. hears My voice. The truth about Jesus is self-authenticating.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 38, '“What is truth?” Pilate disdains the spiritual turn to the discussion. I find no fault. Pilate renders judgment on the case.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 39, 'Pilate offers a face-saving way for the religious leaders to back out of the situation, but they reject it (v. 40), complicating the situation for Pilate. He knows Jesus is innocent, but fears that irritating the Jewish leaders will hurt his relationship with Caesar. See 19:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 18, 40, 'Barabbas. Literally, “son of a father.” He was a rebel (Mark 15:7) and a murderer (Luke 23:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 1, 'See note on 18:1–19:42.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 1, 'scourged. Probably to excite sympathy for Jesus in His opponents. See vv. 4–5; Mark 15:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 2, 'crown of thorns. To mock His claim to royalty. purple robe. Represented royalty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 3, 'Hail … Jews. May echo 12:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 5, '“Behold the Man!” Echoes 1:29, 36.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 6, 'when … saw Him. To forestall any expression of pity. chief priests. See notes on 18:13, 15, 24. You take Him … I find no fault. Pilate tries to shift responsibility to the religious leaders. On the other hand, they need a conviction from him to excuse their own actions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 7, 'according to our law. Pilate had repeatedly judged Jesus innocent of the civil charges (18:38; 19:4, 6), so they switch to a religious one. Pilate was bound by Roman law to protect Jewish religion from sacrilege.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 8, 'more afraid. Pilate was politically vulnerable here, as his insensitivity to Jewish religious concerns had stirred up trouble in the past.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 9, 'Pilate tries to extract something from Jesus that will ease his dilemma. See note on 18:39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 11, 'greater sin. Jesus thoughtfully absolves Pilate of blame to the degree possible under the circumstances.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 12, 'From then on. Pilate was moved by Jesus’ words in v. 11. Caesar’s friend. They return to the civil charge and argue that Caesar would side with them on this issue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 13, 'Determining that he must choose between Jesus and his own interests, Pilate acts in his own interests.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 14, 'Preparation Day. The Friday of Passover week (see 18:28). “Behold your King!” Again echoes 1:29, 36. See 19:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 15, 'no king but Caesar. A foolish admission of their obligation to serve Caesar rather than a king of their own. Caiaphas had argued (11:48–52) that one man should be sacrificed to save the nation; now he is willing to sacrifice the nation in order to destroy one man.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 16, 'crucified. A peculiarly Roman form of execution. Stripped naked and tied or nailed to a wooden stake, the victims had to exert strength in order to breathe. With exhaustion, death came by suffocation. It was a slow, humiliating, and painful death. See also Matt. 27:35; Mark 15:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 17, 'a place … Golgotha. A small hill outside the city wall. See v. 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 19, 'wrote a title. A mocking assertion of Rome’s triumph over Jewish political hopes. See vv. 21–22 and note on v. 15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 23, 'crucified … garments. See note on v. 16. tunic. Close-fitting undergarment that went from the neck to the knees.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 24, 'the Scripture … fulfilled. Ps. 22:18. Jesus’ death was voluntary, purposeful, and according to the Scriptures.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 26, 'the disciple … loved. The author of the Gospel (21:20–24). mother … Woman. See 2:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 27, 'The beautiful selflessness of Jesus. At the height of His suffering He turns His attention to taking care of His mother.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 28, 'knowing … accomplished. From beginning to end, Jesus remained in full control of the situation. See v. 11 and notes on 18:2, 4, 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 29, 'sour wine. Wine vinegar. hyssop. A branch of a small bush with aromatic leaves.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 30, 'finished. The fulfillment of the prophecies regarding Christ’s atoning sacrifice on the cross, the central event of the plan of salvation. spirit. Literally, “breath” here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 31, 'Preparation. See note on v. 14. legs … broken. To hasten death by suffocation so the bodies could be removed before Sabbath. See note on v. 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 33, 'saw … dead. The reality of Jesus’ death was verified by disinterested experts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 34, 'Further verification that Jesus was truly dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 35, 'he knows … truth. John, the author of the Gospel, knows because he was there (13:23; 18:15–16; 19:26; 21:20–24). See also note on 1:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 36, 'Scripture … fulfilled. See note on v. 24. See also Ex. 12:46; Num. 9:12; Zech. 12:10; 13:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 38, 'Joseph. See Matt. 27:57; Mark 15:43. secretly. But now openly. See John 12:32–33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 39, 'Nicodemus. See 3:1–2; 7:50–52. a hundred pounds. About 33 kg.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 41, 'new tomb. See Is. 53:9; Matt. 27:59–60.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 19, 42, 'Preparation Day. See note on v. 14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 1, 'The NT bears witness to eleven total post-resurrection appearances of Jesus, four of which are mentioned in these two chapters. These appearances were occasional and unexpected. The many different witnesses to Jesus’ resurrection assure the reality of the event, along with the fact that the tomb was and is empty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 1, 'The NT bears witness to eleven total post-resurrection appearances of Jesus, four of which are mentioned in these two chapters. These appearances were occasional and unexpected. The many different witnesses to Jesus’ resurrection assure the reality of the event, along with the fact that the tomb was and is empty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 1, 'Mary Magdalene. See 19:25; Luke 8:2. stone … tomb. Not so Jesus could get out, but so the disciples could get in.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 2, 'whom Jesus loved. See notes on 1:40; 19:35. we do not know. The resurrection was a surprise to the disciples. we. Other women were with her, but are not mentioned here. See Matt. 28:1; Mark 16:1; Luke 24:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 5, 'linen cloths. See 19:40. If people had stolen the body, they would not have taken the time to unwrap it first.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 7, 'folded together. Again, not the actions of a thief (see note on v. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 8, 'believed. John was the first disciple to believe in the resurrection of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 9, 'the Scripture. The disciples’ reading of Scripture did not lead them to expect a resurrection. Later Jesus taught them how His life and death fulfilled the OT. See Luke 24:25–27, 44–45.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 13, 'they have taken. At this point she believed Jesus’ body had been stolen, not resurrected.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 15, 'Woman … weeping? Echoes 20:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 16, 'The physical presence of Jesus was of no meaning to Mary until she gave attention to His word. “Rabboni!” An Aramaic term that John translates into Greek for his readers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 17, 'Do not cling. “Do not continue holding on.” ascended. May suggest a brief ascension in which Jesus receives assurance from His Father that His sacrifice on the cross was accepted. brethren. In spite of their forsaking Him in His hour of need, Jesus has elevated His disciples from “servants” (13:16) to “disciples” (13:35) to “friends” (15:15) to “brethren” (20:17)—family.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 19, 'Jesus … stood. Miraculous, since the doors were locked.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 20, 'He showed … side. He had a physical body after His resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 22, 'He breathed … Spirit. A foretaste of Pentecost. See Acts 2:1–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 23, 'Only God can ultimately forgive sins (Acts 8:21–22). But it is through the preaching of the gospel that God’s forgiveness is made available to others. Rejection of that gospel certifies final rejection of God’s forgiveness. See also 3:16–21; 2 Cor. 2:15–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 25, '“Unless I see … I will not believe.” A very firm expression of unbelief.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 26, '“Peace to you!” A common greeting in Jewish culture of the time. See also vv. 19, 21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 27, 'Repeats Thomas’s wording in v. 25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 28, '“My Lord and my God!” A fitting counterpart to the Prologue of the Gospel (1:1–2, 14), which affirms Jesus’ divinity. In 20:29 Jesus commends this statement of belief.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 29, 'Blessed are those. The ones whose belief is not based on physical contact with Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 30, 'The author’s own statement of purpose for the book.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 30, 'And truly. Literally, “therefore.” signs. See note on 2:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 20, 31, 'that. Translates a specific expression of purpose: “in order that …” Christ. See notes on 1:20; Matt. 1:16. Through the Holy Spirit, this book will accomplish in our lives everything that could happen were Jesus with us in the flesh. See Introduction, “Audience.” life. For more on the role of the BIBLE in the Christian life, see Luke 24:44; Rom. 15:4; 2 Tim. 3:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 1, 'See note on 20:1–21:25. The Epilogue of the Gospel, since it comes after what reads like a concluding statement (20:30–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 1, 'Sea of Tiberias. See note on 6:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 2, 'sons of Zebedee. James and John. The latter was the author of this Gospel. See Matt. 10:2; Mark 3:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 3, 'that night. Net fishing is best done at night, when the fish can’t see as well.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 4, 'morning. The best opportunity to catch fish would be in the shadow of the boat.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 6, 'Cast … boat. Jesus goes against best fishing practice (see notes on vv. 3–4). multitude of fish. Jesus exerts the dominion of the original Adam. See Gen. 1:26–28; Rom. 5:14–18; 1 Cor. 15:45.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 7, 'disciple … loved. See Introduction, “Author.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 8, 'two hundred cubits. About 300 feet (90 m).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 9, 'coals. Charcoal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 13, 'took … them. See note on 6:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 14, 'third time … disciples. As a group. See 20:19–23, 24–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 15, 'when … breakfast. Vv. 15–17 take place in the presence of the other disciples. Peter needed to regain their confidence after the denials of 18:15–18, 25–27. By 21:20, Jesus and Peter are walking down the beach, away from the others. these. Neuter in Greek; may refer to the other disciples as a group or to fishing as a vocation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 16, 'Tend. Lambs need to be fed (v. 15), and sheep need to be watched and guided.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 17, 'grieved. Jesus’ probing questions were painful to Peter. Suffering and pain can be opportunities for spiritual growth. third time. In response to Peter’s three denials. See 18:15–18, 25–27. love. A different Greek word than in the earlier questions (vv. 15–16). In John, however, these two words are synonyms.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 18, 'another will gird you. Bound as a criminal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 19, 'death. See 2 Pet. 1:13–14. “Follow Me.” Peter’s ultimate task in life: Do what Jesus did.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 20, 'disciple … loved. See Introduction, “Author.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 23, 'See Introduction, “Audience.” Jesus did not say. No human statement, not even from Jesus Himself, is totally immune to misunderstanding.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 24, 'This … disciple. See Introduction, “Author.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'João', 21, 25, 'the world … written. John’s Gospel is truthful, but it is far from containing the whole story.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 0, NULL, 'The Acts of the Apostles is a story full of drama and fascinating characters. It is a story with a purpose. Acts is the continuation to the Gospel of Luke, showing that God’s new work on earth does not end when Jesus is taken up into heaven. The work continues through the Holy Spirit, poured out on His faithful followers. Acts is the only New Testament book that tells the story of the first thirty years in the life of the church, when Jesus’ followers grew from a small, frightened group to become a dynamic movement, with Spirit-given power to turn “the world upside down” (see 17:6). Acts invites us to faith in Jesus as risen Lord (4:12) and to become part of the exciting action of Christian mission.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 0, NULL, 'Although the author is not named, there are several sections where he speaks as one who traveled with Paul (16:10–17; 20:5–15; 21:1–18; 27:1–28:16). Paul himself mentions “Luke the beloved physician” in a context that suggests Luke was a Gentile (Col. 4:10–11, 14; see also 2 Tim. 4:11; Philem. 24). It is this Luke who was identified by the early church as the author of the Gospel of Luke and the book of Acts (see Introduction to Luke, “Author”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 0, NULL, 'Acts is dedicated to Theophilus (1:1), as is the Gospel of Luke (Luke 1:3), though it is clearly meant for a wide audience. It seems especially designed to address Gentiles and also Jews living outside of Palestine (e.g., 1:19; see Introduction to Luke, “Audience”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 0, NULL, 'The simplest answer to when and where the book was written is that it was composed before Paul’s death (ca. A.D. 67), in about A.D. 62 or 63, in Rome, as that is where the book ends. Since Luke never mentions the terrible persecution of Christians by the Emperor Nero (A.D. 64) or the tragic fall of Jerusalem (A.D. 70), this judgment seems correct.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 0, NULL, 'Even in seemingly impossible circumstances, God is at work to ensure a hearing for the story of Jesus. This is the central message of Acts: God is powerfully at work through Spirit-inspired believers to bring the message of salvation in Jesus to everyone, because Jesus is the Savior of all. Against the backdrop of that central message, the following themes emerge:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 0, NULL, 'I.      The church is born (1:1–5:42)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 1, 'Acts opens with four scenes, the first three replaying Luke 24:1–53: (1) introduction (vv. 1–5); (2) the ascension of Jesus (vv. 6–11); (3) the twelve and others at prayer (vv. 12–14); and (4) the selection of Matthias (vv. 15–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 1, 'Theophilus. Luke addresses Acts to Theophilus, just as he did his Gospel (see note on Luke 1:1–4), presenting a seamless story about Jesus, who continues to work wonders (e.g., Acts 3:12–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 4, 'wait. The disciples are to await the action of God, who alone initiates Christian mission. The Father (v. 4), the Holy Spirit (vv. 2, 4–5), and Jesus are all highlighted. Christian witness is fueled by the certainty of Jesus’ resurrection (v. 3) and the power of the Holy Spirit (vv. 4–5; see Luke 11:13; 24:49).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 6, 'Israel. The disciples, left to themselves, would have remained fixated on Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 7, 'times. Jesus gently reminds them that mission, not time-setting, is to be the passion of His followers (compare Mark 13:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 8, 'power. The gift of power is central to the work of the Holy Spirit. For aspects of the Spirit’s empowerment, see Gal. 5:21–23; Eph. 4:11–12. For more on the HOLY SPIRIT, see Introduction, “Message,” and note on John 14:16–18.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 11, 'will so come. In Acts, all the efforts to proclaim Jesus are motivated by His Second Coming. He will not appear on earth (see Jesus’ own warning: Matt. 24:23–27; Mark 13:21–23; Luke 21:8) but in like manner as He departed, that is, in the clouds (Luke 21:27; 1 Thess. 4:16–18; Rev. 1:7). For more on the SECOND COMING, see note on Matt. 24–25; 1 Thess. 4:13–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 12, 'The church prepares for its mission by uniting in prayer (vv. 12–14) and by replacing Judas among the twelve as an eyewitness of Jesus’ life and resurrection (vv. 15–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 12, 'Sabbath day’s journey. Understood in Jewish custom to be 2,000 cubits or 3,000 feet (900 m).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 14, 'women. Had been among Jesus’ disciples from the beginning (9:36; Luke 8:1–3; 23:55–24:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 15, 'Peter applies Ps. 69:25 and 109:8 to Judas, urging selection of someone to take his place among the twelve. Jesus had appointed twelve apostles to lead the church (Luke 6:12–16) as the twelve sons of Jacob were leaders of Israel (Luke 22:28–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 23, 'they proposed two. All the believers seem to be involved in the decision, which is preceded by prayer (compare Luke 6:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 24, 'That God knows the hearts of all is an important thought in Acts (see 15:7–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 26, 'lots. The practice of casting lots—throwing down small stones or pieces of wood—was used during OT times (Lev. 16:8; Josh. 18:6; 1 Chr. 25:8; Prov. 16:33), but is rarely mentioned in the NT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 1, 'The Holy Spirit—in the form of a rushing mighty wind and tongues … of fire—descends upon believers (vv. 1–6) and empowers them to speak in a wide variety of foreign languages (vv. 7–12). This signals Jesus’ intention that believers are to be His witnesses “to the end of the earth” (1:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 1, 'Pentecost (Greek for “fiftieth”) was a name for the Feast of Weeks (Lev. 23:15–21), which celebrated the spring harvest. It took place 50 days after the presentation of the sheaf of firstfruits at Passover (Lev. 23:9–14; see Acts 20:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 4, 'all. All believers—not just the apostles—were baptized with the Holy Spirit and spoke with other tongues (see note on vv. 16–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 7, 'Galileans. The miracle is especially impressive since Galileans were known for a heavy accent and poor language skills (Matt. 26:73).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 13, 'The charge is illogical since drunkenness yields confused speech, not skill in using foreign language (see v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 14, 'Speeches are important in Acts (see 13:16–41 and note) and Peter’s speech sets a pattern: It reviews the events of Jesus’ life and, based on OT passages, argues for His resurrection and enthronement at God’s right hand. These events explain the strange phenomenon they have heard: the risen, enthroned Jesus is pouring out His Holy Spirit (v. 33). This conviction—that their Friend and Lord, Jesus, was risen and enthroned at the right hand of God—motivated the courageous witness of early Christians. Are we as convinced of these central truths today?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 16, 'Joel. Peter powerfully employs the prophecy of Joel 2:28–32, which promises the outpouring of God’s Spirit in the last days. In this outpouring on all flesh, God ignores distinctions of age, gender, and status. Instead, the Spirit is poured out on everyone, signaling a theme at the heart of Acts: God will use all (vv. 17–18) to bring salvation to all (v. 21). For more on SPIRITUAL GIFTS see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 19, 'See Luke 21:25–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 20, 'Before the coming … of the LORD. We may pray for a fresh baptism of the Holy Spirit before Christ’s Second Coming. See note on Joel 2:28–31.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 25, 'Peter cites Ps. 16:8–11 (Acts 2:25–28, 31) and Ps. 110:1 (Acts 2:34–35) to show that Jesus’ resurrection and enthronement at God’s right hand were predicted in Scripture. Hades (vv. 27, 31) is the grave (not a “hell” that is currently burning, an unbiblical idea since hell occurs at the end of time; 2 Pet. 2:4; Rev. 20:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 31, 'Hades. See note on vv. 25–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 34, 'See note on vv. 25–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 38, 'Repent. Turn away from sin. To be baptized (meaning to be immersed; see note on 8:38–39) signals faith in Jesus for remission or forgiveness of sins. Having been baptized, the believer receives the gift of the Holy Spirit. Repentance indicates that belief comes before baptism, something impossible for infants. Compare 16:30–33. For more on BAPTISM, see Rom. 6:3–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 41, 'three thousand. Peter’s speech is effective and his invitation to baptism (vv. 38–39) is accepted by 3,000 people. The number of believers had earlier been stated as 120 (1:15); see 4:4 for the continued growth of the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 44, 'had all things in common. See note on 4:32–5:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 3, 1, 'This detailed story has four major scenes: (1) the healing (3:1–10); (2) Peter’s testimony (3:11–26); (3) Peter and John arrested and released (4:1–22); and (4) thanksgiving prayer (4:23–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 3, 1, 'This detailed story has four major scenes: (1) the healing (3:1–10); (2) Peter’s testimony (3:11–26); (3) Peter and John arrested and released (4:1–22); and (4) thanksgiving prayer (4:23–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 3, 1, 'the ninth hour. 3:00 p.m., one of the three daily times of prayer for Jews of the time (the other times being the third hour [9:00 a.m.] and sunset). The Beautiful gate was the main gate on the east side of the temple complex that led from the outer court of the Gentiles into the court of women.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 3, 6, 'The miracle is performed in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, by His power, not the apostles’ own (see 3:12, 16; 4:7, 10; Luke 9:1–2). Like the lame man, do we sometimes ask God for something small when He longs to give us something grand? (See John 14:13–14; 15:7; 16:24.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 3, 8, 'Once healed, the man followed Peter and John into the inner courts of the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 3, 20, 'that He may send Jesus Christ. The Return of Jesus, which will bring the restoration of all things, represents the great Christian hope for the future and has been part of the Christian message from the earliest days (see note on 1:11). For more on the Second Coming, see 1:11; Matt. 24–25; 1 Thess. 4:13–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 1, 'The Sadducees were a group within Judaism known for opposing the idea of resurrection (see 23:6–8), so they are especially irritated by Peter’s speech about Jesus’ resurrection (see esp. 3:14–15). The high priest and other chief priests were Sadducees (5:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 4, 'The arrest of Peter and John did not inhibit the growth of the fledgling Christian movement, which had grown considerably since the 3,000 converts at Pentecost (2:41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 5, 'rulers, elders, scribes, etc. This group of people, called the council or Sanhedrin in v. 15, is the same group that tried Jesus. The Sanhedrin had considerable power. It largely controlled the internal government of the Jewish nation and possessed some authority outside Judea (22:4–5). Annas, who had earlier served officially as high priest (A.D. 6–15), was still recognized by the Jewish population as the high priest, though his son-in-law, Caiaphas, was then actually serving in that office (A.D. 18–36). The identities of John and Alexander are uncertain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 10, 'you crucified … God raised. Peter bluntly confronts the Sanhedrin for their role in condemning and crucifying Jesus. He wants them to know that they have not destroyed Jesus. Christ is alive and still performs miracles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 11, 'Peter quotes Ps. 118:22, cited frequently in the NT as a messianic prophecy (e.g., Matt. 21:42; see also Rom. 9:33). Christ as Cornerstone or Foundation is an important image elsewhere in the NT and is joined to the idea of the church as God’s temple (1 Cor. 3:9–17; Eph. 2:19–22; 1 Pet. 2:4–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 12, 'no other name. This exclusive claim for Jesus does not sit well in our age, which imagines many pathways to salvation (see John 14:6; 1 Tim. 2:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 13, 'Are onlookers able to see that our lives have been transformed and to trace that change to time spent with Jesus?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 19, 'Compare 5:29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 23, 'they raised their voice to God. We gain a rare glimpse of early Christians gathered in prayer. They begin their prayer meeting in a most significant manner, reciting together words drawn from the Fourth Commandment (about the Sabbath, Ex. 20:8–11; also Gen. 14:19; Ps. 146:6; Is. 37:16): Lord, You are God, who made heaven and earth and the sea, and all that is in them. (Paul cites very similar words in Acts 14:15; 17:24.) Their worship is rooted in the acknowledgement of God as the Creator of all things, a conviction that they acknowledge week-by-week as they worship on the seventh-day Sabbath (as evidenced by Paul’s own practice reflected in 13:14, 27, 42, 44; 16:13; 17:2; 18:4; 24:14; 28:17). For more on the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 27, 'Herod. Herod Antipas, tetrarch of Galilee and Perea, who had killed John the Baptist (Mark 6:14–29) and questioned Jesus during His trial (see Luke 23:7–12, 15). Pontius Pilate was the governor of Judea who authorized the Roman legal proceedings against Jesus (see Luke 23:1–5, 13–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 29, 'grant … boldness. They ask the God who worked the wonders of creation (v. 24) and sent Jesus to die for humankind (vv. 25–28) to be active in their own story by giving them boldness to witness about Jesus and to confirm that witness with miracles. Could such a prayer ever remain unanswered? Note that they do not pray for deliverance from persecution.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 32, 'This section describes how the early Christians in Jerusalem had all things in common (4:32) and how property owners sold their possessions to support the community (4:34–35). The passage offers two case studies, the positive one of Barnabas (4:36–37) and the longer, negative story of Ananias and Sapphira (5:1–11), which qualifies as the most disturbing story in Acts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 4, 36, 'Barnabas. This is the first mention of Barnabas, who became an encourager and fellow missionary of Paul (see 9:26–27; 11:25–26; 13:4–14:28; 15:36–41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 1, 'Ananias. This must have been a relatively common name, for there are two other individuals who bear that name in Acts—the follower of Jesus in Damascus (9:10–19) and the high priest in 23:2; 24:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 2, 'kept back … proceeds. Ananias and Sapphira promise to sell a piece of land and contribute the sale price to the common fund. However, they cannot bring themselves to give the full amount. They give a smaller amount, representing it as the full sale price. Their tragic deaths follow this deception. The story may be compared with two others in Acts in which individuals are judged—Simon the sorcerer (8:9–25) and Herod Agrippa I (12:19–24). All three stories illustrate the need to acknowledge the sovereignty of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 3, 'the Holy Spirit. Notice carefully that both the personhood and the divinity of the Spirit are obvious in this passage. Like other persons or beings, the Spirit may be lied to. The two parallel statements affirm that the Spirit is God: To lie to the Holy Spirit (v. 3) is to lie to God (v. 4). Other passages of the Bible use Holy Spirit and God interchangeably. In 1 Cor. 3:16–17 the phrase “God’s temple” is equated with “a temple of the Holy Spirit” and in Luke 1:35 “the Holy Spirit” and “the power of the Most High” are synonymous. In Acts, the Holy Spirit often gives specific instructions for preaching the gospel (see Introduction, “Message”). For more on the HOLY SPIRIT, see 1:8; Matt. 28:19; John 14:16–18; 2 Cor. 3:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 5, 'great fear came upon all. The reputation of the church grows through positive miracles (speaking in foreign languages, chap. 2; the healing of the lame man, chap. 3). And it grows through this negative story as well, since both believers and non-believers learn that Christian faith commitments are not to be ignored (see v. 11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 11, 'great fear. See note on v. 5. church. Used here for the first time in Acts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 12, 'In several ways this story parallels the earlier one of Peter and John healing the lame man (3:1–4:31; compare also Peter’s later escape from prison, 12:1–19). On the heels of more miracle-working by the apostles and their increasing popularity (vv. 12–16), the high priest imprisons them (vv. 17–18). Freed by an angel and commanded to preach in the temple, the apostles do so (vv. 19–21). Meanwhile, in a series of rather comical scenes, the council convenes and calls for the prisoners, whose absence from prison is discovered and whose preaching in the temple becomes known (vv. 21–25). Finally summoned before the council and quizzed about their disobedience (see 4:18), Peter responds, “We ought to obey God rather than men” (v. 29). Peter’s straightforward witness about Jesus only infuriates the members of the council (vv. 30–33). In executive session (with the apostles removed from the chamber), Gamaliel, a council member, argues carefully from recent historical events to support his plea to keep away from these men and let them alone (vv. 34–39). Gamaliel’s speech wins the day and the apostles, having escaped with a beating and a reprimand, continue teaching and preaching Jesus as the Christ (vv. 40–42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 17, 'On the Sadducees, see note on 4:1–2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 21, 'The council here is the Sanhedrin council, which had condemned Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 34, 'Gamaliel, an important figure in the Judaism of the first century, is described in some detail. Later in Acts, Paul describes Gamaliel as his teacher (22:3). Again and again in Acts, God intervenes on behalf of Christian believers by using various people (see note on 23:12–30). Here, a member of the Jewish Sanhedrin is used by God to rescue the apostles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 36, 'By pointing to two historical figures, Gamaliel makes the point that misguided religious movements usually come to a rapid end. Theudas appeared sometime before A.D. 6 and quickly faded (the ancient historian Josephus mentions a different “imposter” named Theudas who appeared ca. A.D. 45, promised to separate the waters of the Jordan River, and was slain by the Romans). Judas of Galilee (also described by Josephus) argued that it was wrong to submit to the census (A.D. 6) and led an armed rebellion against Rome—one that was quickly put down.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 40, 'beaten. The beating the apostles received was, presumably, the standard Jewish punishment—“forty stripes minus one” (2 Cor. 11:24). Jesus had warned His disciples that they would be mistreated in this way (Matt. 10:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 41, 'rejoicing. Note well the reaction of the apostles to this first recorded physical suffering of Christians for their faith. They regard it a privilege to suffer shame for His name and continue to fulfill Christ’s commission to share the gospel.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 6, NULL, '6:1–8:1a The ministry of Stephen is described in four scenes: (1) the appointment of the seven deacons (6:1–7); (2) Stephen’s powerful witness and resulting arrest (6:8–12); (3) Stephen’s trial before the council (6:13–7:56); and (4) Stephen’s execution by stoning (7:57–8:1a). This is an important story since Stephen’s thought lays the groundwork for the rest of the plot of Acts, which will celebrate the geographical and cultural expansion of Christian faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 6, 1, 'Hebrews … Hellenists. Among the early Christians, who were all Jews, tension developed between Hebrews (Jewish Christians from Palestine who spoke mainly Aramaic and/or Hebrew) and Hellenists (Jewish Christians from outside Palestine who spoke mainly Greek). widows. The church had assumed the responsibility for their care (4:32–34; 1 Tim. 5:3–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 6, 2, 'The verb translated to serve tables is closely related to the word for deacon, so the Seven are generally thought of as the first deacons (see Phil. 1:1; 1 Tim. 3:8–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 6, 5, 'The complaint about unfair treatment had come from Greek-speaking, Jewish Christians, so it is significant that all of the seven have Greek names. Stephen and Philip are discussed in detail in 6:8–8:1a and 8:1b–40. Nicolas is described as a proselyte, that is, a Gentile who had converted to Judaism. Antioch. See note on 13:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 6, 6, 'laid hands on them. Moses had laid his hands on Joshua, commissioning him for office and giving him a new status and authority (Num. 27:12–23; Deut. 34:9). Something similar is intended in the NT where individuals are dedicated by laying on of hands (Acts 13:3; 1 Tim. 5:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 6, 8, 'Stephen, whose ministry had expanded beyond distributing food to widows (vv. 1–7), disputed the case for Jesus in the Synagogue of the Freedmen. Freedmen were former slaves who had been set free by their owners. The Synagogue members came from Cyrene (a port city in N Africa), Alexandria (a port city in Egypt), Cilicia (a region in SE Asia Minor bordering the Mediterranean Sea; Tarsus, the hometown of Paul, was located there), and Asia (the Roman province occupying the western part of Asia Minor). Unable to counter Stephen’s arguments for Jesus, Synagogue members spread false charges about him. In the resulting furor, Stephen is arrested and taken to the Sanhedrin council (see note on 4:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 6, 13, 'false witnesses … we have heard him say. While the charges against Stephen are clearly false, they do hint at his views. For Stephen, Jesus, who is at the right hand of the Father in heaven (7:55–56), is the “Prophet like me” that Moses himself predicted would come (7:37), the defining Person of history whose significance moves well beyond Judaism. Similarly, neither the tabernacle in the wilderness nor Solomon’s temple can contain God (7:44–50). Stephen is filled with conviction that the culmination of the whole story of the OT is reached in Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 1, 'The high priest gives Stephen an opportunity to respond to the charges (v. 1). His response outlines the history of God’s relationship with the Jews. He reviews in brief the stories of Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and Joseph (vv. 2–16), as well as the enslavement of the Israelites in Egypt and the story of Moses (vv. 17–34). To this point, there would be nothing with which his audience could disagree.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 35, 'As Stephen continues to review Israel’s history, points of conflict emerge. He accents the Jews’ rejection of Moses and shares Moses’ prediction of another prophet appearing. The implication seems clear enough: just as the Jews of yesteryear rejected Moses, Stephen’s hearers are in danger of rejecting his Successor, Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 40, 'Stephen continues by describing how the rejection of Moses led to idolatrous worship complete with its own idols, sacrifices, and tabernacle.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 44, 'Stephen relates the history of the tabernacle in the wilderness and Solomon’s temple. Just as the rejection of Moses in the past led to idolatrous worship and a corrupt temple (see note on vv. 40–43), so the rejection of his Successor, Jesus, had led to the same.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 48, 'the Most High … hands. Stephen begins to apply his story with this statement, a point he substantiates with a powerful quotation from Is. 66:1–2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 51, 'Stephen’s speech seems to break off at this point as he perceives the rising anger of his audience. He moves quickly to direct accusation—they have resisted the Holy Spirit just as their forefathers did. As their forefathers killed the prophets who foretold the coming of the Just One (Jesus), they are themselves guilty of murdering that Just One. They have received the law, but by denying its witness to Jesus, they have not kept it. Stephen’s accusations infuriate his audience and they gnashed at him with their teeth (see Luke 13:28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 55, 'gazed into heaven. In this dire moment, Stephen is granted a vision of heaven itself (compare 1 Kin. 22:19; Is. 6:1–13; Rev. 4–5). The title the Son of Man is language borrowed from the heavenly court scene of Dan. 7:13–14. The scene of the earthly trial before the Sanhedrin melts away and Stephen sees the Son of Man, Jesus, standing in the heavenly court pleading his case (see Luke 12:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 57, 'In their rage, the members of the Sanhedrin council forego any reasoned discussion of Stephen’s fate. In ironic attention to the law Stephen has just accused them of ignoring (v. 53), they immediately take him out of the city and stone him (Lev. 24:14). The witnesses, who were required to cast the first stones (Deut. 17:6–7), leave their outer garments at the feet of Saul, who will be the focus of much of the rest of Acts (chaps. 9; 13–28) and who will recall this scene with pain (22:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 7, 60, 'Stephen’s final words of prayer mirror those of Jesus (Luke 23:34, 46). he fell asleep. For more on DEATH as sleep, see Dan. 12:2; John 11:11–14; 1 Thess. 4:13–14. For more on DEATH, see Eccl. 9:5–6; Rom. 6:23.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, NULL, '8:1a This darkest of moments harbors hope as we are introduced to the man who will become the greatest of Christian missionaries—the Apostle Paul (see 7:58).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, 5, 'Philip. The Philip of this story is not the apostle Philip (1:13; John 1:43–46). All we know of him comes to us from Acts 6:1–7 (where he is one of the seven deacons), this passage, and Acts 21:8–9. In 21:8–9 he is “Philip the evangelist,” has settled in Caesarea (see 8:40), and has four daughters who prophesied. the city of Samaria. The identity is uncertain (see translation note). It may be Sebaste (the former Samaria), Shechem, Gitta, or Sychar. Samaritans were continuously at odds with the Jews (see John 4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, 9, 'Simon. A successful practitioner of magical arts, Simon literally made a name for himself—the great power of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, 18, 'Simon offers money to be able to convey the Spirit (vv. 18–19). Peter condemns him and calls him to repent (vv. 20–23). Simon responds by requesting prayer (v. 24), though the outcome of that request is not clear.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, 27, 'a man of Ethiopia, a eunuch. Philip is directed to minister to an important official from Ethiopia (the ancient kingdom of Meroë on the upper Nile), thought of as “the end of the earth” (see 1:8). The Ethiopian is a man of prestige, influence, wealth, education (he is reading from a scroll), and piety (having come to worship; vv. 27–28). Candace is a title for the queen mother, not a personal name. The story foreshadows the mission to the Gentiles and God’s plan to include all people; it also fulfills prophecy (Ps. 68:31; Zeph. 3:10). African Christianity did not begin with missionaries of the colonial era but traces its roots to the time of the apostles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, 29, 'Philip is directed to the Ethiopian’s chariot (probably part of a larger caravan) and hears him reading from Is. 53:7–8 (in ancient times, it was usual to read aloud).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, 38, 'baptized. The NT knows only one form of Christian water baptism and that is by immersion: They went down into the water and, afterwards, came up out of the water. For more on BAPTISM see Rom. 6:3–8. Philip’s arrival seems to have been by natural means (vv. 26–28), but his departure is supernatural since the Spirit of the Lord caught Philip away.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 8, 40, 'Azotus. A city near the Mediterranean coast between Gaza and Joppa. Caesarea is some 60 miles (100 km) further north (28:8–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 1, 'This is the first of three tellings of the story of the conversion and call of Saul of Tarsus in Acts (see also 22:1–21; 26:1–23). In addition, Paul reflects on this important event in his own letters (1 Cor. 15:8–10; Gal. 1:11–17; Phil. 3:4–14). Aside from the story of Jesus Himself, it is arguably the most important narrative in all of the NT. Through the intervention of God, the great persecutor of Christians becomes the great champion of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 1, 'Saul, having been successful in his work of terrorizing Christians in Jerusalem (8:1–4), extends his work to the city of Damascus in Syria, under the auspices of the high priest, Caiaphas (see note on 4:5–6). Damascus was some 150 miles (240 km) NE of Jerusalem. To reach it would have required several days of travel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 3, 'Near the end of the trip, Saul is confronted by the One whose followers he is persecuting. On his way to take prisoners whose names he does not know, he becomes the prisoner of One who knows his name and calls him by it. Jesus identifies with His followers in asking, “Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting Me?” (v. 4), showing His awareness of their trials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 10, 'The risen Jesus gives very specific directions to His disciple, Ananias, instructing him to lay healing hands on Saul. Ananias resists the commission because he has heard of Saul’s ferocious persecution of Christians. The Lord foretells Saul’s career as a witness to the Christian gospel, including his testimony before kings, fulfilled in Paul’s later testimony before Agrippa (26:1–32) and Caesar in Rome (25:11–12; 28:19; see note on 25:13–26:32). The street called Straight (v. 11) is probably to be identified with the long street that still today runs from east to west through Damascus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 17, 'In this sacred scene, we watch as the risen Lord brings Saul and Ananias together. Ananias begins the story convinced that Saul is evil and ends it with his hands of healing on Saul’s head. Saul begins the story as a sworn enemy of the Way and ends the story himself a follower of it. These are case studies of God’s transforming grace, which is still active today!');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 23, 'many days. Likely an extended period of some three years, which Paul spent in Arabia (see Gal. 1:17–18), probably doing early evangelistic work in Nabatean Arabia, of which Petra was the capital city. Paul’s escape from Damascus (v. 25) occurs at the end of this time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 25, 'See 2 Cor. 11:32–33, where Paul himself tells the story of his escape.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 27, 'Barnabas has already made an appearance in Acts (4:36–37) and will become a fellow missionary of Paul (see 11:25–26; chaps. 13–14; 15:36–41). Here he plays the crucial role of championing Saul within the Christian community in Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 32, 'Having introduced Saul of Tarsus, Luke here turns to the important role Peter plays in the beginnings of Gentile Christianity, a story told in five segments: (1) Peter’s healing of Aeneas and Dorcas (9:32–43); (2) the story of Cornelius (10:1–48); (3) Peter’s ministry to Cornelius affirmed at Jerusalem (11:1–18); (4) Antioch as a Christian center (11:19–30); and (5) Herod Agrippa I and the church (12:1–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 32, 'Lydda. 11 miles (18 km) SE of Joppa; a major seaport for Judea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 35, 'Sharon. Refers to the plain of Sharon, extending along the coast from Joppa to Mount Carmel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 9, 36, 'Healing a paralyzed man (vv. 32–35) was a great miracle, but raising Tabitha (Aramaic, Dorcas) from the dead was even more astounding and resulted in many conversions. It is apparently on this same stay in Joppa that Peter experiences the vision of animals in a great sheet (10:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 1, 'The story of Cornelius is very important. It is told with considerable narrative skill. It is unfolded in seven scenes and one “echo”: 10:1–8; 10:9–16; 10:17–23a; 10:23b–33; 10:34–43; 10:44–48; 11:1–18; 15:7–11. The story does not mean that OT dietary laws have been abolished (for more on HEALTH, see Gen. 1:29–30; Lev. 11:1–47; 1 Cor. 10:31). Peter believes the OT dietary counsel to be from God (Acts 10:14); yet the divine voice tells him, “Rise, Peter; kill and eat” and “What God has cleansed you must not call common” (10:13–15). That tension sparks the true insight: “God has shown me that I should not call any man common or unclean” (10:28). What changes is not Peter’s diet, but the people with whom he is willing to associate. He now understands that “God shows no partiality” and that Jesus Christ is “Lord of all” (10:34–36). And this is precisely the point on which he is later questioned: “You went in to uncircumcised men and ate with them!” (11:3). They do not question what he ate but with whom he ate. In response, Peter again summarizes what he learned on the rooftop: “Then the Spirit told me to go with them [the servants of Cornelius], doubting nothing” (11:12; see notes on Matt. 15:11; Mark 7:15–23; and 1 Tim. 4:1–5).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 1, 'The story of Cornelius is very important. It is told with considerable narrative skill. It is unfolded in seven scenes and one “echo”: 10:1–8; 10:9–16; 10:17–23a; 10:23b–33; 10:34–43; 10:44–48; 11:1–18; 15:7–11. The story does not mean that OT dietary laws have been abolished (for more on HEALTH, see Gen. 1:29–30; Lev. 11:1–47; 1 Cor. 10:31). Peter believes the OT dietary counsel to be from God (Acts 10:14); yet the divine voice tells him, “Rise, Peter; kill and eat” and “What God has cleansed you must not call common” (10:13–15). That tension sparks the true insight: “God has shown me that I should not call any man common or unclean” (10:28). What changes is not Peter’s diet, but the people with whom he is willing to associate. He now understands that “God shows no partiality” and that Jesus Christ is “Lord of all” (10:34–36). And this is precisely the point on which he is later questioned: “You went in to uncircumcised men and ate with them!” (11:3). They do not question what he ate but with whom he ate. In response, Peter again summarizes what he learned on the rooftop: “Then the Spirit told me to go with them [the servants of Cornelius], doubting nothing” (11:12; see notes on Matt. 15:11; Mark 7:15–23; and 1 Tim. 4:1–5).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 1, 'Cornelius was a centurion, the commander of a Roman “centuria,” implying a group of 100 soldiers (though records suggest the typical number was around 80). Centurions were the professional soldiers of the Roman army and are mentioned positively in the NT (27:1–6; Matt. 8:5–13; Mark 15:39; Luke 7:2–5). Caesarea. See note on 23:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 3, 'ninth hour. See note on 3:1–2. angel of God. The Dead Sea Scrolls indicate that a common belief among some faithful Jews was that angels would not associate with Gentiles; thus, the willingness of an angel to come to Cornelius and address him is significant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 9, 'housetop. The roof of the home would likely have been flat, constructed of clay, branches, and wooden beams. It was accessed by an outside stairway. sixth hour. Noon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 10, 'God brings Peter into an altered state of consciousness, a trance (compare 22:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 14, 'common or unclean. The “great sheet” (v. 11) may have contained both clean and unclean animals (see Lev. 11), with the clean animals then judged as common by association with the unclean animals.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 16, 'three times. The repetition reinforces the lesson. It is interesting that the number of repetitions of the vision matches the number of messengers that arrive momentarily from Cornelius (v. 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 23, 'invited them in. That Peter would invite them to stay in Simon’s home (v. 6) suggests that his practice is already changing, for such close contact with non-Jews would have been frowned upon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 30, 'Cornelius retells the vision, related first in vv. 3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 47, 'For more on BAPTISM, see 8:36–39; Matt. 28:19–20; John 3:5; Rom. 6:3–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 1, 'Peter was not alone in affirming the important new direction for Christian mission in preaching to Gentiles. The church at Jerusalem questioned him closely (vv. 1–3), listened to his story (vv. 4–17), and affirmed that it was God who was behind it all (v. 18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 19, 'the persecution. See 8:1b–4. Phoenicia. A region on the coast north of Caesarea. Cyprus. An island off the coast (see 13:4–12). Antioch. A coastal city in Syria.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 20, 'Hellenists. The mission expanded beyond “the Jews only” (v. 19) to the Hellenists, or Gentiles (compare 6:1, where Hellenists refers to Greek-speaking Jews). These missionaries were originally from Cyprus (see v. 20) and Cyrene (a port in N Africa).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 22, 'Barnabas. See note on 4:36–37. He, characteristically, encourages the new converts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 25, 'Barnabas goes to Saul’s (Paul’s) hometown of Tarsus (to the NW of Antioch) and recruits him to help at Antioch. The term Christians joins a Greek word (“Christos”), which translates a Hebrew concept (“Messiah”), with a Latin ending (-ians), illustrating the widening Christian mission.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 27, 'prophets. The first mention of prophets or prophecy in Acts (see 13:1; 15:32; 19:6). Agabus. See 21:9–11. Claudius was Roman emperor (A.D. 41–54).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 29, 'Judea. Compare the visits by Paul to Judea and Jerusalem described in Gal. 1:11–2:10. See note on Acts 15:1–35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 1, 'Another set of stories—this one about Herod Agrippa I—illustrates God’s leading in the midst of persecution. An introduction (vv. 1–3) gives way to the story of Peter’s imprisonment and release (vv. 4–19) and the story of Herod’s death (vv. 20–23), which contrasts with the advance of the church (v. 24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 1, 'Herod the king. Herod Agrippa I, the grandson of Herod the Great (see Matt. 2:1–12) and son of Aristobulus. He was nephew to Herod Antipas who executed John the Baptist (Matt. 14:1–12) and participated in the trials of Jesus (Luke 23:6–12). After his grandfather (Herod the Great) killed his father (Aristobulus), he was brought up in Rome among the emperor’s family and was favored by emperors Caligula (A.D. 37–41) and Claudius (A.D. 41–54). His son, Agrippa II, is a central character in 25:13–26:32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 2, 'James the brother of John. One of the twelve (Matt. 4:21–22) and one of Jesus’ inner circle (Peter, James, and John).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 3, 'Days of Unleavened Bread. The feast following the Passover and lasting for seven days (Ex. 12:17–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 4, 'The story of Peter’s imprisonment and release is told with interesting detail and humor. Note that Peter sleeps soundly on the night before his scheduled execution. Herod’s extreme security measures (four squads of soldiers, v. 4; bound with two chains between two soldiers, v. 6; first and second guard posts, v. 10; etc.) only heighten the wonder of Peter’s miraculous release.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 12, 'John … Mark. See note on 15:36–41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 15, 'his angel. This incident may reflect the idea that each person has a “guardian angel” (Matt. 18:10; Heb. 1:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 16, 'astonished. We identify with those early believers, who pray earnestly for Peter’s release but struggle to understand that their prayers have been answered.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 17, 'James. This is not James, one of the twelve (he had been executed, v. 2), but James the brother of Jesus (see 15:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 20, 'Herod (see note on v. 1) is struck down for failing to praise God (A.D. 44); see note on 5:2. Paul and Barnabas provide a contrast in refusing blasphemous worship (14:13–18). Herod dies because of ignoring God’s sovereignty; the church flourishes because they acknowledge it. With those early Christians, we should pray, “Lord, You are God …” (4:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 12, 25, 'Barnabas and Saul return from the visit to Jerusalem mentioned in 11:28–30. John … Mark. See note on 15:36–41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 1, 'Here, Luke’s story shifts to highlight Antioch as a base of Christian mission and Paul as missionary hero. Barnabas. See note on 4:36–37. Herod the tetrarch. Herod Antipas (see note on 4:27). Saul of Tarsus, who would become the Apostle Paul. Of the others we know only what is mentioned here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 2, 'As they ministered to the Lord and fasted. Worship and fasting precede the Spirit’s directive. Fasting and prayer follow it. This is a crucial moment in Luke’s story—the beginning of Paul’s missionary journeys. There may be a lesson here: the success of missionary endeavors may have something to do with how they begin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 4, 'The story of Paul’s first missionary journey (ca. A.D. 45–47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 4, 'Barnabas, who was a native of the island (4:36), and Saul go to Cyprus. In Salamis, on the east of the island, they begin the pattern of preaching in the synagogues of the Jews. John. John Mark; see note on 15:36–41.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 6, 'Paphos. A city on the west side of Cyprus. Here the missionaries had a confrontation with a sorcerer and false prophet, Bar-Jesus or Elymas, a counselor to the proconsul (or governor) of the island, Sergius Paulus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 9, 'Note the switch from the apostle’s Jewish name, Saul, to his Roman one, Paul.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 11, 'you shall be blind. Paul, who himself had been struck blind (9:1–9), announced that Elymas would become temporarily blind. When this occurs, the proconsul believes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 13, 'Leaving Cyprus, the missionary party sails NW to the mainland. Note that Luke now portrays Paul as in the lead (Paul and his party). John (John Mark) who had been assisting them (v. 5), leaves, an act Paul viewed as desertion and which later leads to a split with Barnabas (see note on 15:36–41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 14, 'This story of the ministry of Paul and Barnabas in Pisidian Antioch reveals a pattern of evangelistic work that is often reflected in Paul’s future ministry: (1) Paul and Barnabas begin by preaching in the synagogue on the Sabbath (vv. 14–41); (2) they experience success and opposition (vv. 42–45); (3) they leave the synagogue and focus on the Gentiles (vv. 46–49); (4) while they experience further success, opposition incited by the Jews leads to their departure (vv. 50–51); and (5) they leave behind a church strengthened by the Holy Spirit (v. 52).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 14, 'the Sabbath day. The Sabbath is mentioned several other times in Acts (vv. 27, 42, 44; 1:12; 15:21; 16:13; 17:2; 18:4). Acts refers repeatedly to Paul’s practice of worshiping and witnessing in the synagogue on the Sabbath (here, vv. 42, 44; 16:13; 18:4; and esp. see note on 17:2). Some would dismiss these occurrences as simply reflecting missionary strategy. However, they identify the established practice of Paul and his churches and, even more importantly, they mirror his profound commitment to worship God as the Creator. Early Christians use the words of the Sabbath commandment in prayer (4:24; compare Ex. 20:8–11) and Paul draws on its language and ideas in preaching to pagans in Lystra (Acts 14:15) and Athens (17:24). For more on the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 16, 'Then Paul … said. Speeches play an important role in Acts. See following chart: “Speeches in Acts.” Nearly 30 speeches comprise about a third of the book, with 11 of these given by Paul. The speech here is Paul’s first and longest. It has important similarities to Peter’s speech at Pentecost and Stephen’s speech. The only one of Paul’s speeches given in a synagogue, it seems intended by Luke to illustrate synagogue preaching.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 16, 'In the first part of the speech, Paul gives a sketch of Jewish history to the time of David.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 13, 23, 'In this second portion of the speech, Paul moves from King David to a Savior—Jesus (v. 23). He shares the testimony of John the Baptist (vv. 24–25) and tells the story of Jesus’ death, burial, resurrection, and post-resurrection appearances (vv. 26–31), using evidence from the Psalms to contrast David’s death and Jesus’ resurrection (vv. 32–37).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 1, 16, 'Jerusalem
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 2, 14, 'Jerusalem
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 3, 12, 'Jerusalem
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 5, 29, 'Jerusalem
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 10, 34, 'Caesarea
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 11, 5, 'Jerusalem
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 14, 1, 'Iconium. The story of ministry here is told in enough detail to see the emerging pattern—preaching in the synagogue, success, persecution, departure (see note on 13:14–52).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 14, 4, 'apostles. Note that both Paul and Barnabas are referred to as apostles (see also v. 14; Rom. 16:7; Gal. 1:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 14, 6, 'Lystra (vv. 8–20a) and Derbe (v. 20b) appear to have had little Jewish influence. With the story of Paul’s ministry in Athens (17:16–34), the story of Paul’s ministry in these two cities (chap. 14) illustrates his approach when addressing a pagan audience (see note on vv. 14–18). Lycaonia. Refers here to a portion of the Roman province of Galatia. Paul later returns to the area (16:1–5; 18:23). Timothy was from the region (16:1–3). The Epistle to the Galatians may have been written to the Christians here (see Introduction to Galatians).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 14, 8, 'In this pagan setting, the healing of a man crippled from birth (vv. 8–10) leads the pagan population to arrange a sacrifice to Paul and Barnabas because they believe them to be the gods Zeus and Hermes (vv. 11–13). Among Greek deities, Hermes, the son of Zeus, was considered the spokesman or the messenger of the gods. Thus, the crowd in their excitement viewed Paul as speaking on behalf of all gods, including Zeus, the king of gods.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 14, 14, 'Paul and Barnabas refuse the arranged worship and offer an energized speech exhorting their pagan hearers to turn from these useless things to the living God, who made the heaven, the earth, the sea, and all things that are in them. Drawing on the language and concepts of the Sabbath commandment, Paul and Barnabas accent the theme of God as the Creator of all things and the need to turn from false gods to serve this one, true God (see notes on 17:22–31; see also 4:23–24; 13:14; 19:24–27). Their ministry here models the universal, end-time message of Rev. 14:6–7. In its major points, the speech seems to draw on Ps. 146:3, 5–7. For more on the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 14, 19, 'they stoned Paul. In a radical swing of public opinion, the citizens of Lystra, under the influence of Jews from Antioch (13:13–52) and Iconium (14:1–7), stone Paul and leave him for dead (see 2 Cor. 11:25). However, he revives and the missionary team continues its work in Derbe.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 1, 'A group from Judea comes to Antioch and claims that circumcision is necessary for salvation. To solve the resulting controversy, a delegation is appointed to visit Jerusalem (vv. 1–2). Much of the passage tells the story of the delegation’s trip to Jerusalem, the meeting there (often referred to as the Jerusalem council), and their return (vv. 3–35). This is a crucial story. What is to be required of Gentiles who wish to become Christians? (Compare Gal. 2:1–10, which is often regarded as another account of the Jerusalem council. Alternatively, it may describe Paul’s visit to Jerusalem summarized in Acts 11:29–30.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 3, 'The delegation from Antioch is received warmly during their journey and when they reach Jerusalem. Their detractors are a minor, dissenting part of the church there (vv. 1, 5, 24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 6, 'Peter. Early in the meeting, Peter recalls his ministry to the household of Cornelius and the lessons he learned (10:1–11:18). He argues that God, who is the expert in knowing human hearts (1:24), has already settled the issue by giving them the Holy Spirit, by making no distinction between us and them, and by purifying their hearts by faith. All—Jews and Gentiles alike—are saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 13, 'James (the brother of Jesus and leader of the Christian community in Jerusalem; see note on 12:17) offers his authoritative opinion (vv. 13–18) and decision about what should be done (vv. 19–21). Clearly the council decides that Gentiles do not need to become Jewish converts, obeying all the aspects of the ceremonial law—including circumcision—in order to become Christians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 20, 'Gentile believers were to abstain from things polluted by (or “offered to”) idols, from sexual immorality, from things strangled, and from blood (see v. 29; 21:25). What is the purpose of these prohibitions? They seem to focus on the need to foster Gentile-Jewish fellowship, especially between Christian Gentiles and non-Christian Jews. Following these four rules will help Gentiles and Jews to maintain fellowship. Two observations are helpful: (1) The prohibitions are modeled on the laws that pertain to both Jews and “the strangers who dwell among you” in Lev. 17–18. The Jerusalem council seems to adopt this OT model, arguing that following these rules will help non-Jews live and worship without offense among their Jewish neighbors. (2) All four items listed are associated with pagan temples. Abstaining from these—and steering clear of pagan temples—will make it obvious that Gentile Christian believers have turned from idolatry to worship the one, true God (see note on 17:22–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 21, 'Sabbath. On the Sabbath in Acts, see note on 13:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 22, 'Barsabas. Means “Son of the Sabbath.” He may have been related to Joseph Barsabas (1:23). Silas. Known as Silvanus in Paul’s letters, he accompanies Paul on his second missionary journey (15:40–18:22) and is listed as a co-author of the letters to Thessalonica (1 Thess. 1:1; 2 Thess. 1:1). See vv. 32–34.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 29, 'See note on v. 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 32, 'See note on v. 22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 36, 'John called Mark. John Mark, a relative of Barnabas (Col. 4:10) and author of the second Gospel, left early in their last missionary journey (13:13; see also 12:12, 25). Even disagreements between Christian leaders further the gospel, since two missionary teams are now formed, with Paul recruiting Silas (15:22) to accompany him. Paul eventually changes his opinion and requests John Mark’s presence because “he is useful to me for ministry” (2 Tim. 4:11; compare Col. 4:10; Philem. 24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 15, 40, 'Paul’s second missionary journey (ca. A.D. 49–52), during which the gospel is carried to cities on the Aegean Sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 1, 'Timothy. While revisiting churches at Lystra and Derbe founded on Paul’s first missionary journey (14:6–21), Timothy is added to the missionary team. He becomes one of Paul’s most trusted companions and representatives (see, e.g., 1 Cor. 4:17; Phil. 2:19–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 4, 'See note on 15:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 6, 'The newly formed missionary team of Paul, Silas, and Timothy (15:40–16:3) move NW across Asia Minor. They attempt to go west, into the Roman province of Asia (probably to evangelize Ephesus), but are forbidden by the Holy Spirit. Next they attempt to turn NE, with the same result. Luke does not elaborate on just how the Spirit expressed guidance. By default, they arrive at Troas, a seaport on the Aegean Sea (see also 20:5–12; 2 Cor. 2:12–13; 2 Tim. 4:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 9, 'In Troas, Paul sees in a vision a man of Macedonia requesting help. we. Luke, “the beloved physician” (Col. 4:14), has been describing the missionary endeavors in the third person (“he,” “they”). Now he adopts the first person, apparently indicating that he has joined the missionary team (see Introduction, “Author”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 12, 'Philippi. Described as a colony. Located on the Via Egnatia, the major road from Rome to the eastern provinces, it became a Roman colony in 42 B.C. As a colony, it had been granted self-government and other privileges, had many retired Roman legionnaires living there, and was considered to be a part of Italy or Rome (see Phil. 3:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 13, 'The meeting place suggests the Jewish community was not large. (It took ten men to form a synagogue.) On the Sabbath in Acts, see notes on 4:23–24; 13:14; 14:14–18. For more on the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 14, 'Lydia. A businesswoman who becomes the first convert in Philippi. She worshiped God, likely meaning that she was a Gentile who had been attracted to worshiping the one, true God on the Sabbath.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 16, 'divination … fortune-telling. Commonplace in the first century, which was dominated by an astrological worldview. Paul becomes greatly annoyed, probably because the interruptions and the association with demonic activity threatened to limit the proclamation of the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 19, 'The magistrates, responding to the slave owners and the mob, have Paul and Silas beaten with rods (see 2 Cor. 11:24–25) and thrown into prison.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 25, 'Paul and Silas set a great example for believers experiencing difficult times. They are praying and singing hymns to God and God seems to take this praise as reason for a miraculous earthquake which frees all the prisoners.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 27, 'If prisoners escaped, the jailer’s life would have been forfeited (12:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 30, 'What had appeared to be a great defeat—being imprisoned—becomes an opportunity to preach the gospel and to win the jailer and his household to faith in Jesus.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 33, 'baptized. Note the necessity of belief (v. 31) before baptism. Compare 2:38. For more on BAPTISM, see Rom. 6:3–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 16, 35, 'The magistrates send word to release Paul and Silas and send them away, but Paul replies with a message telling them he and Silas are Roman citizens (compare 22:25) and demanding they come personally and release them (perhaps to ensure more respectful treatment of the believers they will leave behind). The magistrates do so and Paul and Silas agree to depart from Philippi.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 1, 'Thessalonica. A large city, which was the capital of the province of Macedonia.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 2, 'as his custom was. Paul’s custom or habit, like that of Jesus Himself, was to worship in the synagogue on the Sabbath (see Luke 4:16). For the importance of Creation theology and the Sabbath in Acts, see the notes on vv. 22–31; 4:23–24; 13:14; 14:14–18. For more on the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 5, 'Jason seems to have been hosting Paul and Silas in his home. The descriptions of Paul and Silas as having turned the world upside down (v. 6) and preaching that there is another king—Jesus (v. 7) inspire us to more dedicated witness of Christ as the true King of all.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 10, 'Berea. Located about 50 miles (80 km) west of Thessalonica. The now familiar pattern plays itself out—Paul and Silas preach in the synagogue; they experience success, winning converts to the gospel; persecution follows; they depart (see note on 13:14–52).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 16, 'Paul’s ministry in Athens. Once politically powerful, Athens had dwindled to a city of some 25,000 inhabitants that looked back on its golden age. Forested with temples, Athens suffered from too many gods. One ancient writer quipped that it was easier to find a god in Athens than a man (see v. 16). However, the city could still claim to be the cultural heart of the Greco-Roman world and Paul seems eager to confront this citadel of pagan worldview. Again we watch Paul evangelize in a largely pagan setting (see note on 14:14–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 19, 'Paul’s marketplace preaching (v. 17) catches the ear of philosophers and leads to an invitation for him to address the Areopagus, the city council of Athens (the term also referred to a rocky hill in Athens, Mars Hill).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 22, 'In his speech before the council, Paul compliments their piety and he refers to an altar inscribed TO THE UNKNOWN GOD. He declares he has come to speak about that God. Paraphrasing the language and concepts of the Sabbath commandment (Ex. 20:8–11), Paul announces God to be the Creator who—unlike the gods so prevalent in Athens—cannot be contained by temples nor needs the service of priests and priestesses (Acts 17:24–25). Paul uses citations from their own, ancient poets (Epimenides, Aratus, Cleanthes) to underline the point that God as Creator is the Source of all human life (vv. 26–28; compare 14:15). He argues against worshiping idols: How can we, who are created by God, create gods and worship them? (see v. 29; note on 19:24–27; compare Is. 44:9–20). Through the speech he has shown the beginning of the human story—Creation. As he concludes, he highlights the end of the human story—God’s appointed day for judgment and the determinative role of Jesus (Acts 17:30–31). Even here, Paul witnesses boldly about Jesus. For more on the centrality of JUDGMENT, see Eccl. 12:13–14; Rom. 14:10, 12; Heb. 9:27; Rev. 14:6–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 17, 32, 'The resurrection is a sticking point for his audience and Paul’s departure seems premature. However, a few converts were won by the speech, including at least one member of the council, Dionysius the Areopagite.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 1, 'Corinth. The bustling capital of the province of Achaia and a Roman colony. It was one of the largest cities of the Roman Empire. Located near the 4.5-mile-wide (7 km) isthmus connecting central Greece with the Peloponnese, the city controlled commerce moving by land, north to south, and by sea, east to west (small ships were transported across on wooden rollers; larger ships were unloaded and their cargo portaged across the isthmus). Like other seaports of the time, Corinth was known for its immorality. Paul wrote two letters, 1 and 2 Corinthians, to believers in the city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 2, 'Aquila … Priscilla. They would become Paul’s valued coworkers (vv. 18, 26; Rom. 16:3; 2 Tim. 4:19). The ancient historian Suetonius writes that Claudius expelled the Jews from Rome because of tumult among them “at the instigation of Chrestus,” often taken as referring to controversy caused by the preaching of Christ (“Christos,” in Greek).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 4, 'every Sabbath. On the Sabbath in Acts, see note on 17:2. For more on the SABBATH, see Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 8, 'Crispus. Paul himself baptized Crispus (1 Cor. 1:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 9, 'Paul seems to lose his courage and may have been tempted to mute his witness to Jesus. The Lord appears to him in a vision and encourages him. Paul takes the promise to heart and remains for 18 months.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 12, 'The promise (vv. 9–10) was tested when Paul was dragged before Gallio, a noted Roman jurist. An inscription found at Delphi mentions him and allows this incident to be dated to ca. A.D. 51–52.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 14, 'Luke regards Gallio’s opinion exonerating Christianity as an important legal precedent (see note on 19:35–41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 17, 'Sosthenes. This could be the same man mentioned as a Christian in 1 Cor. 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 18, 'Cenchrea. East of Corinth, it served as its seaport on the Saronic Gulf and had its own Christian congregation (Rom. 16:1). vow. Probably a temporary Nazirite vow (Num. 6:1–21), with the shaving of the head marking its end. Compare Acts 21:23–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 19, 'Ephesus. See 19:1. Paul had been trying to get to Ephesus for some time (see 16:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 23, 'Paul’s third missionary journey (ca. A.D. 53–58).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 18, 24, 'Luke takes a brief break from discussing Paul’s ministry to highlight the work of Apollos, whose knowledge about Christianity was expanded by his contact with Aquila and Priscilla. Alexandria, Apollos’s hometown, was in Egypt. It was the second largest city in the Roman Empire and had a large Jewish population. The presence of Apollos in Achaia (the area of which Corinth was capital, v. 27; 19:1) is alluded to by Paul in his letters (1 Cor. 1:12; 3:1–23; 16:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 1, 'Ephesus. Perhaps the fourth largest city in the Roman Empire (behind Rome, Alexandria, and Antioch), it was the capital of the Roman province of Asia and the commercial center for the richest region of the empire. It was also the center for worship of the goddess Artemis (known by the Romans as Diana, 19:27). some disciples. When Paul arrives, he finds some disciples (v. 1; about twelve in number, v. 7) who, like Apollos (18:24–26), had an incomplete understanding, having not been baptized in Jesus’ name nor having received the Holy Spirit. Paul re-baptizes them into this fuller understanding (see Introduction, “Message”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 8, 'Paul preaches in the synagogue longer than some other places (compare 17:1–4). When opposition arises, he teaches in the school of Tyrannus for two years, leading to widespread knowledge about Jesus in the Roman province of Asia.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 11, 'The evangelistic methods that God uses in Ephesus may seem strange—healings by handkerchiefs or aprons (v. 12) and fleeing exorcists (vv. 13–16)—but they were effective. The exorcists attempted to use the name of Jesus to exorcise an evil spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 17, 'In view of the dramatic failure of the exorcists (vv. 13–17), the citizens of Ephesus learned not to trifle with the name of Jesus (see 4:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 19, 'The superiority of Jesus to all other gods was so clear that many of those who had practiced magic—for which Ephesus was well known—cast their expensive magic manuals in a fire. Since the coin referred to here, the drachma, was worth a day’s wage for a working person, 50,000 of these pieces of silver represented a very large sum of money.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 21, 'The exciting story of the riot in Ephesus is testimony to the success of Paul’s proclamation in winning the population over to the worship of the Creator (vv. 23–41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 21, 'Rome. The first indication of Paul’s ultimate destination (28:11–31). Timothy. See note on 16:1–3. Erastus. May be the Christian who was “the treasurer of the city” of Corinth (Rom. 16:23; 2 Tim. 4:20). An inscription may refer to him (“Erastus, commissioner of public works, bore the expense of this pavement”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 23, 'the Way. A name for the Christian message (19:9, 23; 22:4; 24:22) or community (9:2; 24:14). Christians follow Jesus as “the Way” to eternal life (see John 14:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 24, 'Diana (the goddess’s Roman name), or “Artemis” (Greek), was an important fertility goddess. A large temple in Ephesus, one of the wonders of the ancient world, was headquarters for her worship. Annual festivals, which worshiped Diana/Artemis, brought wealth to the city (v. 35). Demetrius, in a telling summary, says that Paul preaches that they are not gods which are made with hands (v. 26). Paul’s preaching elsewhere (see notes on 14:14–18; 17:22–31) confirms the accuracy of this summary and mirrors the conviction of all early Christians (see notes on 4:23–24) that God is the Creator of all things and so is worthy of worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 29, 'theater. In Ephesus, this was large, accommodating 24,000. Gaius. Probably not the same as the man mentioned in 20:4 (see also Rom. 16:23; 1 Cor. 1:14; 3 John 1). Aristarchus. See 20:4, 6; 27:2; Col. 4:10; Philem. 24. He was a coworker, travel companion, and sometime fellow-prisoner of Paul.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 31, 'Paul seems to have followed this counsel and did not go to the theater.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 33, 'Alexander. His role in the story demonstrates that the riot was a clash between polytheism (the worship of many gods, idolatry) and monotheism (the worship of one, true God, the Creator; see note on vv. 24–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 19, 35, 'As in the earlier story about Corinth (18:12–17), the charges against believers are dismissed and Christianity is exonerated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 1, 'Paul’s itinerary for this new trip and the need for a large delegation (v. 4) are due in large part to “the collection for the saints” (1 Cor. 16:1), an important famine relief project for believers in Judea that consumed much of Paul’s time and attention (Acts 24:17; Rom. 15:25–28; 1 Cor. 16:1–4; 2 Cor. 8:1–9:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 4, 'The men mentioned seem to be “messengers of the churches” (2 Cor. 8:23) or delegates who accompany the collection to Jerusalem. See the following passages and notes on these travel companions: Sopater (perhaps the same person as Sosipater in Rom. 16:21); Aristarchus (Acts 19:28–34); Secundus (not mentioned elsewhere); Gaius (19:28–34); Timothy (16:1–3); Tychicus (Titus 3:12); Trophimus (21:29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 7, 'At a night gathering and meal in Troas, a young believer, Eutychus, falls asleep and tumbles out of an upper window to his apparent death (v. 9). Paul restores him to life (vv. 10, 12); compare the resurrection of Dorcas by Peter (9:40–42).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 7, 'This passage mentions the first day of the week, as do several others in the NT. Most of these simply identify the first day as the day of Christ’s resurrection (Matt. 28:1; Mark 16:2, 9; Luke 24:1; John 20:1, 19; see note on 1 Cor. 16:2). This is the only explicit reference in the NT to Christians actually gathering on the first day of the week. In Acts, to break bread seems to indicate a meal rather than, specifically, the celebration of the Lord’s Supper (v. 11; 2:42, 46; 27:35; compare Luke 22:14–23; 24:35; 1 Cor. 10:16; 11:20–34).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 16, 'Day of Pentecost. See note on 2:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 17, 'Paul does not have time to visit Ephesus (v. 16), but wishes to bid farewell to the leaders of the congregations there and invites them to Miletus (vv. 15, 17; 2 Tim. 4:20), a port some 35 miles (55 km) south of Ephesus. Paul appointed elders or bishops as leaders in the churches he founded (e.g., 14:23). For their role, see v. 28, 1 Tim. 3:1–7 (written to Timothy while he was at Ephesus); Titus 1:5–9. Paul encourages the elders to follow his own example (see esp. Acts 20:35). While this sounds egotistical to us, a teacher in the first century was expected to cite his own example as a pattern for students. Paul carefully exhorts, “Imitate me, just as I also imitate Christ” (1 Cor. 11:1; see also 1 Cor. 4:16; Phil. 3:17; 4:9; 1 Thess. 1:6; 2 Thess. 3:7, 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 22, 'Paul received warnings that chains and tribulations awaited him in Jerusalem (see 21:4, 10–12). In spite of knowing what was ahead, Paul says he felt called to finish my race (compare 2 Tim. 4:6–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 31, 'three years. Compare 19:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 20, 32, 'Paul had great confidence in the power of God in the lives of believers (Phil. 1:6), and so should we.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 1, 'Paul’s journey to Jerusalem develops a pattern begun in his interview with the elders of Ephesus (20:17–38): prophecies and worries about the persecutions that are ahead for Paul blend with tearful farewells.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 7, 'Ptolemais. Modern Acre, a port on the Mediterranean coast, 35 miles (55 km) south of Tyre (v. 3) and 40 miles (65 km) north of Caesarea (v. 8; see note on 23:23). Philip the evangelist was one of the seven deacons (6:1–6) and was a pioneer evangelist in Samaria (8:4–13) and elsewhere (8:40) and to the Ethiopian official (8:26–40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 10, 'Agabus. A prophet who acts out the prediction others have already given, that Paul would be imprisoned in Jerusalem (20:23; 21:4). As before, Paul shares his sense of calling to continue (v. 13; compare 23:24). His final, determined journey to Jerusalem seems to mirror that of Jesus Himself (Luke 9:21–22, 43–44; 18:31–33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 16, 'Mnason of Cyprus. Mentioned only here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 17, 'This section describes Paul’s final visit in Jerusalem (ca. A.D. 58) in six scenes: (1) his visit to James and the temple (21:17–26); (2) his arrest in the temple (21:27–36); (3) his defense (21:37–22:21); (4) his conversation with the Roman tribune (22:22–29); (5) his hearing before the Jewish council (22:30–23:11); and (6) a murderous conspiracy against him (23:12–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 17, 'Paul’s visit to James and the temple. Paul’s reception in Jerusalem is warm and gracious (vv. 17–19), though tensions emerge in the conversation with James (the brother of Jesus and leader of the church in Jerusalem; 17:17; 15:13–21) and the elders. They seem anxious to match Paul’s missionary accomplishments (v. 20) and relate that their Jewish converts have been informed that Paul teaches all the Jews who are among the Gentiles to forsake Moses (v. 21). James and the elders have already put a plan into place to clarify matters. They propose that Paul join four men who have taken a vow in purification ceremonies in the temple (v. 23) and pay their expenses (v. 24) as a way of demonstrating that he is a law-observant Jew. Apparently without any protest, Paul accepts the plan (v. 26; compare 18:18, where he had done something similar on his own initiative).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 27, 'Paul’s arrest in the temple. Keeping Gentiles out of the inner courts of the temple was taken very seriously. A low fence or balustrade separated the court of the Gentiles from the inner courts accessible only to Jews. At intervals, a prohibition was posted on this fence banning foreigners and warning that “whoever is caught [entering the inner courts] will have himself to blame for his ensuing death.” Jews from the Roman province of Asia (16:6–8; 19:1–7) falsely accuse Paul of taking Trophimus, a Gentile, into the temple; they stir up a mob, which attacks Paul with deadly intent (vv. 27–31a; see 26:21). Paul is rescued by the quick action of the Roman tribune, or commanding officer, who arrests him (vv. 31b–36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 37, 'Paul’s defense. After clarifying his identity (21:37–39), Paul is granted opportunity to defend himself, which he does in Hebrew or Aramaic (21:40). He tells the stories of his early life in Judaism and his persecution of Christians (22:1–5), his call and conversion near Damascus (22:6–16), and of a trance in which the Lord warned him to get out of Jerusalem quickly (22:17–21). The stories hold the crowd’s attention until he mentions his divine commission to go to the Gentiles (v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 21, 37, 'barracks. These were probably part of the Fortress of Antonia, which was connected to the northern side of the temple.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 3, 'Cilicia. A region in SE Asia Minor bordering the Mediterranean Sea. Tarsus. Capital of Cilicia and Paul’s hometown (9:11, 30; 11:25; 21:39). It was strategically located near the Cilician Gates, a major pass, and at the convergence of the Cydnus River and important roads.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 4, 'this Way. See note on 19:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 6, 'The second of three versions of the story of Paul’s call and conversion begins here (vv. 6–16). The first follows on the narrative of Saul’s persecution of the church (see note on 9:1–31) and the last as part of Paul’s defense before Agrippa (26:1–23). The repetition allows Luke to underline the importance of Paul to Christian mission and a favorite theme, the power of the risen Christ. The first account (9:1–19) highlights Saul’s relationship with Ananias and the Christian community; the second here (22:6–16), in Paul’s defense before his fellow Jews, emphasizes his Jewishness; the third (26:1–23) advances the argument that the mission to the Gentiles is of divine origin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 16, 'Arise and be baptized. Compare baptism in relationship to forgiveness with 2:38. For more on BAPTISM, see 8:36–39; Matt. 3:16; Rom. 6:3–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 17, 'praying in the temple. Since the temple was, for Paul, a place of prayer, he would have been unlikely to profane it (21:28). in a trance. Apart from this event and his vision of the risen Christ as part of his call/conversion (see note on v. 6; compare Gal. 1:12, 16; 2:2), Acts records other visions received by Paul: 9:12; 16:9–10; 18:9–10; 23:11; and 27:23–24 (see also 2 Cor. 12:1–7). Luke wants his readers to know that Paul is directly guided by God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 18, 'To Jesus’ warning (v. 18), Paul responds that he is especially credible as a witness to his fellow Jews in Jerusalem (vv. 19–20). Stephen. Paul recaps his role in the death of Stephen (see 7:58; 8:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 21, 'Paul argues that he left Jerusalem and took up the mission to go far from here to the Gentiles in obedience to a divine command. (For other factors contributing to Paul’s departure, see 9:29–31.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 22, 'Paul’s conversation with the Roman tribune. On the role of a centurion (vv. 25–26), see note on 10:1. The scourge used to extract a confession was a whip of leather thongs to which were attached pieces of metal and bone (compare Mark 15:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 22, 30, 'Paul’s hearing before the Jewish council (see note on 4:5–6). Paul cleverly exploits the differences between the Sadducees and the Pharisees, two groups or sects within Judaism represented on the council, on the topic of the resurrection. The Sadducees denied there was a resurrection from the dead and the existence of spiritual beings such as angels and spirits; while the Pharisees—with whom Paul explicitly identifies (23:6)—believed in these (23:8; 4:1–2) and come to Paul’s defense (23:9). The Roman tribune or commanding officer rescues Paul from the resulting commotion (23:10). The Lord reassures Paul in a dream or vision and confirms that his ultimate destination is Rome (23:11; compare 18:9–10; see note on 22:17–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 23, 1, 'the high priest Ananias. See note on 24:1–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 23, 12, 'A murderous conspiracy against Paul. In Acts, God protects Paul through a variety of means: the aid of believers (9:23–25, 30); an earthquake (16:26); Roman citizenship (16:37; 22:25); intervention of officials (18:12–17; 19:31, 35–41); and even immunity from a viper’s venom (28:3–6). Here, as in 9:23–25 and 14:6, Paul is protected by advance warning of a deadly plot against him. The news is shared by his sister’s son (v. 16). Paul arranges for him to tell the story to the commander (vv. 17–22), which triggers preparation for Paul’s transfer to Caesarea accompanied by an escort of overwhelming force (vv. 23–24) and a letter from the commander, Claudius Lysias, to the Roman governor Felix, explaining the situation (vv. 25–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 23, 23, 'Caesarea. The city housed the residence of the governor or procurator and the headquarters of the Roman garrison in Palestine. It was built by Herod the Great on the shores of the Mediterranean and named after Caesar Augustus. Herod equipped the city well with palaces, temples, public buildings, a large amphitheater, an artificial harbor, and an aqueduct. Caesarea was the home of Cornelius (10:1–11:18) and Philip the evangelist (8:40; 21:8) and was frequented by Paul (9:30; 18:22; 21:8–14). The scenes of 23:23–26:32 are sited in Caesarea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 23, 24, 'Felix the governor. Procurator of Judea (A.D. 52 to ca. 59). He and his brother, Pallas, were Greeks and once the slaves of Antonia, the mother of Emperor Claudius. As a result, Claudius favored them. The ancient historian Tacitus said of Felix that “he reveled in cruelty and lust, and wielded the power of a king with the mind of a slave.” He was merciless in crushing opposition and his rule helped fuel the Jewish revolt that began in A.D. 66.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 23, 31, 'Paul’s two-year stay (24:27) in Caesarea (ca. A.D. 58–60) is described in three detailed scenes of which this is the first: (1) before Felix (23:31–24:27); (2) before Festus (25:1–12); and (3) before Festus, Agrippa, and Bernice (25:13–26:32). Throughout, Paul provides an inspiring example as a faithful witness to Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 23, 31, 'Antipatris. About 25 miles (40 km) south of Caesarea, the city functioned as a way station between Caesarea and Jerusalem. Here, the 400 foot soldiers leave Paul in the care of the 70 mounted troops (see v. 23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 23, 34, 'Felix’s reaction to the letter (vv. 26–30) reminds us of Pilate’s attempt to assign the case of Jesus to Herod Antipas (Luke 23:5–12). Since Cilicia is within Felix’s jurisdiction, he must deal with Paul’s case.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 1, 'In the formal hearing before Felix, Paul’s accusers are represented by Ananias the high priest in the company of the elders and Tertullus. Paul had appeared earlier before Ananias (23:2–5), who served as high priest (ca. A.D. 47–58). Tertullus was a professional orator and attorney who probably knew Felix. While his speech (vv. 2–8) is laced with flattery at odds with Felix’s reputation as a harsh ruler (see note on 23:24), it is masterful and presents the charges against Paul in a way that is crafted to gain a hearing in a Roman court.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 10, 'In contrast to Tertullus (vv. 2–8), Paul does not flatter Felix, but simply acknowledges his many years of rule. Paul wisely focuses on the false charge about profaning the temple rather than the accusation that he was “a creator of dissension among all the Jews throughout the world” (v. 5), which would have been more difficult to deny.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 14, 'believing all things. In Acts, Paul repeatedly claims that he has been faithful to Judaism (25:8; 26:22; 28:17), while his critics or enemies claimed otherwise (21:21, 28–29). Paul would not likely make these claims if he had been teaching Gentiles to forsake the Sabbath, as some claim (see note on 20:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 15, 'Compare John 5:28–29; Rev. 20:4–6 on the two resurrections. For more on RESURRECTION, see John 5:28–29; 1 Cor. 15:51–55; 1 Thess. 4:13–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 17, 'to bring alms and offerings. See note on 20:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 22, 'Felix delays decision on the case but the new freedoms he grants Paul suggest that he believes Paul is innocent, something he later confirms (25:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 24, 'Drusilla. Felix’s third wife and the sister of King Agrippa II and Bernice (25:23). Against Jewish law, Drusilla had left her first husband, Azizus, king of Syrian Emesa, for Felix. The story seems to echo that of John the Baptist’s private preaching to Herod concerning his unlawful relationship with Herodias (Mark 6:17–20). We can only admire Paul’s courageous witness to the gospel and be pained at Felix’s response.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 24, 26, 'money would be given him. Felix’s interest in Paul was motivated more by greed than spiritual hunger. That Paul had brought “the collection for the saints” to Jerusalem may have suggested to Felix that he was a person of means (v. 17; see note on 20:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 1, 'Paul before Festus. Porcius Festus (24:27) was procurator or governor of the Roman province of Judea (ca. A.D. 59–62). The ancient author Josephus portrays Festus as a conscientious ruler who struggled to keep order in the unruly province.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 2, 'lay in ambush. Paul’s enemies still hoped to kill him en route to a hearing in Jerusalem (compare 23:12–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 8, 'On Paul’s claim to have not offended against the law of the Jews, see note on 24:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 11, 'I appeal to Caesar. When Festus asks Paul if he will go to Jerusalem to be tried, Paul declares, I appeal to Caesar. Roman citizens had the right to appeal to the emperor in Rome, though Paul’s is unusual because it occurs before a verdict. Paul’s plan to witness in Rome (19:21) had been confirmed by the Lord in a vision (23:11). An appeal to Caesar must have occurred to Paul as a way to pursue this plan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 13, 'Paul before Festus, Agrippa, and Bernice. Paul’s appeal to Caesar left Festus both relieved to pass the difficult case on to Rome, and in a quandary about what to say to the emperor about it (25:26). The arrival of King Agrippa and Bernice gives Festus ready-made advisers. Festus reviews the story with the two in private (25:13–22) before convening a public hearing (25:23–26:32), much of which consists of Paul’s self defense (26:1–23) and his appeal to Agrippa (26:24–29).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 13, 'King Agrippa. King Agrippa II, the son of Agrippa I and great grandson of Herod the Great. In his youth he was a friend of Titus, who would conquer Jerusalem (A.D. 70) and become emperor. He became ruler of northern and eastern Palestine. Bernice. She was both King Agrippa’s sister (as was Drusilla, the wife of Felix; see note on 24:24–25) and his consort, or presumed mistress. She was renowned for her beauty and had been married to two kings. Later, when Titus (just mentioned) returned to Rome after putting down the Jewish Revolt (A.D. 66–70), he took Bernice along as his mistress.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 21, 'Augustus. A Latin term for the emperor meaning “His majesty the emperor.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 25, 23, 'In addition to Festus (see note on vv. 1–12) and Agrippa and Bernice (see note on v. 13), this show trial features the Roman commanders and prominent men of the city. Over against this wealthy, influential audience adorned in their finery, Paul, who is in chains (26:29), must have appeared a study in contrasts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 26, 4, 'Compare 22:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 26, 12, 'The third telling in Acts of Paul’s call and conversion (see notes on 9:1–31; 22:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 26, 24, 'Festus accuses Paul of insanity, perhaps based on Paul’s claim that the Messiah rose from the dead (v. 23). Paul, ever the faithful witness to the gospel, is not daunted by the charge. He sharpens his appeal to Agrippa who responds famously, “You almost persuade me to become a Christian” (v. 28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 26, 30, 'As they depart, the notable people involved in the hearing affirm Paul’s innocence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 1, 'Paul, having appealed to Caesar (25:10–12), is transported to Rome by ship. The passage describes two voyages, the first from Caesarea to Myra (vv. 1–5). The second, a long and dangerous passage, departs Myra and ends in shipwreck at Malta (vv. 6–44). Throughout there are many indications of God’s continuing watchful care over Paul.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 1, 'centurion. See note on 10:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 2, 'Adramyttium. A seaport on the west coast of Asia Minor, more than 100 miles (160 km) north of Ephesus. Paul’s travel companions include Luke (since this is one of the “we” sections of Acts; see Introduction, “Author”) and Aristarchus (see note on 19:29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 6, 'an Alexandrian ship. Alexandria, the large city in Egypt, played a key role in supplying Rome with grain, an ever present challenge for the empire (see 28:11). The mention of four stern anchors with others at the prow (vv. 29–30), a mainsail, and rudder ropes holding dual, oar-like rudders (v. 40), as well as 276 passengers (v. 37) and considerable cargo space (vv. 18, 38), suggests a ship of considerable size and sophistication.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 9, 'the Fast. The Fast, or Day of Atonement, would have been in late September or early October, well into the risky season of travel. The Romans regarded May 27 to Sept. 14 as the safe season for travel by ship. To do so between Sept. 14 and Nov. 11 was risky and after Nov. 11, life-threatening. Paul warns from experience. He elsewhere reported, “Three times I was shipwrecked; a night and a day I have been in the deep” (2 Cor. 11:25; none of this is reported in Acts though some nine prior voyages of his are mentioned).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 14, 'Euroclydon. Also known as a northeaster, a type of violent storm on the Mediterranean Sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 16, 'Paul’s ship gains brief respite near Clauda, a small island south of Crete, time which the crew uses to secure the skiff or lifeboat, to stretch cables or ropes to strengthen the hull, and to take down some sail. Syrtis Sands. Two shallow gulfs on the coast of Africa (off modern Libya and Tunisia), greatly feared for their destructive rocks and sandbanks.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 19, 'ship’s tackle. This may have included both essential fittings (sails, etc.) and other equipment. Compare v. 38, where the wheat is jettisoned.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 20, 'The absence of sun and stars means that the ship had no navigation. Advancing desperation culminates in a total loss of hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 21, 'Paul encourages his shipmates with a heartening prophecy based on a divine revelation—there will be no loss of life. In the darkest moment, when all human help is past, God enters the chaos and brings hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 27, 'Adriatic Sea. In ancient times, the Adriatic Sea referred to the sea between Italy, Greece, Crete, and Malta.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 28, 'twenty fathoms. About 120 feet (37 m). fifteen fathoms. About 90 feet (27 m).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 30, 'Confidence in God’s revelation (vv. 23–24) does not keep Paul from taking preventive action to keep the crew from deserting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 33, 'Paul, who had ministered to the sagging spirits of his shipmates (vv. 21–26), now encourages them to eat, offering thanks to God for the nourishment. Here Paul models two important survival skills for the storms of life: (1) caring for one’s physical needs, and (2) giving thanks to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 40, 'See note on v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 27, 42, 'Just as God had promised (vv. 22–25), all 276 people on board (v. 37) survive the shipwreck.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 1, 'God, who has been with Paul through so much, is with him still. Once again, God’s providence transforms apparent disaster—in this case, shipwreck and a viper bite—into an opportunity to preach the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 1, 'The shipwreck has left Paul and his shipmates on Malta where they are welcomed around a fire (vv. 1–2). Malta is a rocky island, 17 miles (27 km) long and 9 miles (14 km) wide, about 60 miles (100 km) south of Sicily.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 3, 'Paul’s survival from a viper bite leads the citizens of Malta to conclude that he is a god (compare 14:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 7, 'Paul cures the father of Publius, the leading citizen of the island, which leads to a wider healing ministry during the three months spent wintering on Malta (see v. 11). Compare Paul’s healings at Lystra (14:8–10), Ephesus (19:11–12), and Troas (20:9–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 11, 'Paul arrives in Rome (vv. 11–16) and conducts initial (vv. 17–22) and follow-up (vv. 23–29) meetings with the leaders of the Jews (v. 17). Acts concludes with Paul the prisoner preaching the gospel for two whole years with no one forbidding him (vv. 30–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 11, 'Twin Brothers. Castor and Pollux, patron gods of sailors. From recent experience (27:1–44), Luke knows well the identity of the real Guardian of seafarers. Puteoli. A port that served Rome, though it was 140 miles (225 km) away.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 15, 'The Appii Forum was 40 miles (65 km) and Three Inns 30 miles (48 km) from Rome. Paul appreciated the need for Christian support and fellowship, and so should we.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 16, 'Compare vv. 30–31. Note that Paul is bound with a chain (v. 20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 17, 'See note on 24:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 23, 'The second meeting, at which Paul argues the case for Jesus, lasting from morning till evening (v. 23). The scene is an appropriate conclusion to Acts as it illustrates Paul’s consistent evangelistic strategy (“for the Jew first and also for the Greek”; Rom. 1:16), his consistent message (Jesus as Messiah and Savior; see Acts 9:22), and the usual response (mixed; 28:24). Paul quotes Is. 6:9–10 (compare Matt. 13:14–15). As earlier (Acts 22:21–22), mention of salvation to the Gentiles breaks up the session (vv. 28–29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Atos', 28, 30, 'See note on v. 16. Acts ends triumphantly. Paul had expressed his desire, “I must also see Rome” (19:21; Rom. 15:23). The route had been difficult, but God answered Paul’s prayer to minister at Rome. Though imprisoned, Paul, at the heart of the Roman Empire, proclaims the Lord Jesus Christ with all confidence, no one forbidding him. The gospel has penetrated to the center of the world. From here, it will spread everywhere (see Introduction, “Date and Place of Writing”).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 0, NULL, 'Paul’s authorship of Romans has never been seriously challenged, either by scholars or by the early church fathers. The second-century church fathers uniformly attributed Romans to Paul. Moreover, the letter’s argumentative style, doctrinal concerns, and references to Paul’s itinerary are consistent with what we find in his other letters and the accounts of his ministry in Acts. Tertius (mentioned in 16:22), who claims to have written the letter, appears to have been Paul’s secretary, who took down the dictation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 0, NULL, 'The Roman historian Suetonius writes that Claudius expelled the Jews from Rome “at the instigation of Chrestus” in A.D. 49. If “Chrestus” here refers to Christ, as most scholars believe, it means that there were already enough Christians living in Rome by this time to cause an uproar between them and the Jews.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 0, NULL, 'Paul probably wrote Romans from Corinth around A.D. 57–58. The epistle was certainly written after A.D. 52, probably toward the end of his third missionary journey (Acts 20:2–3) in A.D. 57 or 58, during his three-month stay in Corinth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 0, NULL, 'A long and complex letter, Romans has multiple purposes. Paul wrote Romans to:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction (1:1–17)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 1, 'Paul opens his letter in the customary way (vv. 1–7), gives thanks as he usually does in his letters (vv. 8–15), and then states his main theme (vv. 16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 1, 'bondservant … called … separated. Descriptions of a prophetic figure (see note on Gal. 1:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 2, 'promised before. The gospel fulfills the OT hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 3, 'concerning His Son. The gospel is about Jesus, especially His death and resurrection. the flesh. His human nature.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 4, 'the Spirit of holiness. Denotes Christ’s divine origin in contrast to “the flesh” in v. 3. The resurrection reveals Jesus’ identity as the Son of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 5, 'obedience to the faith. Submission to the gospel. See note on 15:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 7, 'beloved … called. Denotes election. See 11:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 9, 'my spirit. Life led by the Holy Spirit. See 8:9–11. in the gospel of His Son. In the service of the gospel. without ceasing. Whenever he prays, he never omits (ceases) praying for them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 11, 'An inseparable relationship exists between faith, spiritual gifts, and the sharing of the faith between saints. See note on 12:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 13, 'fruit. Harvest of Gentiles. Paul is diplomatically asserting that he is the apostle of the Gentiles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 14, 'Paul is obligated to share the power of the gospel with all types of people, rich and poor, educated and uneducated. See notes on chap. 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 16, 'not ashamed. Openly confesses. The gospel was ridiculed because it preached the crucified Messiah. See 1 Cor. 1:23. power. The power to effect resurrection. believes. That Jesus has been resurrected. See notes on Rom. 10:10–13. the Jew first. (1) Paul preached the gospel to the Jews first (Acts 13:5, 14; 14:1–2; 17:1, 10, 16–17; 18:1–4; 19:8); and (2) God visited judgment and salvation upon the Jews first (Rom. 2:9–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 17, 'righteousness of God. Meaning (1) both God’s righteous character; and (2) the righteousness that God bestows on us. faith. God’s faithfulness to His promises as well as our faith in Christ. Both meanings apply here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 18, 'Clearly outlines the desperate need for a God-given righteousness by showing the specific sin of Gentiles (1:18–32) and Jews (2:1–3:8), as well as the whole world (3:9–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 18, 'wrath. Judgment. revealed. The gospel simultaneously reveals forgiveness (v. 17) and judgment. ungodliness. Refusal to worship God as Creator. See note on vv. 21–23. unrighteousness. See vv. 29–31 for a list of unrighteousness. suppress the truth. With lies and excuses. An activity opposite of faith (see 3:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 19, 'God has shown it to them. Even the ungodly person is already familiar with God because God reveals Himself to them through creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 20, 'invisible attributes. God’s love, power, and wrath. eternal power and Godhead. His eternal and powerful existence. For more on the revelation of God in CREATION, see Gen. 1; Ps. 19:1–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 21, 'Examples of how the suppression of truth takes place: (1) refusal to recognize God as Creator; (2) claims of wisdom; (3) substituting human creativity and false gods for the Creator God’s majesty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 24, 'gave them up. God expresses His wrath by releasing sinners to their own destructive desires and mindsets (see v. 18). According to vv. 24–28, God manifests His wrath in three successive stages by giving up sinners to: (1) their impure thoughts (v. 24); (2) unnatural sexual activities (v. 26); and (3) socially destructive mindsets (v. 28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 26, 'These verses condemn homosexual activities. Unnatural sexuality, however, goes beyond homosexual behaviors. The NT sanctions only the monogamous marriage between male and female. It condemns all other forms of sexual activity, whether homosexual or heterosexual, as sinful. See notes on v. 24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 28, 'a debased mind. A worthless mind given to self-deception. See notes on v. 24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 29, 'all unrighteousness. Reference back to v. 18. Disintegration of society and its basic moral values represents the final stage of God’s wrath.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 1, 32, 'righteous judgment. Righteous requirement (see 8:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 1, 'whoever you are who judge. Anyone who disapproves the behaviors described in 1:19–32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 2, 'judgment. Wrath. See v. 5 (compare to 1:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 3, 'Passing judgment on others is another clever way to suppress the truth about ourselves. See note on 1:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 5, 'revelation of the righteous judgment of God. At the Second Coming, God’s righteous, judicial decisions made in the heavenly court will be revealed or made known. These decisions are based on an investigation of human deeds (v. 6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 6, 'according to his deeds. Salvation is by grace, but many NT passages teach that works are the basis of JUDGMENT. See Matt. 7:24–27; 16:27; 25:31–46; James 2:12; Rev. 20:12. Thus, our works are examined. God judges us based on our works, and He saves us based on His grace. These two convictions form the unshakable foundation of Paul’s theology.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 7, 'doing good. The sole criterion of divine judgment. See note on vv. 9–11. glory, honor, and immortality. Attributes of God. See Job 40:10; Ps. 96:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 8, 'truth … unrighteousness … wrath. Refer back to 1:18. When we judge others, we suppress and misrepresent the truth about the absolute impartiality of God’s judgment. See note on 2:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 9, 'As Creator of all, God judges everyone impartially based on their deeds irrespective of their race or religion. See v. 7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 12, 'law. These verses contrast Jews (who had ready access to the laws of God contained in the OT) and Gentiles. the sight of God. The purpose of the law is to summon sinners before God’s tribunal. doers of the law will be justified. Grace can be fully appreciated only against the backdrop of judgment by law. See note on v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 15, 'the work of the law. The moral requirements of the law. written. Known (see Jer. 31:33). conscience … bearing witness. The conscience functions as a witness that, deep inside their hearts, humans are aware of the will of God. between themselves. Between approving and disapproving thoughts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 16, 'secrets. Secret deeds as well as hidden thoughts. by Jesus Christ. Jesus is Judge as well as Savior. my gospel. Paul’s gospel is as much about judgment as it is about salvation. See note on v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 17, 'boast in God. The Jews mistakenly believed that God would show favoritism toward them in the judgment because they were His people. See note on vv. 9–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 18, 'approve the things that are excellent. Be able to distinguish between good and evil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 20, 'the form. The highest form.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 21, 'Not every Jew who taught the law stole, committed adultery, and robbed the temple. Paul’s point is that being able to teach others about Scripture does not exempt one from God’s impartial judgment, which is based on deeds. See note on v. 7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 23, 'Paul once again returns to deeds as the criterion of God’s judgment. See note on v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 25, 'circumcision. An essential part of Jewish identity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 26, 'righteous requirements. Moral and spiritual (as opposed to ceremonial) requirements. counted. Reckoned. This verse anticipates the argument of 4:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 27, 'written code and circumcision. Literal observance of the law of circumcision.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 2, 29, 'inwardly. Not in a way physically visible. not … letter. This dichotomy applies only to the ceremonial law. In the case of the moral law like the Decalogue, one is required to fulfill it spiritually and literally.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 1, 'profit. Worth or benefit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 2, 'Having the OT as their Scripture gave the Jews a huge advantage over their non-Jewish counterparts. But a spiritual advantage does not always lead to salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 3, 'God’s faithfulness to His covenant does not depend on the faithfulness of His human agents.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 4, 'overcome. Emerge victorious.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 5, 'our unrighteousness demonstrates the righteousness of God. For example, human wickedness shown at the cross only served to demonstrate God’s righteousness even more gloriously (see 5:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 6, 'Certainly not. Answers the question whether God is unjust: God is just. how will God judge the world? God can judge the world because He is just.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 7, 'increased through my lie. Our lies can only serve to advance God’s truth; they cannot stop it. God uses our wickedness to advance His kingdom; as long as He chooses to work through sinful humans, He has no other choice because everyone is a sinner. See note on v. 5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 8, 'do evil that good may come. A reference to a statement like “where sin abounded, grace abounded much more” (5:20). It is erroneous to conclude from such a statement that one needs to sin to experience God’s grace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 9, 'we. The Jews. they. The Gentiles. previously charged. In 1:18–2:29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 10, 'There is none righteous. The summation of vv. 11–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 11, 'none who understands … none who seeks. Everyone sins in secret as though God did not see. See Ps. 14:1–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 12, 'turned aside. From God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 13, 'open tomb. Full of impurity and profanity. poison of asps. Violent words.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 15, 'swift to shed blood. Refers to the fundamentally competitive nature of human activity that ultimately produces disruptions and destruction in human history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 19, 'law. Psalms and Ecclesiastes (quoted in vv. 10–18) are considered law because they interpret the Law of Moses. that … all the world may become guilty. When Scripture condemns Israel, it also condemns the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 20, 'God gave the law to help us recognize our sinfulness before Him. For more on the relationship between LAW and SIN, see 1 John 3:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 21, 'Along with Eph. 2:4–10, this passage is Paul’s most complete statement on the plan of SALVATION. It contains the following elements: (1) Righteousness is “apart from the law” (Rom. 3:21), meaning not earned by law keeping (v. 20; Gal. 2:16). (2) Rather, it comes “through faith in Jesus” (Rom. 3:22; Gal. 2:16; Eph. 2:8). (3) Since all have sinned (Rom. 3:23), all deserve death (6:23; Gal. 3:10). (4) God does not give people the death they deserve, but what they do not deserve, the free gift of grace (Rom. 3:24; Eph. 2:8), which they accept by faith (Rom. 3:25; see also v. 22). (5) The gift is provided because of Christ’s death on the cross. His atoning sacrifice not only turned away the penalty of sin (“propitiation,” v. 25; 1 John 2:2; 4:10), but also provided for human “redemption” (a word signifying the purchase of a captive, Rom. 3:24; Mark 10:45; Eph. 1:7), “justification” (a word meaning to count as righteous, Rom. 3:24; 4:6), “reconciliation” (a healing of relationships, 5:10; 2 Cor. 5:19), and cleansing from the pollution of sin (Heb. 9:13–14, 22–23; 10:1–2). (6) Central to God’s saving work in Christ is His sacrificial “blood” (Rom. 3:25). It is His shed blood that stands as the foundation for each of the metaphors of SALVATION mentioned in point 5 above (3:25; 5:9; Eph. 1:7; Col. 1:20; Heb. 9:13–14). In other words, in His death Christ died the death that belongs to sinners that they might have life (2 Cor. 5:21; Gal. 3:10–13). On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 21, 'now. In Christ. righteousness of God. See note on 1:17. apart from the law. Without requiring performance of all the ceremonial requirements of the Law of Moses. See 3:22. witnessed. Prophesied. The Law of Moses is not a method of salvation but a prophecy concerning Christ and His righteousness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 22, 'through faith. Means the same as “without the works of the law.” there is no difference. God’s grace is as impartial as His judgment. See note on 2:9–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 23, 'the glory of God. The image of God. Sin robs us of the image of God in which we were created. On the universal effect of SIN on humanity, see 5:12; Gen. 3:1–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 24, 'justified. Acquitted or declared not guilty. freely. Without our contribution. redemption. Forgiveness. The word implies not only release from guilt and liability to judgment but also freedom from slavery to sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 25, 'propitiation. The word literally means “the Mercy Seat,” which was the cover or lid of the ark of the covenant in the OT tabernacle. On the Day of Atonement, blood was sprinkled there (Lev. 16:14–15). by His blood. Emphasizes the sacrificial death of Jesus. faith. See note on Rom. 1:17. Both definitions apply here. righteousness. See notes on 1:17; 3:21–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 26, 'The cross demonstrates God’s righteous character. See note on 1:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 27, 'When the law is understood as demanding works, human performance becomes central; but when the law is correctly understood as demanding faith, Christ becomes central.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 28, 'faith apart from the deeds of the law. See note on v. 22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 29, 'there is one God. To believe in one God means to believe in His impartiality in judgment and grace. See note on 2:9–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 3, 31, 'the law. Although faith lays aside ceremonial requirements of the Law of');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 1, 'Abraham discovered that he had nothing to boast about.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 2, 'before God. Before God’s impartial judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 3, 'Gen. 15:6 is the only passage in Genesis that refers to Abraham as righteous. accounted. Credited to his record. See note on Rom. 4:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 4, 'Describes two basic types of religions: (1) one that views salvation as a reward or wages; and (2) one that views it as an undeserved gift.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 5, 'does not work but believes. Two sequential actions: (1) stop “working,” that is, cease trying to earn one’s own salvation, and (2) turn to God. Faith requires both actions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 6, 'For more on SALVATION, see note on 3:21–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 7, 'forgiven. Justified. Justification is forgiveness, and vice versa. See also 3:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 8, 'shall not impute sin. That is, shall forgive sin. Abraham was righteous because he was forgiven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 9, 'blessedness. Of justification. See note on v. 7. the circumcised. The Jews. the uncircumcised. The Gentiles. faith. Rather than circumcision.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 10, 'Abraham was still uncircumcised in Gen. 15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 11, 'received … circumcision. In Gen. 17:22–27. sign. Circumcision is meaningless apart from the righteousness of faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 12, 'father of circumcision. Father of the Jews.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 13, 'the promise. Refers to all the promises made to Abraham. the law. The Law of Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 14, 'if those who are of the law are heirs. God never intended to make Israel heirs through the law. His purpose from the start was to fulfill His promise to Abraham through Christ (Gal. 3:17–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 15, 'law brings about wrath. One purpose of the law is to condemn sin and bring judgment upon sinners. transgression. Of the express will of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 16, 'it. Righteousness. be sure. Be guaranteed. The basis of the fulfillment of the promises is God’s faithfulness, which is absolutely reliable, not our performance, which is not reliable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 17, '“I have made you a father of many nations.” Refers to the promise in Gen. 17:4. Abraham’s faith consisted of: (1) constantly living in the presence of God (“in the presence of Him”); (2) a firm belief in the resurrection (“God, who gives life to the dead”); and (3) an unwavering trust in God as Creator who created all things out of nothing (“calls those things which do not exist”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 18, 'contrary to hope. In spite of hopelessness. Refers to the fact that God gave the promise to Abraham that he would become the father of many nations (Gen. 17:4) when Abraham and Sarah were well past their childbearing age (Gen. 17:15–17). in hope believed. Full of hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 19, 'he did not consider. We can understand vv. 19–20 in the following sense: “he considered his own body and the deadness of Sarah’s womb, but he did not waver.” giving glory to God. Expressing faith in God as Creator (see Rev. 14:7). The belief in the resurrection of Christ is an expression of faith in God as Creator who is able to raise the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 22, 'it was accounted to him. His faith was counted in his favor. for righteousness. Compare to Gen. 15:6. Abraham’s circumcision in Gen. 17 was a reconfirmation of the faith that he had in 15:5–6. In Gen. 17, Abraham’s circumcision functions as an outward expression of his conviction that God is able to give him a son through barren Sarah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 4, 23, 'The Christian faith in the resurrection of Christ is ultimately faith in God as Creator who is able to raise the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 1, 'Peace and joy come to believers in Jesus and they are assured of future glory and life eternal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 1, 'Peace and joy come to believers in Jesus and they are assured of future glory and life eternal.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 1, 'justified. Forgiven and acquitted, see also 3:24. See note on 4:7. by faith. Without the works of the law. See note on 3:22. peace. Friendship resulting from the removal of wrath (see 1:18; 2:2, 5; 4:15). with God. Before the judgment seat of God. See note on 2:12–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 2, 'access. Bold approach to the throne of God. this grace. Friendship. See note on v. 1. rejoice. Boast. We boast in our hope instead of our own works (see 3:27). the glory of God. Faith enables us to shift our focus from our own interests to the vindication (proof of fairness, truth, and grace) of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 3, 'glory. Rejoice. tribulations. Ordinary hardships as well as persecutions. produces. Suffering cannot produce transformation by itself. Only God’s grace can.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 4, 'character. A disposition that has been tested and approved by God. hope. The crowning prize of our earthly journey.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 5, 'does not disappoint. Is not an illusion. the love of God … in our hearts. Refers to the calming effect of the Holy Spirit on the sufferer. This is the most compelling proof of God’s existence, especially for those who suffer (see 8:39).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 6, 'due time. See notes on Dan. 9:26; Gal. 4:2, 4. ungodly. Those who refuse to worship God. See note on Rom. 1:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 8, 'demonstrates. On the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 9, 'shall be saved from wrath. Refers to the final judgment. through Him. By Christ’s resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 10, 'enemies. Estranged. For more on sinners being the enemy of God, see 8:7. reconciled. For more on SALVATION, see note on 3:21–26. See also 2 Cor. 5:19. shall be saved. Shall be resurrected.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 11, 'And not only that. Not only will we be raised in the future. the reconciliation. With God. Friendship with God has been restored—an image similar to justification.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 12, 'one man. Adam. because all sinned. The logic of this verse is: (1) The sin that entered the world through Adam caused death (Gen. 3:1–6); (2) everyone must have sinned because everyone dies. It is because of this condition of human nature, traceable to Adam’s sin, that human beings must be born again (Rom. 3:23; 5:1). On the extent of SIN, see note on Gen. 3:1–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 13, 'until the law. Before the law was given to Moses. imputed. Accounted as such.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 14, 'Sin existed in the world before anyone ever transgressed the law, as it was given to Moses. the transgression of Adam. Adam’s violation of God’s express command (see Gen. 2:17; 3:17). type. Pattern or prototype.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 15, 'the free gift. The death of Christ. the offense. The fall of Adam.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 17, 'The reign of Christ counteracts the effects of sin and death in the lives of the believers in this life and will completely wipe them out at the Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 18, 'one … to all. This formula is based on the principle of curses and blessings in Deuteronomy, according to which one act of transgression (such as Achan’s; Josh. 7) results in the condemnation of all Israel, and one act of obedience (such as Phinehas’s in Num. 25:7–13) results in the blessing of all Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 19, 'one Man’s obedience. Christ’s obedient death on the cross (see Phil. 2:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 5, 20, 'the law entered. Through Moses (see v. 14). the offense. Transgressions. See note on v. 14. sin abounded. In the form of transgressions. grace. The work of Christ. This verse refers to the law’s function in Israel’s history and should not be understood in personal terms. For example, the verse does not mean that God makes you sin, so that you can more fully appreciate the cross. Hence the objection in 6:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 1, 'continue in sin that grace may abound. The way some misunderstood Paul’s teachings on grace. See note on 5:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 2, 'died to sin. During baptism. See note on v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 3, 'into Christ … into His death … buried … raised. Through baptism the believer identifies himself with the death, burial, and resurrection of Jesus (Col. 2:12–13) and becomes a member of His body (1 Cor. 12:13). Like burial, baptism marks an end to the former way of life. On BAPTISM as a new way of life, see John 3:3, 5; Gal. 3:27. On Christ as an example in BAPTISM, see Matt. 3:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 4, 'the glory of the Father. The power of God to create and resurrect. See note on 4:23–25. newness of life. The present experience of resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 5, 'we also shall be. Refers to the future resurrection at the Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 6, 'By dying and rising, Christ crossed over from the old realm of sin and death to the new realm of righteousness and life. our old man. Our former existence and way of life under the old realm of sin and death (see Eph. 4:22). crucified with Him. By faith, Christ’s death becomes ours and we die to the old realm during baptism. the body of sin. The infectious power sin had over our bodies. See note on 7:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 7, 'he who has died. Through baptism.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 12, 'its lusts. The bodily desires. We experience Adam’s sin through the impure desires that arise in us. For a list of impure desires, see 1:26–30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 13, 'members. Different body parts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 14, 'sin shall not have dominion. For the believer living in the realm of righteousness. not under law. Not under law as a way of salvation. Law reveals sin (3:20), but cannot forgive it or help in overcoming it. The sanctuary system revealed in the law (Torah) lacked actual power to free humans from sin. See Heb. 9:9–10. under grace. Denotes the new existence under the control of the Holy Spirit. See note on Rom. 7:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 15, 'Shall we sin. Shall we willfully sin?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 16, 'present yourselves. Denotes repeated, continuous giving of oneself. slaves. The language of slavery depicts the idea of belonging. Like slaves belong to their masters, we belong either to the old realm of sin or the new realm of righteousness. There can be no neutral existence in this life. On the effects of SIN, see v. 23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 17, 'that form of doctrine. Jesus’ life and teachings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 18, 'Slavery to righteousness must be as total and immediate as the former slavery to sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 19, 'slaves of uncleanness, and of lawlessness. Sin contaminates the body with uncleanness and wickedness, ultimately resulting in death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 20, 'free in regard to righteousness. So much under the absolute domination of sin that righteousness had no control or influence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 21, 'the end. In both verses, the term “end” refers to the final judgment and resurrection at the end of time. Whether we will receive death or eternal life will depend on to whom we belonged in this life—sin or God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 6, 23, 'wages. Continuation of the imagery of slavery. Sin gives wages to its slaves, but God gives a gift in Christ. sin. On the nature of SIN, see 5:12; Gen. 3. death. Sin pays death, not eternal life. This contrasts sharply with the common but unbiblical idea that sin and condemnation result in an eternal life of conscious torment. For the biblical understanding of the notion of HELL, see Matt. 10:28. The death mentioned here is the final penalty of sin at the end of time. On the second DEATH, see note on John 11:26 and Rev. 20:14. gift. Eternal life is God’s gift. Compare John 3:36. For more on the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21. For more on SALVATION, see Rom. 3:21–26; Eph. 2:4–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 2, 'woman. Paul offers an allegorical interpretation in which the woman represents the people of God, both ancient and present. husband. The old realm of sin and death. See note on 6:6. bound by the law. Placed under condemnation by the law. the law of her husband. The law regulating the woman’s marriage to her husband. By analogy, “the law of sin and death” in 7:23 and 8:2 refers to the law given to Moses to regulate our “marriage” to the realm of sin and death, our first husband, as it were. Similarly, “the law of the Spirit of life” in 8:2 also refers to the law as given to Moses but in its new function of regulating our “new marriage” to the realm of the Spirit and the life that Christ has inaugurated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 3, 'Here Paul explains why the law, as given to Moses, was binding in its totality in the OT times but is not anymore. if … she marries another man. If a Jew tried to live free of the ceremonial laws in the OT times, as though Christ had come. while her husband lives. While the old realm is still standing. will be called an adulteress. Would have been a transgressor. if her husband dies, she is free. Because Christ has dismantled the old realm of sin and death and inaugurated the new realm of righteousness and life, the believer is no longer bound by all the provisions of the law as given to Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 4, 'you also have become dead. Through baptism. to the law. To the law as a tool of condemnation or as a way of salvation. through the body of Christ. Through union with Christ. married to another. Transferred to a new realm of life and righteousness. See notes on 6:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 5, 'in the flesh. Living in the old realm of sin (see note on v. 2). aroused. Made aware experientially (not merely intellectually). bear fruit to death. Kill us (compare with 6:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 6, 'delivered from the law. Delivered from the condemnation of the law. what we were held by. The old realm of sin and death. serve. Serve God. in the newness of the Spirit. Under the guidance of the Holy Spirit. See Gal. 5:22. the oldness of the letter. The ceremonial requirements of the law as given to Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 8, 'taking opportunity. Opportunity to rebel against God. was dead. Lay hidden, like a sleeping serpent. The commandment exposes sin’s presence in us by arousing it into action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 9, 'I was alive once. I used to be blameless (see Phil. 3:6). without the law. Refers to not knowing the true intent of the law. when the commandment came, sin revived. The law awakens us to the presence of sin. I died. Condemned to death by the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 10, 'which was to bring life. The Law of Moses promises life to those who obey it (Lev. 18:5; Deut. 4:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 11, 'taking occasion by the commandment. See notes on vv. 8–9. deceived me. Into desiring what the law forbids.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 12, 'the law … the commandment. Since Paul earlier cites the law, “You shall not covet” (v. 7), he especially has the Decalogue or Ten Commandments in view. The fact that the law is able to provoke and arouse sin proves that it is holy, righteous, and good—the exact opposite of sin. For other texts indicating Paul’s understanding of a Christian’s relationship to the LAW, see 3:20, 31; 7:14; Eph. 2:8–10. For more on LAW, see Ex. 20:1–17; Ps. 19:7–11; Matt. 5:17–19; Heb. 8:8–10; James 2:10, 12; 1 John 2:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 13, 'become death. Become the cause of death. The law is not the cause of death; sin is. sin … might appear sin. That I may know that I am a sinner. producing death in me. Causing me to come under God’s condemnation. what is good. The law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 14, 'carnal, sold under sin. Belong to the old realm of sin and death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 15, 'what I will to do, that I do not practice. I do not perform what I intend. This observation coincides with 1:22–23, which describes the ungodly who, intending to be wise, become fools and commit idolatry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 16, 'I agree with the law. My conscience concurs with the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 17, 'But now. Since I agree with the law. no longer I. I am no longer deceived into thinking that I am acting alone when I sin. but sin that dwells in me. The true culprit is sin that resides in me. Paul is not absolving humans of the responsibility for sin. Rather, he wants to defend the law by explaining what the law does when it condemns us to death. The law essentially says: (1) do not make excuses for your sins, for they belong to your sinful existence; (2) have hope by looking to Christ who will deliver you from your sinful existence through the resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 18, 'I know. Because of the law. in my flesh. In my members infected with sin. to will is present with me. I can intend to obey the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 21, 'I find then a law. I find this to be the way the law works in relation to the old realm of sin and death.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 23, 'This verse finally clinches the argument why the law, granted to Moses, is not to be identified with sin and death. another law. The law’s effect on flesh described in vv. 8–11. the law of my mind. The law’s positive effect on the mind described in vv. 12, 22. the law of sin. May refer to sin, as though it were a law, operative in one’s life. Or it refers to the law, as given to Moses, in that it was to regulate the old realm of sin. See note on v. 2. which is in my members. Which is there by virtue of my sinful members.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 7, 25, 'I myself serve. I belong to. See notes on 6:16, 19. but with the flesh the law of sin. In spite of baptism and the Holy Spirit (7:6), sin remains active in us in this present evil realm.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 1, 'no condemnation. Even when we succumb to sin from time to time. in Christ. We are not under the condemnation of the old realm because we now belong to Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 2, 'the law of the Spirit of life. The law, as given to Moses, in its new function of regulating our existence in the new realm of life in the Spirit. the law of sin and death. The law functioning in the old realm of sin. See notes on 7:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 3, 'what the law could not do. I.e., give life (see 7:10). through the flesh. Because of sinful human existence. in the likeness of sinful flesh. Flesh susceptible to sin; refers to Jesus’ birth in the old realm of sin. The term “likeness” or “form” denies that Jesus was actually sinful. on account of sin. As a sin offering. Denotes transfer of sin from the sinner to the sacrificial offering. condemned sin in the flesh. Refers to the cruel manner in which Christ died.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 4, 'the righteous requirement of the law. The moral and spiritual demands of the law as given to Moses. fulfilled. In terms of love. See note on 13:8–10. according to the flesh. According to the sinful desires. according to the Spirit. According to the new desires created by the Spirit. See note on 8:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 5, 'set their minds. Keep thinking about.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 9, 'dwells. Refers to the body as a dwelling place of the Holy Spirit. See 1 Cor. 6:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 10, 'body is dead. Spiritually.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 11, 'if the Spirit … dwells in you. The presence of the Spirit subdues the sinful desires of our bodies. This is an initial experience of the resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 14, 'led by the Spirit. The Spirit becomes the dominant force in the life, which now exhibits the fruit of the Spirit. See Gal. 5:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 15, 'Abba, Father. “Abba” expresses a sense of endearment and deep respect and means “daddy” in Aramaic, Jesus’ mother tongue. Jesus used “Abba” in His prayers to address God (Mark 14:36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 16, 'Christian ASSURANCE is based on what the Spirit does for us and what He testifies in us, as indicated here and in 1 John 5:10–13. See also Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 17, 'suffer with Him. Not denying Him in the face of persecution.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 19, 'earnest expectation. Intense longing. revealing of the sons of God. The future resurrection of the believers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 20, 'futility. Emptiness of existence. See Eccl. 1:1ff. because of Him. Refers to God’s cursing of the earth after Adam’s fall. See Gen. 3:17–19. in hope. Refers to the promise of redemption. See Gen. 3:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 22, 'birth pangs. Turmoil in nature like earthquakes, hurricanes, famines, and other catastrophes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 23, 'we also who have. The believers including the apostles. the firstfruits of the Spirit. The fruits of the Holy Spirit constitute the initial experience of the resurrection. See Gal. 5:22. the redemption of our body. The final resurrection. See 1 Cor. 15:54.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 24, 'We persevere in the hope that God is able to create the future He has promised. See 2 Cor. 5:7; Heb. 11:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 26, 'The Spirit transforms our prayers and makes them acceptable to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 28, 'As our sighs and groanings intensify, so does our hope. His purpose. To save the world. See Eph. 1:11; 3:11; 2 Tim. 1:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 29, 'predestined. Set apart. See note on 9:17 regarding the purpose of God’s election. See note on Eph. 1:5 regarding predestination. to be conformed to the image of His Son. Refers to the restoration of the image of God in us, which was lost at the fall of Adam. that He might be the firstborn. Christ is the firstborn because our resurrection is dependent on His.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 30, 'Election does not guarantee salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 31, 'The Christian doctrine of ASSURANCE has several aspects. Some deal with confidence in approaching God (Heb. 4:16), others are based upon the reality of present salvation (John 3:36; 5:24; Eph. 2:8–9; 1 John 4:17; 5:13), and yet others upon the internal witness of the Spirit (Rom. 8:16; 1 John 5:10–12). See also 1 Pet. 1:3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 34, 'at the right hand of God. Reference to Ps. 110:1, denoting Christ’s triumph over His enemies. intercession. High Priestly ministry. See Eph. 1:20; Col. 3:1; Heb. 1:3. On Christ’s intercessory ministry in the heavenly SANCTUARY, see Heb. 4:14–16; 7:25; 8:1–5; 1 John 2:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 8, 35, 'In Judaism, suffering was often thought to represent the curse of God, a sign of God’s rejection (see Deut. 28–29). Paul reiterates the point he made in Rom. 8:1: there is no condemnation for those in Christ, even when they suffer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 1, 'Since it is clear that Gentiles can be saved by faith in Jesus, Paul now turns to answer the questions about how the Jews fit into God’s plan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 1, 'Since it is clear that Gentiles can be saved by faith in Jesus, Paul now turns to answer the questions about how the Jews fit into God’s plan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 1, 'my conscience also bearing me witness. See note on 2:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 3, 'accursed. Like Moses (Ex. 32:32). my brethren. The unbelieving Jews (see note on Acts 11:1–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 4, 'adoption. As the children of Israel. the glory. Of the temple. the covenants. With Abraham, Moses, Aaron, David, etc. the giving of the law. At Sinai. service. Worship of One God. the promises. Of posterity, land, and blessing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 5, 'who is … blessed God. Could also be translated as “God who is above all, be blessed forever.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 6, 'taken no effect. That Christianity came into being is evidence that God’s promises have been fulfilled. who are of Israel. Who are of physical Israel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 7, 'Promise, rather than physical descent, defines the identity of Israel. For example, Ishmael, Esau, and the sons of Keturah (Gen. 25:1–2) were rejected although they were physical children of Abraham.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 11, 'the purpose of God. See note on v. 17. not of works … calls. The call of God, rather than their performance, determines the identity of the elect.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 13, 'loved. Chosen. hated. Not chosen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 14, 'unrighteousness. Injustice or unfairness. God is always just, never unfair. All true biblical interpretation begins from this premise.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 15, 'have mercy. Forgiveness is the basis of election.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 16, 'Election is a gift, not an obligation on the part of God for the works performed (see 4:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 17, 'this very purpose. The purpose of election is (1) to proclaim the name of God in all the earth and (2) to have all come to the knowledge of the truth (1 Tim. 2:4). See also Rom. 9:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 18, 'hardens. By allowing them to believe in flattering lies. Applies to Jews and Christians (see Heb. 3:8, 13). This verse anticipates Rom. 11:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 21, 'As Creator, God reserves the right to create as He wishes. This point should not be broadly interpreted to apply to every human situation, such as those born with congenital diseases. Paul’s point is limited to certain individuals and nations, such as Israel, Moses, and Pharaoh who were elected to reveal His glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 22, 'endured … destruction. God has acted in the way He has in order to show mercy (“longsuffering”) toward all and lead them to repentance (2:4). That the “vessels of wrath” were “prepared for destruction” does not necessarily mean they will be destroyed. For example, God did not destroy Nebuchadnezzar, but bore him with longsuffering so that he could come to the knowledge of God and be saved (see Dan. 4:28–37).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 23, 'Whether in the case of Pharaoh (v. 17) or the church, the purpose of God’s election remains the same: the vindication of God (see Col. 1:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 25, 'not My people. Not part of Israel. not beloved. Not chosen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 26, 'they shall be called. See note on v. 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 27, 'Election does not guarantee salvation. Israel was God’s elect, but not every Israelite will be saved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 28, 'cut it short. Execute decisively. a short work. A swift judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 31, 'the law of righteousness. The law as given to Moses. has not attained to the law of righteousness. Has missed the point of the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 9, 32, 'that stumbling stone. The crucified Christ. See Is. 8:13–14; 28:16; 1 Cor. 1:22–23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 2, 'knowledge. Correct understanding of Scripture.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 3, 'God’s righteousness. Righteousness that God grants. See note on 1:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 4, 'end of the law. That to which the law points. for righteousness. This phrase describes “Christ” rather than “the end of the law.” The verse may be understood as follows: the law points to Christ, who is righteousness unto everyone who believes. For more on law, see 7:12, 14; Ex. 20:1–17; Matt. 5:17–19; Heb. 8:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 5, 'The law, given through Moses, presents the reader with a choice between two types of righteousness: righteousness by law and righteousness by faith. There cannot be a combination of both, such as righteousness by law plus faith. We must choose.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 6, 'bring Christ down. There is no need to do so, since Christ has already come in the incarnation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 7, 'bring Christ up. There is no need to do so, since Christ has already been raised from the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 8, 'word is near you. This statement from Deut. 30:14 is a prophecy about a time after the exile, when God will have removed the curses from Israel (Deut. 30:1–2). Here Paul implies that such a promised time of blessing has now begun in Christ. Christ has come to earth and has been raised from the dead (Rom. 10:6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 9, 'confess … the Lord Jesus. Read: confess the Lord as Jesus. In other words, confess that Yahweh of the OT, translated there as “the LORD,” is Jesus. See vv. 11–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 10, 'believes. That Jesus has been raised. See v. 9. confession is made. That Jesus is divine.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 11, 'From Is. 28:16 and Joel 2:32. According to these texts, people will believe in and call upon Yahweh (the LORD) for their salvation at the end time. To believe in Jesus means to believe that He is Yahweh (the LORD) who has been crucified for our sins and raised for our salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 15, 'good things. The good news that God has come to Israel in the person of Jesus and comforted His people (Is. 52:8–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 16, 'they. The Jews.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 17, 'by hearing. By reading the OT Scriptures in the light of Jesus, which is what Paul has done in this chapter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 10, 18, 'OT Scriptures prophesy that many in Israel will reject the good news that their God has returned to redeem and to comfort them. They will fail to convert their election into salvation because they trust in their election.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 1, 'On the remnant idea at the end of time, see Rev. 12:17. On remnant in the OT, see Is. 10:20–22; 37:31–32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 2, 'He foreknew. He alone knew.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 3, 'The remnant consists of those who refuse to bow the knee to a state-enforced idolatry, whatever form it may take (1 Kin. 19:18; see also Rev. 14:11–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 5, 'remnant. The remnant: (1) may be visible like Elijah; (2) is chosen based on God’s grace; and (3) is not the only group who will be saved. God knows many more besides the visible remnant (e.g., 7,000) who will be saved (see v. 4; 1 Kin. 19:18). On the REMNANT in end times who refuses to bow the knee to any enforced idolatry, see Rev. 12:17; 14:9–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 7, 'what it seeks. Righteousness. the elect. Jewish Christians including Paul and the twelve apostles. the rest. The unbelieving Jews. blinded. Allowed to believe their own lies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 8, 'God is simply giving those who reject the truth what their sinful hearts desire (see note on 1:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 11, 'have they stumbled that they should fall? Has the unbelieving Israel been rejected? provoke them to jealousy. Cause Israel to desire righteousness by faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 13, 'my ministry. Ministry to the Gentiles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 15, 'the reconciling of the world. Proclamation of the gospel to the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 17, 'some of the branches. God never rejected the Jews as a whole.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 18, 'boast against. Despise. the branches. The rejected Jews. the root. Israel. The church draws upon the richness of Israel’s sacred traditions (see 9:4), particularly the OT (see 3:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 20, 'unbelief. Lack of faith. The basis of inclusion in the people of God is always faith, for both Jews and Gentiles. Do not be haughty. When we gloat over or despise anyone, we make clear that we are no longer living by faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 22, 'goodness. Sparing. severity. Not sparing. continue in His goodness. Live remembering God’s kindness towards you.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 23, 'they. Unbelieving Jews.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 24, 'contrary to nature. Contrary to their own culture and religious backgrounds.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 25, 'mystery. Revelation. blindness. “Hardening” in Greek (see notes on vv. 8–11). the fullness of the Gentiles. Every Gentile possible who will respond to the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 26, 'all Israel. The true people of God consisting of Jews and Gentiles. The Deliverer will come out of Zion. This expression underscores (1) the Jewish origin of Christ and (2) Christ as the Savior of the Jews also.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 28, 'they are beloved. God has a special regard for the Jews because of their ancestors.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 29, 'See note on v. 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 30, 'through their disobedience. Both Jews and Gentiles have been disobedient and therefore are in need of forgiveness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 31, 'obtain mercy. Become part of the people of God; see note on 9:15. Israel will be included again in the people of God on the same basis as the Gentiles—Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 32, 'has committed. Has condemned (see Gal. 3:22). on all. On all who call upon Jesus (see notes on Rom. 9:15; 10:11). Not everyone upon whom God has mercy will be saved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 11, 35, 'repaid to him. Who allegedly lent to God. The point is that God does not owe anyone anything.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 1, 'Those saved by faith in Jesus are expected, as a result, to live a new life of godly behavior. This section spells out some of the details of that life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 1, 'Those saved by faith in Jesus are expected, as a result, to live a new life of godly behavior. This section spells out some of the details of that life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 1, 'On transformed living, see 6:1–11; Eph. 2:10; 1 John 2:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 1, 'a living sacrifice. As opposed to a blood sacrifice. reasonable service. Spiritual worship as opposed to a physical temple service. Christian worship consists of holy conduct.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 2, 'this world. The conduct and practices of the unbelievers. renewing of your mind. Adopting a new set of thoughts and judgments. prove. Examine and determine.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 3, 'through the grace given to me. As an apostle to the Gentiles (see note on Gal. 2:9). soberly. Honestly and prayerfully. faith. Here, faith is expressed through spiritual gifts (see note on v. 6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 4, 'Compare the lists of spiritual gifts in 1 Cor. 12:4–11, 27–30; Eph. 4:11. On the purpose of the SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 6, 'in proportion to our faith. According to our spiritual gifts and calling. In this chapter, Paul uses the term “faith” in the sense of our understanding of spiritual gifts because we are expected to express our faith through the spiritual gifts that we have received (see 1:11–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 9, 'These verses offer concrete examples of how to properly express one’s love toward fellow believers, God, and outsiders who may be either friendly or hostile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 9, 'love. Love toward both God and man. Abhor … good. Love, guided by a capacity to correctly distinguish between good and evil, forms the foundation of Christian conduct.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 10, 'brotherly love. Affection that we share with our own family members. giving preference to one another. Treating and honor. In Greco-Roman society, respect and honor were reserved for people occupying the upper classes of society. Most early Christians were slaves or former slaves (see chap. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 11, 'Love for God must be expressed through diligent and passionate labor for Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 12, 'Hope, joy, patience, and constant prayer in the face of adverse circumstances are the fruit of the Spirit’s presence in the life (Gal. 5:22–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 13, 'Denotes the generous sharing of food and means that characterized early Christian fellowship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 14, 'The aim of Christian conduct is to bring blessings to others, even to those who persecute you.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 15, 'This verse describes in concrete terms what it means to be compassionate; the opposite of compassion is a spirit of competition that finds pleasure in the misery of others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 16, 'Be of the same mind. Do not compete with each other. The test that one’s Christianity has not become an intellectual religion of respectability is that one can freely mingle with the poor and the downtrodden.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 17, 'Have regard for. Try to do. On good and evil, see note on v. 9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 18, 'Christians should not initiate hostilities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 19, 'give place to wrath. Christians should not take vengeance into their own hands.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 12, 21, 'This verse sums up the life of Jesus, especially His death on the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 1, 'Nero was the emperor in Rome (A.D. 54–68) when Paul penned these words. Nero eventually persecuted Christians and executed Paul. Here Paul speaks against unnecessary resistance to government. He wants Christians to be seen as much as possible by the pagan world as peace-loving, law-abiding citizens who are beneficial to society.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 1, 'the governing authorities. Civil authorities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 2, 'ordinance. Decree. judgment. Condemnation from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 3, 'While good deeds cannot earn one’s own salvation, they have much value in building a strong society.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 4, 'bear the sword. Paul sanctions civil authorities’ use of force to maintain order.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 5, 'subject. Subject to the authorities. wrath. Punishment. for conscience’ sake. So that we may have a clear conscience before God and humans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 6, 'this very thing. Maintaining order in society by punishing evil (see v. 4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 7, 'honor. See note on 12:10. It is not wrong to show respect and honor to dignitaries and government officials, when such respect does not detract from ultimate loyalty to God (Acts 5:29; 1 Pet. 2:17). Somewhat later in history, Christians were often persecuted for failing to worship the emperor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 8, 'There is a delicate balance between the literal observance of the law and its fulfillment. No amount of observance of the law can fulfill the law if the observance is devoid of love. At the same time, love does not provide license to break the law. For example, love cannot be used as a pretext for committing immorality, theft, or murder. The Decalogue (Ex. 20:1–17) needs to be observed literally as well as in principle. For more on the fulfillment of the LAW, see Matt. 22:36–40; Gal. 5:14; 1 John 5:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 12, 'night. This present age. day. The day of the Second Coming of Jesus. the works of darkness. Former conduct as unbelievers. armor of light. As light dissipates darkness and armor protects against assaults, Christian conduct born of compassion and pure conscience has power to dispel and reverse the moral decay of the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 13, 'walk … day. Christian life is to be characterized by moral purity and transparency.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 13, 14, 'put on the Lord Jesus Christ. Adopt the conduct and spirit of Jesus (see note on 12:9–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 1, 'weak in the faith. May refer to Jewish converts who had doubts about some religious practices. doubtful things. Matters of opinion which are not of immediate relevance to faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 2, 'all things. Including food forbidden in the Law of Moses, such as the firstfruits (Num. 18:8–15; Deut. 18:3–4), improperly slaughtered animals (Lev. 17:12–16), and food prepared from un-tithed foodstuffs (Deut. 26:13–14) or by a menstruating woman (Lev. 15:19–23). Eating animals forbidden in Lev. 11 cannot have been the problem addressed by this passage: (1) There is no convincing evidence that Christians began eating pork or the flesh of other forbidden animals as early as Paul’s time; and (2) the fact that the weak only ate vegetables and that the controversy also involved beverages (Rom. 14:17, 21) is evidence that the concern focused on ceremonial impurity. See note on Lev. 11:1–47.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 3, 'stand. Stand before the judgment seat of Christ (see note on vv. 10, 12; compare to 2 Cor. 5:10). The individual latitude prescribed here has its limits. It does not apply, for example, to the question of immorality. Paul is not asking the Roman believers to tolerate those who commit adultery. See note on 1 Cor. 5:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 5, 'day. The word used here cannot refer to the seventh-day Sabbath, as Paul’s own practice and writing regularly attest. It is probably a reference to the various Jewish feast days such as Passover and the Day of Atonement, and the days of fasting that often preceded them. There is no serious evidence that Christians in a major way were abandoning the Sabbath as early as Paul’s time. As the Gospels make clear, the main controversy over the Sabbath in the early church was over how to keep it; early Christians rejected the Pharisaical ways of keeping Sabbath. Unquestionable evidence of Sunday-keeping by Christians surfaces only in the second century A.D., after the apostles were gone. In the context of this passage, Paul’s intent in this verse is to urge tolerance for those who are sincere and conscientious in observance of certain ceremonial days, even if those observances are among the “doubtful things” (see note on v. 1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 6, 'to the Lord. Besides the meaning of “belonging to the Lord,” this phrase has a connotation of judgment. Each believer is individually accountable to the Lord Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 9, 'Lord of. Judge over.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 14, 'See notes on vv. 1 and 2. The Greek word used here for “unclean,” koinos, is not used in the Greek Septuagint translation of the OT to refer to unclean animals. But it is used in Mark 7:2–5 to refer to the uncleanness of food eaten with unwashed hands (compare to Lev. 15:19–23). Paul is probably using the term in the same sense as Mark, to reject the Jewish notion that ceremonial or ritual uncleanness could be transmitted to food through touch.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 17, 'Paul is defining purity as inner holiness rather than ritual external purity. This verse gives no license for unhealthy dietary habits or intemperance (see 16:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 18, 'in these things. Through righteousness and peace and joy in the Holy Spirit (v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 20, 'for the sake of food. Arguments about dietary matters have the potential to harm Christian community. All things indeed are pure. While a specific dietary practice may be appropriate, matters of one’s own conscience (vv. 22–23) and the impact of the practice on others (v. 21; 15:1–3) must be in view. evil. Harmful. Refers to injury done to the conscience. With regard to biblical food laws, see note on Mark 7:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 22, 'before God. Before God the Judge. See note on vv. 10, 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 14, 23, 'he who doubts. He whose conscience is troubled. Refers to the act of eating certain foods without a clear conscience. from faith. With a clear conscience before God. Doing what cannot be done with a clear conscience before Christ is sin for the person who is doing it and also for the person who is making him or her do it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 1, 'strong … weak. See note on 14:1. to please. To benefit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 2, 'The essence of Christianity is a release from selfishness. Christians live to benefit others. But see also note on Gal. 1:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 3, 'reproaches. Insults, especially on the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 4, 'patience and comfort of the Scriptures. For more on the role of the BIBLE in the Christian life, see Luke 24:44; John 20:31; 2 Tim. 3:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 5, 'according to Christ Jesus. Like Christ Jesus. The discovery of a new vision of God, that He is patient and encouraging, brings peace and harmony to a community.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 6, 'These verses offer a vision of the end-time community of Jesus and the significance of glorifying God; see Rev. 14:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 8, 'Jesus Christ has become. In the Incarnation. Jesus was born a Jew, becoming a servant to the circumcision. However, His ministry and the salvation He offered extended to both Jews and Gentiles. As Jesus, a Jew, ministered to Gentiles, members of one group—Jews or Gentiles, weak or strong (v. 1)—should willingly serve members of the other.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 9, 'The quotations provide biblical support for the presence of both Jews and Gentiles in the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 13, 'The attributes described in this verse—joy, peace, believing, hope—are due to the presence of the Spirit in the believer’s life (see 8:16–17; 1 Cor. 12:8; Gal. 5:22–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 14, 'filled with all knowledge. Of the reality of the New Covenant (see Jer. 31:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 15, 'because of the grace given to me by God. Paul’s calling as an apostle of the Gentiles (see Gal. 2:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 16, 'ministering. Performing the priestly ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 17, 'the things which pertain to God. Paul’s priestly ministry (see Heb. 2:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 18, 'make the Gentiles obedient. The chief aim of Paul’s priestly ministry is the purification of the Gentiles through the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 19, 'fully. In every major city (see Acts 19:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 21, 'Paul quotes Is. 52:15 to point out that his preaching of the crucified Christ to the Gentiles fulfills the prophecy of Is. 52:13–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 23, 'a place in these parts. Paul has no major cities left in which to work in Asia Minor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 24, 'to be helped. Paul hopes to make Rome his missionary base for his work in the West. He had used Antioch and Ephesus as bases in his previous missionary work.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 25, 'minister. By bringing the relief money to them. See note on v. 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 26, 'Macedonia. The house churches in Berea, Philippi, Thessalonica, and Neapolis. Achaia. The house churches in Corinth. Paul plans to deliver the money collected from these churches to the Jerusalem church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 15, 31, 'saints. The Jewish Christians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 1, 'I commend to you Phoebe. This is a recommendation letter for Phoebe who is probably the carrier of the letter and a new immigrant to Rome. Rome offered no postal service for the Empire; important letters like Romans had to be hand-carried by a trusted personal envoy like Phoebe. servant. Deaconess, a female deacon. On the functions of deacons in the early church, see Acts 6:1–10. Cenchrea. A port city six miles (10 km) east of Corinth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 3, 'Priscilla and Aquila. Jewish Christians from Rome who worked with Paul in Corinth (Acts 18:2–3). They left Rome when Claudius expelled the Jews around A.D. 49. At the time of this letter, they were back in Rome. Paul mentions Priscilla, the wife, first to recognize her prominence in the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 5, 'in their house. Prominent leaders of the early church often owned private homes that were used as churches. It is likely that Priscilla and Aquila used their home in Corinth as a house church (Acts 18:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 6, 'Mary. Could be Jewish but, if Latin, she could be a freed female Gentile slave who used to belong to the Roman family of Marius. The Roman freedmen frequently adopted the names of their former masters as their last name.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 7, 'Junia. A woman. Some translators assume this is a man, Junias, a shortened form of Junianus, but the masculine name has not been found in the extensive Latin texts and inscriptions from the period. countrymen. Fellow Jews.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 8, 'Amplias. Probably a person of slave background.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 9, 'Urbanus. A Roman or Latin name, meaning that he was not a slave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 11, 'Herodion. Probably a person of slave background.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 12, 'Tryphena and Tryphosa. Probably persons of slave background. Persis. Probably a slave.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 13, 'Rufus. Perhaps a son of Simon the Cyrene who carried Jesus’ cross (Mark 15:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 14, 'The names in these verses belong to immigrants from the east and those with slave background. Of these, Hermes and Nereus were common slave names.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 16, 'a holy kiss. Usually on the cheek, this kiss performed the function of a handshake in the ancient world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 17, 'avoid them. Avoid getting into controversy with them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 18, 'their own belly. Intemperance. See note on 14:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 19, 'good … evil. See note on 12:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 22, 'Tertius. Paul’s secretary who wrote down Paul’s dictation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 23, 'Gaius. Baptized by Paul in Corinth (1 Cor. 1:14); may be the same person as God-fearer Justus (Acts 18:7). Paul wrote the letter to the Romans in Corinth while staying with Gaius. Erastus. May be the same person as the city commissioner whose name was found on a stone slab outside the Corinthian forum.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 24, 'Some manuscripts end Romans at this point.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 25, 'my gospel. The gospel as taught by Paul in his letters, especially in this letter to the Romans. mystery kept secret. The way Christ saved the world through the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 26, 'by the prophetic Scriptures. The OT certainly testifies of the gospel (3:21), but the gospel—that the Son of God died on the cross for our sins—is no mere interpretation of the OT. It is a revelation (compare to Gal. 1:12). See Rom. 16:25. for obedience to the faith. See note on 15:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Romanos', 16, 27, 'to God … be glory. Enabling people to return glory to God is the ultimate aim of the gospel (see Rev. 14:7).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'The author of 1 Corinthians is Paul. Suggestions have been made that the letter may be a compilation of several letters that Paul sent to Corinth, but there is no conclusive evidence to support these claims.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'The letter is addressed to Christians living in the city of Corinth. The ancient Greek city of Corinth was destroyed by the Romans in 146 B.C. However, in 44 B.C. Julius Caesar established a Roman colony on the site. It is this rebuilt, Roman city that is the Corinth of the New Testament.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'Paul wrote 1 Corinthians from Ephesus in the spring (16:8), in about A.D. 55. According to Acts 18:12, Paul was tried before Gallio in Corinth. This fact serves as a key reference point for dating this letter. Gallio probably served as proconsul of Achaia in A.D. 51–52. This means that Paul’s ministry in Ephesus, which began soon after his ministry in Corinth (Acts 19:1), would likely have begun in A.D. 52. First Corinthians was written toward the end of his three-year stay in Ephesus (16:8; Acts 20:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'Paul wrote 1 Corinthians to address the issues presented to him by envoys from the church (see 1:11; 16:17). These issues included schisms in the church (1:10–4:21), incest (5:1ff.), lawsuits between members (6:1–8), sexual immorality (6:9–20), marriage and divorce (chap. 7), idolatry (chaps. 8–10), spiritual gifts (chaps. 12–14), and the question of whether the resurrection of Christians has already happened (chap. 15). Gnosticism is probably not the cause of these issues because Christian Gnosticism did not develop until the second century A.D.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'I.      Letter opening (1:1–9)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 1, 'Paul opens his letter in the usual way with greeting and thanksgiving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 1, 'apostle. One sent by the risen Christ to preach the gospel (15:8–10). Sosthenes. Possibly the same person as in Acts 18:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 2, 'Corinth. See Introduction, “Audience.” sanctified … called. The rebuke that follows should be understood in the context of this affirmation. call on … Christ. See notes on Rom. 10:11–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 4, 'grace … to you. Your spiritual gifts (see v. 7). Spiritual gifts are concrete manifestations of grace experienced and expressed in relation to our fellow believers. See chap. 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 6, 'even as. That is to say. testimony of Christ. Christ’s resurrection. confirmed. Through transformation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 7, 'gift. Spiritual gift. eagerly waiting. Indicates that this earth is not our home (see also Phil. 3:20). revelation. Return of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 8, 'confirm you. Enrich you through the spiritual gifts. See vv. 5–6. day … Lord. Judgment at the Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 9, 'faithful. Expressed through kindness and resourcefulness (10:13). into the fellowship of. For a relationship with.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 10, 'The first issue that Paul addresses is the divisions in the church based on preference for, or loyalty to, various preachers. The church has forgotten the centrality of Jesus by exalting human leaders.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 10, 'brethren. Indicates a change of subject. by … Christ. By the authority and meekness of Christ (Rom. 12:1; 2 Cor. 10:1). same. Refers to unity, not uniformity. same … judgment. Unity in spirit; the issue here is not with doctrine but with factions, cliques, or statuses within the church based on evangelistic “pedigree.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 11, 'Chloe’s. Unknown; perhaps part of the envoys from Corinth (16:17). See Introduction, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 12, 'Apollos. Eloquent preacher from Alexandria (Acts 18:24; 19:1). Cephas. Peter. See note on Gal. 2:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 13, 'in the name. Expresses ownership.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 14, 'baptized. Personally baptized. Crispus. Leader of the synagogue in Corinth (Acts 18:8). Gaius. Paul’s friend and host in Corinth (Rom. 16:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 16, 'Yes. Conversational style indicates that Paul dictated the letter. household. Generally included grandparents, parents, children, and slaves; nothing here indicates anything other than the baptism of persons old enough to make a considered decision for Christ. It does not indicate infant baptism. Stephanas. Paul’s first convert in Corinth (16:15); he was among the envoys from Corinth to Paul (16:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 17, 'wisdom of words. Well-crafted rhetoric reflecting the world’s values. cross … no effect. To preach smooth words of the world’s wisdom would be to undermine the challenging message of the cross. The message of the cross is undermined when we think it must be presented in a manner the world considers “wise.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 18, 'power of God. Power to resurrect: the focal point of the gospel. In the book of Romans Paul defines God’s power in relation to righteousness and faith (Rom. 1:16–17), but in 1 Corinthians he does so in relation to wisdom. This was because the threat facing the believers in Rome was Jewish legalism; but for the Corinthian church, it was the Hellenistic culture’s fascination with the pursuit of wisdom. But note the mention of the term “righteousness” in v. 30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 20, 'this age. This fallen world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 21, 'wisdom of God. God’s power of creation, manifested through the cross (Rom. 1:20–21; 2 Cor. 5:17ff.). foolishness. The shame and offense, according to the standards of worldly wisdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 22, 'request … seek. As proof of their deliverance. sign. Miracles (compare to Mark 8:11; John 2:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 23, 'we. Those being saved (v. 18). Christ crucified. The message of the crucified Messiah is unexpected to both Jews and Greeks. stumbling block. An offense because Jews could not imagine that their Messiah would be shamed and mistreated as Christ actually was.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 24, 'called. To salvation. power … wisdom. Also refer specifically to the resurrection (see Matt. 12:39).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 25, 'foolishness … weakness. Refer to the crucifixion. stronger. Refers to the resurrection triumphing over the shame of the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 26, 'Most early Christian converts were slaves or women without status.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 27, 'The character and makeup of the church will remain consistent with God’s “foolish” and “weak” mode of operation revealed in the cross and throughout the OT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 28, 'base. Ignoble. despised. Rejected. things … not. God created the world out of nothing; His new creation is no exception.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 29, 'glory. Boast. Paul dismisses the Corinthians’ factional behavior (v. 12) as an act of boasting, an act of putting on a show of power and wisdom to compare and compete with other humans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 30, 'wisdom … redemption. The power of God. See v. 18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 1, 31, 'LORD. The crucified Jesus. See note on Rom. 10:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 1, 'testimony of God. About the crucified Messiah (see 1:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 2, 'not to know anything. To reject other sophistries and depend on nothing else (see Phil. 3:8). Jesus … crucified. The cross of Jesus is the central message to the Christian faith and to Paul’s and the believer’s very being.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 3, 'Paul may have been ill during this period (see Gal. 4:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 4, 'demonstration … power. Visions, impartation of spiritual gifts, and miracles accompanied Paul’s preaching during this time (Acts 18:9; 19:6, 11–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 6, 'mature. Those who have the Spirit (see vv. 10, 12). this age. This fallen world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 7, 'in a mystery. As something revealed and requiring more than human wisdom. hidden. From the wise of this world. the ages. Creation of the world. glory. Adoption as children of God (see Rom. 8:18, 23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 9, 'Only those who love God can see the cross for what it is: God’s most powerful expression of love toward humans. Love is a work of the Spirit in our hearts (see v. 10; Rom. 5:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 10, 'us. Those who love God (v. 9). deep things. Thoughts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 11, 'things. Thoughts. spirit of the man. The person’s mind and consciousness. However, Paul is not saying that the Spirit is simply the mind of God, but only that the Spirit knows God in the same way as the human mind is aware of its own thought processes (see v. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 12, 'know. Recognize. things … God. Revelations that originate from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 13, 'comparing. We recognize the present activities of the Spirit (spiritual things) by comparing them with the past activities of the Spirit (spiritual) in Scripture, history, nature, and our lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 14, 'natural man. A person wise in worldly wisdom, without the Holy Spirit. spiritually. With the help of the Spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 15, 'he who is spiritual. Anyone who has the Holy Spirit. no one. Who lacks the Spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 2, 16, 'we. Those who have the Holy Spirit. mind. See note on v. 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 1, 'spiritual. Having the Holy Spirit. carnal. Belonging to this fallen world. babes. Ignorant. in Christ. Affirms their calling and standing as saints despite their immaturity (see 1:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 2, 'milk. Basic Christian instruction. solid food. More advanced, challenging instruction in the Christian faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 3, 'carnal. See note on v. 1. envy, strife, and divisions. Indicate belonging to this fallen world. men. People without the Holy Spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 6, 'gave the increase. Steadily supplied the growth. The true engine of church growth is God’s creative power through the Spirit (for “power of God,” see 1:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 8, 'one. United by a common goal, which is to build up the church. reward. God’s approval. God saves us by grace and rewards our labor with success.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 9, 'building. Temple (v. 16). The imagery of building is used as a metaphor in the following verses to explain Paul’s relationship to the Corinthians. The imagery of the field in vv. 5–8 was used to explain his relationship to Apollos (among others). On metaphors used for the CHURCH, see note on Eph. 2:19–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 10, 'grace … me. Paul’s calling as an apostle to the Gentiles. master builder. Architect. foundation. Foundational understanding of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 11, 'Christ. Christ crucified, believed on as the Savior of the world (compare to 1:23; 2:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 12, 'gold … straw. Given in the order of durability and value; truth endures (see note on v. 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 13, 'Day. The day of reckoning and judgment. fire. Tribulations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 14, 'work. Building up the church through teaching. endures. Proves to be true in the midst of tribulation. reward. God’s approval.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 15, 'loss. Disapproval from God. he … saved. One’s work for the gospel may prove to be worthless, but as long as one maintains faith in Christ through trials (i.e., is built on the foundation), he or she will be saved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 16, 'temple … you. Here, the Christian community or church is a temple (see also 2 Cor. 6:16; Eph. 2:19–22; Ezek. 37:26–27). In 1 Cor. 6:19 the image of a temple is used for the individual Christian. On metaphors used for the CHURCH, see note on Eph. 2:19–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 17, 'defiles. With “envy, strife, and divisions” (v. 3), which are products of worldly wisdom. destroy. For an example, see Acts 5:1–11. holy. State derived from the Holy Spirit (see v. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 18, 'fool. One who embraces the weakness and offense of the cross, as in 1:23 and 2 Cor. 12:5–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 19, 'wisdom of this world. What appears as wisdom to fallen humans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 21, 'A foretaste of the time when the entire universe will return to the state of complete harmony under God’s rule (see 15:28); division and infighting is a manifestation of sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 3, 21, 'boast in men. Refers to the factionalism as in 1:12. all. See v. 22. yours. Those who give up worldly wisdom and power to receive the Spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 1, 'mysteries. Truths only known through God’s revelation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 2, 'faithful. To God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 3, 'judged. Investigated—evaluated and compared to Apollos or Cephas. I … myself. Paul realizes that human verdicts, including his own, count as nothing in the final judgment (see vv. 4–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 4, 'I … myself. My conscience is clear. justified. Vindicated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 5, 'judge nothing. Quotes the teachings of Jesus on judging (see Matt. 7:1). time. Final judgment. bring to light. Through investigation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 6, 'these things. (1) The teachings of Jesus on judging referred to in v. 5; and (2) the imageries of the field and temple in 3:5–17. figuratively transferred. Applied. what is written. The boundaries that God has determined for each creature (see Acts 17:26; Jude 6); for example, Paul’s calling as an apostle of the Gentiles constitutes the boundary of his ministry. puffed up … against the other. Division in the community originates from pride and a spirit of judgment (see 1:12–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 7, 'who … differ. Who makes you superior?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 8, 'The Corinthians had confused grace with prestige. They were already rich in God’s grace, so Paul is emphasizing the relative unimportance of prestige.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 9, 'men condemned to death … a spectacle. This graphic picture of a man standing in an arena condemned to death depicts how the apostles visualized accepting Christ’s mission for them—as an absolute loss of all worldly advantages (Phil. 3:7–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 10, 'This verse contrasts the apostles’ correct understanding of grace with an ironic statement of the Corinthians’ misunderstanding of it. They mistakenly think they are wise, strong, and distinguished.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 11, 'Vivid descriptions of the hardships faced by Paul and his coworkers (“apostles,” v. 9). To accept the mission to preach the gospel meant that one was condemned and rejected by the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 14, 'warn you. Of the harm caused by worldly wisdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 15, 'instructors. These supervisors or custodians (the same Greek term is translated as “tutor” in Gal. 3:24–25) were sometimes harsh. fathers. Trusted, loving mentors. begotten you. Facilitated your rebirth (see John 3:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 16, 'imitate me. Adopt the way of life described in vv. 11–13. The converts to early Christianity accepted not only the apostles’ teachings but also their way of life (v. 17; 11:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 17, 'Timothy. Paul’s trusted assistant whose mother was a Jewess (Acts 16:1; Phil. 2:17–23; 1 Tim. 1:2). remind you. Through his own life. my ways in Christ. See vv. 11–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 18, 'puffed up. See note on v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 19, 'word … puffed up. How wise their words are. power. To effect change. See note on 1:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 20, 'is not in. Is not about. word. Wise-sounding words. power. The power of the resurrection (see note on 1:18), which is found in weakness (1 Cor. 1:24–25; 2 Cor. 13:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 4, 21, 'rod. E.g., the harsh tone of v. 10. love … gentleness. E.g., the understanding tone of vv. 14–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 3, 'in spirit. See note on v. 4. judged. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 4, 'Paul is carrying out the command of Jesus in Matt. 18:17–20. along with my spirit. Though I am physically absent, my spirit is present with these words.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 5, 'deliver such a one to Satan. Since the world was thought of as under Satan’s influence, this appears to be a figurative way to describe removing this person from church membership or fellowship. destruction of the flesh. Paul hopes that removing the man from church fellowship will awaken his conscience and result in destroying the flesh, or the man’s sinful inclinations. his spirit. Or “the spirit.” The whole person is viewed as a spiritual being in communion with God. Paul hopes that the individual will return to faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 6, 'glorying. The Corinthians may have prided themselves on their broadmindedness and toleration of sexual immorality.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 7, 'purge out. Concerning removal of leaven, see Ex. 12:15–20. old … new. Transition from old to new occurs through the Spirit (see Rom. 7:6). Christ … us. Christ has delivered us from the slavery of sin (see Ex. 12:11ff.).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 8, 'let … feast. Let us daily celebrate our deliverance from the slavery of sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 9, 'my epistle. Now lost; or perhaps partly preserved in 2 Cor. 6:14–7:1. sexually immoral people. For definition, see vv. 10–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 10, 'covetous … idolaters. Forms of immorality (see 6:9–10; Rom. 1:29–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 5, 13, 'judges. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 1, 'unrighteous. Secular judges. before the saints. The members of the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 2, 'saints will judge. With God and Christ (see Matt. 19:28; Rev. 20:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 3, 'angels. Fallen angels (see Jude 6). This takes place during the Millennium after the Second Coming of Christ. On the MILLENNIUM, see Rev. 20:4–6; 21:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 4, 'judgments. Lawsuits. do you appoint … judge? The answer is no. But even the “least esteemed” in the church is greater than the highest secular judge, because the former is a future judge of the world and angels.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 5, 'Is it … brethren? Paul chides the Corinthians for the irony that they boast of many spiritual gifts, and yet have no one in their church who has the gift of wisdom and judgment (compare to 12:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 7, 'failure. Of the Christian commitment to forgiveness. Forgiveness means that, if the choice came down to being wronged versus doing wrong, we should choose to be wronged. This definition of forgiveness is based on the example of Jesus who accepted the death on the cross, so that He might not destroy us but forgive us.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 9, 'The final judgment is based on deeds (2 Cor. 5:10). homosexuals. Passive male homosexuals. sodomites. Dominant male homosexuals.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 11, 'washed. Baptized. sanctified. Purified by the Spirit. justified. Cleared of debt and sin. Jesus … Spirit … God. The entire Godhead is mentioned in connection with the three saving acts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 12, 'all … lawful. A slogan used by the Corinthians, misrepresenting Christian freedom. all … not helpful. Paul’s counterargument. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 13, 'Foods … them. This slogan was used by the Corinthians to condone sexual immorality. They apparently argued that sexual immorality (“food”) and our sinful flesh (“stomach”) needed each other and that this was harmless because God was going to destroy both at the end of time. To this Paul offers three counterarguments: (1) the redeemed body of the believer needs Christ, not immorality (v. 13b); (2) the believer’s body will not be destroyed but resurrected (v. 14); and (3) the believer’s body belongs to Christ (v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 16, 'joined. Through sexual intercourse. two … one. See Gen. 2:21ff.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 17, 'joined. Through baptism.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 18, 'sin … outside the body. Probably another slogan used by the Corinthians to argue that sin could not harm Christians’ bodies. he … body. Paul’s counterargument is that sin can harm the body. sexual immorality. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 6, 19, 'temple. Sacred dwelling place. not your own. You do not own your body. It is the Christian’s responsibility to care for the body and keep it in good HEALTH. See 10:31. Here we have a connection between the physical and the spiritual. In 3:16 Paul identifies the whole church as the temple of God. In 6:19, however, each believer is a temple in which God dwells. The word used actually means a sacred shrine, which makes the body the abode of the divine. One of Paul’s arguments against immorality is that, since everywhere they go believers bear within themselves the Holy Spirit, any conduct that is not appropriate to the temple of God must be ruled out. They are the members of Christ (v. 15) and temples of the Holy Spirit given by God (see John 14:16–17). Every sin that is committed against our bodies is a sin against the One who gave it to us and against the Holy Spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 1, 'Using the issue of food offered to idols as a starting point, Paul talks about how Christians should love and care for fellow believers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 1, 'offered to idols. The apostles forbade eating anything that had been offered to idols (Acts 15:20, 29). knowledge. That idols are just manmade objects (see vv. 4–7). puffs up. Self-absorbed pride creates division and spiritual complacency in the community (see 4:6; 5:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 3, 'is known by Him. Believers should rejoice not in their own limited knowledge (vv. 1–2) but in God’s knowledge of them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 4, 'an idol is nothing. OT doctrine; see also Is. 44:13; Jer. 10:9–10. no other God. OT doctrine; see Deut. 6:4. See note on 1 Cor. 9:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 5, 'many gods. Many false gods. lords. Deities of mystery religions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 7, 'consciousness of the idol. Still fearful of the supernatural power of the idol. weak. Susceptible to doubts and fears. defiled. By doubts. Committing an act that your conscience condemns is sin even if the act is not intrinsically sinful (Rom. 14:23). For many, festivals at a pagan temple were opportunities to eat meat, a luxury in the ancient world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 9, 'stumbling block. Occasion to violate the conscience. weak. See note on v. 7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 10, 'emboldened. Pressured into eating against their conscience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 11, 'knowledge. See note on v. 1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 12, 'sin against the brethren. Offend your brethren. wound … conscience. Cause them to violate their conscience. sin against Christ. See Matt. 18:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 8, 13, 'eat meat. See note on v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 1, 'apostle. One directly sent by the risen Jesus to preach the gospel. free. Having apostolic rights. seen Jesus Christ our Lord. Met the resurrected Jesus (see 15:5–9; Acts 9:3–6). Are … Lord? Meaning, Did you not experience the power of resurrection through my preaching?');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 2, 'not … to others. Some rejected Paul’s claim to apostleship because he had not been with Jesus during His earthly ministry (Acts 1:21ff.). you are the seal. Your holy life certifies.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 3, 'defense. Proof of apostleship. examine me. Question my apostleship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 4, 'eat and drink. To receive financial support.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 5, 'Paul and Barnabas secured the approval of the apostles for their Gentile ministry (see Gal. 2:7–9), entitling them to the same apostolic privileges. brothers of the Lord. James, Joses, Simon, and Judas (see Matt. 13:55; Mark 6:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 6, 'refrain from working. And receive wages from the communities we serve.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 7, 'war … vineyard … flock. These examples from life highlight Paul’s right to demand payment from the Corinthians for his service to them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 8, 'Gospel workers have the right to material support from those who benefit from their ministry (though Paul himself has not exercised this right, vv. 15–18). See also Gal. 6:6; 1 Tim. 5:18. For the OT background of this STEWARDSHIP principle, in support of the ministry, see Num. 18:24; Deut. 18:1; Mal. 3:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 8, 'as … man. As a matter of selfishness or resentment. law. The Law of Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 9, 'written. In Deut. 25:4. Since elsewhere some Corinthians were using OT arguments to support their position regarding meat offered to idols (see notes on 1 Cor. 8:4), now Paul also appeals to an OT Scripture to argue his case about the support of the workers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 10, 'our sakes. What would God say about our situation? The same system of remuneration especially applies to us. hope. Hope for fair remuneration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 12, 'others. Other missionaries from Jerusalem working in Corinth. we have not used this right. The main thrust of Paul’s discussion here. endure all things. Especially injustice. lest … Christ. The criterion of all of Paul’s decisions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 13, 'For the OT background of these verses, see Num. 18:24; Deut. 18:1; and references to the storehouse in Mal. 3:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 13, 'those who minister. The priests.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 14, 'Lord has commanded. See Matt. 10:10. Demanding a payment for the labor rendered is a minister’s right.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 15, 'none of these things. None of the rights that are Paul’s by virtue of common sense (v. 7), OT Scriptures (vv. 9, 13), and the command of Jesus (v. 14). that it should be done so to me. In order to demand money. better for me to die. Expresses that his ministry is a pure labor of love.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 17, 'willingly. Out of love for Christ. reward. A reason to boast (see v. 18). against my will. As a mere duty. stewardship. Of the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 18, 'without charge. Paul did receive financial support from Philippi (see 2 Cor. 11:9; Phil. 4:15–16). Paul refused monetary support from Corinth because it might further divide the congregation. In fact, Paul even avoided personally baptizing them (see 1:16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 19, 'free from all. Under obligation to none.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 20, 'became as. Does not indicate hypocrisy or dissemblance but conveys the meaning of giving up the rights he has. For example, “became as a Jew” means “gave up my right to live as a Roman,” or as under the law means “gave up the right to live as a person accountable only to the Law of Christ.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 20, 'law. Of Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 22, 'weak. Susceptible to doubts and fears (see 8:7). became all things. Gave up all rights including the right to his own identity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 23, 'gospel’s sake. See v. 12. partaker of it. A beneficiary of grace. See note on 4:11–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 9, 24, 'Giving up one’s rights in order to save others requires the same type of self-control and discipline found in sports.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 1, 'all. The repeated use of this word emphasizes that all the children of Israel had the opportunity of being part of God’s people, even those God eventually destroyed (see v. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 1, 'our fathers. Claims the Jewish ancestors as the spiritual ancestors of the Gentile Christians (see notes on Gal. 6:16; Phil. 3:3). cloud … sea. See Ex. 14:19ff. The cloud represents God’s presence (compare to Ex. 40:34; Mark 9:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 2, 'were baptized. Into a new life free of slavery (see note on Rom. 6:3–4). into Moses. Through Moses; denotes the agent through which one participates in this experience. cloud. God’s presence. in the sea. By means of immersion in the sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 3, 'spiritual food. Manna (see Ex. 16:31–35); alludes to the bread used in communion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 4, 'spiritual drink. See Ex. 17:6; alludes to the wine used in communion. Rock that followed them. According to Jewish tradition. Rock was Christ. The Lord of the OT was Christ (see note on Rom. 10:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 5, 'not well pleased. The baptism in the Red Sea, the manna, and the spiritual drink from the Rock did not help them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 6, 'our examples. Warning us who also have been baptized and have partaken in communion. evil things. Especially immoral activities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 7, 'play. Ancient idolatry was often a system of rituals designed to arouse immoral impulses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 8, 'commit sexual immorality. Implies that the activities described in 6:15ff. were committed while dining in pagan temples. See note on 6:13–15. twenty-three thousand. See Num. 25:1–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 9, 'nor … tempt. Remember that Jesus, meek and mild, is also the Lord of the OT. See note on Rom. 10:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 10, 'complain. About the hardships of Christianity, like the hardships of the wilderness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 11, 'examples. Patterns of sin and divine judgment. ends … ages. The new era of Christ and imminent day of His coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 12, 'Reminder that the journey through the wilderness, the place of temptation, continues until Christ returns.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 13, 'common to man. Especially the OT saints. faithful. Kind and longsuffering.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 15, 'wise … judge. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 16, 'communion of. Participation in. blood … body. Signifying His death. The LORD’S SUPPER and baptism serve the same function, allowing us to participate in the benefits of the death of Christ (see Rom. 6:3–4). For more on the LORD’S SUPPER, see Matt. 26:26–29; 1 Cor. 11:23–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 18, 'Makes two points: (1) sacred objects like the Lord’s Supper, the altar, and pagan idols lack intrinsic value; but (2) eating foods that have been prayed over in immediate connection with these institutions cause one to participate in the life and community of the deities to whom the prayers were offered.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 22, 'Lord. Jesus. jealousy. Compare to Ex. 20:5; see note on 1 Cor. 10:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 23, 'All … me. Uttered by the “strong” who went to eat meat at pagan temples. See notes on v. 8 and 6:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 24, 'The essence of Christian ethics.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 25, 'in the meat market. Where no pagan rituals take place. conscience’ sake. See 8:7. earth is the LORD’s. You may eat food offered to idols (1) if it is sold in a neutral place where no pagan ritual is being performed; and (2) if you have no knowledge that it was in fact offered to idols.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 27, 'asking … conscience’ sake. Conscience is affected by information, not food, per se.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 28, 'anyone. Either the host or a “weak” Christian who was invited with you. This … idols. Pagan Roman households (see v. 27) routinely offered a portion of their meals to their household idols before eating.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 29, 'other. Weak brother or sister invited with you. why … conscience? Paul asks why an established believer would allow himself to be criticized by weaker believers on the basis of what he eats.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 30, 'evil spoken of. Judged. See note on v. 29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 31, 'all to the glory of God. The whole purpose of Christian existence. The apostle provides a principle that should guide every aspect of the Christian’s life. The overarching principle of action for the Christian is the upholding of the glory of God. Christians are not concerned about their rights; neither do they have the license to follow the suggestions of the natural, unconverted heart and the impulses of the unregenerate body. Thought, word, and deed are all to be brought into harmony with God’s revealed will, thereby advancing the glory of God. For the reason people should glorify God, see 6:19–20. On God’s original dietary plan given to preserve life and HEALTH, see Gen. 1:29–30. On drinking to God’s glory, see Prov. 20:1; 23:29–30; Is. 19:14; Luke 21:34; Rom. 13:13; 1 Cor. 6:10; Eph. 5:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 32, 'Give no offense. Over frivolous matters.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 10, 33, 'just … own profit. Just as I give up my rights. See note on 9:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 1, 'See note on 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 2, 'Paul deals with improper corporate worship behavior. This discussion provides an opportunity for Paul to explain the true nature of the church and how believers should live.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 2, 'traditions … delivered. Teachings passed on (see 15:3). The teaching in question is about gender difference in worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 3, 'I want you to know. A formula introducing vital new information. head. (1) Source of the “image and glory” (v. 7); (2) a representative.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 4, 'Apparently, men uncovered their heads and women covered their heads while they prayed or prophesied. This could have been an attempt to symbolically approximate the genderless, angelic existence that was to be lived after the resurrection (Mark 12:25). dishonors his head. Embarrasses Christ and God (v. 3). How we dress during worship either honors or dishonors God. dishonors her head. Embarrasses her husband. shaved. She would look like she were either a prostitute or transgender.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 6, 'if … covered. During worship. if it is shameful. In public. Paul is asking: How can we present ourselves in worship in ways we would not even appear in public? Point: Though led by a correct theology, we could end up creating a worship service that defies common sense.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 7, 'See notes on vv. 3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 8, 'See Gen. 2:20–23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 10, 'For this reason. Because of the reasons stated in vv. 7–9. authority. Out of respect for their husbands and marriages, veils were worn by married women in Roman culture. because of the angels. In recognition that angels are present in worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 11, 'independent of. Without influence on. How a man or a woman dresses and behaves in worship brings honor or dishonor to the other worshipers and to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 12, 'man also … woman. Paul is not arguing for men’s superiority over women but rather the interdependence of each upon the other as created by God. Every human being was created in the image of God (see Gen. 1:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 13, 'Judge. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 14, 'nature itself teach. Through inherent gender differences (see Rom. 1:26–27). has long hair. Looks like a woman.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 15, 'has long hair. Looks feminine. covering. Sign of her respect (see note on v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 16, 'we … God. Worship practices may not deviate too widely from the accepted practices of the church, even if they have theologically valid reasons.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 17, 'these instructions. On the Lord’s Supper. come together. For communion (see v. 20). the better. Spiritual and moral improvement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 18, 'divisions. Between wealthy and poor (see 1:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 19, 'must also be. However difficult, factions in a congregation can help to identify those with the Christian strength of character to work as leaders and bring people together. approved. Honorable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 20, 'it is not to eat. The Lord’s Supper has value only when it is celebrated with the right understanding. See notes on vv. 24–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 21, 'ahead of others. The poor members did not get much of the Supper because others apparently ate most of the food intemperately and even became drunk.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 22, 'Do you not have houses. This is generally understood to mean that Paul was chiding them for their poor manners and for treating the Supper as just another opportunity for intemperance at a festive meal. It is also possible that the Corinthians may have misunderstood the Supper to be food for the “spiritually resurrected” (see 10:3), which may have caused people to gorge themselves with this “magical food.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 23, 'Likely the earliest account of the LORD’S SUPPER. See also 1 Cor. 10:16–17. For the inauguration of the Lord’s Supper, see Matt. 26:26–29; Luke 22:19–24; John 13:1–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 23, 'received from the Lord. Teachings of Jesus received directly by the apostles. delivered. See note on v. 2. Lord … bread. Compare to Mark 14:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 24, 'given thanks. Communion means nothing if not received with thanksgiving. broke it. From one loaf, to symbolize unity and mutual partnership. Take. To receive forgiveness. eat. To participate. broken. At the cross, to heal division (see 1:11ff.; Eph. 2:14–16). for you. So that we might also suffer for Him (Rom. 8:17; Phil. 1:29). in remembrance of Me. To reenact His life, death, and resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 25, 'manner. Holy manner. cup. Wine. new covenant. The promise of (1) the Spirit; and (2) obedience from the heart. For more on the new COVENANT, see Jer. 31:31–34; 2 Cor. 3:6; Heb. 8:8–10ff. in My blood. Ratified with the blood of the Son of God. in remembrance of Me. See note on v. 24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 26, 'proclaim. The focus of the Lord’s Supper is on the death of Christ. His resurrection makes possible the resurrection of His followers, but that will not happen till He comes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 27, 'unworthy manner. Puffed up, as described in vv. 18–22. guilty. As if the person had betrayed Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 29, 'judgment. Condemnation. not discerning. Not recognizing the food and drink as representing Christ’s sacrifice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 30, 'weak … sleep. Quite contrary to their magical expectations (see note on v. 22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 32, 'when. Even when. chastened. Merely chastened.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 11, 33, 'wait … another. Treat everyone equally, regardless of social standing (see note on v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 1, 'spiritual gifts. Skills and talents sanctified or specially given by the Spirit for use in ministry. I … ignorant. Signals an important revelation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 2, 'carried away. By a demonic force. led. Led away, like prisoners. The rational experience of the Holy Spirit differs from the frenzied ecstasies and trances found in pagan rituals.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 3, 'Jesus accursed. This may have been an expression used by some in a misguided attempt to more fully experience Christ’s Spirit by deemphasizing the flesh, or literal personhood, of Jesus (Rom. 8:3–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 4, 'These verses outline the organization of God’s redemptive work. God originates the work of redemption. Christ organizes this work into different ministries assigned to “ministers” in various capacities (see 3:5). The Spirit organizes and interweaves the various spiritual gifts around these ministries (see 12:7, 11). But note the overlap among the spiritual gifts as well as among the functions within the Trinity (vv. 18, 28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 7, 'See also vv. 27–30. Compare with lists of SPIRITUAL GIFTS in Rom. 12:4–8; Eph. 4:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 8, 'word of wisdom. The ability to think carefully and offer Spirit-inspired judgment (see 6:5). word of knowledge. Capacity to teach. Both gifts seek to understand, expound, and implement the “foolishness” of the cross (see 1:18–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 9, 'faith. A special capacity for confidence and encouragement (beyond the faith possessed by all believers). healings. Natural and supernatural.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 10, 'prophecy. Spirit-inspired revelations given to specific people. discerning of spirits. See Introduction, “Message.” tongues. The gift of tongues—the gift of speaking foreign languages without having learned them—is listed last to emphasize the relative unimportance of this gift.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 11, 'same Spirit works. To eliminate competition.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 12, 'members. Body parts and vital organs. all … body. Emphasis on all. so … Christ. Christ is present in the world through the members of His church, who, in their great variety, make up “the body of Christ” (see Matt. 18:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 13, 'one Spirit. The same Spirit. all … body. Emphasis on all: no division or competition is allowed. baptized. Through baptism we are united with Christ’s body, the church (v. 27; Eph. 1:22–23; Col. 1:18, 24). For more on BAPTISM, see Matt. 28:19–20; Rom. 6:3–8. Jews … free. None, however insignificant, is excluded. drink. Perhaps alludes to drinking from one cup, symbolizing unity (see 10:16, 21; Mark 14:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 18, 'God. See note on vv. 4–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 19, 'if … one member. If everyone had the same gift, however important. where … be. The church would cease to exist.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 20, 'now indeed. In reality. many members. Many types of spiritual gifts. one body. One church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 23, 'Just as people cover or guard certain parts of their bodies, so special attention should be given to the humbler, weaker members of the church as is necessary and important (see vv. 21–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 25, 'schism in the body. Division, a key problem of Christianity. care. In place of competition.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 26, 'Definitive description of an ideal church community.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 27, 'For other lists of SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see vv. 8–10; Rom. 12:4–8; Eph 4:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 27, '(1) The church is the sum of its members; and (2) every member, however insignificant, is a vital and indispensable part of Christ’s body (see notes on v. 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 28, 'church. Viewed universally throughout history as well as locally. first. Listed in the order of importance relative to the proclamation of the gospel. apostles. Those who have been sent by the resurrected Christ, either in person or by vision (see 9:1; 15:9). prophets. God’s messengers. teachers … tongues. See notes on vv. 8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 12, 31, 'best gifts. Gifts that most directly contribute to the building up of the community. more excellent way. The best way to experience the Spirit is through love (see 13:1ff.).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 2, 'knowledge. See also 8:1; John 17:3. faith. Compare to Matt. 17:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 3, 'bestow all my goods. See also Mark 10:21. give … burned. Become a martyr.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 4, 'Love. A quality that promotes community and freedom simultaneously.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 4, 'suffers … kind. Like Christ (see Ex. 34:6). envy. E.g., 1 Cor. 3:3. puffed up. E.g., 4:6, 18–19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 5, 'behave rudely. E.g., 11:21. not seek its own. As in 9:12, in contrast to 6:12 (see note). thinks no evil. E.g., 6:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 6, 'iniquity. E.g., Rom. 1:18ff.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 7, 'Describes how Jesus deals with us; to love means to see past the present failings of a person to what he or she shall be as God’s new creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 8, 'Love never fails. Of our spiritual gifts, only love will survive as part of the new creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 9, 'Our knowledge and prophecies will become obsolete because of their partial, i.e., imperfect, nature (v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 10, 'that which is perfect. The full revelation of God’s glory, toward which history is moving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 11, 'I spoke … understood … thought. I used to use my powers of speech, knowledge, and understanding (see 12:8–10). as a child. In an immature and selfish way (see 14:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 12, 'dimly. (1) Distorted: ancient mirrors, generally made from brass, produced distorted images; and (2) indirectly, only as reflected images.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 13, 13, 'faith, hope, love. The fruit of the Spirit that center on God rather than us (see Rom. 5:5; Gal. 5:5–6, 22). greatest. Because God is love (1 John 4:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 1, 'Pursue … gifts. Reminder that love does not take the place of spiritual gifts. prophesy. Impart spiritual understanding and conviction to others (see vv. 3, 24–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 2, 'tongue. A spiritual gift (12:10, 28; see note on 14:5). to God. Privately with God. no one understands. Unless there is an interpreter (vv. 5, 28). mysteries. Things that are incomprehensible unless revealed by God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 3, 'edification and exhortation and comfort. Examples of different functions of prophecy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 4, 'edifies. Builds up.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 5, 'tongues. In line with the manifestation of the gift elsewhere in the Bible (Mark 16:17; Acts 2:1–13; 10:44–48; 19:6) and because the term used for “tongue” here means “language,” the gift of tongues in 1 Corinthians may be understood as the Spirit-granted ability to speak foreign languages (see also 12:10, 28, 30). However, other views (the ability to speak the language of angels or other ecstatic or unintelligible utterances) have also been defended in the context of this chapter.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 6, 'by revelation, by knowledge, by prophesying, or by teaching. Examples of different types of speaking or preaching.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 7, 'Language, including the language of music, is for clear communication, and the gift of tongues (see note on v. 2) is no exception to this rule.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 12, 'excel. Abound in spiritual gifts. A congregation grows not simply because it has people with exceptional abilities, but because the members encourage each other and build each other up.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 13, 'interpret. Includes understanding what he or she is saying and communicating it intelligibly to others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 14, 'my spirit prays. Unarticulated inner feelings and thoughts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 15, 'In Christian public prayer, the spirit—the deep inner feelings and thoughts—needs to be articulated by comprehensible words, just as Jesus expressed His deep agony with intelligible words in His prayer (Mark 14:36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 16, 'spirit. See note on v. 14. he … uninformed. One who has not had the same experience or, perhaps, a non-Christian.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 19, 'with my understanding. That can be understood. tongue. Language that cannot be understood.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 20, 'do … understanding. Do not let your thinking be childish. Speaking in unintelligible tongues in public worship represents a childish, selfish thinking.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 21, 'law. OT Scripture. With … lips. Paul uses Is. 28:11 (which prophesies the exile of God’s people) to point out that the word of God spoken in unfamiliar languages is a sign not of blessing but of curse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 23, 'uninformed. See note on v. 16. out of your mind. Experiencing an alternate mental state common in false worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 24, 'Describes the work of the “prophets” (see v. 29).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 26, 'psalm. Hymn or song (e.g., Phil. 2:5–11; 1 Tim. 3:16). revelation. A special instruction from God that may be short or long, explicit or symbolic (see Gal. 2:2; Rev. 1:1ff.). interpretation. Of tongues.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 28, 'to himself and to God. Privately.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 29, 'two or three. Unlike modern forms, the worship of the early church included many spontaneous prophetic discourses; Paul wants to limit them to two or three (see v. 27). prophets. For their functions, see vv. 24–25. judge. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 30, 'revealed. See note on v. 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 31, 'all … encouraged. From each other.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 32, 'subject to. (1) Controlled by; (2) tested by (1 Thess. 5:20–21; 1 John 4:1); see Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 33, 'Manifestations of the Spirit are never chaotic.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 34, 'Note that earlier Paul does assume that women will pray and prophesy in public worship (11:5, 13). These verses perhaps address a problem that arose when women prophets tried to judge, test, and silence the prophetic utterances of their husbands and other men during public worship (see 14:29–30). See note on 1 Tim. 2:8–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 37, 'let him acknowledge. He will recognize; see Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 14, 40, 'done decently and in order. Indicates a spirit-filled Christian life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 1, 'In the face of unbelief and questions about a bodily resurrection, Paul teaches the truth and significance of the resurrection of Jesus and His followers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 1, 'hold fast. Guard against spiritualizing the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 3, 'A simple declaration of the essence of Christ’s mission. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 5, 'Cephas. Peter (Luke 24:34). twelve. The eleven original disciples plus Matthias (Acts 1:13–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 6, 'over five hundred. Mentioned only here in the NT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 7, 'James. The brother of Jesus (Gal. 1:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 8, 'born out of due time. Refers to Paul’s unexpected induction into the apostolic inner circle (Gal. 2:7–9). Encountering the resurrected Jesus, who commissioned Paul to preach the gospel, gave apostolic rights to Paul (see 1 Cor. 9:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 9, 'apostles. See note on 1:1. not worthy. Captures the attitude with which one must receive grace. I persecuted. See Acts 9:1ff.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 11, 'they. The other apostles. so. (1) In terms of grace; and (2) in terms specified in vv. 3–7; these two points constitute an apostolic agreement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 12, 'Believing that Christ was resurrected means also believing that the dead will be raised at Christ’s coming. no resurrection. No future resurrection. Some Corinthians believed that the resurrection takes place at baptism. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 13, 'no resurrection. No future resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 14, 'our preaching. All the expounding of the gospel and OT Scripture.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 15, 'false witnesses of God. False interpreters of the OT. do not rise. In the future, bodily resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 16, 'if … rise. If there is no future resurrection. Christ is not risen. What we have preached about Jesus would be false.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 17, 'your faith. In the Christ-event. in your sins. (1) Under the wrath of God; and (2) in a rejected state as Gentiles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 20, 'the firstfruits. A representation and guarantee of the future resurrection. Although others were resurrected before Christ, Christ is the One through whom all resurrections, whether past or future, are possible.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 21, 'by … death. Refers to Adam’s fall. See Rom. 5:12. Man … resurection. Christ’s resurrection ends the reign of death initiated by Adam.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 22, 'all. Both righteous and unrighteous.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 23, 'at His coming. Believers who have died will experience resurrection at Christ’s Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 24, 'Then … end. The complete end of the present evil age (see Gal. 1:4) does not happen until the saints have been bodily raised from the dead.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 25, 'He must … feet. From Ps. 110:1. enemies. In the present context, Paul broadens the definition of the “enemies” in Ps. 110:1–2 to apply to all created things, animate and inanimate, visible and invisible, that defy the will of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 26, 'last … destroyed. Like the resurrection, the eradication of evil also takes place in its “own order” (v. 23). Complete eradication of evil in all its forms is an essential part of Christ’s High Priestly ministry (see Ps. 110:5–6; Rev. 19:15–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 27, 'He … feet. Paul takes this quote from Ps. 8:6 as referring to the final victory by Christ, the Second Adam, who will resume the dominion lost by Adam (Heb. 2:8–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 28, 'all … subject to Him. All creation and all humanity—some through obedience to Him and others through their final destruction (see v. 26). Son. As the Second Adam, Christ will represent humanity in perfect submission to God. The reference is not to Christ’s role in the Trinity but His function as the Second Adam. subject. Or submissive. For expressions of the submission of the Son to GOD THE FATHER, see John 3:16. God … all. God’s will may be absolutely done (see Matt. 6:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 29, 'for the dead. One possible explanation is that some Christians were baptized in Corinth so that they would meet dead loved ones at the first resurrection. Paul neither approves nor recommends the practice. He simply argues—in his overall discussion about the resurrection—that this practice is worthless if there is no final resurrection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 30, 'Reminder that Christianity is about martyrdom, not simply about spiritual bliss and ethical uprightness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 32, 'beasts. Refers figuratively to the opposition Paul experienced at Ephesus (Acts 19:23–41) or to an event not recorded in Acts. “Let … die!” Why endure all of this if there is no future hope and no resurrection? Unlike many modern Christians, the early Christians often suffered privation and martyrdom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 15, 33, 'Evil company. Paul does indeed believe there is a future hope (v. 32) and so current behavior matters, including choosing good friends, avoiding sin, and gaining a knowledge of God (see 6:12–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 1, 'collection. Money collected for the impoverished Jewish Christians in Jerusalem (see notes on Gal. 2:10; see also Acts 20:1; Rom. 15:26–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 2, 'On … week. The Jews did not handle money on the Sabbath, especially during worship. Paul’s counsel demonstrates that the early Gentile and Jewish Christians worshiped together on the Sabbath. lay something aside. Privately at home.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 3, 'Compare to 2 Cor. 8:18–19. your gift. The offering was voluntary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 4, 'Shortly after he wrote this letter, Paul decided to accompany the men to Jerusalem (Acts 19:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 5, 'Macedonia. The churches in Philippi and Thessalonica.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 6, 'I … with you. Eventually, Paul went directly to Macedonia from Ephesus via Troas before coming to Corinth (see 2 Cor. 2:1, 12). winter. Unsafe time for ships to voyage (Acts 27:12ff.). wherever I go. Paul eventually decided to go to Rome and then to Spain after delivering the collection money to Jerusalem (Rom. 15:25–28; see also Acts 19:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 7, 'I … way. See v. 5. Lord permits. See James 4:13–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 8, 'in Ephesus. Probably toward the end of his three-year stay there (see Acts 20:31). Pentecost. Fifty days from Passover (see Deut. 16:8–10), probably in May.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 9, 'See Acts 19 for Paul’s work and trials in Ephesus (compare to 15:32).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 10, 'if Timothy comes. Timothy was Paul’s trusted associate (see note on Phil. 2:19). He was sent before the letter was completed (4:17). He traveled from Ephesus to Corinth via Macedonia (Acts 19:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 11, 'despise him. Because of his youthful age (1 Tim. 4:12). the brethren. All the believers from Corinth (see vv. 15–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 12, 'brother. Expresses deep and mutual affection (see 3:4). Apollos. A Jewish preacher from Alexandria (Acts 18:24). quite unwilling. Lest he exacerbate the schism in Corinth (see 1 Cor. 1:12; 4:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 13, 'stand fast. In the truth and freedom of the gospel (see 15:1; Gal. 5:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 15, 'household of Stephanas. See note on 1:16; his house was used as a church. Achaia. See Introduction, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 16, 'submit. In love; no coercion is implied here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 17, 'Stephanas … Achaicus. Visitors from Corinth who brought Paul the questions of the Corinthian church. what was lacking. Warmth and fellowship, which the Corinthians only reluctantly gave Paul.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 18, 'acknowledge such men. As if they were me.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 19, 'churches of Asia. Those listed in Acts 13–14; Rev. 1:11; as well as the church in Colosse. Aquila and Priscilla. See note on Rom. 16:3. church … house. See note on Rom. 16:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 20, 'holy kiss. See note on Rom. 16:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Coríntios', 16, 22, 'be accursed. Removed from the roster of the saved (Ex. 32:32; Rom. 9:2). O Lord, come! Prayer of invitation for the presence of Christ in our lives and worship, as well as a prayer of yearning for His Second Coming.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'Paul’s authorship of 2 Corinthians is virtually uncontested. The church fathers, as well as nearly all scholars, consider Paul to be the author of this letter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'Things went badly during Paul’s second visit to Corinth (2:1–4; 13:2). The Corinthian believers turned their backs on him because they suspected him of insincerity and fraud. The problem was largely caused by false teachers who had come in (11:13ff.). It is unclear, however, whether they were already there when Paul wrote 1 Corinthians and whether they may have been the cause of some of the issues addressed by the letter.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'Paul wrote 2 Corinthians from Macedonia (7:5ff.) within a year of writing 1 Corinthians (see 2 Cor. 8:10; 9:2), between A.D. 56 and 57. See “Date and Place of Writing” in the Introductions to Romans and 1 Corinthians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'The problems in Corinth (see “Audience” above) do not fully explain the contents of 2 Corinthians because discouraging and trying circumstances do not necessarily produce such uplifting thoughts as expressed in this letter. Second Corinthians is a deposit of the inspired words and attitudes that this great apostle generated from his gospel and used to overcome obstacles threatening his ministry and congregation.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction (1:1–2:13)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 1, 'After a brief salutation and introduction, Paul gives a lengthy explanation of his conduct and a defense of his apostolic ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 1, 'apostles. See note on 1 Cor. 1:1. Timothy. See Phil. 2:19–20 and notes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 4, 'Suffering endured in hope results in (1) being able to comfort and encourage others who experience similar suffering (vv. 4, 6–7); (2) deeper appreciation of Christ’s suffering (v. 5); (3) the salvation of others (v. 6); (4) patience (v. 6); (5) greater hope and certainty (v. 7); (6) experience of deliverance (v. 10); and (7) prayer and thanksgiving (v. 11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 4, 'comforts. Encourages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 5, 'sufferings of Christ. Paul talks about sharing in Christ’s sufferings through suffering for Christ. See note on Col. 1:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 6, 'which … enduring. The consolation becomes effective by enduring.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 9, 'sentence of death. A figure of speech; see 11:23–27; Acts 19:24–41; 1 Cor. 15:32; may also be referring to sickness (Gal. 4:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 11, 'gift. Of encouragement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 12, 'testimony … conscience. Christians’ most important self-defense (Rom. 9:1). fleshly wisdom. The accusation of worldly wisdom in 1 Cor. 2:4ff. was flung back at Paul for changing his travel plans (see 2 Cor. 1:15–24). grace of God. Humility before God; see Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 15, 'second benefit. (1) A second, longer visit (1 Cor. 16:6); (2) an added blessing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 16, 'Paul did not return to Corinth as originally promised (1 Cor. 16:5–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 17, 'Yes … No. Probably this is the way some Corinthians mocked Paul’s change of plans. The precise nature of the charges is unclear.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 18, 'God is faithful. Anticipates vv. 20–22; see notes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 19, 'Silvanus. Silas, Paul’s traveling companion (e.g., Acts 15:35–40). Timothy. See note on v. 1. not Yes and No. E.g., raised and not raised; coming again and not coming again. Yes. As in: yes, He is raised; yes, He is coming again.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 20, 'all … God. Every OT promise. Yes. Fulfilled, not literally but in terms of God’s purpose; so were Paul’s promised travel plans (see note on v. 16). Amen. Meaning “it is true.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 21, 'us. Used repeatedly in vv. 21–22 to emphasize the bond between him and the Corinthians; there is no animosity (v. 24). anointed. Like kings, prophets, and priests in the OT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 22, 'sealed us. As God’s own possessions. guarantee. Of our salvation, which is guaranteed by the presence of the Holy Spirit in our lives. Compare Eph. 1:13–14; 4:30.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 23, 'came no more. See note on vv. 15–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 1, 24, 'your joy. Rather than your sorrow (2:2, 5). by faith. By your own faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 1, 'come … you. See note on 1:16. in sorrow. (1) To grieve you; (2) to be grieved. See note on 1:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 3, 'I wrote. Paul is not referring to 1 Corinthians. Some believe that the letter of “many tears” may have been partly preserved in 2 Cor. 10–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 5, 'Returns to the theme of solidarity (1:21–22; see notes) and to the notion of the body (1 Cor. 12:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 6, 'majority. Supporters of Paul. such a man. Perhaps the man described in 1 Cor. 5:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 7, 'comfort. Encourage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 8, 'reaffirm your love. Through forgiveness and encouragement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 9, 'I also wrote. This letter was not preserved.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 10, 'When a congregation (even consisting of just two or three) forgives an offender, God also forgives him or her (Matt. 18:18–20). presence of Christ. See Matt. 18:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 11, 'his devices. That quietly transform a forgiving, compassionate community into an unforgiving and uncaring one.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 12, 'Troas. Port city connecting Asia and Macedonia.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 13, 'I … spirit. Because of the situation in Corinth (7:7). Titus. Paul’s trusted associate, sent to Corinth, perhaps with the tearful letter (2:4; 8:23). taking … them. Cutting short the work in Troas. I … Macedonia. To meet up with Titus (7:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 14, 'fragrance. Describes: (1) the ability of the gospel to spread and penetrate quickly and widely; and (2) the gospel ministry as an odor pleasing to God, a sacrifice that God accepts (Lev. 1:9, 13, 17; Rom. 15:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 2, 17, 'peddling … God. Paul is not selling the gospel for gain with insincerity as his opponents do.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 1, 'epistles of commendation. Paul’s opponents apparently carried letters of endorsement, perhaps from Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 2, 'written … hearts. Carried in our hearts; expresses affection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 3, 'New covenant language. For more on the new COVENANT, see Jer. 31:31–34; Ezek. 36:26. epistle of Christ. His letter of recommendation to the world. ministered. (1) Delivered; (2) prepared. tablets of stone. The law was originally written by God on two tablets of stone (Ex. 24:12; 31:18). tablets of flesh. Living, responsive hearts, restored to life through the Spirit. Compare Heb. 8:10, in which the law is written in the hearts of new covenant believers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 6, 'new covenant. That works transformation (v. 18). letter. Refers to legalism or the written record of the law. Spirit. Giver of life and freedom (vv. 17–18). When people pervert the law by using it in a legalistic way it can become a ministry of death. Since one of the law’s major functions is to identify sin (Rom. 3:20), it was never given as the way to have salvation. For more on the new COVENANT, see Jer. 31:31–34.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 7, 'of death. Of condemning sinners to death. engraved. With God’s finger (Ex. 31:18). on stones. Refers to the Decalogue that could not give life. was glorious. Because the glory of Christ was revealed, to a degree, in the types. children … countenance. See Ex. 34:29–35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 8, 'ministry … Spirit. Ministry imparting life and transformation (v. 18; Rom. 8:2ff.). more glorious. Because it imparts pardon and life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 9, 'ministry of condemnation. Work of the Mosaic system. ministry of righteousness. Work of the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 10, 'what … glorious. The face of Moses (see Ex. 34:29, 35): the law. glory that excels. The face of Jesus: the gospel (2 Cor. 4:4, 6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 11, 'what … away. The Mosaic system (see vv. 7–8). what remains. Ministry of Christ through the Spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 12, 'such hope. Hope of new creation (5:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 13, 'put a veil. Moses put on a veil so the Israelites would not see that the brightness (or “glory”) of his face was fading (v. 7; Ex. 34:29–35). Like Moses’ face, the glory of the Mosaic system has faded (2 Cor. 3:7). Paul and his coworkers, who serve in the “more glorious” “ministry of the Spirit” (v. 8), need no such strategy but present themselves openly with “great boldness of speech” (v. 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 14, 'blinded. To avoid seeing the condemnation that would have humbled them before God. veil. Barrier. See note on v. 13. in Christ. Who pardons sinners; it is the certainty of pardon that allows us to face the severity of God’s condemnation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 15, 'veil. Here, blindness (4:3–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 16, 'Lord. See Ex. 34:34. The Lord God whom Moses beheld with his unveiled face was the Lord Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 17, 'Lord. Mentioned in Ex. 34:34. Spirit. Not the letter (2 Cor. 3:6). Spirit … Lord. Spirit of Jesus (see note on v. 16; Rom. 8:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 3, 18, 'glory. Image of God in which Adam was created (Gen. 1:26). Spirit … Lord. Also rendered, “from the Lord, the Spirit.” Identifying the Spirit with the “Lord” indicates the personhood and divinity of the HOLY SPIRIT. See also Matt. 28:19; John 14:16–18; Acts 5:3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 1, 'this ministry. Of the new covenant (3:6). mercy. Pardon and renewal. lose heart. Become discouraged.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 2, 'craftiness … deceitfully. Accusations leveled against Paul (12:16). manifestation … truth. Openness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 3, 'our … veiled. May refer to an accusation by some that Paul hid the plain meaning of the OT, like the veil that covered Moses’ face. If so, Paul offers a rebuttal in 3:12ff.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 4, 'god of this age. Satan. glory … God. See note on 3:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 5, 'preach. Promote. your … sake. Your servants as Jesus commanded (Mark 10:42–45).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 6, 'God … darkness. Christ is the light of new creation (5:17; John 1:4–5). glory … Christ. See note on 3:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 7, 'treasure. The “knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ” (v. 6). earthen. Fragile or humble. vessels. Bodies (vv. 10–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 8, 'not crushed. Not emotionally devastated or despondent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 10, 'See note on v. 7. Jesus’ death and resurrection is the treasure; the body is the vessel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 11, 'Not a figure of speech; see 1:9–10. for Jesus’ sake. Like Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 12, 'Just as Jesus died to give us life and encouragement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 13, 'we also believe. See 1 Cor. 15:3ff.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 14, 'raise … Jesus. At Jesus’ return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 15, 'Paul’s personal mission statement: to (1) serve others; (2) spread grace; (3) increase thanksgiving; and (4) glorify God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 16, 'outward man. Body. inward man. Mind (Rom. 12:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 4, 17, 'Faith demands an eternal perspective.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 1, 'if. When. earthly house, this tent. Earthly or mortal body. destroyed. Either at death or the Second Coming (1 Cor. 15:51–52). we have. We will receive. building from God. Resurrected body.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 2, 'in this. In this mortal body. groan. In hope. See Rom. 8:23. clothed. Refers to the resurrection (1 Cor. 15:53–54). our … heaven. The resurrected body.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 3, 'naked. Without a body. Seems to be Paul’s image for death—neither being clothed with the mortal or the immortal body, but naked. Since to have “life” (v. 4) and to be “present with the Lord” (v. 8) require possession of the immortal body (vv. 1–2), the passage denies the idea of a bodiless soul enjoying eternal life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 4, 'tent. Mortal body. groan. See also Rom. 8:23–24. unclothed. See note on 2 Cor. 5:3. clothed. See note on v. 2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 5, 'guarantee. First payment, the resurrection being the final installment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 6, 'at home … from the Lord. Some see in 4:16–5:10 (and especially in 5:6, 8) the view that at death the soul of the believer is freed from the body and returns to God, an unbiblical perspective (see notes on John 11:11–14; 1 Cor. 15:51–55). The passage must be read in conjunction with 1 Cor. 15:12–58, Paul’s discussion of the resurrection, which he penned to these same believers a short time before and where he is painstakingly clear that the resurrection and the beginning of eternal life occur at the Second Coming (see esp. 1 Cor. 15:52–53). Here Paul is contrasting earthly life (“at home in the [earthly] body we are absent from the Lord”) with the heavenly existence he desires to experience (“to be absent from the [earthly] body and to be present with the Lord”; see Rom. 8:18–23) and is not interested in the timing of when the exchange takes place. See note on Phil. 1:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 7, 'walk. Conduct ourselves.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 8, 'be absent … body. Put on the resurrected body. be … Lord. See Jesus face to face (1 Cor. 13:12). Paul would like to move from one mode of existence to the other without experiencing death. To “be present with the Lord” means to enjoy full personal fellowship with Him in His immediate presence. This will take place at the resurrection from the dead (1 Thess. 4:16–17). See notes on 2 Cor. 5:6; Phil. 1:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 9, 'present or absent. Living or dying (Rom. 14:7–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 10, 'we must all. Including Christians. judgment … Christ. At the end of time Christ the merciful Savior will become the Judge of all humanity (1 Cor. 6:2). For more on the certainty of JUDGMENT, see Acts 17:31; Rom. 14:10. each one. Everyone individually. what … done. Whether or not the body was used as (1) a temple of the Spirit (1 Cor. 6:19); and (2) a sacrifice offered to God (Rom. 12:1, 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 11, 'terror … Lord. See note on 1 Cor. 10:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 12, 'boast in appearance. See 2 Cor. 10:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 13, 'A completely unselfish life dedicated to God can sometimes appear inconsistent, even insane (Mark 3:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 14, 'love of Christ. For the world (v. 19). compels us. To live for others (v. 15). all died. However, everyone has the potential for a new life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 15, 'those who live. Those raised to a new life by baptism (Rom. 6:3–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 16, 'according … flesh. As a mere human being.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 17, 'in Christ. By uniting with Him in death and resurrection. new creation. Part of new creation. Compare with John 3:3, 5; Rom. 6:1–11. old … new. The new arrived through Jesus’ death and resurrection. God was reconciling the world through Him (2 Cor. 5:18). For more on SALVATION, see note on Rom. 3:21–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 18, 'all things. New creation: see vv. 14–17. ministry of reconciliation. (1) Facilitates new creation; and (2) removes hostility toward God and replaces it with peace with God (v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 19, 'God. Taking the initiative. in Christ. In human weakness, although not all recognize or appreciate it. reconciling. For more on SALVATION, see note on Rom. 3:21–26. not imputing … to them. Christ assumed the penalty for sins on the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 20, 'ambassadors. Has the same meaning as apostles, both being sent by Christ; one who accepts the ambassador accepts the sender (Mark 9:37; John 13:20). Paul and his co-workers represent Christ and deliver His message. through us. Through our suffering ministry (2 Cor. 6:4ff.). implore. By persuasion, not force. be … God. A central message of 2 Corinthians: be reconciled to God by being reconciled to us, His ambassadors (6:11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 5, 21, 'sin for us. Jesus became our sin offering on the cross and vicariously accepted our punishment. This is the grand exchange of the gospel—we exchange our sin for His righteousness. For more on SALVATION through Christ’s substitutionary atonement, see Mark 10:45; Rom. 3:21–26; Gal. 3:10–13; Eph. 1:7. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21. righteousness. New creations (2 Cor. 5:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 6, 1, 'not … vain. By rejecting Paul’s apostleship and message (vv. 11–13; see note on 5:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 6, 2, 'See note on 5:10; compare to Is. 49:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 6, 4, 'A true minister of God displays: (1) patience in adversity (v. 4); (2) the fruit of the Holy Spirit in all adverse relationships (v. 6; see also Gal. 5:22–23); (3) courage in the face of threats (2 Cor. 6:7; see Eph. 6:13–17); and (4) the miracle of regeneration, the ability to rebound, that testifies to the reality of the resurrection (2 Cor. 6:8–10). See 11:23–33 for further details about Paul’s hardships. Paul’s sufferings were voluntary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 6, 11, 'See note on 5:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 6, 14, 'be … together. Refers to marriage but can also apply to other forms of intimate associations with unbelievers (see note on 1 Cor. 10:18–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 6, 15, 'Belial. Satan; occurs only here in the NT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 6, 16, 'Marriage between believers and unbelievers is forbidden because: (1) marriage is not simply between two individuals but also their communities (vv. 15–16); (2) the church is God’s holy temple (v. 16); and (3) as a dwelling place for God, the church must maintain a clear separation from the world in terms of its makeup and character.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 1, 'perfecting holiness. Fulfilling the law through love (Rom. 12:2ff.; 13:8–10). in … God. With a clean conscience (see 2 Cor. 5:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 2, 'Alludes to the charges made against Paul: wronged. With harsh and unjust treatment (see 1 Cor. 5:3ff.). corrupted. With his “law-free” gospel. cheated. By taking up a large collection (compare to 1 Cor. 16:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 3, 'said before. In 6:11. die … live together. Expresses: (1) solidarity; and (2) deep affection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 4, 'Expresses the pride and affection that lie behind every genuine Christian rebuke.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 5, 'Compare to 2:13. The book of Acts does not report the incidents described here. Paul gladly reveals his weakness (see 12:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 6, 'coming of Titus. Compare to 2:13. God often encourages us by the arrival of a long-awaited friend bearing good news.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 7, 'desire. For (1) Paul; and (2) Paul’s gospel. mourning. Repentance (see note on v. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 8, 'my letter. See note on 2:3–4. regret. Source of Paul’s fears (see 7:5). though … while. Genuine “godly sorrow” soon gives way to joy (see vv. 9–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 9, 'Now. Thanks to Titus’s report (v. 7). repentance. They experienced a change of heart in their attitude toward Paul. suffer … nothing. Do well.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 10, 'godly sorrow. (1) Sorrow that comes from God; and (2) sorrow that God approves. produces. Describes sorrow as a process. repentance. See note on v. 9. salvation. New life. not … regretted. Bringing more joy as its result.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 11, 'Describes the effects of repentance. diligence. To do God’s bidding. clearing. Of misunderstandings. indignation. Against evil. fear. Of God. desire. For reconciliation. vindication. Of Paul’s character. clear. Legally and before God (see v. 2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 13, 'Describes three aspects of Christian rejoicing: (1) encouragement; (2) expression of joy; and (3) refreshing of the spirit. Titus. Compare to v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 14, 'found true. Indicates Paul’s commitment to speaking the truth in all matters.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 15, 'fear and trembling. Describes their recognition of Paul’s apostolic authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 7, 16, 'confidence in you. Expresses approval and forgiveness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 1, 'Paul calls for generous giving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 1, 'Paul calls for generous giving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 1, 'grace. The grace of God was at work in them and became evident through their liberal gifts (see note on v. 2). churches of Macedonia. In Thessalonica (Acts 17:2–9), Philippi (Acts 16:11ff.), and Berea (Acts 17:10–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 2, 'great … affliction. See Acts 16:16–17:14. their joy. See Introduction to Philippians, “Message.” deep poverty. Hints that the Corinthians are wealthy by comparison (see also v. 14). riches … liberality. Their riches were really their spirit of generosity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 3, 'Defines generosity as giving: (1) beyond one’s means; and (2) voluntarily.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 4, 'with much urgency. Over our objections. saints. Jewish Christians in Jerusalem (see note on Gal. 2:10; compare to Rom. 15:26; 1 Cor. 16:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 5, 'themselves. Not only money. by … God. As God gave them opportunities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 6, 'Titus. See v. 23. he had begun. In Macedonia. grace. See note on v. 1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 7, 'faith … diligence. Refers to spiritual gifts (Rom. 12:6ff.; 1 Cor. 12:8–10): giving is a gift that enriches both the giver and the receiver (Acts 20:35; Rom. 12:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 8, 'testing. See 1 Thess. 5:20–21. An offering tests the genuineness of our spiritual gifts of love and diligence (Rom. 12:2, 9–10). of others. Of the Macedonians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 9, 'grace. Gift. He was rich. As the pre-existent Son of God. He became poor. By giving everything He had, including His life, for us (Phil. 2:6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 10, 'Defines the spiritual gift of diligence (see notes on vv. 7, 16–17) in terms of desire (vv. 10–11) and willingness (v. 12). The true worth of an offering is measured by the spirit in which it is given, not its amount (Mark 12:41–44).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 10, 'began … year ago. See 1 Cor. 16:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 14, 'equality. By taking turns at giving and helping (v. 15). at this time. But perhaps not in the future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 15, 'Quotes Ex. 16:18. The gathering of manna in the wilderness shows how to achieve economic equality: (1) take what you need; and (2) share the rest with those in need.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 16, 'earnest care. Diligence. Titus’s willingness further illustrates the gift of diligence; see note on vv. 10–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 18, 'brother. Identity unknown; see note on v. 19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 19, 'who … us. Probably one of the men mentioned in Acts 20:4, three of whom were Macedonians (2 Cor. 9:4). gift. Grace; see note on 8:1. ready mind. See note on vv. 10–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 20, 'avoiding this. To keep a good reputation (v. 21). blame us in. Blame us for mishandling or embezzling.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 22, 'them. Titus and the brother of vv. 18–19. our brother. Identity unknown; see note on v. 19. diligent. See note on vv. 10–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 23, 'Titus. See note on 2:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 8, 24, 'the churches. Those that (1) look to you for leadership (9:2); (2) have doubts about you. proof of your love. See note on 8:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 1, 'saints. See note on 8:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 2, 'willingness. See note on 8:10–12. Macedonians. See note on 8:1. Achaia. See Introduction to 1 Corinthians, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 3, 'brethren. See note on 8:19. our boasting. About your generosity and willingness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 4, 'Macedonians … unprepared. See note on 8:19; interestingly, Acts 20:4 does not list any representative from Corinth. boasting. See note on 2 Cor. 9:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 5, 'necessary. To ensure the purely voluntary character of the gift.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 6, 'sparingly. Due to worries that one might not have enough. bountifully. Because one trusts and praises God. reap. Experience happiness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 7, 'each one. (1) According to their means; (2) according to their measure of faith (Rom. 12:3). cheerful giver. See note on 8:10–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 8, 'God is able. Our offerings affirm our faith in God’s ability to care for us. always … things. To meet our daily needs.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 9, 'See Ps. 112:9. God remembers and rewards almsgiving (Matt. 25:34).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 11, 'enriched in everything. God’s intention for all His creatures.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 12, 'Besides supplying the needs of saints, offerings generate (1) thanksgiving; (2) praise; and (3) intercessory prayers by the recipients.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 13, 'proof of this ministry. Safe and secure delivery of the donations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 9, 15, 'Thanks be to God. Praise, our ultimate offering. His indescribable gift. Christ, God’s ultimate gift.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 1, 'Paul again appeals to questioners to accept his apostolic legitimacy and authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 1, 'Paul again appeals to questioners to accept his apostolic legitimacy and authority.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 1, 'Christ. Who became man in weakness (8:9). who … toward you. Like Jesus, God Incarnate. Paul shows that he is imitating Christ’s humility.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 2, 'walked. Lived. according … flesh. (1) As ordinary humans; (2) without the Spirit (Rom. 8:5–8; 1 Cor. 3:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 3, 'war. Against evil. according … flesh. See note on v. 2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 4, 'weapons. (1) Meekness and gentleness (v. 1); (2) gospel. warfare. See vv. 4b–6. carnal. See note on v. 2. strongholds. Every God-resistant factor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 5, 'knowledge of God. Gospel (see also 2:14). bringing … Christ. Establishing complete obedience of faith (Rom. 1:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 6, 'Paul will punish the rebellion in Corinth when the entire community is behind him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 7, 'look … appearance. Source of the problem in Corinth; also true of our world. he is Christ’s. May refer to those claiming to be Christ’s apostles (11:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 9, 'by letters. (1) Only in my absence (v. 1); (2) by words only (v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 10, 'they. Paul’s Jewish-Christian opponents (11:22–23) who flaunted spiritual manifestations and abusive practices (11:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 12, 'Carnal ministers (1) commend themselves; and (2) compare themselves with others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 13, 'limits … us. Boundary God has assigned for our unique calling (compare to Rom. 12:3; 1 Cor. 7:17). a sphere … you. You belong within my boundary of labor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 10, 14, 'Gives guidelines for boasting: (1) keep to the work that God has given you; and (2) boast about God (Jer. 9:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 1, 'bear with me. As you do with the deceivers (v. 4). folly. Boasting (v. 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 2, 'I am jealous for you. I love you (v. 11). betrothed you. With the gospel. present you. When Christ comes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 3, 'serpent … craftiness. See v. 14; Gen. 3:1ff. corrupted. Deceived into turning away.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 4, 'he who comes. False apostles; implies more than one; see v. 13 and note on 10:10. Jesus … spirit. See 1 Cor. 12:3. different gospel. See Gal. 1:6–9; the problem in Corinth was divisions, not the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 6, 'untrained. Ordinary. knowledge. Of Scripture and Christ. manifested. As fair and transparent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 7, 'commit sin. See v. 8. humbling myself. Working menial labor jobs (1 Cor. 4:12). preached … free. See 1 Cor. 9:14–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 8, 'other churches. Macedonian churches; see v. 9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 9, 'what I lacked … supplied. See Phil. 4:15–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 10, 'this boasting. See 1 Cor. 9:14–18. Achaia. See Introduction to 1 Corinthians, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 11, 'Point of chapters 11–12: Paul loves the Corinthians (12:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 12, 'what I do. Preach the gospel free of charge. just as we are. As apostles (see v. 13). in … boast. By criticizing us.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 13, 'false apostles. Not sent by Jerusalem or Christ. deceitful. See vv. 3, 14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 14, 'Satan … light. (1) To Eve (v. 3); and (2) to Christ (Matt. 4:3ff.).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 15, 'no great thing. Denotes a common occurrence. righteousness. The gospel; see note on 3:9. end. Destruction (Phil. 3:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 16, 'again. Resuming v. 1. fool. One who boasts. boast. See note on 10:14–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 17, 'according … Lord. Like Jesus. as … foolishly. Like a clown. confidence of boasting. That is, Paul’s unashamed willingness to boast.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 18, 'boast … flesh. Boast about their advantages; see vv. 22ff.; Phil. 3:4ff.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 19, 'fools. Those who boast. wise. Sarcasm; they are really foolish (v. 20; Rom. 1:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 20, 'put … it. Submit. devours. Takes advantage (Gal. 5:15). takes. Exploits. strikes … face. Insulting you. They foolishly were willing to submit to the authority of those who abused them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 21, 'weak. See 10:10; 12:10. bold. In flaunting advantages. foolishly. Acting the part of a fool.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 22, 'Paul’s opponents claimed an elite Jewish Christian pedigree from Jerusalem (Phil. 3:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 23, 'more. Better because of the disadvantages listed in vv. 23b–28; denotes irony. labors. See 1 Thess. 2:9. stripes … prisons. See also 2 Cor. 6:5. in deaths. Exposed to death (1:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 24, 'forty stripes. The maximum number of stripes allowed under Jewish law (see Deut. 25:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 25, 'beaten with rods. By Roman magistrates; e.g., Acts 16:22. shipwrecked. E.g., Acts 27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 26, 'in perils. Badge of true servants of God. false brethren. See v. 13; Gal. 2:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 27, 'toil. See 2 Thess. 3:8. sleeplessness. See 2 Cor. 6:5. hunger and thirst. See 1 Cor. 4:11. fastings. See 2 Cor. 6:5. cold and nakedness. See 1 Cor. 4:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 28, 'other things. Not mentioned by Paul or Acts. my … churches. E.g., 2:13; 7:5–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 29, 'weak. (1) Suffering; (2) vulnerable. is made to stumble. (1) Is offended; (2) gives up the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 30, 'the things … infirmity. (1) Objects of ridicule; (2) what makes him know Christ’s power (Phil. 3:10–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 31, 'God … knows. I swear before God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 32, 'Damascus. In Syria. governor. In ca. A.D. 37. Aretas. Aretas IV, ethnarch of Nabatea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 11, 33, 'let … wall. An act of cowardice by Roman and Greek standards. Point: after conversion, Paul became “weak” by giving up empty boasting and violence (v. 30; compare to v. 20; Acts 9:1–2, 15–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 1, 'visions. See also Acts 26:19. revelations. See Gal. 1:12. of the Lord. From the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 2, 'man. Paul speaking of himself, in the third person. fourteen years ago. Around the time of Acts 11:25–30 (ca. A.D. 43). in the body … out of the body. Physically or only in the mind; depicts vividness. caught up. Depicts suddenness. third heaven. Highest heaven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 4, 'Paradise. Garden of Eden (see Rev. 2:7). inexpressible. Untranslatable (1 Cor. 14:27–28). not lawful. Forbidden; see Rev. 10:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 6, 'I … truth. Unlike my opponents (11:13). I refrain. From listing more visions (12:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 7, 'thorn … flesh. Some painful ailment, perhaps an eye ailment (Gal. 4:14–15). messenger of Satan. Helps God, against His wish. A device of Satan that God, in His sovereignty, used to accomplish something necessary in Paul’s life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 8, 'Lord. Christ. three times. Like Jesus in Gethsemane (Mark 14:35ff.); denotes extreme intensity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 9, 'He. Christ. said to me. Full of comfort. sufficient. (1) Just right; (2) more than enough; a prayer with a negative answer is still an answered prayer. strength. Power of resurrection (see Phil. 3:10–11). made perfect. (1) More powerfully manifested; (2) more fully experienced. most gladly. Not only patiently; compare Rom. 5:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 10, 'take pleasure. Embrace. needs … distresses. See 11:23b–27. when … weak. Most dependent on Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 11, 'compelled. By demanding credentials (11:22–23b). commended. Recommended (3:1–3). most eminent. The twelve.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 12, 'signs of an apostle. Something that the Corinthians felt that Paul lacked. signs … deeds. Miracles (Acts 2:43; Rom. 15:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 13, 'were inferior to. Less loved than (v. 15). burdensome. See vv. 14–18; 11:7–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 14, 'third time. Trip one in Acts 18; trip two, see 1:15–16; 2:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 15, 'spend … spent. Like parents who willingly sacrifice for their children (v. 14b; 1 Cor. 4:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 17, 'Refers to Paul’s collection efforts through Titus and other emissaries; see 8:6, 18–24; 1 Cor. 16:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 20, 'such as I wish. Spiritual (1 Cor. 3:1; Gal. 5:22–23). contentions … tumults. Indicates fleshly existence (Rom. 1:29–31; Gal. 5:19–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 12, 21, 'humble. Through (1) excessive sorrow (2:1–5); and (2) failed ministry (3:1–6). uncleanness … lewdness. Fleshly lust (Gal. 5:19–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 1, 'third time. See note on 12:14. witnesses. Warnings, as in Matt. 18:16 (see Deut. 19:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 2, 'I … before. See 2:1–4. foretell. Forewarn. will not spare. I will use my authority to employ church discipline (10:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 3, 'proof. Beyond the proofs already provided: (1) changed lives (3:1–3); (2) pedigrees (11:22–23a); (3) abundant suffering (11:23b–27); and (4) visions and revelations (12:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 4, 'weak in Him. Crucified with Him. shall live. And so be vindicated. toward you. Before you.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 5, 'yourselves. Each other. know yourselves. Know your true identity. disqualified. Already disqualified.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 6, 'disqualified. Living an unexamined life (v. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 7, 'though … disqualified. To the false teachers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 9, 'glad … strong. See 6:10; 12:10. complete. (1) By removing impurities (7:1); (2) through weakness (12:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 10, 'absent … present. See also 10:1–2. sharpness. Strong rebukes (Titus 1:13). authority … destruction. See 2 Cor. 10:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 11, 'complete. (1) Unified (1 Cor. 1:10); (2) purified (see note on 2 Cor. 13:9). one mind. See note on Phil. 2:2. live in peace. With others (Rom. 12:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 12, 'holy kiss. See note on Rom. 16:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 13, 'saints. Christian believers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Coríntios', 13, 14, 'Grace, love, and fellowship are qualities that create community; the Trinity exemplifies a harmonious community. For more on the TRINITY working together in unity of purpose, see 1 Pet. 1:2. See also note on Matt. 28:19.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 0, NULL, 'It is almost unanimously believed that Galatians was written by Paul. It is quite probable that he wrote this letter with the help of an assistant or secretary. In the original copy of the letter, the text from 6:11 to the end of the letter would have been written in Paul’s own handwriting, distinguishable from the handwriting used in the rest of the letter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 0, NULL, 'There are two major views regarding the recipients of the letter. The older view, known as the “North Galatian” theory, is that the recipients were the ethnic Gauls, a people of Celtic descent, who lived up in the northeastern reaches of Asia Minor (modern Turkey). This is the area described as “Phrygia and the region of Galatia” in Acts 16:6 and 18:23. Acts, however, tells us little about the churches in this area.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 0, NULL, 'The dating of Galatians is challenging. There is no consensus among scholars as to when Galatians was written. The most we can say is that Galatians is one of Paul’s earliest letters, usually dated between A.D. 48 and 52. The question of whether Paul wrote to the North Galatians or the South Galatians has no direct bearing on the dating of the letter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 0, NULL, 'Unfortunately, Galatians has been a source for anti-Semitism (anti-Judaism) in Christianity. The main reason is that the letter contains many very harsh statements against circumcision and Judaism. These remarks have been taken by many Christians as permission, or in some cases as a requirement, to ban Jews and Jewish practices from Christianity. This has been the most tragic legacy of Galatians. There is little doubt that Paul’s opponents were Jews, and that Paul spoke against them, but two things must be remembered before assuming that the letter is anti-Semitic.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 0, NULL, 'I.      Greetings and introduction (1:1–14)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 1, 'not from men. Not sent by humans. nor through man. Not appointed by humans. but through Jesus. Who called him on the road to Damascus (Acts 9:3–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 2, 'brethren … with me. Paul’s message is not his private opinion but one that has the support of the community at large. churches. House churches.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 3, 'God the Father and our Lord Jesus Christ. Stresses the identity and consistency between the God of the OT and Christ of the NT.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 4, 'gave Himself. In sacrificial death. this present evil age. The evil age that began with Adam’s fall.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 5, 'to whom be glory. Bringing glory to God is the ultimate aim of Paul’s gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 6, 'I marvel. Expresses Paul’s deep displeasure. turning away so soon. After Paul’s second visit to Galatia (4:13). different gospel. Paul’s opponents taught that circumcision is an act that perfects faith in Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 7, 'not another. There is only one gospel. some who trouble you. The Judaizers were working in Galatia when Paul wrote this letter. pervert. By requiring circumcision and turning the true gospel into a “gospel” of the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 8, 'angel from heaven. The Judaizers appear to have claimed some form of supernatural revelation. accursed. Condemned to eternal damnation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 9, 'See 2:16 for a summary of Paul’s gospel that sets the parameters of Christian proclamation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 10, 'persuade. Seek to gain approval. A person overly concerned with how they are being perceived by humans cannot serve God (see 6:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 11, 'gospel … preached by me. See 2:16. not according to man. Did not originate with humans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 12, 'through the revelation of Jesus Christ. Refers to Paul’s encounter with the risen Jesus on the road to Damascus. See Acts 9:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 13, 'See Acts 9:1–2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 14, 'I advanced in Judaism. Paul may have been a member of the Sanhedrin, the highest Jewish authority at the time. zealous. Refers to Paul being a Pharisee. traditions of my fathers. Reference to Pharisaism, which observed Jewish traditions in addition to Scripture.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 15, 'separated me from my mother’s womb. Alludes to Jeremiah’s consecration in his mother’s womb (Jer. 1:5). called me. Paul’s encounter with Jesus on the road to Damascus (Acts 9:3–6) was not a mere conversion experience but a call to be “a prophet to the nations [the Gentiles]” like Jeremiah (Jer. 1:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 16, 'reveal His Son in me. The basis for Paul’s claim to be an apostle: the risen Christ met him personally and commissioned him to preach the gospel. flesh and blood. Human beings. Paul received the gospel from Christians that preceded him (1 Cor. 15:1), but his unique message and mission to the Gentiles came directly from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 17, 'Arabia. Arabia probably refers to the Nabatean kingdom that roughly coincides with the southern parts of modern Israel and Jordan. See note on 4:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 18, 'after three years I went up to Jerusalem. Paul’s first trip to Jerusalem mentioned in Acts 9:26 must have taken place after Paul’s three-year stay in Arabia, around A.D. 37. to see Peter. It may have been Barnabas who arranged this meeting (see Acts 9:27). remained … fifteen days. Paul probably learned the details of the life and teachings of Jesus during this time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 19, 'James, the Lord’s brother. Mentioned in Mark 6:3. James did not believe in Jesus during His earthly ministry (John 7:3–5), but he met the resurrected Jesus (1 Cor. 15:7), and became one of the apostles. He eventually became a central figure in the Jerusalem church (Gal. 2:9, 12; Acts 15:13; 21:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 20, 'before God. Paul guarantees the truth of his account of travels in 1:17–2:10 with an oath: Paul only made two trips to Jerusalem before he wrote Galatians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 21, 'regions of Syria and Cilicia. See Acts 9:30. Paul went to Caesarea and Tarsus after leaving Jerusalem. Tarsus was the capital city of the Roman province of Cilicia.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 1, 24, 'they glorified God in me. The church quickly embraced him as its own.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 1, 'after fourteen years. Exact date uncertain. Barnabas. Companion of Paul during his first missionary journey. Known also as Joses (Joseph), a Levite from Cyprus (see Acts 4:36). Titus. Paul’s Gentile understudy whom he called “a true son in our common faith” (Titus 1:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 2, 'by revelation. May be a reference to the revelation of Agabus in Acts 11:28. privately. The private nature of this meeting differs from the formal meeting in Jerusalem in Acts 15. those … of reputation. Apostolic leadership.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 3, 'not … compelled. Paul did not give in because he felt that the apostles did not require the circumcision of the Gentiles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 4, 'false brethren secretly brought in. By Jewish authorities. spy out. Jewish authorities often implanted spies in church gatherings (see Luke 20:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 5, 'yield submission. Bow to pressure. truth of the gospel. That our salvation is through Christ and His works alone.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 6, 'those who seemed to be something. The Jerusalem apostles (see v. 9). God shows personal favoritism to no man. Reference to God’s judgment. added nothing to me. A claim that his concept of the law is a direct revelation from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 7, 'uncircumcised. Gentiles. circumcised. Jews. Peter’s gospel was especially formulated to meet the needs of the Jews. Peter. Paul’s customary way of referring to Peter is Cephas (v. 9; 1 Cor. 1:12; 3:22; 9:5; 15:5). The Greek (i.e., official) name Peter reminds the reader of the official nature of the decision cited in this verse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 8, 'worked effectively. Produced results. Peter … to the circumcised … me toward the Gentiles. Peter was put in charge of the Jewish arm of the church, and Paul, the Gentile arm.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 9, 'James, Cephas, and John. The three who remained in Jerusalem during the persecution (Acts 8:1). For James, see Gal. 1:19. pillars. Apostles. grace … given to me. Paul’s spiritual gift as an apostle of the Gentiles (Eph. 4:11). right hand of fellowship. Into the inner circle of the apostles. we … to the Gentiles … they to the circumcised. At this meeting, two divisions of the work were created, one for the Gentiles and one for the Jews.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 10, 'poor. The Jewish believers in Jerusalem who had sold all their possessions for Christ (Acts 2:44–47). I also was eager to do. Paul carried out his end of the promise by taking up collections from his churches (Rom. 15:25–27; 1 Cor. 16:1–3; 2 Cor. 8–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 11, 'Antioch. The hub of Gentile Christianity (see Acts 11:26); the third most important city in the Roman Empire after Rome and Alexandria. he was to be blamed. Peter recognized that he was in the wrong and did not fight back.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 12, 'men … from James. Those pressuring James in Jerusalem. those of the … circumcision. Jews.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 13, 'played the hypocrite. In other words, the other Jews did the same thing that Peter did. even Barnabas. Paul’s trusted partner in the Gentile work (see 2:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 14, 'being a Jew. Who believes that one God created all humanity. manner of Gentiles. Living as though God did not see.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 15, 'We who are Jews by nature, and not sinners of the Gentiles. A derogatory slogan used by the Jewish-Christians who frowned upon the large influxes of uncircumcised Gentiles in the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 16, 'justified. Acquitted and accepted by God. works of the law. Performance of the law. by faith in Christ … by the works of the law. Two mutually exclusive principles of salvation. For more on SALVATION, see Rom. 3:21–26; Eph. 2:4–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 17, 'found sinners. Commit sin again. Christ … a minister of sin? Did Christ make us sin? Certainly not! A vehement denial of the charges.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 18, 'things which I destroyed. The former life of sin. If one went back to sinning after accepting Christ, it would be due to one’s own wrong choices and not because the Christ-centered gospel was faulty.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 19, 'through the law. Through the condemnation of the law. died to the law. Christ’s substitution met the law’s demand for the death of the sinner. live to God. In Christ; reference to baptism (v. 20). All Christians are dead to the law as a way of salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 2, 20, 'crucified with Christ. In baptism. See note on Rom. 6:6. Christ lives in me. Through the Holy Spirit. life … in the flesh. Our existence in this evil age (see 1:4). faith in the Son of God. A conviction that Christ, who died on the cross, is the Son of God. loved me and gave Himself for me. The success of our sanctification depends on being able to see the cross as Christ’s expression of love for us personally.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 1, 'the truth. The gospel. before whose eyes. The cross was preached in a vivid and central manner (1 Cor. 2:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 2, 'works of the law. Performance of the law. hearing of faith. Accepting in faith. The Spirit came immediately when people accepted the gospel (Acts 19:4–6). The experience was an obvious one or this question would not make sense.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 3, 'made perfect. Moved to a more advanced stage of faith. by the flesh. By circumcision.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 4, 'suffered. Been persecuted (for the gospel).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 5, 'works miracles. Miracles often followed the coming of the Spirit in the apostolic times. For other spiritual gifts, see 1 Cor. 12:10, 28–29. works … hearing. See note on v. 2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 6, 'Abraham believed God. He believed that God exists and that He is the Creator who has power to raise the dead. accounted. Accepted by God as righteousness, even though he was an uncircumcised Gentile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 7, 'sons of Abraham. The term “sons” in Hebrew and Aramaic does not denote physical descent but attributes (see Mark 3:17). The children of Abraham share his spiritual attributes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 8, 'nations … blessed. This is the goal of the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 9, 'those … of faith. The Gentiles. blessed. Become recipients of the promised blessing. believing Abraham. Who was justified before circumcision.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 10, 'Compare John 1:29; 2 Cor. 5:21; and see note on Rom. 3:21–26. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21. For more on SALVATION, see Rom. 3:21–26; Eph. 2:4–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 10, 'as many as … law. The Jews. under the curse. Separated from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 11, 'no one is justified by the law. A fundamental principle of salvation. in the sight of God. Before God’s judgment. the just shall live by faith. Like Abraham in Gen. 15:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 12, 'the law. Law of Moses. man who does them. Those who keep the laws of Moses; the law requires performance, not faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 13, 'the curse of the law. E.g., curses of Lev. 26:14–39; Deut. 27:11–26. become a curse for us … on a tree. Refers to Deut. 21:22. Paul persecuted the church because it worshiped someone God had cursed on a tree for blasphemy. In bearing the punishment for sin, Christ became a substitute for the sinner. The curse that we deserve fell upon Him. For more on SALVATION through Christ’s substitutionary atonement, see Mark 10:45; 2 Cor. 5:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 14, 'the blessing of Abraham. The blessing promised to Abraham (Gen. 12:3; 18:18; 22:18) and the promise that he would be the father of the Gentiles (Gen. 17:4). the promise of the Spirit. The blessing promised to Abraham was the same as the Spirit promised in Ezek. 36:26–27 and Joel 2:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 15, 'Brethren. Indicates a new paragraph. I speak … manner of men. I give you an example. a man’s covenant. Such as a person’s legal will. annuls or adds. Unilaterally alters or revokes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 16, 'not … of many, but as of one. Clarifies that the promise to Abraham referenced here did not relate to Israel, as a nation of many people. Seed … Christ. The promise concerns the Messiah (compare to Gen. 3:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 17, 'the law. The Law of Moses. four hundred and thirty years. See Gen. 15:13; Ex. 12:40. covenant … confirmed. In Gen. 15:9–21. make the promise of no effect. Does not compete with the promise made to Abraham. See also Gen. 17:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 18, 'inheritance. Deliverance from sin. gave … by promise. Unconditionally without the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 19, 'added. Introduced. because of transgressions. To keep sin in check. till the Seed. Only Christ can solve the sin problem (see note on v. 25). it was appointed through angels. Reference to the Mosaic legal codes in general (Acts 7:53). mediator. Moses. for one only. Alone. There were other mediators of revelation besides Moses (Heb. 1:1). God is one. All revelation comes from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 21, 'if … law … could have given life. It was never the purpose of the law to give life. righteousness … by the law. Righteousness would have been possible by the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 22, 'Scripture. The Law of Moses. confined all under sin. Condemns everyone as being under sin. promise by faith in Jesus. Promise fulfilled by trusting in Jesus. The term “faith” here is used in the sense of “hope.” those who believe. That Jesus is risen.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 23, 'before faith came. Before faith in Jesus became reality (see v. 22). kept under guard. Kept in check. by the law. The Law of Moses. kept for the faith. Placed under a curse until Christ came.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 24, 'the law was our tutor. With power to punish as well as instruct. bring us to Christ. Preserve Israel’s hope until Christ came.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 25, 'under a tutor. Under the Law of Moses. The Mosaic Law represents a complex web of laws that include everything from hygienic codes to agricultural laws to civil ordinances, as well as a system of curses and blessings. Historically, the law functioned as a tutor to lead the Jews to Christ. When Christ came it lost its historical function as a tutor. It is also true today that the law leads people to Him for forgiveness, since the role of the law is to point out sin (Rom. 3:20). When believers come to Him in confession, the law loses its tutor function until they sin again. But as Paul explains in other places, it still remains a “holy” standard in their lives (see Rom. 3:31; 7:12, 14; 13:8–10). Christians are not lawless. For more on law, see Ex. 20:1–17; Matt. 5:17–19; Heb. 8:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 3, 26, 'all sons of God. Christ our Master now teaches us directly and personally. The law');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 1, 'heir. Israel. child. Refers to the time before Christ. slave. Gentile.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 2, 'guardians and stewards. The Law of Moses. Compare to 3:22–23. until the time appointed. According to God’s eternal purpose and timeline (Dan. 7:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 3, 'we. Jews and Gentiles alike. bondage. See 3:22–23. Immaturity and ignorance. elements of the world. Ritualistic religions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 4, 'fullness of the time. According to God’s master calendar. born of a woman. Fully human. under the law. Under the curse of the law. See note on 3:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 5, 'adoption as sons. Become Abraham’s children.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 6, 'sons. Abraham’s children. crying out. A kind of spontaneous prayer. Abba, Father! “Abba” was used to express a sense of endearment and deep respect. Jesus addressed God as “Abba” (Mark 14:36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 7, 'slave. Person in subservient fear. a son. Who enjoys intimate access and relationship to his parent. heir of God. God, not land, is our inheritance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 8, 'then. Before accepting the gospel. not know God. Had no dealings with God. those … not gods. Idols and objects of nature worshiped by the pagans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 9, 'known God. Worship God. known by God. Accepted by God (Rom. 5:10–11). weak and beggarly elements. Bankrupt and ritualistic religions of the Greco-Roman world. It appears that some Galatians not only accepted Jewish circumcision but resumed practicing some of their pre-Christian rituals from other religions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 10, 'You observe days. Good and bad luck days (the solstice, equinox, etc.) months. Some months were viewed as evil. seasons and years. Seasonal and annual feast days. This was not the weekly Sabbath, which both Jesus and Paul observed (Luke 4:16; Acts 13:14, 42, 44; 16:13; 17:2; 18:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 11, 'labored … in vain. Refers to Galatians’ return to former religions. Paul wonders if all his effort to introduce them to the gospel has been wasted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 12, 'become like me. Living by faith and braving persecutions. I became like you. Refers to living among and eating with the Gentiles. You have not injured me. Understatement of the Galatians’ warm acceptance of Paul in the past.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 13, 'physical infirmity. Perhaps an eye ailment (v. 15). at the first. Allusion that Paul made two visits to Galatia.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 14, 'my trial … my flesh. His physical appearance was marred by the illness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 15, 'plucked out your own eyes. An expression of deep affection and a reference to him possibly having an eye condition.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 16, 'truth. The good news of the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 17, 'they. Judaizers. exclude you. From Paul’s influence. zealous for them. Love them instead. Paul accuses his opponents of political and financial motives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 19, 'I labor in birth. Paul changes his tone from fatherly rebuke to motherly compassion. until Christ is formed. Until the image of God is restored. Paul’s aim was the total transformation of the heart, not a mere legalistic adherence to the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 20, 'have doubts about you. Whether you were ever truly converted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 21, 'under the law. Under the curse of the law. See note on 3:13. People who try to save themselves by their own works are like the bondwoman of 4:22–24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 22, 'it is written. In Gen. 16; 21. two sons. Ishmael and Isaac. bondwoman. Hagar. freewoman. Sarah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 23, 'he … of the bondwoman. Ishmael. according to the flesh. By Abraham’s own human initiative. he of the freewoman. Isaac. through promise. By the initiative and act of God. The righteousness obtained by one’s own effort is like Ishmael, illegitimate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 24, 'two covenants. The promise to Abraham and the Law of Moses. gives birth to bondage. See note on vv. 22–23. Hagar. Just as Hagar was a handmaid of Sarah, so is the law a handmaid of the promise.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 25, 'Hagar is Mount Sinai. Hagar represents the Law of Moses. Arabia. The Nabatean kingdom, near Petra in modern Jordan, biblical Kadesh Barnea (Deut. 1:19), the place to which Hagar fled from Sarah (Gen. 16:14). Jerusalem. The theology of some in Jerusalem made the law central to their teaching. her children. Those converted to the law-centered theology.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 26, 'Jerusalem above. Refers to the heavenly Jerusalem, of which the earthly Jerusalem is a mere model. mother of us all. Everyone saved through Christ is a child of Sarah.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 27, 'it is written. In Is. 54:1. barren. Sarah. As with Sarah, the curse will be removed through God’s intervention rather than by human effort. desolate. Sarah. she who has a husband. Hagar. Hagar is mother of everyone who tries to achieve righteousness apart from Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 28, 'as Isaac was. He existed through God’s miraculous intervention.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 29, 'born. Into the household of Abraham. according to the flesh. By human effort. persecuted him. Because of jealousy. according to the Spirit. The Spirit is the fulfillment of the promise given to Abraham. See 3:14. even so it is now. The Judaizers are doing the same thing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 30, 'Scripture say. Gen. 21:10. All children of Abraham are free.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 4, 31, 'we are … free. The gospel is about freedom. Any religion that imparts fear and subservience is not of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 1, 'yoke of bondage. The law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 2, 'become circumcised. In order to be justified. It is acceptable to be circumcised for any other reason.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 3, 'See 4:21–31. There is actually only one way to obtain righteousness, which is through Christ; we absolutely cannot change our sinful hearts by trying to keep the law.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 5, 'through the Spirit. The only way to experience transformation. eagerly wait. This is all we can do. God will do the rest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 6, 'faith. In Christ. love. See 1 Cor. 13:4–7 for a definition. Note the hope … faith … love pattern in vv. 5–6, a pattern found in 1 Cor. 13:13. 1 Cor. 13 describes the fulfillment of the law in fuller terms. working. See Eph. 2:10 and note on Eph. 2:4–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 7, 'obeying the truth. Living according to the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 8, 'persuasion. Conviction of the need to be circumcised.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 9, 'A little leaven. The false teachings of the Judaizers. whole lump. The congregation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 10, 'no other mind. Paul has confidence that they will accept his word without confusion. he who troubles you. The Judaizers. whoever he is. Paul does not know or care about their identity or reputation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 11, 'offense of the cross. The offense of Paul’s preaching that presented the crucified Christ as God’s solution for the sin problem (see 1 Cor. 1:21–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 12, 'cut.… off. Castrate themselves.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 13, 'for the flesh. Refers to taking pleasure in impure desires (see vv. 19–21). It is easy to interpret the gospel as permission to violate the law. Christian freedom means that the Spirit cleanses us from the sinful desires that hold us captive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 14, 'all the law. The entire Law of Moses. fulfilled. Summed up. one word. One sentence. love your neighbor. Quote from Lev. 19:18. All the ceremonial laws of Moses center on the moral mandate of this verse. For more on the fulfillment of the LAW, see Matt. 22:36–40; Rom. 13:8–10; 1 John 2:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 15, 'bite … devour. All the relational problems in the church, in spite of the Galatian believers’ efforts to keep the law, point out how wrong they have been in their theology.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 16, 'Walk in the Spirit. Constantly think about Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 17, 'flesh. Sinful self. lusts against. Cancels out. What the Spirit and our sinful self want is so completely opposite of each other that they are mutually exclusive. We cannot change ourselves, but we can choose whose hand we will clasp, the flesh’s or the Spirit’s.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 18, 'led by the Spirit. In your desires. not under the law. Not under the condemnation of the law. Even if we violate the ceremonial laws of Moses (the specific case here is the law of circumcision), we will not come under a curse.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 19, 'No one who enjoys the immoral lifestyle described in these verses can expect to be in the kingdom. Christians are expected to live according to the moral requirements of the Law of Moses. See Eph. 2:14–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 22, 'The fruit of the Spirit is only one of the gifts imparted by the third Person of the Trinity. For others, see Acts 1:8; Eph. 4:11–12. We can overcome our sinful desires only through the Spirit. The fruits of the Spirit are the result of the softening presence of the Holy Spirit on the human heart in agitation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 23, 'Against such there is no law. The law cannot condemn us as long as we are bearing the fruit of the Spirit (see Rom. 8:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 24, 'crucified the flesh. Reference to baptism (see also Rom. 6:4–6). True baptism occurs when the Spirit is with us and stops our sinful desires.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 5, 25, 'We cannot claim to have received the Holy Spirit if our lives are dominated by the works of the flesh described in vv. 19–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 1, 'overtaken. By mistake. spiritual. Led by the Holy Spirit. you also be tempted. A judgmental spirit is an expression of overconfidence, which often leads to downfall (compare to 1 Cor. 10:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 2, 'fulfill. Through love. See Rom. 13:10. law of Christ. The law as lived and taught by Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 4, 'examine. Before God. work. Deeds. in himself alone. In private thanksgiving to God. not in another. Not in competition or comparison with another person.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 5, 'shall bear. At the final judgment (see 2 Cor. 5:10). his own load. His own guilt and self-approval (Rom. 14:12–13). Don’t be judgmental; leave the judging to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 6, 'all good things. Food and shelter (see Matt. 10:10). For supporting the gospel ministry as an expression of STEWARDSHIP, see 1 Cor. 9:8–14; 1 Tim. 5:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 7, 'God is not mocked. God’s judgment is not a mock trial. he will also reap. In this life and at the final judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 8, 'sows to his flesh. Perform the deeds described in 5:19–21. will … reap. In this life and at the final judgment. sows to the Spirit. Cultivates the qualities described in 5:22–23. reap everlasting life. In this life and at the final judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 9, 'doing good. The summation of the law. in due season. In this life as well at the Second Coming of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 11, 'large letters. Paul’s unique autograph. with my own hand. To this point, the letter was written by a secretary who took down Paul’s dictation. In this final section (vv. 11–18), Paul tries to sum up the main points of his argument.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 12, 'good showing in the flesh. This phrase has a double meaning: (1) enhancing one’s standing in this life; and (2) being able to boast of circumcision. suffer persecution. From the Jews. for the cross of Christ. For believing that Jesus the crucified Messiah (as opposed to the law) is the only solution for our sin problem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 13, 'those who are circumcised. Those who received circumcision at the urging of the Judaizers. keep the law. Keep all the laws of Moses (see 5:3). boast in your flesh. In the fact that you received circumcision.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 14, 'cross. The utterly forsaken manner in which Jesus died. world. This present age with its lures (see 1:4). world … crucified to me. By being crucified with Christ (see 2:20), I have rejected the world as unworthy. I to the world. I have been rejected as unworthy by the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 15, 'new creation. Work of the Spirit (compare to 5:23–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 16, 'this rule. The new standards set by Christ. Israel of God. Consisting of Jews and Gentiles plucked from this evil age.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Gálatas', 6, 17, 'trouble me. Make my labor more difficult. marks of the Lord Jesus. The marks of persecution received from those who belong to the world.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 0, NULL, 'Paul identifies himself as the author of Ephesians in the greeting (1:1) and, in a major segment of the letter, discusses his ministry to the Gentiles, calling himself “the prisoner of Christ Jesus for you Gentiles” (3:1). Similarly, he labels himself as “the prisoner of the Lord” (4:1) and “an ambassador in chains” (6:20). It is likely that Paul was in prison in Rome when he wrote Ephesians. This and the other letters which Paul wrote during this imprisonment (Philippians, Colossians, and Philemon) are known as the “Prison Epistles.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 0, NULL, 'The absence of the words “in Ephesus” in some manuscripts has led to discussion about the destination and nature of the letter. That discussion has noted that Paul does not seem to know the receivers of the letter personally (1:15; 3:2) even after having spent some three years ministering in Ephesus (Acts 19:8–10). It seems best to understand that the letter was intended to circulate among Christian congregations in and around Ephesus. Many members of these house churches would have become believers after Paul’s ministry there.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 0, NULL, 'The date and place of writing should be considered in light of the special, literary relationship between the letters to the Ephesians and the Colossians. They appear to be written from a similar outline, often treating the same topics from a different vantage point. Tychicus plays a role as messenger and, probably, letter bearer in both (6:21–22; Col. 4:7–8). So it seems best to conclude that the two letters (and Philemon as well) were composed at about the same time by Paul (the early 60s) while he was imprisoned in Rome.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 0, NULL, 'Paul’s letter to the Ephesians is in some respects unusual among the thirteen New Testament letters that bear his name. Whereas most of Paul’s other letters address circumstances and crises in specific congregations, there seems to be no pressing crisis in view here and the letter does not take up the details of the local situation. It is also unusual in the degree to which it uses the exalted language of prayer and worship. While such language is used elsewhere by Paul (e.g., Rom. 8:38–39), its use is more concentrated in Ephesians (see esp. 1:3–23; 3:14–21). The reader can easily be swept along by a tide of exalted language without understanding clearly the meaning. To appreciate the letter, one must enter into the exuberant, joy-filled spirit of worship it exhibits.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 0, NULL, 'I.      Opening greeting (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 1, 'The introduction, following the ancient pattern, lists the sender (Paul) and the receivers (the “saints” or holy ones, Paul’s usual name for church members), and offers a profound greeting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 3, 'In a brief summary of the plan of redemption, Paul models the tone of praise that should mark Christian worship. He shares blessings of the Father (vv. 3–6), the Son (vv. 7–12), and the Spirit (vv. 13–14) planned “before the foundation of the world” (v. 4) and culminating with God gathering together “all things in Christ” (v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 3, 'the heavenly places. See v. 20; 2:6; 3:10; 6:12. Christians are already blessed through their identification with the risen, exalted Jesus (see 2:1–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 5, 'having predestined us. Paul’s readers would have regarded themselves as “predestined” by the stars and planets, which they regarded as gods. The idea that God has acted to offer them a different future would have been very good news. Paul does not mean that they have no choice in the matter, nor that salvation is not accessible to all (1 Tim. 2:4). Paul’s emphasis is on God’s gracious predetermined purpose.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 7, 'redemption. For more on SALVATION, see Mark 10:45 and note on Rom. 3:21–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 11, 'God’s gracious plan (vv. 3–10) has blessed those who, like Paul, believed early on (largely Jews; vv. 11–12) and those believing later (mostly Gentiles; vv. 13–14). Timing is the only difference.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 11, 'predestined. See note on v. 5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 13, 'sealed with the Holy Spirit. When we believe, the Holy Spirit “seals” or marks us as God’s own. guarantee. The Holy Spirit is the down payment of future redemption (see 4:30; Rom. 8:23; 2 Cor. 1:22; 5:5; 2 Tim. 2:19; for the end-time sealing, see Rev. 7:1–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 15, 'Paul offers a “prayer report” as though sharing an entry from his prayer journal. As in vv. 3–14, the passage is marked by a tone of praise and thanksgiving that should be reflected in our own prayers. Using exalted language, Paul prays that his readers will understand the nature of God’s power at work for them, the same power that resulted in Christ’s resurrection and coronation. We should lay claim to this energizing promise today: God offers us resurrection power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 20, 'the heavenly places. See v. 3; 2:6; 3:10; 6:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 21, 'Christ is exalted over every power for all time. This is very good news for Christians who need not obey any spiritual powers over whom Christ is already Lord. See 3:10; 6:12; Rom. 8:38; Phil. 2:5–11; Col. 1:16; 2:13–15; 1 Pet. 3:22. See note on Eph. 6:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 1, 22, 'under His feet. See Ps. 8:5–6; Heb. 2:6–9. These verses represent the first use in Ephesians of the body metaphor in which Christ is the head and the church is His body. See note on Eph. 4:11–16. On metaphors used for the CHURCH, see note on 2:19–22. See also 1 Cor. 3:9, 16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 1, 'who were dead. Paul offers “before” and “after” sketches of the lives of his readers (compare vv. 11–22). Before (vv. 1–3), they were “dead in trespasses and sins” (v. 1) and they were “children of wrath” (v. 3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 4, 'But God. After God’s rescue of them (vv. 4–10), believers are so identified with Christ that Paul sees them as sharing in the great events of His life—Christ’s death (v. 5), resurrection (“made us alive together with Christ,” v. 5), ascension (“raised us up together,” v. 6), and coronation (“made us sit together in the heavenly places,” v. 6). The great challenge and privilege of the Christian life is to lay claim to this alternate identity, graciously offered to us through Christ. Compare vv. 11–22; 4:17–5:21 where Paul again contrasts the pre-Christian and Christian lives of his readers. Along with Rom. 3:21–26, this passage is Paul’s most complete statement on the plan of SALVATION. It contains the following elements: (1) People are naturally “dead” in sin (v. 5; Rom. 3:23; 6:23); (2) God brings them to life in Christ (Eph. 2:5; John 3:3, 5; Rom. 6:4–5; 2 Cor. 5:17); (3) in Christ, they have a place and standing in heaven (Eph. 2:6; Rom. 3:21–26); (4) they are saved by the free gift of God’s grace; they do not receive what they deserve (death, Rom. 6:23; Gal. 3:10), but what they do not deserve (grace) costs them absolutely nothing (Eph. 2:8; Rom. 3:24); (5) they most certainly never earn it by their effort, an impossibility (Eph. 2:9, Rom. 3:20; Gal. 2:10); (6) but even though they are not saved by works, their new relationship to God and Christ will lead to a transformed life that works for God (Eph. 2:10; Rom. 6:1–11; 12:1–2; 1 John 2:3–6). Thus they live a life characterized by “obedience to the faith” (Rom. 1:5; 16:26) and they exhibit a life of “faith working through love” (Gal. 5:6, 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 8, 'have been saved. The verb form represents an expression of assurance. For more on Christian ASSURANCE, see John 5:24; Heb. 4:16; 1 John 5:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 10, 'good works. These are not at odds with the gospel when seen as the fruit of the Christian life (rather than the means of salvation). For more on the role of the LAW in Christian life, see John 14:15; 1 John 2:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 11, 'Paul again describes the sorry condition of his Gentile readers (vv. 11–12) before God’s intervention in Christ Jesus (v. 13; compare vv. 1–10). He then expands on this conviction about the transforming work of Christ, which impacts not only our relationship to God but our relationships with others (vv. 14–18). Once distant from the Jews, his Gentile readers are now “brought near” (v. 13) and form “one new man” or humanity (v. 15) together in the church. Now, both Gentile and Jewish believers “have access by one Spirit to the Father” (v. 18). Paul concludes with a series of metaphors to illustrate these new realities (vv. 19–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 12, 'covenants of promise. For different promises of God’s COVENANT, see Gen. 17:1–7; Jer. 31:31–34; Heb. 8:6–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 14, 'middle wall of separation. The phrase refers to the dividing wall in Herod’s temple that excluded Gentiles from the inner courts (see Acts 21:27–36); in Christ it has come down. Gentiles are to be accepted in the church as full partners with their Jewish brothers and sisters. one new man. Christ’s work on Calvary creates one new man out of the contrasting parts of the human race—Jews and Gentiles—in His church (compare 3:5–6). This principle extends today to all tribes, nations, ethnic groups, and social classes. This is possible because Christ, on the cross, “abolished … the law of commandments contained in ordinances” (2:15). This does not mean that the Ten Commandments (Ex. 20:1–17; Deut. 5:6–21) are abolished, as Paul clearly demonstrates their continuing significance. He quotes the commandment about honoring one’s parents (Eph. 6:1–3) and alludes to others as well (adultery, 4:17–24; 5:3–5, 21–33; bearing false witness, 4:25; stealing, 4:28). It does mean that the whole system of OT law, insofar as it was used to separate Jews from Gentiles, has come to an end. For more on law, see Matt. 5:17–19; Rom. 7:12, 14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 2, 19, 'Political (“strangers and foreigners”; “fellow citizens”) and family images (“members of the household of God”) give way to a further image or metaphor for the church in these verses, which is “a holy temple” and “a dwelling place of God in the Spirit” (see 1 Cor. 3:9–17; 2 Cor. 6:14–7:1; 1 Pet. 2:4–8). This is one of four major metaphors for the church in Ephesians. The church is also the body of Christ (Eph. 4:1–16), the bride of Christ (5:23–27), and an army combating evil (6:10–20). Ephesians pictures Christ as Head of the Church (1:22; 5:23) with the members existing in fellowship with each other (2:19). Peter describes the church as a holy nation (1 Pet. 2:9). On the relationship between the CHURCH and the realm of heaven, see note on Matt. 16:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 3, 1, 'In v. 1, Paul begins a fresh report of his prayers for his readers (see 1:15–23), one he will return to in 3:14–21. He interrupts himself to talk about his own work in preaching the “mystery” of the gospel (vv. 2–13), long “hidden in God” (v. 9) but now an open secret. He marvels at the mystery that God has included Gentiles with Jews in His church (see 2:11–22). He feels blessed to have learned this mystery by revelation (3:3) and to have been commissioned to share it (vv. 7–8). In our distant day, this “mystery” may have lost its awe. Pray for the ability to recapture Paul’s wonder at God’s work in forming His church from widely differing ethnic, social, and cultural groups and to rejoice in being part of it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 3, 9, 'God who created all things through Jesus Christ. For the role of Christ in CREATION, see John 1:1–3; Col. 1:15–17; Heb. 1:1–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 3, 10, 'principalities and powers. Since in 6:10–20 these are evil, the church’s role seems to be—since it is made up of Jews and Gentiles—to announce God’s wider plan to unite all things in Christ (1:10) and thus the coming doom of these powers (see 6:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 3, 12, 'boldness … confidence. See Heb. 4:14–16; 10:19–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 3, 14, 'For this reason I bow my knees. Paul returns to a report of his prayers for his readers (1:15–23). They are, apparently, tempted to become discouraged at Paul’s imprisonment (3:1, 13). Paul here envisions a very different experience for them (and for us): one bathed in power and love from the Father, the Lord Jesus Christ, and the Spirit. He concludes with an inspiring doxology, a prayer of praise to God (vv. 20–21; compare Rom. 16:25–27; Phil. 4:19–20; 1 Pet. 5:10–11; Jude 24–25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 1, 'Paul expresses his profound interest in the unity of the church. He begins with an opening exhortation to unity (vv. 1–3) and follows with a poetic list of seven elements that unite believers (vv. 4–6). The remainder of the passage (vv. 7–16) uses the metaphor of the church as Christ’s body to discuss the identity and unifying function of spiritual gifts. Unity is simultaneously something the church already possesses (vv. 4–6), something that must be constantly nourished and maintained (vv. 1–3), and the future goal toward which we move (v. 13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 4, 'one Spirit … one Lord … one God and Father of all. On the TRINITY, see Matt. 28:19; 1 Cor. 12:12–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 7, 'to each one. Paul’s treatment of spiritual gifts is framed by reminders that each church member is gifted and that the health of the body depends on each part functioning well (vv. 7, 16; compare 1 Cor. 12:12–27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 11, 'He Himself gave some to be. In this fresh use of the body metaphor, Paul identifies church members as body parts, the head as Christ, and the gifted individuals identified in v. 11 as joints or ligaments that help to unite the body (see esp. vv. 15–16; compare Col. 2:18–19). On other metaphors used for the CHURCH, see note on Eph. 2:19–22. apostles, some prophets, some evangelists, and some pastors and teachers. As gifts of the ascended Christ (vv. 8–10), these are to continue their unifying roles until the church reaches full unity and maturity (v. 13), presumably at Christ’s Return (compare 1 John 3:2–3). This passage establishes the continued function of the spiritual gifts, including the gift of prophecy. For the presence of the gift of prophecy among God’s people at the end of time, see Rev. 12:17; 19:10. On the existence of false prophets, see Matt. 24:24.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 16, 'effective working … every part. See note on v. 7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 17, 'The first half of Ephesians emphasizes Christian doctrine or teaching, especially God’s creation of the church as the community of the redeemed (1:1–3:21). In the last half, Paul turns more directly to Christian behavior, giving instructions to the church in the world (4:1–6:24). He has already contrasted the lives of his readers before they became Christians with their lives after they believed (2:1–10, 11–13). In 4:17–32, he contrasts a Gentile pattern of life (which they would have followed earlier, vv. 17–19, 22), with the Christ-inspired pattern (vv. 20–21, 23–32). People who are watching believers should be able to tell that they have “learned Christ” (v. 20). Paul’s counsel suggests that this does not happen instantly but that we believers are to continue to be “taught by Him” (v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 22, 'put off … put on. Using the imagery of a change of clothing, Paul exhorts believers to put off (v. 22) the old pattern of life and put on (v. 24) the new, Christ-inspired one (compare Rom. 6:1–14; 13:12; Col. 3:9–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 26, '“Be angry, and do not sin.” Paul’s use of language from Ps. 4:4 should be understood to mean, “If you should become angry, do not sin,” rather than a direct command to exercise anger. Anger, especially when allowed to fester, can harm the Christian community. Paul’s wise exhortation, “do not let the sun go down on your wrath,” deserves to be carefully followed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 30, 'See note on 1:13–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 4, 31, 'Few passages of Scripture address personal relationships in as touching a way as this brief exhortation. The forgiveness so graciously provided us is to be lived out in our relationships with others (see Matt. 6:12, 14–15; 18:21–35; Mark 11:25; Col. 3:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 1, 'Here Paul uses the term “walk” in the sense of to “live” or “behave.” The passage contrasts a Christian manner of “walking” or “living” with a non-believing lifestyle in three ways: love vs. lust (vv. 1–7); light vs. darkness (vv. 8–14); and wisdom vs. foolishness (vv. 15–21). As you read, you may be tempted to become discouraged, feeling that you can never be as pure and wise as these verses require. Instead of approaching the passage as a daunting checklist, pray through it in the spirit of thanksgiving Paul advocates. Thank God that He is at work to bring His purity and wisdom into your life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 1, 'imitators of God. The standard for Christian behavior is exceptionally high, since we are to be imitators of God, mirroring Christ’s love for us (vv. 1–2). So it should come as no surprise that the standard of sexual purity for believers is high as well (vv. 3–7). As Christians practice a godly life of love, they will exclude lust from their lives. In the place of the coarse talk so prevalent in our time, believers will practice the “giving of thanks” (v. 4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 8, 'children of light. Living as such will banish darkness. Instead of participating in shameful “works of darkness” (v. 11), Christians are to be “finding out what is acceptable to the Lord” (v. 10). That quest can be ever so much more rewarding than anything the world has to offer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 15, 'Believers are to avoid foolishness and drunkenness. They are to “walk circumspectly” (v. 15), to “understand what the will of the Lord is” (v. 17), and to sing praises to the Lord (see v. 19). redeeming the time. This term is borrowed from the marketplace (see also Col. 4:5). Believers who await the Lord’s Return are to be as energized and careful in their use of the remaining time as are bargain hunters during a brief sale offering large discounts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 18, 'drunk. A temporary, alcohol-induced high can never compete with the lasting rewards offered by such a joy-filled life, lived under the inspiration of the Spirit. For more on God’s ideal for HEALTH, see 1 Cor. 10:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 22, 'Other ancient and NT authors composed lists of rules for social structures (for NT examples see Col. 3:18–4:1; 1 Tim. 6:1–2; Titus 2:1–10; 1 Pet. 2:18–3:7). Paul’s list contrasts with non-biblical examples, which advocate harsh treatment in order to protect the honor of the husband, father, and slave owner. While Paul acknowledges the social structures of his time, he advocates filling those relationships with the values of the gospel. Wives, children, and slaves should offer appropriate submission and obedience motivated by their commitment to Christ. Husbands, parents, and slave owners are not to misuse their authority, but are to deal tenderly with those in their care. Note that the conclusion to the previous section (Eph. 5:21) calls on the whole church to be mutually submissive. Submission is clearly not something only for wives and slaves but for husbands and masters as well.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 25, 'just as Christ also loved the church. Christ is everything to the church. In addition to being her Bridegroom, He is the bride price paid for her (“Christ … gave Himself for her,” v. 25), the one who administers the bridal bath (v. 26), and the one who presents her to Himself (v. 27). In his counsel to Christian wives and husbands (vv. 22–33), Paul offers as a model the relationship between Christ, portrayed as bridegroom/husband, and His church, portrayed as bride/wife. In doing so, he reflects an important stream of OT thought (see esp. Is. 61:10; 62:4–5; Ezek. 16:1–14) and develops a metaphor for the church that he has used earlier (2 Cor. 11:2–5; see also Matt. 22:1–14; Mark 2:18–20; John 3:29–30; Rev. 19:7–9; 21:2, 9–21). As in 2 Cor. 11:2–5, the church is the betrothed bride, legally promised to Christ and awaiting the wedding at His Return. The divine Bridegroom anticipates eagerly the moment when, having prepared His glorious bride who exhibits no spot, wrinkle, or blemish (Eph. 5:27), He comes to claim her as His own. We, who are His bride, should be inspired by His eagerness and preparation for that grand event. For more examples of Christ’s care and provision for the CHURCH, see note on 4:11–16. On other metaphors used for the CHURCH, see note on 2:19–22. On the close connection between the holy city and the church, see note on Rev. 21:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 28, 'as their own bodies. These verses get to the heart of Paul’s counsel to Christian husbands. Drawing on Gen. 2:24, he advocates a “one flesh” model of marriage in which husbands love their wives as tenderly as they do themselves.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 5, 33, 'The advice rings true today: what a wife most desires is the tender love of a devoted husband. What a husband most needs is the respect of a caring wife.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 6, 1, 'Children, obey. Paul cites the fifth commandment (Ex. 20:12) and describes it as “the first commandment with promise.” This gives evidence of his respect for the Ten Commandments and their continuing importance for Christians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 6, 5, 'Bondservants. Slaves. Compare Col. 3:22–4:1; 1 Tim. 6:1–2; Titus 2:9–10; Philem.; 1 Pet. 2:18–25. It may startle us today that early Christian congregations included Christian slaves and slave masters. Paul underlines the true identity of Christian slaves as “bondservants of Christ” (Eph. 6:6) and warns Christian slave masters that they themselves have a divine Master (v. 9) who values slaves as much as He does slave masters.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 6, 10, 'Put on the whole armor of God. In a moving conclusion describing the great controversy between good and evil, Paul portrays the church as a well-equipped army (see Rom. 13:11–14; 1 Thess. 5:6–11). This metaphor for the church takes its place with other major ones in Ephesians (temple of God, Eph. 2:19–22; body of Christ, 4:1–16; bride of Christ, 5:25–27). Paul mimics OT battle speeches (for example, Deut. 20:2–4; 2 Chr. 20:13–19; 32:6–8) as he exhorts believers to stand (Eph. 6:11, 13–14) fast in the church’s battle against the devil and his forces. While offering a realistic assessment of the struggle, the passage emphasizes the promise of God’s presence and strength (v. 10) and God’s own weapons (v. 11; see Is. 59:17). Believers battle in the assurance of Christ’s victory at Calvary in the past (Col. 2:13–15) and final victory at His Return (Rev. 19:11–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 6, 12, 'principalities. This list of agencies or powers or spirits seems to summarize all satanic and demonic supernatural agencies arrayed against Jesus. It was very good news to all Christians that they need not fear such powers, because of Jesus’ victory over them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 6, 14, 'truth … righteousness … the gospel of peace … faith … salvation … the Spirit … the word of God. Paul identifies elements of the “whole armor” (vv. 11, 13) to be donned by believers. Christians will be victorious as they access these infallible weapons and support one another in the struggle. While portrayed as an army, the church’s actual role is to proclaim “the gospel of peace” (v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 6, 18, 'praying always. In ancient times, soldiers preparing for battle would pray to the gods. Here, Paul exhorts Christian soldiers to pray to God for their fellow believers and, especially, for him. Believers are not to be lone soldiers, but to support one another in prayer and deed as they join in the battle against evil. Compare Col. 4:2–4. the mystery of the gospel. See note on Eph. 3:1–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Efésios', 6, 21, 'Tychicus. In the closing to the letter, Paul introduces the carrier of it, Tychicus (see Acts 20:4; Col. 4:7–9), and offers a final, touching blessing.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 0, NULL, 'All the church fathers and the overwhelming majority of scholars agree that Paul wrote Philippians. At the same time, many scholars feel that the letter as found in modern Bibles may be a compilation of two or three separate letters that Paul sent to the Philippian church. For example, some feel that 3:1 should be connected to 4:4 rather than to the verses that immediately follow. Nevertheless, Philippians should be regarded as a unified letter, since the various attempts to separate it into different letters have failed to produce a consensus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 0, NULL, 'Although the population of Phillipi was relatively small (perhaps ca. 2,000), it was an important city in Macedonia. It was transformed into a Roman colony (Acts 16:12) in 42 B.C. by Mark Anthony and Octavian after their victory over the killers of Julius Caesar. After becoming emperor, Octavian (later Augustus) reorganized the colony. On both occasions, veterans were settled there in what was essentially a small copy of Rome.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 0, NULL, 'Paul wrote Philippians in prison while awaiting a trial (1:7, 13–14, 19). The letter, however, gives little indication where he was imprisoned when he wrote it. Christian tradition has long held that Paul wrote Philippians during his two-year house arrest in Rome (Acts 28:16, 30–31). But this tradition has been challenged on two grounds. First, the distance between Philippi and Rome is too great and two years is too short a time to accommodate all the trips that Timothy and Epaphroditus apparently made between the two cities. Second, Paul’s plans to return to Philippi after his release (Phil. 1:26) do not coincide with his plans to travel westward to Spain (Rom. 15:23–24) after his visit in Rome. Had Paul’s plans changed? Why should he want to return to Macedonia?
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 0, NULL, 'There are three important themes that run throughout the letter. The first is that of rejoicing. The Greek word for “rejoice” (chairo) occurs twenty-nine times in Paul’s letters. Of these, nine are found in Philippians, almost a third of the total occurrences. Rejoicing expresses many aspects of faith in Philippians. It expresses tolerance (1:18), a bond of fellowship in difficult times (2:17–18), firm commitment to faith (3:1), friendship and thanksgiving (2:28; 4:10), and the joy of belonging to Christ (4:4). Inasmuch as Philippians was written during a very difficult imprisonment, these aspects of rejoicing describe Christian hope in action.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 0, NULL, 'I.      Letter opening (1:1–11)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 1, 'Timothy. Paul’s associate whose mother was Jewish (Acts 16:1–3). Philippi. See Introduction. bishops. Overseers of churches. deacons. For their qualifications, see 1 Tim. 3:1–13 (compare to Titus 1:7–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 3, 'remembrance … prayer. Reveals the secret of Paul’s constant thanksgiving and prayer (see 1 Thess. 5:18): he habitually reflected on the people and events that he had touched and shaped through the gospel, and gave thanks and prayed for them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 5, 'your fellowship. Your partnership. The Philippian church became a constant source of friendship and financial support for Paul’s ministry (4:15; 2 Cor. 11:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 6, 'complete it. Refers to the perfection of the church through the gospel rather than the perfection of the individual.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 7, 'chains. Describes the way Paul was imprisoned when he dictated this letter. defense … gospel. E.g., see Acts 22:1–21; 24:10–21; 26:1–32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 8, 'long … Jesus. Being filled with affection like Jesus is one of the marks of Christian perfection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 9, 'love. Basis of the right moral choice. knowledge. Of the will of God. all discernment. Between right and wrong.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 10, 'may approve. Decide for yourself. things … excellent. The will of God. sincere. Of clear conscience. day of Christ. Second Coming of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 11, 'fruits of righteousness. Fruits of the Spirit. See Gal. 5:22–23. Only a person bearing the fruit of the Spirit can make the right ethical choice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 12, 'things which happened. Paul’s imprisonment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 13, 'palace guard. Several thousand elite Roman soldiers known as the Praetorian Guard, which functioned as the emperor’s bodyguard and Rome’s police. my chains are in Christ. I am innocent in charges of civil law breaking.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 14, 'become … chains. Christ-like behavior shown during trials provides encouragement to fellow believers who suffer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 15, 'Some … ambition. Refers to Paul’s opponents who tried to gain control over his territory and converts during his absence (Gal. 1:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 17, 'appointed. Imprisoned.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 18, 'in pretense or in truth. The success of the gospel depends not on the preacher’s motive but the gospel’s content. rejoice. A theme of Philippians; joy accompanies hope that intensifies in trials.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 20, 'body. Has a double meaning here: a body engaged in active labor, or the corpse of a martyr. Paul was eager to offer his body for either.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 21, 'Christ. To preach Christ. to die. As a martyr. gain. Honor. Paul apparently wished to end his life as a martyr, but he considered this somewhat selfish since it concerned his reputation. Yet, he loathed the idea of letting the opportunity of martyrdom slip away and dying from an accident or other natural causes like an ordinary person.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 22, 'what I shall choose. What should I pray for; see v. 25. Paul was released after about two years of house arrest in Rome. He was eventually martyred in Rome around A.D. 67.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 23, 'depart. From labors; die. be with Christ. Paul verbalizes his desire to leave this present troubled existence and be with Christ, without reference to any lapse of time that may occur between the two events. This verse does not teach that Paul expected to go to heaven at death. He was very clear that he would not receive his reward until the Second Coming (2 Tim. 4:8). Here he is saying that the next thing he would know after departing (death) would be Christ coming in the clouds of heaven to raise the dead, when he would “be with the Lord” (1 Thess. 4:17). It should also be noted that the Bible writers at times refer to two events together that may be separated by a long period of time. Compare, for example, Luke 4:17–19 with Is. 61:1–2. Jesus only quoted part of Isaiah because the remainder would not be fulfilled until the Second Coming. See also John 5:28–29 which gives no indication that the two resurrections are separated by 1,000 years as John explains in Rev. 20:4–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 24, 'Faced with two very attractive choices (fruitful labor or martyrdom), he chose the one that would benefit others most.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 27, 'conduct … gospel. Conduct that promotes love and unity in the community. one spirit. Without dividing. one mind. One purpose. faith of the gospel. Faith in the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 28, 'not … terrified. By prospect of martyrdom. proof of perdition. Proof that they will perish. The calm manner in which the martyrs died sowed the seeds of conversion in the hearts of the spectators.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 29, 'To be allowed to suffer or die for Christ is just as much a gift of God as to be saved by faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 1, 30, 'same … me. E.g., see Acts 16:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 1, 'fellowship of the Spirit. Spiritual fellowship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 2, 'like-minded … one mind. Does not refer to intellectual uniformity or lack of diversity but the qualities described in vv. 3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 3, 'esteem others better. Treat others as though they were your superiors.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 4, 'Christians live for others, as Christ did. For more on the incarnation of the Divine Christ, see Matt. 1:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 5, 'Let this mind. The selflessness described in vv. 3–4, which characterized the life of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 6, 'Love finds its most noble expression in freely sacrificing oneself for the one loved. Here Jesus’ love for the Father becomes a model for how believers should love one another.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 6, 'form of God. Same nature as God; contrasts Christ with Adam and Eve who, being merely created in God’s image, wanted divine power (Gen. 3:5–6). robbery. Taking by force.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 7, 'made … reputation. Christ gave up equality with God the Father, setting aside His divine powers during the Incarnation but never ceasing to be God. taking … bondservant. Refers to Jesus’ low station in life. in the likeness of men. Assuming the same nature as humans with ability to suffer like a human being. For more on the humanity of the PERSON OF CHRIST, see John 1:14; Heb. 4:14–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 8, 'found. Made. appearance. Refers to Jesus’ body consisting of flesh and bones (see also Luke 24:39). humbled Himself. Allowed Himself to be humiliated. became obedient. In love. to … death. Voluntarily. even … the cross. Extreme humiliation and abuse, all out of love for the Father (compare Heb. 12:2). See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 9, 'highly exalted. Through His resurrection, ascension, and enthronement. name. The title Lord Jesus, expressing the proof of His honor.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 10, 'those under the earth. Demons.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 11, 'Lord. See v. 9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 12, 'work … salvation. Without looking for help from any authority figure. with fear and trembling. (1) In weakness, i.e., with humility (1 Cor. 2:3); (2) in respectful obedience (2 Cor. 7:5); and (3) in sincerity (Eph. 6:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 13, 'good pleasure. To bless others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 15, 'This verse vividly depicts not only how the church is to be, but how it is to be seen in the eyes of the pagan world. blameless … harmless … without fault. So as not to give grounds for accusation to the opponents of Christianity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 16, 'word of life. The gospel. in the day of Christ. Upon resurrection at Jesus’ Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 17, 'poured out. Refers to his death by beheading. on the sacrifice. As a sacrificial victim.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 18, 'rejoice. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 19, 'trust in the Lord Jesus. Means absolute trust. Timothy. Paul’s trusted associate who began his ministry in Philippi. See v. 22; Acts 16:1–15; 1 Tim. 1:2. shortly. Indicates that Paul badly needs him now. know your state. Hear that you are strong in the faith: an indirect plea not to give up the faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 20, 'like-minded. As Paul. sincerely. With genuine affection. Compare v. 22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 21, 'all. Many; veiled reference to the disappointment Paul felt at the desertion of many.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 22, 'proven character. Timothy’s ability to endure hardship with joy. See Rom. 5:3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 23, 'how … me. Things are not well with Paul; he may have been ill.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 24, 'trust in the Lord. Shows absolute trust. Vv. 19–24 indicate that Paul was more confident of his release than whether he could do without Timothy. The sending of Timothy was Paul’s supreme gift to the Philippians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 25, 'Epaphroditus. Unknown outside of Philippians. your messenger. The Philippian church sent Epaphroditus with money to provide personal assistance to Paul during his imprisonment. See 4:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 26, 'It appears that Epaphroditus fell ill soon after arriving in Rome, unable to provide much assistance to Paul. sorrow upon sorrow. What reality was like for Paul: (1) Christian rejoicing does not depend on circumstance; (2) Christian rejoicing often goes hand in hand with sorrow: greater the sorrow, greater the joy, and vice versa.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 28, 'sent … eagerly … again. Even though Paul needed Epaphroditus; this gives a window into Paul’s unselfish life. sorrowful. See note on vv. 26–27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 29, 'in the Lord. In warm Christian fellowship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 2, 30, 'for the work of Christ. Not because of weakness or homesickness. supply … service. Probably a quote of what the Philippians said when they sent Epaphroditus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 1, 'Finally. And now. rejoice. See Introduction, “Message.” same things. Repeated call to rejoice. is safe. Reminder keeps you from errors and discouragements.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 2, 'dogs. Reference to defilement. Some Jews regarded the Gentiles as dogs. Paul dismisses his opponents, who claim perfection (v. 15), as defiled and deformed because they do not have the Spirit in their lives (v. 3). evil workers. Refers to Paul’s opponents who exclude the Gentile believers. mutilation. Physical circumcision. Paul’s opponents in Philippi were Jewish.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 3, 'we are the circumcision. Believers of whatever cultural background are truly God’s covenant people and deserve the label “the circumcision” (see Rom. 2:28–29). Spirit. Of life and resurrection (Rom. 8:2, 10–11). rejoice. Trust. flesh. His own status and performance such as circumcision (Phil. 3:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 4, 'confidence. Grounds for boasting. in the flesh. In terms of worldly status and achievements.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 5, 'Hebrew of the Hebrews. Paul’s parents were both Jews; the Hebrews were the “elect” of God (see Deut. 7:6; 14:2). Pharisee. Known for their strict observance of the Mosaic laws and the Jewish scribal traditions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 6, 'zeal. For Judaism. persecuting the church. See Acts 9:1–2. the law. The Law of Moses. blameless. Thanks to his Pharisaical legal manipulation of the law, Paul thought that he had achieved perfection. Strangely, however, it was this “perfection” that led him to become a persecutor of the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 7, 'gain. Advantage or profit; business language. loss. Disadvantage or a write-off; business language.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 8, 'all things. All advantageous things, see vv. 5–6. excellence. Extreme desirability. knowledge of Christ. Experience and union with Christ. my Lord. Figuratively expresses Christ as a cherished personal possession. loss … gain. See note on v. 7. rubbish. Repulsive. gain Christ. To win Christ as one’s most valuable possession, one must reject all of his earthly advantages as worthless.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 9, 'found. Accepted by God. law. The Law of Moses. faith in Christ. Explained by the next phrase. righteousness … faith. Faith in Christ means removal of all other grounds of confidence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 10, 'know. Experience. fellowship of. Participation in. conformed to His death. Crucified with Him; see Rom. 6:3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 11, 'resurrection. Refers to the present experience of perfect conformity to Christ as well as the resurrection at the end of time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 12, 'attained. Christ is expressed figuratively as an object of possession. perfected. Through conformity to Christ. lay hold of that. Perfect righteousness; the phrase denotes a burning desire.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 13, 'not count … apprehended. I do not believe I have achieved perfection. forgetting those things. Achievements as well as mistakes. reaching. Indicates a striving for Christian perfection (see v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 14, 'goal. Of perfect conformity to Christ, see v. 21. upward call. (1) To rise from the dead and ascend to heaven (vv. 20–21; 1 Thess. 4:16); (2) to perfection (Phil. 3:12–13). in Christ. By grace.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 15, 'mature. Maturing toward perfection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 16, 'attained. Perfection. same rule. See vv. 12–13. same mind. Unity is also an attribute of perfection.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 17, 'my example. See vv. 5–13. us for a pattern. Apostolic pattern (see 1 Cor. 4:9–13 and notes).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 18, 'walk. Claim perfection. These immoral and greedy opponents of Paul claimed that they had achieved perfection through circumcision (see vv. 2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 20, 'our citizenship is in heaven. Not in earthly Israel through physical signs of covenant like circumcision (see vv. 5–6), or in Rome through birth, power, and money; the early Gentile Christians were aliens rejected by virtually all cultures.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 3, 21, 'conformed … body. Describes the resurrection of the righteous dead or the transformation of the righteous living as achieving perfection. subdue … Himself. Bring into perfect conformity to His will. For more on the bodily nature of the RESURRECTION, see Is. 26:19; 1 Cor. 15:51–55; 1 Thess. 4:13–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 1, 'Therefore. Since you are citizens of heaven (see 3:20–21). joy and crown. Crown of joy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 2, 'Euodia … Syntyche. Prominent Christian women, perhaps the owners of homes where the church met (such as Lydia in Acts 16:14–15, 40); their identities and quarrels are unknown.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 3, 'you … Clement. Unknown persons. the rest. The Book of Life contains the names of many unknown persons like these. Book of Life. A heavenly record of the saved. For more on the books used in the JUDGMENT, see Ex. 32:32–33; Dan. 7:9–14; Luke 10:20; Rev. 3:5; 20:15; 21:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 4, 'Rejoice. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 5, 'gentleness. Spirit of forgiveness and acceptance. Lord is at hand. (1) We must leave the judging to God; and (2) forgive others so we can obtain forgiveness at the final judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 7, 'understanding. Expectation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 8, 'All things good, beautiful, and true come from God, wherever or in whatever form they may be found. meditate. Think carefully about; consider.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 9, 'learned and received and heard and saw. Four ways by which doctrine is accepted.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 10, 'at last. After a rather long interruption.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 11, 'The secret of Paul’s constant contentment was thanksgiving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 13, 'do all things. Cope with every situation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 15, 'gospel. Paul’s distinctive preaching on the acceptance of the Gentiles. giving. Financial support. receiving. Recognition.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 16, 'even in Thessalonica. See 1 Thess. 2:9. once and again. Occasionally.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 17, 'Not … gift. He was not asking for more money. Ancient prisoners relied on food and supplies from friends and family. account. Advantage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 18, 'I have all. I received everything you sent. Epaphroditus. See Introduction, “Date and Place of Writing,” and 2:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filipenses', 4, 22, 'Caesar’s household. Families of the Praetorian Guard. See note on 1:13.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 0, NULL, 'Paul composed the epistle to the Colossians, in company with Timothy (1:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 0, NULL, 'Paul addresses the letter to Christians he seems not to have met (1:4). The church in Colosse was founded by one of Paul’s converts, Epaphras, a man Paul respected deeply, who likely evangelized the area while Paul was working in Ephesus (1:7–8; 4:12; Philem. 23; see Acts 19:1–41). The letter is written in response to a visit by Epaphras, who brings good news about the faith of the Christians in Colosse (1:3–8) but also communicates concern about teachings that are threatening to undermine that faith (see esp. 2:8–23). The purpose of Paul’s letter is to address that threat.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 0, NULL, 'The epistle to the Colossians was probably composed about A.D. 61, during Paul’s first imprisonment in Rome (Colossians is one of the Prison Epistles since it, as Ephesians, Philippians, and Philemon, was written from prison; see 4:3, 18.) For more on the date, see the Introduction to Ephesians, “Date and Place of Writing.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 0, NULL, 'The message of the letter is well understood by noting each time Christ is mentioned. In Paul’s reports of his prayers and work, it is Christ who is discussed and praised (1:3–2:5). When Paul deals with the threatening heresy, his central worry is that believers will be drawn away from Christ (2:6–23). Paul discusses the heresy briefly but uses more words talking about Jesus as the defining Person, “the head of all principality and power” (2:10). When, in the final two chapters, Paul concentrates on offering pastoral counsel, Christ remains central (3:1–4:6). Even in his conclusion, offering instructions and exchanging greetings, Paul cannot help but mention Christ again and again (4:7–18). The role and importance of Christ constitute the central theme of the letter. God has acted in Christ to redeem the believers. Christ is the active agent of both creation and redemption (1:15–20). All the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are to be found in Him (2:3). As we study the letter, we must never lose sight of this theme: Colossians is all about Jesus.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 0, NULL, 'I.      Greetings and introduction (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 1, 'Paul is identified as author, together with Timothy, one of his trusted coworkers (see Acts 16:1–5; 1 & 2 Tim.). As is the pattern for ancient letters, the recipients of the letter are then identified.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 3, 'We give thanks. Paul begins the body of his letter with a thanksgiving and prayer for the recipients, as he often does. In vv. 3–8, he describes what prompts his worship: he has heard of their acceptance of the gospel preached by Epaphras and their growth in Christian faith (see Introduction, “Audience”). In vv. 9–12, he turns to the topics of his prayers for them—that they might know and follow God’s will.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 13, 'A comparison with Ex. 20:1–11 suggests that Paul is reflecting on the Ten Commandments. As He did for the Israelites, God has freed believers from the power of darkness and given them redemption in Christ (vv. 13–14; Ex. 20:1–3). The Ten Commandments continue, “You shall not make for yourself a carved image” (Ex. 20:4–6); Paul speaks of Jesus as the true image of the invisible God (v. 15; see John 1:18; 14:9; 2 Cor. 4:4; Heb. 1:3). The Sabbath commandment (Ex. 20:8–11) commemorates God’s creation of “the heavens and the earth, the sea, and all that is in them” (v. 11). Paul borrows this language to honor Jesus as the One who creates and reconciles all things … that are in heaven and that are on earth (see Acts 4:23–24). Christians, then, should worship Jesus on the Sabbath as their Creator and Redeemer. True Sabbath worship is all about Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 15, 'This hymn-like passage celebrates two central aspects of the work of Christ—CREATION (vv. 15–17) and redemption (vv. 18–20). For the role of Christ in CREATION, see note on Gen. 1:3. For more on SALVATION through Christ’s substitutionary atonement, see Rom. 3:21–26; Eph. 2:4–10. The threatened heresy at Colosse tended to accent the importance of other powers and, apparently, diminish the importance of Christ. Paul’s hymn sings of the cosmic significance of Jesus as Creator and Redeemer (compare John 1:1–18; Heb. 1:1–4). Since every other being or power was created by Him (vv. 15–16), none can compete with Him. He alone is the image of the invisible God and the firstborn over all Creation (v. 15), and worthy of our worship. Firstborn over all Creation is not to be taken literally as having to do with actual birth, but instead refers to the privileges and rights of the firstborn son, like the Davidic reference in Ps. 89:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 21, 'if … you continue. Paul’s “if” is a reminder that the saved can be lost, and that once saved does not mean always saved. preached to every creature under heaven. Paul celebrates what God has done for believers. Caught up in the wonderful progress of the gospel, Paul uses hyperbole (or overstatement).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 24, 'In spite of a lack of personal history with the Christians in Colosse (see 2:1 and the Introduction, “Audience”), Paul is deeply concerned about them. His sufferings and conflict are endured for them (1:24; 2:1), and he understands his commission as the apostle to the Gentiles to include them (1:25–28). He even imagines being present with them in spirit (2:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 24, 'what is lacking in the afflictions of Christ. Paul seems to claim that Christ’s death did not accomplish all that was necessary and that he, Paul, must make up the difference by his own sufferings. But this is not what he is addressing. Paul believes that by the death of Christ on Calvary, God provides all that is essential for our salvation; he also uses other terms like “blood,” “cross,” and “death” to describe Christ’s redeeming death (vv. 13–14, 19–22; 2:13–14; Rom. 3:21–26; Phil. 3:8–10). He means something different by the phrase the afflictions of Christ. On the road to Damascus, Paul learned that Jesus identifies with His people in their suffering when the risen Jesus said to him, “Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting Me?” (Acts 9:4; 22:7; 26:14). So the sufferings of Paul and other believers, experienced as they witness about Christ, can be called the afflictions of Christ. In Paul’s mind, a certain quantity of these sufferings must be experienced until Christ returns. The thought that in his own sufferings he may be hastening the Return of Christ and sparing other believers increases his joy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 1, 26, 'the mystery. See Eph. 3:1–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 1, 'Laodicea. See Introduction, “Audience,” and 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 3, 'The false teaching circulating at Colosse seems to have diminished the worship of Christ by honoring “principalities and powers” (2:15), as well as “angels” (2:18), and likely argued that the worship of these beings gave special wisdom and knowledge. Paul makes it clear that all wisdom and knowledge reside in Jesus and that believers must rely on that Source of wisdom to manage their lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 6, 'Paul turns to practical matters by inviting believers to recall their conversion—focused as it was on Christ Jesus the Lord—and to mirror that time in their current experience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 8, 'Here Paul engages most directly the erroneous views impacting believers at Colosse as he issues three warnings to them (vv. 8, 16, 18). The erroneous views threaten to draw believers away from Christ, and Paul wishes to underline the lordship of Christ in their lives. Anything that detracts from their devotion to Him is to be shunned. They should be holding fast to the Head, Christ (v. 19). Defining the erroneous views closely is a challenging task since Paul’s descriptions are brief and not detailed. The false teachings seem to feature (1) human additions to Christian teaching (vv. 8, 22); (2) undue respect for beings or powers other than Christ (all principality and power, v. 10; principalities and powers, v. 15; angels, v. 18; and, perhaps, the basic principles of the world, vv. 8, 20); (3) misuse of religious holy days, perhaps to honor such beings or powers (v. 16); and (4) asceticism, or overly harsh treatment of the body (vv. 16, 20–23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 12, 'buried. Compare Rom. 6:3–8. For more on BAPTISM, see Acts 8:38–39; Matt. 3:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 13, 'Paul illustrates the forgiveness believers have experienced by saying that God wiped out the handwriting of requirements that was against us and nailed it to the cross. This may refer to the ceremonial law of Moses and the cessation at the cross of such ceremonial requirements as sacrifices. Or the picturesque language may describe a certificate of debt or promissory note that lists the debt owed for our sins, an obligation canceled by Christ on His cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 15, 'principalities and powers. We are painfully aware that evil powers still threaten us. We must not forget that they are defeated foes. Christ, on the cross, appeared to be mastered by evil and demonic powers, while in fact, just the opposite was occurring. Using language describing a Roman triumphal parade, Paul declares that Christ, on Calvary, disarmed these powers and put them on display as His conquered enemies. We Christians have no need to serve powers already defeated by our Lord (see Matt. 12:29; Luke 10:18; Rom. 16:20; Eph. 6:10–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 2, 16, 'let no one judge you … sabbaths. Paul’s warning may mean that Christians should not be drawn into keeping ceremonial events—festivals, new moons, and ceremonial sabbaths—described in the OT (see esp. Lev. 23). Since those observances, collectively a shadow of things to come, pointed forward to the work and sacrifice of Christ, they ended with His death. By way of contrast, the weekly, seventh-day Sabbath points backward to the creation event (Gen. 2:1–3; Ex. 20:8–11). It is possible, though, that the term “sabbaths” could include the weekly, seventh-day Sabbath. If so, Paul’s concern is with any religious ceremony, the weekly Sabbath included, that is misused to honor “principalities and powers” (v. 15) and is not focused on the worship of Christ. In neither case is he addressing the moral obligation to properly observe the weekly Sabbath. See Introduction, “Message,” for comments on how chap. 1 aids in understanding this passage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 3, 1, 'Paul turns to practical implications of his statements that believers have been raised with Christ and made alive with Him (see also 2:12–13). How should Christians live in view of this reality? An opening exhortation on this theme (vv. 1–4) is followed by the call to put to death or put off the old pattern of life (vv. 5–9) and to put on the new pattern marked by forgiveness and love (vv. 10–17). Throughout the passage, Paul invites believers to live the reality of what they have already received by faith. He declares that they have been raised with Christ (v. 1) and invites them to live in the light of that fact. Compare Rom. 6:1–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 3, 1, 'Compare Eph. 2:1–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 3, 5, 'Two lists of sins or vices to be shunned by believers are provided (vv. 5, 8; compare Rom. 13:13; Gal. 5:19–21; Eph. 4:25–5:5). Since Christians have been “raised with Christ” (v. 1) and have put off the old man with his deeds (v. 9), such evil need no longer dominate them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 3, 10, 'put on the new man. Compare Eph. 4:22–24. The new man or new Christian pattern of life is to be modeled after the image of Him who created him, Christ (see also Col. 1:15–16). Since everyone is modeled after Christ, this new pattern is marked by the removal of cultural, religious, and class divisions. See Eph. 2:14–18; 3:1–13. barbarian, Scythian. A barbarian was someone who did not speak Greek and so was thought of as uncivilized, while the Scythian was characterized as especially savage and uncouth. In Christian community, all such stereotypes are obsolete.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 3, 12, 'put on tender mercies. The new pattern of life (see note on vv. 10–11) features kindness, forgiveness, and love toward one’s fellow believers (for similar lists of virtues, see Gal. 5:22–23; Eph. 4:32–5:1; Phil. 4:8; Titus 3:1–2). Christ again serves as the model (v. 13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 3, 15, 'Living according to the new pattern (vv. 10–14) leads to peace in the body of Christ, the church (v. 15), and to inspiring times of worship (v. 16). Paul clearly believes that the power of the risen Christ, expressed in the lives of His people, is more than a match for the power of evil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 3, 18, 'See Eph. 5:22–6:9 and notes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 2, 'prayer. Paul asks the believers to pray that God will open a door for him to proclaim the gospel. He does not seem to mind being in chains if “the word of God is not chained” (2 Tim. 2:9). Compare Eph. 6:19–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 7, 'Tychicus. The carrier of the letter (compare Acts 20:4; Eph. 6:21–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 9, 'Onesimus. A runaway slave from Colosse, described in the epistle to Philemon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 10, 'Aristarchus. A frequent companion of Paul (Acts 19:29; 20:4; 27:2). Mark. See note on 2 Tim. 4:9–12. Barnabas. See note on Acts 4:36–37.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 11, 'Jesus who is called Justus. Only mentioned here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 12, 'Epaphras. See 1:3–8 and Introduction, “Audience.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 14, 'Luke. It is from this passage that we learn Luke’s identity as the beloved physician. Demas. Later deserts Paul (2 Tim. 4:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 15, 'Nymphas. Only mentioned here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 16, 'Some believe Ephesians to be the letter to Laodicea since the words “in Ephesus” (Eph. 1:1) are left out of some early manuscripts and one early Christian calls Ephesians the epistle to the Laodiceans.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Colossenses', 4, 17, 'Archippus. Called “our fellow soldier” in Philem. 2.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'First Thessalonians came to the church at Thessalonica from three authors—Paul, Silvanus, and Timothy (1:1, see also 2 Thess. 1:1). Paul was clearly the primary author of the letter (2:18; 3:5; 5:27), but he was happy to share the stage with his ministry colleagues.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'The historical context of 1 and 2 Thessalonians is described in Acts 17 and 18 and hinted at in the letters as well. After a painful experience in Philippi (Acts 16:12–40; 1 Thess. 2:2), Paul and his companions (Silas and Timothy) moved on to the city of Thessalonica, which had a Jewish synagogue (Acts 17:1). There Paul preached from the Bible that it was necessary for the Messiah to suffer first and then rise from the dead. He also attempted to show that Jesus fulfilled what the Messiah was supposed to be like (Acts 17:2–3). A number of Jews and Greeks believed (Acts 17:4), but rioting broke out (Acts 17:5–6) and the city authorities intervened (Acts 17:8–9), with the result that Paul and Silas (and presumably Timothy) left the city immediately (Acts 17:10).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'In Paul’s day Greece was divided into two Roman provinces. The northern province, including Thessalonica and Philippi, was called Macedonia. The southern province, which included Athens and Corinth, was called Achaia. Both provinces are also mentioned together in this letter, as well as in Acts 19:21, Romans 15:26, and in 2 Corinthians 9:2 and 11:9–10. Thessalonica was the largest city in Macedonia, with a port on the Aegean Sea and a major highway (the Egnatian Way) leading to the Adriatic Sea and, from there, on to Rome.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'It is natural for new believers to face serious challenges in the early weeks following their conversion. Paul knew this. Since he was not able to be with them, he first sent Timothy to get information on how they were doing. Timothy reported on their faithfulness and also on some challenges they were facing. Paul then responded to their questions and experiences with the letter we know as 1 Thessalonians.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'I.      Greetings and thanksgiving (1:1–5)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 1, 'Paul opens in the customary form of an ancient Greek letter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 1, 'Silvanus. See Introduction, “Authors.” church … Thessalonians. See Introduction, “Date and Place of Writing.” grace. Greek greeting. peace. Hebrew greeting.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 2, 'give thanks. Main verb of a sentence that was nine verses long in Greek (vv. 2–10). His praise here eases into his rebuke for them later.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 3, 'work of faith. Paul is not against works as long as they spring from faith (Rom. 1:5; 2 Thess. 1:11), but he is against attempts to earn salvation by works (Rom. 4:1–5). faith … love … hope. Paul often unites this trio of Christian virtues. See 5:8; Rom. 5:2–5; 1 Cor. 13:13; Gal. 5:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 4, 'election. Coming to faith is a miracle of God, yet it is also a choice (v. 9; 2:13; 5:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 5, 'our gospel. See “my” gospel in Rom. 16:25. Paul’s understanding of the gospel was received by direct revelation (Gal. 1:11–12). For the content of Paul’s gospel in Thessalonica, see Acts 17:2–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 6, 'followers. Literally, “imitators.” affliction. Either outward oppression or inward distress. joy. Joy in the context of suffering is not natural; it is clear evidence of the Holy Spirit’s presence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 7, 'Macedonia and Achaia. See Introduction, “Date and Place of Writing.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 1, 10, 'the wrath to come. The final judgment (see also 2:16). This wrath is judicial, not irrational.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 1, 'The focus moves from the Thessalonians (chap. 1) to Paul’s apostolic calling. He responds to attacks on his motives for preaching to them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 1, 'vain. Powerless, without results.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 2, 'at Philippi. See Acts 16:22–24. bold. Free in speech in spite of intimidation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 3, 'error. An intellectual mistake. uncleanness. Impure motives, which may have a sexual connotation. in deceit. Intentionally misleading.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 4, 'Paul is motivated to please God rather than human beings. hearts. In the ancient Hebrew context, the “heart” is the inner self, the seat of feelings, character, intentions, and motivations (see Gen. 6:5–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 5, 'flattering words. Opposite of “bold” speech (v. 2). covetousness. Monetary greed. God is witness. No one but God can truly know the motives of another. glory from men. Underlines v. 4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 9, 'The Greeks despised manual labor, but Paul did it anyway to demonstrate his care and concern for them. Genuine love is demonstrated by self-sacrifice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 10, 'devoutly. Pleasing to God. justly. Fair in human terms.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 11, 'every one … children. A good father individualizes his nurture and discipline. See 1 Cor. 9:19–23. See also 1 Thess. 2:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 12, 'that. Introduces the purpose of vv. 10–11. kingdom. The primary topic of Jesus’ teaching (see Matt. 4:23; 13:11; Mark 1:15; Luke 4:43).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 13, 'Paul declares that his teaching is the word of God. His writings became part of the Scriptures. Compare 2 Pet. 3:15–16. For more on the BIBLE being the word of God, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17; 2 Pet. 1:20–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 14, 'suffered the same things. Persecution and suffering play no favorites.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 15, 'Discussion of the Jewish persecution of Christians (compare Matt. 23:29–38; Acts 17:5). Paul is clearly not speaking about all Jews, as he was one himself. He assures believers here that God has marked the acts of particular persecutors for ultimate judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 17, 'taken away. Literally, “orphaned.” In this chapter, Paul likened himself to a mother (v. 7), a father (v. 11), and now an orphan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 18, 'Satan hindered us. Since Timothy was able to return (3:2, 6), the “us” here is actually referring only to Paul.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 2, 19, 'crown. See note on Rev. 2:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 1, 'Timothy returned to Thessalonica as a substitute for Paul. Vv. 3–4 summarize what Paul would tell them about suffering if he were able to return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 1, 'Therefore. Refers back to 2:17–18. no longer endure. Repeated in v. 5. we. Here the plural clearly refers only to Paul, not his colleagues. See v. 5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 2, 'to establish … faith. The purpose of Timothy’s visit. Paul is worried the Thessalonians’ faith may be shaken by suffering (see vv. 3–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 3, 'afflictions … tribulation. Believers should expect challenging circumstances from Satan (v. 5), human opposition (2:1–2), and the contrary nature of sinful society (4:10–12). appointed. Implies God’s purpose (Luke 2:34; Phil. 1:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 5, 'tempter. Satan. in vain. A further motive for Timothy’s visit was Paul’s concern whether the results of his work would survive. See 2:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 6, 'now. Timothy’s return immediately prompted the letter. greatly desiring. Paul seems to have worried about their reaction to his sudden departure (Acts 17:6–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 7, 'affliction and distress. A word pair associated with the end time in 1 Cor. 7:26–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 9, 'Paul’s prayers are filled with thanks, rejoicing, and requests relating to the details of his ministry. what is lacking. They needed guidance in areas of morality (4:1–12), the Second Coming (4:13–5:11), and practical church affairs (5:12–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 3, 11, 'Paul’s list of prayer requests. holiness. Opposite of “uncleanness” in 4:7. See also 2 Cor. 7:1. saints. Refers to angels here (see Zech. 14:5). See Matt. 24:30–31; Mark 8:38.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 1, 'Finally. Transition to the practical advice portion of the letter. walk. Hebrew expression for everyday behavior; elaborated on in vv. 3–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 2, 'commandments … Jesus. The oral tradition about what Jesus said and did (later summarized in the four Gospels) was the foundation for Paul’s practical teachings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 3, 'sanctification. Same Greek word as “holiness” in v. 7. sexual immorality. The Greek word (porneia) covers a wide range of unlawful sexual activity including homosexual behavior, prostitution, and adultery.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 4, 'possess … vessel. Can be translated “acquire your own wife” (1 Pet. 3:7) or “control your own body” (Rom. 9:21; 2 Tim. 2:21). If Paul meant the former, this would parallel the advice of 1 Cor. 7:9, 25–40. passion of lust. See Rom. 1:24. The prevailing, sexually permissive Roman culture presented a challenge for the church in instilling the ideal of sexual purity among its members.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 6, 'defraud. By adultery. brother. In the broadest sense, anyone for whom Christ died (Rom. 14:15; 1 Cor. 8:11). avenger. We are accountable to God for how we treat others sexually.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 7, 'holiness. See vv. 3–4; 3:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 8, 'Sexual practices affect one’s relationship with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 9, 'brotherly love. Translates a Greek word that means love for blood relations. In the church it came to mean love for fellow believers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 11, 'quiet life. The Thessalonian church seems to have had some disruptive and lazy individuals. See 2 Thess. 3:6–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 12, 'walk. See note on 4:1. lack nothing. Enthusiasm for the return of Jesus (2 Thess. 2:1–2) may have led some Thessalonian Christians to quit their jobs and depend on non-believing neighbors for support.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 13, 'Major passage on the events related to the SECOND COMING of Christ. The Second Coming of Christ is the Christian hope. At that moment, those who died in Christ will be resurrected, join those who are still alive, and together they meet Christ in the air. The blessing of being reunited with relatives, friends, and the Savior will bring indescribable joy (see Titus 2:13). For more on the SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24–25; Mark 13:1–37; Luke 21:5–36.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 13, 'fallen asleep. An ancient metaphor for death. For more on DEATH as sleep, see Dan. 12:2; John 11:11–16; Acts 7:60; 1 Cor. 15:51. sorrow. Actually “continue to sorrow.” Sorrow is normal when losing a loved one, but Christian faith provides hope in the midst of sorrow.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 14, 'For. This verse gives the reason for Christian hope. bring with Him those who sleep. That is, with Jesus. Christian hope in death is focused on the resurrection of the body at the Second Coming of Christ, not on immediate transport of a disembodied spirit to heaven at death. For more on the RESURRECTION as waking from sleep, see John 11:11–14; 1 Cor. 15:51–55. If the dead believers were already in heaven, as some teach, Paul would certainly have said so here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 15, 'For other NT summary passages dealing with the resurrection of Christ’s followers, see 1 Cor. 15:51–55; Phil. 3:21. See also Is. 26:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 15, 'the word of the Lord. A saying of Jesus while on earth. precede. The Thessalonians seem to be worried that believers who have died will be disadvantaged in some way when Jesus comes back.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 16, 'shout … trumpet. This event is certainly no secret. archangel. See Jude 9 for the only other use of the term in the Bible. rise first. Those who die in faith rise first to fully participate with the living believers in the Second Coming of Jesus. This is a physical resurrection which restores bodily existence. For more on the RESURRECTION in the NT, see Matt. 24:31; John 5:28–29; 1 Cor. 15:51–55.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 4, 17, 'in the clouds. See Rev. 1:7. to meet … air. John 14:1–3 makes it clear that after this event Jesus and the believers continue ascending up to heaven. They remain there with Him for a thousand years (Rev. 20:4–6) and then descend back to earth with Him in the New Jerusalem (Rev. 21:2). Compare the reward of the righteous at Christ’s SECOND COMING with that of the wicked in 2 Thess. 1:7–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 1, 'The central theme is the SECOND COMING, with a focus on the need for constant readiness. See also Matt. 24:42–44; 25:1–30; 2 Pet. 3:8–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 1, 'the times … seasons. Typical language for the timing of the end. See Acts 1:6–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 2, 'the day … Lord. OT phrase representing the last days (Is. 13:10–13; 34:4; Ezek. 32:7–8; Amos 8:8–10). as a thief. The reality of the Second Coming is certain; its timing is a surprise for unbelievers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 3, 'See Luke 21:34–36. Paul is building on sayings of Jesus here. they. Paul is speaking about unbelievers, not himself or his readers. labor pains. They are sudden and inevitable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 4, 'True believers will be ready for that day and will not be caught unprepared. Day … thief. See v. 2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 6, 'sleep. The opposite of being watchful. sober. Self-controlled.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 8, 'putting on. Completes a trio of symbolic directions: Be awake, sober (v. 6), and armed (v. 8, see Eph. 6:10–17) as the end approaches. faith … love … hope. See note on 1:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 9, 'salvation. Salvation is defined here as avoiding wrath (v. 9) and living with Jesus (v. 10). wake or sleep. Live or die as the coming of Jesus approaches.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 11, 'edify. Literally, “build up.” How to do this is spelled out in vv. 12–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 12, 'recognize. Literally, “to see.” To notice and respect. those. The early church practiced group leadership. People often resent those who try to correct them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 13, 'for their work’s sake. Whether or not we like church leaders, we are to esteem them on account of the responsibility they carry for us before the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 14, 'unruly. Those who openly disobey community rules or are willfully difficult. fainthearted. Literally, “short of breath.” Those who are anxious or of low self-confidence. weak. Those who are gullible, easily discouraged by hardship, fearful of the unfamiliar.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 15, 'Church leaders may be tempted to retaliate when their efforts are not appreciated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 16, 'See Gal. 5:22; Phil. 4:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 17, 'Live every moment in the consciousness of God’s presence. Paul lived by this principle (1:2–3; 2:13). See also Luke 18:1–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 18, 'Lack of thankfulness is the root of idolatry and immorality (Rom. 1:21–27). If we cannot be thankful for everything, we can still be thankful in everything.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 19, 'Do not resist the Spirit’s influence, particularly in prophecies (vv. 20–21). Test. The Bible presents at least four tests on the genuineness of a prophet (see Is. 8:20; Jer. 28:9; Matt. 7:20; 1 John 4:1–2). For more on SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 21, 'what is good. Whatever “builds up” the church (see note on v. 11). See also 1 Cor. 14:4–5, 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 23, 'whole spirit, soul, and body. Paul is not thinking of separate parts that make up a person, but of the whole person. He does not believe that a “spirit” or “soul” can exist apart from the body. A believer rests in death (4:13) until the resurrection (4:13–18). preserved blameless. Paul is describing what the Thessalonians already have.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 26, 'holy kiss. The kind that family members (“brethren”) would share.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Tessalonicenses', 5, 27, 'read to all. No believer should be left out.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'Like 1 Thessalonians, 2 Thessalonians came to the church at Thessalonica from three authors: Paul, Silvanus, and Timothy (1:1; see also 1 Thess. 1:1). See Introduction to 1 Thessalonians, “Authors.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'See Introduction to 1 Thessalonians for the larger picture. After Paul wrote the first letter to the Thessalonians, the problems in the church got worse instead of better. Some Thessalonians, probably using the Second Coming as an excuse, refused to work and expected others to feed them. In this letter Paul moves from warning such people (1 Thess. 4:11–12; 5:14–15) to commanding the church to use severe discipline (2 Thess. 3:6–13).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'See Introduction to 1 Thessalonians. It is likely that the second letter was written from Corinth a few weeks or months after the first letter, in A.D. 50–51.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'Since Jesus’ return was delayed in the experience of the church, Paul addressed in 2 Thessalonians how the church should live in the interim between Paul’s day and the Second Coming. They should behave in ways that glorify Christ (1:12), they should not be shaken or disturbed by the events around them (2:2), they should stand fast in the teachings they have learned from Paul (2:15; 3:14) and in prayer (3:1), and they should work hard, live quiet lives, and mind their own business (3:6–13).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 0, NULL, 'I.      Greetings and thanksgiving (1:1–12)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 1, 'Paul opens in the customary form of an ancient Greek letter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 1, 'See notes on 1 Thess. 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 2, 'grace … peace. See notes on 1 Thess. 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 3, 'A single sentence in the Greek. The main verb is the phrase “are bound to thank.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 3, 'because. Vv. 3–4 give the grounds for Paul’s thanksgiving in this letter. faith. Paul was once worried about their faith (1 Thess. 3:1–10), but now he rejoices at its steady growth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 4, 'your patience … endure. Either the severity of their persecution or the quality of their response was unique among the churches Paul worked with.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 5, 'Paul discusses the fate of both the Thessalonians and their persecutors in the final judgment. As in 1 Thess. 4:16–17, the Second Coming is not secretive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 5, 'manifest evidence. Their patience and faith in trial (clearly a spiritual gift) is advance evidence of the just and positive verdict they will receive in the judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 6, 'a righteous thing. It is just for God to afflict those who afflict others.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 7, 'revealed. At the Second Coming (see 1 Thess. 4:16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 8, 'flaming fire. Symbol of judgment. The wicked are completely destroyed at the Second Coming (see notes on v. 6; 2:8; Rev. 19:17–21; 20:4–6, 9). Compare with the reward of the righteous at Christ’s SECOND COMING in 1 Thess. 4:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 1, 9, 'everlasting destruction. The wicked are not tormented forever. Their destruction in death is complete and lasts forever. By death they are eternally removed from the universe. The effect of their complete destruction or annihilation lasts for eternity. For more on HELL and the punishment of the wicked, see Is. 34:9–10; Matt. 3:12; 10:28; 25:41; Mark 9:43; Jude 7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 1, 'Paul’s most detailed outline of final events. See Introduction, “Message,” for the bigger picture and a discussion about interpreting this passage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 1, 'gathering together. At the Second Coming; an allusion to 1 Thess. 4:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 2, 'shaken … troubled. Language of Jesus (Matt. 24:6; Mark 13:7). spirit. Prophetic teaching. had come. Some Thessalonians probably believed that Jesus had already come in a non-visible way.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 3, 'falling away. Translates Greek word for “apostasy.” man of sin. Same as “lawless one” of v. 8. perdition. Destruction or ruin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 4, 'opposes. Used of Satan in Zech. 3:1 and 1 Tim. 5:14. temple of God. In NT terms, the church. Paul foretells a great counterfeit gospel that will come to the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 6, 'what is restraining. This “restraining force” is a new element in the passage. The purpose of the restraint is to control the time when the “man of sin” (vv. 3–4) is revealed.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 7, 'mystery of lawlessness. Equivalent of “man of sin” in v. 3. The lawlessness of the man of sin was already at work in Paul’s day, but was being held in check. He who now restrains. Christ, the personal equivalent of the restraining force in v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 8, 'then. The time when the Restrainer stops restraining. lawless one. Same as “man of sin” in v. 3. revealed. See v. 3. coming. The Second Coming of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 9, 'power … lying wonders. See Matt. 24:24–27; Acts 2:22; Rev. 13:13–14. A satanic counterfeit of Jesus’ ministry on earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 10, 'receive … saved. The deception is received by those who have rejected the final proclamation of the gospel (Matt. 24:14; 1 Thess. 1:6; 2:13; Rev. 14:6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 11, 'God will send … delusion. God uses the end-time, satanic deception to publicly expose the unbelief of those who had already rejected the gospel in their hearts.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 2, 13, 'chose. See note on 1 Thess. 1:4. sanctification. A process of increasing, intentional conformity to the will and character of God by the power of the Holy Spirit. our gospel. See note on 1 Thess. 1:5. traditions. See note on 1 Thess. 4:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 1, 'pray. Prayer is a crucial element for Paul in all his dealings with the churches.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 2, 'unreasonable. Literally, “out of place.” men. The Greek word implies Paul has specific individuals in mind.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 3, 'the evil one. Probably a reference to Satan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 5, 'patience. Endurance, steadfastness. Can imply a focus on the end time (Rev. 13:10; 14:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 6, 'command. Elaborates on v. 4. withdraw. Stronger language than 1 Thess. 4:11–12; 5:14–15. The milder rebukes there evidently did not work. disorderly. The same word is translated “unruly” in 1 Thess. 5:14. tradition. See note on 1 Thess. 4:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 7, 'follow. Literally, “imitate.” See 1 Thess. 1:6–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 11, 'for we hear. Explains why Paul wrote vv. 6–10. disorderly. See note on v. 6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 12, 'quietness. Not interfering in the affairs of others. See notes on 1 Thess. 4:11–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Tessalonicenses', 3, 14, 'There are times when the church must limit relationships for the sake of the gospel. This is not a license for abuse; it is possible to be courteous without being best friends. obey … epistle. Paul claims absolute authority for his letter. brother. A soul for whom Christ died (Rom. 14:15; 1 Cor. 8:11).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'First Timothy was written by Paul to his younger associate in ministry, Timothy, to encourage him and give him instructions about how to organize the congregations in Ephesus and combat false teachers there. The letter gives us a front row seat to watch Paul apply the gospel to the real challenges confronted in a large city.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'The Pastoral Epistles (1 and 2 Timothy and Titus) are part of the wider group of thirteen letters in the New Testament that claim Paul as author (Romans; 1, 2 Corinthians; Galatians; Ephesians; Philippians; Colossians; 1, 2 Thessalonians; 1, 2 Timothy; Titus; Philemon). Of these, scholars most frequently question whether the three Pastoral Epistles were actually written by Paul, because they judge these letters to differ significantly from Paul’s major epistles in theology, vocabulary, style, and the view they give of church organization. However, when the complexities of ancient documents and contexts are considered, there seems to be no reason to deny these letters to Paul.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'Paul seems to have written 1 Timothy from Macedonia (1 Tim. 1:3) and Titus from Nicopolis in Greece (Titus 3:12). While there have been attempts to fit the Pastoral Epistles into the events mentioned in Acts and the other Pauline Epistles, 1 Timothy and Titus seem to be written later, between Paul’s first imprisonment in Rome (Acts 28) and his final imprisonment there (2 Tim. 1:8, 15–18; 4:9–18). These two letters were probably written about A.D. 65–66. Second Timothy, written during the final imprisonment near the end of Paul’s life, was probably composed later, around A.D. 66–67.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'A native of Lystra, Timothy had probably become a Christian during Paul’s visit there on his first missionary journey (Acts 14). Timothy’s mother was a Jew, but his father was a Greek (Acts 16:1). His mother and grandmother had passed on to him a rich heritage of faith and Bible study (2 Tim. 1:5; 3:14–15). After Paul’s split with Barnabas and John Mark, he recruited Timothy to join him and Silas on the second missionary journey (Acts 16:1–5) and Timothy quickly became a most trusted coworker and friend of Paul.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'Paul discusses a wide range of topics having to do with the health of the congregations in Ephesus, the large capital of the Roman province of Asia. He instructs Timothy on the topic of dealing with false teaching (1:3–11; 4:1–5), the conducting of public worship (2:1–15), and the selection and treatment of church leaders (3:1–13; 5:17–25). He instructs Timothy on how he should approach various groups within the churches (5:1–6:2), and includes a lengthy discussion about administering aid to widows (5:3–16).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'I.      Opening greeting (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 1, 'a true son in the faith. Paul writes to Timothy. Notice that God the Father and Jesus Christ are mentioned together in both verses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 3, 'Paul repeats a message given earlier to Timothy in Macedonia (the northern part of modern Greece where Paul had founded churches) concerning Timothy’s work in Ephesus (the capital of the Roman province of Asia, located in modern Asia Minor). Paul explores three themes: (1) the need to stop false teaching (vv. 3–11); (2) his gratitude for God’s mercy (vv. 12–17); and (3) what motivates his instructions (vv. 18–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 3, 'godly edification. To counter erroneous views, Paul contrasts true and false teaching. True teaching is godly training, motivated by faith and God-centered desires. fables and endless genealogies. False teaching, motivated by a desire for status, focuses on myths or fables (compare 2 Tim. 4:4) and only stirs up trouble (1 Tim. 1:3–7). teachers of the law. The false teachers wish to teach the law, indicating that use of the law of God seems a particular point of conflict (v. 7). Paul makes the clever point that the law must be used lawfully (v. 8), in line with its true purpose, to convict sinners (vv. 9–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 8, 'For a verse dealing with an unlawful use of the LAW, see Rom. 3:31. On its proper use, see John 14:5. For more on LAW, see Ex. 20:1–17; Matt. 5:17–19; Rom. 7:12, 14; Heb. 8:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 12, 'Paul models offering thanksgiving to God, affirming the truth of Rom. 5:8 (“while we were still sinners, Christ died for us”) in his own story. Earlier, Paul had referred to himself as “the least of the apostles” (1 Cor. 15:9) and, later, “the least of all the saints” (Eph. 3:8). Now, near the end of his life, he perceives himself to be the chief of sinners (1 Tim. 1:15), a prototype of God’s grace (“in me first,” v. 16). Paul offers this truth: No matter how awful our past, God forgives us and has a plan for our future. The wonder of God’s grace brings an outburst of praise (v. 17; compare Rom. 16:27; Eph. 3:21; Phil. 4:20), just as tracing God’s mercy in our own lives should do for us. On Paul’s earlier role as a persecutor of the church, compare Acts 8:3; 9:1–2; 22:4–5; 26:9–11; 1 Cor. 15:9; Gal. 1:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 17, 'immortal. God alone inherently possesses immortality (see 6:16). By extension this divine attribute also belongs to Christ and the Holy Spirit. At the resurrection God will bestow immortality upon the righteous (1 Cor. 15:53–54). For other attributes of GOD THE FATHER, see Ex. 34:6–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 18, 'See 4:14. On Hymenaeus, see 2 Tim. 2:16–19. Alexander may also be mentioned in 2 Tim. 4:14. Paul has, apparently, excluded these two from the church in hopes that they might repent.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 2, 1, 'Believers are to pray for all because God extends the offer of salvation to everyone (v. 4) and Jesus has provided “ransom for all” (v. 6; see John 3:16; Rev. 14:6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 2, 5, 'one Mediator. After His ascension to heaven, Jesus serves as our mediator, interceding on our behalf in the presence of God (Rom. 8:34). This important role of Jesus is amplified in the epistle to the Hebrews (see Introduction to Hebrews, “Message,” and esp. Heb. 4:14–16; 6:19–20; 7:23–25; 9:11–15, 24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 2, 8, 'The passage emphasizes how men (v. 8) and women (vv. 9–15; see 1 Cor. 14:33–36) should conduct themselves in public worship. We should consider all the Bible offers on the topic before reaching conclusions about the roles women should play in public worship today. Women filled key roles in the early Christian church as prophetesses (Philip’s daughters, Acts 21:9) and leaders/patronesses of house churches (Mary in Acts 12:12; Phoebe in Rom. 16:1–2; Priscilla, with Aquila, in 1 Cor. 16:19). Many of Paul’s coworkers were women (Rom. 16:1–16; Phil. 4:2–3) and he could declare that, in Christ, “there is neither male nor female” (Gal. 3:28). In 1 Cor. 11:2–16 Paul assumes that women will be praying and prophesying in Christian congregations and, while giving there the argument of creation order, he also modifies it significantly (see 1 Cor. 11:11–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 2, 9, 'modest apparel. Paul advocates modesty in dressing and replaces jewelry as adornment with good works. Believers are to give glory to God in the way they dress and adorn themselves not only in worship but in their daily lives. braided hair. Designates a hairstyle which included plaiting the hair with gold and other adornments. costly clothing. Refers to ostentatious apparel. Compare 1 Pet. 3:3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 2, 11, 'Let a woman learn in silence. In Paul’s churches women had the privilege to learn, which, in itself may have been progressive for his setting. As students they were expected to learn in silence, that is, quietly. Apparently they were to acknowledge the authority of the teacher, submit to it, and abstain from assuming the role of teacher. In interpreting passages such as this, the historical and cultural contexts, both then and now, must be taken into consideration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 3, 1, 'Compare Titus 1:5–9. bishop. Or, overseer; someone who helped to “oversee” the work of a local congregation (see Acts 20:17–18; 1 Pet. 5:1–4 where the term “elder” is used). Here, Paul is concerned with the personal qualities such leaders should demonstrate.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 3, 8, 'Paul describes the traits that should characterize deacons and their wives (though the Greek simply uses “the women,” so deaconesses may be in view).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 3, 14, 'Paul gives the purpose of his letter and describes the church as a family (the house of God) and, using architectural language, as the pillar and ground of the truth (see Eph. 2:19–22 and the note there). 1 Tim. 3:16 offers a wonderful affirmation and hymn about Christ. manifested in the flesh. Describes His Incarnation, His coming to earth in human form. Justified in the Spirit. Seems to describe Christ’s resurrection (see Rom. 1:3–4; 1 Pet. 3:18). Seen by angels. Points to Christ’s return to heaven immediately following His resurrection, where He received the praise of angels (see Phil. 2:9–11). Preached among the Gentiles, believed on in the world. Indicates the progress of the gospel message. The last phrase, received up in glory, provides a triumphant, final reminder that Jesus Christ is now exalted in glory.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 4, 1, 'Heretical teachers and teachings are a major concern of 1 Timothy (see 1:3–11; 6:3–10, 20–21). Paul warns of an apostasy in latter times that is inspired by evil powers (4:1). Believers will be justifiably concerned with healthful living and the counsel the Bible gives on it (1 Cor. 10:31). However, this apostasy is marked by overly stringent rules—abstaining from marriage and from foods God identifies as edible (1 Tim. 4:3; see Col. 2:20–23). Paul does not oppose the counsel God gives concerning what foods are appropriate to consume (Lev. 11; Deut. 14:3–21; compare Acts 10:14). We are free to eat what God created to be received with thanksgiving (1 Tim. 4:3) and that which is sanctified by the word of God and prayer (v. 5). We are to look to the counsel of God to guide our choices in healthful living.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 4, 6, 'In an already personal letter, Paul focuses direct attention on Timothy himself and especially the content and manner of his teaching. Timothy is to reject profane and old wives’ fables (v. 7), apparently a colorful description of the heresies circulating in Ephesus (1:3–11; 4:1–5; 6:3–10, 20–21). Instead, he is to train toward godliness (4:7–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 4, 8, 'The faithful saying applauded in v. 9 refers to the statement about godliness in v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 4, 12, 'Compare Titus 2:7–8, 15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 4, 13, 'The reading mentioned here is the public reading of Scripture. Paul sees the reading of Scripture, preaching (exhortation), and teaching (doctrine) as central in worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 4, 14, 'Compare 1:18–19; 2 Tim. 1:6. It is inspiring to think of an early Christian elders’ meeting praying over a young adult and envisioning God’s will in that young person’s life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 5, 1, 'Paul provides counsel concerning groups within the church including older and younger men and women (5:1–2), widows (5:3–16), elders (5:17–22), and Christian slaves (6:1–2), and he inserts a personal word about Timothy’s health (5:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 5, 1, 'Paul provides counsel concerning groups within the church including older and younger men and women (5:1–2), widows (5:3–16), elders (5:17–22), and Christian slaves (6:1–2), and he inserts a personal word about Timothy’s health (5:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 5, 3, 'Early Christians cared for widows in their midst, but administering this aid proved challenging (see Acts 6:1–7). Timothy is to encourage family members to care for relatives who are widows (1 Tim. 5:4, 8, 16) and to ensure that aid is given to women who are really widows (v. 5). Those who are to be placed on the list of widows approved for aid (taken into the number, v. 9) are to meet certain qualifications. Paul advises against including women less than 60 years of age and prefers that younger women remarry (vv. 9, 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 5, 17, 'Elders who rule well are to be treated well (vv. 17–18) and care should be taken that they not be falsely accused (v. 19), an issue of some importance (v. 21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 5, 18, 'Cites Deut. 25:4 and a saying of Jesus recorded in Luke 10:7. For supporting the gospel ministry as an expression of STEWARDSHIP, see 1 Cor. 9:8–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 5, 23, 'Probably in view of the poor drinking water, Paul advised Timothy to use a little wine. The term “wine” (Greek, oinos) could refer to either fermented or unfermented grape juice. Some argue that Paul suggested the medicinal use of fermented wine, while others hold that Paul advocated the use of unfermented grape juice in line with other biblical counsel (see Prov. 20:1; 23:29–35). To see here a defense of drinking alcohol would be to misapply Paul’s counsel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 5, 24, 'In selecting leaders (v. 22), one must be careful because sin is not always obvious.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 6, 1, 'See Eph. 6:5–9 and the note there.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 6, 3, 'Paul is again describing people who are teaching error in Ephesus (compare vv. 20–21; 1:3–11; 4:1–5, 7). That Paul intertwines this counsel with advice about avoiding the love of money (6:10) implies that the false teachers were greedy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 6, 12, 'Timothy has confessed the good confession of faith (v. 12), perhaps at his baptism, in a way similar to Jesus’ steadfast testimony offered before the Roman governor, Pilate (v. 13; compare John 18:28–19:16). In his urgent word of counsel, Paul discloses what should be the focus of every Christian’s life—to fight the good fight and lay hold on eternal life (1 Tim. 6:12) until the return of Christ, the timing of which is on God’s schedule, not our own (vv. 14–15). Pray that the Spirit of God may apply this timely word of counsel to your own experience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 6, 16, 'immortality. See notes on 1:17 and 1 Cor. 15:51–55. Nowhere in Scripture is immortality indicated as an attribute of humanity. Immortality belongs to the Godhead alone, who will grant it to the righteous only at the Second Coming of Christ. The unbiblical concept of an immortal human soul separate from the body came into Christian belief primarily from Greek philosophy. For other attributes of GOD THE FATHER, see Ex. 34:6–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 6, 20, 'Given Paul’s concern about false teachings in Ephesus, it is not surprising that he concludes with a final word of warning about them (compare 1:3–11; 4:1–5, 7; 6:3–19). Guard … your trust. In ancient times, to be given someone’s treasured possession for safe-keeping was a sacred trust. Paul urges Timothy to be just as careful with regard to the truth with which he has been entrusted.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Timóteo', 1, 1, 'Compare 1 Tim. 1:1–2. Paul emphasizes the blessings that come from God the');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'Paul is in difficult circumstances as he writes a final letter to his younger missionary associate, Timothy. See the Introduction to 1 Timothy for information about Timothy himself and the authorship and dates of the Pastoral Epistles.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'Paul is imprisoned in Rome (1:8, 17; 2:9), is in need of basic items like warm clothing (4:13, 21), and has endured alone a first hearing in the imperial court (4:16–18). While he hopes a second hearing will go better (4:18), there is a sense of finality about the current crisis. He writes that “the time of my departure is at hand” and looks toward the heavenly reward as final vindication (4:6–8).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 1, 1, 'Compare 1 Tim. 1:1–2. Paul emphasizes the blessings that come from God the Father and Christ Jesus and describes Timothy as a beloved son in faith and ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 1, 3, 'Paul’s prayers for Timothy can inspire us to pray for Christian missionaries and leaders. Timothy was from Lystra (in Lycaonia, then part of the Roman province of Galatia), and so his mother and grandmother were likely early converts of Paul there, though his father was probably not a Christian (Acts 16:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 1, 6, 'Timothy seems to be naturally timid. In this passage, Paul urges Timothy to join him in being willing to suffer for the gospel. Paul encourages boldness rooted in Timothy’s call to ministry (vv. 6–7), the truths of the gospel (vv. 8–11), and Paul’s own example (vv. 12–14). He concludes with details of his current suffering (vv. 15–18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 1, 8, 'the gospel. This summary of the gospel (compare Rom. 1:16–17; Eph. 2:8–10) is an inspiring reminder of God’s gift of eternal life (see John 3:16). That we are not saved through our own works but through God’s grace in Christ Jesus (v. 9) is central to Paul’s understanding (see Rom. 3:21–31; 4:2–5, 16; 9:16; 11:6; Gal. 2:16; Eph. 2:8–9; 2 Tim. 1:8–11). Use these verses as a focus to thank God and oppose legalism.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 1, 12, 'that Day. With Paul, the goal of the Christian is to be faithful until Christ’s Return. Our faith does not happen through our own strength, but through the power of God and His Spirit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 1, 15, 'Phygellus and Hermogenes. We know nothing more about these two.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 1, 16, 'Onesiphorus. See 4:19. In ancient times, prisoners were expected to provide their own food and clothing. Onesiphorus visited Paul and provided for him, giving a vivid example of Christ-like caring.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 2, 1, 'Paul illustrates the need to endure hardship (v. 3) by describing three occupations—soldier (vv. 3–4), athlete (v. 5), and farmer (v. 6). He offers his own example (vv. 8–10) and concludes with a faithful saying (vv. 11–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 2, 3, 'Like soldiers, believers need to be properly clothed and equipped and on the alert (see Rom. 13:11–14; 2 Cor. 10:3–6; Eph. 6:10–20; 1 Thess. 5:8). Paul uses two features of the soldier’s life to challenge Timothy: the need to endure hardship and the need to focus on being a soldier. Paul points to the athlete (compare 1 Cor. 9:24–27) and the farmer to emphasize the idea that suffering, discipline, and hard work in the present will yield a reward at the Return of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 2, 11, 'The idea that there is a reward for faithfulness at the end is supported by a faithful saying. Christians are able to make wise decisions about the present because we understand that history culminates in Christ’s Return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 2, 14, 'Paul warns against error (2:14–26) and godlessness in the last days (3:1–9), invites Timothy to follow his own example in persecution (3:10–17), and gives a solemn charge to preach the word! (4:1–8).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 2, 15, 'the word of truth. Compare John 17:17. For more on the importance of studying the BIBLE, see 3:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 2, 17, 'Hymenaeus and Philetus. Paul offers one case study of “idle babblings” (v. 16), that of Hymenaeus (see 1 Tim. 1:20) and Philetus who held that the resurrection is already past (compare 1 Cor. 15:12–19; 2 Thess. 2:2). For them, the resurrection seems to have been viewed as spiritual, not physical, and they use this idea to defend participation in sin (see v. 19). The solution to such error is “rightly dividing” (or properly handling) “the word of truth” (v. 15); seeking “righteousness, faith, love, peace” (v. 22); and displaying the Christian virtues of gentleness, patience, and humility in attempting to correct the erring (vv. 24–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 3, 1, 'In the last days, people will exhibit evil traits, including having only a form of godliness but denying its power (compare 4:2–4). Timothy already confronted people displaying such traits. He is to turn away from them (v. 5) since they prey on gullible women (v. 6) and resist truth. For other passages dealing with world conditions at the end of time before Christ’sSECOND COMING, see Matt. 24:21–25; 37–39; Luke 21:25–27; 1 Thess. 5:3; 2 Pet. 3:3–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 3, 8, 'Jannes and Jambres. According to Jewish tradition, these two were the Egyptian magicians who opposed Moses as he sought to free the children of Israel from slavery (see Ex. 7:11, 22; 8:7, 18; 9:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 3, 10, 'at Antioch, at Iconium, at Lystra. Timothy had been faithful to Christ—and to Paul—during difficult times of persecution in his hometown of Lystra and in Antioch and Iconium, all cities visited by Paul during his missionary journeys (Acts 13–14; 16:1–6; 18:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 3, 15, 'To resist the ungodly pattern of life in the last days, Timothy must study the Holy Scriptures (at that time the phrase meant the OT) as he has done since childhood to find the knowledge of salvation and instruction in righteousness. The Bible is a book of many purposes. Not only does it instruct people in the way of salvation, but it also guides their daily lives. The Bible offers Christians the resources needed for spiritual maturity and the difficult times ahead (v. 17). Believers must commit themselves to regular Bible study. On the need to study the BIBLE, see 2:15. On the proper attitude to study it, see Is. 66:2. For more on the BIBLE and its purpose, see Ps. 119:11, 105; Luke 24:44; John 17:17; 20:31; Rom. 15:4; Heb. 4:12; 1 Pet. 1:23; Rev. 1:1. On its inspiration, see 2 Pet. 1:20–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 1, 'His appearing. To all believers, Christ’s Return gives a sense of urgency and motivation for bold witness (see 1:12–14). On Christ’s role as Judge, see Matt. 25:31–46; John 5:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 2, 'Preach the word! There is a special need for Timothy to do so since the spiritual rebellion Paul described earlier (3:1–5) will only deepen (4:3–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 4, 'fables. Compare 1 Tim. 1:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 6, 'a drink offering. Drink offerings are described in the OT (e.g., Lev. 23:13, 18, 37) and were a common feature of ancient worship in which wine, oil, or water was poured out as an offering to a god. Compare Phil. 2:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 8, 'The crown of righteousness will be given to Paul on the day of Christ’s appearing or Return. See 1 Cor. 9:24–27; 1 Thess. 2:19. on that Day. Paul is clear about when he expects to receive his reward.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 9, 'Paul gives final instructions (vv. 9–18), offers an exchange of greetings (vv. 19–21), and concludes with a blessing (v. 22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 9, 'Paul is lonely, though he has the companionship of Luke, the “beloved physician” (Col. 4:14). Demas, earlier one of his companions (Col. 4:14; Philem. 24), deserted him while Crescens and Titus left for presumably more positive reasons, as had Tychicus (see Eph. 6:21; Col. 4:7). The request for Mark’s presence (v. 11) represents a change of heart for Paul who had held his earlier desertion against him (Acts 13:13; 15:36–41).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 14, 'Alexander the coppersmith. He may be mentioned in 1 Tim. 1:19–20 and may have been a witness against Paul at his trial (vv. 16–17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 16, 'my first defense. Acts ends with Paul under house arrest in Rome, awaiting trial. Judging from the Pastoral Epistles, Paul must have been released after that first trial but was later arrested again. Here he refers to an initial hearing conducted after this second arrest. Paul rejoices that his ultimate destiny is determined by the Lord.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Timóteo', 4, 21, 'Paul pleads for Timothy to come before winter (compare v. 9). He needs the supplies he has requested (v. 13), but even more, he needs Timothy’s companionship.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 0, NULL, 'Paul wrote this Pastoral Epistle late in life to Titus, a beloved coworker, at about the same time that he wrote his first letter to Timothy (see Introduction to 1 Timothy, “');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 0, NULL, 'It appears that Paul wrote Titus from Nicopolis (3:12), probably the city on the western coast of Greece. Since Paul was free to travel at the time he wrote, the letter seems to be written between his first imprisonment in Rome (Acts 28) and his final imprisonment there (2 Tim. 1:8, 15–18; 4:9–18), in about A.D. 65.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 0, NULL, 'Paul gives counsel about selecting elders (1:5–9), dealing with false doctrine and divisive people (1:10–16; 2:1; 3:9–11), and teaching various groups (2:1–15). He wishes Cretans to think well about Christians, presumably so they might themselves accept the gospel (2:3–9; 3:1–8). The final words (“Grace be with you all”; 3:15) suggest that the letter was intended to be read aloud in the congregations, to encourage them to support Titus in his tasks.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 0, NULL, 'I.      Opening greeting (1:1–4)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 1, 1, 'bondservant … apostle. In addition to the usual label of apostle, Paul calls himself a bondservant or slave of God as a way of emphasizing his total commitment to obey God’s call. The greeting is longer than usual (compare 1 Tim. 1:1–2; 2 Tim. 1:1–2) in that Paul sketches out the history of the gospel hope of eternal life. It was promised before time began and eventually came to Paul who was commissioned by God to proclaim it. The mention that God cannot lie contrasts with Paul’s later description of the people of Crete as liars (1:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 1, 5, 'appoint elders. This is one of the principal tasks Titus is to accomplish in the Christian congregations on the island of Crete (notice that the terms “elder” and “bishop” are used interchangeably in this passage). Paul wishes Titus to do this in every city on the island, suggesting that the Christian church had grown and congregations had been established in a number of locations. The list of qualifications for the elder or bishop is similar to that in 1 Tim. 3:1–7, though here there is a greater emphasis on the bishop’s commitment to sound doctrine and correcting those who undermine it (v. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 1, 10, 'idle talkers and deceivers. Paul’s description of deceptive teachers contains quite a lot of detail, but seems, without saying so, to be addressing more than just the issue of circumcision. It is clear that whatever the deceivers have been teaching, they have been successful at convincing whole households, and Paul insists that they must be stopped (v. 11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 1, 12, 'a prophet of their own. Paul quotes Epimenides, a Cretan poet who lived some 600 years before Paul’s time, and labels him a “prophet of their own.” The quote presents a logical riddle because if it is true—as Paul says it is—it is false. If Cretans are always liars, then this Cretan poet cannot be speaking the truth! So perhaps Paul shares the quote with a touch of humor, though he is quite serious about applying the full quotation to the heretical teachers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 1, 15, 'defiled. On the corruption of the heart caused by SIN, compare Jer. 17:9; Mark 7:21; James 4:1–2; and note on Matt. 12:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 2, 1, 'In contrast to the teachers of heresy Paul has been discussing, Titus is to teach sound doctrine. Paul sketches out the various groups within the Christian congregations on Crete that Titus should train: older men (v. 2), older women (including the young women they minister to, vv. 3–5), young men (vv. 6–8), and bondservants or slaves (vv. 9–10; see notes on Eph. 6:5–9). Paul demonstrates a repeated concern for the reputation Christians and Christianity have with outsiders (vv. 5, 8; compare 1 Tim. 6:1). This is no simple concern with public image. Paul wishes the gospel message to have access to every heart and hopes to avoid unnecessary barriers to it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 2, 11, 'the grace of God … has appeared. Paul next describes how the two comings (or appearings) of Jesus—His first one in human form (vv. 11–12) and His Second Coming in glory (vv. 13–14)—should impact the behavior of believers. Looking back to Christ’s first coming (the Incarnation), we praise God for the salvation He provided in Christ. The knowledge of that salvation is not to be restricted to a few, but has appeared to all men (compare 1 Tim. 2:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 2, 13, 'the blessed hope and glorious appearing. Christians live in expectation, looking ahead to the SECOND COMING, the blessed hope, and this motivates them to live for God in the present (see note on vv. 11–12). The idea that we should orient our lives toward the Return of Christ is a common theme in the Pastoral Epistles (1 Tim. 6:13–16; 2 Tim. 1:11–12; 2:1–13; 4:1–8). True believers should live each day in the light of the blessed hope of Christ’s Return. For Christ’s fullest discussion of His SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24–25. For insights on why it is called “blessed hope,” see 1 Thess. 4:13–18. For further counsel on how to live before that event, see Matt. 25; 2 Pet. 3:11–14; Rev. 12:7; 14:12. our great God and Savior Jesus Christ. In Greek, one definite article (“the”) governs the nouns “God” and “Savior,” indicating that a single person—rather than two separate persons—is being discussed. Understood in this way, Paul affirms strongly the divinity of Jesus. For more on the person and divinity of Jesus, see Matt. 1:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 3, 1, 'Paul’s desire to guard the reputation of the gospel continues here (compare 2:5, 8; 1 Tim. 6:1). With this in view, believers should obey rulers and authorities and be peaceable people (compare 1 Tim. 2:1–2; Rom. 13:1–7; but see Acts 5:27–29). Since God seeks the salvation of all (2:11; 1 Tim. 2:3–4), it is appropriate for believers to show all humility to all men (v. 2), remembering that we ourselves were once lost just as they are still (v. 3; compare Eph. 2:1–3). Inspired by the kindness and the love of God our Savior (v. 4), we believers should no longer be hateful and hating (v. 3), but gracious, gentle, and forgiving toward others. Paul is clear that works do not save us (vv. 5–6), but is equally clear that Christians should be careful to maintain good works (v. 8, compare v. 14) as a witness to the world. Our behavior toward others should reflect God’s graciousness toward us.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 3, 5, 'On being saved by God’s mercy rather than works, see notes on 2 Tim. 1:8–11. The Holy Spirit is the active agent in our salvation and is poured out on us abundantly, a phrase that recalls Jesus’ comment in Luke 11:13 about the Father’s willingness to give the Holy Spirit to those who ask.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 3, 9, 'We are to avoid foolish disputes and contentions (see 1 Tim. 6:4; 2 Tim. 2:14, 23), controversy over genealogies (see 1 Tim. 1:4), and strivings about the law (see 1 Tim. 1:6–11). After two warnings, a person who remains divisive is to be rejected (compare Rom. 16:17–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tito', 3, 12, 'Paul mentions four people in these closing words. These are the only mentions of Artemas and Zenas the lawyer. Tychicus was a devoted coworker of Paul’s who traveled with him (Acts 20:4) or on his behalf (Eph. 6:21; Col. 4:7; 2 Tim. 4:12). Apollos was a native of Alexandria who became a well-educated and eloquent Christian spokesperson (Acts 18:24–28). Though the teachings of Apollos were sometimes twisted and used against Paul by others, Apollos himself sometimes worked in collaboration with him (Acts 19:1; 1 Cor. 1:11–12; 3:1–9; 16:12). That Paul is in Nicopolis (probably the city by that name on the west coast of Greece) suggests that he is free to travel (see Introduction, “Date and Place of Writing”). On the need to maintain good works, see notes on vv. 1–8.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filemom', 0, NULL, 'Philemon is the shortest and the most personal and endearing letter in the section of the New Testament that contains the writings of the Apostle Paul. Philemon is a letter that focuses on acceptance, reconciliation, and forgiveness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filemom', 0, NULL, 'The position that Paul is the author of this correspondence and that he wrote it while he was in prison in Rome is almost universally accepted. Philemon, along with Ephesians, Philippians, and Colossians, are classified as “Prison Epistles.” The letter was likely composed sometime between A.D. 59 and 63.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filemom', 0, NULL, 'The letter is primarily addressed to Philemon, a believer who possibly lived in Colosse (Col. 4:9). But it is also addressed to Apphia (whom some believe to be Philemon’s wife) and Archippus (possibly the son of Philemon and Apphia). But verse 2 makes it clear that it was more than a personal letter to Philemon; it is also addressed to the entire congregation that met for church at his house. Besides, the use of the second-person plural in verses 3, 22, and 25 demonstrates that it was not meant to be private, but to be read to all church members.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filemom', 0, NULL, 'Paul calls for reconciliation between the estranged Philemon and Onesimus. There is very wide, though not unanimous, agreement among interpreters that Onesimus was a fugitive slave belonging to Philemon, and that Paul was trying to arrange some kind of reconciliation between the two men. A less common, alternate view is that Onesimus was not a literal slave, but merely an estranged Christian brother. In this view, verse 16 should not be interpreted as evidence of actual slave status, but rather as the text literally says “as [or like]” a slave. Slavery is then metaphorical for treating someone else poorly. Paul does the same thing in the very next verse (v. 17) when he tells Philemon to receive Onesimus “as” he would Paul. Debates over the meaning of Paul’s letter figured prominently in the great movements to eliminate the institution of slavery in Great Britain and the United States in the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Filemom', 0, NULL, 'I.      Salutations and thanksgiving (vv. 1–7)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 0, NULL, 'Scripture does not say who the author of Hebrews was. Tradition attributes it to Paul (see also 13:23 and note), but the issue has been debated since early times.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 0, NULL, 'Hebrews also does not say to whom it was written. It does explain that the readers had accepted the gospel about Jesus in the past (2:3–4) and had shown strong commitment to Him in the face of persecution (10:32–34), but now the contempt and hostility of the surrounding society (12:2–4; 13:3, 13, 18–19) had subtly undermined their faith (2:1–2; 5:11–14; 6:12; 10:25). The author feels the need to warn them against the danger of total apostasy (6:4–8; 10:26–31; 12:15–17).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 0, NULL, 'The author’s reference to Jesus’ death (A.D. 31; Heb. 12:2), and the use of Hebrews by an early Christian document traditionally dated around A.D. 96, establishes a broad frame of time for its origin.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 0, NULL, 'Hebrews both encourages readers to hold fast to their faith in Jesus and warns them about drifting away from Him (see “Audience” above). The author reflects this double purpose—encouraging and warning—by two different types of writing: (1) the teaching, or doctrinal, sections explain God’s word and theology and describe the great salvation God has brought about in Jesus; (2) the exhortation sections call for response and action to that truth. Exhortation means to “urge earnestly” and “advise strongly,” and these sections argue repeatedly that if disobedience brought about punishment under the first covenant, “how much more” will that be the case under the new covenant. Hebrews describes itself as a “word of exhortation” (13:22).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction: God has spoken to us in the Son (1:1–4)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 1, 'These verses explain that Jesus is the climax of God’s revelation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 1, 'at various times and in various ways. The messages of the prophets of the OT were like pieces of a puzzle—all fragmentary and diverse—whose assemblage into the full description of God was only possible when the Son came (v. 3). For more on Hebrew Scriptures, see Luke 24:44. For more on the source and inspiration of the BIBLE, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17; 2 Pet. 1:20–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 2, 'Son. The prophets revealed that God would act decisively to save His people through a “Son”/seed (Gen. 3:15; 22:15–18; 2 Sam. 7:12–16; Is. 7:14; 9:6–7). The NT claims that God fulfilled those promises in Jesus (Matt. 1:20–23; Luke 1:31–35; Gal. 3:13–16), inaugurating the “latter days” when He would act for salvation (Dan. 2:28). made the worlds. For the role of Christ in CREATION, see John 1:3; Eph. 3:9; Col. 1:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 3, 'brightness of His glory. Just as no one has seen the sun except through its “brightness,” it is only through Jesus that we have known the Father (John 1:18). express image of His person. The original refers to an exact representation—like the impress a seal leaves on an object—of the essence or very being of a person. Jesus is an exact representation of who God is because He is God (1:3–4, 8, 10–12). For more on the WORK OF CHRIST in revealing the Father, see John 14:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 5, 'Describes the enthronement of Jesus over the angels—who were considered powerful beings that controlled the world (Eph. 6:12; Col. 2:15)—and its implications for the salvation of human beings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 5, 'Today I have begotten you. “Son of God” is also a royal title promised to David’s son (2 Sam. 7:14; Ps. 2:7). Jesus assumed this title through the resurrection from the dead (Acts 13:33–34; Rom. 1:4) when He was seated on the eternal throne promised to David’s Son (Luke 1:30–33). In this royal sense of the title, Jesus was “begotten” at His enthronement.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 6, 'firstborn. Not referring to literal birth but a royal Davidic title applied to Jesus that denotes His primacy over the universe (Ps. 89:27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 1, 8, 'God. On the divinity of the PERSON OF CHRIST, see Is. 9:6; Matt. 1:23; John 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 2, 1, 'The author interrupts his message to give his first warning against neglecting the message given through the Son.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 2, 1, 'lest we drift away. The real danger was not in brash acts of apostasy but in a subtle “drifting away” from the word of God, in becoming “dull of hearing” and refusing to mature (5:11–6:3), and in the sometimes imperceptible hardening of the heart (3:8). Later, the author notes that even those who had good teachers or a genuine spiritual experience are in danger (6:4–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 2, 7, 'set him over the works of Your hands. Argues from Ps. 8:4–6 that God’s original purpose was to crown humans “with glory and honor” as rulers over creation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 2, 8, 'we do not yet see. Admits that we have not yet seen the fulfillment of this plan (see v. 7). In fact, the readers have experienced the opposite: persecution and shame (see 10:32–34; 12:2–3; 13:3, 13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 2, 9, 'But we see Jesus. God’s plan, however, has been fulfilled in Jesus (see v. 7). He is the “man/son of man” who was “crowned with glory and honor.” In becoming human, Jesus was made “a little lower than the angels” so that He “might taste death for everyone” (vv. 6–9) and lead us to “glory” (v. 10). Thus, Jesus became both our representative/substitute and our example. He died “for everyone” (v. 9) and lived a perfect life (4:15; 7:26). He endured suffering and shame so that we might “consider” Him and follow Him through suffering into glory (3:1; 12:1–4). Note, also, that Jesus was (1:1–4) and is greater than the angels as He has been exalted above them (1:5–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 2, 10, 'perfect through sufferings. His death on the cross enabled Jesus to become the Savior of His “brethren.” In this sense, His “sufferings” perfected or qualified Jesus to become our Savior.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 2, 17, 'in all things. Jesus is fully human. He experienced suffering and death (vv. 14–16). He is not a sinner, however. The author also says that Jesus is “separate from sinners” because He did not sin (7:26–28; also 4:15); otherwise, He could not have been a perfect sacrifice in our behalf (9:14; 10:5–10). that He might be a merciful and faithful High Priest. Sufferings did not make Jesus merciful but evidenced His mercy toward us.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 1, 'An exhortation to remain faithful to Jesus so that the readers may receive what God has promised.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 1, 'An exhortation to remain faithful to Jesus so that the readers may receive what God has promised.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 1, 'Jesus as an example of faithfulness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 2, 'as Moses. God testified about His “servant” Moses: “He is faithful in all My house” (Num. 12:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 3, 'worthy of more glory. Jesus is superior to Moses because He is faithful as a “Son over His own house” (v. 6). This refers to the promise of 1 Sam. 2:35 that God would “raise up for Myself a faithful priest” and “build him a sure house [priestly line].” Believers are that “house” God promised Jesus, the “faithful High Priest” (Heb. 2:17–18), if they remain faithful to the end (see 10:19–25; 13:10–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 7, 'Israel’s desert generation as an example of apostasy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 7, 'The author interprets Ps. 95:7–11 in the light of the rebellion at Kadesh (Num. 13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 7, 'if you will hear His voice. God had promised Canaan to Israel as their homeland (Ex. 6:1–8); but Israel did not believe God and refused to enter (Num. 14:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 8, 'Do not harden your hearts. Israel had seen God’s miracles in their favor; however, they attributed to God the evil intention of planning to kill them in the desert (Num. 14:3). Rather than believing in God, they believed the word of the spies who had toured the land only 40 days. Israel rejected Moses and decided to choose another leader to return to Egypt (Num. 14:4). Finally, when Joshua and Caleb eagerly exhorted the people to believe in God, the people talked of stoning them (Num. 14:6–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 11, 'They shall not enter My rest. God’s oath only confirmed the wish of the people to die in the wilderness (Num. 14:2). The repentance of the people was not genuine; a second time they were disobedient to God’s command and rejected His appointed leaders (Num. 14:40–45).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 12, 'Beware. The first antidote against apostasy is an analysis of our own heart (see 12:15). unbelief. Does not refer here to lack of faith, but to an act of rejection; that is, a rejection of God’s promises and power in our favor, Jesus’ leadership, and the Spirit’s intercession. It is the same sin of 6:4–8; 10:26–31; 12:14–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 3, 13, 'exhort one another. The second antidote (see v. 12) is a continuous encouragement of one another (see 10:25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 1, 'An exhortation to faithfulness and accepting God’s offer of salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 1, 'a promise remains. The repetition of the promise in Ps. 95 (Heb. 4:6–7) means that it continues to be valid. His rest. Denotes the promise of the “gospel” which is salvation (v. 2; 1:14; 2:3–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 3, 'we who have believed. Those who believe begin to enjoy the “rest” of God because they receive the “gifts of the Holy Spirit” (2:4) which are themselves the first installment of salvation/redemption (2 Cor. 1:22; Eph. 1:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 4, 'God rested on the seventh day. God’s rest was established at the SABBATH of creation and it has been available to believers since then. See Gen. 2:1–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 9, 'a rest. Literally, a “Sabbath rest.” The SABBATH rest symbolizes and celebrates God’s goodness and salvation (Ex. 20:11; Deut. 5:15). As we observe it, Sabbath rest expresses our faith in two ways. First, it testifies that we have received God’s goodness and salvation. Second, it demonstrates that we are waiting for the consummation of those gifts at the Second Coming. For more on the SABBATH rest, see Ex. 20:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 12, 'living and powerful. The word of God enables those who hear it to do what it commands (11:3; 1 Pet. 1:23). sharper than any two-edged sword. The word of God—especially our response to it—reveals what is in the deepest parts of our heart. For more on the purposes of the BIBLE, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 13, 'no creature hidden. For other attributes of GOD THE FATHER, see Ex. 34:6–7; Ps. 147:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 14, 'For aspects of Christ’s priestly ministry, see 7:25 and note on 8:1–2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 14, 'On Christ’s intercessory ministry in the heavenly SANCTUARY, see 7:25; 8:1–5; 1 John 2:1. For more on the humanity of the PERSON OF CHRIST, see notes on Matt. 1:23; Heb. 2:17; Phil. 2:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 14, 'a great High Priest. Jesus is a great leader for those who want to enter into God’s rest. He has two great qualifications: He has already entered the heavenly sanctuary (9:24), which is the resting place of God (2 Chr. 6:41; Ps. 132:7–14; Is. 66:1), and He was “tempted as we are” but did not sin (Heb. 4:15; 7:26–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 4, 16, 'Invitation for believers to “come boldly to the throne of grace” in the heavenly sanctuary. Ps. 95, which is the basis for the argument of Heb. 3–4, is an invitation to enter God’s rest. Believers can boldly enter God’s presence and relate personally to Him because of Jesus. Christian ASSURANCE is based upon both the reality of what they already possess in Christ (John 3:36; Eph. 2:8) and the internal witness of the Spirit (Rom. 8:16; 1 John 5:10–13). See also Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 1, 'The characteristics of a high priest are defined.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 5, 'Jesus meets the requirements to be a high priest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 5, 'You are My Son. This declaration establishes Jesus as the heir of the throne of David (see 1:5 and note; 2 Sam. 7:14). As such, He receives the office of priest “according to the order of Melchizedek” (Ps. 110:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 7, 'and was heard. Even though Jesus died despite His prayer, God resurrected Him (13:20) and exalted Him at His right hand (1:3–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 8, 'though He was a Son. Jesus’ eternal exalted status did not include the concept of “obedience” as we understand it, but He “learned obedience” in the sense that He experienced it when He came into the world to do God’s will (10:5–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 9, 'having been perfected. Jesus’ death on the cross “perfected” Him to be our High Priest in the sense that it fully equipped Him for this task.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 11, 'The author inserts an exhortation and warning to hold fast to the hope (6:19) that has been offered us in the appointment of Jesus as High Priest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 11, 'The author admonishes the readers for their lack of maturity and knowledge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 5, 14, 'solid food. Advanced knowledge of Scriptures. full age. The original word also means perfection or maturity (6:1). have their senses exercised. Maturity involves the proper application of the knowledge of Scriptures. A mature Christian is one whose life evidences a developed ability to distinguish good from evil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 1, 'perfection. The word also means maturity, full age (5:14). elementary principles of Christ. The six basic teachings listed in 6:1–2, “repentance … eternal judgment,” span the whole process of the Christian life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 4, 'The author warns that refusal to advance into spiritual maturity (5:11–6:3) may lead to total apostasy from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 4, 'it is impossible … to renew them again. It is impossible that God would restore them just as “it is impossible for God to lie” (v. 18), and it is impossible to please God “without faith” (11:6). once enlightened … age to come. The five blessings listed here describe a sound knowledge of God’s word as well as a genuine experience of salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 6, 'they crucify again. This is the reason for the impossibility of restoration (v. 4). Christian life requires the crucifixion of self (Mark 8:34; Gal. 2:20). But they crucify Christ; that is, they put to death their relationship with Him. This is not the result of ignorance or lack of experience (see Heb. 6:4). God will not restore them because what God has to offer (vv. 4–5) is what they have already rejected. By denying them restoration, God honors their decision to crucify Christ to themselves (see also 10:26–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 7, 'the earth which drinks in the rain. The illustration reinforces the logic of the warning (vv. 4–6): the ground did not lack any divine gift but rejected it by producing thorns and briers. The only thing left for God to do is judge it (see Is. 5:1–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 9, 'An exhortation to faith and hope.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 13, 'Abraham is presented as a model of faith and patience (11:8–19). God confirmed His promise to Abraham with an oath (Gen. 22:15–18), which He fulfilled by multiplying exceedingly Abraham’s descendants (Ex. 1:7; Rom. 4:16; Gal. 3:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 17, 'an oath. Similarly, God has guaranteed to the believers His solemn promise by an oath: “The LORD has sworn and will not relent, ‘You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek’ ” (Ps. 110:4; Heb. 5:6; 6:20). The appointment of Jesus as our High Priest (5:1–10) and His full access to the presence of God (6:19) were given as a guarantee to us (7:21–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 18, 'it is impossible for God to lie. The judgment of the desert generation (3:7–4:11) and the multiplication of the posterity of Abraham (6:13–15) show this to be true.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 6, 19, 'behind the veil. Whether the allusion is to the veil at the entrance of the Israelite sanctuary (see 9:3) or to the veil separating the Holy Place and Most Holy Place, the point is that there is no longer any barrier into the heavenly sanctuary because Christ has made a way through it (10:20) into the very presence of God (9:24) so that we may “come boldly to the throne of grace” (4:16). Generally in Hebrews, reference is to the entire Israelite sanctuary where God manifested His presence (see also Ex. 40:34; Num. 20:6). On the issue of Christian ASSURANCE, see also Heb. 4:16; Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 1, 'The author resumes his exposition on the priesthood of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 2, 'king of righteousness. The author uses the meaning of Melchizedek’s name to illustrate the sinless priesthood of Jesus (vv. 26–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 3, 'without father … nor end of life. The fact that the OT does not mention Melchizedek’s genealogy and death makes him an illustration of who Jesus was going to be: an eternally pre-existent and enduring High Priest. The author does not argue here that Melchizedek was a divine being. The account of Gen. 14 makes the figure of Melchizedek to resemble the future Son of God, just like the OT sacrifices resemble the future sacrifice of Jesus (Heb. 10:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 4, 'the patriarch Abraham. Abraham, the ancestor of Aaron and all the Levitical priests, paid the tithe to Melchizedek, which evidences the superiority of the priesthood of Jesus over that of the Levitical priests. Abraham represents as well the believers and the relationship that they should have to Christ (Rom. 4:16; Gal. 3:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 12, 'a change of the law. Law refers in this section to the law of the priesthood (Num. 18) prescribing that only Aaron and his sons could serve as priests. The author argues that the arrival of Jesus’ priesthood signaled a change in this law. This change in the law prepares the reader for the transition to the covenant brought about by Christ (see Heb. 8:7–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 16, 'fleshly commandment. It was “fleshly” because it appointed mortal and sinful human beings (vv. 23–28). Scripture associates these two characteristics with the concept of “flesh” (2 Cor. 10:3; Gal. 5:13–25). the power of an endless life. Jesus’ priesthood is based on the fact that God resurrected Him from the dead (Heb. 2:14–16; 13:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 19, 'the law made nothing perfect. The law of priesthood (see v. 12). The intercession of the Levitical priests through animal sacrifices could not “perfect” worshipers regarding their conscience (9:9; see note on 10:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 20, 'without an oath. See 6:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 22, 'surety of a better covenant. The appointment of Jesus as High Priest guarantees that what God promises to us will be fulfilled. These promises include at least two things: cleansing our conscience from sin (9:14) and confident access to the presence of God (10:19–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 25, 'make intercession for them. Often overlooked is the important biblical theme of what Jesus is doing for believers now—interceding for them as High Priest in the heavenly SANCTUARY. See Introduction, “Message”; 8:1–5; Rom. 8:34; 1 Tim. 2:5; 1 John 2:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 7, 26, 'holy, harmless, undefiled, separate from sinners. This enables Jesus to act as our intercessor. Sinful human beings cannot be appropriate intercessors before a holy God (Is. 59:1–2). Most world religions have priests that mediate between the worshiper and God. The requirements to be a priest vary among different religions (e.g., belonging to the Brahman caste in Hinduism, asceticism in various Buddhist groups, etc.) but generally do not include moral qualifications since they are expected to be basically experts in religious ritual. Hebrews, however, emphasizes the need of a sinless priest.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 1, 'An exposition of the institution of the new covenant as the benefit of the priesthood of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 1, 'An exposition of the institution of the new covenant as the benefit of the priesthood of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 1, 'The Bible’s teaching on the sanctuary is an all-pervasive theme, running from the sacrificial symbolism of Gen. 3–4 to the last chapters of Revelation. In Moses’ time the sacrificial service was focused on the earthly sanctuary of Ex. 25, which was patterned after the model of the heavenly sanctuary (Ex. 25:8–9, 40). Solomon’s temple and the one in the time of Christ were built upon the same general model. But it is the book of Hebrews that helps us understand the full significance of the earthly sanctuary (Heb. 8:1–5), since it deals with the heavenly original as better than the earthly in every way (v. 6; 9:1–28). This includes a better sacrifice that did not have to be repeatedly offered, since Christ died once for all (9:22–24, 26; 10:10), and a better priest (Christ), whose ministry could actually take away sin (4:14–5:10; 10:11–14). That ministry was based upon His sacrificial death on Calvary (John 1:29; 2 Cor. 5:17; Gal. 3:10–13; 1 John 2:2; 4:10) and His priesthood in the heavenly sanctuary, which had two aspects: intercession for sin on a continual basis (Heb. 7:25; Dan. 8:11–12; Rom. 8:34; 1 John 1:9; 2:1) and judgment at the end of the Jewish year, symbolizing the end of human history (Lev. 16; Dan. 7:5–27; 8:13–14). It is significant that Rev. 11:19 moves the sanctuary symbolism from the Holy Place to the Most Holy Place at the end of time. It should be noted that Hebrews describes the earthly sanctuary service as a shadow (Heb. 10:1) or parable/symbol (9:9) of the real thing in heaven. Thus, while it allows humans to understand the general shape of the plan of salvation, it does not provide a complete understanding of all that happens in heaven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 1, 'High Priest. Compare 9:11. See 9:26 for Christ as sacrificial victim. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 2, 'true tabernacle. The author argues that the heavenly sanctuary is the reality of which the earthly tabernacle was only a “copy and shadow” (v. 5). Temples are very important for most world religions (e.g., Hinduism) as a residence—or representation—of the divine. In Hebrews, there is no temple on earth for believers (see v. 5; 13:10–16). Jesus ministers in the heavenly temple and believers have access to Him through faith (10:19–23; see Rev. 11:19). On Christ’s intercessory ministry in the heavenly SANCTUARY, see Heb. 4:14–16; 7:25; 1 John 2:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 5, 'copy and shadow. Reference to the Mosaic sanctuary which, like other OT institutions, served as an imperfect and temporary example of what was going to come in the future through Jesus (see 10:1; Col. 2:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 6, 'established on better promises. The promises of the new covenant mentioned in vv. 8–13 (quoting Jer. 31:31–34) were all included in the first covenant: “I will put My laws in their mind” (Deut. 30:6; Ps. 40:8; Is. 51:7; see Ex. 31:12–13; Lev. 20:7–8), “I will be their God” (Lev. 26:12), “shall all know Me” (that is, a covenant that implied salvation to all nations; Ex. 19:5–6; Ps. 67:1–2), “I will be merciful to their unrighteousness” (Ex. 34:1–7; see Lev. 4–5). Israel, however, forgot or neglected those promises (see Heb. 8:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 8, 'finding fault with them. The problem with the first covenant was that Israel did not appropriate the promises but understood the covenant as consisting in rituals and institutions. They forgot that it was God Himself who provided forgiveness and power to fulfill the covenant. They did not seek righteousness through faith in God but through the works of the law (Rom. 9:31–32). Thus, the first covenant was “faulty” because one of the covenant partners (the people) was found at fault in how they related to it (Heb. 8:7, 9; 9:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 10, 'on their hearts. The new covenant experience does not exist apart from God’s law but is intimately connected to it (compare Matt. 22:36–40; Rom. 13:8–10; 2 Cor. 3:3, 6). For more on the new COVENANT, see Jer. 31:31–34; 1 Cor. 11:25; Heb. 9:15, 20–22; 10:11–18. For more on the Christian’s relationship to God’s LAW, see Matt. 5:17–19; John 14:15; Eph. 2:8–10; James 2:10, 12; 1 John 2:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 8, 13, 'obsolete … ready to vanish away. The first covenant, understood as consisting of rituals that cannot perfect the conscience (9:9–10), became ineffective and obsolete with the arrival of Jesus. He has brought a superior covenant whose institutions (priesthood, sanctuary, and sacrifices) are not earthly/fleshly but heavenly (Heb. 7:16; 8:1–2; 9:9–10; 10:1–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 4, 'the golden censer. Also commonly understood here as the golden altar of incense that was in the Holy Place (Ex. 30:6; see also 1 Kin. 6:22). The author may be associating the function of the altar of incense with the Most Holy Place, because daily on this altar was burning special incense intended for the Most Holy Place where God manifested Himself (Ex. 30:7–10, 34–38; 40:5; compare Lev. 16:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 8, 'Holiest of All. More precisely rendered “sanctuary”; see v. 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 9, 'It was symbolic for the present time. The Greek term translated “symbolic” means literally “parable.” The author argues that the first sanctuary and its rituals (vv. 2–7) were a “parable” that illustrated the impermanent nature of the old covenant (8:13; 9:9–10). The parable uses the words “first” and “second” to picture the transition from the daily to the yearly rituals that occurred on the Day of Atonement (vv. 6–7; see Lev. 16). This yearly ritual prescribed that no one could be in the first room while the high priest ministered in the second room (Lev. 16:17). According to Heb. 9:8, this meant that as long as the first tabernacle (the earthly tabernacle of the first covenant; see v. 1) continued to have ritual standing there could not be access to the second tabernacle (the heavenly sanctuary of the new covenant; see 8:1–6). The antitheses that the author identified in 9:6–7 are also part of this parable.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 11, 'more perfect tabernacle. The heavenly sanctuary.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 12, 'Most Holy Place. More precisely rendered “the holy” or “the holies,” referring to the whole sanctuary and not a portion of it (see v. 8). For Jesus’ ministry in heaven, see note on vv. 24–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 13, 'On the role of Christ’s sacrificial blood in the plan of SALVATION, see note on Rom. 3:21–26. See also Eph. 1:7; Col. 1:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 15, 'the death of the testator. The Greek term translated “testator” also means “covenant maker.” Ancient covenant making invariably included the swearing of an oath of self-curse for the transgressor. This was often represented through the shedding of blood (vv. 18–22; Gen. 15:9–21; Ex. 24:1–11; Jer. 34:18–20). Based on this, Heb. 9:16–17 argues that a valid covenant requires the death of the covenant maker who transgressed the covenant. Therefore, Jesus’ death on behalf of the transgressors both attested to the validity of the old covenant (v. 17) and redeemed them from death (vv. 15, 22). Jesus’ death also inaugurated the new covenant (v. 15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 18, 'The author argues that the earthly sanctuary underwent a purification when it was first inaugurated (v. 21; Ex. 40; Lev. 8–9). In ancient Near East thought, cleansing was part of the preparation and qualification of something or someone for divine service (Ex. 29:36–37; 40:12–13; Lev. 8:6, 15; Ezek. 43:26; 45:18–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 22, 'The typological meaning of the Day of Atonement is fulfilled by Jesus in the heavenly sanctuary. The cleansing of the heavenly sanctuary points to a second function of His work and includes a work of judgment: the remission of sin (v. 22). This is explained in vv. 24–28. The passage (esp. v. 23) establishes the principle that the heavenly sanctuary needs to be cleansed. For a discussion of Christ’s special work of cleansing the heavenly sanctuary on the end-time Day of Atonement, see note on Dan. 8:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 24, 'Two “appearances” of Jesus are described. First, He appears before God “to put away sin” (vv. 24–26). This implies a work in the believers so that the promise of the new covenant—“I will put My laws in their mind” (8:8–12; 10:16–17; Jer. 31:31–34)—is fulfilled. Second, He appears “a second time … for salvation” (v. 28), something the author describes as judgment (v. 27). This likely refers to the Second Coming when He comes to save (in a final and ultimate sense) “those who eagerly wait for Him.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 9, 26, 'Compare vv. 22–23; 10:12. Although Christ was foreordained from the foundation of the world as the forever sacrificial victim (1 Pet. 1:19–20), His one sacrifice is all-sufficient. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21. Finally, note that the theological argument of Heb. 9–10 parallels Dan. 9:24–27, that Jesus’ sacrifice has inaugurated a new covenant (Heb. 9:15; compare Luke 22:20) and a new heavenly sanctuary (Heb. 10:20), redeemed from transgression (9:15), with sacrifices removed (10:18), and with sin removed (9:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 1, 'a shadow. Shadows are transitory, impermanent. For shadows, the author refers to the Levitical priesthood (7:23–28), the earthly sanctuary (8:5), and the animal sacrifices (9:12–14), all ritual aspects of the law that prefigured the realities of the new covenant (9:9; Col. 2:17). He argues that they were abolished once the “real thing” came (Heb. 7:11–19; 9:8; 10:9, 18). The law itself, however, was confirmed by being written on the hearts of believers (vv. 16–17; 8:7–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 2, 'consciousness of sins. Jesus’ sacrifice purifies the conscience from guilt. No performance of rituals can accomplish that (v. 3; 9:9–10, 14). Also, Jesus’ heavenly intercession provides the triumph over sin (see note on 9:24–28). Amazingly, this is obtained through faith (chap. 11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 9, 'takes away the first. Animal sacrifices and offerings (v. 8) have been taken away because they were ineffective (vv. 1–4, 11; see note on v. 1). Real sacrifice—like Jesus’ sacrifice—consists in obedience, gratitude, and good works (vv. 5–10; 12:28; 13:15–16; 1 Sam. 15:22; Ps. 50:8–15; Is. 1:10–17; Jer. 7:21–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 10, 'By that will. By God’s will. We are saved because of Jesus’ perfect obedience to God’s will (vv. 5–7). God and Jesus work together for our salvation (see note on 2:9; see Matt. 18:14; Gal. 1:4; Eph. 1:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 14, 'He has perfected. Jesus has perfected the believers—something that the old sacrifices could not accomplish (vv. 1–4; 7:11, 19; 9:9–10)—by cleansing their consciences (see notes on 10:2, 19–25). The full effects of His sacrifice, however, still await consummation (v. 13; see note on 9:24–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 15, 'The author quotes Jer. 31:33–34 for a second time (see Heb. 8:8–12). These two quotations mark the central section of his work (8:1–10:18) where he presents his most important argument: Jesus’ sacrifice and ascension have inaugurated a new covenant with better promises (7:18–22; 8:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 18, 'no longer an offering for sin. Refers to the abolition of sacrifices. See notes on vv. 1, 9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 19, 'Exhortation to claim the benefits of Jesus’ heavenly ministry.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 19, 'the Holiest. The original language here refers to the entire sanctuary, not just the Most Holy Place (see 9:8, 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 20, 'new and living way. Jesus’ sacrifice inaugurated a “new” and “living” covenant (9:15–23) that replaced the “obsolete” and “growing old” first covenant (8:13). This new covenant is a way into the heavenly sanctuary; that is, it provides us total access to the presence of God (4:16). through the veil. Either the veil at the entrance of the sanctuary or the veil at the entrance of the Most Holy Place, which contained the ark of the covenant representing God’s throne and embodying His presence (Num. 10:35–36; 1 Sam. 4:4; 1 Chr. 28:2). If the latter, the reference here would be to Jesus entering the Most Holy Place after His ascension in order to inaugurate the heavenly sanctuary. His flesh. Jesus’ earthly existence and suffering (Heb. 2:14; 5:7). Jesus’ “flesh” is not the veil but it makes access possible through the veil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 22, 'our bodies washed. Priests were “washed with pure water” and “sprinkled” with blood at their consecration so that they could “draw near” and minister before God (Ex. 40; Lev. 8–9). Today, faith in Jesus’ blood and the water of baptism (Eph. 5:26; Titus 3:5; 1 Pet. 3:20–21) consecrates/sanctifies believers so that they can “draw near” and perform a spiritual ministry of praise, good works, and thanksgiving before God (Heb. 9:13–14; 12:28; 13:15–16; Rom. 12:1; 1 Pet. 2:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 26, 'if we sin willfully. The author does not refer here to all types of deliberate sin. V. 29 explains that this sin specifically consists in the rejection of the Son of God, the blood of His sacrifice, and the “Spirit of grace.” God installed Jesus as Lord and intercessor at His “right hand,” but this sinner treats Him as an enemy to be trampled as a footstool (vv. 12–13; 1:13). Jesus’ blood redeemed from “the transgressions under the first covenant” (9:15), but this sinner treats it as “a common thing” (10:29) devoid of sanctification power. The Holy Spirit has exhorted, taught, and witnessed with grace/kindness about the great salvation secured by Jesus (v. 15; 2:1–4; 3:7; 9:8), but this sinner has insulted Him. The sin is serious because it comes after a full “knowledge of the truth” (10:26; also 6:4–5) and an experience of its power—this sinner had already been “sanctified” (10:29, 32; 6:4–5). When a sinner has rejected all the resources provided in his behalf, he is left to endure the judgment of God. See also 6:4–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 10, 38, 'the just shall live by faith. Faith has two dimensions. It means “confidence” in God’s promises (v. 35) and “endurance” in doing the will of God (v. 36).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 1, 'The author presents a list of OT figures whose faith should be imitated. This list contrasts with the negative example of the desert generation in 3:7–19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 1, 'Faith is the assurance or confidence that God will fulfill His promises (Rom. 4:20–21). For more on Christian ASSURANCE, see Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 3, 'By faith we understand. Readers are the first example in the list of faith heroes. Note also that faith is what produces understanding, not the other way around. not made of things which are visible. That is, made out of nothing by the “word of God.” For more on CREATION by the word of God, see Gen. 1:3; Ps. 33:6, 9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 8, 'Abraham is an important example to follow since the readers are themselves Abraham’s children (2:16) and heirs of the promises God made to him (see 1:14; 9:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 12, 'as good as dead. Abraham was 100 years old when Isaac was born (Gen. 21:5). As regards having children, he was “as good as dead.” By giving him Isaac, God showed His power to bring life out of death and, thus, His capability of being faithful to His promises (Heb. 11:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 19, 'in a figurative sense. Literally, “in parable.” Isaac was Abraham’s “only begotten son” (v. 17). His deliverance from death (Gen. 22:1–19) became a parable illustrating the resurrection of Jesus—God’s “only begotten son.” God’s order to Abraham to offer Isaac in sacrifice plainly contradicted His promises to him, but Abraham concluded that God could and would resurrect Isaac. He had learned from Isaac’s birth that God could bring life out from death (see Heb. 11:12; Rom. 4:17). God’s power to resurrect the dead is key to the faith of the believers (compare Heb. 11:39–40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 23, 'a beautiful child. “Beautiful” could refer to physical beauty as well as to good character. Acts 7:20 explains that the child was “well pleasing to God.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 24, 'refused to be called the son of Pharaoh’s daughter. Moses did not lose his position because of circumstances beyond his control (see Ex. 2:11–15). When Moses renounced his sonship he also renounced honor, influence, and inheritance; instead, he chose “the reproach of Christ” (Heb. 11:26). Christians are exhorted to do the same (13:13–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 27, 'seeing Him who is invisible. The paradox of “seeing” someone who cannot be seen is appropriate because it explains what Christians must do in order to endure. They need to be “looking unto Jesus” who has been exalted at the “right hand of the throne of God” to help them (12:2; see also 2:9), something that can only be done through faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 32, 'This section refers to three kinds of heroes: (1) triumphant heroes (vv. 32–35a); (2) suffering heroes (vv. 35b–38); and (3) “us” who are “made perfect” with them (v. 40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 32, 'Gideon … Barak … Samson … Jephthah … David. These figures from the OT committed serious mistakes. The author demonstrates that faith is for the ordinary people who may commit serious mistakes but still are able to obtain “good testimony through faith” (v. 39).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 35, 'Others. This list of suffering heroes may have been more important for the readers who were suffering themselves (10:32–34). The important thing is that suffering is not an evidence of God’s displeasure. To the contrary, those who suffered “obtained a good testimony through faith” (v. 39).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 11, 40, 'something better for us. In Hebrews “better” refers to the blessings of the new covenant: a better sacrifice, high priest, and promises (9:11–14). It is not that OT believers did not have access to these blessings, but that they received them only in figure or “shadow” (10:1; see 8:5). Instead, we have received through Christ the “substance” (11:1) or reality of those promises.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 1, 'The focus changes now from the example provided by the “great … cloud of witnesses” of the past (chap. 11) to “we” who participate in the same race they ran.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 1, 'the race that is set before us. A race is a great metaphor for the Christian life (see Acts 20:24; 1 Cor. 9:24; Gal. 2:2; Phil. 2:16; 2 Tim. 4:7). It emphasizes the need for endurance and the greatness of the prize. The struggle is fierce, not against other runners, but against sin, and it involves pain in the form of persecution, shame, even blood. The reward is entering God’s “rest” (chaps. 3–4), having access “behind the veil” to the presence of God (chaps. 5–10), and inheriting the heavenly homeland (chap. 11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 2, 'looking unto Jesus. Jesus is the greatest of the heroes of faith. author. He opened the way for others through His sacrifice. finisher of our faith. He guaranteed the reality of the prize by sitting at the right hand of God. despising the shame. By crucifying Jesus, the world passed judgment on His worthiness. By suffering willingly, Jesus passed judgment on the worthiness of the world. Believers should do the same (10:32–34; 13:13–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 10, 'they … chastened us as seemed best to them. In vv. 5–6 the author cited Prov. 3:11–12 to show that God may use trials to instruct His children just as human fathers do. The difference is that human fathers do what seems “best to them,” but their perception may be flawed. God’s discipline, instead, is always “for our profit.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 14, 'A final warning against spiritual carelessness.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 17, 'though he sought it diligently with tears. What Esau sought diligently was not repentance but the blessing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 18, 'the mountain. Here, Israel’s experience of fear at Mount Sinai (Ex. 19) illustrates the experience of those who lack Jesus and the blessings of the new covenant.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 22, 'Mount Zion. Represents the experience of believers under the new covenant because they have confidence to draw near before God through the sacrifice of Jesus (10:19–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 23, 'the spirits of just men made perfect. Part of the privilege of believers is a sense of spiritual oneness with holy people past and present. It obviously does not refer to righteous people who have died, because 11:39–40 explains that they did not receive “the promise” nor have they been “made perfect.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 26, 'once more I [will] shake. See translation note. This refers to God’s judgment that will involve the whole cosmos (see 9:27–28; also Is. 13; 34; Ezek. 7; 30:1–9; Joel 2:1–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 12, 27, 'the removal of those things that are being shaken. Refers to the purging or elimination of sin and what is contaminated by it. things which cannot be shaken. Refers to Jesus’ kingdom which will remain forever (v. 28; see 1:11–12). The shaking of the universe denotes God’s final and total victory over His enemies (10:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 13, 4, 'bed undefiled. The passage presupposes that sexual relations within marriage are acceptable, while those outside of marriage are defiling (see Lev. 18:24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 13, 9, 'strange doctrines … not with foods. It is not clear what these “strange doctrines” about “foods” were. The context suggests that these “foods” had become either a substitute or a complement to the grace offered through Christ and were probably related to Jerusalem’s altar (v. 10). It is possible that some were advocating participation at Jewish cultic meals—which were related to the sacrifices at the temple—as mediating grace. The author is very clear, however, that Jesus’ “once for all” sacrifice brought the value of the sacrifices at the temple to an end (10:1–18). Therefore, these foods and drinks as well as other ordinances related to those sacrifices had no value for salvation but had been imposed only until the coming of Christ (9:9–10). Another possibility is that the author had in mind ascetic teachings regarding foods—also considered to mediate grace—similar to those combated in Col. 2:16–23 and 1 Tim. 4:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 13, 10, 'We have an altar. This “altar” is wherever believers meet to offer sacrifices to God. These sacrifices consist of praising God, thanksgiving, good deeds, and sharing (vv. 15–16; see 10:22). Other NT books also describe the Christian life as a priestly service to God (Rom. 12:1–2; 1 Pet. 2:5, 9; Rev. 5:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 13, 13, 'let us go … outside the camp. Believers go “outside the camp” whenever they are denounced for Jesus. They may lose their (social) home, but they obtain a city “whose builder and maker is God” (11:10, 16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 13, 20, 'everlasting covenant. Ratified by the blood of Jesus. For more on the everlasting COVENANT, see Gen. 17:1–7; Heb. 9:22–28; 10:11–18. For more on the COVENANT ratified by blood, see Luke 22:20; Eph. 2:12–13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 13, 22, 'word of exhortation. It may be a technical term for a sermon as in Acts 13:15. Exhortation means to earnestly urge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Hebreus', 13, 23, 'our brother Timothy. Probably the companion of Paul (Acts 16:1–3).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 0, NULL, 'James has not always been well received as canonical Scripture. It was one of the last books to be accepted as');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 0, NULL, 'Two individuals named “James” were prominent in the early Christian church: one was the son of Zebedee, a disciple of Jesus; the other James was a highly respected leader in the Jerusalem community in the A.D. 30s and 40s (Acts 12:17; 15:13; 21:18). The son of Zebedee was martyred in A.D. 44, and there is no evidence that he played a significant role after the death and resurrection of Jesus. The other James, who was possibly Jesus’ brother (Gal. 1:19), is probably the author of this letter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 0, NULL, 'In typical Greco-Roman letter-writing style, James addresses the letter to his audience at the outset: “To the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad.” Many interpret the twelve tribes metaphorically, as being addressed solely to a Christian community. If the letter is as early as suggested below, it is possible that the “twelve tribes” is meant to be literal Jews, both the followers and non-followers of Christ. For James they were all his “brothers,” just as Peter addressed his Jewish audience on the day of Pentecost (Acts 2:29). James then, by using the phrase “the twelve tribes,” would be simply following a popular way of addressing his people. The same is true for the phrase “scattered abroad” (diaspora). In later Christianity the phrase was used metaphorically for their status as strangers and aliens in other parts of the world (Heb. 11:13; 13:14; 1 Pet. 1:1; 1:17; 2:11). But if the letter is addressed to the same audience as that of the day of Pentecost, then the diaspora would simply refer to Jews scattered throughout Palestine who were undergoing significant suffering.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 0, NULL, 'The internal evidence of the book in terms of the setting and context seems to argue for a very early date, possibly as the first book of the New Testament. James seems to be the most Jewish of all the New Testament writings. Both in content and illustrations, its material is rooted in the Old Testament and in the writings produced in the period between the Testaments. James reads like a transition document, one that bridges the Old and New Testaments. It seems possible that it was written very early in the life of the primitive church, probably before the council in Jerusalem in A.D. 49, and before there was a clear distinction between Judaism and Christianity. This would explain the lack of the major Christian theological themes that are prevalent in the rest of the New Testament. The social setting also suggests an origin before the destruction of Jerusalem in A.D. 70, when the socioeconomic situation was dire and the tensions between the rich and the poor as depicted in so much of the letter were intense. The violence and nationalistic conflict between the Jewish party of the Zealots and the ruling Sadducean elite that were present during this period support the position of an early date for James.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 0, NULL, 'As mentioned above, many major New Testament themes are missing in James: for example, the cross, the resurrection, the gift and ministry of the Holy Spirit, baptism, and church organization. It is possible that these are not mentioned because James’s focus is ethical and practical rather than theological. But it is also possible that an early date for the letter might negate a discussion of themes that were prominent later. The themes that James deals with are many that Jesus dealt with as reported in the Gospels: treatment of one’s fellow human beings, poverty and wealth, and problems of suffering. He uses many wisdom sayings to address issues like that of the tongue, trials and temptation, discrimination and favoritism, pride, and so on. As a result, some scholars like to classify this letter with the Old Testament Wisdom literature (e.g., Job and Proverbs).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 0, NULL, 'I.      Salutations (1:1)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 1, 'James. Probably the leader of the Jerusalem church (Acts 12:17; 15:13; 21:18). See Introduction, “Author.” twelve tribes. Either the literal Jews (following a popular designation), if he is writing to a broad Jewish audience, or a metaphor for Christians as the new Israel (see Introduction, “Audience”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 2, 'count it all joy. Consider it as cause for joy; look on the positive side (Matt. 5:11–12; Rom. 5:3–5). trials. The same Greek word can be translated “trials” or “temptation.” The context will determine its use. Here trials are external afflictions rather than inner enticement to sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 4, 'perfect. “Mature,” not “sinlessly perfect”; the focus is on a mature character.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 5, 'wisdom. Not philosophical speculation, but a practical gift that only God can give (Prov. 1:2–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 9, 'exaltation … humiliation. James, like Luke (Luke 1:46–55; 6:20–24), highlights the “Great Reversal” of the haves and the have-nots. Although this could be spiritualized, the fact that much of James references the social situation of his readers indicates that the primary meaning of this passage is economic.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 12, 'temptation. See v. 2. Here it could be both trials and temptations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 13, 'tempted. Blame for temptations should not be placed on God (2 Sam. 24:1; 1 Chr. 21:1), but on the individual.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 19, 'speak. Misuse of the tongue seems to be the cause of some of the suffering in James’s community (3:2–12), which may have led to wrath, anger, and violence (4:1–2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 25, 'perfect law. All God’s will as revealed in Scripture (and summarized in the Ten Commandments) is His law, and it is perfect (Ps. 19:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 1, 26, 'Pure … religion. This goes beyond traditional religious duties, practices, and observances. True religion means control of the tongue (see v. 19), taking care of the poor, and rejecting the value system of the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 1, 'the faith of. Those who possess a faith like Jesus. partiality. Plural in Greek; various partialities are being practiced. The word can be equated to modern discrimination and snobbery: economic, social, educational, physical and health, religious, etc.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 2, 'assembly. Literally, “synagogue.” Synagogues were used for religious services and secular gatherings. Ancient Jewish texts have almost exactly the same illustrations as mentioned here in James of discrimination in a legal proceeding with the same condemnation. The setting here seems to be a court of law between a rich plaintiff and a poor defendant. James’s condemnation is not limited to a worship setting; prejudiced, racist, and class-denigrating attitudes in everyday life have no place in the life of the believer. rings. Extravagant displays of jewelry and expensive clothing were signs of wealth in James’s world, and set the rich apart from the poor; just as houses, cars (or other material goods), education, status, fame, and power can set the rich apart from the suffering in today’s world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 5, 'chosen. The focus is not so much on the present situation of the poor, but the purpose for which they are chosen—to be rich in their trust relationship with God and thus inheritors of the kingdom. Yet God’s special concern for the poor is clearly expressed throughout Scripture (Deut. 10:18; Ps. 68:5; Amos 2:6–7; Luke 1:51–53; 6:20; 1 Cor. 1:26–31). Today’s poor—refugees, orphans, school dropouts, and struggling single parents—can be assured of God’s special care and concern in this life and hope for a better life if they put their trust in Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 6, 'oppress. The prophets consistently denounced exploitation of the poor, strangers, widows, and orphans (Jer. 7:6; 22:3; Ezek. 18:12; Amos 4:1; 8:4; Mal. 3:5). courts. Legal action over debts, rents, wages, and pledges. Often today the poor have no resources to fight justified legal battles, and thus they land in prisons or on the streets, homeless, and without support.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 8, 'you. Probably James’s readers were justifying their discriminatory action by appealing to these words—the rich being their “neighbor.” royal law. The supreme law of the kingdom, originally given to the Israelites in Lev. 19:18 as the basis of how they should relate to each other. Jesus and Paul articulate the two-part law of loving God and neighbor (Matt. 22:36–40; Mark 12:31; Luke 10:27; John 15:12; Rom. 13:8–10; Gal. 5:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 9, 'partiality. More than a flaw; it is a “sin.” Not showing partiality is an eleventh commandment for James. It’s as serious as murder, adultery (v. 11), or not remembering “the Sabbath day, to keep it holy” (Ex. 20:8–11). Racism, tribal prejudice, ethnic partiality, and classism as well as religious and nationalistic conflict in any time or place have at their root the sin of partiality and are obnoxious to God who loves all and considers all people to be His children.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 10, 'Because the law is an expression of God’s character, to ignore one part is to be in violation of His whole law. For more on the integrity of God’s LAW, see Matt. 5:18–19; 23:23.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 12, 'judged by the law of liberty. There are two standards in the final judgment: the law (Rom. 2:12) and our response to Jesus (John 5:24). The two are closely related because the response of faith to God’s work of salvation in Christ should lead believers to grateful obedience to the loving will of God as revealed in His law. Jesus stated, “He who rejects Me, and does not receive My words, has that which judges him—the word that I have spoken will judge him in the last day” (John 12:48). Rejecting Jesus and His revealed will determines the destiny of the person in the judgment. The fact that breaking one commandment makes a person a transgressor of the law and subject to judgment should lead to careful speaking and action. For more on the LAW as the standard in JUDGMENT, see Eccl. 12:13–14; Rom. 3:20. On the use of evidence in the JUDGMENT, see Dan. 7:9–14; Matt. 16:27; Rom. 2:6, 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 14, 'These verses have been used as an argument to show that a person needs to do something to obtain salvation; thus, this passage is seen as a clarification or contradiction of Paul’s “justified by faith apart from the deeds of the law” (Rom. 3:28–4:25). But it’s not a reaction to Paul; it’s an illustration of “pure and undefiled religion” (James 1:27). James and Paul are not contradicting, or at odds with each other; they are complementary. “Faith working through love” (Gal. 5:6) is Paul’s equivalent to James’s argument.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 14, 'works. When Paul speaks of “works” in Romans and Galatians, he is referring to his readers’ attempts to obtain salvation by keeping “the law.” Works for Paul is theological and legalistic—works of law. For James it’s ethical and social—deeds of compassion. Compare John the Baptist’s call to repentance that includes social concern (Luke 3:8–14). Doing something to relieve homelessness, the scourge of AIDS, or helping those without health care and education would be modern-day examples of the works that James proposes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 19, 'demons. The contrast is between the demons’ faith-response (in Greek, “faith” and “believe” are from the same root) and that of the believer; one is fear and trembling, while the other is positive social action.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 2, 23, 'Abraham … Rahab. Two popular figures in Jewish history—a holy patriarch and founder of the nation, and a “sinful” foreign woman—one from the bottom of the social ladder, and the other at the top of the religious ladder. Both illustrate that faith and deeds are not disjointed. justified. Not a Greek legal, forensic act; but as in the OT, being in a covenant relationship with God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 3, 2, 'perfect. See 1:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 3, 5, 'tongue. See 1:19, 26–27. Control over an unruly and hasty tongue allows mature persons to have control over their whole being.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 3, 6, 'fire. Scripture is filled with similar references to the tongue as a destructive fire (Ps. 39:1–3; 120:2–4; Prov. 16:27; 26:21; Is. 30:27). hell. Not “Hades” (the word used mostly in the NT for hell); rather “Gehenna,” the name of the garbage dump outside of Jerusalem that was constantly burning or smoking. It became a symbol of the final punishment of the wicked.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 3, 13, 'wisdom. See 1:5. The practicality of wisdom is explicated here. False or true wisdom is demonstrated by life and actions (Matt. 11:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 3, 18, 'peace. The Hebrew idea of “shalom,” meaning spiritual and physical wholeness and emotional health. Discord brings suffering—physical, emotional, and spiritual.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 4, 1, 'wars and fights. Traditionally viewed as metaphorical to describe hatred between church members. But the context could be the literal nationalistic conflicts between the Jews and Romans led by the Zealot party. James calls for peace, whether nationally or between individuals (3:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 4, 4, 'Adulterers. Nothing in James’s context suggests a literal interpretation. It is metaphorical for the unfaithfulness of the community. world. Values and actions that are at odds with God’s requirements.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 4, 13, 'buy and sell. James addresses the merchants, a second economic class who brought suffering on the poor (in 2:6–7 it was the financiers and bankers). Their arrogance was attacked; they ignored God’s will and direction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 4, 17, 'sin. For other definitions of SIN, see Is. 14:12–14; Rom. 3:20; 1 John 3:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 5, 1, 'Come now. In the Greek, this is an intense expression of denunciation (see also 4:13). rich. James turns to an economic class, the agriculturalists. He attacks exploitation by the wealthy landowners.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 5, 2, 'Three crimes are condemned here: luxurious living, oppression, and murder. All were intensely condemned in the Torah, by the OT prophets, and by Jesus (Deut. 24:14–15; Amos 6:1–7; Matt. 6:19; Luke 12:33; 16:19–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 5, 7, 'patient. The first response to suffering is patience; in context, this is especially for the oppressed of the above verses. coming of the Lord. The Second Coming, when Jesus will make all wrongs right and punish oppressors.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 5, 12, 'swear. Not profanity in this context, or official oaths. James and Jesus attack using oaths to prove one’s integrity. The simple honesty, sincerity, and reliability of one’s word is all that is required.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 5, 13, 'pray. Another response to suffering. Suffering is to be borne with patience and prayer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 5, 14, 'elders. Leaders in the community, whether house church settings or synagogues. anointing him with oil. The disciples also anointed with oil (Mark 6:13). While olive oil was used for medicinal purposes, the reference here is to divine healing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Tiago', 5, 16, 'Confess. In this context, the trespasses are possibly ones that caused the sickness, but they may also be faults that involve relations with others. In general, confession of sins or faults to persons affected by such indiscretions, or in a small group of people, can bring about healing and restoration; but indiscriminate public confessions of private sins can prove unhelpful.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'The author identifies himself as the apostle Peter (1:1), a shepherd of the flock of God, and one who witnessed the sufferings of Christ (5:1–4). This probably reminded the readers of how Jesus commissioned Peter to tend His flock after His resurrection (John 21:15–19).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'Peter’s first epistle was written to believers in the broad geographical area of “Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia” (1:1), all located in modern Turkey. The purpose of the letter was to strengthen the believers in the face of suffering. Peter reminds them that they stand in the “true grace of God” (5:12), that though society may reject them, God has chosen them as His special people, and that Jesus Himself suffered but was vindicated and exalted by God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'The letter was probably written in the first half of the 60s from “Babylon”—most likely a figurative expression for Rome (5:13). Early Christian tradition consistently relates that Peter ministered at Rome during the last phase of his ministry and died there as a martyr during Nero’s persecution after the great fire of Rome that occurred in A.D. 64. The early church father Papias (ca. A.D. 60–130) and others also accept the tradition that Mark was the interpreter of Peter during his ministry at Rome, transcribing his preaching there.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'The readers were experiencing varying degrees of grief and suffering, mainly slander and unjust treatment (1:6; 2:12, 18; 3:14; 4:4, 12–14). Thus, Peter describes the readers, probably metaphorically, as “sojourners” and “pilgrims” (1:1; 2:11) who endured precarious social conditions in the first-century Mediterranean world (see “Audience” above). The readers should not be surprised by these trials (4:12) but should recognize that Satan is behind them (5:8) and that they can result in God’s glory (1:7; 2:12; 4:14).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 2, 'This verse not only identifies the three persons of the Trinity, but indicates a focal point of each one’s activity in redemption. For more on the TRINITY, see Matt. 28:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 3, 'A long blessing that praises God for the gift of salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 3, 'a living hope. Pagan hope was dead (Eph. 2:12; 1 Thess. 4:13) because it was based in futile things. Christian hope is based on Christ’s resurrection (1 Pet. 1:3). For more on the ASSURANCE of salvation, which is taught here, see Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 6, 'trials. The original word also means “temptation.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 7, 'genuineness of your faith. Trials do not make faith genuine; yet, no genuine faith will exist without them (Acts 14:22; 2 Tim. 3:12). Trials are not an entrance exam or an obstacle course set by God. In fact, Jesus taught us to ask not to be led into them (Matt. 6:13). Peter also notes that whenever we face them “we are kept by the power of God” (1 Pet. 1:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 13, 'Therefore. The exhortation to hope and obedience that comes in vv. 13–21 is based on the salvation described in vv. 3–12. Hope and obedience are not the basis for salvation but its result. The exhortation rest your hope fully upon the grace confirms this. gird up the loins of your mind. An idiomatic expression that means “prepare your minds for action” (Luke 12:35; Eph. 6:14). In that time people wore long robes that needed to be tucked up into a belt in preparation for vigorous activity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 17, 'fear. The original word has also the positive meaning of “reverence,” “awe,” “respect.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 18, 'redeemed. In the ancient world, war prisoners and slaves were often liberated through the payment of a price. This practice became a favorite metaphor in the NT for the salvation of humanity achieved by God through the sacrifice of Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 23, 'word of God. For more on the BIBLE being the word of God, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17; 2 Pet. 1:20–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 1, 24, 'All flesh is as grass … endures forever. A comparison of humans and their fruits to the word of the LORD and its fruit (quoting Is. 40:6–8). Humans—being “corruptible” (1 Pet. 1:23)—“wither” and their fruit—as glorious as it may be—“falls away.” On the other hand, the word of the LORD is an “incorruptible” seed (v. 23). Neither the word itself nor its fruit falls away (compare Luke 21:33). The point here is that believers have been born through the word into an inheritance (fruit) that is “incorruptible … and that does not fade away” (1 Pet. 1:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 2, 'the pure milk of the word. The word of God was the “seed” that produced spiritual rebirth (1:23); now, it is the “milk” that produces spiritual growth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 3, 'Peter does not doubt God’s graciousness. The original expression implies the reality of God’s graciousness, which serves as the basis for the exhortation to desire the “pure milk of the word” (v. 2) of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 4, 'living stone. It refers to Jesus who was rejected and put to death by humans but resurrected by God (1:3; 3:18) and through whom we receive life (1:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 6, 'chief cornerstone. Large stones were carefully chosen and placed at the corners of buildings to give them strength and direction. One of these—in the southern wall of the temple platform at Jerusalem—measured more than 12 yards (over 10 m). This metaphor emphasizes Jesus’ honor and value. He was rejected and shamed but now is chosen and honored. Similarly, believers who are now rejected by society but put their faith in God will be elected and honored by Him (v. 5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 8, 'A stone of stumbling. For those who believe, Jesus is the foundation upon which they are built as God’s household (v. 6). For those who disbelieve, He is the stone on whom they stumble. Our relation to Jesus is the touchstone or criterion that determines our destiny. to which they also were appointed. Not that some are destined to believe and others to reject Jesus but that those who choose not to believe are “appointed” or destined to fall.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 9, 'a royal priesthood. OT priests were established—among other things—to teach the statutes of God (Lev. 10:11), bless others (Num. 6:23–27), and offer sacrifices (Lev. 17–18). Believers are called now to offer “spiritual sacrifices” (1 Pet. 2:5; Rom. 12:1)—like thanksgiving, praise, doing good, and sharing (Heb. 13:15–16)—to proclaim the mercy of God (1 Pet. 2:9), and bless others (3:9). holy nation. Alluding to Ex. 19:5–6. This passage shows the continuity of the “Israel of God” (Gal. 6:16). On metaphors used for the CHURCH, see note on Eph. 2:19–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 13, 'submit yourselves to every ordinance of man. Subordination to civil authority is an appropriate duty, but it must be understood in the context that believers are first “bondservants of God” (v. 16); therefore, believers should obey God first even in the face of punishment (v. 19; 4:14, 19). See note on 3:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 18, 'be submissive to your masters. See notes on v. 13 and 3:1. with all fear. See note on 1:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 2, 21, 'An allusion to Is. 52:13–53:12. Jesus, the Servant of God, serves as an example to Christian “servants” who may suffer because of their “conscience toward God” (1 Pet. 2:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 1, 'be submissive to your own husbands. Peter’s instruction to wives had the purpose of promoting honorable conduct in the context of the first-century Mediterranean culture. It is exactly the same instruction that he gave to slaves (2:18–25) and Christians in general (2:13–17; see 5:5) and had the same purpose: avoid discrediting the word of God. Foster a positive attitude from outsiders. The three social systems were unfair—autocratic government, slavery, and male domination in marriage—but Peter did not try to overthrow them. Instead, he upheld biblical principles that eventually destroyed them. Peter considered wives as co-heirs with their husbands and, therefore, equal before God (3:7). See note on 2:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 2, 'fear. See note on 1:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 3, 'your adornment. See 1 Tim. 2:9–10. Peter describes the proper behavior of Christian women. His main concern is their proper adornment. The passage is best understood for its meaning as, “Do not adorn yourselves outwardly by”: (1) arranging the hair (a description of extreme hairstyles of the day); (2) wearing gold (jewelry such as necklaces, earrings, bracelets, etc.); and (3) putting on fine apparel, clothing that emphasized social status and that created social distinctions in the church. a gentle and quiet spirit. Peter’s definition of true Christian adornment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 6, 'calling him lord. “Lord” was used for men of importance and high position.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 15, 'sanctify the Lord God in your hearts. Or “sanctify Christ as Lord in your hearts.” Here, “sanctify” means “honor as holy.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 18, 'This is a difficult passage that has received varied interpretations. Its general purpose and meaning, however, are clear: Jesus’ suffering and exaltation confirm the assertion of v. 17: “it is better … to suffer for doing good than for doing evil.” Jesus suffered in order to “bring us to God” (v. 18). Afterwards, God exalted Him “at the right hand of God” (v. 22). Likewise, Christians who suffer because of their conscience toward God will be finally saved (1:3–9), and, in the meantime, they might bring others to God (2:12; 3:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 18, 'flesh … Spirit. The last part could also be understood as “made alive in the Spirit.” The contrast between flesh and spirit does not refer to material and immaterial aspects of Jesus’ person (e.g., “body” vs. “soul”). Jesus was raised with a body (Luke 24:36–43). The contrast denotes elsewhere in Scripture two modes of existence: physical mortal existence ruled by sin versus spiritual existence empowered and controlled by the Holy Spirit (1 Pet. 4:2; Rom. 8:1–15; Gal. 5:16–25; 1 Tim. 3:16). Spiritual existence is not bodiless. Jesus died in the physical earthly mode of human existence and was resurrected in the spiritual mode with the transformed body that the saved will enjoy (1 Cor. 15:42–54).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 19, 'preached. Christ spoke by His Spirit through Noah, proclaiming the message of salvation. This passage cannot be used to teach that between His crucifixion and His resurrection Christ went and preached to the immortal souls of the people of Noah’s time. Apart from the fact that the Bible does not support the concept that the soul is immortal, in his second epistle Peter excludes the possibility that the people of Noah’s time had a second chance and could still be saved by assuring us that, like the angels who sinned and the inhabitants of Sodom and Gomorrah, God has consigned the ungodly of Noah’s time to be punished on the judgment day (2 Pet. 2:4–9). spirits in prison. The word used for spirit often has reference to human beings (e.g., 1 Cor. 14:32; Heb. 12:23; 1 John 4:1). The spirits (people) in Noah’s time were held in the prison house of sin. According to Scripture, only eight persons escaped from it (1 Pet. 3:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 3, 21, 'an antitype. That is, a “counterpart.” Peter makes a parallel between the salvation of Noah in the flood and baptism. As in the case of those who entered the ark, those who are baptized will be saved—provided this includes a good conscience toward God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 4, 1, 'he who has suffered … from sin. A difficult sentence. “He” probably refers to Jesus who suffered while on earth (3:18). Jesus “ceased from sin” not in that He stopped sinning (see Heb. 4:15; 7:26) but in that He stopped dealing with sin once for all at the cross.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 4, 6, 'those who are dead. Christian converts who died after hearing the gospel. judged … in the flesh. That is, judged by pagans according to human sinful standards (see note on 3:18). live … in the spirit. That is, resurrect (see note on 3:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 4, 17, 'judgment to begin at the house of God. An idea based on Ezek. 9:6 (see Mal. 3:1–6). Judgment has both a negative and a positive aspect. On one hand, as in Ezek. 9, it sifts out hypocrites. On the other hand, it vindicates and delivers the people of God (1 Pet. 4:6; also 1:6–9; 2:11–12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 5, 1, 'elders. Leaders of the church.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 5, 2, 'Shepherd the flock of God. This should be done in three ways: (1) willingly (despite the fact that leaders had a greater risk of being persecuted); (2) eagerly (that is, eager to serve others; the original word was used in secular writings of persons eager to provide time and money for civic duties); and (3) being examples.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 5, 6, 'humble yourselves. It means to accept suffering—when this is the result of being faithful to God—as part of God’s will. This is not an unwilling resignation to His will but casting all our cares upon Him knowing that He cares for us. It is self-forgetfulness based on trust (Ps. 55:22; Matt. 6:25–33; Phil. 4:6). This is important, lest the seed of the gospel chokes in our lives (Mark 4:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 5, 8, 'Be sober, be vigilant. A call to have a clear mind. Christians should recognize that behind their sufferings stands Satan trying to prey on them. The lion roars to scatter the sheep in panic and prey on them. Clear-minded sheep will stay calm and come close to their shepherd, trusting His protection. On the working of the devil, see Gen. 3:1–6; Luke 22:3; Acts 5:3; 2 Thess. 2:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 5, 9, 'Resist him. Christians must oppose the devil. Sheep are invited to participate in the victory of their pastor (3:18–22). Resistance takes the form of standing firm in faith. In the end, however, even that resistance is powered by God (5:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 5, 12, 'Silvanus. Probably the companion of Paul, known as Silas (Acts 15:22, 32). in which you stand. Could also be translated as a command: Stand fast in it! Peter summarizes his message as an exhortation to stand fast in God’s grace. Victory in the midst of suffering is not achieved through human ability, but it is an act of the grace of God to which we must cling (1 Pet. 1:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 Pedro', 5, 13, 'Babylon. Figurative expression for Rome. Mark. Probably John Mark to whom tradition has attributed the second Gospel (Acts 12:12, 25; Col. 4:10). Early tradition holds that Mark was an interpreter of Peter in Rome.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'The epistle itself identifies Peter, the apostle, as its author. The differences in literary style (even more evident in the original language), vocabulary, and subject matter between 1 and 2 Peter have led many throughout the history of the Christian church to question Peter’s authorship of this epistle. These differences, however, are not surprising once we understand the differences in purpose between 1 and 2 Peter and the broad function that literary assistants could have in those times (for the use of a literary assistant by an apostle, see Rom. 16:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'Second Peter 3:1 may suggest that it was written to those who had received 1 Peter—believers in a broad region of Asia Minor (see 1 Pet. 1:1; see also Introduction to 1 Peter, “Audience”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'Peter mentions in 1:13–14 that he expects to die soon. Early Christian tradition consistently relates that Peter died in Rome under Nero’s persecution—likely in A.D. 65. Second Peter was probably written shortly before that from Rome (see Introduction to 1 Peter).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'Second Peter is Peter’s last testament (1:13–14). In it, he warns believers about the false teachers that will come to destroy the church and exhorts them to stand firm in the faith and to “grow in the grace and knowledge” of Jesus (3:17–18). Other last testaments express similar concerns and advice (1 Sam. 12; Acts 20:17–38; 2 Timothy). He treats the following themes:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 0, NULL, 'I.      Address and salutation (1:1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 1, 'To those. From 3:1 we might infer that these are the same people who received 1 Peter. who have obtained like precious faith with us. As an apostle and eyewitness of Jesus’ majesty (1:16), Peter’s faith was just as precious before God as that of the readers who had not seen Jesus (John 20:29). Faith is something believers “obtain” from God who is righteous—that is, fair and impartial. When it comes to salvation, all of us have the same opportunity and standing before God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 3, 'has given to us all things. God has made a perfect provision for our spiritual life. This provision is received through Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 4, 'divine nature. Either the moral attributes listed in vv. 5–7 or the immortality that believers will receive at Jesus’ Second Coming (Rom. 2:7; 1 Cor. 15:53–54).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 5, 'add to your faith … love. Spiritual growth begins with faith, which is our first response to the gospel, and climaxes in love, which is the greatest virtue (1 Cor. 13:13), the fulfillment of the law (Rom. 13:10), and the evidence of Christian perfection (Matt. 5:44–48).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 10, 'make your call and election sure. This is done by growing spiritually through the steps mentioned in vv. 5–7. Note that spiritual growth is not the basis for our call and election. Nevertheless, we may repudiate our election by refusing to grow.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 14, 'I must put off my tent. A tent is a temporal dwelling—an appropriate metaphor for the briefness of life (2 Cor. 5:1–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 16, 'Peter replies to a first objection of the false teachers: that the Second Coming is a myth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 16, 'cunningly devised fables. Ancient religions used myths—fictional stories about their gods—to express religious, moral, or philosophical truth (see 1 Tim. 1:4; 4:7; Titus 1:14). Many, however, repudiated these myths as childish and a strategy to control others. Here, probably, Peter responds to the charge that the apostles had craftily invented the Second Coming of Jesus to their own advantage. eyewitnesses of His majesty. Peter argues that the transfiguration (Mark 9:1–8) was a preview and announcement of Jesus’ Second Coming. There, God confirmed the appointment of Jesus as “Son of God” (Ps. 2:7), the eschatological King (see note on Heb. 1:5), and bestowed upon Him “honor and glory” (2 Pet. 1:17). This appointment will have its culmination at Jesus’ Second Coming when He will sit “on the throne of His glory” (Matt. 25:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 19, 'the prophetic word confirmed. It may also be translated “the prophetic word more fully confirmed.” Peter suggests that prophecy is even a more certain basis for the belief in the Second Coming than his own witness of Jesus’ glory at the transfiguration. until the day dawns. Prophecy has a function in our hearts until its fulfillment arrives. It should make Jesus—“the morning star” and “light” of hope—shine in our everyday lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 1, 20, 'Peter replies to a second objection: that prophecy is of human origin. private interpretation. It refers to the charge that prophets interpreted their own dreams and visions and, therefore, prophecy had a human origin. spoke as they were moved by the Holy Spirit. Peter argues that the prophetic word is the result of the work and power of the Holy Spirit. For more on the source of the BIBLE, see John 17:17; 2 Tim. 3:15–17; Heb. 1:1. On the various divisions of inspired Scripture, see 2 Pet. 3:15–16; Luke 24:44. On the purpose of the BIBLE, see 2 Tim. 3:15. For other roles of the HOLY SPIRIT, see John 14:16–18.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 2, NULL, '2:1–3a Peter predicts the coming of false teachers. He describes them as secretive, as conducting shameful behavior, as covetous, and as taking advantage of believers through their teaching. They will also be popular. See also 2:10–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 2, 1, 'false prophets … false teachers. Peter’s warning against false teachers brings to mind the warnings against false prophets by Moses (Deut. 13:1–4), Jeremiah (Jer. 6:13–15; 14:14; 23:14), Ezekiel (Ezek. 22:28), and Jesus (Matt. 7:15–23). A crucial task for the believer is to discern between good and false teachers, and the Scriptures give us enough information to do this. denying the Lord. Not necessarily by their words, but by their actions (see 2 Pet. 2:3–10).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 2, 3, 'deceptive words. Literally, “made-up words.” their destruction does not slumber. False teachers claimed that if there was going to be a judgment, it should have come by then. Peter asserts the certainty of judgment by giving three examples: the banishment of evil angels from heaven, the flood, and the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah (see also 3:5–10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 2, 4, 'hell. Peter describes it here as a place of “darkness.” For evil angels, this earth is their “hell.” Rev. 12:9–12 says that God threw evil angels to this earth, and Rev. 20 says that they will remain chained here until the day of judgment. For more on hell, see notes on Matt. 10:28; 25:41.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 2, 10, 'speak evil of dignitaries. The original word for “dignitaries” means, literally, “glorious ones.” The meaning of vv. 10–11 is not entirely clear. It probably refers to false teachers scoffing at evil angelic powers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 2, 17, 'wells … clouds. Both metaphors refer to the inability of the false teachers to provide the “liberty” they promise (v. 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 2, 20, 'the latter end is worse. See Matt. 12:43–45. There is hope for apostates, however. Peter himself recovered after having denied Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 1, 'Peter predicts the coming of scoffers of the Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 1, 'this second epistle. The first was probably 1 Peter.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 3, 'In the last days, people will show no respect to the promise of the SECOND COMING of Christ. For other passages dealing with world conditions in the last days before the SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24:21–25, 37–39; 2 Tim. 3:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 4, 'Where is. This was a common taunt in the OT when the righteous suffered (Ps. 42:3, 10; 79:10; 115:2). since the fathers fell asleep. “Fathers” could refer either to OT patriarchs or the first generation of Christians. It is not clear who Peter meant here. If “fathers” refers to the first generation of Christians, it would probably be because of the sayings where Jesus seemed to predict His Second Coming during the lifetime of the first generation (Matt. 16:28; 24:34; John 21:22–23). If this is the case, Peter does not answer directly the objection, though later says that the false teachers “twist” the Scriptures (2 Pet. 3:16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 5, 'Peter replies to a fourth objection (v. 4): that Jesus has not been faithful to His promise. He will not come again. Peter answers the objection by referring to three actions of God through His word: the creation of the heavens and earth (v. 5), the flood (v. 6), and the preservation of the world until the day of judgment (v. 7). This shows that “all things” have not remained the same since creation (v. 4) and that God’s word is powerful enough to fulfill its promises.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 8, 'Peter explains two reasons for the delay: (1) God and humans experience time differently (v. 8) and (2) God is patient with us, waiting for us to repent (v. 9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 10, 'For more on Christ’s SECOND COMING, see Matt. 24–25; Mark 13:1–37; Luke 21:5–36; John 14:1–3; Acts 1:6–11; 1 Thess. 4:13–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 11, 'Compare with characteristics of God’s last-day REMNANT in Rev. 12:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 12, 'On HELL and the timing of the hell fire, see Rev. 20:9–10. See also note on Matt. 10:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 13, 'For more on the NEW EARTH, see Rev. 21:1–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 15, 'twist. On the need for careful BIBLE study, see 2 Tim. 2:15. the rest of the Scriptures. Peter classified Paul’s writings as part of the Bible. On the inspiration and scope of the BIBLE, see 2 Pet. 1:20–21; 2 Tim. 3:15–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 Pedro', 3, 18, 'grow. Here, Peter reminds us of the opening exhortation at 1:5–7. Spiritual growth is the best defense against apostasy; however, in order to be effective, growth needs to have two dimensions: knowledge and the experience of grace.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 0, NULL, 'The First Epistle of John is a favorite of many readers for its clear message of love and assurance in Jesus. For people struggling with competing ideas about the gospel, 1 John lifts up the central message of God’s love in sending His own Son Jesus to save the world. In 1 John, the options are clear: to believe in Jesus and begin to experience eternal life and love and cleansing today, or to hold on to the sin and hatred that is destroying our world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 0, NULL, 'The letter’s opening words suggest that the author was an eyewitness who personally knew Jesus (1:1–4). Although his name is not given in the letter, there is good evidence that he was John son of Zebedee, an apostle of Jesus (Mark 1:16–20; 3:17). First John is much like the Gospel of John in its opening prologue (1 John 1:1–4; John 1:1–18), simple Greek style, and vocabulary and ideas (love, life, light, abiding in Him, etc.). These similarities indicate that the author was the same “beloved apostle” who is believed to have written the Gospel (John 21:20, 24; see Introduction to John, “Author”).The writings of the early church support this understanding, for nowhere do they argue for any author other than John, the beloved apostle.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 0, NULL, 'Since the letter lacks the greetings normal in a personal letter (such as in 2, 3 John), it was likely intended to be passed around among a number of house churches. This may have been in the area of Ephesus, where early Christian writers suggest John settled after the destruction of Jerusalem. The audience knew and looked up to the author, for he addresses them as “beloved” (3:2, 21) and “my little children” (2:1; 3:18) and is well acquainted with their problems and strengths (2:13–14, 26). Many had been believers for some time (2:7, 13) and were being tempted to care more for what the world offered than for the things of God and the needs of their fellow believers (2:15–17; 5:21). Their most pressing struggle at the time of the letter was to deal with the division and discord caused by some who had rejected what John had taught them about Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 0, NULL, 'It is not certain when John wrote this letter. Likely it was late in the author’s life, for it speaks of the audience as “little children” and deals with issues not raised in the earliest years of the church. Early church writings suggest that John was the longest lived of the apostles, which means the letter was likely written in the last part of the first century A.D. This late date is also supported by the author’s reference to himself as “the elder” in 2 and 3 John. These letters share the same issues and wording as 1 John, and were thus likely written by him at around the same time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 0, NULL, 'First John warns of some who had left the fellowship of believers (2:19; 4:1) and were seeking to win the church over to their own ways of thinking (2:26). John calls these men “antichrists” and “false prophets” (2:18; 4:1–3). He spoke so strongly because they had rejected the center of the Christian faith—that Jesus is the Son of God (5:10); that He came as a real human being (2:22); and that He was the Christ whom God sent to save the world (4:1–3). Some apparently claimed to be in fellowship with God and to be without sin (1:6–10; 2:4), yet many acted in unrighteous and hateful ways (2:4, 11; 3:6–10, 15). These arguments generally reflected ideas from within their own cultures that they were unwilling to exchange for the teachings of Christ. Similarities can be seen with heretical groups known from later historical documents (such as Cerinthians, Docetists, or Gnostics). However, because 1 John wisely focuses on Christ rather than the wrong teachings of the opponents, it is impossible to be certain whether it is dealing with any of these.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 0, NULL, 'I.      Introduction (1:1–4)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 1, 'An introduction declaring the letter’s reliability and purpose.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 1, 'from the beginning. Of Jesus’ ministry, and further back to creation and before (John 1:1–3). heard … seen. Not mere theory, but something experienced personally. the Word of life. John’s message is about Jesus, in whom eternal life is available to all who believe (1 John 1:2; 5:11; compare John 1:1, 4, 14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 3, 'fellowship. A close relationship based on mutual interests and care for one other.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 5, 'This first main section of the letter outlines a series of wrong claims people are making, and contrasts them with what is really and joyfully true.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 5, 'light. Symbolizes God’s glory and allows truth to be clearly seen (Is. 2:5; 60:1, 19; Hab. 3:4; John 8:12). darkness. Hides evil and untruths, but cannot exist where there is light (1 John 2:9–11; John 3:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 7, 'we have fellowship. Cleansing can be ours today.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 8, 'have no sin. If we are no longer sinning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 9, 'confess. Admit the truth. faithful and just. No matter how unfaithful we may be, God is always faithful to His promises to forgive. For more on SIN and forgiveness, see Matt. 12:31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 1, 10, 'liar. The Bible says all have sinned (Is. 53:6; Rom. 3:23).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 1, 'an Advocate. A helper who intercedes on someone’s behalf. This verse provides a valuable picture of Christ’s work as Mediator in the heavenly sanctuary. When Christ ascends to heaven, He does not lose interest in His people but serves as their Advocate with the Father. See Rom. 8:34; 1 Tim. 2:5. On Christ’s intercessory ministry in the heavenly SANCTUARY, see Heb. 4:14–16; 7:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 2, 'propitiation. Jesus acted as our Advocate by offering Himself as the sacrifice to pay the penalty for sin. This was not to appease an angry God, but because both justice and mercy must be upheld (1:7; 4:10; Zech. 3:1–5). For more on SALVATION, see note on Rom. 3:21–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 3, 'know Him. Not just knowing about Him, but knowing Him personally. keep His commandments. For more on obedience to God’s LAW, see Matt. 5:17–19; John 14:15; Eph. 2:10; James 2:10, 12; Rev. 12:17; 14:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 5, 'is perfected. Has accomplished its purpose (4:16–19; 5:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 6, 'abides in. Remains in constant close relationship with (John 15:4–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 7, 'word. Message.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 8, 'Again. On the other hand. Jesus reminded people of the ancient love commandment (John 13:34; compare Lev. 19:18), and now makes it real in the lives of believers (1 John 4:7–10; see also Jer. 31:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 12, 'What believers have in God, versus what the world has to offer.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 12, 'little children. Often used of the whole audience (e.g., vv. 1, 28). fathers … young men. Refers to maturity either of faith, or of age. overcome. Through faith in Jesus (5:3–4). I write … have written. John writes what he has always taught.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 15, 'the world. Here represents all that stands in opposition to God and His ways (5:19; John 1:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 16, 'lust. Desire. Used here of desires contrary to the life-giving will of the Father (compare v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 18, 'Instruction on how to recognize and deal with those whom this epistle calls “antichrists.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 18, 'last hour. Begins with Jesus’ first coming (Acts 2:17; Heb. 1:2), though in some passages it refers specifically to the days just before His return (2 Tim. 3:1; 2 Pet. 3:3–4). Antichrist. One who works against Christ and, ultimately, tries to stand in His place. Jesus and other Bible writers prophesied of such men, including one who would appear just before Jesus returns (Dan. 7:25–26; 8:23–25; Matt. 24:4–5; 2 Thess. 2:1–10; 2 Tim. 3:1–8; Rev. 13:1–6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 19, 'made manifest. Shown as who they truly are.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 20, 'an anointing … Holy One. Probably the Holy Spirit, who guides them to the truth about Jesus (2:27; John 16:13–14; Acts 10:38). all things. All John is discussing here.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 22, 'the Christ. The one chosen by God to save the world, the center of John’s whole message (e.g., 2:1–2; 4:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 23, 'does not have the Father. By rejecting Jesus whom the Father sent, they reject the Father as well.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 24, 'that … which you heard. The truth about Jesus and eternal life (vv. 23, 25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 27, 'the anointing … Him. See note on v. 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 2, 29, 'everyone who practices righteousness. Doing right is evidence that one has been born of God through faith in Jesus (5:1; John 1:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 1, 'These verses celebrate the amazing privilege of being children of God and the purity from sin that this relationship will develop in believers’ lives.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 1, 'does not know us. Being a child of God makes one a misfit in the world.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 2, 'we shall be like Him. A promise of what shall be, for no sinful person can see God and live (1:10; Ex. 33:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 3, 'purifies himself. It is the awesome promise of Christ’s coming that motivates His children to seek purity from sin (Ex. 19:10–11; Deut. 23:14; Matt. 5:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 4, 'lawlessness. Rebellion against God and His law (Rom. 6:19; 2 Cor. 6:14; 2 Thess. 2:7–8). For other definitions of SIN, see Gen. 3:1–6; Is. 14:12–14; James 4:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 5, 'no sin. He was the sinless sacrifice for our sins (Ex. 12:5; John 1:29; 2 Cor. 5:21; 1 Pet. 1:19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 6, 'does not sin. See also 5:18. Speaks of the ultimate reality that God’s children will be pure like their Father (1:5–6; 3:2–3). The point stressed is that God’s children do not willingly go on sinning. Nevertheless, 1 John recognizes that at the present time believers are not yet fully without sin and can only reach this ideal through Jesus (1:7–2:2; 5:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 8, 'works of the devil. All evil will be destroyed when Jesus returns (2 Thess. 2:8; Rev. 20:10–15). For more on SIN and its results, see Gen. 3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 9, 'His seed. Likely refers to the Holy Spirit (John 3:5–8; 14:17). cannot sin. See note on v. 6. The main point in this verse is the good news that those born of God will inherit their Father’s character and sin will become unattractive to them (5:1; John 1:12; 2 Cor. 5:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 10, 'A second part of God’s character, inherited by those who become His children, is His incredible love.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 10, 'children of God … children of the devil. In reality there are only two choices: to hold onto sin in rebellion against God as the devil did (3:4; Gen. 3:13; Rev. 12:7–9); or to become a child of God, overcoming sin through faith in Jesus (5:1; John 8:44).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 13, 'hates you. Like Cain became angry with Abel (Gen. 4:4–8), the world resents those who do right (see note on 2:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 14, 'because we love. Believers’ love demonstrates that they have indeed received the promised gift of eternal life (v. 15; 5:11–13; John 5:24–26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 16, 'lay down our lives. Live our lives to benefit others (see also v. 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 19, 'by this. By love in action. assure our hearts. God’s love reflected in one’s actions gives confidence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 22, 'we receive. The Father gives graciously to His obedient children, though not always in the time or manner they might imagine (5:14–15; John 15:7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 3, 23, 'His commandment. John sums up God’s will in these two points.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 1, 'This passage explains that one who has God’s Spirit will speak the truth about Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 1, 'test the spirits. Many claim to be guided by the Holy Spirit, when in reality the spirit that guides them is not of God (1 Tim. 4:1). prophets. People who bring a message from God. Every spirit that confesses … Jesus. A spirit may be tested by whether the person’s words about Jesus agree with what John and the other Bible writers say. Other important tests for spirits and prophets are found in Is. 8:20; Jer. 28:9; Matt. 7:20. For more on other SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 3, 'the Antichrist. See note on 2:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 4, 'have overcome them. Have resisted the false prophets’ teachings.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 6, 'By this. By whether a person is in harmony with what John teaches.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 7, 'A call to God’s children to love as their Father loves.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 7, 'everyone. John is speaking of believers here (2:3; 5:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 8, 'God is love. Love is the essence of what God is all about. For other attributes of GOD THE FATHER, see Ex. 34:6–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 10, 'propitiation for our sins. For more on SALVATION, see 2:2 and note on Rom. 3:21–26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 12, 'No one has seen God. Yet love for one another evidences the awesome truth that God dwells in His children. perfected. The circle of God’s love has been made complete.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 13, 'By this we know. A reminder that one who abides with God has His Spirit and speaks the truth about Jesus (3:24–4:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 17, 'have boldness. Trust in God’s love, as it fills our lives, takes away all fear of judgment (Heb. 4:15–16). On other aspects of Christian ASSURANCE, see Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 4, 19, 'We love … because. God’s incredible love arouses our love in response, a love that overflows all around us.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 1, 'A summary of chaps. 1–4 showing how love for others, love for God, and keeping God’s commands are inseparably intertwined in the lives of those who believe in Jesus. For more on the fulfillment of God’s LAW, see Matt. 22:36–40; John 14:15; Rom. 13:8–10; Gal. 5:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 1, 'believes. An active ongoing faith, rather than an occasional thought or feeling. the Christ. See note on 2:22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 3, 'not burdensome. Because those born of God will naturally grow to love and resemble their Father (3:9; 4:7–8; 5:1; Matt. 11:28–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 4, 'overcomes the world. Lives according to the Father’s love and commandments rather than the ways of the world (2:15–16). A promise claimed by faith.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 6, 'The sure testimony that the one who believes in the Son of God has eternal life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 6, 'water and blood. Likely represents the events of Jesus’ baptism, which began His earthly ministry, and His death on the cross, which accomplished its completion (John 19:34). Spirit … bears witness. About Jesus (John 15:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 8, 'bear witness. About who Jesus is (1:1–3; 5:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 10, 'The sure testimony is that the one who believes in the Son of God has eternal life. Jesus abides in those who believe in Him, and through His presence believers have assurance because they experience eternal life today. For more on the Christian ASSURANCE of salvation, see John 3:14; 5:22; Rom. 8:31–39.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 14, 'A summary of the certainties believers have through Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 15, 'we have. God can be counted on to grant believers’ petitions that are according to His will (5:14; compare 3:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 16, 'his brother. A fellow child of God (2:29; 5:1–2). He will give him life. The original language is not as clear as this translation regarding the identity of who is represented by the pronouns in this phrase. “He” could refer to the Son of God, or the one praying. “Him” could refer to the one praying or to the sinner. However it is to be understood, the passage clearly affirms the value of intercessory prayer for those entangled in sin. sin leading to death. Rejecting the testimony of the Spirit, as John’s opponents were doing, can lead only to death (4:1–3; Matt. 12:31; John 16:7–8). For more on the results of SIN, see Rom. 6:23; Gen. 3:1–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 18, 'does not sin. See note on 3:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '1 João', 5, 21, 'from idols. From giving preference to someone (2:22; 4:1) or something (2:15–16) other than God.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 João', 0, NULL, 'The Second Epistle of John is a treasure in a small package, calling readers in every age to accept and live by the love and truth of Jesus Christ. Like 1 John, 2 John celebrates the command to love one another which has been around from the beginning and which is put into practice through keeping the commandments (vv. 5–6; compare 1 John 2:7–10; John 13:34). It also warns against some who reject what John and the other New Testament writers have taught about Jesus, and who do not confess Jesus Christ as coming “in the flesh” (vv. 7–11; see 1 John 4:3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 João', 0, NULL, 'Although his name is not given in the letter, several clues help identify the author. The strong similarities with 1 John in style and wording (see above) are most simply explained by recognizing that both letters were written by the same person, identified as John the apostle who also wrote the Gospel of John (see Introduction to 1 John, “Author”). His reference to himself as “the elder” (2 John 1:1) fits with this picture. For while at that time groups of elders led out in churches as in Jewish communities and synagogues (Ex. 3:16; Acts 4:5; 14:23; 1 Pet. 5:1), being known as “the elder” recognizes him as set apart even from these leaders. This is just what one would expect of an apostle whose maturity and authority in the faith naturally surpassed that of the others and who was by the time of this writing quite elderly.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 João', 0, NULL, 'The author addresses the letter to “the elect lady [Greek kuria] and her children.” Kuria is normally a title of honor given to a noble woman, and this letter may have been written to just such a woman and her family. On the other hand, “the elect lady” may point symbolically to a church, for the Bible elsewhere uses a woman to symbolize God’s people (Is. 54:5–8; Rev. 12:17), though never with the term kuria. A third possibility is that the letter was written to a woman in whose home a house church met (Rom. 16:3–5), and the members of the church are referred to as her “children” (1 John 2:1, 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 João', 0, NULL, 'Like 1 John, 2 John addresses the problem of false teachers with teaching most typical of the later New Testament church. Likely, then, they were written near the same time, sometime in the last part of the first century A.D. It is often suggested that 2 John was written shortly after 1 John, to warn outlying areas of these false teachers, but this is not certain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 João', 0, NULL, 'Second John calls the “lady” and her “children” to a more steadfast love and faithfulness before the coming of some he calls “deceivers,” who denied that Jesus Christ came as a human being in the flesh. (See Introduction to 1 John, “Message.”)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '2 João', 0, NULL, 'I.      Greetings (vv. 1–3)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '3 João', 0, NULL, 'The numerous similarities with 2 John show that the two letters are written by the same person. Like 2 John it begins with greetings from “the Elder,” who loves Gaius “in truth” and rejoices that Gaius walks “in the truth.” It also closes like 2 John, expressing the wish to “speak face to face” instead of writing, and passing along personal greetings. This author is then most likely John the apostle, who is also believed to have written 1 and 2 John and the Gospel of John. (See the Introductions to 1 and 2 John, “Author.”)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '3 João', 0, NULL, 'Third John is one of the few New Testament letters written to a single individual. The letter gives evidence that this individual, Gaius, is a loyal friend of John and of “the brethren” John mentions. He was apparently wealthy enough to offer hospitality when these brothers came to visit. Gaius is a common Roman name; and though there is a Gaius mentioned in four other places in the New Testament (Acts 19:29; 20:4; Rom. 16:23; 1 Cor. 1:14), it is impossible to know whether this Gaius was one of these.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '3 João', 0, NULL, 'Like John’s other epistles, 3 John was likely written in the late first century A.D.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '3 João', 0, NULL, 'At the time 3 John was composed, the other apostles, along with many other “brethren,” were traveling about preaching the name of Jesus. These teachers were dependent on the hospitality of other believers. This letter praises Gaius for his hospitality to them and urges him to continue even in the face of Diotrephes’s selfish rivalry. There is no evidence that Diotrephes was one of the “deceivers” spoken of in 1 and 2 John, for no false teaching is mentioned. He does, however, exemplify the unloving behavior 1 John (2:9; 3:14) warned against.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', '3 João', 0, NULL, 'Third John follows the standard form for a personal letter of the day.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Judas', 0, NULL, 'Jude, or Judas, was a common name in New Testament times. The New Testament, however, only mentions one Jude with a brother named James: the one who was also a brother of Jesus Christ (Matt. 13:55; Mark 6:3). Judas and James were probably stepbrothers of Jesus through an earlier marriage of Joseph because they treated him as a younger brother (Mark 3:21, 31–35). It is also probable that Jude shared with his brothers a critical view of Jesus and did not believe in Him during His ministry on earth (John 7:3–5). Paul tells us that Jesus appeared to James after His resurrection (1 Cor. 15:7), something that would explain their conversion. One way or another, James became a leader of the church (Acts 12:17; 15:13; 21:18; Gal. 1:19; 2:9) and church tradition relates that Jesus’ relatives continued to be leaders of the church in Israel for generations. If this is true, it is noteworthy that Jude does not take any advantage of a special relationship with Jesus, but describes himself simply as a “bondservant of Jesus Christ” (Jude 1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Judas', 0, NULL, 'We have no information to identify the audience of this letter. The only information about the date of writing is based on the relationship between Jude and 2 Peter. It is evident that 2 Peter 2:1–3:3 and Jude have some kind of interdependence. They share the same ideas, use similar vocabulary, and have the same sequence of ideas. A majority of scholars believe that Peter used and adapted Jude to address a similar danger elsewhere. This would suggest a date for Jude before A.D. 65.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Judas', 0, NULL, 'Jude probably had a particular situation in mind. He had planned to write about Christian salvation, but disturbing news of the infiltration of false teachers among the churches forced him to change the subject (vv. 3–4). These false teachers claimed to be spiritual (v. 19), but were sexually corrupt (vv. 7–8, 18), lovers of money (vv. 11–12, 16), loud and boastful (v. 16), and perverted the communion meal (v. 12). They probably also claimed to have visions (v. 8). All of this trouble in the church does not necessarily suggest a late date. Paul attacks similar problems in 1 and 2 Corinthians.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Judas', 0, NULL, 'In verses 14–15, Jude quotes from 1 Enoch 1:9. And Jude 9 refers to a story that was narrated in The Assumption of Moses, also called The Testament of Moses. Both 1 Enoch and The Assumption of Moses are non-canonical (that is, not included in the Bible) Jewish literature. Jude is not unique in quoting such sources. Paul quoted Greek authors: Epimenides (Acts 17:28a; Titus 1:12) and Aratus (Acts 17:28c). Peter and Paul believed, guided by the Holy Spirit (2 Pet. 1:21), that those quotations and allusions were appropriate for their messages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Judas', 0, NULL, 'I.      Greetings (vv. 1–2)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 0, NULL, 'Early manuscripts title this book “The Apocalypse of John.” Later manuscript tradition gave it the better title drawn from the first verse—“The Revelation of Jesus Christ”—because Christ is the central figure of the book. It is a revelation both from and about Him. The Greek word transliterated apocalypsis or “apocalypse,” often translated “revelation,” literally means “unveiling.” This great ending of the Bible, in a dazzling vision of the end of all things, is strongly associated with the book of Daniel for its similarity in literary genre and its great emphasis on the “unveiling” or revelation of the future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 0, NULL, 'John received the visions of Revelation on the island of Patmos (Rev. 1:9), in the Aegean Sea off the coast of Asia Minor, modern-day Turkey. While the Bible does not tell us who this “John” was, most early historical sources identify him with the John of the Gospels, brother of James, son of Zebedee, a disciple of Jesus (Matt. 4:21–22; 17:1; Mark 3:17; 10:35–41; Luke 9:28, 54; 22:8; John 13:23–25; 18:15–17). This identification is supported by the evidence of Revelation itself, which contains many parallels of language with the Gospel of John.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 0, NULL, 'The historical sources also tell us that John, spiritual leader of the churches in western Asia Minor, was in exile on Patmos because of his faith in Jesus. Jesus came to him in a vision there, sharing a message for those churches that would have a powerful impact on readers right down to our day (1:3, 11). The cities where these churches were located form a rough circle with about a 50-mile (80 km) radius across western Turkey (see map on p. 1658). They are listed in Revelation in clockwise order beginning with Ephesus in the southwest.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 0, NULL, '1. The book is a divine revelation (1:1–3). The symbols were chosen by God (1:1). John received the content through visions (1:1–2; 11–19).
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 0, NULL, 'In the course of Christian history there have been four main ways that people have tried to apply the visions of this book.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 0, NULL, 'Major themes taken up in Revelation include:
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 0, NULL, 'I.      The prologue (1:1–8)
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 1, 'of Jesus Christ. Can mean the message comes from Jesus, is about Him, or both. must shortly take place. The Bible always presents the end as near (Matt. 16:28; 1 Thess. 4:16–17). Allusion to Dan. 2:28, 45. Revelation is modeled on the style and content of Dan. 2: sequences of history in symbolic format. signified it. Using symbolic language about the future (John 21:19; Acts 11:28). John. See Introduction, “Author.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 2, 'the word of God. Probably the OT. the testimony of Jesus Christ, to all things that he saw. John’s visionary experience of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 3, 'Blessed. The first of seven “beatitudes” in Revelation (see also 14:13; 16:15; 19:9; 20:6; 22:7, 14). The number seven is very central to the book. he who reads. Revelation was intended to be read aloud in worship. the time is near. See note on 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 4, 'A plain-language summary of the major themes of the book: the Godhead, the roles and actions of Jesus, and His Second Coming.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 4, 'seven churches. See Introduction, “Date and Place of Writing.” Asia. A Roman province located in western Turkey. Grace. Greek greeting. peace. Hebrew greeting. Him who is … is to come. A rough paraphrase of God’s name in Ex. 3:14–15. seven Spirits. Symbolic reference to the Holy Spirit. Note that Rev. 1:4–5 mentions each member of the Godhead or Trinity—the Father (as “Him who is and was and is to come”), the Spirit (“the seven Spirits”), and Jesus Christ. See 4:2, 5; 5:5–6; 22:16–21. For more on the Trinity, see Matt. 28:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 5, 'the faithful witness … kings of the earth. Jesus’ qualifications to rule us: His death, resurrection, and heavenly reign. To Him who. Begins a list of Jesus’ actions in our behalf: He loves us, died for us, and raised us to the highest possible status. kings and priests. See Ex. 19:6; 1 Pet. 2:9. Kings have the highest status in the political realm and priests have the highest status in the religious realm.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 7, 'coming with clouds. Literary allusion to Dan. 7:13, even if not referring to the same event. See also Matt. 24:30; 26:64; Mark 13:26; 14:62. every eye. The SECOND COMING of Jesus will be a universally visible event, unlike His first one. For more on the manner of Christ’s SECOND COMING, see Matt. 25:31; 1 Thess. 4:16–17; 2 Thess. 1:8. pierced Him. Allusion to Zech. 12:10 and Jesus’ crucifixion (John 19:34). This language suggests a special resurrection of select individuals just before Jesus’ return.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 8, 'the Alpha and the Omega. First and last letters of the Greek alphabet. See also 22:13. God is in full control of the past, the present, and the future.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 9, 'tribulation. John and his readers have suffered for the gospel. Suffering is never pleasant, but it can produce patience. Patmos. See Introduction, “Date and Place of Writing.” for the word of God. Implies the gospel is the reason he is on the island (in exile). testimony. Translated “witness” in v. 5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 10, 'in the Spirit. The first of four references. Probably indicates onset of a vision (see also 4:2; 17:3; 21:10). the Lord’s Day. While later Christians came to associate the Lord’s Day with Sunday, in the Bible the Lord’s Day is the seventh-day SABBATH. See also Is. 58:13–14; Mark 2:27–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 11, 'What you see … book. Precedes the vision of Christ (vv. 12–18). in Asia. This is the first listing of the cities in which the seven churches are found.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 12, 'seven golden lampstands. Allusion to the lampstand in the OT sanctuary (Ex. 25:31–40; 27:20–21; 37:17–24; 1 Kin. 7:49). The seven lampstands represent the seven churches (Rev. 1:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 13, 'This has numerous similarities to the descriptions in Dan. 7:9–14; 10:5–7. The characteristics of Christ in this vision are related to the individual churches in Rev. 2–3. Each church receives a unique picture of Jesus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 13, 'Son of Man. Jesus’ favorite self-designation (see Mark 2:28; Luke 19:10) and an allusion to Dan. 7:13. The glorified Jesus is dressed like the OT high priest (see Ex. 28:1–30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 16, 'seven stars. See v. 20. two-edged sword. Represents the judging power of God’s word (Heb. 4:12–13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 17, 'the First and the Last. Applied to God in the OT (Is. 44:6; 48:12). Though God, Jesus is also fully human (see Phil. 2:6–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 18, 'Hades. The grave (Job 17:13; Song 8:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 19, 'have seen. See v. 11. Completion of the vision.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 1, 20, 'angels. Could mean the guardian angels who watch over the churches (see Dan. 10:13, 20), or the respective human leaders of those churches. See Rev. 2:1, 8, etc.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 1, 'These seven messages are in the form of ancient letters. Addressed to seven churches in Roman Asia, the messages have universal value (2:7, 11) and also offer a symbolic preview of major turns in the future of the Christian church. The promises “to him who overcomes” increase in number and value through the progression of these letters (2:7, 11, 17, 27–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 1, 'These seven messages are in the form of ancient letters. Addressed to seven churches in Roman Asia, the messages have universal value (2:7, 11) and also offer a symbolic preview of major turns in the future of the Christian church. The promises “to him who overcomes” increase in number and value through the progression of these letters (2:7, 11, 17, 27–28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 1, 'Ephesus. Most important city of Roman Asia, a great seaport. Paul spent three years there (Acts 20:31). May represent the relative purity of the church in the first century. seven stars … lampstands. See Rev. 1:12, 16, 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 2, 'I know your works. Repeated in each letter. Jesus has full understanding of each situation. you have tested. Believers need to test those who claim to speak for God (1 John 4:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 4, 'first love. Compare Eph. 1:15–16; 6:24. Love is the first thing to slip when one turns away from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 5, 'remove your lampstand. The Bible does not teach “once saved always saved.” Faith and love must be continually nurtured.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 6, 'Nicolaitans. A group within the church who compromised with the popular religious culture. See vv. 14–15. To keep its message and practice clear, a church sometimes has to discipline members.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 7, 'let him hear. The messages to all seven churches can apply to every reader. overcomes. Perseveres in faith and obedience to Christ. tree of life. Allusion to the garden of Eden (Gen. 2:9; 3:22, 24). The original conditions will be restored when sin is removed from the earth (see Rev. 22:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 8, 'Smyrna. Important seaport 30 miles (50 km) north of Ephesus with a large Jewish population in John’s day. May represent the persecuted church of the second and third centuries. Along with the church of Philadelphia (3:7–13), it received no correction from Jesus. the First … came to life. See 1:17–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 9, 'tribulation. This church was suffering persecution. say they are Jews. See Rom. 2:28–29. synagogue of Satan. These harsh words suggest the Jewish population may have led a persecution of Christians in Smyrna.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 10, 'tribulation ten days. See note on 11:2. This may be the ten-year persecution of Christians by Emperor Diocletian, A.D. 303–313. crown. Not the royal crown, but the garland awarded to the winner in athletic contests.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 11, 'second death. See note on 20:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 12, 'Pergamos. 50 miles (80 km) north of Smyrna. Built on a cone-shaped, 1,000-foot (300 m) hill. May represent the church’s compromises with the world in the centuries following Diocletian’s persecutions (A.D. 303–313). two-edged sword. See 1:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 13, 'Satan’s throne. Pergamos was famous for its Great Altar of Zeus. This expression probably refers to that altar, which is now located in a Berlin museum.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 14, 'Balaam. Allusions to the OT narrative in Num. 22–25. to eat things … immorality. Traditional Roman religion centered on idolatry and sexual immorality. Christians were, therefore, tempted to compromise in these areas.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 15, 'Nicolaitans. Christian counterparts of Balaam because of their immoral influence. The names have similar meanings, Balaam meaning “not of the people” and Nicolaitans meaning “destruction of the people.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 17, 'hidden manna. See Ex. 16:1–36. A portion of manna was “hidden” in front of the ark of the covenant as a memorial. white stone. In ancient times, a judge gave a white stone to indicate his decision of legal acquittal or pardon. He presented a black stone if the defendant was guilty. name. See note on Rev. 14:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 18, 'Thyatira. 30 miles (50 km) southeast of Pergamos. May represent the apostasy of the medieval church, but also the progress of the Reformation (v. 19). eyes … fine brass. See 1:14–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 19, 'the last are more than the first. Although the condition of this church is grave, it is improving.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 20, 'Jezebel. Model of wickedness in the OT (1 Kin. 16:31–33; 18:4; 21:25–26; 2 Kin. 9:22). sexual … idols. See notes on Rev. 2:14–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 22, 'tribulation. Those who compromise to avoid suffering find Satan also torments those who serve him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 23, 'her children. Those who follow the teachings of Jezebel; her spiritual children.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 24, 'depths of Satan. Satan tempts people with the promise of greater wisdom if they follow his ways. See Gen. 3:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 27, 'Reference to Ps. 2:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 2, 28, 'morning star. Christ Himself. See 22:16; Dan. 12:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 1, 'Sardis. 30 miles (50 km) southeast of Thyatira; impressive mountaintop acropolis, like Pergamos. It may represent the sterility of Protestant orthodoxy after the Reformation (17th–18th centuries). seven Spirits … seven stars. See 1:4, 20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 3, 'come … as a thief. Second Coming language (Matt. 24:42–43; 1 Thess. 5:2; 2 Pet. 3:10) applied to the local situation of a specific church. The church’s judgment will come at an unexpected time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 4, 'not defiled their garments. Were faithful to Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 5, 'white garments. See v. 18; 6:11; 7:9; 19:8. not blot out. See Ex. 32:32–33. Not take away their heavenly citizenship. the Book of Life. Lists the names of the saved, like an ancient register of citizens. For more on the books used in the JUDGMENT, see 20:15; 21:27; Dan. 7:9–14; Luke 10:20. confess. We have a personal advocate in the judgment. See Matt. 10:32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 7, 'Philadelphia. Means “brotherly love.” 25 miles (40 km) southeast of Sardis. May represent the missionary fervor and gospel advancement of 19th-century Christianity. holy … true. See 6:10; Is. 40:25. key of David. Symbolic of the power to control access to the OT kingdom of David (Is. 22:20–22), a position similar to the chief of staff. Jesus, the Son of David, controls access to the kingdom of God and its storehouse of blessings (Matt. 16:19; 2 Cor. 1:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 8, 'open door. Door of witness or missionary opportunity. See 1 Cor. 16:9; Col. 4:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 9, 'synagogue of Satan. Jewish opponents of the faithful in Philadelphia (see also 2:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 10, 'keep you. God’s protective care for His people. hour of trial. Great time of trouble for God’s people at the end of time (7:14; Dan. 12:1; Matt. 24:21). God will not allow us to face more than we can handle (John 16:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 11, 'I am coming quickly. See 1:1; 22:7, 11–12, 20. For our sakes, God always presents the end as near. Hold fast. Not “once saved always saved” teaching. One can lose one’s salvation by letting go of Christ. This church is called to persevere in faith and action. crown. See note on 2:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 12, 'pillar … temple. The weak (v. 8) become strong and vitally useful. New Jerusalem. See 21:2; 21:9–22:5. The overcomer will be placed at the governing center of the universe.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 14, 'Laodiceans. Laodicea was about 45 miles (70 km) southeast of Philadelphia and 90 miles (140 km) east of Ephesus. It was known for its wealth, medical school, and lukewarm water supply. It may represent the lukewarm condition of the church in today’s world. Witness. See 1:5. Beginning. Greek can mean “first in time” (“beginning”) or “first in rank” (“ruler”). Here the term functions as a title of Jesus who is the Creator and Ruler of all things (1:17; Col. 1:15–16). The phraseology should not be misunderstood to suggest that Christ is a created being, but rather the one who “begins” or initiates the creation of God (John 1:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 16, 'lukewarm. Hot and cold waters are refreshing and medicinal. Lukewarm water is usually disappointing or even repulsive.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 17, 'need of nothing. Monetary wealth is often associated with spiritual poverty; the rich don’t feel their need. Self-deception is the natural human condition (Jer. 17:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 18, 'gold. Represents saving faith (1 Pet. 1:7). white garments. See note on Rev. 3:5. eye salve. So Laodicea can see her own lack of authenticity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 19, 'God’s love is the source of His discipline (see Heb. 12:5–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 20, 'stand … knock. See Song 5:2–6. Laodicea has locked God out. He does not force His way in. Elsewhere in the NT, Christ’s position at the door or at the gates shows the nearness of His Return (Luke 12:36; compare Matt. 24:33; Mark 13:29; James 5:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 3, 21, 'See Phil. 2:6–11. This text sets the stage for the next four chapters, which concern God’s throne (Rev. 4), the Lamb joining God on His throne (chap. 5), and how the saints overcome and join Jesus on His throne (chaps. 6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 1, 'Introduction to the seven seals of 6:1–8:1. See Introduction, “Message.” The immediate focus moves from earthly things (chaps. 2–3) to the heavenly throne room (see 4:1). The primary focus also moves from the “things which are” to the “things which will take place after this” (1:19; 4:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 1, 'Introduction to the seven seals of 6:1–8:1. See Introduction, “Message.” The immediate focus moves from earthly things (chaps. 2–3) to the heavenly throne room (see 4:1). The primary focus also moves from the “things which are” to the “things which will take place after this” (1:19; 4:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 1, 'Chap. 4 does not describe an event, but is a general description of the throne room. The heavenly throne is the central feature of this chapter; it is the symbol of royal authority. God is worthy of worship because He made all things.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 1, 'standing open. The door is already open when John views it. Come up here. In a vision John ascends to heaven.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 2, 'in the Spirit. See note on 1:10. throne. Appears 14 times in the Greek; represents royal power and authority. set. The Greek implies the throne was already there. Note that all three members of the Godhead or Trinity appear in the sanctuary/throne room scenes of chaps. 4–5 (God the Father, 4:2; the Holy Spirit, 4:5; 5:6; Jesus, 5:5–6). See also 1:4–5; 22:16–21. For more on the Trinity, see Matt. 28:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 3, 'rainbow. Symbol of God’s mercy after the flood (Gen. 9:8–17). See also Rev. 10:1; Ezek. 1:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 4, 'twenty-four elders. Probably representatives of redeemed humanity. See Matt. 27:51–53; Eph. 4:8. crowns. See note on Rev. 2:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 5, 'lightnings … voices. See also 8:5; 11:19; 16:18. lamps. Different Greek word here than for “lampstands” in chap. 1, probably indicating that these lamps are not the same as the earlier ones, which were to represent the seven churches. seven Spirits. Symbolic reference to the Holy Spirit. See notes on 1:4–5; 5:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 6, 'sea of glass. Also in 15:2. Perhaps similar to what is described in Ex. 24:10, or the laver (water basin) in the OT Sanctuary (Ex. 30:17–21; 1 Kin. 7:23–25). four living creatures. See Ezek. 1:5–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 7, 'flying eagle. Can be translated eagle or vulture.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 8, 'six wings … Holy, holy, holy. See Is. 6:2–3. Combines features of Ezek. 1 and Is. 6. Who was … to come. See note on Rev. 1:4–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 9, 'Whenever. Suggests that John is giving a general description of the heavenly throne room.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 10, 'cast their crowns. A willing surrender of authority to the only One worthy of worship.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 4, 11, 'God is worshiped because He is Creator.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 1, 'A moment of crisis in the heavenly throne room. In contrast to chap. 4, a specific point in time is in view here. It is heaven’s response to the cross of Christ. This scene takes place in heaven at the ascension of Jesus (see Acts 1:9–11) in A.D. 31.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 1, 'scroll … seven seals. Cannot be read until all seven seals are broken. Possible meanings of the scroll: the Book of Life (13:8; 21:27), the Book of the Covenant (Deut. 17:18–20; 2 Kin. 11:12–17), the title deed to the universe (see Jer. 32:6–15), the judgment scroll of Ezekiel (Ezek. 2:9–3:3), or the record of human history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 2, 'strong angel. See 10:1; 18:2, 21. High ranking and authoritative.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 3, 'heaven … earth … under the earth. Expresses the universal scope of the problem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 5, 'Lion … of Judah. A Messianic title. See Gen. 49:8–10. Root of David. See Is. 11:1, 10. The Messiah comes from the line of David. prevailed. Same word as “overcame” in 3:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 6, 'Lamb. Used 28 times in Revelation to refer to the crucified, risen, and glorified Jesus Christ. slain. Sacrificed for sin (Is. 53:7; John 1:29). seven horns. The Lamb is all-powerful (Dan. 7:8, 20, 24). seven eyes … seven Spirits of God. The Holy Spirit who bears witness to Christ (John 15:26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 7, 'By taking the scroll, the Lamb demonstrates that judgment and authority on earth have been assigned to Him.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 8, 'golden bowls … incense. OT sanctuary images. prayers. The actions of the Lamb are in response to the prayers of the saints.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 9, 'new song. In response to the one-time act of the cross. The sacrifice of Christ on the cross is the decisive moment of the cosmic conflict (see 12:7–10). In 4:11 worship occurs on account of creation. Here it occurs on account of salvation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 10, 'kings and priests. See note on 1:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 11, 'ten thousand … thousands. An indefinitely large number. See Dan. 7:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 5, 12, 'These verses look forward to the glorious conclusion of the cosmic conflict. The Lamb and the Father receive worship and praise together.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 1, 'Focus moves from events in heaven to the events on earth that occur as results of the breaking of the seven seals on the scroll. These events fit within the context of the curses of the covenant (Lev. 26:21–26; Deut. 32:23–25, 41–43; Ezek. 14:12–21) and Jesus’ end-time sermon (Matt. 24; Mark 13; Luke 21). They span events from the cross (Rev. 5:5–6) to the Second Coming (6:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 1, 'The series of four horses recalls Zech. 1:8–17; 6:1–8. Colors related to the character of each rider.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 1, 'Lamb. See notes on 5:6–7. one of … creatures. See notes on 4:6–8. voice like thunder. Probably the lion-like creature of 4:6–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 2, 'white horse. Probably represents the victory of Christ or the gospel, as these symbols are positive in Revelation: “white” (3:4–5; 19:11), “crown” (2:10; 14:14), “conquering” (overcoming; 3:21; 5:5). See Ps. 45:3–5. Unlike the other three, this horse produces no afflictions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 4, 'fiery red. May represent war and bloodshed. peace. Probably the peace of the gospel (1:4). kill one another. Civil war. See Matt. 10:34–36; Luke 12:49–51. Rejection of the gospel produces strife, persecution, and confusion.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 5, 'scales. A crossbar with scales at either end for determining weights.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 6, 'quart. About 1 liter. Roughly the amount of grain a person eats in a day. denarius. Daily wage of a laborer. These are famine conditions, perhaps famine for the word of God (Amos 8:11). Grain, oil, and wine were the three main crops of Canaan (Deut. 7:13; Hos. 2:8, 22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 8, 'pale. Yellowish-green, the ashen appearance of the dead. Hades. The grave (Job 17:13; Song 8:6). Rejection of the gospel results in death. The four horses well represent the initial surge of the gospel followed by the decline and apostasy of the church into the Middle Ages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 9, 'altar. Altar of burnt offering (Ex. 27:1–8; 29:12). souls. In the Hebrew context, the soul is the whole person (see Gen. 2:7). These represent the martyrs of earlier ages (Rev. 6:4), whose shed blood (16:6; John 16:2) cries out to God for justice as did Abel’s (Gen. 4:10). This passage does not comment on the state of man in death but is a symbolic picture of God’s care for the martyrs, His memory of them, and the need for justice and judgment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 10, 'judge and avenge. This cry for God’s JUDGMENT ascends to heaven in 8:3–4; final fulfillment is in 19:1–2. those who dwell on the earth. Description of the wicked in Revelation (3:10; 13:8, 12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 11, 'white robe. Symbol of Christ’s righteousness (3:4–5; Matt. 22:11–13). rest. God’s justice comes at the time of His choosing.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 12, 'The language here recalls the day of the Lord passages in the OT (Is. 13:10–13; 34:4; Ezek. 32:7–8; Amos 8:8–10). great earthquake. See also Rev. 11:13; 16:18. sun … moon … stars. Similar events are described in Matt. 24:29. Bible scholars associated these harbingers of the end time with specific and remarkable events occurring in the 18th and 19th centuries: the great Lisbon earthquake in 1755, the “Dark Day” in 1780, and the famous “falling of the stars” in 1833. Rev. 6:14 makes clear that even greater catastrophes will occur just before the end.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 16, 'wrath. God sometimes allows and sometimes enforces the consequences of sin. This is “anger” in a measured, legal sense, not mindless rage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 6, 17, 'who is able to stand? At the Second Coming. See note on 7:1–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 1, 'There are two sections in the chapter: the 144,000 (vv. 1–8) and the great multitude (vv. 9–17). They are both metaphors for God’s end-time people. Both provide the answer to the question of 6:17: “Who is able to stand?”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 1, 'four angels. May correspond to the four living creatures of 6:1–8. four winds. Agents of God’s judgment; may correspond to the four horses of 6:1–8. earth … sea. Sources of the two beasts of chap. 13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 2, 'another angel. Commander of the four, probably Christ. seal. Sign of God’s ownership and authority (9:4; 14:1; 2 Tim. 2:19). Possible reference to the Sabbath; see note on Rev. 13:16–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 3, 'Do not harm … till. The final judgments on the wicked do not take place until God’s people are ready. See Ezek. 9:1–7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 4, 'I heard the number. Of God’s end-time army. See also 9:16 and context. One hundred and forty-four thousand. 12 × 12 × 1000. Probably represents the totality of Israel. A symbol of God’s end-time people. The New Jerusalem commemorates the twelve tribes and the twelve apostles (21:12–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 5, 'The twelve tribes are not listed in birth order. Joseph and Levi replace Dan and Ephraim in the usual list of twelve (Num. 34:13–29; Josh. 13–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 9, 'looked. John only heard the number of the 144,000, but he sees the great multitude. great multitude. Appears again in 19:1–7. no one could number. Contrast with 144,000. all nations … tongues. Contrast with twelve tribes of Israel. palm branches. Used for feasts and celebrations (Lev. 23:40; John 12:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 13, 'elders. See note on 4:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 14, 'out of the great tribulation. See note on 3:10. washed their robes. Represents the righteousness of Christ which is received by faith that expresses itself in righteous action (19:7–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 15, 'A description of the condition and activity of God’s redeemed people after the Second Coming of Christ. For more on the NEW EARTH, see 21:1–5; Is. 11:6–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 15, 'the throne … temple. See 3:21. Saved humanity will receive privileged places in the governing center of the universe. serve. Greek form expresses priestly, sacrificial service. Before someone can be trusted to rule, they must learn how to serve. dwell. In Greek means to live in a tent. Used to express His acceptance and protection of them. See 7:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 7, 17, 'Lamb … throne. Re-affirms deity of Christ. Vv. 15–17 use “God” and “Lamb” interchangeably. tear. See 21:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 1, 'silence. Contrast the loud praise of chap. 5. The final act of history is left unsaid, to be presented later.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 2, 'Introduces the seven trumpets, which are judgments on the wicked in response to the prayers of the saints in 6:9–10 (see 9:4, 20–21). They span the time from the cross (the beginning of intercession, see note on 8:3–4) to the Second Coming (11:15–19). The combined imagery of 8:3–5 is that of intercession followed by judgment. See Introduction, “Message.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 2, 'trumpets. In the OT, trumpets are used in worship, battle, and celebrations. Here they are associated with prayer and introduce judgment. See Num. 10:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 3, 'Probably based on the daily service in the OT sanctuary, which occurred in the courtyard and Holy Place (Ex. 29:38–42; 30:1–8). Introductory visions in Revelation use imagery from the Holy Place until 11:19, where the focus shifts to the Most Holy Place. See Introduction, “Message” and “Outline.” See note on 11:19. censer. A fire pan for burning incense. altar. Golden incense altar of the OT sanctuary (9:13; Ex. 30:1–8). incense. Probably represents the righteousness of Christ, which makes the prayers of the saints acceptable to God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 5, 'The response to the prayers of the saints is judgment on the wicked. See Ezek. 10:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 7, 'hail … earth. Recalls Ex. 9:22–26 and Ezek. 38:22–23. a third. Indicates judgment that is not full and final. See also Rev. 8:8–12; 9:15, 18. May represent the judgments on Jerusalem in the first century (see Luke 21:20–24; 23:28–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 8, 'something like. Indicates the language is not to be taken literally here. great mountain … fire. Volcanic imagery. See Jer. 51:24–25, 63–64. blood. Allusion to Ex. 7:17–21. May represent the fall of Rome in the fifth century A.D.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 10, 'star fell. See note on 9:1. See also Is. 14:12–15. rivers … springs. Images of spiritual nourishment (Ps. 1:3; John 7:37–39). Wormwood. Bitter-tasting plant; a metaphor for idolatry (Deut. 29:17–18). May represent the apostasy of the church in the Middle Ages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 12, 'Recalls Ex. 10:21–23. Intensity of light reduced. sun … moon … stars. Images of God’s blessing (Ps. 84:11; Is. 30:26). May represent the deepening apostasy of the Middle Ages or the skepticism of the Renaissance and the Enlightenment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 8, 13, 'angel. Many Greek manuscripts have “eagle” or “vulture” here. Woe. Disaster or judgment. The three woes correspond to the fifth through seventh trumpets (9:12; 11:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 1, 'The fifth trumpet uses the imagery of a demonic attack to demonstrate Satan’s tormenting power in the lives of the wicked (vv. 5–6), but also his helplessness against the faithful people of God (v. 4). This section may represent the reality and spiritual consequences of the secularism and evolutionary skepticism that have become increasingly dominant since the 19th century. It has also been interpreted as the political and military impact of militant non-Christians on apostate Christendom.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 1, 'star fallen. Refers back to the star of the third trumpet (8:10). The events of the fifth trumpet are a consequence of the third. was given. Divine passive. God is in full control of this plague; see also 9:3, 5. bottomless pit. Where the demons are confined (Luke 8:31). Place from which the beasts come (Rev. 11:7; 17:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 2, 'smoke … furnace. Recalls Sodom and Sinai (Gen. 19:28; Ex. 19:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 3, 'locusts. Recalls the plague on Egypt (Ex. 10:12–15; see also Joel 2:1–11). scorpions. Demonic imagery; see Luke 10:17–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 4, 'seal of God. See note on 7:2. Demons cannot harm God’s faithful people. See also Rom. 8:35–39. Normal locusts harm vegetation; these attack people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 5, 'five months. May represent the lifespan of locusts, the duration of the flood (Gen. 7:24; 8:3), or 150 years (see note on year-day principle in Rev. 11:2). In those days … not find it. This plague results in psychological, rather than physical, torment.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 7, 'Horrific collection of images that recalls Joel 2:1–11. Heightens the horror of this plague.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 10, 'tails. Instruments of torture. Represent lying prophets in Is. 9:15. Satan uses false beliefs to torment people. five months. See note on 9:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 11, 'angel. Probably Satan. The actions of Satan and demons are subject to God’s limits and control. Literal locusts have no leader (Prov. 30:27), so these locusts are figurative. Abaddon … Apollyon. Hebrew and Greek names that mean “destroyer.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 12, 'woe. See note on 8:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 13, 'The sixth trumpet is parallel to the sixth bowl of 16:12–16: a mixture of demonic and military language combined with a reference to the river Euphrates. The sixth trumpet, therefore, describes events leading up to the battle of Armageddon. It may represent end-time religious opposition to God and His people. The sixth trumpet has also been interpreted in relation to the Middle East.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 13, 'voice. Probably Christ’s. golden altar. See note on 8:3–4. Intercession is still going on; see also 10:11 and 11:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 14, 'four angels. Recalls 7:1–3. Euphrates. See 16:12. The northeastern boundary of the promised land (Gen. 15:18). Beyond it lay ancient Babylon and Assyria (Is. 8:5–8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 15, 'the hour … year. Has been interpreted as a span of 391 years (based on 12 months of 30 days). See notes on 11:2 and Dan. 7:25. Another view is that the expression refers to a specific point in time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 16, 'two hundred million. Demonic counterpart of God’s army (144,000). I heard … them. See 7:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 17, 'Parallels description of vv. 7–9. their mouth … tails. Adds a new weapon (mouth) to the power of the previous plague (v. 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 9, 20, 'References to OT Babylon (Is. 47:9–12; Dan. 5:23–24). murders … thefts. Recalls the sixth, seventh, and ninth commandments (Ex. 20:13–16).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 1, 'Part of the sixth trumpet. See note on 8:13. This section provides a view of God’s people toward the end of history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 1, 'Part of the sixth trumpet. See note on 8:13. This section provides a view of God’s people toward the end of history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 1, 'another mighty angel. See 5:2. This scene also parallels the picture of Christ in 1:13–16. cloud. See 1:7; 14:14; Matt. 17:5; 24:30. rainbow. See note on Rev. 4:3. pillars of fire. See Ex. 13:21–22; 14:24.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 2, 'little book open. Contrast 5:1–4; Dan. 12:4. The scroll is already open when the vision begins. It should be noted that after the little book is opened, the book of Daniel is featured in Revelation in a way that it was not prior to chap. 10. See, for example, the relationships between Dan. 7:7 and Rev. 12:3; 13:1; Dan. 7:14, 27 and Rev. 11:15; Dan. 7:3–6 and Rev. 13:1–2; Dan. 7:13–14 and Rev. 14:14. Consider the issue of worship in Dan. 3 and Rev. 13:4, 8, 12, 15; 14:7, 9, 11, and the connection between the sealed parts of Daniel (8:26; 9:24; 12:4, 9) and Revelation (10:4; 22:10). Compare Dan. 12:7; 7:25 with Rev. 11:2–3; 12:6, 14; 13:5–7; 12:17; 14:12, 7 regarding the persecution of God’s people over time (e.g., 42 months, 1,260 days, etc.). Also compare Dan. 8:11–14, 26 with Rev. 11:1, 19; 13:6 (a focus on the sanctuary/temple).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 3, 'lion. See 5:5. seven thunders. Revelation gives no clue as to their meaning. But see Ps. 29:3–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 4, 'voice from heaven. Not the mighty angel of 10:1–2; either God or Christ.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 5, 'raised up … and ever. Strong allusion to Dan. 12:4–7. delay no longer. Literally “time will be no more.” This is not the end of history, but the point in history where the time prophecies of Dan. 8–12 come to an end (Dan. 8:13–14; 12:7–12). Using the historicist method of interpretation, many scholars identify the end of two of Daniel’s major prophecies (1,260-day and 2,300-day; see note on Dan. 12:4) in the years A.D. 1798 and 1844. The period that follows is “the time of the end” (Dan. 11:40; 12:4, 9). See note on 11:2 concerning the year-day principle.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 7, 'but. Despite the declaration of v. 6, Daniel’s “time of the end,” time continues. mystery of God. The gospel (Rom. 16:25–27; Eph. 3:3–7). finished. Preaching of the gospel ends just before the seventh trumpet. declared. In Greek, the verb form of “gospel.” The “time of the end” includes the final proclamation of the gospel (see Rev. 14:6–7).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 8, 'book. Understood to be the prophecies of Daniel (see Dan. 12:4), because the book of Daniel has now become a strong emphasis in Revelation (see note on 10:2). bitter … honey. John’s experience eating the book of Daniel was similar to that of Ezekiel (see Ezek. 2:8–3:3), and prophetically anticipates the time when Daniel’s prophecies came to an end in the mid-19th century. At that time, many looked for the Second Coming of Christ and were “bitterly” disappointed. See note on 10:5–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 10, 11, 'prophesy again about. The last word could also be translated “to,” as it is in 14:6. In fact, the similar wording of 14:6 (“nation, tribe, tongue, and people”) indicates that the content of that passage is the message that needs to be given. John’s experience and responsibility become representative of God’s end-time remnant. Those who experienced the bitterness of disappointment in regard to Daniel’s time prophecies must continue to preach and prophesy, as they are represented as doing in the three angels of 14:6–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 1, 'Part of the sixth trumpet; see note on 8:13. Explains the “prophesy again” of 10:11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 1, 'reed. Bamboo-like cane. See 21:15 and Ezek. 40:3–5. In Ezekiel the temple was measured in order to restore it (Ezek. 43:7–9). altar. Burnt-offering altar in the court outside the OT sanctuary. those who worship there. The people of God, the remnant, who must “prophesy again” (Rev. 10:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 2, 'But. Really “and” in Greek. The measuring is a work of judgment, separating the faithful from the unfaithful. leave out. Same word as “cast out” in John 9:34–35. tread … underfoot. See Rev. 14:20; 19:15; Luke 21:24. the holy city. See Rev. 21:2. Here represents the faithful people of God, in contrast to the great city (11:8). forty-two months. See also 13:5. Same as 1,260 days (11:3; 12:6) or 3 1/2 years (12:14; Dan. 7:25 and 12:7). Prophetic days often mean literal years (Lev. 25:1–7; Num. 14:34; Ezek. 4:4–6). Historicist interpreters, therefore, have generally understood the period of 1,260 prophetic days to mean 1,260 literal years running from A.D. 538 to 1798. See also note on Dan 7:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 3, 'Has been applied to the French Revolution (enemy of Scripture) and the final proclamation of the gospel.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 3, 'two witnesses. Based on Moses and Elijah (see vv. 5–6). Could represent Scripture (Law and Prophets) or God’s people (Acts 1:8). one thousand … days. See notes on Rev. 11:2 and Dan 7:25. sackcloth. Signifies mourning (Gen. 37:34; Is. 22:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 4, 'two olive … lampstands. See Zech. 4:2–3. Trees (Rev. 9:4) and lampstands (1:20) represent God’s people in Revelation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 5, 'fire … enemies. 2 Kin. 1:10–12. shut heaven. 1 Kin. 17:1. blood. Ex. 7:17–21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 7, 'finish. See 10:7. bottomless pit. See note on 9:1. kill them. The same fate as their Lord in 11:8. in the street. Denial of burial shows great disrespect (Ps. 79:2–13; Jer. 8:1–2). the great city. Usually Babylon in Revelation (14:8; 18:1–4). Symbol of end-time, religious opposition to God. where … crucified. Jerusalem.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 10, 'those who … earth. See note on 6:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 11, 'breath of life. Gen. 2:7; Ezek. 37:5–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 12, 'ascended … cloud. Acts 1:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 13, 'great earthquake. See 6:12. the rest. “Remnant.” See 12:17. afraid … glory. The response called for in 14:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 14, 'woe. See note on 8:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 16, 'twenty-four elders. See note on 4:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 17, 'One who … to come. 1:4, 8; 4:8; 16:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 18, 'While the seventh trumpet signals the close of human probation (10:7), there are still events that must take place before the Second Coming. nations … angry. See 12:17. Your wrath. The seven bowl plagues (see 15:1). dead … judged. The JUDGMENT of the dead is detailed in 20:11–15. reward. 22:11–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 11, 19, 'Sanctuary introduction to chaps. 12–14. The reference is to the heavenly sanctuary. Up to this point in Revelation all the sanctuary introductions have alluded to the Holy Place of the sanctuary. This one, as indicated by the presence of the ark, moves to the Most Holy Place. For more on the SANCTUARY, see Ex. 25:8–9; Dan. 8:14. the ark. God’s “throne” in the OT sanctuary (Ex. 25:10–22). Contained the Ten Commandments (Ex. 20:1–17) which are featured in Rev. 12:17; 14:12. lightnings … hail. See note on 4:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 1, 'An overview of history from the birth of Christ to the final events. Includes allusions to the original conflict in heaven (vv. 4, 7–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 1, 'sign. Noun form of “signified” in 1:1. The vision to follow uses much symbolism. woman. Represents the people of God (Song 6:10; Is. 54:5–6), both before (Rev. 12:5) and after (v. 6) the earthly ministry of Jesus. clothed … sun. See 10:1. twelve stars. Twelve tribes of Israel. See 21:12–14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 2, 'in pain … birth. See Gen. 3:16; Mic. 4:8–10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 3, 'dragon. Represents Satan (v. 9; Gen. 3:15). Also represents the civil power of Rome (Rev. 12:5; Matt. 2:3–18). seven … horns. Allusion to the total number of heads and horns in Dan. 7:3–7. diadems. Royal crowns.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 4, 'third. Satan encouraged the rebellion of one-third of the angels (see 1:20) in heaven before creation (13:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 5, 'male Child. Reference to the birth of Christ. See also Ps. 2:7–9. caught up. Ascension of Christ (Acts 1:8–11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 6, 'Parallels v. 14. woman. Ongoing experience of the church. wilderness. Place of protection (Hos. 2:14). one thousand … days. See notes on Rev. 11:2 and Dan. 7:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 7, 'war … in heaven. Immediate context is the ascension of Christ and His enthronement (vv. 5, 10). But allusions to Is. 14:12–19 and Gen. 3:1–15 indicate a flashback to the original war in heaven, because through the incarnation Christ forever settled the issues raised by this war. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21. For more on the origin of SIN, see Ezek. 28:12–19. Michael. Christ. serpent. Recalls Gen. 3:1–15. cast out. Satan’s influence focused on earth as a result of the war and Adam’s fall; he and his angels were excluded from heaven on account of the cross and the ascension (Rev. 12:10–12; compare John 12:31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 10, 'kingdom … Christ. The enthronement of Christ at the ascension (3:21; 5:1–14). accuser … cast down. Though expelled from heaven at the beginning, Satan’s accusations retained influence in heaven until the cross (Job 1:9–11). His accusations are no longer to be taken seriously (Rom. 8:35–39; Col. 2:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 11, 'overcame him. This verse offers a “how to” of overcoming (see 2:7). blood … death. Believers overcome through faith in Christ’s death and resurrection, which frees them from the fear of death, enabling them to present a fearless testimony.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 12, 'Woe. The earth now has Satan’s undivided attention. short time. While evil and suffering may seem endless, their doom is sure.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 13, 'The scene returns to earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 14, 'two wings … nourished. Allusions to the exodus (Ex. 19:4). time … time. Strong allusion to Dan. 7:25 and 12:7. See notes on Rev. 11:2 and Dan. 7:25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 15, 'serpent … mouth. Allusion to the deceptive words of the serpent in Gen. 3:1–6. earth helped. Toward the end of the 1,260 years (see note on Rev. 11:2), many forces combined to bring an end to much religious persecution: the Reformation, the Enlightenment, and the American Revolution.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 12, 17, 'dragon … offspring. Two sides in the final conflict on earth. war. Not only will the commandments be important at the end of time, but there will be a conflict over them. This verse sets the stage for the next two chapters, which feature the agents and activities of the dragon in chap. 13 and the identity and message of the last-day woman (the church) in chap. 14. For more on the REMNANT of Israel, see Is. 10:20–34; Rom. 11:2–5. On the character of the REMNANT, see 2 Pet. 3:10–14. On its mission, see Rev. 14:6–12; 18:1–4. For more on obedience to God’s LAW, see 14:12; Ex. 20:1–17; Matt. 5:17–19; 1 John 2:3–6. For more on prophecy in the end time, see 19:10. On other SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16. the rest of her offspring. Same as “remnant,” one of several designations for the end-time people of God. Two chief characteristics of the end-time remnant: obedience to the commandments of God and possession of the testimony of Jesus Christ or the “spirit of prophecy” (see Rev. 19:10)—a visionary gift like John’s (see note on 1:2). In addition, the context suggests that the REMNANT appear in history after the 1,260 days/years (12:14; see note on 11:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 1, 'This chapter elaborates on chap. 12, particularly the end-time war of the last verse (12:17). In this chapter the dragon gathers two allies for the final conflict. The beast from the sea (an apparent parody of Christ) and the beast from the earth (an apparent parody of the Holy Spirit) suggest a false trinity (16:13–14) in collusion to deceive the world (13:13–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 1, 'Written in past tense, this section describes the history of the sea beast before its end-time activity. It arises after the dragon (imperial Rome) and usurps the work of Christ. Protestant scholars through the centuries have identified this beast with the papacy of the Middle Ages. (Compare descriptions with Dan. 7:3–7, 25; 8:11–14.)');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 1, 'I stood. I.e., John. Most manuscripts say “he [the dragon] stood.” seven … horns. See note on 12:3. This beast looks like the dragon (compare John 14:9). crowns. The crowns have shifted from the heads to the horns (see Rev. 12:3; Dan. 7:4–8). blasphemous name. A religious power in opposition to God and His people. See Dan. 7:8, 25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 2, 'the beast … lion. A composite image of Daniel’s four beasts (Dan. 7:4–8). dragon gave … authority. See Matt. 28:18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 3, 'mortally wounded. Literally, “slaughtered to death,” an allusion to the cross (v. 8). healed. Recovers, almost miraculously, from an otherwise mortal wound. marveled. The beast’s reappearance at the end is a surprise.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 4, 'Who … beast? The deliberate contrast to Christ is striking, for the name Michael (Christ; 12:7) means “Who is like God?”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 5, 'Clear allusions to Daniel (Dan. 7:8, 20–21, 25; 8:9–12). was given … was granted. Divine passives. God is in ultimate control. forty-two months. See notes on Rev. 11:2 and Dan. 7:25. tabernacle. The heavenly sanctuary. Whatever the boasts of this beast’s power, God monitors and controls earth’s history from the vantage point of the heavenly sanctuary/throne room (see Introduction, “Message”; and Rev. 4–5). authority … nation. Area of beast’s control is object of final gospel proclamation (14:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 8, 'Verbs switch to present and future tenses. The healed and revived beast is central to the final events. Book of Life. See note on 3:5. foundation of the world. The pain of the cross has been in the heart of God from the beginning.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 11, 'Written in the past tense, this verse describes the shorter history of the earth beast before its end-time activity. It initially parodies the work of the Holy Spirit, and later becomes the false prophet of 16:13. The initial positive symbolic language (like a lamb) and strong connections with 12:16 (“out of the earth”) have suggested to many interpreters that the description of this beast is a reference to the United States of America, as a Protestant nation with its religious influence. The other 28 references to “lamb” in Revelation refer to Christ. like a dragon. Later actions of this beast are not positive, which contrasts with its earlier lamb-like qualities.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 12, 'Present and future verb tenses. These are the actions of the earth beast in the final battle.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 12, 'he exercises … first beast. The sea beast is reactivated for final events, but the earth beast, an apparent third player in a false end-time trinity, takes a leading and visible role in its behalf. See John 16:13–14. deadly wound. See note on Rev. 13:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 13, 'See Matt. 24:24; 2 Thess. 2:8–10. signs. This earthly power performs persuasive miracles that establish authority. See note on John 2:11. fire … from heaven. Perhaps an intentional similarity to the role of the Holy Spirit in the tongues of fire at Pentecost (Acts 2); as impressive and convincing as Elijah’s fire on Mount Carmel (1 Kin. 18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 14, 'those … earth. See note on 6:10. breath. Same Greek word as “spirit.” Allusion to Gen. 2:7. Rev. 13:15–17 represents the climax of the war of 12:17. image to the beast. A new character in the drama. It functions at the end as a persecutor and an object of worship, the way the sea beast did earlier. This is a strong allusion to the image-worship in Dan. 3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 16, 'mark. The counterfeit of the seal of God, which the Sabbath command represented (Ex. 20:8–11). At the heart of ancient covenants was a seal containing the name, title, and basis for authority of the covenant maker. The Sabbath command plays that role in the Ten Commandments. See note on Rev. 11:19. name. See note on 14:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 13, 18, '666. God’s number in Revelation is seven, so multiples of the number six may represent and emphasize counterfeit and falling short (see notes on vv. 1–17). Note the sixes in Dan. 3:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 1, 'Chap. 13 focuses on the dragon’s side in the war of 12:17. Chap. 14 focuses on the remnant’s side of the final conflict.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 1, 'Mount Zion. Synonym for Jerusalem; allusion to Joel 2:32. one hundred … thousand. Based on Joel 2:32, this group is the same as the “remnant” of Rev. 12:17. See also note on 7:4. name. In the Hebrew context, name represents character. To have the name or seal of God (see 7:1–3) on the forehead (contrast 13:17) means to reflect God’s character in life.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 3, 'new song. See note on 5:9. New songs arise from new experiences. throne … elders. See notes on 4:1–4. no one … song. It requires the experience of salvation. redeemed from the earth. To a heavenly location, either literally after the Second Coming or spiritually during the final crisis of 12:17 (see Eph. 2:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 4, 'virgins. Symbol of spiritual purity and loyalty (2 Cor. 11:2). They are ready for the wedding of the Lamb (Rev. 19:7–8). firstfruits. First portion of a larger crop (see 14:14–20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 5, 'no deceit. Lying is excluded from the New Jerusalem (21:27; 22:15). Contrast 13:13–14; 16:14. without fault. First, the 144,000 are accepted in Christ. Second, in following Jesus they have become like Him (see 19:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 6, 'The three last messages to be preached to the earth by the end-time remnant before the Son of Man arrives in the clouds (14:14). These are the messages that were commissioned in 10:11. For more on the mission of the REMNANT, see 18:1–4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 6, 'gospel. Fulfills Matt. 24:14. The first of three angels who proclaim God’s final message to the world. In the context of Revelation, the gospel includes the good news of Christ’s Second Coming, in which He rescues His people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 7, 'Fear God. Means “revere, respect, hold in awe” (Ps. 111:10; Prov. 3:7; 9:10). But it can also mean “Be afraid” for those who do not take Him seriously (Rev. 6:15–17). hour … has come. The final judgment begins before the Second Coming. It is the context for the final proclamation of the gospel. For the various stages of God’s JUDGMENT, see note on Rom. 14:10, 12. worship … sea. This language makes an unmistakable allusion to the SABBATH command with reference to CREATION (Ex. 20:8–11), thus indicating that the Sabbath has particular relevance in end-time gospel proclamation. To rest from one’s works is the ideal response to the gospel (see Heb. 4:1–11). The admonitions to “fear” and to “worship” in this verse are placed directly in the larger immediate context of keeping God’s commandments (see Rev. 12:17; 14:12), with obvious references to the Decalogue.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 8, 'Babylon … great city. The first mention of Babylon, the focus of God’s judgments in Rev. 16–18. See note on 11:7–8. This is a condemnation of corporate opposition to God. Gospel and judgment are closely related in Scripture (see John 3:18–21). all nations. End-time Babylon precipitates worldwide opposition to God. Babylon in the OT also represented the oppressor of God’s people and “confusion” (see Gen. 11:1–9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 9, 'If anyone. The focus shifts to individual responsibility in the final crisis. See note on v. 8. worships … hand. Recalls 13:12–18. wrath of God. The seven bowl plagues of chap. 16 (see 11:18; 15:1). cup … indignation. Allusion to Is. 51:17–23. brimstone. Sulphur. See also Rev. 19:20; Gen. 19:24. forever. An obvious allusion to Is. 34:8–10. In the Hebrew context, forever is not necessarily absolute; it is long enough to accomplish God’s purpose. For more on HELL and the punishment of the wicked, see Rev. 20:10; Matt. 10:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 12, 'patience. Endurance in the context of suffering (13:9–10). Might also imply patiently waiting for Christ’s return. keep the commandments of God. Obedience to God’s law is an essential characteristic of God’s people in the end time. faith of Jesus. Can mean “faith in Jesus” or “Jesus’ faith,” meaning what Jesus believed—the doctrines held and taught by Jesus. V. 12 has a contextual relation to 12:17 and the conflict pictured in chap. 13. For more on obedience to God’s LAW, see 12:17; Ex. 20:1–17; Matt. 5:17–19; 1 John 2:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 13, 'Blessed. See note on 1:3. from now on. Those who die in this context (14:6–12) will not have to experience the trials of the final events. But this may be a reflection of the persecution of 13:15. their works follow them. Their salvation is secure. See 1 Tim. 5:24–25.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 14, 'cloud … Son of Man. The travels of the Son of Man are often portrayed in the context of clouds, as in His coming to judge (Dan. 7:13–14), His ascension (Acts 1), and His Second Coming (Matt. 24:30). crown. See note on Rev. 2:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 15, 'temple. Recalls 11:19. harvest. See Joel 3:12–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 18, 'angel … fire. Probably the angel of 8:3–5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 19, 'winepress. Usually carved out of rock. An upper trough for pressing grapes with a channel to a lower trough for collecting juice. wrath of God. Anticipates the seven bowl plagues of 15:1–16:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 14, 20, 'outside the city. The city is Jerusalem. See Joel 3:12–17. one thousand six hundred furlongs. About 180 miles (300 km). Approximate length of Palestine from north to south. This is a multiple of the number four, which represents worldwide events (Rev. 7:1–3; 14:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 1, 'Climax of chaps. 12–14 and introduction to the seven bowl plagues.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 1, 'seven last plagues. 16:1–21. complete. See 11:18. Up to now God has always mitigated judgment with mercy.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 2, 'sea … fire. See note on 4:6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 3, 'song of Moses. Recalls Ex. 15:1–18. The judgments of the end are compared with the events of the exodus. Just … ways. God’s actions at the end are acknowledged to be in full harmony with His law and character. This is the bottom line of the cosmic conflict.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 4, 'fear … glorify … worship … judgments. Recalls the key words of 14:7. The last message has received a positive response from many.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 5, 'After … looked. Major break. Vv. 5–8 is a sanctuary introduction to the seven plagues of chap. 16. temple … testimony. A combined reference to Solomon’s temple (1 Kin. 6–8) and the Mosaic tabernacle (Ex. 25–40). “Testimony” refers to the Ten Commandments given to Moses and placed in the tabernacle (Ex. 31:18; 32:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 6, 'out of the temple. The plagues are the consequence of violating the governing principles of the universe. pure bright linen. In most cases violence is repugnant, but in this case it is required by pure, spotless justice.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 7, 'four living creatures. See notes on 4:6–8. wrath of God. See 15:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 15, 8, 'no one … completed. See Ex. 40:34–35; 1 Kin. 8:10–11. An empty temple indicates the end of heavenly intercession, signaling the close of human probation on earth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 1, 'Many parallels with seven trumpets of chaps. 8–11. Trumpets affect thirds of the earth; these plagues affect the whole earth. The first four bowl plagues are probably to be taken literally, as there are no clear symbolic meanings. The last three bowl plagues do have clear symbolic implications.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 1, 'pour out. Connects this scene with 15:5–8. Events in heaven affect events on earth. The setting of chap. 16 is a series of events beyond the close of probation.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 2, 'sore. Same word as “boils” of Ex. 9:9–11 and indicates leprosy in Lev. 13:18–27. This is probably literal. came upon … image. Recipients of the plagues are the oppressors of Rev. 13:14–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 3, 'See 8:8–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 4, 'blood. Instead of bitterness; compare 8:10–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 5, 'The punishment here is appropriate to the crime and recalls the question of 6:10. It also contains many echoes of 15:3–4. angel of the waters. Same as the angel of third bowl. who is … to be. See 1:4, 8; 4:8; 11:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 8, 'scorch men. In contrast with the fourth trumpet (8:12), here the sun’s intensity is increased. blasphemed … God … did not repent. Reverse of 11:13, where judgments led to repentance. Nothing God does changes the hearts of the wicked anymore. The close of probation is grounded in human choice. glory. See 14:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 10, 'throne. Seat of authority, normally associated with God in Revelation. beast. See 13:2. darkness. Not literal, as it is somehow a challenge to the beast’s authority. The plagues are undermining the beast’s control of the earth (13:12).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 12, 'Euphrates. See note on 9:14. The river was Babylon’s defense system (Jer. 50:35–38). Here it represents the political powers of the world (Rev. 17:1, 15). dried up. See Is. 44:24–28. Indicates Babylon’s loss of political support (Rev. 17:16). east. Literally, “sunrise.” See Matt. 24:27; Luke 1:78.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 13, 'The symbolism implies the possibility of human decision, so these verses describe events prior to the sixth plague and Armageddon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 13, 'unclean spirits. See 18:2. Agents of the dragon, beast, and false prophet. frogs. Recalls Ex. 8:1–9. dragon … beast … false prophet. The false trinity of Rev. 12–13. Together they make up end-time Babylon (16:19). They meet their end in 19:20; 20:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 14, 'demons. Satanic angels and counterparts to the three angels of 14:6–12. signs. See 13:13–14. kings of the earth. Equivalent to Euphrates River of 16:12, as defined in 17:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 15, 'thief. Recalls Matt. 24:43–44; Luke 12:38–40; 1 Thess. 5:1–6. Blessed. See note on Rev. 1:3. garments … naked … shame. Allusion to 3:17–18.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 16, 'they. Or “he.” Armageddon. A Hebrew/Greek mixture probably meaning “mountain of Megiddo.” Could recall Mount Carmel (1 Kin. 18) or OT battles at Megiddo (Judg. 5:19). Here the intention is not to identify a geographical location but to indicate a decisive battle between good and evil.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 17, 'out of the temple. See 15:6. throne. There is no distinction in Revelation between the heavenly temple and God’s throne room.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 18, 'noises … earthquake. See 4:5; 8:5; 11:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 19, 'great city. See note on 11:8. three parts. See 16:13. Babylon. Summary fulfillment of 14:8–11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 16, 21, 'a talent. About 75 pounds (34 kg). blasphemed. Confirms unwillingness of the wicked to repent during the plagues.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 1, 'This chapter elaborates on the sixth and seventh plagues (16:12–21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 1, 'one of … bowls. Probably the sixth bowl angel (16:12), as the waters of this verse are the Euphrates River. harlot. Prostitute. See 17:4–5. many waters. See v. 15; Jer. 51:13. The river of Babylon (Euphrates).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 2, 'See 14:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 3, 'A vision of the prostitute Babylon riding on a beast.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 3, 'in the Spirit. See note on 1:10. wilderness. See 12:6, 14. woman. Same as the prostitute of 17:1, 5. scarlet beast. See 12:3 and 13:1–2. Represents worldwide political power in support of end-time Babylon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 4, 'There are many parallels between the prostitute Babylon and the OT high priest (Ex. 28:1–43). A counterfeit end-time religious system (see Rev. 16:13, 19), the heir of the medieval union of church and state. forehead a name. In Hebrew thought, a reflection of character.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 6, 'the martyrs of Jesus. See 6:9–11. The medieval church produced many martyrs.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 7, 'Explanation of the vision of vv. 3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 7, 'angel. The angel of v. 1 explains the vision of vv. 3–6. carries her. She is in a position of temporary dominance (until v. 16). Religion controls the political realm, just as it did in the Middle Ages.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 8, 'ascend … pit. See 11:7. perdition. The eternal destruction of the wicked. Book of Life. See note on 3:5. was, and is not. A parody of God (1:4; 4:8).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 10, 'one is. The seven heads are consecutive. Since John is the point of reference, the “one is” would be the Rome of his day (v. 18). The five fallen kings would then be Egypt, Assyria, Babylon, Medo-Persia, and Greece.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 11, 'eighth. The eighth head, the beast of the final crisis. of the seven. The summation and climax of the prior seven, it functions as an oppressive power.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 12, 'ten horns. See vv. 3–11; 12:3; 13:2; Dan. 7:7–8. no kingdom as yet. These powers did not exist in John’s day. with the beast. Time of the eighth head (Rev. 17:11), the final crisis.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 14, 'Lord … kings. See 19:16–21. called … faithful. The saints, who are sub-kings with the Lamb (1:6; 5:10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 15, 'waters … sits. See note on v. 1. The River Euphrates represents end-time, worldwide political power. ten horns. See note on vv. 12–13. hate … fire. This event is parallel to the drying up of the River Euphrates in 16:12. burn her. See Lev. 21:9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 17, 'God is ultimately in control of the events of history, including satanic actions (9:1–5; 2 Thess. 2:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 17, 18, 'great city. See note on 11:8. Rome was the Babylon of John’s day. End-time Babylon will be a religious counterfeit of gospel faith. See notes on 13:1–7, 18; 14:8; 17:4–5; 18:1–4. reigns. Continuous present tense. The great city lies behind all opposition to God throughout history.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 1, 'The fall of Babylon theme continues from chap. 17. There the key image is the execution of a prostitute (17:1, 4–5, 16). Here the metaphor is the sacking of a wealthy city (18:9–19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 1, 'another angel. Different from 17:1. illuminated. Foretaste of the New Earth (21:23; 22:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 2, 'Babylon … fallen. Not yet the physical fall, but a declaration of her spiritual condition. dwelling place … bird. This demonic character is startling in an organization that proclaims itself on God’s side (see note on 17:18). drunk. See 14:8; 17:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 4, 'my people. God distinguishes between an organization which opposes Him and individuals in that organization who are faithful to God. This is the final call of the gospel to join God’s remnant people. For more on the REMNANT, see 12:17; 14:6–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 5, 'For. In Greek “because.” Vv. 5–7 list the actions that have provoked Babylon’s legal sentence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 6, 'Render … to you. The punishment fits the crime. repay her double. An ancient metaphor for full and final punishment (Jer. 17:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 7, 'glorified … luxuriously. Her glory and luxury came at the expense of others, so it becomes a basis of her legal sentence.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 8, 'plagues. The plagues of chap. 16 are still future at this point. fire. See 17:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 9, 'Describes the outcome of the sentence pronounced in vv. 5–8 in the style of an ancient lament (see Lamentations; Ezek. 27).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 9, 'kings of the earth … weep. The political rulers of the world caused Babylon’s destruction (17:16), but now mourn the consequences of their action. lived luxuriously with her. See 17:2. one hour. Expresses the rapidness of the final destruction (compare 18:17, 19).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 11, 'merchants of the earth. The economic side of Babylon was not emphasized in chap. 17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 12, 'Similar list in Ezek. 27:12–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 16, 'clothed. See 17:4–5. fine linen. The dress of the righteous (19:8, 14). Babylon uses a Christian face to deceive the world (16:14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 18, 'like this. See note on 13:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 19, 'threw dust. Expression of great sorrow (see Ezek. 27:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 20, 'Babylon’s fall described from the perspective of her victims.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 21, 'The finality of Babylon’s fall.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 21, 'Recalls Jer. 51:59–64.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 22, 'Babylon’s fall is complete—no more music, workmanship, food production, or marriage. bridegroom. Contrast 19:7–9. sorcery. Magical arts. See 9:21; 21:8; 22:15. deceived. See 13:13–14; 16:14; 19:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 18, 24, 'blood. See 6:10; 17:6; 19:2. The main ground for Babylon’s punishment is the way she treated God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 1, 'Elaborates the celebration over Babylon’s fall in 18:20. The actions that destroyed Babylon in chap. 18 delivered the people of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 1, 'great multitude. See 7:9–17. Alleluia. Based on Hebrew for “praise the Lord.” Salvation … our God. Recalls 7:10, 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 2, 'true … judgments. Recalls wording of 15:3–4 and 16:5–7. For other attributes of GOD THE FATHER, see Ex. 34:6–7. judged … avenged. This text announces the final fulfillment of what was requested in Rev. 6:9–10. For more on JUDGMENT, see Dan. 7:9–14, 22, 26–27. great harlot … fornication. Recalls Rev. 17:1–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 3, 'smoke … ever. Recalls 14:11. In chap. 14 individuals are in view; here it is Babylon as a corporate body that is judged.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 4, 'twenty-four elders … living creatures. See notes on 4:4–8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 7, 'wife … ready. Weddings are ready when both the bride and the place are prepared. The focus here is on the readiness of the bride; in John 14:1–3 the focus is on the place. See also Hos. 2:19–20; Eph. 5:31–32.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 8, 'fine linen. See 18:12, 16; 19:14. Defined as “the righteous acts of the saints.” clean and bright. See 15:6. saints. See 21:9–10; Heb. 12:22–23. City and bride are two metaphors for the people of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 9, 'Blessed. See note on 1:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 10, 'Compare 22:8–9. your brethren. Called “prophets” in 22:9. testimony of Jesus. See notes on 1:2 and 12:17. The testimony of Jesus is the prophetic gift. On other SPIRITUAL GIFTS, see Eph. 4:11–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 11, 'This verse answers the question of 13:4. heaven opened. See 4:1. white horse. See note on 6:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 12, 'Another description of Jesus. flame of fire. See 1:14. many crowns. These are royal crowns, in contrast to 6:2. no one knew. There are aspects of Christ’s character too deep for us to understand.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 13, 'dipped in blood. Either that of the enemy (14:14–20; Is. 63:1–3) or the atoning blood of Christ. The Word of God. See John 1:1–5, 14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 14, 'fine linen. See note on v. 8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 15, 'sharp sword … rod of iron. Images of Jesus in Revelation (1:16; 2:27; 12:5). The rod of iron is a scepter, a symbol of royal authority. strike the nations. See 14:17–20; 16:17–21. Babylon was dealt with in chaps. 17–18; the attention here is on the rest of the wicked. winepress. See 14:17–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 16, 'KING … LORDS. See 17:14. This scene completes what was begun in 17:12–17. On different aspects of the WORK OF CHRIST, see note on Matt. 1:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 17, 'supper. See Ezek. 39:17–20. Contrast Rev. 19:9. free … great. Recalls 6:15. These verses emphasize the final destruction of earth’s political and military powers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 19, 20, 'beast … false prophet. Components of Babylon (see 16:13, 19) still seem to remain after 17:16 and chap. 18. The description of chaps. 17–19 is parallel and describes the fall of Babylon and its members from different angles. who worked … image. See 13:13–17. lake of fire. See 20:14–15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 1, 'The MILLENNIUM, or one thousand years, is a unique contribution of this chapter. It connects with the biblical picture of the end as follows: at the Second Coming of Jesus, the wicked are destroyed (19:21; 2 Thess. 1:7–8) and the saints go to heaven (John 14:1–3). For a thousand years Satan is confined to this earth (Rev. 20:1–3), the earth itself lies ruined and desolate (v. 5; Jer. 4:23–27), and the saints are judging in heaven (John 14:1–3; 1 Cor. 6:2–3). At the end of the thousand years, the New Jerusalem comes down from heaven (Rev. 21:2; 20:9), the wicked are resurrected (v. 5), Satan is released to deceive the nations and attack the city (vv. 7–9), and the wicked are judged and destroyed by fire (vv. 9–15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 1, 'key … pit. See note on 9:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 2, 'the dragon … Satan. See 12:7–9.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 3, 'cast him … shut him up. Reversal of 9:1–11. must be released. Satan’s release serves God’s purpose as a final demonstration of the character of sin and its consequences.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 4, 'This passage presents an important glimpse of some of what takes place in heaven after the Second Coming of Christ. judgment. See 1 Cor. 6:2–3. For more on various stages of the JUDGMENT, see note on Rom. 14:10, 12. beheaded. Implies physical death and resurrection. their witness … God. See Rev. 6:9. worshiped … hands. See 13:15–17. lived. More accurately “came to life,” physical resurrection (20:5). For more on RESURRECTION, see Matt. 24:31; 1 Cor. 15:51–55; 1 Thess. 4:15–17. reigned with Christ. See Rev. 7:15–17. For more events connected to the MILLENNIUM, see John 14:3; 1 Cor. 6:2–3; Rev. 21:1–5. See also note on 20:1–15. the rest of the dead. The wicked.
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 6, 'Blessed. See note on 1:3. first resurrection. Of all of the righteous at the Second Coming. The wicked are not resurrected until the end of the thousand years (20:5). second death. See note on v. 14. priests … reign. See 1:5–6; 5:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 7, 'when … expired. Although not mentioned here, we know from v. 5 that the wicked dead are raised at this time. prison. Vv. 1–3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 8, 'Gog and Magog. Symbolizes the wicked in rebellion against God. See Ezek. 38–39. gather … to battle. Recalls Armageddon (Rev. 16:14, 16). the sand … sea. All the wicked who ever lived. Contrast Gen. 22:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 9, 'beloved city. Described in detail in chap. 21. This scene happens after the city comes down from heaven (21:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 10, 'lake of fire. See vv. 14–15; 19:20. beast … prophet. See 19:20. tormented … and ever. See note on 14:9–11 and Is. 34:9–10. For more on HELL and the punishment of the wicked, see Matt. 10:28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 11, 'earth … fled. Poetic description of the earth’s destruction by fire. See 2 Pet. 3:10–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 12, 'dead. The wicked dead. The righteous are already saved and in the city. books. Books of evidence upon which the fate of the wicked is confirmed. Book of Life. This is when Phil. 2:10–11 will be fulfilled and the justice of God recognized by the whole cosmos, including the wicked and Satan himself. For more on books used in JUDGMENT, see Rev. 3:5; Ex. 32:33; Mal. 3:16–18; Luke 10:20; Phil. 4:3. For more on various stages of the JUDGMENT, see note on Rom. 14:10, 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 13, 'sea … Death and Hades. The universality of this resurrection; includes all the wicked who ever lived. Hades. The grave (Job 17:13; Song 8:6). according to his works. 2 Cor. 5:10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 20, 14, 'Death … fire. To some degree the lake of fire is figurative. The text is saying there will be no more death and no more grave. second death. The permanent penalty for sin, eternal separation from God. The saved are exempt from this (vv. 6, 15). For more on the second DEATH, see notes on John 11:26 and Rom. 6:23. Death signifies nonexistence rather than endless endurance. For more on HELL and the punishment of the wicked, see Matt. 3:12; 13:42; 25:41; Mark 9:43.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 1, 'This is the climax of chap. 20, but also the introduction to the New Jerusalem vision (21:9–22:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 1, 'new earth. For more on the NEW EARTH, see 7:16–17; Is. 11:6–9; 35:4–10; 65:17, 21–25; 2 Pet. 3:13. no more sea. Such as the oceans we have today. Does not rule out smaller bodies of water in the New Earth. Or perhaps this is symbolic: there will be no sea of opposition to God (see Rev. 17:15).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 2, 'the holy city. The description combines elements of OT Jerusalem, its temple (1 Kin. 6–8), and the garden of Eden (Gen. 1–3). coming down … heaven. See John 14:1–3. The place Jesus is preparing for His people only comes to earth after the thousand years known as the Millennium. For more on the MILLENNIUM, see Rev. 20:4–6. bride. See 19:7–8. The bride is a city full of saved people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 3, 'tabernacle. Recalls the Mosaic tabernacle (Ex. 25–40). Fulfills the covenant language of Ezek. 37:26–28.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 4, 'wipe away every tear. See 7:17. Recovering from the past can take time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 6, 'Alpha … End. See note on 1:8. water of life freely. Anticipates 22:1–5. See note on 22:17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 7, 'He who overcomes. Recalls the overcomer promises of chaps. 2–3. See note on 2:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 8, 'A list of those excluded from the city. See also v. 27; 22:15. The Bible does not teach universal salvation. cowardly. Not the naturally timid, this refers to those who choose personal safety over faithfulness to God in the end-time. abominable. Associated with idolatry. sorcerers. Those who practice magic arts. See 9:21; 18:22–23. lake … death. See note on 20:14.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 9, 'The New Jerusalem is described as if approaching from a distance, with details coming more and more into focus.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 9, 'one of … plagues. See 15:1–8; 17:1. This sets up a series of contrasts between Babylon, the harlot city, and Jerusalem, the bride of the Lamb. bride. See 19:7–8 and note on 21:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 10, 'in the Spirit. See note on 1:10. descending out of heaven. Returns to 21:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 11, 'jasper … crystal. See note on v. 18; see also 4:3–6.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 12, 'gates … twelve tribes. See Ezek. 48:30–35. Along with the twelve foundations, the whole OT and NT people of God are represented. apostles. See Eph. 2:20. The New Jerusalem is closely connected to the church. There is no city without people!');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 15, 'reed. See note on 11:1. There the temple of God is measured. With the glory of God at its center, the New Jerusalem itself functions as the temple (see 21:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 16, 'twelve thousand furlongs. About 1,400 miles (2,200 km). It is not clear if that measurement is of each side of the city or of all four sides combined. equal. The city has the shape of a cube. The only other cube in the Bible is the Most Holy Place of the OT sanctuary (1 Kin. 6:20).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 17, 'one hundred and forty-four. See 7:4–8; 14:1. cubits. A cubit was the length from a man’s elbow to the fingertips. The wall measured 144 cubits, about 210 feet (65 m).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 18, 'jasper. A variety of quartz, usually red.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 19, 'The gems here correspond generally to the gems in the breastplate of the OT high priest. The privileges reserved for the high priest are now freely available to all God’s people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 21, 'pearls. Extremely valuable in the ancient world. street … gold. The floor of Solomon’s temple was overlaid with gold (1 Kin. 6:30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 22, 'no temple. Contrast 7:15. The earlier text may reflect the situation in heaven during the Millennium. Here the symbol has given way to the reality of God’s presence (21:23; 22:1–4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 23, 'Recalls 21:11; Is. 60:19–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 24, 'Based on Is. 60:3–14. gates shall not be shut. Indicates universal access and safety. Everything that could threaten the city and its inhabitants has been defeated.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 21, 27, 'See notes on 21:8. Book of Life. See note on 3:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 1, 'river … life. Recalls Gen. 2:8–14; Ezek. 47:1–12; Zech. 14:8; John 7:37–39. the throne … of the Lamb. The first time this phrase is used in Revelation (it is used again in 22:3). The Lamb’s new status is clearly implied by 3:21.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 2, 'tree of life. See 2:7; Gen. 2:9; 3:22. fruit every month. Recalls Is. 66:22–23 and Ezek. 47:12. healing of the nations. Probably the perfect reconciliation of cultures and ethnic groups because of the final elimination of all traces of sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 3, 'no more curse. Gen. 3:14–19. servants … Him. See Rev. 7:15.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 4, 'They shall see His face. The best thing about eternal life is intimate relationship with the greatest of all personalities. name … foreheads. See note on 14:1. Contrast 13:16–17.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 6, 'faithful and true. Applied to Jesus in 3:14 and 19:11. Applied to the words of Revelation also in 21:6. The book of Revelation is as reliable as Jesus is. must shortly take place. See note on 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 7, 'I am coming quickly. See vv. 12, 20; 3:11; and note on 1:1. Blessed. See note on 1:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 8, 'A comparison with 19:10 shows that prophets have the testimony of Jesus (see 12:17). saw. John also saw the testimony of Jesus (1:2).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 10, 'Do not seal the words. Contrast Dan. 8:26; 12:4, 9. Most of the words of Revelation had immediate relevance to first-century readers and its prophecies were not to be sealed. the time is at hand. See note on Rev. 1:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 12, 'I am coming quickly. See note on v. 7. reward … work. See 20:12–13; 2 Cor. 5:10. We are justified by faith, but the evidence of faith is in the works that follow justification. See James 2:18–22.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 13, 'Here Jesus carries the same titles as the Father in 1:8.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 14, 'Blessed. See note on 1:3. do His commandments. See 12:17; 14:12. Salvation motivates obedience, which is rewarded in eternity.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 15, 'See notes on 21:8. dogs. Often used negatively in the ancient world. See Deut. 23:17–18; Matt. 15:26–27; Phil. 3:2.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 16, 'in the churches. Literally “for” or “to” the churches. The entire book is relevant to the experience of the churches. the Root … of David. See Is. 11:1, 10. Jesus is both the ancestor and the descendant of David, the messianic king. the Bright and Morning Star. See Num. 24:17. Once again, in Rev. 22:16–21, the three members of the Godhead or Trinity are mentioned in relation to one another (God the Father, vv. 18–19; Jesus, vv. 16, 20–21; Holy Spirit, v. 17). See 1:4–5; 4:2, 5; 5:5–6. For more on the Trinity, see Matt. 28:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 17, 'bride. See 19:7–8; 21:2, 9–10. water of life freely. While those who love God long to obey Him, salvation is truly free.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 18, 'While written with Revelation in mind, this warning is appropriate to all of Scripture. prophecy. For more on the prophetic origin of the BIBLE, see 2 Tim. 3:15–17; Heb. 1:1; 2 Pet. 1:20–21. Book of Life. See notes on Rev. 3:5. things … book. See 1:3.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Andrews Study Bible', 'Apocalipse', 22, 20, 'I am coming quickly. See note on v. 7. grace. While Revelation discloses judgments and plagues, it focuses on the salvation offered by God and emphasizes grace at the beginning (1:4) and end.
